('c9a4e42e-0dfd-533d-b0cd-b9ff179011cb'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'The spleen functions include:', '["T cell maturation clinical presentation","Filtering blood and immune response","B cell maturation clinical presentation","Digestion clinical presentation","Hormone production clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Filtering blood and immune response', 'The spleen filters blood, removes old RBCs, and mounts immune responses to blood-borne antigens.'),
('b35d8cc7-a2ea-54b9-bf48-586177a42bfd'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'Which immunoglobulin has the highest molecular weight?', '["IgD","IgE","IgA","IgG","IgM"]'::jsonb, 'IgM', 'IgM (pentamer) has the highest molecular weight (~970 kDa).'),
('6e465f74-45b5-5f98-8924-c3804a5b9c34'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'Which immunoglobulin can fix complement most efficiently?', '["IgG1 and IgG3","IgE","IgA","IgG4","IgG2"]'::jsonb, 'IgG1 and IgG3', 'IgG1 and IgG3 subclasses fix complement most efficiently via the classical pathway.'),
('a4899221-8eae-554b-acda-af78da22164c'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'Class switching of immunoglobulins requires:', '["B cell receptor cross-linking","Antigen alone","T cell help via CD40L","Complement","Cytokines only"]'::jsonb, 'T cell help via CD40L', 'CD40L from T cells binding CD40 on B cells is essential for class switching.'),
('42dd1dcd-8750-5a7f-962a-b0110d0fd8cd'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'Somatic hypermutation occurs in which site?', '["Spleen red pulp clinical presentation","Bone marrow clinical presentation","Peripheral blood clinical presentation","Thymus clinical presentation","Germinal centers of lymph nodes"]'::jsonb, 'Germinal centers of lymph nodes', 'Somatic hypermutation in germinal centers increases antibody affinity (affinity maturation).'),
('743575f0-be20-5cce-a358-db438c4b1654'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'VDJ recombination is the process of:', '["T cell receptor gene rearrangement","Phagocytosis","Cytokine gene activation","Apoptosis","Complement activation"]'::jsonb, 'T cell receptor gene rearrangement', 'VDJ recombination generates diverse T cell receptors (and immunoglobulins) by gene rearrangement.'),
('b398f654-c0ec-52f4-8e29-ba0858fcde39'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'RAG-1 and RAG-2 enzymes are essential for:', '["VDJ recombination","Class switching","Somatic hypermutation","Cytokine signaling","Complement activation"]'::jsonb, 'VDJ recombination', 'RAG1/2 initiate VDJ recombination by creating double-strand breaks at recombination signal sequences.'),
('94761657-4fab-5133-9ba4-43eb9d6d5c77'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'Which enzyme cuts DNA during class switch recombination?', '["Terminal deoxynucleotidyl transferase","DNA ligase","AID","RAG2","RAG1"]'::jsonb, 'AID', 'AID is essential for class switching and somatic hypermutation in B cells.'),
('c788d36c-be8e-55fe-8f34-d3da4590f2c7'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'What is the function of TdT (terminal deoxynucleotidyl transferase)?', '["Cutting DNA clinical presentation","Ligating DNA clinical presentation","Antibody secretion clinical presentation","Adding N-nucleotides during VDJ recombination to increase diversity","Cytokine signaling clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Adding N-nucleotides during VDJ recombination to increase diversity', 'TdT adds random nucleotides to VDJ junctions, increasing junctional diversity.'),
('da3169e2-e57d-5f65-b3b0-68691620d063'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'Which surface marker defines hematopoietic stem cells?', '["CD56","CD34","CD19","CD14","CD3"]'::jsonb, 'CD34', 'CD34 is a marker for hematopoietic stem cells used for bone marrow transplantation.'),
('33fb49b8-eb63-536f-a3ce-1243e096e153'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'Which of the following is an acute phase reactant?', '["Transferrin","Retinol-binding protein","Prealbumin","CRP","Albumin"]'::jsonb, 'CRP', 'CRP, SAA, and fibrinogen are positive acute phase reactants that increase during inflammation.'),
('2d655a31-5b53-5add-9678-08ddbd8db484'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'Humans have how many chromosomes?', '["23 pairs","22 pairs","21 pairs","24 pairs","46 pairs"]'::jsonb, '23 pairs', 'Humans have 23 pairs of chromosomes: 22 autosomes and 1 pair of sex chromosomes.'),
('b1fd8a25-fa79-5647-b859-a348bcd0d6b3'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'An autosomal dominant disorder requires:', '["X-linked inheritance","No genetic mutation","Two mutant alleles","One mutant allele","Mitochondrial inheritance"]'::jsonb, 'One mutant allele', 'Autosomal dominant disorders are expressed when only one copy of the mutant allele is present.'),
('87c00aee-fbca-5060-8aa7-d1a3c2d026d7'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'An autosomal recessive disorder requires:', '["Two mutant alleles","One mutant allele","One X-linked allele","Y-linked inheritance","No mutation"]'::jsonb, 'Two mutant alleles', 'Autosomal recessive disorders require two copies of the mutant gene for expression.'),
('263c7ebb-9faa-5338-9b3e-dc1997d3bf70'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'X-linked recessive disorders affect:', '["Females only","No one","Both males and females equally","Only homozygous females","Males predominantly"]'::jsonb, 'Males predominantly', 'Males are hemizygous for X chromosome and are affected by X-linked recessive disorders.'),
('ceaa3937-5644-53f3-b9d1-b257e8e35012'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'X-linked dominant disorders affect:', '["Only females clinical presentation","Both males and females; females often more mildly","Only males clinical presentation","Only adults clinical presentation","Only children clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Both males and females; females often more mildly', 'X-linked dominant disorders affect both sexes but females are usually more mildly affected due to lyonization.'),
('47066adf-1f87-5e0c-bb1c-5bf8311a22bd'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'Incomplete penetrance means:', '["Some individuals with the genotype do not express the phenotype","Variable severity of expression","All individuals with the genotype express the phenotype","Delayed onset","Sex-limited expression"]'::jsonb, 'Some individuals with the genotype do not express the phenotype', 'Penetrance = % of individuals with genotype who manifest the phenotype.'),
('c29cbf3d-e755-55a5-9daf-3e741604cd9e'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'Variable expressivity means:', '["Severity of phenotype varies among individuals with same genotype","All individuals show same severity clinical presentation","Sex-limited expression clinical presentation","Delayed onset clinical presentation","No expression clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Severity of phenotype varies among individuals with same genotype', 'Expressivity is the degree/severity of phenotypic expression (e.g., neurofibromatosis type 1).'),
('89495d50-3100-5af3-8616-3778bbd3abc1'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'Pleitropy refers to:', '["Epigenetic modification","Chromosomal abnormality","Multiple effects of a single gene","Gene interaction","Multiple genes causing same phenotype"]'::jsonb, 'Multiple effects of a single gene', 'Pleitropy = one gene causing multiple seemingly unrelated phenotypic effects.'),
('209f4ec3-c398-505c-b6af-5b4ef2b84ae8'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'Genetic heterogeneity means:', '["Variable expressivity","Incomplete penetrance","Locus heterogeneity","Same phenotype caused by different genes","Multiple effects of a single gene"]'::jsonb, 'Same phenotype caused by different genes', 'Genetic heterogeneity occurs when mutations in different genes cause the same phenotype.'),
('8b3ac0cb-b895-5370-9a0d-b411f081d0c9'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'Locus heterogeneity is a type of:', '["Variable expressivity","Phenotypic heterogeneity","Genetic heterogeneity","Allelic heterogeneity","Penetrance"]'::jsonb, 'Genetic heterogeneity', 'Locus heterogeneity = mutations at different chromosomal loci cause the same disease.'),
('d2011d63-d45a-544a-b8dc-8cc40b030b73'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'Which disease shows anticipation (increasing severity in generations)?', '["Cystic fibrosis","Neurofibromatosis type 1","Sickle cell disease","Huntington disease","Marfan syndrome"]'::jsonb, 'Huntington disease', 'Anticipation in Huntington (HD) is due to trinucleotide repeat expansion, worsening with each generation.'),
('2b661536-ca15-5e15-bba1-a8b710214ee8'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'Huntington disease is caused by:', '["GAA repeat clinical presentation","CGG repeat clinical presentation","CTG repeat clinical presentation","Deletion clinical presentation","CAG trinucleotide repeat expansion in HTT gene"]'::jsonb, 'CAG trinucleotide repeat expansion in HTT gene', 'HD has >40 CAG repeats in huntingtin gene, with anticipation.'),
('3754132a-c713-5214-b852-bf25396ad503'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'Fragile X syndrome is caused by:', '["CTG repeat clinical presentation","CGG repeat expansion in FMR1 gene","GAA repeat clinical presentation","CAG repeat clinical presentation","Deletion clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'CGG repeat expansion in FMR1 gene', 'Fragile X has >200 CGG repeats in FMR1 gene, with anticipation and methylated silencing.'),
('6dae7860-9ceb-5ae4-a16b-5dad2195f75e'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'Myotonic dystrophy is caused by:', '["Deletion clinical presentation","CTG repeat expansion in DMPK gene","GAA repeat clinical presentation","CAG repeat clinical presentation","CGG repeat clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'CTG repeat expansion in DMPK gene', 'Myotonic dystrophy type 1 has CTG expansion in DMPK gene, showing anticipation.'),
('8efeae30-a6e2-59c4-82e9-a8988604e1f1'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'Friedreich ataxia is caused by:', '["CAG repeat clinical presentation","GAA trinucleotide repeat expansion in frataxin gene","Deletion clinical presentation","CGG repeat clinical presentation","CTG repeat clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'GAA trinucleotide repeat expansion in frataxin gene', 'Friedreich ataxia has GAA repeat expansion in FXN gene, causing decreased frataxin.'),
('f8f81a4a-eff5-5437-a190-3202fa3b8183'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'Marfan syndrome is caused by mutations in:', '["COL3A1","FBN1","COL1A1","COL5A1","FBN2"]'::jsonb, 'FBN1', 'Marfan syndrome results from FBN1 mutations causing defective fibrillin and aortic root dilation.'),
('03c1bd27-be2b-55b7-a66b-8a24c5fe182d'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'Ehlers-Danlos syndrome vascular type is caused by:', '["ADAMTS2 mutation","COL3A1 mutation","COL5A1 mutation","COL1A1 mutation","FBN1 mutation"]'::jsonb, 'COL3A1 mutation', 'Vascular EDS is due to COL3A1 mutation, risk of arterial rupture and organ perforation.'),
('6f627eca-999a-5984-a3d4-9019ce0f92f8'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'Osteogenesis imperfecta is most commonly caused by:', '["FBN1","COL2A1","FGFR3","COL1A1/COL1A2 mutation","COL3A1"]'::jsonb, 'COL1A1/COL1A2 mutation', 'OI is usually due to COL1A1/COL1A2 mutations affecting type I collagen production or structure.'),
('59f0efe7-4e62-50f5-9671-6c00f040253d'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'On diagnostic evaluation, Achondroplasia is caused by mutation in:', '["FGFR2","SHOX","FGFR3","COMP","COL2A1"]'::jsonb, 'FGFR3', 'Achondroplasia results from activating FGFR3 mutation (Gly380Arg), inhibiting chondrocyte proliferation.'),
('86337993-6727-5570-9550-fa61bb2290b3'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'Cystic fibrosis is caused by mutation in:', '["CFTR gene on chromosome 7","CFTR on chromosome 12","CFTR on X chromosome","ENaC gene","SLC26A4"]'::jsonb, 'CFTR gene on chromosome 7', 'CFTR (cystic fibrosis transmembrane conductance regulator) on chromosome 7q.'),
('6d191008-cae9-5a69-b31b-0ac3c63c2248'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'The most common CFTR mutation in Caucasians is:', '["N1303K","R117H","W1282X","DeltaF508","G551D"]'::jsonb, 'DeltaF508', 'DeltaF508 (F508del) accounts for ~70% of CF mutations in Caucasians.'),
('b3972b4c-7f46-5ef3-869e-6f5cfdccb7b0'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'Sickle cell disease mutation is:', '["Nonsense","Glu6Val in beta-globin","Glu6Lys","Frame shift","Val6Glu"]'::jsonb, 'Glu6Val in beta-globin', 'Sickle cell mutation is a GAG to GTG change causing Glu to Val substitution at codon 6.'),
('9ce7d663-2e3f-5488-beca-87d7c61073f7'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'Which hemoglobin is elevated in beta-thalassemia major?', '["HbA","HbH","HbF","HbA2","HbS"]'::jsonb, 'HbF', 'Persistently elevated HbF is characteristic of beta-thalassemia major.'),
('042f1dc0-d27f-583c-80ac-1a4e60407384'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'Hemochromatosis is most commonly due to mutation in:', '["HFE gene","TFR2","HJV","HAMP","FPN1"]'::jsonb, 'HFE gene', 'Hereditary hemochromatosis type 1 is due to C282Y mutation in HFE gene.'),
('60c0db91-32b2-51a2-b266-3473ceee8856'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'Familial hypercholesterolemia results from:', '["PCSK9 gain of function","ApoB mutation","LDL receptor deficiency","ApoE2 homozygosity","All of the above"]'::jsonb, 'LDL receptor deficiency', 'Most cases are due to LDL receptor mutations. All options can cause FH.'),
('f8e1a6aa-0ed0-5ebf-b59f-c3f7ba6874ae'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'Neurofibromatosis type 1 (NF1) is caused by:', '["VHL mutation","NF2 gene mutation","NF1 gene mutation","TSC1 mutation","PTEN mutation"]'::jsonb, 'NF1 gene mutation', 'NF1 gene on 17q encodes neurofibromin, a RAS-GAP tumor suppressor.'),
('979e25e6-c192-5766-8f67-097c3ddef18f'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'Neurofibromatosis type 2 (NF2) is caused by:', '["VHL","PTEN","TSC2","NF1 gene","NF2 gene"]'::jsonb, 'NF2 gene', 'NF2 gene on 22q encodes merlin, causing bilateral acoustic neuromas.'),
('818ac8c7-d066-54da-877c-19e75aa00aba'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'Tuberous sclerosis is caused by mutations in:', '["NF1","TSC1 (hamartin) or TSC2","VHL","PTEN","NF2"]'::jsonb, 'TSC1 (hamartin) or TSC2', 'TSC1/TSC2 mutations cause hamartomas in brain, skin, heart, and kidneys.'),
('5a9ec0a7-f1cb-5ea5-8653-16540b40304d'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'Von Hippel-Lindau disease results from:', '["VHL tumor suppressor gene mutation","TSC2 mutation clinical presentation","RB1 mutation clinical presentation","NF1 mutation clinical presentation","PTEN mutation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'VHL tumor suppressor gene mutation', 'VHL mutation causes hemangioblastomas, renal cell carcinoma, and pheochromocytoma.'),
('2498d68d-e04f-5318-b45e-83b09cc981b4'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'Mutation in RB1 gene causes:', '["Colon cancer","Breast cancer","Retinoblastoma","Lung cancer","Osteosarcoma"]'::jsonb, 'Retinoblastoma', 'RB1 mutation causes familial retinoblastoma via ''two-hit'' hypothesis.'),
('24ce0a8e-ce6f-531b-94f4-2f4a7eabf8ed'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'Li-Fraumeni syndrome involves mutation in:', '["PTEN","RB1","APC","TP53","BRCA1"]'::jsonb, 'TP53', 'Li-Fraumeni is due to germline TP53 mutations, causing multiple early-onset cancers.'),
('568e7eb2-1a9b-5424-8307-3cb4a344c769'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'BRCA1 and BRCA2 mutations are associated with:', '["Prostate cancer","Colon cancer","Breast and ovarian cancer","Pancreatic cancer","Lung cancer"]'::jsonb, 'Breast and ovarian cancer', 'BRCA1/BRCA2 cause hereditary breast and ovarian cancer syndrome (autosomal dominant).'),
('809a3750-92d9-5087-890b-c32df35e2752'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'BRCA1 is located on which chromosome?', '["5q","13q","22q","2p","17q"]'::jsonb, '17q', 'BRCA1 is on 17q21. BRCA2 is on 13q12.3.'),
('6d1d5ddd-ce86-521e-b9ed-2cdea7bfcd24'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'APC gene mutation causes:', '["Familial adenomatous polyposis","Turcot syndrome","Hereditary non-polyposis colon cancer","All of the above","Gardner syndrome"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'APC mutations cause FAP and its variants Gardner and Turcot syndromes.'),
('519f2f8a-56cd-5fbe-951d-f7de7822aacc'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'Hereditary non-polyposis colorectal cancer (Lynch) is due to:', '["SMAD4 mutation","TP53 mutation","Mismatch repair gene mutations","APC mutation","KRAS mutation"]'::jsonb, 'Mismatch repair gene mutations', 'Lynch syndrome results from MLH1, MSH2, MSH6, PMS2 mutations causing microsatellite instability.'),
('16e8c0d2-fabd-5d48-856e-fa507de091c9'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'Down syndrome (Trisomy 21) is caused by:', '["Nondisjunction in meiosis","Translocation","Mosaicism","None","All of the above"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Most cases are due to meiotic nondisjunction, risk increases with maternal age.'),
('a333e583-1d08-513d-a53f-87951bf02786'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'The most common autosomal trisomy at birth is:', '["Trisomy 18","Trisomy 22","Trisomy 13","Trisomy 16","Trisomy 21"]'::jsonb, 'Trisomy 21', 'Trisomy 21 is the most common viable autosomal trisomy.'),
('f5f7cba8-5f12-508f-8438-26dcada358e3'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'Edwards syndrome is trisomy:', '["Trisomy 21","Trisomy 8","Trisomy 18","Trisomy 22","Trisomy 13"]'::jsonb, 'Trisomy 18', 'Trisomy 18 (Edwards) has rocker-bottom feet, overlapping fingers, and severe intellectual disability.'),
('b5a6da81-5bca-5259-bd6b-b69aeb5c6bd6'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'Patau syndrome is trisomy:', '["Trisomy 21","Trisomy 18","Trisomy 9","Trisomy 13","Trisomy 22"]'::jsonb, 'Trisomy 13', 'Trisomy 13 (Patau) has cleft lip/palate, polydactyly, and holoprosencephaly.'),
('01634cce-eced-592c-a5de-9ebc66d87f1c'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'Turner syndrome karyotype is:', '["45,X","47,XYY","46,XY","47,XXX","47,XXY"]'::jsonb, '45,X', 'Turner syndrome is 45,X (monosomy X), causing short stature, webbed neck, and ovarian dysgenesis.'),
('eeb75c75-6302-52c6-b35e-11635102bdbb'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'Klinefelter syndrome karyotype is:', '["46,XX","45,X","47,XXX","47,XXY","47,XYY"]'::jsonb, '47,XXY', 'Klinefelter (47,XXY) causes tall stature, gynecomastia, small testes, and infertility.'),
('515cb4cf-c3c7-51c1-b2a9-a02be1c2efe2'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'Triple X syndrome karyotype is:', '["47,XYY","47,XXY","45,X","46,XY","47,XXX"]'::jsonb, '47,XXX', '47,XXX females are usually phenotypically normal with tall stature.'),
('1e202e22-d896-58fa-a35a-575ba76077f3'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', '47,XYY syndrome males are:', '["Short stature","Tall with normal phenotype","Mentally impaired","Females","Infertile"]'::jsonb, 'Tall with normal phenotype', 'XYY males are tall with normal intelligence and fertility.'),
('836347f6-cdef-5333-8cfe-d4842fbadf50'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'Prader-Willi syndrome is caused by:', '["Loss of paternal 15q11-q13","Trisomy 15","Loss of maternal 15q11-q13","Uniparental disomy 14","Deletion of 7q11.23"]'::jsonb, 'Loss of paternal 15q11-q13', 'Prader-Willi (paternal deletion) features: obesity, hypotonia, small hands/feet.'),
('1258b3d6-50b9-5dfb-bc2d-c025278a9bc4'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'Angelman syndrome is caused by:', '["Loss of maternal 15q11-q13","Loss of paternal 15q11-q13","Trisomy 15","Deletion 7q11.23","Uniparental disomy 15"]'::jsonb, 'Loss of maternal 15q11-q13', 'Angelman syndrome (maternal deletion) features: happy demeanor, seizures, ataxia.'),
('7fbf804f-97bb-5027-9af0-e83f5a7e708d'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'Cri-du-chat syndrome is caused by:', '["Deletion of 22q11.2","Deletion of 15q","Deletion of 7q11.23","Deletion of 4p","Deletion of 5p"]'::jsonb, 'Deletion of 5p', 'Cri-du-chat (5p-) has high-pitched cat-like cry, microcephaly, and intellectual disability.'),
('f4e6ba28-00df-5d3c-b9ac-b571ec8dd2b0'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'Williams syndrome is caused by:', '["Deletion of 4p","Deletion of 5p","Deletion of 15q11","Deletion of 22q11.2","Microdeletion of 7q11.23"]'::jsonb, 'Microdeletion of 7q11.23', 'Williams syndrome features: elfin facies, supravalvular aortic stenosis, hypercalcemia, gregarious personality.'),
('46d87248-4636-5ab3-812d-d5aa3c4ad168'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'DiGeorge (22q11.2 deletion) syndrome features:', '["Short stature, webbed neck","Cat-like cry","Obesity, hypotonia","Elfin facies, SVAS","Thymic aplasia, cardiac defects, hypocalcemia"]'::jsonb, 'Thymic aplasia, cardiac defects, hypocalcemia', '22q11.2 deletion causes DiGeorge syndrome with CATCH-22 features.'),
('f5161221-d053-56a5-b2cd-09b7f4f50ae7'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'Mitochondrial inheritance is characterized by:', '["Transmission only via mothers","Autosomal dominant","X-linked pattern","Equal from both parents","Transmission only via fathers"]'::jsonb, 'Transmission only via mothers', 'Mitochondrial DNA is inherited exclusively from the mother (maternal inheritance).'),
('bbd09ff7-da4a-5067-9ced-f93ba1a0098f'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'Leber hereditary optic neuropathy is an example of:', '["X-linked recessive","Mitochondrial inheritance","Autosomal recessive","Autosomal dominant","Multifactorial"]'::jsonb, 'Mitochondrial inheritance', 'LHON is a mitochondrial disorder causing acute vision loss in young males.'),
('0d03a067-df78-53f5-8dad-d5b439a1649f'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'MELAS syndrome is a mitochondrial disorder characterized by:', '["Deafness, diabetes","Myopathy, encephalopathy, ragged red fibers","Mitochondrial encephalopathy, lactic acidosis, stroke-like episodes","Optic neuropathy","Neuropathy, ataxia, retinitis"]'::jsonb, 'Mitochondrial encephalopathy, lactic acidosis, stroke-like episodes', 'MELAS is a mitochondrial disorder with stroke-like episodes before age 40.'),
('2ec66e94-b362-5fc3-b69e-528e4f472fe6'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'Ragged red fibers on muscle biopsy indicate:', '["Polymyositis","Dermatomyositis","Duchenne muscular dystrophy","Mitochondrial myopathy","Glycogen storage disease"]'::jsonb, 'Mitochondrial myopathy', 'Ragged red fibers result from mitochondrial proliferation, characteristic of mitochondrial myopathies.'),
('7cdb5a10-545e-5103-8a64-078c6f16408e'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'Duchenne muscular dystrophy is caused by:', '["Collagen VI mutation","Laminin mutation","Dystrophin gene mutation","Myotonic dystrophy repeat","Dystrophin deficiency on autosome"]'::jsonb, 'Dystrophin gene mutation', 'Duchenne MD is X-linked recessive due to frameshift deletions in dystrophin gene.'),
('97e0a4a2-738c-5a60-b98e-fe7951216d59'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'Becker muscular dystrophy differs from Duchenne in that:', '["No cardiac involvement","More severe","Earlier onset","Autosomal inheritance","Later onset and milder course"]'::jsonb, 'Later onset and milder course', 'Becker MD has milder phenotype due to in-frame dystrophin mutations preserving some function.'),
('c33b6e8a-2b73-51b3-847a-2a97ecfdbdae'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'Hemophilia A is caused by deficiency of:', '["Factor VII","Factor IX","Factor VIII","von Willebrand factor","Factor XI"]'::jsonb, 'Factor VIII', 'Hemophilia A (factor VIII deficiency) is X-linked recessive, presents with hemarthrosis.'),
('feefefc7-17c2-5be4-8f49-1737d5c17a30'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'Hemophilia B (Christmas disease) is deficiency of:', '["Factor IX","Factor X","Factor XI","Factor VII","Factor VIII"]'::jsonb, 'Factor IX', 'Hemophilia B is factor IX deficiency, also X-linked recessive.'),
('26f0ea70-a28b-5a9b-8380-45ec549f7a5e'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'G6PD deficiency is inherited as:', '["X-linked dominant","Autosomal dominant","Autosomal recessive","Mitochondrial","X-linked recessive"]'::jsonb, 'X-linked recessive', 'G6PD deficiency is X-linked recessive, causing hemolytic anemia after oxidative stress.'),
('2eceadfb-5bea-5d00-a0e3-3c7cd33155bc'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'Which drug can trigger hemolysis in G6PD deficiency?', '["Warfarin","Penicillin","Acetaminophen","Primaquine","Aspirin"]'::jsonb, 'Primaquine', 'Primaquine and other oxidant drugs (sulfonamides, nitrofurantoin) trigger hemolysis in G6PD deficiency.'),
('9e1a7cff-8b32-5d44-b20c-1e8ab831a2ca'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'Spinal muscular atrophy (SMA) is caused by:', '["SMN1 gene deletion on chr 5","Dystrophin mutation","Androgen receptor CAG repeat","SMN2 deletion","CFTR mutation"]'::jsonb, 'SMN1 gene deletion on chr 5', 'SMA is due to SMN1 deletion, causing motor neuron degeneration and muscle weakness.'),
('26f8c09c-40e2-5c12-be1e-73df91a52ed8'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'Autosomal dominant polycystic kidney disease (ADPKD) is caused by:', '["PKHD1 mutation","NPHS2 mutation","WT1 mutation","PKD1 (85%) or PKD2 mutation","NPHS1 mutation"]'::jsonb, 'PKD1 (85%) or PKD2 mutation', 'ADPKD is most commonly due to PKD1 mutation (chromosome 16) encoding polycystin-1.'),
('7ec407b7-747b-573a-a955-ebb25a43b151'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'Alport syndrome is caused by:', '["FBN1","COL4A5 mutation","CFTR","COL1A1","COL4A3/A4"]'::jsonb, 'COL4A5 mutation', 'Alport syndrome (X-linked) is due to collagen type IV alpha-5 mutations, causing nephritis and deafness.'),
('5802c175-aa3e-54dd-a872-56dbcb0061f5'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'Which genetic disorder is associated with increased risk of malignant hyperthermia?', '["Duchenne MD","Central core disease","McArdle disease","Myotonic dystrophy","Malignant hyperthermia"]'::jsonb, 'Central core disease', 'RYR1 mutations cause central core disease and susceptibility to malignant hyperthermia.'),
('a5030a49-7121-5e70-a1d3-c71a2ab44a02'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'Mutation in androgen receptor (AR) gene causes:', '["Androgen insensitivity syndrome","Klinefelter syndrome","5-alpha reductase deficiency","Congenital adrenal hyperplasia","Turner syndrome"]'::jsonb, 'Androgen insensitivity syndrome', 'Complete AIS (46,XY) results from AR mutation, causing female phenotype with testes.'),
('d52176f6-923c-5ced-bd65-0e21f57bf3fb'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'Mutation in CYP21A2 causes:', '["Conn syndrome","Cushing syndrome","Adrenal insufficiency","Congenital adrenal hyperplasia","Androgen insensitivity"]'::jsonb, 'Congenital adrenal hyperplasia', '21-hydroxylase deficiency accounts for >90% of CAH cases, with virilization and salt-wasting.'),
('7838dbfc-8151-5a09-b338-3529d75917d3'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'Mutation in FMR1 gene causes:', '["Fragile X syndrome","Spinal muscular atrophy","Huntington disease","Friedreich ataxia","Myotonic dystrophy"]'::jsonb, 'Fragile X syndrome', 'FMR1 CGG expansion >200 causes Fragile X syndrome, the most common inherited intellectual disability.'),
('1bfb97ea-d166-585e-82c4-7c2b2aeb0100'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'The ''two-hit'' hypothesis in cancer genetics was proposed by:', '["Vogelstein","Watson and Crick","Knudson","Hanahan and Weinberg","Sanger"]'::jsonb, 'Knudson', 'Knudson''s two-hit hypothesis explains tumor suppressor gene inactivation requiring both allele mutations.'),
('e77a6628-662f-54bc-80bc-5382e6e70d6f'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'Loss of heterozygosity (LOH) refers to:', '["Overexpression clinical presentation","Translocation clinical presentation","Amplification clinical presentation","Loss of the normal allele in tumor cells","Gain of function clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Loss of the normal allele in tumor cells', 'LOH is the loss of the wild-type tumor suppressor allele, unmasking the mutant allele.'),
('ee2bc31c-0a15-525d-9f81-db86053107b3'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'Founder effect refers to:', '["Germline mosaicism clinical presentation clinical presentation","De novo mutation clinical presentation clinical presentation","Somatic mutation clinical presentation clinical presentation","High frequency of a mutation in a population descended from a small ancestral group","New mutation clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'High frequency of a mutation in a population descended from a small ancestral group', 'Founder effect explains high prevalence of certain mutations in genetically isolated populations.'),
('1e56772a-b9fb-5093-a4dd-f30e2fe499af'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'Which of the following is an example of uniparental disomy?', '["Translocation clinical presentation","Monosomy clinical presentation","Trisomy clinical presentation","Deletion clinical presentation","Both chromosomes from one parent"]'::jsonb, 'Both chromosomes from one parent', 'Uniparental disomy (UPD) - both homologs from same parent - can cause imprinting disorders.'),
('93ee1e50-940e-5139-9745-d0e0ccf55896'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'Epigenetics refers to:', '["Gene deletion clinical presentation","Gene duplication clinical presentation","Chromosomal abnormality clinical presentation","DNA sequence mutation clinical presentation","Changes in gene expression without DNA sequence change"]'::jsonb, 'Changes in gene expression without DNA sequence change', 'Epigenetic changes (DNA methylation, histone modification) alter gene expression without sequence change.'),
('1cc7f2e2-b4ed-5f4f-be8c-2aaf7ba186af'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'Imprinting means:', '["Parent-of-origin specific gene expression","Gene duplication","Gene deletion","Chromosomal translocation","New mutation"]'::jsonb, 'Parent-of-origin specific gene expression', 'Imprinted genes are expressed only from one parental allele due to epigenetic marking.'),
('be314e3c-d559-537a-a104-d9b26db8d515'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'Nondisjunction in meiosis I results in:', '["Homologous chromosomes fail to separate","Translocation","Chromosome breakage","Deletion","Sister chromatids fail to separate"]'::jsonb, 'Homologous chromosomes fail to separate', 'Meiosis I nondisjunction = homologous pairs fail to separate, causing aneuploidy.'),
('2702ab92-68a0-5fce-988d-2d14ab86e187'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'With respect to management, Robertsonian translocation involves:', '["Reciprocal exchange clinical presentation","Deletion clinical presentation","Fusion of two acrocentric chromosomes at centromeres","Inversion clinical presentation","Duplication clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Fusion of two acrocentric chromosomes at centromeres', 'Robertsonian translocation fuses acrocentric chromosomes (13,14,15,21,22) at the centromere.'),
('6d2a683a-f9ad-5df7-bc31-534708ba4bfd'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'Which population has highest prevalence of sickle cell trait?', '["African descent","Native American","Asian","Pacific Islander","Caucasian"]'::jsonb, 'African descent', 'Sickle cell trait is most prevalent in people of African descent (8-10% in African Americans).'),
('d78a265b-90fc-5d42-a80f-70a975591be5'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'Which population has highest prevalence of cystic fibrosis?', '["Middle Eastern","African descent","Caucasians","Hispanic","Asian"]'::jsonb, 'Caucasians', 'CF is most common in Caucasians of Northern European descent (1 in 25 carriers).'),
('0d3d5c0b-191f-571b-a715-e356863a146d'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'Which population has highest prevalence of Tay-Sachs disease?', '["Caucasian","Asian","African","Hispanic","Ashkenazi Jewish"]'::jsonb, 'Ashkenazi Jewish', 'Tay-Sachs disease carrier frequency is ~1 in 27 in Ashkenazi Jewish population.'),
('321ab5c0-bbce-5008-93e7-9cccc46e943d'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'Beta-thalassemia is most prevalent in:', '["Northern Europe clinical presentation","East Asia clinical presentation","Americas clinical presentation","Mediterranean, Middle East, South Asia","East Africa clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Mediterranean, Middle East, South Asia', 'Beta-thalassemia is prevalent in the Mediterranean basin, Middle East, and South Asia.'),
('50757109-3cd0-5eef-a946-99ccf2dd56f4'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'Alpha-thalassemia is most prevalent in:', '["Mediterranean","Africa","Northern Europe","Southeast Asia and China","South America"]'::jsonb, 'Southeast Asia and China', 'Alpha-thalassemia has high prevalence in Southeast Asia (deletion of alpha-globin genes).'),
('2a59deb0-1cfe-50fb-a3fc-4d8a3d920080'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'Which prenatal diagnostic test is performed at 10-12 weeks of gestation?', '["Ultrasound","Maternal serum screening","Amniocentesis","Fetal blood sampling","Chorionic villus sampling"]'::jsonb, 'Chorionic villus sampling', 'CVS is performed at 10-12 weeks for early diagnosis, while amniocentesis is at 16-18 weeks.'),
('b70f8667-2c27-5eb6-b607-7712fddb1bc2'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'The recurrence risk for autosomal dominant disorders with complete penetrance when one parent is affected is:', '["50%","75%","100%","25%","0%"]'::jsonb, '50%', 'Each child has a 50% chance of inheriting the mutant allele from the affected parent.'),
('44a7edd8-7eb5-51cb-9101-c6e4143dbd9c'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'The recurrence risk for autosomal recessive disorders when both parents are carriers is:', '["50%","25%","75%","100%","0%"]'::jsonb, '25%', 'Each child has 25% risk of being affected, 50% carrier, 25% unaffected non-carrier.'),
('0b437412-bad8-50d2-b129-612d64acf65e'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'Bayesian analysis in genetic counseling is used to:', '["Determine expressivity clinical presentation","Identify mutations clinical presentation","Assess penetrance clinical presentation","Calculate revised risk incorporating test results","Calculate Mendelian ratios clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Calculate revised risk incorporating test results', 'Bayesian analysis combines prior probability with conditional probability to refine risk estimates.'),
('35dd364f-c806-557f-b95a-1e9ad06b046b'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'Fluorescence in situ hybridization (FISH) detects:', '["Whole genome sequence clinical presentation","Protein expression clinical presentation","Histology clinical presentation","Specific DNA sequences/chromosome regions","RNA expression clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Specific DNA sequences/chromosome regions', 'FISH uses fluorescent probes to detect specific DNA sequences or chromosomal abnormalities.'),
('08ae4fce-9503-5249-8ab9-fd5a53f9b9ac'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'Comparative genomic hybridization (CGH) detects:', '["RNA expression","Trinucleotide repeats","Copy number variations","Point mutations","Methylation status"]'::jsonb, 'Copy number variations', 'CGH detects chromosomal copy number changes across the genome.'),
('e5c2ce8d-0a32-5065-9a98-0dd9acbe55fd'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'Karyotype analysis is used to detect:', '["Chromosomal number/structural abnormalities","Point mutations clinical presentation","Single gene mutations clinical presentation","Epigenetic changes clinical presentation","Protein defects clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Chromosomal number/structural abnormalities', 'Karyotyping visualizes chromosome number and structural abnormalities at metaphase.'),
('d3588c33-c1eb-537f-ac27-f4674199d528'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'Multifactorial inheritance involves:', '["Mitochondrial gene","Chromosomal abnormality","Epigenetic change","Multiple genes plus environmental factors","Single gene mutation"]'::jsonb, 'Multiple genes plus environmental factors', 'Multifactorial disorders (e.g., diabetes, hypertension) result from multiple genes and environment.'),
('6eb7cdf3-e762-55d6-b8ed-bcb9e2a12ef2'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'The risk for neural tube defects can be reduced by:', '["Vitamin D clinical presentation clinical presentation","Zinc clinical presentation clinical presentation","Calcium clinical presentation clinical presentation","Iron clinical presentation clinical presentation","Folic acid supplementation before and during early pregnancy"]'::jsonb, 'Folic acid supplementation before and during early pregnancy', 'Folic acid (400-1000 mcg/day) before and during early pregnancy reduces NTD risk by ~70%.'),
('6d40da94-a16a-5d60-ae60-4ea31a19bf63'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'Maternal serum alpha-fetoprotein (MSAFP) is elevated in:', '["Edward syndrome","Patau syndrome","Down syndrome","Neural tube defects","Turner syndrome"]'::jsonb, 'Neural tube defects', 'Elevated MSAFP indicates open neural tube defects. Low MSAFP associated with Down syndrome.'),
('35aff117-f08f-5b73-a9b4-a46a6fd9fcef'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'Second trimester quadruple screen includes:', '["Cell-free DNA","AFP, hCG, estriol, inhibin A","AFP and hCG only","Triple screen","AFP, hCG, estriol"]'::jsonb, 'AFP, hCG, estriol, inhibin A', 'Quad screen: AFP, hCG, unconjugated estriol, and inhibin A for Down/Edward syndrome and NTD.'),
('815732b1-fd6a-5db2-b155-91652736a99a'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'Non-invasive prenatal testing (NIPT) uses:', '["Cell-free fetal DNA from maternal blood","Ultrasound clinical presentation","CVS clinical presentation","Amniocentesis clinical presentation","Fetal blood sampling clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Cell-free fetal DNA from maternal blood', 'NIPT analyzes cell-free fetal DNA in maternal blood for trisomies 21, 18, 13.'),
('bc230d56-2722-55c6-83c4-09eb3edab9ad'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'Which test is diagnostic for cystic fibrosis?', '["Immunoreactive trypsinogen","Stool fat analysis","Sweat chloride test","Chest X-ray","Genetic testing"]'::jsonb, 'Sweat chloride test', 'Sweat chloride >60 mEq/L is diagnostic for CF. Genetic testing confirms mutation.'),
('14fd9a6f-121b-577e-810e-29f2d532b3ea'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'Newborn screening for PKU uses:', '["Sweat test","Guthrie test","Genetic testing","Urine ferric chloride","LFTs"]'::jsonb, 'Guthrie test', 'Guthrie test measures phenylalanine levels from heel prick blood spot for PKU screening.'),
('e867ea28-8b7a-5e02-8767-e86d1f056070'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'Which anti-cancer drug is used in CML and targets BCR-ABL?', '["Imatinib","Cetuximab","Rituximab","Trastuzumab","Bevacizumab"]'::jsonb, 'Imatinib', 'Imatinib inhibits BCR-ABL tyrosine kinase in CML with t(9;22) Philadelphia chromosome.'),
('f5a35265-722d-5d50-ad3a-a8204bd3b2f3'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'For a patient presenting with symptoms, Trastuzumab targets:', '["CD20","HER2/neu receptor","EGFR","BCR-ABL","VEGF"]'::jsonb, 'HER2/neu receptor', 'Trastuzumab (Herceptin) is used in HER2+ breast cancer.'),
('d82cc22c-2bfa-5c95-beec-6a7fdd075894'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'BCR-ABL fusion gene results from:', '["t clinical presentation","t clinical presentation","t clinical presentation","t clinical presentation","t(9;22) Philadelphia chromosome"]'::jsonb, 't(9;22) Philadelphia chromosome', 't(9;22) creates BCR-ABL fusion gene with constitutive tyrosine kinase activity causing CML.'),
('496d884d-4d3c-5a86-9df3-041c25e4492e'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'T(8;14) translocation is associated with:', '["Burkitt lymphoma","Mantle cell lymphoma","APML","Follicular lymphoma","CML"]'::jsonb, 'Burkitt lymphoma', 't(8;14) in Burkitt lymphoma places MYC oncogene next to immunoglobulin heavy chain enhancer.'),
('4f55eb4a-8926-5551-9030-39f3fd50584f'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 't(14;18) translocation is associated with:', '["Mantle cell lymphoma","Follicular lymphoma","CML","Burkitt lymphoma","APML"]'::jsonb, 'Follicular lymphoma', 't(14;18) places BCL-2 under immunoglobulin promoter, preventing apoptosis in follicular lymphoma.'),
('c05cbefd-aa3c-58a3-9743-0e070e490512'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'T(15;17) translocation is associated with:', '["CML","Acute promyelocytic leukemia","AML M2","Follicular lymphoma","Burkitt lymphoma"]'::jsonb, 'Acute promyelocytic leukemia', 't(15;17) creates PML-RARA fusion, causing APML with differentiation block at promyelocyte stage.'),
('67bb2150-733c-56eb-b998-d0cc2489940a'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'Which microorganism is associated with ''minocycline-induced skin pigmentation''?', '["Fungus","Virus","Bacteria","None, it is drug-related","Parasite"]'::jsonb, 'None, it is drug-related', 'Minocycline causes cutaneous pigmentation due to drug accumulation, not infection.'),
('7c7e497d-0305-5691-82e4-dc2d5e7d6f38'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'Which gene mutation is most common in ''familial Mediterranean fever''?', '["MVK gene","NLRP3 gene","TNFRSF1A gene","MEFV gene","CIAS1 gene"]'::jsonb, 'MEFV gene', 'FMF is due to MEFV mutation encoding pyrin, causing recurrent serositis and fever.'),
('2ac575a2-f617-5fd2-8c66-bd663af4d496'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'Which autoinflammatory disease is due to NLRP3 mutation?', '["TRAPS","Cryopyrin","FMF","HIDS","Blau syndrome"]'::jsonb, 'Cryopyrin', 'CAPS includes familial cold autoinflammatory syndrome, Muckle-Wells, and NOMID.'),
('af38cc07-8a81-5478-a83b-458c34d05f2f'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'What is the mechanism of action of intravenous immunoglobulin (IVIG)?', '["Only T cell activation clinical presentation","Only B cell depletion clinical presentation","Only complement inhibition clinical presentation","Only neutralization clinical presentation","Multiple: neutralization, Fc receptor blockade, immunomodulation"]'::jsonb, 'Multiple: neutralization, Fc receptor blockade, immunomodulation', 'IVIG has multiple immunomodulatory mechanisms including Fc blockade, anti-idiotypes, and cytokine modulation.'),
('778268a9-ac73-5c96-847f-bbd006f96885'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'Which complement inhibitor is used for paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria?', '["C1 inhibitor","Factor I","Factor H","C3 inhibitor","Eculizumab"]'::jsonb, 'Eculizumab', 'Eculizumab is a monoclonal antibody against C5, inhibiting MAC formation in PNH.'),
('23b8218b-1e1d-56f9-b7bb-47c437ea38f0'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'In which condition is C1 esterase inhibitor deficiency seen?', '["CGD","PNH","Hereditary angioedema","SLE","LAD"]'::jsonb, 'Hereditary angioedema', 'C1-INH deficiency causes hereditary angioedema with recurrent swelling episodes.'),
('ff753f91-adf2-56fe-b3db-eb346b94d5d3'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'Which drug is used for hereditary angioedema?', '["Antihistamines","Icatibant","IVIG","Corticosteroids","Epinephrine"]'::jsonb, 'Icatibant', 'Icatibant blocks bradykinin B2 receptors, treating acute attacks of hereditary angioedema.'),
('8743fd03-05e1-5b6f-884d-7064c712046b'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'A ''rheumatoid factor'' is an antibody against:', '["IgA","HLA molecules","Complement","IgM","Fc portion of IgG"]'::jsonb, 'Fc portion of IgG', 'Rheumatoid factor (usually IgM) targets the Fc portion of IgG.'),
('72e9d430-c913-5c26-8845-f891f34560c7'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'Anti-CCP antibody is more specific than RF for:', '["Systemic sclerosis","Rheumatoid arthritis","SLE","Vasculitis","Sjogren syndrome"]'::jsonb, 'Rheumatoid arthritis', 'Anti-cyclic citrullinated peptide (CCP) antibody is highly specific for RA (>95%).'),
('5c88e488-3aca-5f71-8fe5-1dedcbaa7dd4'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'HLA-B27 is associated with all EXCEPT:', '["Psoriatic arthritis","Rheumatoid arthritis","Reactive arthritis","Enteropathic arthritis","Ankylosing spondylitis"]'::jsonb, 'Rheumatoid arthritis', 'HLA-B27 is associated with spondyloarthropathies (AS, Reiter, psoriatic, enteropathic) but not RA.'),
('be2d32f3-91be-59fb-a1e0-f675271c1873'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'HLA-DQ2/DQ8 is associated with:', '["Celiac disease","MS","Type 1 diabetes","RA","SLE"]'::jsonb, 'Celiac disease', 'Most celiac patients carry HLA-DQ2 (90%) or DQ8 (5%).'),
('12760447-faa8-5181-8d8e-b0e551987b89'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'Which disease is associated with HLA-DR3?', '["SLE and Type 1 diabetes","Ankylosing spondylitis","RA","MS","Psoriasis"]'::jsonb, 'SLE and Type 1 diabetes', 'HLA-DR3 is associated with SLE, type 1 diabetes, and celiac disease.'),
('31f4fa70-838a-5e8a-b81c-4c44f1759b39'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'Which disease is associated with HLA-DR5?', '["Pernicious anemia","MS","RA","SLE","Hashimoto thyroiditis"]'::jsonb, 'Hashimoto thyroiditis', 'HLA-DR5 is associated with Hashimoto thyroiditis and pernicious anemia.'),
('a82ec816-2246-558e-93d3-6178e02b7210'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'The ''hygiene hypothesis'' suggests that reduced early infections increase risk of:', '["Allergic and autoimmune diseases","Diabetes only","Infections","Cancer","Cardiovascular disease"]'::jsonb, 'Allergic and autoimmune diseases', 'Reduced microbial exposure in early life may dysregulate immune tolerance, increasing allergy/autoimmunity.'),
('37ebcaee-cea7-569e-8af6-9582ec1861b4'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'Which cytokine is the target of the drug ''ustekinumab''?', '["IL-17","IL-1","TNF-alpha","IL-12 and IL-23","IL-6"]'::jsonb, 'IL-12 and IL-23', 'Ustekinumab (anti-IL-12/23 p40) is used for psoriasis and Crohn disease.'),
('96d4476a-6393-51eb-9c1e-ba7d347f24bd'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'Which cytokine is the target of ''secukinumab''?', '["TNF-alpha","IL-23","IL-12","IL-6","IL-17A"]'::jsonb, 'IL-17A', 'Secukinumab is an anti-IL-17A monoclonal antibody used in psoriasis and ankylosing spondylitis.'),
('b0c8dc38-4819-5d5a-b1ee-2f82f8d69234'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'Which drug is an anti-TNF-alpha fusion protein?', '["Infliximab","All are anti-TNF","Etanercept","Certolizumab","Adalimumab"]'::jsonb, 'All are anti-TNF', 'Etanercept is a TNFR2-Fc fusion protein; the others are monoclonal antibodies.'),
('b3f02c26-4ad0-5525-91bd-29517e31efbb'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'What is the target of ''belimumab'' used in SLE?', '["CD25","BAFF/BLyS","CD20","TNF-alpha","IL-6"]'::jsonb, 'BAFF/BLyS', 'Belimumab blocks BAFF/BLyS, reducing B cell survival in SLE.'),
('6b4f22c7-da3a-57c3-93fe-315190b718f6'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'Which vaccine is a live attenuated bacterial vaccine?', '["Influenza inactivated","BCG clinical marker","Typhoid Vi polysaccharide","Tetanus toxoid","Hepatitis B"]'::jsonb, 'BCG clinical marker', 'BCG is a live attenuated vaccine from Mycobacterium bovis.'),
('19c6d0ad-4915-5cad-9606-1b624c2a9d8a'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'Which vaccine is contraindicated in pregnancy?', '["All live attenuated vaccines","Hepatitis B","Inactivated polio","Tetanus toxoid","Rabies vaccine"]'::jsonb, 'All live attenuated vaccines', 'Live vaccines (MMR, varicella, BCG) are contraindicated in pregnancy due to risk to fetus.'),
('ac5a18a6-6d97-57a8-b6c5-ebe2548162ba'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'Which immunoglobulin is present on the surface of mature naive B cells?', '["IgG","IgM and IgD","IgG and IgA","IgE","IgA"]'::jsonb, 'IgM and IgD', 'Mature naive B cells express surface IgM and IgD as antigen receptors.'),
('50ba606c-6c44-5a4b-a277-0bcdab231845'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'Which enzyme is deficient in severe combined immunodeficiency (ADA-SCID)?', '["HGPRT","Adenosine deaminase","Purine nucleoside phosphorylase","XDH","OAT"]'::jsonb, 'Adenosine deaminase', 'ADA deficiency causes accumulation of deoxyadenosine and dATP, toxic to lymphocytes.'),
('72bf0978-4f21-593f-ab3f-8b0b4e448e66'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'Which enzyme deficiency causes ''Lesch-Nyhan syndrome''?', '["XDH","OAT","ADA","G6PD","HGPRT"]'::jsonb, 'HGPRT', 'HGPRT deficiency (X-linked) causes hyperuricemia, self-mutilation, and neurologic dysfunction.'),
('a54501cd-0d03-5943-ab33-233ccf47d51f'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'Which mutation causes ''MERRF'' syndrome (mitochondrial)?', '["MT-ATP6","MT-TK","POLG","MT-CO1","MT-ND1"]'::jsonb, 'MT-TK', 'MERRF (Myoclonus, Epilepsy, Ragged Red Fibers) is mainly due to A8344G mutation in MT-TK.'),
('f7235441-57fb-5571-aa3f-8f4f7530dd25'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'Which gene causes ''Leigh syndrome'' (most common)?', '["Most common: SURF1 mutation","All can cause Leigh","NDUFS","PDHA1","MT-ATP6"]'::jsonb, 'All can cause Leigh', 'Leigh syndrome is a mitochondrial disorder with bilateral basal ganglia lesions; SURF1 is most common nuclear cause.'),
('81a7ae3d-68df-5272-b964-e6d82cf4126a'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'Which genetic test method can detect single nucleotide variants?', '["Karyotype","FISH","Southern blot","Sanger sequencing","CGH array"]'::jsonb, 'Sanger sequencing', 'Sanger sequencing is the gold standard for detecting point mutations and small insertions/deletions.'),
('91c2b947-5afb-552d-8b30-07db88341311'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'Which test detects DNA methylation abnormalities?', '["Sanger sequencing","Karyotype","Southern blot","Methylation-specific PCR","FISH"]'::jsonb, 'Methylation-specific PCR', 'Methylation-specific PCR detects methylation patterns in CpG islands, e.g., in imprinting disorders.'),
('abecbece-e0a6-5b1b-a36b-b0756030c842'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'What is the recurrence risk for a multifactorial disorder like cleft lip?', '["50%","25%","4%","<1%","100%"]'::jsonb, '4%', 'For most multifactorial disorders, recurrence risk after one affected child is ~3-5%.'),
('1aa68010-bfda-55c6-a028-478a6135076f'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'Consanguinity increases the risk of:', '["Chromosomal disorders","Autosomal dominant disorders","Autosomal recessive disorders","Mitochondrial disorders","X-linked disorders"]'::jsonb, 'Autosomal recessive disorders', 'Consanguineous matings increase the risk of rare autosomal recessive disorders.'),
('14b5534e-7ef5-5390-a730-e8335adf59f2'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'What is the carrier frequency for cystic fibrosis in Caucasians?', '["1 in 10","1 in 50","1 in 25","1 in 200","1 in 100"]'::jsonb, '1 in 25', '~1 in 25 Caucasians are CF carriers (1 in 2500 affected).'),
('e245213f-68ee-5c7e-8d53-3fbdaeae306d'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'Germline mosaicism means:', '["Mutation present in some germ cells only","Mutation in somatic cells","Mutation in all cells","Deletion of chromosome","Epigenetic change"]'::jsonb, 'Mutation present in some germ cells only', 'Germline mosaicism can result in unaffected parents having >1 affected child with a dominant disorder.'),
('2b93ea59-a329-5714-b7d8-88cf83bfd595'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'The genetic basis of hereditary spherocytosis involves:', '["Band 3 mutation clinical presentation","Protein 4.2 mutation clinical presentation","Ankyrin (ANK1) or spectrin (SPTB/SPTA1) mutations","Glycophorin mutation clinical presentation","Rh deficiency clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Ankyrin (ANK1) or spectrin (SPTB/SPTA1) mutations', 'Most HS cases are due to ankyrin or spectrin deficiency.'),
('e0ae9551-0218-5db6-a779-e2c3cce5bd00'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'Familial hypercholesterolemia homozygotes have:', '["Severe CHD before age 20","Normal cholesterol","Decreased LDL","Mild elevation of LDL","HDL elevation"]'::jsonb, 'Severe CHD before age 20', 'HoFH patients have extremely high LDL (600-1200 mg/dL) and CHD in childhood.'),
('0b5b7f9b-0890-5ef8-b6c0-2f2eb94b1780'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'Which gene mutation is associated with hereditary pancreatitis?', '["CPA1","CTRC","SPINK1","PRSS1","CFTR"]'::jsonb, 'PRSS1', 'PRSS1 gain-of-function mutation causes hereditary pancreatitis with recurrent acute pancreatitis.'),
('605c976c-b8e1-53cb-b939-20d1d9736499'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'Which cancer syndrome is associated with PTEN mutation?', '["Hereditary breast and ovarian","Li-Fraumeni","Familial adenomatous polyposis","MEN1","Cowden syndrome"]'::jsonb, 'Cowden syndrome', 'Cowden syndrome (PTEN mutation) causes hamartomas and increased risk of breast, thyroid, and endometrial cancer.'),
('3481b50a-9d72-5bb7-a675-16ffd4fb8c64'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'Which syndrome is caused by STK11 mutation?', '["Peutz-Jeghers syndrome","Lynch syndrome","FAP","Juvenile polyposis","Cowden syndrome"]'::jsonb, 'Peutz-Jeghers syndrome', 'Peutz-Jeghers has mucocutaneous pigmentation and hamartomatous GI polyps.'),
('4d067c17-dba9-51a8-b0d2-e0bac7f5005f'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'Multiple endocrine neoplasia type 1 (MEN1) is caused by:', '["RET mutation","NF1 mutation","PTEN mutation","MEN1 gene","VHL mutation"]'::jsonb, 'MEN1 gene', 'MEN1 (Wermer syndrome) involves parathyroid, pituitary, and pancreatic neuroendocrine tumors.'),
('28117e73-8ba4-5e44-be8f-1cdfb28b45c0'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'MEN2A and MEN2B are caused by:', '["NF1","PTEN","RET proto-oncogene mutation","MEN1 mutation","VHL"]'::jsonb, 'RET proto-oncogene mutation', 'RET mutations cause MEN2 with medullary thyroid carcinoma, pheochromocytoma.'),
('0c983ed4-8ff3-511a-9081-ca4a30fa80e4'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'Which mutation causes ''Hirschsprung disease''?', '["GDNF","EDNRB","RET loss-of-function mutation","SOX10","RET gain-of-function"]'::jsonb, 'RET loss-of-function mutation', 'RET loss-of-function is the most common cause of familial Hirschsprung disease.'),
('325ac3f4-7057-566c-8931-40de4c7f45b9'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'Which disorder is associated with the ''ring chromosome''?', '["Ring chromosome 14 syndrome","Turner syndrome","Down syndrome","Klinefelter syndrome","Cri-du-chat"]'::jsonb, 'Ring chromosome 14 syndrome', 'Ring chromosomes (formed by breakage and fusion) cause various deletion syndromes.'),
('8846b3d1-d7b6-5e9e-a142-db8c4872bb27'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'Which disorder is caused by ''microdeletion of 17p11.2''?', '["DiGeorge syndrome","Smith-Magenis syndrome","Cri-du-chat","Prader-Willi","Williams syndrome"]'::jsonb, 'Smith-Magenis syndrome', 'Smith-Magenis (17p11.2 deletion) causes dysmorphic facies, self-hugging, and sleep disturbance.'),
('afa8b5cc-4815-5a03-b6fd-ae7161f69438'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'Which disorder is caused by ''microduplication 17p11.2''?', '["Potocki-Lupski syndrome","Williams","Smith-Magenis","DiGeorge","Angelman"]'::jsonb, 'Potocki-Lupski syndrome', 'Potocki-Lupski (dup 17p11.2) is a mirror of Smith-Magenis with different features.'),
('d8aa23b5-b73b-598d-b725-d1af4432d763'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'Jacobsen syndrome is caused by:', '["11q terminal deletion","5p deletion","15q11 deletion","7q11.23 deletion","22q11.2 deletion"]'::jsonb, '11q terminal deletion', 'Jacobsen syndrome (11q-) causes Paris-Trousseau syndrome with thrombocytopenia and dysmorphism.'),
('081f3f47-e32d-575f-9316-8692dff6711f'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'Miller-Dieker syndrome is caused by:', '["17p13.3 deletion","22q11.2 deletion","7q11.23 deletion","17p11.2 deletion","5p deletion"]'::jsonb, '17p13.3 deletion', 'Miller-Dieker causes lissencephaly (''smooth brain'') with severe intellectual disability.'),
('372cd0c3-c55c-5eac-9b5b-52a2cd554521'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'Hereditary leiomyomatosis and renal cell cancer (HLRCC) is caused by:', '["FH (fumarate hydratase) mutation","TSC1 mutation clinical presentation","VHL mutation clinical presentation","PTEN mutation clinical presentation","MET mutation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'FH (fumarate hydratase) mutation', 'HLRCC due to FH mutation causes cutaneous leiomyomas and aggressive renal cancer.'),
('fc702957-6b7b-5153-afca-bf4632b72c47'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'Birt-Hogg-Dube syndrome is caused by:', '["FH","VHL","TSC1/TSC2","PTEN","FLCN (folliculin) mutation"]'::jsonb, 'FLCN (folliculin) mutation', 'BHD causes fibrofolliculomas, lung cysts, pneumothorax, and renal tumors.'),
('029c1f4a-ee90-50b1-a90e-23882d7d8dc1'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'Which gene duplication causes Charcot-Marie-Tooth type 1A?', '["MPZ mutation","PMP22 duplication","GJB1 mutation","MFN2 mutation","EGR2 mutation"]'::jsonb, 'PMP22 duplication', 'CMT1A is most commonly due to PMP22 duplication, causing demyelinating peripheral neuropathy.'),
('232a2a92-a67b-5e11-91f4-f1b81d5bfa92'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'Hereditary neuropathy with liability to pressure palsies (HNPP) is caused by:', '["PMP22 deletion","PMP22 duplication","MFN2 mutation","MPZ mutation","GJB1 mutation"]'::jsonb, 'PMP22 deletion', 'HNPP is the reciprocal deletion of PMP22, causing episodic pressure-sensitive neuropathies.'),
('95ca0a79-fead-5386-9430-4a8b95c47114'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'Which gene mutation causes ''Rett syndrome''?', '["CDKL5","MECP2 duplication","FOXG1","FMR1","MECP2"]'::jsonb, 'MECP2', 'Rett syndrome (MECP2 mutation) causes regression, hand-wringing, and autism in girls.'),
('eb4148ad-27de-5b95-baf4-89d782110952'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'Angelman syndrome (maternal 15q deletion) is caused by loss of:', '["GABRB3 gene","NDN gene","MAGEL2 gene","SNRPN gene","UBE3A gene"]'::jsonb, 'UBE3A gene', 'UBE3A (ubiquitin protein ligase E3A) is maternally expressed in brain and lost in Angelman.'),
('be47aab9-0ddc-57f5-a7e7-1949b438c68f'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'Prader-Willi syndrome (paternal 15q deletion) involves loss of:', '["SNRPN and NDN genes","UBE3A","HTT","FMR1","MECP2"]'::jsonb, 'SNRPN and NDN genes', 'PWS involves loss of paternally expressed genes (SNRPN, NDN) on 15q11-q13.'),
('a86fecfe-2216-5dde-91bd-f88a2b13a6a6'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'Which gene is associated with familial Alzheimer disease (early onset)?', '["C9orf72 clinical presentation","GRN clinical presentation","APP (amyloid precursor protein), PSEN1, PSEN2","APOE epsilon4 clinical presentation","MAPT clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'APP (amyloid precursor protein), PSEN1, PSEN2', 'Early-onset familial AD is due to mutations in APP, PSEN1, or PSEN2.'),
('c5c97ef0-ccc2-5eb9-86d4-cd5dd09dba3e'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'APOE epsilon4 allele is a risk factor for:', '["ALS","Early-onset AD","Late-onset Alzheimer disease","Parkinson disease","Huntington disease"]'::jsonb, 'Late-onset Alzheimer disease', 'APOE epsilon4 increases risk and lowers age of onset for late-onset AD.'),
('65ae8798-4ad5-58aa-98ed-a3ee94949e0e'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'Which trinucleotide repeat disorder shows no anticipation?', '["Friedreich ataxia","Fragile X","Huntington","Myotonic dystrophy","SCA"]'::jsonb, 'Friedreich ataxia', 'Friedreich ataxia GAA repeat does not show significant anticipation.'),
('7438078e-2b1b-5f18-88bf-f807b59a5c08'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'Which is the most common ''autosomal recessive'' ataxia?', '["DRPLA","Ataxia-telangiectasia","Episodic ataxia","SCA","Friedreich ataxia"]'::jsonb, 'Friedreich ataxia', 'Friedreich ataxia is the most common recessive ataxia, with onset <25 years.'),
('ad3d1bf0-5cae-515f-961d-828b3a026216'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'Which is the most common ''autosomal dominant'' ataxia?', '["DRPLA","Episodic ataxia","Friedreich ataxia","Spinocerebellar ataxia type 3","Ataxia-telangiectasia"]'::jsonb, 'Spinocerebellar ataxia type 3', 'SCA3/MJD is the most common dominant ataxia worldwide.'),
('688cb233-3881-511f-b81c-c7345be54ba9'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Immunology and Genetics', 'Mutation in which gene causes ''familial amyloid polyneuropathy''?', '["GSN","TTR","LYZ","FGA","APOA1"]'::jsonb, 'TTR', 'ATTR amyloidosis is due to TTR mutations, causing cardiomyopathy and neuropathy.'),
('852380e3-0120-5382-85ba-c0987d149594'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Which enzyme is most specific for hepatocellular injury?', '["ALT","AST","LDH","ALP","GGT"]'::jsonb, 'ALT', 'ALT (alanine aminotransferase) is more specific for liver injury than AST, which is also found in heart and muscle.'),
('5b7648eb-0a17-57d5-bb98-716814e2d1bc'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Which enzyme is most sensitive for cholestatic liver disease?', '["LDH","ALT","ALP","CK","AST"]'::jsonb, 'ALP', 'ALP is elevated in cholestasis (biliary obstruction). GGT helps confirm hepatobiliary origin of elevated ALP.'),
('191d9c38-1529-5ebf-8894-f7a3f1c1a766'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'GGT (gamma-glutamyl transferase) is elevated in:', '["Pancreatitis clinical presentation","Muscle injury clinical presentation","Hemolysis clinical presentation","Cholestasis and alcohol-induced liver disease","Hepatocellular injury clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Cholestasis and alcohol-induced liver disease', 'GGT is sensitive for cholestasis and alcohol-related liver disease. It can be used to confirm hepatobiliary origin of elevated ALP.'),
('a1c6e1fe-b19b-547a-a707-f15d50aa7a72'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Bilirubin is derived from breakdown of:', '["Myoglobin","Ferritin","Hemoglobin","Cytochrome P450","Transferrin"]'::jsonb, 'Hemoglobin', 'Bilirubin is produced from heme breakdown by heme oxygenase, mainly from senescent RBCs.'),
('fa54d3e5-85c4-5174-9af2-42a54220c304'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Unconjugated (indirect) bilirubin is characterized by:', '["Conjugated with glucuronic acid","Water-soluble, elevated in obstructive jaundice","Water-soluble, excreted in urine","Direct-reacting","Fat-soluble, bound to albumin, elevated in hemolysis"]'::jsonb, 'Fat-soluble, bound to albumin, elevated in hemolysis', 'Unconjugated bilirubin is fat-soluble, transported bound to albumin, and elevated in hemolysis and Gilbert syndrome.'),
('aaa662ea-1815-5065-b736-4441b3ed9afb'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Conjugated (direct) bilirubin is characterized by:', '["Not excreted in urine","Fat-soluble, elevated in hemolysis","Bound to albumin","Indirect-reacting","Water-soluble, elevated in obstructive jaundice"]'::jsonb, 'Water-soluble, elevated in obstructive jaundice', 'Conjugated bilirubin is water-soluble (glucuronidated), can be excreted in urine, elevated in biliary obstruction.'),
('7e0465b5-25e6-5a69-9cb5-d33a3066aee9'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Which fraction of bilirubin is elevated in obstructive jaundice?', '["Delta bilirubin","Conjugated","None","Unconjugated","Both equally"]'::jsonb, 'Conjugated', 'Obstructive jaundice causes predominantly conjugated hyperbilirubinemia due to impaired excretion of bilirubin into bile.'),
('38b78c1b-2459-51c1-877a-a83f152371d4'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Which fraction of bilirubin is elevated in hemolytic jaundice?', '["Conjugated","Both equally","None","Unconjugated","Delta bilirubin"]'::jsonb, 'Unconjugated', 'Hemolysis increases bilirubin production exceeding hepatic conjugation capacity, causing unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia.'),
('4c8174ab-9cce-52a1-9489-104599333e91'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Which is the normal serum total bilirubin level?', '["<0.5 mg/dL","<3.0 mg/dL","<2.0 mg/dL","<5.0 mg/dL","<1.2 mg/dL"]'::jsonb, '<1.2 mg/dL', 'Normal total bilirubin is <1.2 mg/dL. Levels >2.5-3.0 mg/dL cause visible jaundice.'),
('75fe23cc-ff82-5d6c-9b8a-a36791b82acc'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Which liver enzyme is induced by alcohol?', '["GGT","ALT","AST","ALP","LDH"]'::jsonb, 'GGT', 'GGT is induced by chronic alcohol consumption and is a sensitive marker for alcohol abuse.'),
('f283d8d7-3f64-57b2-a110-c08189d26f67'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'AST:ALT ratio >2 is characteristic of:', '["Acute viral hepatitis","Alcoholic liver disease","Biliary obstruction","Hemochromatosis","Gilbert syndrome"]'::jsonb, 'Alcoholic liver disease', 'AST:ALT >2 (often >3) is characteristic of alcoholic hepatitis. In acute viral hepatitis, ALT > AST.'),
('03dddc93-afbe-5f35-b4cc-25f6c5b1b5dd'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Alkaline phosphatase is also elevated in:', '["Hemolysis","Pregnancy and bone diseases","Renal failure","Muscle injury","Heart attack"]'::jsonb, 'Pregnancy and bone diseases', 'ALP is elevated in cholestasis, bone diseases (Paget, rickets, osteomalacia), and pregnancy (placental ALP).'),
('1d9d30af-baa6-5f27-a8b2-cb6ec3c2af71'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Serum albumin is synthesized by:', '["Pancreas","Bone marrow","Spleen","Kidney","Liver"]'::jsonb, 'Liver', 'Albumin is exclusively synthesized by the liver. Low albumin indicates chronic liver disease or malnutrition.'),
('ab030e40-3418-59e1-8372-b61c1487f135'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Prothrombin time (PT) is prolonged in:', '["Hemophilia A","Platelet disorders","Liver disease and vitamin K deficiency","Hemophilia B","von Willebrand disease"]'::jsonb, 'Liver disease and vitamin K deficiency', 'PT is prolonged in liver disease (decreased factor synthesis) or vitamin K deficiency (factors II, VII, IX, X).'),
('bbaba7d8-d4f0-58ec-bae6-c5f5d8273f46'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Which clotting factor is most sensitive to vitamin K deficiency?', '["Factor VII","Factor X","Factor IX","Factor II","Protein C"]'::jsonb, 'Factor VII', 'Factor VII has the shortest half-life (~4-6 hours) and is the first to decrease in vitamin K deficiency.'),
('4d524ab6-f5fe-5837-a624-50b55f45c8c7'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Ammonia is detoxified in the liver via:', '["Krebs cycle","Urea cycle","Glycolysis","Gluconeogenesis","Beta-oxidation"]'::jsonb, 'Urea cycle', 'Ammonia is converted to urea via the urea cycle in hepatocytes. Elevated ammonia indicates liver failure.'),
('7ebb69e5-236f-52ad-afe8-9695564923bc'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Serum albumin half-life is approximately:', '["7 days","20 days","14 days","3 days","30 days"]'::jsonb, '20 days', 'Albumin has a half-life of ~20 days, so it reflects chronic rather than acute liver function.'),
('2c5737e8-1e2f-5b96-a826-77bb01577d1f'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Which bilirubin fraction is bound to albumin?', '["None","Both","Conjugated bilirubin","Delta bilirubin","Unconjugated bilirubin"]'::jsonb, 'Unconjugated bilirubin', 'Unconjugated bilirubin is water-insoluble and transported bound to albumin. Conjugated bilirubin is water-soluble.'),
('6f65cdc8-8a39-5e9b-ae9e-b958290a9ca7'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Delta bilirubin is:', '["Unconjugated bilirubin clinical presentation","Conjugated bilirubin covalently bound to albumin","Indirect bilirubin clinical presentation","Glucuronidated bilirubin clinical presentation","Direct bilirubin clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Conjugated bilirubin covalently bound to albumin', 'Delta bilirubin forms when conjugated bilirubin covalently binds to albumin, persisting after obstruction resolves.'),
('10f48a4c-afe2-5b67-8fc2-f1ad296ae4e1'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Gilbert syndrome is characterized by:', '["Complete absence of UGT","Biliary obstruction","Decreased UDP-glucuronosyltransferase activity","Conjugated hyperbilirubinemia","Hemolysis"]'::jsonb, 'Decreased UDP-glucuronosyltransferase activity', 'Gilbert syndrome causes mild unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia due to reduced UGT1A1 activity (~30% of normal).'),
('6843d19a-75fb-5c98-b6c4-4e3f0394b2c9'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Crigler-Najjar syndrome type I is:', '["Complete absence of UGT1A1","Partial deficiency","Conjugated bilirubin elevation","Biliary atresia","Neonatal hepatitis"]'::jsonb, 'Complete absence of UGT1A1', 'CN-I has absent UGT activity causing severe unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia (kernicterus risk).'),
('43dc5153-7263-5b4b-a471-85a584103e56'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Dubin-Johnson syndrome is characterized by:', '["Biliary obstruction clinical presentation","Hepatocellular injury clinical presentation","Hemolysis clinical presentation","Conjugated hyperbilirubinemia due to defective bilirubin excretion","Unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Conjugated hyperbilirubinemia due to defective bilirubin excretion', 'Dubin-Johnson is a benign condition with conjugated hyperbilirubinemia due to MRP2 deficiency.'),
('a49e3385-d79a-57bf-8321-8f185c500b63'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Rotor syndrome is similar to Dubin-Johnson but:', '["With hepatic pigmentation","More severe","Unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia","Without hepatic pigmentation","Fatal"]'::jsonb, 'Without hepatic pigmentation', 'Rotor syndrome also causes conjugated hyperbilirubinemia but without the characteristic pigmented liver of Dubin-Johnson.'),
('4ad6aa53-1c87-578d-bf35-84df7a747bfa'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Which test differentiates obstructive from hepatocellular jaundice?', '["Albumin clinical presentation","ALP and GGT (obstructive) vs ALT and AST","Total protein clinical presentation","Bilirubin fraction clinical presentation","PT clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'ALP and GGT (obstructive) vs ALT and AST', 'ALP/GGT are markedly elevated in obstruction; ALT/AST are more elevated in hepatocellular injury.'),
('48acb4c4-9097-5327-bdae-e9bdd856edff'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'In acute viral hepatitis, which enzyme is most elevated?', '["ALT","AST","ALP","GGT","LDH"]'::jsonb, 'ALT', 'ALT is most elevated in acute viral hepatitis (often >1000 U/L), reflecting hepatocellular injury.'),
('fcc686a6-7460-5d49-930c-6799f81ebf2a'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'In alcoholic hepatitis, the typical AST:ALT ratio is:', '["Normal",">2","<1","AST lower than ALT","1:1"]'::jsonb, '>2', 'Alcoholic hepatitis typically shows AST:ALT >2 due to mitochondrial AST release and folate deficiency.'),
('9b7868f9-8841-5c8d-af65-11842ea4ec73'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Which test is used to monitor hepatic synthetic function?', '["Albumin and PT/INR","Bilirubin only","Ammonia","ALP and GGT","ALT and AST"]'::jsonb, 'Albumin and PT/INR', 'Albumin and PT reflect hepatic synthetic function. Low albumin and prolonged PT indicate poor synthesis.'),
('01eb47ce-19ef-5d55-965e-6d4efd51bda5'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Serum albumin decreases in all EXCEPT:', '["Cirrhosis","Malnutrition","Nephrotic syndrome","Dehydration","Protein-losing enteropathy"]'::jsonb, 'Dehydration', 'Albumin is decreased in liver disease, nephrotic syndrome, and malnutrition. Dehydration causes hemoconcentration (elevated albumin).'),
('1f2c094b-ff67-5eee-b07c-f1fe0655beca'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Which of the following causes elevated ALP with normal GGT?', '["Cholestasis","Viral hepatitis","Biliary obstruction","Bone disease","Alcohol abuse"]'::jsonb, 'Bone disease', 'Paget disease and osteomalacia cause elevated ALP of bone origin with normal GGT.'),
('ed418f71-8bd9-5126-8bd6-bd06a6a39027'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Which test is used to assess liver fibrosis non-invasively?', '["ALT","ALP","Albumin","AST-to-platelet ratio index","Bilirubin"]'::jsonb, 'AST-to-platelet ratio index', 'APRI and FIB-4 scores use routine tests to estimate fibrosis without liver biopsy.'),
('82a499ce-ec6c-56f6-8b59-18e1ac6c559d'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Normal fasting blood glucose is:', '["50-70 mg/dL","70-100 mg/dL","200-300 mg/dL","100-126 mg/dL","126-200 mg/dL"]'::jsonb, '70-100 mg/dL', 'Normal fasting glucose is 70-100 mg/dL. 100-126 = impaired fasting glucose. >126 = diabetes.'),
('efb4e8f1-384c-51c9-b143-22b01bd8c51f'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Hypoglycemia is defined as blood glucose:', '["<40 mg/dL","<100 mg/dL","<126 mg/dL","<50 mg/dL","<70 mg/dL"]'::jsonb, '<70 mg/dL', 'Hypoglycemia is defined as blood glucose <70 mg/dL. Severe hypoglycemia is <54 mg/dL.'),
('c8f93e21-2cff-57f1-9ab4-7b3b3d0e50c3'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Which hormone is the main counter-regulatory hormone against insulin?', '["Glucagon","Cortisol","Growth hormone","Epinephrine","Thyroxine"]'::jsonb, 'Glucagon', 'Glucagon from pancreatic alpha cells is the primary counter-regulatory hormone, stimulating glycogenolysis and gluconeogenesis.'),
('87ace96e-932e-5a06-8c28-eb154607ed30'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'HbA1c reflects average blood glucose over:', '["4-6 weeks","1-2 weeks","6 months","1 year","2-3 months"]'::jsonb, '2-3 months', 'HbA1c reflects average glucose over the preceding 8-12 weeks (RBC lifespan ~120 days).'),
('329825d1-384e-5aff-9100-c370486a96c8'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Normal HbA1c level is:', '[">6.5%","<5.7%",">7.0%","<4.0%","5.7-6.4%"]'::jsonb, '<5.7%', 'Normal HbA1c <5.7%. Prediabetes 5.7-6.4%. Diabetes >6.5%.'),
('bccf1aa8-ee2d-5a40-8df5-c387f91ba899'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Serum urea is elevated in:', '["Dehydration and renal impairment","Pregnancy clinical presentation","SIADH clinical presentation","Overhydration clinical presentation","Liver disease clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Dehydration and renal impairment', 'BUN/urea rises in prerenal azotemia (dehydration, heart failure) and renal impairment. Decreased in liver disease.'),
('feac85a3-1190-523c-9762-b1a12a64b6e2'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Serum creatinine is more reliable than urea for renal function because:', '["It is cheaper clinical presentation","It is easier to measure clinical presentation","It is faster clinical presentation","It is not affected by protein intake or hydration","It is more sensitive clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'It is not affected by protein intake or hydration', 'Creatinine is less affected by diet and hydration than urea, making it a more reliable GFR marker.'),
('02f40121-8d55-5cf0-aa6d-b59e9e8dcbd8'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Blood urea nitrogen (BUN):creatinine ratio >20:1 suggests:', '["Postrenal obstruction","Prerenal azotemia","Starvation","Liver disease","Renal parenchymal disease"]'::jsonb, 'Prerenal azotemia', 'High BUN:Cr ratio (>20:1) indicates prerenal cause. Normal is ~10-15:1.'),
('4e874eeb-0cad-5609-a294-637683d76247'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'BUN:creatinine ratio <10:1 suggests:', '["GI bleeding","Acute tubular necrosis","Prerenal azotemia","Liver disease or low protein intake","Postrenal obstruction"]'::jsonb, 'Liver disease or low protein intake', 'Low BUN:Cr ratio occurs in liver disease (decreased urea synthesis) or low protein intake.'),
('ebdcdfe1-5646-5b19-858e-7b36a9da5a46'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Estimated glomerular filtration rate (eGFR) is calculated using:', '["Serum urea only","Creatinine clearance alone","Serum creatinine, age, sex, race","Cystatin C only","BUN only"]'::jsonb, 'Serum creatinine, age, sex, race', 'eGFR uses the CKD-EPI or MDRD equation incorporating creatinine, age, sex, and race.'),
('590c55cb-4a09-5e07-b8c9-272d2fde0665'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Normal eGFR is:', '[">120 mL/min",">60 mL/min",">15 mL/min",">90 mL/min/1.73m2",">30 mL/min"]'::jsonb, '>90 mL/min/1.73m2', 'Normal GFR >90 mL/min/1.73m2. CKD stages based on GFR: 60-89 (G2), 30-59 (G3), 15-29 (G4), <15 (G5).'),
('ae7d7d42-7703-5f54-9cb3-f379a3181bc6'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Which of the following best reflects glomerular function?', '["Urine specific gravity","Serum potassium","Serum albumin","Serum sodium","Creatinine clearance"]'::jsonb, 'Creatinine clearance', 'Creatinine clearance (measured or estimated) reflects GFR, the best indicator of glomerular function.'),
('49b7e243-568a-55aa-9e75-0ace7287a584'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Proteinuria >3.5 g/day indicates:', '["UTI","Normal finding","Dehydration","Nephrotic syndrome","Nephritic syndrome"]'::jsonb, 'Nephrotic syndrome', 'Nephrotic-range proteinuria is defined as >3.5 g/24h (or >3.5 g/g creatinine).'),
('f6749f81-bbe0-5410-a4c4-c93e9d513bbb'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Microalbuminuria is defined as:', '["<30 mg/24h","100-500 mg/24h",">300 mg/24h",">500 mg/24h","30-300 mg albumin/24h"]'::jsonb, '30-300 mg albumin/24h', 'Microalbuminuria (30-300 mg/24h) is an early marker of diabetic nephropathy.'),
('ef5c72b9-b7c2-56ac-9e36-79d24e9bc134'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Which dipstick finding suggests urinary tract infection?', '["Positive nitrite and leukocyte esterase","Positive glucose clinical presentation","Positive ketones clinical presentation","Positive bilirubin clinical presentation","Positive urobilinogen clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Positive nitrite and leukocyte esterase', 'Nitrite (bacterial conversion) and leukocyte esterase (WBCs) suggest UTI on dipstick.'),
('552f8a23-9c2c-515a-8f0d-f94e9193c3c2'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Glycosuria with normal blood glucose suggests:', '["Diabetes mellitus","Hyperthyroidism","Renal tubular defect","Pregnancy","Stress hyperglycemia"]'::jsonb, 'Renal tubular defect', 'Renal glycosuria occurs when proximal tubular glucose reabsorption (SGLT2) is defective.'),
('be6ba037-068e-5163-800e-ce4c857c33a2'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Ketones in urine indicate:', '["Carbohydrate excess","Liver disease","Fat metabolism","Protein intake","Renal failure"]'::jsonb, 'Fat metabolism', 'Ketones (acetoacetate, beta-hydroxybutyrate) appear in starvation, DKA, and alcoholic ketoacidosis.'),
('c42eb7e5-52f0-5cd6-8bc5-5cd643c26cfc'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Which urine finding is characteristic of acute tubular necrosis?', '["Broad casts","RBC casts","Muddy brown granular casts","WBC casts","Hyaline casts"]'::jsonb, 'Muddy brown granular casts', 'Muddy brown granular casts are characteristic of ATN. RBC casts indicate glomerulonephritis.'),
('7e0611ae-89f1-5e12-8839-50b3dea67b00'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'RBC casts in urine indicate:', '["Prostatitis","Renal stones","Glomerular pathology","Lower tract bleeding","UTI"]'::jsonb, 'Glomerular pathology', 'RBC casts form in renal tubules and indicate glomerular origin of hematuria.'),
('80e24d76-fced-5b9c-8e4d-6b63c55a1420'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'WBC casts in urine indicate:', '["Prostatitis clinical presentation","Urethritis clinical presentation","Pyelonephritis or tubulointerstitial nephritis","Glomerulonephritis clinical presentation","Cystitis clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Pyelonephritis or tubulointerstitial nephritis', 'WBC casts indicate inflammation of the renal parenchyma (pyelonephritis, interstitial nephritis).'),
('e410b61e-3493-53fa-b500-ba9f7a01b918'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Hyaline casts in urine are:', '["Always pathological","Indicative of UTI","Indicative of ATN","Indicative of glomerulonephritis","Non-specific, can be normal"]'::jsonb, 'Non-specific, can be normal', 'Hyaline casts can be seen in normal urine (dehydration, exercise, diuretics).'),
('8c6dc284-244c-5412-a888-a853af45e2fd'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Normal urine output per day is:', '["800-2000 mL","200-400 mL","4000-5000 mL","100-200 mL","3000-4000 mL"]'::jsonb, '800-2000 mL', 'Normal urine output is ~800-2000 mL/day. Oliguria <400 mL/day. Anuria <100 mL/day.'),
('67f52ab9-804d-5a56-b9b9-395e9e3891c4'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Specific gravity of urine normally ranges:', '["1.010-1.025","<1.000","1.025-1.050","1.050-1.080","1.000-1.010"]'::jsonb, '1.010-1.025', 'Normal urine specific gravity is 1.010-1.025. Fixed at 1.010 indicates renal concentrating defect.'),
('d7945f48-846b-5f0b-92e8-e844c5a28805'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Urine osmolality in dehydration should be:', '["<200 mOsm/kg","<300 mOsm/kg",">1000 mOsm/kg","300-600 mOsm/kg",">600 mOsm/kg"]'::jsonb, '>600 mOsm/kg', 'In dehydration, urine osmolality should be >600 (concentrated). Fixed low suggests concentrating defect.'),
('1793bab4-cea2-5890-ab70-638ccd34a397'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Serum calcium normal range is:', '["5.0-7.0 mg/dL","6.0-8.0 mg/dL","8.5-10.5 mg/dL","10.5-12.0 mg/dL","12.0-14.0 mg/dL"]'::jsonb, '8.5-10.5 mg/dL', 'Normal serum calcium is 8.5-10.5 mg/dL. Correct for albumin: add 0.8 mg/dL per 1 g/dL albumin below 4.'),
('4ae6f113-9d9e-5afc-800b-8d1dbeb0ab50'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Hypercalcemia is most commonly caused by:', '["Hypoparathyroidism clinical presentation","Osteomalacia clinical presentation","Renal failure clinical presentation","Vitamin D deficiency clinical presentation","Primary hyperparathyroidism or malignancy"]'::jsonb, 'Primary hyperparathyroidism or malignancy', 'Hypercalcemia is most commonly due to hyperparathyroidism or malignancy (bone metastases, PTHrP).'),
('3bce3836-317d-5caf-8e50-221cce9e546f'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Which malignancy most commonly causes hypercalcemia?', '["Lung cancer (squamous), breast, multiple myeloma","Colon cancer clinical presentation","Leukemia clinical presentation","Lymphoma clinical presentation","Prostate cancer clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Lung cancer (squamous), breast, multiple myeloma', 'Squamous cell lung CA (PTHrP), breast CA (bone mets), and multiple myeloma (osteolytic) commonly cause hypercalcemia.'),
('bff145b8-1582-5df2-aaf8-14ae339a22d2'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Serum phosphate normal range is:', '["4.5-6.5 mg/dL","2.5-4.5 mg/dL","6.5-8.5 mg/dL","<1.0 mg/dL","1.0-2.0 mg/dL"]'::jsonb, '2.5-4.5 mg/dL', 'Normal phosphate is 2.5-4.5 mg/dL. Low in hyperparathyroidism, high in renal failure.'),
('5ca92f76-5586-52f3-b973-22383d065f82'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'In primary hyperparathyroidism, calcium is ____ and phosphate is ____:', '["High, Low","High, High","Low, High","Low, Low","Normal, Normal"]'::jsonb, 'High, Low', 'Primary hyperparathyroidism causes hypercalcemia (increased bone resorption) and hypophosphatemia (decreased renal reabsorption).'),
('ebb49c4f-e1ca-5dd1-b531-d22d5734adad'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'In hypoparathyroidism, calcium is ____ and phosphate is ____:', '["Low, High","High, Low","Low, Low","High, High","Normal, Normal"]'::jsonb, 'Low, High', 'Hypoparathyroidism causes hypocalcemia and hyperphosphatemia due to decreased PTH.'),
('e2d0581c-611a-5675-9b43-6a9bc0e755cb'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'PTHrP (parathyroid hormone-related protein) is secreted by:', '["Thyroid C cells","Parathyroid adenoma","Normal parathyroid","Malignant tumors","Kidney"]'::jsonb, 'Malignant tumors', 'PTHrP causes humoral hypercalcemia of malignancy, mimicking PTH but with low 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D.'),
('4c28046f-9138-5a9e-9073-097505cb19cc'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Serum potassium normal range is:', '["3.5-5.0 mEq/L","2.5-3.5 mEq/L","5.0-6.5 mEq/L","6.5-8.0 mEq/L","<2.5 mEq/L"]'::jsonb, '3.5-5.0 mEq/L', 'Normal K+ is 3.5-5.0 mEq/L. Hyperkalemia >5.5 (risk of arrhythmias). Hypokalemia <3.5.'),
('bf6a1dca-eecf-5cc9-99e1-7c16037d753e'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Hyperkalemia causes ECG changes including:', '["Flat T waves","Peaked T waves, widened QRS","ST depression","Prolonged QT","U waves"]'::jsonb, 'Peaked T waves, widened QRS', 'Hyperkalemia: peaked T waves, loss of P wave, widened QRS, sine wave at severe levels.'),
('e64546d8-bdd3-5092-ba5c-93d6435acf1d'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Hypokalemia causes ECG changes including:', '["Widened QRS clinical presentation","Peaked T waves clinical presentation","U waves, ST depression, flat T waves","Sine wave clinical presentation","Prolonged PR clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'U waves, ST depression, flat T waves', 'Hypokalemia shows U waves, ST depression, and flattened T waves on ECG.'),
('6ed38601-ca80-511a-8c99-07aa37f68182'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Which drug is most commonly associated with hyperkalemia?', '["Hydrochlorothiazide","Mannitol","Spironolactone","Furosemide","Acetazolamide"]'::jsonb, 'Spironolactone', 'Spironolactone is a K-sparing diuretic that can cause hyperkalemia, especially in renal impairment.'),
('022a0acc-46c1-57c5-8d18-dfd1dd0e88ca'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Which is the most common cause of hypokalemia?', '["K-sparing diuretics","Diuretic use","Renal failure","Metabolic acidosis","ACE inhibitors"]'::jsonb, 'Diuretic use', 'Loop and thiazide diuretics increase K+ excretion. Hypokalemia also occurs in diarrhea and hyperaldosteronism.'),
('04454bf5-0022-57db-b255-8b17c80a93dd'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Serum sodium normal range is:', '["135-145 mEq/L","120-130 mEq/L","145-155 mEq/L","155-165 mEq/L","<120 mEq/L"]'::jsonb, '135-145 mEq/L', 'Normal Na+ is 135-145 mEq/L. Hyponatremia <135, hypernatremia >145.'),
('06fad770-3c62-5da9-8982-60833979dd0e'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Hyponatremia is most commonly caused by:', '["SIADH","Primary polydipsia","Diabetes insipidus","Excess salt intake","Dehydration"]'::jsonb, 'SIADH', 'SIADH is the most common cause of euvolemic hyponatremia. Other causes: heart failure, cirrhosis, diuretics.'),
('982965af-251d-57b2-89bf-4a90ced8b0d3'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Hypernatremia is most commonly caused by:', '["Free water loss","Primary polydipsia","Adrenal insufficiency","Beer potomania","SIADH"]'::jsonb, 'Free water loss', 'Hypernatremia reflects free water deficit, most often from insensible losses, diarrhea, or diabetes insipidus.'),
('f49bbd7a-6851-5892-a27d-0c1a79405d28'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Serum magnesium normal range is:', '["2.5-3.5 mg/dL","0.5-1.0 mg/dL","3.5-5.0 mg/dL","1.7-2.2 mg/dL","<0.5 mg/dL"]'::jsonb, '1.7-2.2 mg/dL', 'Normal Mg is 1.7-2.2 mg/dL. Hypomagnesemia causes tetany and arrhythmias; hypermagnesemia causes areflexia and hypotension.'),
('069aba7c-a284-5766-8b0d-d3afd0bfa98d'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Which test is used to monitor warfarin therapy?', '["aPTT","PT/INR","Platelet count","BT","TT"]'::jsonb, 'PT/INR', 'Warfarin (vitamin K antagonist) is monitored by PT/INR. Heparin is monitored by aPTT.'),
('f6c3d147-45ea-5436-b883-ee4de87b0fbc'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Which test is used to monitor unfractionated heparin therapy?', '["TT","BT","Platelet count","aPTT","PT/INR"]'::jsonb, 'aPTT', 'aPTT reflects intrinsic pathway and is used to monitor unfractionated heparin.'),
('b8f2f31d-00c9-568d-9bca-82c4c047e03a'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Normal INR (International Normalized Ratio) is:', '["3.0-4.0","2.0-3.0","0.8-1.2",">4.0","1.5-2.0"]'::jsonb, '0.8-1.2', 'Normal INR is 0.8-1.2. Therapeutic warfarin INR is 2.0-3.0 (or 2.5-3.5 for mechanical valves).'),
('f4fd65f3-637c-52ed-86f0-8d628fcb6eb1'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'CRP (C-reactive protein) is a marker of:', '["Autoimmunity","Renal function","Cardiac injury only","Liver function","Inflammation"]'::jsonb, 'Inflammation', 'CRP is an acute phase protein that rises in inflammation, infection, and tissue damage.'),
('d5007b96-2b32-5975-896e-cef2dcd9efaa'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'High-sensitivity CRP (hs-CRP) is used to assess risk of:', '["Cancer","Liver disease","Stroke","Cardiovascular disease","Renal disease"]'::jsonb, 'Cardiovascular disease', 'hs-CRP >2 mg/L indicates increased cardiovascular risk in primary prevention.'),
('717fc165-9958-538a-b564-9a259fedb474'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Troponin I and T are markers of:', '["Pancreatic injury","Renal injury","Skeletal muscle injury","Myocardial injury","Liver injury"]'::jsonb, 'Myocardial injury', 'Troponins are regulatory proteins specific to cardiac muscle, elevated in MI and myocardial injury.'),
('d56a6ed9-75cf-5669-b742-3d23a9020fa8'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Which troponin has the highest cardiac specificity?', '["Myoglobin","Troponin I","Troponin T","Troponin C","CK-MB"]'::jsonb, 'Troponin I', 'Troponin I is most specific; troponin T is also specific but rises earlier in renal failure.'),
('54c796c8-8140-50d6-b210-e2c50e4041f1'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'CK-MB is a marker for:', '["Myocardial infarction","Skeletal muscle injury","Pancreatic injury","Brain injury","Liver injury"]'::jsonb, 'Myocardial infarction', 'CK-MB (creatine kinase MB isoenzyme) is relatively specific for myocardial injury; it rises 4-6 hours after MI.'),
('f55d9986-229c-5afd-a4f3-19aa92cf8992'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Lactate dehydrogenase (LDH) is elevated in:', '["Only myocardial infarction","Many conditions","Only liver disease","Only hemolysis","Only malignancy"]'::jsonb, 'Many conditions', 'LDH is a non-specific enzyme elevated in tissue damage. LDH1 > LDH2 in MI and hemolysis.'),
('271f9c10-8888-5086-b837-adbb2b222d0c'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Serum amylase is elevated in:', '["Perforated ulcer","All of the above","Acute pancreatitis","None","Parotitis"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Amylase is elevated in pancreatitis and also in parotitis, perforated ulcer, and renal failure.'),
('de74e061-ed9e-5795-9645-773214ea37b0'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Serum lipase is more specific than amylase for:', '["Acute pancreatitis","Parotitis","Mumps","Renal failure","Bowel obstruction"]'::jsonb, 'Acute pancreatitis', 'Lipase is more specific for pancreatitis than amylase and remains elevated longer.'),
('c2e68de8-a2a9-550f-bd3b-27192a4f00e3'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Which test is diagnostic for acute pancreatitis?', '["Lipase >3x upper normal","Amylase >3x","CT abdomen","Both lipase and amylase","MRI"]'::jsonb, 'Lipase >3x upper normal', 'Lipase is the preferred diagnostic test for acute pancreatitis (>3x upper limit of normal).'),
('11889196-23b9-525c-99af-93c74e8e44d1'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'CA 19-9 is a tumor marker for:', '["Pancreatic cancer","Colon cancer","Breast cancer","Prostate cancer","Ovarian cancer"]'::jsonb, 'Pancreatic cancer', 'CA 19-9 is primarily used for pancreatic cancer monitoring, though it is non-specific.'),
('8e41cb3a-6f16-50a4-b663-9d0a1b35cfba'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'CEA (carcinoembryonic antigen) is a tumor marker for:', '["Breast cancer","Prostate cancer","Colorectal cancer","Lung cancer","Ovarian cancer"]'::jsonb, 'Colorectal cancer', 'CEA is used for monitoring colorectal cancer recurrence. Also elevated in smokers and other cancers.'),
('9c055c4f-0a6b-57b3-a710-d4749b295764'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'AFP (alpha-fetoprotein) is elevated in:', '["Lung cancer clinical presentation","Hepatocellular carcinoma and yolk sac tumors","Breast cancer clinical presentation","Colon cancer clinical presentation","Prostate cancer clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Hepatocellular carcinoma and yolk sac tumors', 'AFP is elevated in HCC, germ cell tumors (yolk sac), and neural tube defects (prenatal).'),
('8beac753-724b-51a0-b7ec-10fb3fc570ec'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'PSA (prostate-specific antigen) is used for:', '["Breast cancer screening","Lung cancer","Ovarian cancer","Colon cancer screening","Prostate cancer screening and monitoring"]'::jsonb, 'Prostate cancer screening and monitoring', 'PSA >4 ng/mL suggests prostate cancer risk. Also elevated in BPH and prostatitis.'),
('ce76e2c4-a99f-5312-9b3a-299680e54eeb'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Regarding clinical scenarios, CA-125 is a tumor marker for:', '["Pancreatic cancer","Breast cancer","Endometrial cancer","Cervical cancer","Ovarian cancer"]'::jsonb, 'Ovarian cancer', 'CA-125 is used for monitoring epithelial ovarian cancer, but is non-specific (also elevated in endometriosis).'),
('54268d58-e930-53dd-a8b0-0e328f3a648e'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Beta-hCG is a marker for:', '["Prostate cancer","Ovarian cancer","Colon cancer","Breast cancer","Choriocarcinoma and pregnancy"]'::jsonb, 'Choriocarcinoma and pregnancy', 'Beta-hCG is elevated in pregnancy, choriocarcinoma, and germ cell tumors.'),
('00f88971-ccad-5a7e-a3fc-8964bab53e2b'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Which analyte is increased in metabolic acidosis with anion gap?', '["Lactate, ketones, uremic toxins","Chloride only clinical presentation","Bicarbonate only clinical presentation","Sodium only clinical presentation","Calcium clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Lactate, ketones, uremic toxins', 'High anion gap metabolic acidosis is due to unmeasured anions: lactate (shock), ketones (DKA), uremic (renal failure).'),
('12b6b372-728d-502d-8347-00a0f36a4de1'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Anion gap formula is:', '["Na -","Na + K - Cl","Cl + HCO3 - Na","Na - Cl - K","HCO3 - Cl"]'::jsonb, 'Na -', 'AG = Na - (Cl + HCO3). Normal is 8-12 mEq/L. Elevated in metabolic acidosis from unmeasured anions.'),
('d33b9c5c-0663-59d4-bb25-22f3bd1f0467'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Normal serum bicarbonate (HCO3) level is:', '["35-45 mEq/L","22-28 mEq/L","10-18 mEq/L","<10 mEq/L","28-35 mEq/L"]'::jsonb, '22-28 mEq/L', 'Normal HCO3 is 22-28 mEq/L. Decreased in metabolic acidosis, increased in metabolic alkalosis.'),
('5bff5cbb-6827-52c7-9fbc-4ef8bb6e33d0'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'In a surgical context, Normal arterial blood pH is:', '["7.50-7.60","7.35-7.45","7.00-7.20","7.45-7.55","7.20-7.30"]'::jsonb, '7.35-7.45', 'Normal arterial pH is 7.35-7.45. Acidosis <7.35, alkalosis >7.45.'),
('03b52694-7204-5cdc-af24-bbefe4e1c82f'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Normal arterial pCO2 is:', '["25-35 mmHg","55-65 mmHg","35-45 mmHg","45-55 mmHg","20-30 mmHg"]'::jsonb, '35-45 mmHg', 'Normal pCO2 is 35-45 mmHg. Elevated (>45) indicates respiratory acidosis. Low (<35) indicates respiratory alkalosis.'),
('61ab4f23-b405-5a63-bfc2-ec362e00cc43'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'In respiratory acidosis, compensation involves:', '["Decreased renal H+ excretion","Increased renal HCO3 reabsorption","Increased HCO3 excretion","No compensation","Hyperventilation"]'::jsonb, 'Increased renal HCO3 reabsorption', 'Chronic respiratory acidosis is compensated by renal retention of HCO3 (increases over 3-5 days).'),
('6f165661-7ca3-5a13-9c79-b2162933ba39'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'In metabolic acidosis, compensation involves:', '["Hypoventilation","Renal HCO3 retention","Increased renal H+ excretion","No compensation","Hyperventilation"]'::jsonb, 'Hyperventilation', 'Respiratory compensation for metabolic acidosis is hyperventilation, lowering pCO2 (Kussmaul breathing).'),
('93beffeb-d023-5f57-8e5a-2401ca9f8173'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Anion gap metabolic acidosis causes (MUDPILES):', '["Malignancy, Ulcer, Dehydration clinical presentation","Methanol, Uremia, DKA, Paraldehyde, Isoniazid, Lactic acidosis, Ethylene glycol, Salicylates","Meningitis, Urinary retention clinical presentation","Myopathy, Uremia clinical presentation","Mumps, UTI, Diabetes clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Methanol, Uremia, DKA, Paraldehyde, Isoniazid, Lactic acidosis, Ethylene glycol, Salicylates', 'MUDPILES mnemonic for high anion gap metabolic acidosis.'),
('c4de6915-7a9c-51dd-a2a7-7846e673edce'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Non-anion gap metabolic acidosis causes (HARDUP):', '["Ethylene glycol, Isoniazid clinical presentation","Hyperalimentation, Acetazolamide, RTA, Diarrhea, Ureterostomy, Pancreatic fistula","Lactic acidosis clinical presentation","Methanol, Uremia, DKA clinical presentation","Salicylate poisoning clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Hyperalimentation, Acetazolamide, RTA, Diarrhea, Ureterostomy, Pancreatic fistula', 'HARDUP for hyperchloremic (non-AG) metabolic acidosis from HCO3 loss.'),
('f9fec7ce-5a22-5758-825f-86f0ffc8e5b6'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Which test is used to diagnose diabetes mellitus?', '["Fingerstick only clinical presentation","Random glucose clinical presentation","HbA1c alone clinical presentation","Fasting glucose >126 mg/dL, HbA1c >6.5%, or OGTT 2h >200 mg/dL","Urine glucose clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Fasting glucose >126 mg/dL, HbA1c >6.5%, or OGTT 2h >200 mg/dL', 'Diabetes is diagnosed by fasting glucose >126, HbA1c >6.5%, 2h OGTT >200, or random >200 with symptoms.'),
('c3f4ec45-0d0a-5269-b1fd-1c65a3e4b7e4'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'OGTT (oral glucose tolerance test) involves:', '["75 g glucose load, measure at 0 and 2 hours","50 g glucose, measure at 1 hour","100 g glucose, measure at 3 hours","25 g glucose, measure at 30 min","No glucose load"]'::jsonb, '75 g glucose load, measure at 0 and 2 hours', 'OGTT uses 75g glucose (WHO) for diabetes and GDM diagnosis.'),
('fe94f831-104a-5480-9031-75b2a3f53096'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Which lipoprotein is most atherogenic?', '["IDL","Chylomicrons","LDL","HDL","VLDL"]'::jsonb, 'LDL', 'LDL is the most atherogenic lipoprotein. HDL is protective (reverse cholesterol transport).'),
('7e8e3d3f-443d-540d-a53d-322adebf35f2'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Normal total cholesterol is:', '["<100 mg/dL","<250 mg/dL","<300 mg/dL","<200 mg/dL","<150 mg/dL"]'::jsonb, '<200 mg/dL', 'Desirable total cholesterol <200 mg/dL. Borderline 200-239. High >240.'),
('7e50c33d-4735-5a7e-969f-0a5d1733aea8'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Normal LDL cholesterol is:', '["<130 mg/dL","<70 mg/dL","<190 mg/dL","<160 mg/dL","<100 mg/dL"]'::jsonb, '<130 mg/dL', 'Optimal LDL <100 mg/dL. Near optimal 100-129. Borderline 130-159. High >160.'),
('6554125a-28ed-5638-a1f9-36afee5542a8'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Normal HDL cholesterol is:', '[">40 mg/dL (men), >50 mg/dL",">60 mg/dL",">30 mg/dL",">20 mg/dL",">70 mg/dL"]'::jsonb, '>40 mg/dL (men), >50 mg/dL', 'Low HDL <40 (men), <50 (women). High HDL >60 is protective.'),
('f60749cf-0425-584c-a042-45a1f73e7fb9'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Normal triglycerides are:', '["<50 mg/dL","<200 mg/dL","<150 mg/dL","<100 mg/dL","<300 mg/dL"]'::jsonb, '<150 mg/dL', 'Normal TG <150 mg/dL. Borderline 150-199. High 200-499. Very high >500.'),
('03c1cb3c-c446-554f-a1e7-67db62d9e030'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Which drug is first-line for hypercholesterolemia?', '["Niacin","Fibrates","Statins","Ezetimibe","Bile acid sequestrants"]'::jsonb, 'Statins', 'Statins are first-line for LDL reduction by inhibiting cholesterol synthesis.'),
('4d03a283-6370-5948-89ad-b3d6b6c7a873'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Which LFT pattern suggests acute viral hepatitis?', '["ALP markedly elevated, ALT mildly elevated","Normal LFTs","Low ALT, high ALP","GGT isolated elevation","ALT/AST markedly elevated (>500), ALP mildly elevated"]'::jsonb, 'ALT/AST markedly elevated (>500), ALP mildly elevated', 'Acute viral hepatitis shows massive ALT/AST rise (500-2000+ U/L) with mild ALP elevation.'),
('031fe2f7-9e33-500c-b278-afa6192c7a13'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Which LFT pattern suggests alcoholic hepatitis?', '["AST > ALT (ratio >2), GGT elevated","ALT > AST, GGT normal","ALP > AST","Normal LFTs","Low albumin alone"]'::jsonb, 'AST > ALT (ratio >2), GGT elevated', 'Alcoholic hepatitis: AST:ALT >2, GGT elevated (induced by alcohol), bilirubin may be high.'),
('03734043-ee8a-5285-9957-f491cdf43f57'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Which LFT pattern suggests extrahepatic biliary obstruction?', '["ALP and GGT markedly elevated, bilirubin elevated (direct), ALT/AST mildly elevated","ALT/AST >1000 clinical presentation clinical presentation","Normal LFTs clinical presentation clinical presentation","Low albumin clinical presentation clinical presentation","Elevated ammonia alone clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'ALP and GGT markedly elevated, bilirubin elevated (direct), ALT/AST mildly elevated', 'Cholestatic pattern: ALP and GGT disproportionately high (>3x normal) compared to ALT/AST.'),
('3bc5d71a-f663-589d-ae2c-6c01b432393e'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Which LFT pattern suggests end-stage cirrhosis?', '["High ALP, normal PT clinical presentation","Normal all clinical presentation","High GGT only clinical presentation","High ALT, normal albumin clinical presentation","Low albumin, prolonged PT, bilirubin elevated, ALT/AST may be normal"]'::jsonb, 'Low albumin, prolonged PT, bilirubin elevated, ALT/AST may be normal', 'Cirrhosis shows decreased synthetic function (low albumin, prolonged PT) with variable transaminases.'),
('98188863-951b-5031-b09f-df5edb35e80c'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'A core needle biopsy provides:', '["Fluid for culture clinical presentation","Only superficial cells clinical presentation","Cells for cytology only clinical presentation","No diagnostic material clinical presentation","A cylindrical core of tissue for histology"]'::jsonb, 'A cylindrical core of tissue for histology', 'Core needle biopsy obtains a tissue core preserving architecture for histopathologic diagnosis.'),
('54414267-f743-510e-8eaa-f7368e48f591'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Fine needle aspiration (FNA) provides:', '["Full organ section","Immunohistochemistry only","Cells for cytologic examination","Frozen section","Tissue architecture"]'::jsonb, 'Cells for cytologic examination', 'FNA aspirates cells for cytology, not tissue architecture. Useful for rapid diagnosis of masses.'),
('cbfd3b06-2ccb-52a2-9d4d-c0ebc8bd85da'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Which biopsy method is preferred for diagnosing breast cancer?', '["Punch biopsy","Excisional biopsy","Core needle biopsy","Incisional biopsy","FNA"]'::jsonb, 'Core needle biopsy', 'Core needle biopsy provides enough tissue for histology and receptor studies in breast cancer.'),
('4325e1de-5aff-501a-8f1a-ec6ecfe19e17'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Excisional biopsy involves:', '["Complete removal of the entire lesion","Removal of a small tissue piece","Needle aspiration","Shave biopsy","Punch biopsy"]'::jsonb, 'Complete removal of the entire lesion', 'Excisional biopsy removes the entire lesion, used for small lesions (<2 cm).'),
('104a1148-5614-55f6-8878-d2748fcfc2ef'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Incisional biopsy involves:', '["Complete lesion removal clinical presentation","Needle aspiration clinical presentation","Removal of a small wedge from a larger lesion","Frozen section clinical presentation","Cytology brush clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Removal of a small wedge from a larger lesion', 'Incisional biopsy samples part of a large lesion to establish diagnosis before definitive surgery.'),
('2178bbda-95ad-53fa-beea-c02684089361'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Shave biopsy is used for:', '["Superficial skin lesions","Deep subcutaneous masses","Bone lesions","Liver masses","Lung masses"]'::jsonb, 'Superficial skin lesions', 'Shave biopsy removes superficial skin lesions level with the skin surface.'),
('51b65167-7252-5f00-b63e-b7b520f1755d'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Punch biopsy is used for:', '["Lymph nodes","Breast masses","Deep organ masses","Bone marrow","Skin lesions"]'::jsonb, 'Skin lesions', 'Punch biopsy (3-6 mm) provides full-thickness skin specimen for dermal pathology.'),
('4bdb95ae-7b4f-50e3-9b07-48038ec835bd'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Endoscopic biopsy is commonly used for:', '["GI tract and bronchial lesions","Skin lesions","Bone lesions","Breast masses","Thyroid nodules"]'::jsonb, 'GI tract and bronchial lesions', 'Endoscopic forceps biopsy samples mucosal lesions during endoscopy/colonoscopy/bronchoscopy.'),
('76077423-329a-5d05-ab29-5f80d3ef2259'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Frozen section biopsy is used for:', '["Research only","Screening","Intraoperative diagnosis","Genetic testing","Routine diagnosis"]'::jsonb, 'Intraoperative diagnosis', 'Frozen section provides rapid intraoperative diagnosis to guide surgical decision-making.'),
('2f16eb6b-f311-5fc5-a746-b93db71719c2'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Which tissue is not suitable for frozen section?', '["Fatty tissue","Liver","Lymph node","Breast","Skin"]'::jsonb, 'Fatty tissue', 'Fatty tissue freezes poorly, causing artifacts. Bone also requires decalcification unsuitable for frozen section.'),
('a15b51b7-ba1c-59ac-aa24-c2a04dea86e8'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Advantage of frozen section over routine paraffin section is:', '["Larger specimen","Speed","Easier to interpret","Cheaper","Better morphology"]'::jsonb, 'Speed', 'Frozen section takes 20-30 minutes; paraffin section takes 24-48+ hours.'),
('016da8b7-edbd-5150-8e73-f0c63e6f3df3'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Which fixative is most commonly used for routine histopathology?', '["10% neutral buffered formalin","Alcohol","Glutaraldehyde","Bouin solution","Zenker solution"]'::jsonb, '10% neutral buffered formalin', '10% NBF is the standard fixative for routine H&E histopathology.'),
('b01e5712-0789-57ea-9a25-83233263da7b'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Glutaraldehyde is preferred for:', '["Routine histology","Cytology","Electron microscopy","Frozen section","Immunofluorescence"]'::jsonb, 'Electron microscopy', 'Glutaraldehyde provides excellent ultrastructural preservation for electron microscopy.'),
('d9fb8387-d7dd-5362-89fe-55fa46c3b396'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Carnoy fixative is used for:', '["Routine histology clinical presentation","Immunohistochemistry clinical presentation","Preserving glycogen and nucleic acids","Frozen section clinical presentation","EM clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Preserving glycogen and nucleic acids', 'Carnoy (ethanol:chloroform:acetic acid) preserves glycogen and DNA/RNA well.'),
('547ef103-35ab-5db9-8229-99ad1475a16a'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Bouin fixative is used for:', '["Routine histology","Frozen section","Testicular and GI biopsies","Cytology","EM"]'::jsonb, 'Testicular and GI biopsies', 'Bouin fixative (picric acid) preserves nuclear detail well, often used for testis and GI biopsies.'),
('6ba020b6-50e3-5e08-b964-7305e8611478'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'The process of tissue processing for paraffin embedding involves:', '["Fixation, dehydration, clearing, infiltration, embedding","Embedding in paraffin directly clinical presentation","Freezing only clinical presentation","Fixation only clinical presentation","Dehydration only clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Fixation, dehydration, clearing, infiltration, embedding', 'Standard processing: fix → dehydrate (alcohol) → clear (xylene) → infiltrate (paraffin) → embed.'),
('06546c18-c8f1-5040-94f1-5db7840c5b47'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Which step in tissue processing removes alcohol from tissue?', '["Fixation","Clearing","Embedding","Staining","Sectioning"]'::jsonb, 'Clearing', 'Xylene (clearing agent) removes alcohol and makes tissue miscible with paraffin.'),
('2f3d5f84-ae02-50c1-959e-08b0c6891410'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Microtome is used for:', '["Examining slides","Cutting thin tissue sections","Staining slides","Embedding tissue in paraffin","Fixing tissue"]'::jsonb, 'Cutting thin tissue sections', 'Microtome cuts paraffin-embedded tissue into thin sections (typically 4-5 microns).'),
('bf36bed7-3a76-5fc7-ba47-1d8eebe690a0'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Cryostat is used for:', '["Embedding tissue clinical presentation","Fixation clinical presentation","Paraffin sectioning clinical presentation","Cutting frozen sections for rapid diagnosis","Staining slides clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Cutting frozen sections for rapid diagnosis', 'Cryostat is a freezing microtome for frozen sections during intraoperative consultation.'),
('7ab5fa72-8da8-5346-afc2-9c77e3988c59'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'H&E staining involves:', '["Eosin only clinical presentation","Special stain clinical presentation","Hematoxylin (nuclei blue) and Eosin","Gram stain clinical presentation","Hematoxylin only clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Hematoxylin (nuclei blue) and Eosin', 'H&E is the standard stain: hematoxylin stains nuclei blue/purple; eosin stains cytoplasm pink.'),
('dd2eb475-e31a-5711-bfac-17b3f0317975'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Which stain is used for reticulin fibers?', '["van Gieson","Congo red","Masson''s trichrome","Silver stain","PAS"]'::jsonb, 'Silver stain', 'Reticulin (type III collagen) is demonstrated by silver impregnation (reticulin stain).'),
('bcb5b04c-466e-5400-afa6-61eab9f92b0d'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Which stain is used for collagen?', '["Giemsa","Silver stain","Masson''s trichrome","PAS","Congo red"]'::jsonb, 'Masson''s trichrome', 'Masson''s trichrome stains collagen blue/green, muscle red.'),
('dbe268b6-7015-5d16-aac2-40c8e0a084b9'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Which stain is used for mucin?', '["Congo red clinical presentation","PAS (periodic acid-Schiff) and Alcian blue","Silver stain clinical presentation","Masson''s trichrome clinical presentation","Gram stain clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'PAS (periodic acid-Schiff) and Alcian blue', 'PAS stains neutral mucins magenta; Alcian blue stains acidic mucins blue.'),
('7f2ca81a-2a6f-5190-ba56-e0e8266966ec'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Which stain is used for amyloid?', '["Congo red","PAS","Masson''s trichrome","Silver stain","H&E"]'::jsonb, 'Congo red', 'Congo red under polarized light shows apple-green birefringence diagnostic of amyloid.'),
('b70b50e8-1f1f-559a-a21a-6b5d3a2b4ee5'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Which stain is used for acid-fast bacilli?', '["Ziehl-Neelsen","Gram stain","Giemsa","PAS","Silver stain"]'::jsonb, 'Ziehl-Neelsen', 'ZN stain uses hot carbol fuchsin; AFB appear red against blue counterstain.'),
('fcfdfb5b-46b1-53a0-a2ef-2a8ccfe52ebc'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Which stain is used for Helicobacter pylori detection?', '["Gram stain clinical presentation","PAS clinical presentation","Giemsa or Warthin-Starry silver stain","Congo red clinical presentation","ZN stain clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Giemsa or Warthin-Starry silver stain', 'H. pylori is well seen on Giemsa stain or Warthin-Starry silver stain in gastric biopsies.'),
('25b00912-4bf4-5629-865f-48ed55251ada'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Immunohistochemistry (IHC) detects:', '["DNA sequences","Microorganisms only","Specific proteins using antibodies","Morphology only","RNA sequences"]'::jsonb, 'Specific proteins using antibodies', 'IHC uses antibodies to detect specific antigens in tissue sections (e.g., cytokeratin, HER2).'),
('7b882f75-5ece-57db-adb7-ebcb4f29f6f3'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'In situ hybridization (ISH) detects:', '["Carbohydrates clinical presentation","Lipids clinical presentation","Specific nucleic acid sequences in tissue","All of the above clinical presentation","Proteins clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Specific nucleic acid sequences in tissue', 'ISH uses labeled DNA/RNA probes to detect specific nucleotide sequences (e.g., EBV EBER).'),
('4cf6b65c-5a7e-54b4-b38d-86820ebcf5ab'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'FISH (fluorescence in situ hybridization) detects:', '["Chromosomal abnormalities","Point mutations","Protein expression","Methylation","RNA expression"]'::jsonb, 'Chromosomal abnormalities', 'FISH detects chromosomal aberrations like HER2 amplification or BCR-ABL translocation.'),
('f75a8c0d-c91f-5900-b571-abe6da3c3b6d'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'PCR in pathology is used for:', '["Electrolyte measurement clinical presentation","Protein detection clinical presentation","Lipid analysis clinical presentation","Carbohydrate analysis clinical presentation","Detecting infectious agents, gene rearrangements, mutations"]'::jsonb, 'Detecting infectious agents, gene rearrangements, mutations', 'PCR amplifies DNA/RNA for detecting pathogens, clonal rearrangements, and mutations.'),
('e7b972a9-bfce-53d3-ab43-6f1e009fc0db'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Flow cytometry is used for:', '["Immunophenotyping of cells in suspension","Genetic testing","Tissue histology","Protein structure analysis","Metabolic analysis"]'::jsonb, 'Immunophenotyping of cells in suspension', 'Flow cytometry detects cell surface markers (CD antigens) for leukemia/lymphoma classification.'),
('20e43678-3cc5-5653-a177-7347d8d34687'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Electron microscopy in pathology is used for:', '["Ultrastructural analysis","Routine histology","Protein analysis","DNA sequencing","H&E stain"]'::jsonb, 'Ultrastructural analysis', 'EM is used for specific indications: ciliary defects, renal diseases, viral identification.'),
('1604534c-c721-503f-acc0-b7f9274dcbd1'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Which biopsy type is required for lymphoma diagnosis?', '["Core needle biopsy","Punch biopsy","Shave biopsy","Excisional biopsy of lymph node","FNA"]'::jsonb, 'Excisional biopsy of lymph node', 'Lymphoma diagnosis requires excisional biopsy for architecture (follicular vs diffuse pattern) and immunophenotyping.'),
('ca2ad534-88f7-54c5-bdfc-ea94e8553229'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Which biopsy is preferred for pigmented skin lesions (suspected melanoma)?', '["FNA clinical presentation","Excisional biopsy with 2 mm margin","Incisional biopsy clinical presentation","Punch biopsy clinical presentation","Shave biopsy clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Excisional biopsy with 2 mm margin', 'Suspected melanoma requires excisional biopsy for accurate Breslow thickness measurement.'),
('6ff81eaf-f30e-5ea9-a2e7-e6a50a7c0306'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Shave biopsy is contraindicated for:', '["Benign nevi","Suspected melanoma","Seborrheic keratosis","Skin tags","Warts"]'::jsonb, 'Suspected melanoma', 'Shave biopsy transects the deep margin, preventing accurate Breslow thickness in melanoma.'),
('245cca63-187e-5563-ba3d-d06049a832e4'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Core needle biopsy is typically performed under:', '["Image guidance","Direct vision","Blind technique","Endoscopic guidance","Palpation only"]'::jsonb, 'Image guidance', 'Image-guided core needle biopsy ensures accurate sampling of deep or non-palpable lesions.'),
('85b694ec-575a-5681-b1ae-83df7f6f3155'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'What is the gauge of a typical core needle biopsy?', '["25-30 gauge","10-12 gauge","14-18 gauge","20-25 gauge","7-10 gauge"]'::jsonb, '14-18 gauge', 'Core needles are typically 14-18G. FNA uses 22-25G. Larger gauge = smaller diameter.'),
('238a077f-16db-5a35-984c-c4008bbd20b6'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Which fixative is NOT suitable for immunohistochemistry?', '["10% NBF","Zinc formalin","Formalin","Bouin fixative","Alcohol"]'::jsonb, 'Bouin fixative', 'Bouin fixative (picric acid) is not suitable for IHC as it damages protein epitopes.'),
('e5ecf2e0-c1d0-51fa-a9ce-f5799ee14d0a'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Antigen retrieval in IHC involves:', '["Heat-induced (HIER) or enzymatic (PIER) methods to unmask epitopes","Sectioning clinical presentation","Dewaxing clinical presentation","Hydration clinical presentation","Counterstaining clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Heat-induced (HIER) or enzymatic (PIER) methods to unmask epitopes', 'Antigen retrieval is needed to reverse formalin-induced protein crosslinking for antibody binding.'),
('01ddcb93-017f-5828-baae-9fbafc94d32c'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'DAB (diaminobenzidine) in IHC produces:', '["Black color clinical presentation","Green color clinical presentation","Brown color at the site of antigen-antibody reaction","Red color clinical presentation","Blue color clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Brown color at the site of antigen-antibody reaction', 'DAB is the most common chromogen in IHC, producing a brown reaction product.'),
('4854721f-02a1-56ef-9f35-a3279aa411d9'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'A pathology request form should include all EXCEPT:', '["Patient''s income","Clinical history","Patient name, age, sex","Relevant investigations","Specimen type and site"]'::jsonb, 'Patient''s income', 'Request forms need clinical info, not financial data. Proper ID and history are essential.'),
('43bd1222-c082-5385-82e0-e11dee11bfe9'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Which information is essential on a specimen label?', '["Patient name, ID, specimen type, site, date","Only patient name clinical presentation","Only diagnosis clinical presentation","Only hospital name clinical presentation","Only doctor name clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Patient name, ID, specimen type, site, date', 'Correct labeling requires at least 2 patient identifiers, specimen type, site, and date/time.'),
('ee03ed16-7e21-5c02-8e80-06f45a445a20'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Specimens for histopathology should be placed in:', '["Nothing","Saline","Alcohol","Water","10% neutral buffered formalin"]'::jsonb, '10% neutral buffered formalin', 'Formalin fixation volume should be 10-20x the specimen volume for adequate fixation.'),
('7069dfa9-8fc8-5a49-8e66-c2ef10d5aab3'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'The ideal fixation time for routine histology is:', '["1 hour","1 minute","6-24 hours","1 month","1 week"]'::jsonb, '6-24 hours', 'Tissue <1 cm thick fixes in 6-24 hours. Under-fixation causes autolysis; over-fixation causes antigen masking.'),
('8b69d6ef-7cfb-5c9a-8718-494b92d7db55'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Specimens for immunofluorescence should be placed in:', '["Alcohol clinical presentation","Michel transport medium or saline","Bouin clinical presentation","Glutaraldehyde clinical presentation","Formalin clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Michel transport medium or saline', 'Immunofluorescence requires unfixed frozen tissue. Michel medium preserves antigens for transport.'),
('7bfb9dae-bedb-586b-aae9-8f275ed6285a'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Specimens for electron microscopy should be placed in:', '["Formalin","Saline","Glutaraldehyde","Bouin","Alcohol"]'::jsonb, 'Glutaraldehyde', 'Glutaraldehyde provides best ultrastructural preservation for EM. Tissue should be <1 mm cubes.'),
('3de3c250-fc72-56dc-bf45-993e155232cc'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Which specimen is NOT fixed and sent fresh for flow cytometry?', '["Lymph node for lymphoma workup","Routine skin biopsy","Liver biopsy","Kidney biopsy","Thyroid lobe"]'::jsonb, 'Lymph node for lymphoma workup', 'Lymphoma specimens need fresh tissue for flow cytometry. Fixation kills cells.'),
('58de335b-3d68-5c5b-8dd8-07d6f52b4ebe'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Decalcification of bone specimens is achieved with:', '["Alcohol","EDTA or acid","Xylene","Paraffin","Formalin"]'::jsonb, 'EDTA or acid', 'Acid decalcification (formic/nitric) is faster but harsher; EDTA is slower but preserves antigenicity.'),
('d73e4478-5f38-5933-aa97-400bd8d38da8'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Which biopsy is contraindicated in suspected hydatid cyst?', '["All biopsies","FNA clinical marker","Incisional biopsy","Core needle biopsy","Excisional biopsy"]'::jsonb, 'FNA clinical marker', 'FNA of hydatid cyst risks anaphylaxis from cyst fluid leakage. Serology is preferred for diagnosis.'),
('1a2d16fb-fedb-5547-8c34-d0ee6e25fa86'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Which procedure is performed under ultrasound guidance to obtain fetal cells?', '["Core biopsy clinical presentation","Laparoscopy clinical presentation","Amniocentesis (16-18 wks) or CVS","Excisional biopsy clinical presentation","FNA clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Amniocentesis (16-18 wks) or CVS', 'Amniocentesis and CVS are prenatal diagnostic procedures for genetic testing.'),
('c334b759-9955-5528-b8f5-bb480d0a9353'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Which specimen must be sent for culture in a sterile container?', '["Bone marrow for aspirate","Urine for cytology","Tissue for microbiology","Blood for chemistry","Tissue for histology"]'::jsonb, 'Tissue for microbiology', 'Microbiology specimens require sterile collection to avoid contamination.'),
('d5ce380f-c2fb-5316-9e50-e52162173be9'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'How should a mastectomy specimen be oriented for pathology?', '["Cut into pieces","Placed in small container","No orientation needed","Sent dry","Sutures or ink marks for margins orientation"]'::jsonb, 'Sutures or ink marks for margins orientation', 'Surgical margins should be marked (ink, sutures) for margin assessment. Specimen should be intact.'),
('edfe5b5b-ca2d-5230-beea-1932b2735389'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Which specimen must be sent immediately for cryoglobulin testing?', '["Serum frozen","Blood kept at 37°C","Blood on ice","Blood at room temp","Plasma on ice"]'::jsonb, 'Blood kept at 37°C', 'Cryoglobulins precipitate at cold temperatures; blood must be kept at 37°C for accurate detection.'),
('f3a22bc1-6bb3-5364-bb7f-dee160661dc6'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'The ''triple test'' for breast diagnosis involves:', '["Blood tests only clinical presentation","Only imaging clinical presentation","Clinical exam, imaging (mammography/US), and FNA/core biopsy","Only biopsy clinical presentation","Only clinical exam clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Clinical exam, imaging (mammography/US), and FNA/core biopsy', 'Triple assessment (clinical, imaging, pathology) is the gold standard for breast lump evaluation.'),
('21b6d76e-30d3-5a0f-9b5d-34d1e69874de'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Which specimen requires immediate transport to pathology?', '["Gallstones","Testicular biopsy for infertility","Skin tag","All of the above","Routine gallbladder"]'::jsonb, 'Testicular biopsy for infertility', 'Testicular biopsy for sperm extraction requires immediate processing to prevent drying.'),
('afe29851-59b3-5eca-9488-aa52ee248692'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Bone marrow biopsy is typically taken from:', '["Posterior superior iliac crest","Sternum","Femur","Anterior iliac crest","Tibia"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior superior iliac crest', 'Posterior superior iliac crest is the preferred site for bone marrow biopsy and aspiration.'),
('5a559b35-bd67-5dfc-9ae2-5a158eef020b'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Bone marrow aspiration should be performed before biopsy because:', '["Aspiration disrupts architecture if done after biopsy","It is easier clinical presentation","Both are same clinical presentation","No reason clinical presentation","It is more painful clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Aspiration disrupts architecture if done after biopsy', 'Aspiration should precede trephine biopsy to avoid blood contamination from biopsy site.'),
('5d2d1e8f-6d5c-5c34-b12c-b281c27940a1'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) analysis includes:', '["Only protein clinical presentation","Only glucose clinical presentation","Only cell count clinical presentation","Only culture clinical presentation","Cell count, protein, glucose, culture, cytology"]'::jsonb, 'Cell count, protein, glucose, culture, cytology', 'CSF analysis: cells, protein, glucose (compare with serum), Gram stain, culture, and cytology.'),
('a9333593-cdbe-5567-b73c-cff0ee1ba1a2'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'CSF protein is elevated in:', '["Only in trauma","Normal finding","Meningitis (bacterial, TB, fungal) and MS","In viral infections only","Hypoglycemia"]'::jsonb, 'Meningitis (bacterial, TB, fungal) and MS', 'Elevated CSF protein indicates blood-brain barrier disruption (infection, inflammation, tumor).'),
('a01e6f33-bf04-5e9f-aee2-485e06bd38d6'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'CSF glucose is decreased in:', '["Multiple sclerosis clinical presentation","Normal CSF clinical presentation","Migraine clinical presentation","Bacterial, TB, and fungal meningitis","Viral meningitis clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Bacterial, TB, and fungal meningitis', 'Low CSF glucose (<40% of serum) indicates bacterial/TB/fungal meningitis (not viral).'),
('2302db6d-b772-5e2b-81ba-84792e0c11c1'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Xanthochromia of CSF indicates:', '["Normal CSF","Traumatic tap","Infection","Subarachnoid hemorrhage","Tumor"]'::jsonb, 'Subarachnoid hemorrhage', 'Xanthochromia (yellow CSF) from bilirubin indicates SAH vs traumatic tap (clear supernatant).'),
('4ce93659-4b78-559f-a9f8-262f708a796c'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'What is the proper handling of an amputated limb for pathology?', '["Discard without examination clinical presentation","Only send a small piece clinical presentation","Fix whole limb in formalin clinical presentation","Freeze without fixation clinical presentation","Send intact for margin assessment and then section"]'::jsonb, 'Send intact for margin assessment and then section', 'Limb amputation specimens require margin assessment and systematic dissection for pathology.'),
('bd23c598-b355-5c56-ba38-4b44b15033b4'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'How should a radical prostatectomy specimen be processed?', '["Random biopsy clinical presentation","Entirely submitted after inking and serial sectioning","Only the tumor clinical presentation","Only the margins clinical presentation","FNA only clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Entirely submitted after inking and serial sectioning', 'Radical prostatectomy requires complete embedding for accurate Gleason grading and staging.'),
('dc2eed18-dfee-57d5-9c76-f0ea9c852717'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Which specimen is submitted entirely for microscopic examination?', '["Small biopsies","Large organ specimens","Amputated limbs","Mastectomy specimens","Colectomy specimens"]'::jsonb, 'Small biopsies', 'Small biopsies (GI, bronchial, skin punch, core needles) are submitted in toto for diagnosis.'),
('e87b8170-9c72-525d-aa91-62f4aaac0f84'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'What is the purpose of ''tissue microarray'' (TMA)?', '["High-throughput analysis of multiple tissue samples on one slide","3D tissue reconstruction clinical presentation","Electron microscopy clinical presentation","Protein sequencing clinical presentation","Gene sequencing clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'High-throughput analysis of multiple tissue samples on one slide', 'TMA cores from multiple paraffin blocks are arrayed on one slide for efficient IHC or FISH.'),
('66dfd626-238c-5e73-900d-cf997d675860'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'What is the role of telepathology?', '["Electronic health records","Automated fixation","Genetic testing","Remote digital pathology consultation","Robotic surgery"]'::jsonb, 'Remote digital pathology consultation', 'Telepathology uses digital imaging for remote diagnosis and consultation.'),
('503b6a9f-33c6-5f2d-8c10-ef023c76589d'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Which sample is used for ''liquid biopsy'' in cancer?', '["Tissue biopsy clinical presentation","Stool clinical presentation","Saliva clinical presentation","Circulating tumor DNA (ctDNA) from blood","Urine clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Circulating tumor DNA (ctDNA) from blood', 'Liquid biopsy detects ctDNA in blood for molecular profiling and monitoring without tissue biopsy.'),
('742b235a-c3cf-5084-bd88-82d5e937d0c4'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'What is ''formalin pigment'' and how is it removed?', '["Acid formaldehyde hematin","A type of stain","A fixative","A processing artifact","Normal tissue component"]'::jsonb, 'Acid formaldehyde hematin', 'Formalin pigment (black/brown crystals) forms in acidic formalin. Removed by treating with saturated picric acid.'),
('ef341164-adb6-53ba-a03c-91b603d5eeea'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'How should a nephrectomy specimen for tumor be processed?', '["Inked, bivalved, sampled from tumor, renal vein, capsule, and ureter","Discard clinical presentation","Only tumor biopsy clinical presentation","Only normal kidney clinical presentation","Frozen entire clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Inked, bivalved, sampled from tumor, renal vein, capsule, and ureter', 'Nephrectomy for RCC requires sampling tumor, margin, renal vein (for invasion), capsule, and normal kidney.'),
('093f10dd-d92a-573e-ad91-b28ffcae9425'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Immunohistochemistry panel for carcinoma of unknown primary includes:', '["Vimentin only clinical presentation","p53 only clinical presentation","Ki67 only clinical presentation","LCA only clinical presentation","Cytokeratin (AE1/AE3), CK7, CK20, TTF1, GATA3, etc."]'::jsonb, 'Cytokeratin (AE1/AE3), CK7, CK20, TTF1, GATA3, etc.', 'IHC panel identifies primary site by cytokeratin pattern and organ-specific markers (TTF1=lung, GATA3=breast).'),
('5e45db21-6548-5447-9c71-0ca401db4050'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Which cytokeratin is expressed in adenocarcinoma of lung?', '["CK7 and CK20 both positive","CK5/6 positive","CK20 positive, CK7 negative","CK7 and CK20 both negative","CK7 positive, CK20 negative"]'::jsonb, 'CK7 positive, CK20 negative', 'Lung adenocarcinoma is CK7+/CK20-. Colon adenocarcinoma is CK20+/CK7-.'),
('0dfee13e-3bf2-5388-a91d-71ef13c96773'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Which immunostain is specific for prostate cancer?', '["PSAP clinical presentation","GATA3 clinical presentation","CK7 clinical presentation","PSA (prostate-specific antigen) and NKX3.1","AMACR clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'PSA (prostate-specific antigen) and NKX3.1', 'PSA and NKX3.1 are sensitive/specific markers for prostatic origin.'),
('f546c66e-11dd-5bbb-83d5-28441142b80b'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Which IHC marker identifies breast cancer origin?', '["CDX2","TTF1","Vimentin","PSA","GATA3, mammaglobin, ER, PR"]'::jsonb, 'GATA3, mammaglobin, ER, PR', 'GATA3 and mammaglobin are breast-specific markers. ER/PR indicate hormone receptor status.'),
('b36bc261-48de-524e-8004-d28bc321ed42'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Which IHC marker identifies neuroendocrine differentiation?', '["Chromogranin A and synaptophysin","Cytokeratin clinical presentation","Vimentin clinical presentation","LCA clinical presentation","S100 clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Chromogranin A and synaptophysin', 'Chromogranin and synaptophysin are markers of neuroendocrine differentiation.'),
('1d4416de-c3da-5b9a-8403-d4da1c3b260e'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Which IHC marker is used for melanoma diagnosis?', '["S100, HMB45, Melan-A, SOX10","Cytokeratin","LCA","Vimentin","CD34"]'::jsonb, 'S100, HMB45, Melan-A, SOX10', 'Melanoma markers: S100 (sensitive), HMB45, Melan-A/MART1, SOX10.'),
('dc12f92d-8d96-523a-a1fa-2c0a0f3932d5'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Which IHC marker is specific for Ewing sarcoma?', '["CD99","CD45","CD20","CD3","CD34"]'::jsonb, 'CD99', 'CD99 is expressed in Ewing sarcoma/PNET. Also positive in some lymphomas, so used in context.'),
('df953efb-cc73-5117-8ee8-69fee34cc133'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Which IHC marker identifies gastrointestinal stromal tumor (GIST)?', '["S100","Cytokeratin","CD34","Desmin","CD117 (c-kit) and DOG1"]'::jsonb, 'CD117 (c-kit) and DOG1', 'GIST is CD117 (c-kit) positive in >95%. DOG1 is a more specific marker.'),
('2f76ad8e-7ece-5907-90f4-a43dfbd295e5'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Which IHC marker identifies smooth muscle tumors?', '["Vimentin","Desmin and SMA","Cytokeratin","CD34","S100"]'::jsonb, 'Desmin and SMA', 'Desmin and SMA are markers of smooth muscle differentiation (leiomyoma, leiomyosarcoma).'),
('57e6c035-57f0-56d8-ace8-d615bd46843b'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Which IHC marker identifies skeletal muscle tumors?', '["CD34","S100","SMA","Desmin, myogenin, MyoD1","Cytokeratin"]'::jsonb, 'Desmin, myogenin, MyoD1', 'Myogenin and MyoD1 are nuclear markers for rhabdomyosarcoma (skeletal muscle).'),
('dfa18db5-4684-58e8-90de-1b1b230c78ec'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Which IHC marker identifies vascular tumors?', '["S100","LCA","Cytokeratin","CD31, CD34, ERG, Factor VIII","Desmin"]'::jsonb, 'CD31, CD34, ERG, Factor VIII', 'CD31 and ERG are the most sensitive/specific endothelial markers. CD34 is less specific.'),
('214acd45-ed49-521f-be47-10dfca1adf27'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Which IHC marker identifies lymphoid cells?', '["S100","Cytokeratin","CD34","Vimentin","LCA"]'::jsonb, 'LCA', 'LCA (leukocyte common antigen/CD45) is expressed on all hematopoietic cells except plasma cells.'),
('1e22ee10-656d-5cf7-93b7-d1809351d7d5'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Which IHC marker identifies T cells?', '["CD138","CD3","CD68","CD34","CD20"]'::jsonb, 'CD3', 'CD3 is the pan-T cell marker. CD20 is pan-B cell marker.'),
('c560a209-ebc8-5586-9d8a-dab28cd5db91'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Which IHC marker identifies B cells?', '["CD138","CD34","CD68","CD20","CD3"]'::jsonb, 'CD20', 'CD20 is expressed on mature B cells. CD138 is for plasma cells.'),
('57d37eb2-8164-5527-a1df-3730efb243ba'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Which IHC marker identifies plasma cells?', '["CD20","CD3","CD138","CD34","CD68"]'::jsonb, 'CD138', 'CD138 is a sensitive marker for plasma cells. MUM1 is also used.'),
('ba04d847-958d-562f-9659-46a698b8c5f3'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Which IHC marker identifies macrophages/histiocytes?', '["CD20","CD45","CD34","CD68","CD3"]'::jsonb, 'CD68', 'CD68 is a marker of macrophages, monocytes, and histiocytic tumors.'),
('d54a3abc-37d9-5fd0-8af6-fa6b1fec4b40'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Which IHC marker identifies myeloperoxidase in AML?', '["MPO","CD68","CD3","CD20","CD34"]'::jsonb, 'MPO', 'MPO is specific for myeloid differentiation (AML). TdT is for lymphoblastic leukemia.'),
('ed0c19a1-35fb-5b54-bb66-c33305d26510'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Ki67 (MIB-1) proliferation index indicates:', '["Metastasis risk","Apoptosis rate","Hormone receptor status","Tumor size","Tumor aggressiveness"]'::jsonb, 'Tumor aggressiveness', 'Ki67 labels all non-G0 cells. High Ki67 indicates high proliferation and more aggressive tumors.'),
('a5e590b5-ff5a-535e-9c2e-2f85d667e9c3'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'p53 IHC overexpression usually indicates:', '["p53 deletion","TP53 mutation","p53 methylation","Wild-type p53","Normal p53 function"]'::jsonb, 'TP53 mutation', 'Mutant p53 protein accumulates and is detected by IHC as nuclear overexpression.'),
('25cfc0b9-4a8e-5fbe-bb07-c75ff441cf95'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Which factor determines if a core needle biopsy is adequate for diagnosis?', '["Fixation time clinical presentation","Size of needle only clinical presentation","Time of day clinical presentation","Patient age clinical presentation","Number of cores and lesional tissue representation"]'::jsonb, 'Number of cores and lesional tissue representation', 'Adequate biopsy must contain lesional tissue. Multiple cores improve diagnostic yield.'),
('b12ee5f2-9674-5583-a222-f68822caf31e'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'What is the recommended fixative volume to tissue ratio?', '["100:1","1:1","5:1","10:1 to 20:1","50:1"]'::jsonb, '10:1 to 20:1', 'Adequate fixation requires 10-20x the tissue volume of formalin for proper penetration.'),
('0a52eaf6-a7c6-5fdb-8bfe-54422b9b6a04'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Fresh tissue for cryostat should be transported:', '["On ice or in saline-moistened gauze","In formalin clinical presentation","In alcohol clinical presentation","Dry at room temp clinical presentation","In glutaraldehyde clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'On ice or in saline-moistened gauze', 'Fresh tissue for frozen section should be transported unfixed, in saline or on ice.'),
('737a3f69-6b97-576c-a45c-591b6120f111'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'What is the primary purpose of margin assessment in cancer pathology?', '["To determine grade clinical presentation","To assess differentiation clinical presentation","To count mitoses clinical presentation","To determine if tumor extends to surgical margin","To measure tumor size clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'To determine if tumor extends to surgical margin', 'Positive margin means tumor cells at cut surface, indicating incomplete resection.'),
('c126897a-67fc-5c19-b825-818fdad9215a'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Which specimen must be sent for hormone receptor analysis in breast cancer?', '["Only FNA","Tumor in formalin (for IHC) and fresh/frozen for gene expression","Only formalin-fixed","Only frozen","Blood only"]'::jsonb, 'Only formalin-fixed', 'Hormone receptors (ER, PR) are assessed by IHC on formalin-fixed tissue. Fresh tissue is optional.'),
('76714e79-9f1d-54c8-9d36-67d0675ad6a0'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'What is the role of tumor banking?', '["Patient treatment only clinical presentation","Disposal only clinical presentation","Collecting and storing tissue for research","Administration clinical presentation","Routine diagnosis clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Collecting and storing tissue for research', 'Tumor banks collect, process, and store tissues with patient consent for biomedical research.'),
('734582d8-1e26-5828-a61e-ed3406247d28'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Which specimen is used for cytogenetic analysis in leukemia?', '["Core biopsy only","Bone marrow aspirate","FNA only","Blood only","Fixed tissue"]'::jsonb, 'Bone marrow aspirate', 'Bone marrow aspirate is preferred for cytogenetic (karyotype, FISH) analysis in hem malignancies.'),
('8c5dbaa0-9cb3-5793-9567-c9a425e6de7b'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'What is normal serum ferritin level in men?', '["500-1000 ng/mL","30-300 ng/mL","300-500 ng/mL","10-30 ng/mL","<10 ng/mL"]'::jsonb, '30-300 ng/mL', 'Normal ferritin: men 30-300 ng/mL, women 15-150 ng/mL. Low in iron deficiency, high in hemochromatosis.'),
('929bd4f8-148f-5899-a66b-d75d0e0fcc51'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Which test is the gold standard for iron deficiency diagnosis?', '["Transferrin saturation","Serum ferritin","TIBC","Bone marrow iron stain","Serum iron"]'::jsonb, 'Bone marrow iron stain', 'Absent bone marrow iron is the gold standard, but ferritin <15 is highly specific for iron deficiency.'),
('853732c5-ec40-5460-939b-c5dc08c2da16'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Serum vitamin B12 normal range is:', '["50-150 pg/mL","<100 pg/mL","900-1500 pg/mL","1500-2000 pg/mL","200-900 pg/mL"]'::jsonb, '200-900 pg/mL', 'Normal B12 is 200-900 pg/mL. Low in pernicious anemia, malabsorption. Methylmalonic acid is a more sensitive marker.'),
('a2a040c7-7e38-516e-96c3-93c43ba9c9af'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Serum folate normal range is:', '["1-3 ng/mL","50-100 ng/mL","3-20 ng/mL","20-50 ng/mL","<2 ng/mL"]'::jsonb, '3-20 ng/mL', 'Normal folate >4 ng/mL. RBC folate is a better measure of tissue stores (reflects last 3 months).'),
('9ee418b3-6ec0-5c58-8211-aeedf9154168'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Which enzyme is elevated in acute pancreatitis and also in parotitis?', '["Lipase","Chymotrypsin","Trypsin","Elastase","Amylase"]'::jsonb, 'Amylase', 'Amylase is elevated in pancreatitis, but also parotitis (mumps), perforated ulcer, and renal failure.'),
('34029698-6a26-55ac-9286-b0f458909281'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Which tumor marker is elevated in medullary carcinoma of thyroid?', '["CA 19-9","Thyroglobulin","Calcitonin","CEA","AFP"]'::jsonb, 'Calcitonin', 'Calcitonin is the marker for medullary thyroid carcinoma (MTC), from parafollicular C cells.'),
('d35f82c8-b2f5-51db-a4f0-d0b58b0bc561'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Which tumor marker is used for monitoring testicular seminoma?', '["AFP","CA-125","CEA","Beta-hCG","LDH"]'::jsonb, 'LDH', 'Seminoma may have elevated hCG but never AFP (AFP indicates non-seminomatous). LDH helps stratify risk.'),
('6277a435-1aa2-527c-82d0-7b815a807c28'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Which tumor marker is elevated in cholangiocarcinoma?', '["PSA","CA 19-9","CEA","CA-125","AFP"]'::jsonb, 'CA 19-9', 'CA 19-9 is elevated in cholangiocarcinoma and pancreatic cancer, but non-specific.'),
('05899476-4995-594a-9309-0e8403793f47'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Which tumor marker is elevated in choriocarcinoma?', '["Beta-hCG","AFP","CEA","CA-125","LDH"]'::jsonb, 'Beta-hCG', 'Beta-hCG is the sensitive marker for choriocarcinoma and gestational trophoblastic disease.'),
('b8a741ea-0131-5d9f-9b0b-9ee20c1995ea'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Which tumor marker is elevated in yolk sac (endodermal sinus) tumor?', '["AFP","Beta-hCG","CA-125","CEA","PLAP"]'::jsonb, 'AFP', 'AFP is elevated in yolk sac tumors and hepatocellular carcinoma.'),
('850243aa-390b-55a8-ba64-fa1b059e80dd'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Which test is used to diagnose hereditary hemochromatosis?', '["Serum iron only clinical presentation","Transferrin saturation >45% and ferritin, confirmed by HFE gene testing","Ferritin only clinical presentation","Complete blood count clinical presentation","TIBC only clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Transferrin saturation >45% and ferritin, confirmed by HFE gene testing', 'Screening: transferrin saturation >45% with elevated ferritin. Confirm with HFE gene mutation (C282Y).'),
('1c2c44d6-14f9-5040-b813-a8ed1cce8c26'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Which test is used to diagnose Wilson disease?', '["Serum copper clinical presentation clinical presentation","Ceruloplasmin (low), 24h urine copper (high), Kayser-Fleischer rings","GGT clinical presentation clinical presentation","Bilirubin clinical presentation clinical presentation","ALT/AST clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Ceruloplasmin (low), 24h urine copper (high), Kayser-Fleischer rings', 'Low ceruloplasmin (<20 mg/dL) and high 24h urine copper (>100 mcg) suggest Wilson disease.'),
('8d87a531-a1fa-5ba3-a538-1f773219bd59'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Which test for alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency?', '["LFTs","Alpha-1 globulin on SPEP","Serum AAT level and genotyping","ABGs","Chest X-ray"]'::jsonb, 'Serum AAT level and genotyping', 'Low AAT level (<50 mg/dL) with PiZZ genotype confirms diagnosis.'),
('14e51267-2364-596e-bae5-e95ba45134de'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Which test is used to screen for multiple myeloma?', '["Bone marrow biopsy clinical presentation","Serum protein electrophoresis (SPEP) with immunofixation","CBC clinical presentation","Calcium clinical presentation","X-ray skeleton clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Serum protein electrophoresis (SPEP) with immunofixation', 'SPEP shows M-spike. Immunofixation confirms monoclonal immunoglobulin type. Serum free light chains are also used.'),
('b161da09-785f-5d3b-a19e-a7054148f918'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'What is the normal serum protein electrophoresis pattern?', '["Albumin > alpha-1 > alpha-2 > beta > gamma","Gamma highest clinical presentation","Alpha-1 > albumin clinical presentation","Gamma > beta > alpha clinical presentation","Equal proportions clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Albumin > alpha-1 > alpha-2 > beta > gamma', 'Normal SPEP: albumin (~60%), alpha-1 (~3-5%), alpha-2 (~6-10%), beta (~8-12%), gamma (~15-20%).'),
('3954f199-90e4-5e22-a39f-60244980c3b6'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Which test confirms oligoclonal bands in CSF for multiple sclerosis?', '["CSF IgG index and oligoclonal banding by electrophoresis","CSF cell count clinical presentation","Serum SPEP clinical presentation","CSF glucose clinical presentation","MRI only clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'CSF IgG index and oligoclonal banding by electrophoresis', 'Oligoclonal bands in CSF (not serum) are present in >90% of MS patients.'),
('555c971e-604d-5c2f-8ca7-5dbc13098893'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Which enzyme is deficient in acute intermittent porphyria (AIP)?', '["PBG deaminase","ALA dehydratase","Uroporphyrinogen synthase","Ferrochelatase","ALA synthase"]'::jsonb, 'PBG deaminase', 'AIP is autosomal dominant with PBG deaminase deficiency, causing acute neurovisceral attacks.'),
('9ebe06d6-f65c-572e-a6b3-e6c65a87984e'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Which test is diagnostic for porphyria cutanea tarda (PCT)?', '["PBG in urine","Erythrocyte porphyrin","Fecal protoporphyrin","Urine uroporphyrin elevated","ALA in urine"]'::jsonb, 'Urine uroporphyrin elevated', 'PCT (UROD deficiency) shows elevated uroporphyrin in urine with normal PBG.'),
('c71a3dfc-7692-5964-afda-2714eb134b60'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Which test is used for lead poisoning screening?', '["Hair lead","Blood lead level","RBC lead","Urine lead","Bone lead"]'::jsonb, 'Blood lead level', 'Blood lead level is the best test for current lead exposure. >5 mcg/dL requires investigation.'),
('19c3f009-5ed5-5ada-a019-7783d2c19a93'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'What is the normal CD4 count in healthy adults?', '["500-1500 cells/uL","200-500 cells/uL","<200 cells/uL",">1500 cells/uL","1000-2000 cells/uL"]'::jsonb, '500-1500 cells/uL', 'Normal CD4 is 500-1500 cells/uL. <200 indicates AIDS diagnosis in HIV patients.'),
('a906237e-1c00-58d3-a9cb-fe25d64f97c4'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'HIV viral load is measured by:', '["RT-PCR","ELISA","Western blot","p24 antigen","CD4 count"]'::jsonb, 'RT-PCR', 'HIV RNA by RT-PCR quantifies viral load for monitoring treatment response.'),
('39bb7e39-4cab-5540-9ffd-f1f365dde0ca'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Which test is used to diagnose Helicobacter pylori infection non-invasively?', '["Serology","Urea breath test","Stool antigen","None","All of the above"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Urea breath test, stool antigen, and endoscopy with biopsy are options. Breath test has high sensitivity/specificity.'),
('e05fb9f0-1b3e-5f2f-9177-594094c0a930'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Which test differentiates bacterial from viral meningitis?', '["CSF glucose only clinical presentation","CSF protein only clinical presentation","Blood culture clinical presentation","CSF cell count only clinical presentation","CSF Gram stain, culture, PCR, and cell differential"]'::jsonb, 'CSF Gram stain, culture, PCR, and cell differential', 'Bacterial: PMN predominance, low glucose, high protein, positive Gram stain. Viral: lymphs, normal glucose.'),
('cbf7d47c-514b-55c0-8de5-4b1067730503'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Normal CSF glucose is:', '[">100 mg/dL clinical presentation","Same as blood always clinical presentation","200 mg/dL clinical presentation","50-80 mg/dL or 60-70% of blood glucose","<40 mg/dL clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, '50-80 mg/dL or 60-70% of blood glucose', 'CSF glucose is normally about 60-70% of serum glucose. Low in bacterial/TB/fungal meningitis.'),
('16d88015-3538-51ff-a1bd-f64dc3273aae'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Which type of jaundice has elevated urine bilirubin (conjugated)?', '["Hemolytic jaundice clinical presentation","Crigler-Najjar clinical presentation","All types clinical presentation","Obstructive and hepatocellular jaundice","Gilbert syndrome clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Obstructive and hepatocellular jaundice', 'Conjugated (water-soluble) bilirubin appears in urine. Unconjugated bilirubin is not filtered by kidneys.'),
('c627e4e7-3a9d-5220-8e71-4d217ddc01da'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Which type of jaundice has elevated urine urobilinogen?', '["Hemolytic jaundice","Obstructive jaundice","Gilbert syndrome","Crigler-Najjar","Dubin-Johnson"]'::jsonb, 'Hemolytic jaundice', 'Urobilinogen is increased in hemolysis (more bilirubin reaching gut). Decreased in obstruction (less bilirubin reaches gut).'),
('0e48b09f-e412-5dd5-ad83-3a5cee06ff32'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Which urine dipstick finding suggests hemolysis?', '["Positive ketones clinical presentation","Positive glucose clinical presentation","Positive urobilinogen, negative bilirubin","Positive bilirubin clinical presentation","Positive nitrite clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Positive urobilinogen, negative bilirubin', 'Hemolysis: increased urobilinogen (from increased bilirubin), no bilirubin (unconjugated not water-soluble).'),
('ff972c3d-3f35-51cd-80d7-8f105f2e0220'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Which vitamin deficiency causes increased MCV and macrocytic anemia?', '["Vitamin B6 deficiency","Vitamin B12 and folate deficiency","Vitamin D deficiency","Vitamin C deficiency","Iron deficiency"]'::jsonb, 'Vitamin B12 and folate deficiency', 'Macrocytic anemia (MCV >100) occurs in B12, folate deficiency, and also alcohol/liver disease.'),
('446546b4-882c-5e77-b9f6-e836c1369e02'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Increase in RDW (red cell distribution width) indicates:', '["Polycythemia","Macrocytosis","Anisocytosis","Microcytosis","Normal variation"]'::jsonb, 'Anisocytosis', 'High RDW indicates increased anisocytosis, seen in iron deficiency anemia (early).'),
('39085f45-b7ab-5cc2-a44a-7c6f6a83225e'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Which test differentiates iron deficiency anemia from anemia of chronic disease?', '["Serum ferritin, iron, TIBC, transferrin saturation, and soluble transferrin receptor","Platelet count clinical presentation clinical presentation","Hb level clinical presentation clinical presentation","MCV clinical presentation clinical presentation","RDW clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Serum ferritin, iron, TIBC, transferrin saturation, and soluble transferrin receptor', 'IDA: low ferritin, low iron, high TIBC, low sat. ACD: ferritin normal/high, low iron, low TIBC.'),
('0f9329ee-05bb-55e7-840e-ca2a0c763ad2'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Which test is used to diagnose Cushing syndrome?', '["MRI pituitary clinical presentation clinical presentation","ACTH level only clinical presentation clinical presentation","Cortisol after CRH clinical presentation clinical presentation","Random serum cortisol clinical presentation clinical presentation","Overnight dexamethasone suppression test, 24h urine cortisol, midnight salivary cortisol"]'::jsonb, 'Overnight dexamethasone suppression test, 24h urine cortisol, midnight salivary cortisol', 'Screening tests: low-dose DST, 24h UFC, late-night salivary cortisol. Confirm with CRH stimulation or high-dose DST.'),
('7a7df001-b035-53fd-998a-5a71e45401fe'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Which test differentiates pituitary from ectopic ACTH?', '["MRI clinical presentation","ACTH level alone clinical presentation","Cortisol level alone clinical presentation","Petrosal sinus sampling clinical presentation","High-dose dexamethasone suppression test and CRH stimulation test"]'::jsonb, 'High-dose dexamethasone suppression test and CRH stimulation test', 'Pituitary ACTH is suppressed by high-dose dexamethasone and stimulated by CRH. Ectopic ACTH is typically not suppressed.'),
('c270979a-6c19-55db-9b6d-e26d25438af1'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Which test is used to diagnose diabetes insipidus?', '["Urine glucose","Random serum osmolality","HbA1c","Plasma ADH","Water deprivation test"]'::jsonb, 'Water deprivation test', 'DI: inability to concentrate urine (osmolality <300) after water deprivation. Responds to desmopressin in central but not nephrogenic DI.'),
('bb01586f-c9ff-56a4-94cb-5bd4e9629eaa'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Which test is used for pheochromocytoma diagnosis?', '["CT abdomen clinical presentation","MIBG scan clinical presentation","Urine VMA clinical presentation","Serum catecholamines clinical presentation","Plasma metanephrines or 24h urine metanephrines"]'::jsonb, 'Plasma metanephrines or 24h urine metanephrines', 'Plasma free metanephrines have highest sensitivity (~99%) for pheochromocytoma diagnosis.'),
('2be03fca-4eec-5b9b-b704-c74ead82e95a'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Which tumor marker is elevated in pheochromocytoma?', '["None","AFP","CA 19-9","CEA","CA-125"]'::jsonb, 'None', 'Pheochromocytoma is diagnosed by elevated metanephrines (not serum tumor markers).'),
('4256880e-7b5a-5d5b-9157-a3bb33f33c44'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Which test is used to diagnose carcinoid syndrome?', '["24h urine 5-HIAA","Serum serotonin","Urine catecholamines","Plasma chromogranin A","CT scan"]'::jsonb, '24h urine 5-HIAA', 'Elevated urine 5-HIAA is diagnostic for carcinoid syndrome from serotonin overproduction.'),
('bce4bb7b-135a-5344-9444-76a6a5bbd7bc'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Which test is used to monitor heparin-induced thrombocytopenia (HIT)?', '["ELISA for anti-PF4/heparin antibodies, serotonin release assay","Platelet count alone clinical presentation","aPTT clinical presentation","PT/INR clinical presentation","Bleeding time clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'ELISA for anti-PF4/heparin antibodies, serotonin release assay', 'HIT ELISA detects PF4-heparin antibodies. Functional serotonin release assay confirms diagnosis.'),
('b990748b-86c8-5d11-b60b-ec47a0851c12'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Which coagulation factor is measured by PT (prothrombin time)?', '["Extrinsic and common pathway: factors VII, X, II, V, fibrinogen","Intrinsic pathway clinical presentation","Factor VIII clinical presentation","Factor IX clinical presentation","Factor XIII clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Extrinsic and common pathway: factors VII, X, II, V, fibrinogen', 'PT evaluates factors VII, X, V, II, and fibrinogen (extrinsic + common pathway).'),
('64188843-daec-5496-80c4-1a8b4e27dea1'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Which coagulation factor is measured by aPTT?', '["Intrinsic and common pathway: factors XII, XI, IX, VIII, X, V, II, fibrinogen","Extrinsic pathway clinical presentation clinical presentation","Factor VII clinical presentation clinical presentation","Factor XIII clinical presentation clinical presentation","All factors clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Intrinsic and common pathway: factors XII, XI, IX, VIII, X, V, II, fibrinogen', 'aPTT evaluates intrinsic pathway (factors XII, XI, IX, VIII) and common pathway.'),
('de11fb69-2d72-5770-85fa-32497cc28b3f'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Which factor is missing in both PT and aPTT prolongation with TT normal?', '["Fibrinogen","Factor VIII","Factor IX","Factor X, V, II","Factor VII"]'::jsonb, 'Factor X, V, II', 'Prolonged PT and aPTT with normal TT suggests deficiency of common pathway factors (X, V, II) or combined deficiencies.'),
('8dbc699b-6e7f-55bc-8fb1-02e275890a43'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Which factor deficiency causes prolonged TT (thrombin time)?', '["Factor VIII clinical presentation","Factor VII clinical presentation","Factor XIII clinical presentation","Fibrinogen deficiency, dysfibrinogenemia, or heparin","Factor IX clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Fibrinogen deficiency, dysfibrinogenemia, or heparin', 'TT tests the conversion of fibrinogen to fibrin. Prolonged in hypo/dysfibrinogenemia, heparin, and DIC.'),
('55596d89-fe58-5b62-bc75-f424783e7fb0'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Which factor deficiency is NOT detected by PT or aPTT?', '["Factor VIII","Factor VII","Factor XII","Factor IX","Factor XIII deficiency"]'::jsonb, 'Factor XIII deficiency', 'FXIII deficiency (clot stability defect) requires a urea solubility test; PT and aPTT are normal.'),
('0ebe39f4-77e2-535d-9236-e05231afc4da'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Which test is used for von Willebrand disease diagnosis?', '["PT clinical presentation clinical presentation","Platelet count clinical presentation clinical presentation","vWF antigen, vWF activity (ristocetin cofactor), factor VIII, multimer analysis","aPTT clinical presentation clinical presentation","Bleeding time clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'vWF antigen, vWF activity (ristocetin cofactor), factor VIII, multimer analysis', 'vWD diagnosis requires vWF Ag, vWF:RCo activity, FVIII levels, and multimer analysis.'),
('903d7a84-4f5d-50bd-9f78-4f3087919132'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Which test is used for lupus anticoagulant detection?', '["dRVVT (dilute Russell viper venom time) with mixing/confirmation","aPTT clinical presentation","PT clinical presentation","TT clinical presentation","Platelet count clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'dRVVT (dilute Russell viper venom time) with mixing/confirmation', 'dRVVT is the most specific for lupus anticoagulant. Mixing studies and confirmatory phospholipid tests are needed.'),
('2cd35a84-9d0c-536b-abf1-3758e6133b53'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Which test differentiates heparin effect from lupus anticoagulant?', '["PT","dRVVT","aPTT","TT","Factor assays"]'::jsonb, 'TT', 'Heparin prolongs TT (due to antithrombin activation); lupus anticoagulant does not.'),
('e4cb78d7-2476-58c5-a4d6-7b37b14ef202'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'What does ''reptilase time'' measure?', '["Fibrinogen to fibrin conversion","Platelet function","Factor VIII","Factor XIII","von Willebrand factor"]'::jsonb, 'Fibrinogen to fibrin conversion', 'Reptilase time tests fibrinogen conversion like TT but is NOT affected by heparin.'),
('2436addd-4bea-5bd2-9bbb-29a72fb2a292'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Which test is used to monitor direct oral anticoagulants (DOAKs)?', '["aPTT clinical presentation","TT clinical presentation","Do not require routine monitoring","PT clinical presentation","Platelet count clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Do not require routine monitoring', 'DOACs generally don''t need monitoring. For emergencies, dilute TT (dabigatran) or anti-Xa (rivaroxaban, apixaban).'),
('70fbed4c-b475-53b1-8f77-e5830649bd8c'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Which test is used for diagnosis of antiphospholipid syndrome?', '["Lupus anticoagulant (dRVVT) + anticardiolipin IgG/M + anti-beta2 glycoprotein I","aPTT alone clinical presentation clinical presentation","PT alone clinical presentation clinical presentation","Factor V Leiden clinical presentation clinical presentation","Homocysteine clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Lupus anticoagulant (dRVVT) + anticardiolipin IgG/M + anti-beta2 glycoprotein I', 'APS diagnosis requires at least one lab criterion: lupus anticoagulant, aCL, or anti-beta2GPI.'),
('f8f42135-9833-53e1-811f-e0405f28949a'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'What is the confirmatory test for syphilis?', '["TPHA (Treponema pallidum hemagglutination assay) or FTA-ABS","VDRL clinical presentation","RPR clinical presentation","USR clinical presentation","Dark-field microscopy clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'TPHA (Treponema pallidum hemagglutination assay) or FTA-ABS', 'VDRL/RPR (non-treponemal) are screening tests. TPHA/FTA-ABS confirm past or current infection.'),
('453229ed-98e5-54d2-9bef-9da11ac06930'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Which test has highest sensitivity for TB detection?', '["GeneXpert (MTB/RIF) PCR","AFB smear","Culture on Lowenstein-Jensen","Mantoux test","IGRA"]'::jsonb, 'GeneXpert (MTB/RIF) PCR', 'GeneXpert detects TB DNA and rifampin resistance in 2 hours with high sensitivity.'),
('8c7f5929-9126-59cc-8f94-016c7ec8a614'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Which test differentiates latent from active TB?', '["Mantoux test clinical presentation clinical presentation","TB PCR clinical presentation clinical presentation","IGRA clinical presentation clinical presentation","Serology clinical presentation clinical presentation","Clinical symptoms, chest X-ray, and microbiologic confirmation"]'::jsonb, 'Clinical symptoms, chest X-ray, and microbiologic confirmation', 'Mantoux/IGRA identify infection but can''t differentiate latent from active. Active requires microbiologic confirmation.'),
('31dc6b9f-4b24-5d39-9c1f-a41662d2ed16'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Which hepatitis serology indicates acute HAV infection?', '["Anti-HAV IgM","Anti-HBs","HBsAg","Anti-HAV IgG","HAV RNA"]'::jsonb, 'Anti-HAV IgM', 'Anti-HAV IgM indicates recent/acute HAV. IgG indicates past infection or vaccination.'),
('50840316-dc79-5c01-bb1f-7c7ee1fbc422'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Which hepatitis serology indicates immunity to HBV from vaccination?', '["Anti-HBc IgG clinical presentation","Anti-HBs positive, HBsAg negative, anti-HBc negative","HBeAg positive clinical presentation","HBsAg positive clinical presentation","Anti-HBe positive clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Anti-HBs positive, HBsAg negative, anti-HBc negative', 'Vaccine response: only anti-HBs positive. Natural infection: anti-HBc also positive.'),
('7687be49-c9c4-537a-a8e5-6d024004868d'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Which test confirms HCV infection (active)?', '["HCV RNA by PCR","Anti-HCV antibody","HCV genotype","ALT alone","HCV core antigen"]'::jsonb, 'HCV RNA by PCR', 'Anti-HCV indicates exposure. HCV RNA confirms active/viremic infection and guides treatment.'),
('e6f1242f-18d2-5b45-b017-a983f7c590d3'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Which test differentiates HBV acute from chronic infection?', '["HBsAg alone clinical presentation","Duration of HBsAg (chronic >6 months) and anti-HBc IgM vs IgG","ALT level clinical presentation","HBeAg alone clinical presentation","HBV DNA level clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Duration of HBsAg (chronic >6 months) and anti-HBc IgM vs IgG', 'Acute: HBsAg+, anti-HBc IgM+. Chronic: HBsAg >6 months, anti-HBc IgG+.'),
('c1394ace-7a7b-5c43-a059-da9e8e6a2f27'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Which test is the earliest marker for HBV infection?', '["HBsAg","Anti-HBc IgM","Anti-HBs","HBeAg","HBV DNA"]'::jsonb, 'HBsAg', 'HBsAg is first detectable marker at 2-8 weeks post-infection, before symptoms.'),
('07c2fff1-ae13-5324-93f8-b4ae1f02f975'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Which hepatitis serology is the ''window period'' marker?', '["HBsAg","Anti-HBe","Anti-HBc IgM only","HBV DNA","Anti-HBs"]'::jsonb, 'Anti-HBc IgM only', 'Window period: HBsAg has disappeared, anti-HBs not yet appeared. Only anti-HBc IgM is positive.'),
('fde4a3ff-e964-5ba0-bfcf-9dab15b69c81'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Which test is used to monitor HBV treatment response?', '["HBV DNA quantification","ALT only","Anti-HBe","HBsAg","HBeAg"]'::jsonb, 'HBV DNA quantification', 'HBV DNA levels monitor virologic response to antiviral therapy and detect resistance.'),
('2be2e0c7-37a4-5eda-bd35-61d853dd37cb'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Which test is used for HCV genotype determination?', '["ALT level clinical presentation","Anti-HCV titer clinical presentation","FibroScan clinical presentation","HCV genotyping by sequencing or line probe assay","HCV RNA level clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'HCV genotyping by sequencing or line probe assay', 'HCV genotype (1-6) guides treatment duration and predicts response to therapy.'),
('8e5049a0-ab3b-5af9-94cc-5e1607be53cc'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Which test confirms a diagnosis of acute cholecystitis?', '["HIDA scan","CT abdomen","Ultrasound","LFTs","Serum amylase"]'::jsonb, 'Ultrasound', 'Ultrasound is first-line: wall >4 mm, gallstones, sonographic Murphy sign.'),
('484fa2c8-4be4-5230-b398-c8776c8efb60'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Which imaging is best for choledocholithiasis detection?', '["HIDA scan","ERCP","CT scan","Ultrasound","MRCP"]'::jsonb, 'MRCP', 'MRCP is non-invasive with high sensitivity/specificity for bile duct stones.'),
('b949ce96-512f-5c3a-b876-a90f1c9db3b5'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Which test is used for pulmonary embolism diagnosis?', '["V/Q scan","Doppler US","D-dimer","Chest X-ray","CT pulmonary angiography"]'::jsonb, 'CT pulmonary angiography', 'CTPA is the first-line imaging for PE. D-dimer is a screening test to exclude PE (high sensitivity, low specificity).'),
('a0b98b02-a278-5a31-8f48-f44ad481edb9'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Which test is used for DVT diagnosis?', '["Compression duplex ultrasound","D-dimer","CT venography","MRI","Venogram"]'::jsonb, 'Compression duplex ultrasound', 'Ultrasound with compression is first-line for DVT diagnosis (non-invasive, sensitive).'),
('bc452ba8-fb53-5dfd-82fd-9f9bfb168eac'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Which test is used to screen for colorectal cancer?', '["Colonoscopy or FIT","CEA level","CT colonography","Stool DNA","Barium enema"]'::jsonb, 'Colonoscopy or FIT', 'Colonoscopy is gold standard for CRC screening and prevention (polypectomy). FIT detects blood in stool.'),
('eb3e6d11-7dc1-597b-ab82-8521c25d1e66'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Which test is used for cervical cancer screening?', '["Biopsy clinical presentation","Pap smear (cervical cytology) and HPV testing","Colposcopy clinical presentation","MRI clinical presentation","CT scan clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Pap smear (cervical cytology) and HPV testing', 'Cervical screening: Pap smear (cytology) and high-risk HPV DNA testing.'),
('6cd2e2a5-5152-51e5-ac1b-dabc97a36473'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Which test is used for prenatal Down syndrome screening?', '["Amniocentesis clinical presentation","Chorionic villus sampling clinical presentation","Ultrasound only clinical presentation","Quadruple screen (AFP, hCG, estriol, inhibin A) or NIPT","Maternal age only clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Quadruple screen (AFP, hCG, estriol, inhibin A) or NIPT', 'Quad screen and NIPT are screening tests. CVS and amniocentesis are diagnostic.'),
('53bd011a-d064-5b5c-a6f1-808e753337cc'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Which test is used for gestational diabetes screening?', '["Fasting glucose only","75g OGTT at 24-28 weeks","Random glucose","Urine glucose","HbA1c"]'::jsonb, '75g OGTT at 24-28 weeks', '75g OGTT at 24-28 wks: fasting >92, 1h >180, 2h >153 mg/dL for GDM diagnosis.'),
('7c4f4f99-6f54-5639-b2c0-878e8a4989f8'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Which prenatal imaging is used for fetal anomaly screening?', '["CT","Ultrasound at 18-22 weeks","MRI","X-ray","Amniocentesis"]'::jsonb, 'Ultrasound at 18-22 weeks', 'Mid-trimester anomaly scan (18-22 wks) detects major structural fetal anomalies.'),
('b894be8c-866b-5603-9241-01c2e97202e5'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Which test is used for newborn hearing screening?', '["Auditory brainstem response","Behavioral audiometry","Tympanometry","Otoacoustic emissions (OAE) and automated ABR","Clinical assessment"]'::jsonb, 'Otoacoustic emissions (OAE) and automated ABR', 'Universal newborn hearing screening uses OAE or automated ABR within first month.'),
('20655594-663b-5e86-8ff6-8d0c45e4c0dc'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Which test is part of newborn blood spot screening?', '["CBC only clinical presentation","Calcium only clinical presentation","Bilirubin only clinical presentation","PKU, congenital hypothyroidism, CF, G6PD, and others","Glucose only clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'PKU, congenital hypothyroidism, CF, G6PD, and others', 'Newborn screening includes PKU, hypothyroidism, CF, G6PD, and metabolic disorders.'),
('02b20099-055e-5227-9712-f9668760d903'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Which test is used to diagnose tuberculosis pleuritis?', '["Pleural fluid cell count clinical presentation","Pleural fluid ADA (adenosine deaminase) >40 U/L and culture/PCR","Mantoux clinical presentation","IGRA clinical presentation","Chest X-ray clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Pleural fluid ADA (adenosine deaminase) >40 U/L and culture/PCR', 'Pleural fluid ADA >40 U/L is highly sensitive for TB pleuritis. Confirm with culture/PCR.'),
('4ba6142b-7b8a-5193-8038-33a6595bacb3'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Which test is diagnostic for Whipple disease?', '["Stool culture clinical presentation clinical presentation","Blood culture clinical presentation clinical presentation","Serology clinical presentation clinical presentation","Endoscopy only clinical presentation clinical presentation","Small bowel biopsy showing PAS-positive macrophages with Tropheryma whipplei PCR"]'::jsonb, 'Small bowel biopsy showing PAS-positive macrophages with Tropheryma whipplei PCR', 'Duodenal biopsy with PAS-positive macrophages containing T. whipplei is diagnostic.'),
('fde78ce0-3fb0-5e8f-8df3-cc681d4af105'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Which test is used to diagnose celiac disease?', '["CBC clinical presentation","D-xylose absorption clinical presentation","Stool fat clinical presentation","Serum anti-tTG IgA (tissue transglutaminase) and duodenal biopsy","Anti-gliadin antibodies clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Serum anti-tTG IgA (tissue transglutaminase) and duodenal biopsy', 'First: anti-tTG IgA and total IgA (exclude IgA deficiency). Confirm with duodenal biopsy.'),
('cb43989d-7734-5f31-987c-8bbb71fa4714'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Which test is used to diagnose primary biliary cholangitis?', '["ANA clinical presentation","p-ANCA clinical presentation","Anti-mitochondrial antibodies (AMA-M2), elevated ALP, GGT","Anti-LKM1 clinical presentation","Anti-smooth muscle antibodies clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Anti-mitochondrial antibodies (AMA-M2), elevated ALP, GGT', 'AMA-M2 is highly specific (>95%) for PBC. ALP and GGT are elevated cholestatic enzymes.'),
('77550038-d538-54a8-946a-08a6627e0360'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Which test is used to diagnose autoimmune hepatitis?', '["ANA alone clinical presentation clinical presentation","AMA clinical presentation clinical presentation","p-ANCA clinical presentation clinical presentation","ALP alone clinical presentation clinical presentation","Elevated AST/ALT, IgG, anti-smooth muscle or anti-LKM1, and liver biopsy"]'::jsonb, 'Elevated AST/ALT, IgG, anti-smooth muscle or anti-LKM1, and liver biopsy', 'AIH: elevated transaminases, IgG, autoantibodies (ASMA in type 1, LKM1 in type 2), and interface hepatitis.'),
('930ddd2a-b99a-5602-a2ed-9979c7bf33a0'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Which test is used to diagnose hereditary angioedema?', '["Low C4, low C1 esterase inhibitor","Normal C3, C4","Low CH50","Anti-C1q antibodies","Elevated IgE"]'::jsonb, 'Low C4, low C1 esterase inhibitor', 'HAE: low C4 and low C1-INH levels (type I) or dysfunctional C1-INH (type II).'),
('951dce01-154c-5976-acb1-f5ea35e25aac'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Which test is used for diagnosis of myasthenia gravis?', '["Tensilon test","All of the above","Anti-MuSK antibodies","Single-fiber EMG","Anti-AChR antibodies and repetitive nerve stimulation"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'MG: anti-AChR + (85%), anti-MuSK + (some), RNS shows decrement, SFEMG most sensitive.'),
('877b9872-fbac-5fdc-8b90-e7330728c935'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Which test is used to diagnose Lambert-Eaton myasthenic syndrome (LEMS)?', '["Repetitive nerve stimulation clinical presentation","Anti-MuSK clinical presentation","Anti-AChR antibodies clinical presentation","Anti-voltage-gated calcium channel (VGCC) antibodies","Edrophonium test clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Anti-voltage-gated calcium channel (VGCC) antibodies', 'LEMS: anti-VGCC antibodies (P/Q type) cause presynaptic neuromuscular junction defect.'),
('9a9f2d4c-3a79-5dde-9bc1-c23f9fbaf616'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Which test is used to diagnose Guillain-Barre syndrome?', '["All of the above","CSF protein (elevated) with normal cell count","EMG/NCS","None","Anti-ganglioside antibodies"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'GBS: CSF albuminocytologic dissociation, NCS shows demyelinating pattern, anti-GM1 in some.'),
('97d6009f-b83a-5d9f-a686-df69f2855cd9'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Which test is used for diagnosis of multiple sclerosis?', '["MRI brain/spine (T2/FLAIR lesions), CSF oligoclonal bands, evoked potentials","CT brain clinical presentation clinical presentation","X-ray clinical presentation clinical presentation","CSF cell count clinical presentation clinical presentation","Serum autoantibodies clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'MRI brain/spine (T2/FLAIR lesions), CSF oligoclonal bands, evoked potentials', 'MS diagnosis: dissemination in space/time by MRI, CSF OCBs, and sometimes VEP.'),
('eecaa305-6fc4-5fa5-9ff0-c615d439a6a7'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Which test is used to diagnose Parkinson disease?', '["MRI","EMG","DaTscan","Clinical diagnosis","CSF alpha-synuclein"]'::jsonb, 'Clinical diagnosis', 'PD is primarily clinical. DaTscan can help differentiate from essential tremor.'),
('89f6a36d-4a04-588d-ad16-57a16f36f51d'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Which test is used to diagnose Alzheimer disease?', '["Clinical criteria, MRI (atrophy), PET (amyloid/tau), CSF Abeta/tau","B12 level clinical presentation clinical presentation","TSH clinical presentation clinical presentation","CT scan clinical presentation clinical presentation","EEG clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Clinical criteria, MRI (atrophy), PET (amyloid/tau), CSF Abeta/tau', 'AD: CSF shows low Abeta42 and high tau. Amyloid PET is positive.'),
('990d5d0a-f138-50c7-bf54-1d52e9a2f9df'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Which hormone is elevated in primary hyperaldosteronism (Conn syndrome)?', '["ADH clinical presentation","Cortisol clinical presentation","Aldosterone with suppressed plasma renin activity","ACTH clinical presentation","Renin clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Aldosterone with suppressed plasma renin activity', 'Conn: high aldosterone, low renin, hypokalemia, hypertension. Aldosterone:renin ratio is the screening test.'),
('8a5c6ae1-3bcd-5106-a932-706ef9259d08'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Which test confirms primary hyperaldosteronism?', '["Aldosterone:renin ratio >20-30 with saline suppression test","Serum potassium clinical presentation","Aldosterone alone clinical presentation","Renin alone clinical presentation","CT adrenal clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Aldosterone:renin ratio >20-30 with saline suppression test', 'Screen with ARR. Confirm by demonstrating inappropriate aldosterone with saline or captopril challenge.'),
('68027854-e5c4-5805-84c7-9f6a4e3ed7bd'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Which test is used for acromegaly diagnosis?', '["MRI pituitary clinical presentation","Prolactin clinical presentation","IGF-1 (insulin-like growth factor 1) and GH nadir after OGTT","TSH clinical presentation","Random GH clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'IGF-1 (insulin-like growth factor 1) and GH nadir after OGTT', 'IGF-1 is a good screening test. Failure to suppress GH <1 ng/mL after 75g OGTT confirms acromegaly.'),
('00ef801c-ae0e-5ff1-8da5-b00b8b3ce1f2'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Which test is used for prolactinoma diagnosis?', '["Serum prolactin (elevated) and pituitary MRI","IGF-1 clinical presentation","Random prolactin clinical presentation","TSH clinical presentation","ACTH clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Serum prolactin (elevated) and pituitary MRI', 'Serum prolactin >200 ng/mL suggests prolactinoma. MRI confirms pituitary adenoma.'),
('9dd92a6a-2e64-532d-9716-e8a42bc19ead'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Which test is used for diagnosis of growth hormone deficiency?', '["All of the above","IGFBP-3","GH stimulation test","Random GH","IGF-1"]'::jsonb, 'GH stimulation test', 'GHD requires stimulation test (peak GH <5 ng/mL). IGF-1 is a screening test.'),
('89a5f183-a34f-5912-903d-979120ef9c39'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Which test is used for adrenal insufficiency diagnosis?', '["ACTH level clinical presentation clinical presentation","ACTH stimulation (cosyntropin) test, cortisol <18 mcg/dL at 30/60 min","Random cortisol clinical presentation clinical presentation","Renin clinical presentation clinical presentation","Aldosterone clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'ACTH stimulation (cosyntropin) test, cortisol <18 mcg/dL at 30/60 min', 'ACTH stim: cortisol <18 mcg/dL after 250 mcg cosyntropin suggests adrenal insufficiency.'),
('9d495255-c860-5739-ac10-ed8875bdf46f'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Which test differentiates primary from secondary adrenal insufficiency?', '["ACTH level","Aldosterone","DHEAS","Renin","Cortisol after ACTH"]'::jsonb, 'ACTH level', 'Primary: low cortisol, high ACTH, elevated renin. Secondary: low cortisol, low/normal ACTH.'),
('70d3dccb-3e33-5b5a-975d-ddf2c7d6b521'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Which test is used for diagnosis of Cushing syndrome (pituitary)?', '["All","CRH test","High-dose DST","MRI pituitary (adenoma) and IPSS","CT adrenal"]'::jsonb, 'CRH test', 'MRI detects pituitary microadenoma. IPSS is gold standard for confirming pituitary origin.'),
('ea2a06b8-3a3b-5e3b-9dcc-b35dd0f6e173'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Which test is used for MEN1 syndrome screening?', '["Genetic testing for MEN1 mutation, Ca/PTH, pituitary MRI, pancreatic hormones","Insulin","Gastrin","All of the above","Prolactin"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'MEN1: MEN1 gene test. Screen for hyperparathyroidism (Ca, PTH), pituitary, and pancreatic tumors.'),
('7bbb80a6-9b3e-57c2-972d-d3e1e0f2b667'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Which test is used for MEN2 syndrome screening?', '["Gastrin clinical presentation clinical presentation","RET proto-oncogene testing, calcitonin (MTC), plasma metanephrines, Ca","PTH clinical presentation clinical presentation","Prolactin clinical presentation clinical presentation","All of the above clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'RET proto-oncogene testing, calcitonin (MTC), plasma metanephrines, Ca', 'MEN2: RET mutation, elevated calcitonin (medullary thyroid CA), screen for pheochromocytoma.'),
('46a6e236-563c-5e99-9825-0d92a6f75ba0'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Which test is used for diagnosis of polycystic ovary syndrome (PCOS)?', '["Free androgen index clinical presentation clinical presentation","Rotterdam criteria: oligo/anovulation, hyperandrogenism (clinical/biochemical), PCO on US","Random testosterone clinical presentation clinical presentation","SHBG clinical presentation clinical presentation","LH:FSH ratio clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Rotterdam criteria: oligo/anovulation, hyperandrogenism (clinical/biochemical), PCO on US', 'PCOS: two of three: oligo-ovulation, hyperandrogenism, polycystic ovaries on ultrasound.'),
('09c768eb-f7c7-5f86-b0e3-149cd8af9a86'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Which test is used for diagnosis of premature ovarian failure?', '["Low LH clinical presentation","High AMH clinical presentation","Elevated FSH >40 IU/L in women <40 years","Low estradiol clinical presentation","Low FSH clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Elevated FSH >40 IU/L in women <40 years', 'Elevated FSH (>40) with amenorrhea and low estradiol in <40 years indicates POI.'),
('7c6c4942-471f-5fe5-bc5c-887554d87568'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'Which test is used for diagnosis of male hypogonadism?', '["Prolactin clinical presentation clinical presentation","Free testosterone clinical presentation clinical presentation","SHBG clinical presentation clinical presentation","Estradiol clinical presentation clinical presentation","Morning total testosterone (low) with elevated LH/FSH (primary) or low LH"]'::jsonb, 'Morning total testosterone (low) with elevated LH/FSH (primary) or low LH', 'Low morning T with high LH/FSH = primary. Low T with low LH = secondary hypogonadism.'),
('bd56ad51-5918-5171-950b-df9a2f83efaf'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Clinical Pathology', 'The most sensitive marker for myocardial infarction is:', '["AST","CK-MB","Troponin I/T","Myoglobin","LDH"]'::jsonb, 'Troponin I/T', 'Troponin (cTnI/cTnT) is the gold standard biomarker for MI. Highly sensitive and specific. Rises 3-6 hours, peaks 12-24 hours, remains elevated 7-14 days. cTnI > 99th percentile = MI diagnosis.'),
('16e1131c-4a82-5ce0-934a-3a788b7000ae'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Normal hemoglobin level in adult male is:', '["13-17 g/dL","10-13 g/dL","11-15 g/dL","15-19 g/dL","17-21 g/dL"]'::jsonb, '13-17 g/dL', 'Normal Hb: men 13-17 g/dL, women 12-15.5 g/dL. Anemia defined as Hb <13 in men, <12 in women.'),
('321928d6-b948-5fee-891c-e69085c20a90'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Which is the most common type of anemia worldwide?', '["Aplastic anemia","Megaloblastic anemia","Anemia of chronic disease","Iron deficiency anemia","Thalassemia"]'::jsonb, 'Iron deficiency anemia', 'Iron deficiency is the most common anemia globally, affecting ~2 billion people.'),
('499d0be0-21dc-5538-9123-4b059ee8ae65'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Microcytic hypochromic anemia is characteristic of:', '["Sickle cell anemia","Iron deficiency anemia","Anemia of chronic disease","Megaloblastic anemia","Aplastic anemia"]'::jsonb, 'Iron deficiency anemia', 'Low MCV (<80), low MCH (<27) = microcytic hypochromic. DDx: IDA, thalassemia, ACD, sideroblastic.'),
('8d551bc3-aa76-541c-9635-95428e81e51a'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Which RBC parameter is most useful for differentiating iron deficiency from thalassemia?', '["MCH","Hb level","MCHC","MCV","RDW"]'::jsonb, 'RDW', 'RDW is elevated in IDA (mixed population) but normal in thalassemia trait (uniform microcytosis).'),
('f47242ca-1f49-553d-bbb4-5f70e1195b72'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'The earliest laboratory abnormality in iron deficiency anemia is:', '["Low hemoglobin","Low serum iron","Decreased serum ferritin","Low MCV","Low MCH"]'::jsonb, 'Decreased serum ferritin', 'Ferritin is a sensitive marker for iron stores. It decreases before Hb, MCV, or MCH changes.'),
('e4da1c8c-81ac-5640-910e-e78f6fdf44df'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Which test differentiates iron deficiency from anemia of chronic disease?', '["MCV clinical presentation","Soluble transferrin receptor (sTfR) - elevated in IDA","Platelet count clinical presentation","RDW clinical presentation","Hb clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Soluble transferrin receptor (sTfR) - elevated in IDA', 'sTfR is elevated in IDA (increased erythropoiesis) but normal in ACD.'),
('3ba736a5-3c7f-5418-824a-f73ffcaad875'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'What is the earliest change in iron deficiency?', '["Decreased MCH","Increased TIBC","Decreased Hb","Decreased serum ferritin","Decreased MCV"]'::jsonb, 'Decreased serum ferritin', 'Stage 1: ferritin decreases (iron store depletion). Then serum iron decreases, TIBC increases, then MCV/MCH drop, finally Hb.'),
('be265b78-22a7-598a-952e-ec348ec9240d'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Which RBC indices are found in thalassemia major?', '["Normocytic","Microcytic, hypochromic, with target cells","Macrocytic","Macrocytic, hypochromic","Microcytic, normochromic"]'::jsonb, 'Microcytic, hypochromic, with target cells', 'Thalassemia: severe microcytosis (MCV very low), hypochromia, target cells, basophilic stippling.'),
('d2abd942-226c-5237-8ab3-6fc20a62cbe3'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Mentzer index (MCV/RBC) <13 suggests:', '["Iron deficiency anemia","Megaloblastic anemia","Thalassemia trait","Anemia of chronic disease","Sickle cell disease"]'::jsonb, 'Thalassemia trait', 'Mentzer index <13 favors thalassemia trait; >13 favors iron deficiency.'),
('d44ba3b6-142a-5cbb-9f65-71cdc530e6b6'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Which test confirms beta-thalassemia trait?', '["RBC count >5 clinical presentation","HbA decrease clinical presentation","HbF elevation clinical presentation","MCV <80 clinical presentation","HbA2 >3.5% on Hb electrophoresis"]'::jsonb, 'HbA2 >3.5% on Hb electrophoresis', 'Beta-thalassemia trait: elevated HbA2 (3.5-7%) with or without mild HbF elevation.'),
('7fae94dd-1dd5-5071-8191-255fa093ba25'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'On diagnostic evaluation, Which hemoglobin is elevated in beta-thalassemia major?', '["HbA2","HbF","HbA","HbH","HbS"]'::jsonb, 'HbF', 'HbF is markedly elevated in beta-thalassemia major (often >90%), with absent/reduced HbA.'),
('6920fde5-aa3b-51a2-bd11-0a0f65667cb0'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Alpha-thalassemia is due to:', '["Deletion of alpha-globin genes on chromosome 16","Insertion","Duplication","Mutation in beta-globin gene","Point mutation in alpha-globin"]'::jsonb, 'Deletion of alpha-globin genes on chromosome 16', 'Alpha-thalassemia most commonly results from deletion of 1-4 alpha-globin genes on chr 16.'),
('5befc784-0e95-59c4-8206-baf21ed2847d'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Which hemoglobin is present in HbH disease (alpha-thalassemia)?', '["Hb Bart","HbH","HbA","HbA2","HbF"]'::jsonb, 'HbH', 'Three alpha gene deletions cause HbH disease. Excess beta chains form HbH (unstable, precipitates).'),
('54ebd2c0-24e2-543e-b77b-7c38bfde8449'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Hemoglobin Bart''s (gamma4) is associated with:', '["Hydrops fetalis","Sickle cell disease","HbH disease","Beta-thalassemia major","HPFH"]'::jsonb, 'Hydrops fetalis', 'Four alpha gene deletions cause hydrops fetalis with Hb Bart''s (gamma4), incompatible with life.'),
('c2545266-23b8-52cc-bbb8-5e3660a3c2cf'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Sickle cell disease is caused by:', '["Insertion","Val6Glu mutation","Frameshift","Glu6Val mutation in beta-globin gene","Deletion of beta-globin"]'::jsonb, 'Glu6Val mutation in beta-globin gene', 'GAG to GTG at codon 6 of beta-globin causes Glu to Val, forming HbS.'),
('6e898a52-366e-5b9b-8893-ea4c346d7def'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Which test confirms sickle cell disease?', '["Sickle cell prep","Peripheral smear","Hb electrophoresis showing HbS >50%","Hemoglobin solubility test","CBC"]'::jsonb, 'Hb electrophoresis showing HbS >50%', 'Hb electrophoresis/HLPC confirms diagnosis. HbS >50% = sickle cell disease; <50% = trait.'),
('06869ab7-dee6-5e09-8581-79c28728addb'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'What triggers sickling in HbS?', '["Oxygenation, alkalosis clinical presentation","High pH clinical presentation","Deoxygenation, dehydration, acidosis, cold","Low temperature clinical presentation","High glucose clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Deoxygenation, dehydration, acidosis, cold', 'Sickling occurs when deoxygenated HbS polymerizes. Factors: hypoxia, acidosis, dehydration, cold, fever.'),
('2aa6cb82-9cb1-593d-bbfe-94c78ccee6fb'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Which complication of sickle cell disease is the most common cause of death?', '["Renal failure","Acute chest syndrome","Stroke","Infection","Painful crisis"]'::jsonb, 'Acute chest syndrome', 'Acute chest syndrome (pulmonary sickling) is the leading cause of death in sickle cell disease.'),
('3ead9847-5f98-5c87-9674-5e0f80eee6c4'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Which organism most commonly causes osteomyelitis in sickle cell disease?', '["Streptococcus pneumoniae","E. coli","Staphylococcus aureus","Salmonella species","Haemophilus influenzae"]'::jsonb, 'Salmonella species', 'Salmonella osteomyelitis is classic in sickle cell disease due to bone infarcts.'),
('0aedd64b-3f12-540a-9e0a-b0e1b4465ef7'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Sickle cell trait (HbAS) confers protection against:', '["Bacterial infections","Viral infections","Severe falciparum malaria","All infections","Autoimmune diseases"]'::jsonb, 'Severe falciparum malaria', 'HbAS provides significant protection against severe P. falciparum malaria, maintaining its prevalence.'),
('d77b3e50-7540-5244-a23c-7afe287c799e'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Which hemoglobin is elevated in hereditary persistence of fetal hemoglobin (HPFH)?', '["HbA","HbF","HbA2","HbS","HbH"]'::jsonb, 'HbF', 'HPFH: persistent HbF production in adults due to deletions/mutations in beta-globin gene cluster.'),
('da1a86f3-5cfc-5435-a20f-e905da9e0a98'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Parvovirus B19 infection in sickle cell disease causes:', '["Splenic sequestration","Acute chest syndrome","Aplastic crisis","Painful crisis","Hemolytic crisis"]'::jsonb, 'Aplastic crisis', 'Parvovirus B19 infects erythroid precursors causing transient aplastic crisis (sudden Hb drop, reticulocytopenia).'),
('a69e5b38-27ba-51de-babd-e533856d3cba'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Acute splenic sequestration in sickle cell disease presents with:', '["Fever only clinical presentation","Jaundice only clinical presentation","No symptoms clinical presentation","Sudden splenomegaly and rapid Hb drop","Abdominal pain only clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Sudden splenomegaly and rapid Hb drop', 'Sequestration: sudden pooling of blood in spleen causing rapid Hb drop, splenomegaly, and hypovolemia.'),
('b0504782-002a-5a87-a32d-d1de02ad11ff'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Hereditary spherocytosis is characterized by:', '["Sickle cells clinical presentation clinical presentation","Schistocytes clinical presentation clinical presentation","Target cells clinical presentation clinical presentation","Spherocytes on smear, increased osmotic fragility, family history","Elliptocytes clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Spherocytes on smear, increased osmotic fragility, family history', 'HS: spherical RBCs (no central pallor), increased osmotic fragility, family history. Defect in spectrin/ankyrin.'),
('12df1b88-aa6f-5489-b246-d41935fcd441'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Which RBC membrane protein defect is most common in hereditary spherocytosis?', '["Ankyrin (ANK1) deficiency","Spectrin deficiency","Glycophorin deficiency","Protein 4.2 deficiency","Band 3 deficiency"]'::jsonb, 'Ankyrin (ANK1) deficiency', 'Ankyrin deficiency accounts for ~50% of HS cases. Spectrin deficiency is ~20%.'),
('0c94dc78-ae4f-5812-bc22-6705c2261b90'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Hereditary elliptocytosis is associated with:', '["Band 3 deficiency","Protein 4.1 deficiency","Ankyrin deficiency","Glycophorin C deficiency","Deficiency of spectrin"]'::jsonb, 'Deficiency of spectrin', 'HE results from defective spectrin tetramer formation, producing elliptical RBCs.'),
('6debe593-fd9f-5e26-a148-bb3980fadf7b'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria (PNH) is caused by:', '["G6PD deficiency","PK deficiency","Mutation in PIG-A gene","Spectrin deficiency","Hemoglobin mutation"]'::jsonb, 'Mutation in PIG-A gene', 'PIG-A mutation prevents GPI anchor attachment of CD55/CD59, leading to complement-mediated hemolysis.'),
('a5d78756-de7f-5d11-86ca-02e44b413d22'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Which test confirms PNH?', '["Ham test clinical presentation","LDH clinical presentation","Coombs test clinical presentation","Flow cytometry for CD55/CD59 or FLAER","Sucrose lysis test clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Flow cytometry for CD55/CD59 or FLAER', 'Flow cytometry showing CD55/CD59-deficient cells is the gold standard for PNH diagnosis.'),
('45de385a-f00a-59f5-a6d7-19ff5f6b1b81'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Autoimmune hemolytic anemia (AIHA) warm type is mediated by:', '["IgG antibodies","IgE antibodies","IgM antibodies","IgA antibodies","Complement only"]'::jsonb, 'IgG antibodies', 'Warm AIHA: IgG autoantibodies against RBCs, positive DAT (direct Coombs test).'),
('7575c6b8-a691-5ee0-9e20-f75f7dc2dad5'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Which test confirms AIHA?', '["CBC","Peripheral smear","LDH","Indirect Coombs test","Direct antiglobulin test"]'::jsonb, 'Direct antiglobulin test', 'DAT detects IgG or complement on RBC surface. Positive in warm AIHA (IgG) or CAD (C3d).'),
('42bca71f-58cf-514c-87cb-1a72d30299cf'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Cold agglutinin disease is associated with:', '["T-cell mediated","Complement alone","IgG antibodies","IgM autoantibodies","NK cell mediated"]'::jsonb, 'IgM autoantibodies', 'Cold agglutinins (IgM) bind RBCs in cooler extremities, causing complement activation and hemolysis.'),
('54abce61-85c5-5fd2-b614-efd8a9f581ea'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Which disease is associated with cold agglutinin AIHA?', '["None","Mycoplasma pneumoniae infection","All of the above","SLE","CLL"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Cold AIHA is associated with Mycoplasma, EBV, and lymphoproliferative disorders.'),
('468d3787-6d14-5f55-914c-74d2d1ac77cb'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'With respect to management, G6PD deficiency is inherited as:', '["Autosomal recessive","Mitochondrial","X-linked recessive","Autosomal dominant","X-linked dominant"]'::jsonb, 'X-linked recessive', 'G6PD deficiency is X-linked recessive, affecting males predominantly.'),
('9361243f-d569-586c-a372-094bc3b8fd30'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Which oxidative stress triggers hemolysis in G6PD deficiency?', '["Iron supplements clinical presentation","Exercise clinical presentation","Cold exposure clinical presentation","Normal diet clinical presentation","Antimalarials (primaquine), sulfonamides, fava beans"]'::jsonb, 'Antimalarials (primaquine), sulfonamides, fava beans', 'Oxidant drugs (primaquine, sulfa, nitrofurantoin) and fava beans trigger hemolysis in G6PD deficiency.'),
('2f55ec48-8adb-53b8-a96a-1c04ca748417'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'The morphology of RBCs in G6PD deficiency during hemolysis shows:', '["Target cells clinical presentation","Sickle cells clinical presentation","Spherocytes clinical presentation","Schistocytes clinical presentation","Bite cells (blister cells) and Heinz bodies"]'::jsonb, 'Bite cells (blister cells) and Heinz bodies', 'Heinz bodies (denatured hemoglobin) visible with supravital stain. Bite cells on regular smear.'),
('1a1a51a6-01a1-5112-a5e9-7d48fb8cdd73'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Which test is diagnostic for G6PD deficiency?', '["G6PD enzyme assay","Bite cells on smear","Heinz body stain","Bilirubin","CBC"]'::jsonb, 'G6PD enzyme assay', 'Quantitative G6PD assay is definitive. Must be done when NOT in acute hemolysis (reticulocytes have normal G6PD).'),
('547f8b24-1368-5f9c-8360-2b9aca3e8f46'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Pyruvate kinase (PK) deficiency causes:', '["Microcytic anemia","Megaloblastic anemia","Spherocytic hemolytic anemia","Aplastic anemia","Congenital non-spherocytic hemolytic anemia"]'::jsonb, 'Congenital non-spherocytic hemolytic anemia', 'PK deficiency is the second most common RBC enzyme defect (after G6PD), causing hemolytic anemia.'),
('3b0fc1af-14a8-55d6-b971-3008610d22f2'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Which enzyme defect in the Embden-Meyerhof pathway causes hemolytic anemia?', '["Pyruvate kinase deficiency","G6PD deficiency","Aldolase deficiency","PFK deficiency","Hexokinase deficiency"]'::jsonb, 'Pyruvate kinase deficiency', 'PK deficiency blocks the final step of glycolysis, reducing ATP production in RBCs.'),
('26c250b7-0c55-5d51-8545-e5611b4e16ec'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Lead poisoning anemia is characterized by:', '["Aplastic anemia clinical presentation","Hemolytic anemia clinical presentation","Normocytic anemia clinical presentation","Megaloblastic anemia clinical presentation","Microcytic hypochromic anemia with basophilic stippling"]'::jsonb, 'Microcytic hypochromic anemia with basophilic stippling', 'Lead inhibits enzymes in heme synthesis, causing microcytic anemia with coarse basophilic stippling.'),
('ad2e3dec-79a7-548f-9ccc-85f5b9e3b6f6'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'What is the characteristic peripheral smear finding in lead poisoning?', '["Schistocytes","Basophilic stippling","Target cells","Acanthocytes","Spherocytes"]'::jsonb, 'Basophilic stippling', 'Lead inhibits pyrimidine 5''-nucleotidase, causing RNA precipitation (basophilic stippling).'),
('0f23fb04-54c4-5836-8e01-5c01393113ad'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Megaloblastic anemia is characterized by:', '["Macrocytic RBCs (MCV >100) with hypersegmented neutrophils","Target cells clinical presentation","Sickle cells clinical presentation","Microcytic RBCs clinical presentation","Normocytic RBCs clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Macrocytic RBCs (MCV >100) with hypersegmented neutrophils', 'Megaloblastic: MCV >100, hypersegmented neutrophils (>5 lobes), oval macrocytes.'),
('a00de71e-e6d0-54fc-a06e-d66aa2476c86'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'The most common cause of megaloblastic anemia is:', '["Aplastic anemia clinical presentation","Chronic disease clinical presentation","Iron deficiency clinical presentation","Vitamin B12 and folate deficiency","Thalassemia clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Vitamin B12 and folate deficiency', 'B12 and folate are essential for DNA synthesis. Deficiency causes megaloblastic change.'),
('64058d6d-e29c-592b-9555-f1c6c9009fef'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'What is the earliest laboratory abnormality in B12/folate deficiency?', '["Hypersegmented neutrophils","Low methylmalonic acid","Increased MCV","Low Hb","Low reticulocyte count"]'::jsonb, 'Hypersegmented neutrophils', 'Hypersegmented neutrophils (>5% with >5 lobes) appear before anemia or MCV increase.'),
('810613ee-da22-577d-9333-28e11c6d8120'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Pernicious anemia is caused by:', '["Dietary B12 deficiency clinical presentation","Autoimmune destruction of gastric parietal cells","Folate deficiency clinical presentation","Gastrectomy clinical presentation","Malabsorption clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Autoimmune destruction of gastric parietal cells', 'PA: Type A (autoimmune) chronic gastritis with antibodies to parietal cells and intrinsic factor.'),
('daa7b67b-9e58-5458-b3ab-710648bc3363'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Which test differentiates B12 deficiency from folate deficiency?', '["Serum B12, RBC folate, methylmalonic acid (MMA), homocysteine","Platelet count clinical presentation","Reticulocyte count clinical presentation","MCV clinical presentation","Hb level clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Serum B12, RBC folate, methylmalonic acid (MMA), homocysteine', 'B12 deficiency: low B12, high MMA, high homocysteine. Folate deficiency: low folate, normal MMA, high homocysteine.'),
('8aa6dff2-ce86-5346-8609-f15e2e698dac'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Neurologic symptoms occur in which deficiency but NOT in folate deficiency?', '["Vitamin B12 deficiency","Iron deficiency","Vitamin E deficiency","Vitamin B6 deficiency","Folate deficiency"]'::jsonb, 'Vitamin B12 deficiency', 'B12 deficiency causes neurologic (SCD) and psychiatric symptoms due to impaired myelin synthesis.'),
('feae5b54-19f7-5260-9747-83410097dda9'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Which vitamin deficiency causes elevated methylmalonic acid (MMA)?', '["Folate deficiency","Riboflavin deficiency","Iron deficiency","Vitamin B6 deficiency","Vitamin B12 deficiency"]'::jsonb, 'Vitamin B12 deficiency', 'MMA is elevated in B12 deficiency (B12 is cofactor for MMA coenzyme A mutase). Normal in folate deficiency.'),
('a548e677-ba03-5b4d-8e44-baf49e591b07'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Which is the most common nutritional deficiency in the elderly?', '["Vitamin B12 deficiency","Folate deficiency","Zinc deficiency","Iron deficiency","Vitamin D deficiency"]'::jsonb, 'Vitamin B12 deficiency', 'B12 deficiency is common in elderly due to atrophic gastritis and decreased intrinsic factor.'),
('2e287051-c4d3-5fa1-94b2-99cc2b2d6f66'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Aplastic anemia is characterized by:', '["Anemia only","Leukopenia only","Pancytopenia with hypocellular bone marrow","Pancytopenia with hypercellular marrow","Thrombocytopenia only"]'::jsonb, 'Pancytopenia with hypocellular bone marrow', 'Aplastic anemia: pancytopenia with empty bone marrow (hypocellular, replaced by fat).'),
('a1344635-f083-5bca-8f0c-6fecffc9b065'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'The most common cause of acquired aplastic anemia is:', '["Nutritional deficiency clinical presentation","Infection clinical presentation","Genetic mutation clinical presentation","Autoimmunity clinical presentation","Idiopathic, drugs (chemotherapy), radiation, viruses"]'::jsonb, 'Idiopathic, drugs (chemotherapy), radiation, viruses', 'Most cases are idiopathic (immune-mediated). Secondary causes: drugs, toxins, radiation, infections.'),
('49c836b8-246c-57c9-8947-89394e03584b'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Fanconi anemia is characterized by:', '["Pure red cell aplasia","Acquired aplastic anemia","Immune-mediated aplasia","Paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria","Congenital aplastic anemia with chromosomal instability"]'::jsonb, 'Congenital aplastic anemia with chromosomal instability', 'Fanconi: autosomal recessive, congenital anomalies, progressive BM failure, cancer predisposition.'),
('7ed7176a-07a7-52a8-8f1b-d69dbdd3b769'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Which drug is most commonly associated with aplastic anemia?', '["Aspirin","Heparin","Penicillin","Warfarin","Chloramphenicol"]'::jsonb, 'Chloramphenicol', 'Chloramphenicol causes dose-dependent reversible BM suppression and idiosyncratic aplastic anemia.'),
('a822ed3e-99c9-5d04-a273-12140cb5e69c'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Pure red cell aplasia (PRCA) is characterized by:', '["Pancytopenia clinical presentation","Leukopenia only clinical presentation","Thrombocytopenia only clinical presentation","Microcytic anemia clinical presentation","Normocytic anemia with absent erythroblasts in BM"]'::jsonb, 'Normocytic anemia with absent erythroblasts in BM', 'PRCA: anemia, reticulocytopenia, and absent RBC precursors in BM (normal WBC/platelets).'),
('2c883596-c1f0-5abe-aacf-c0c2d08bb213'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Parvovirus B19 can cause aplastic crisis in:', '["Iron deficiency","Hemolytic anemias","Hemochromatosis","Aplastic anemia","Megaloblastic anemia"]'::jsonb, 'Hemolytic anemias', 'Parvovirus B19 infects erythroid progenitors, causing transient aplastic crisis in hemolytic anemias.'),
('841b9e9f-3d65-54f3-82e9-145811b8aab3'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Anemia of chronic disease (ACD) is characterized by:', '["Pancytopenia","Microcytic hypochromic with low ferritin","Normocytic/normochromic anemia with low iron, low TIBC, high ferritin","Hemolytic","Macrocytic"]'::jsonb, 'Normocytic/normochromic anemia with low iron, low TIBC, high ferritin', 'ACD: mild normocytic anemia (MCV normal or slightly low), low iron, low TIBC, high ferritin (retention).'),
('dda15981-0b32-51a9-97ef-eb3bffde84a7'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Which cytokine mediates anemia of chronic disease?', '["IL-6","IFN-gamma","IL-1","TNF-alpha","TGF-beta"]'::jsonb, 'IL-6', 'IL-6 stimulates hepcidin production, which blocks iron absorption and release from macrophages.'),
('7746ee70-e6aa-563f-a497-12c77e4f8150'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Polycythemia is diagnosed when Hb exceeds:', '[">16.5 g/dL (men), >16 g/dL",">20 g/dL",">18 g/dL",">15 g/dL",">14 g/dL"]'::jsonb, '>16.5 g/dL (men), >16 g/dL', 'Polycythemia: increased RBC mass. Primary: polycythemia vera. Secondary: hypoxia, EPO excess.'),
('cc855a2c-3b73-5571-981b-cfb68022d48b'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Polycythemia vera is a:', '["Secondary polycythemia clinical presentation","Inflammatory condition clinical presentation","Reactive process clinical presentation","Myeloproliferative neoplasm with JAK2 V617F mutation","Relative polycythemia clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Myeloproliferative neoplasm with JAK2 V617F mutation', 'PV is a clonal stem cell disorder with JAK2 mutation in >95%.'),
('97bae3db-0c14-5df4-94ff-bb52df98c6d8'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Which lab finding distinguishes polycythemia vera from secondary polycythemia?', '["Normal platelets","High Hb","Low/normal serum EPO","Normal WBC","High RBC count"]'::jsonb, 'Low/normal serum EPO', 'PV: low EPO (autonomous proliferation). Secondary: high EPO (appropriate response to hypoxia).'),
('5653ec0c-0e77-51b2-aa64-9c60c1bae45c'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Complications of polycythemia vera include:', '["Thrombocytopenia clinical presentation","Infection clinical presentation","Leukopenia clinical presentation","Thrombosis (stroke, MI, DVT) and bleeding","Anemia clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Thrombosis (stroke, MI, DVT) and bleeding', 'Hyperviscosity causes thrombosis in PV. Can also transform to myelofibrosis or AML.'),
('70cfdb9c-c848-5307-9649-65d8e5958841'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Relative polycythemia (stress polycythemia) is due to:', '["Increased EPO","Kidney tumor","Hypoxia","Hemoconcentration","JAK2 mutation"]'::jsonb, 'Hemoconcentration', 'Relative polycythemia: normal RBC mass but decreased plasma volume (dehydration, diuretics).'),
('d6615fbc-0c16-5d7a-a28b-fa19935cc236'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Leukocytosis is defined as WBC count >:', '["11,000/uL","15,000/uL","12,000/uL","20,000/uL","10,000/uL"]'::jsonb, '11,000/uL', 'Normal WBC: 4,000-11,000/uL. Leukocytosis >11,000 is a reactive or neoplastic process.'),
('327905cb-80ab-5f2b-ab54-5b88ba6ab17a'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'The most common cause of leukocytosis is:', '["Steroid use clinical presentation","Infection (bacterial) causing neutrophilia","Stress clinical presentation","Leukemia clinical presentation","Lymphoma clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Infection (bacterial) causing neutrophilia', 'Bacterial infection causes neutrophilia (left shift with bands). Viral causes lymphocytosis.'),
('57b70e1d-531f-5147-a931-14ce16603b52'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Leukemoid reaction is defined as:', '["WBC >50,000/uL","WBC >20,000","WBC >100,000","WBC >1 million","WBC >200,000"]'::jsonb, 'WBC >50,000/uL', 'Leukemoid reaction: extreme leukocytosis (50,000-100,000/uL) from severe infection/inflammation.'),
('847c96e2-1871-5dd7-833c-dd24f9f105fe'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Which test differentiates leukemoid reaction from CML?', '["Leukocyte alkaline phosphatase (LAP) score","CBC clinical presentation","Flow cytometry clinical presentation","Peripheral smear clinical presentation","WBC count clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Leukocyte alkaline phosphatase (LAP) score', 'LAP: high in reactive states, low in CML. Also, CML has basophilia and splenomegaly.'),
('5b4d6dd5-142e-5f47-8a62-95f429f0b22f'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Neutropenia is defined as absolute neutrophil count <:', '["2,000/uL","500/uL","1,500/uL","3,000/uL","1,000/uL"]'::jsonb, '1,500/uL', 'Neutropenia: ANC <1500. Severe: <500. Risk of infection increases when ANC <500.'),
('5629e50b-6b26-58d0-a14c-a9a88efe21e6'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Which drug most commonly causes agranulocytosis?', '["Clozapine","Ibuprofen","Warfarin","Aspirin","Acetaminophen"]'::jsonb, 'Clozapine', 'Clozapine causes agranulocytosis in 1-2% of patients, requiring weekly CBC monitoring.'),
('3cc7ea08-2d01-522a-bd66-f65a5ac72b56'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Infectious mononucleosis is caused by:', '["HHV-6","Epstein-Barr virus","CMV","Adenovirus","HIV"]'::jsonb, 'Epstein-Barr virus', 'EBV causes infectious mononucleosis: fever, pharyngitis, lymphadenopathy, atypical lymphocytes.'),
('94eeddfc-cdd3-5cdb-a808-d3f007d9d4db'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'The atypical lymphocytes in infectious mononucleosis are:', '["EBV-infected B cells","Reactive CD8+ T cells","Monocytes","NK cells","Plasma cells"]'::jsonb, 'Reactive CD8+ T cells', 'The atypical lymphocytes are activated CD8+ T cells responding to EBV-infected B cells.'),
('aa527ea4-4a6b-5c36-9139-225bf705e178'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Which test confirms infectious mononucleosis?', '["Throat culture clinical presentation","PCR clinical presentation","Peripheral smear clinical presentation","Monospot test (heterophile antibody) and EBV serology","CBC clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Monospot test (heterophile antibody) and EBV serology', 'Monospot detects heterophile antibodies. Serology: VCA IgM (recent), EBNA IgG (past).'),
('a903eb8c-1cb6-5a9b-8718-9d3ca3ebba59'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Lymphocytosis with smudge cells on smear suggests:', '["Hairy cell leukemia","CML","ALL","CLL","AML"]'::jsonb, 'CLL', 'CLL: lymphocytosis with smudge/basket cells (fragile lymphocytes). Disease of elderly.'),
('65e2a7d8-1d88-5085-95b6-5bdbeeb13142'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Which is the most common leukemia in adults in the Western world?', '["CLL","Hairy cell leukemia","AML","ALL","CML"]'::jsonb, 'CLL', 'CLL is the most common leukemia in Western adults (median age 70).'),
('c9fe37b9-079b-5f40-b353-9c37d508b1b7'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Which immunophenotype is characteristic of CLL?', '["CD3+, CD7+","CD5+, CD19+, CD23+, CD20 weak","CD34+, CD33+","CD10+, CD19+","CD138+, CD38+"]'::jsonb, 'CD5+, CD19+, CD23+, CD20 weak', 'CLL cells co-express CD5 (T cell marker) and B cell markers (CD19, CD20 weak, CD23).'),
('86576b44-2bce-5558-98b7-fe6d2670098a'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Multiple myeloma is characterized by:', '["T cell proliferation clinical presentation","NK cell proliferation clinical presentation","B cell proliferation clinical presentation","Myeloid proliferation clinical presentation","Monoclonal plasma cell proliferation with monoclonal immunoglobulin"]'::jsonb, 'Monoclonal plasma cell proliferation with monoclonal immunoglobulin', 'Myeloma: malignant plasma cells in BM producing monoclonal Ig (M-spike on SPEP).'),
('9350a2d2-0b64-53bf-a180-c56d0b1eb89d'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Which lab finding is diagnostic for multiple myeloma?', '["Hypercalcemia clinical presentation","Elevated beta-2 microglobulin clinical presentation","Lytic bone lesions clinical presentation","M spike on serum protein electrophoresis with immunofixation","Renal failure clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'M spike on serum protein electrophoresis with immunofixation', 'M-spike (monoclonal Ig) on SPEP confirmed by immunofixation is the hallmark.'),
('fc57db94-29ae-5dfa-b6e4-fa772b8bacb7'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'CRAB criteria in multiple myeloma stand for:', '["Cytopenia, Recurrence, Alk Phos, Bence Jones","Calcium, Renal, Anemia, Bone lesions","Complete, Remission, Atypical, Biopsy","Creatinine, RBC, Albumin, Biopsy","None"]'::jsonb, 'Calcium, Renal, Anemia, Bone lesions', 'CRAB: Hypercalcemia, Renal insufficiency, Anemia, Bone lesions (end organ damage in myeloma).'),
('40cc8f06-8c31-5821-ae42-88ca20ea9d56'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Which immunoglobulin is most commonly produced in multiple myeloma?', '["IgM","IgA","IgG","IgD","IgE"]'::jsonb, 'IgG', 'IgG myeloma: 50-60%. IgA: 20%. Light chain only: 15-20%. IgD, IgE rare.'),
('859934e7-4a29-5327-9f64-64d0a7d034c2'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Bence Jones protein in urine represents:', '["Free monoclonal light chains","Albumin","Beta-2 microglobulin","Intact monoclonal immunoglobulin","Myoglobin"]'::jsonb, 'Free monoclonal light chains', 'Bence Jones: free light chains in urine, detected by urine protein electrophoresis/immunofixation.'),
('bfa35693-7586-5879-a7dc-555725c48299'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Which plasma cell leukemia marker?', '["Lytic lesions clinical presentation","Bone pain clinical presentation","Renal failure clinical presentation","Circulating plasma cells >20% or absolute >2x10^9/L","High paraprotein clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Circulating plasma cells >20% or absolute >2x10^9/L', 'Plasma cell leukemia: >20% plasma cells in peripheral blood or absolute count >2x10^9/L.'),
('3e3158d3-b3a6-539f-b03b-ea2125883c87'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'MGUS (monoclonal gammopathy of undetermined significance) differs from myeloma by:', '["M-spike <3 g/dL, <10% plasma cells in BM, no CRAB","More BM involvement clinical presentation","More lytic lesions clinical presentation","Higher M-spike clinical presentation","Same as myeloma clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'M-spike <3 g/dL, <10% plasma cells in BM, no CRAB', 'MGUS: pre-malignant, M-spike <3, BM plasma cells <10%, no end organ damage.'),
('ea5c4664-eadf-5301-a2d7-4b34680392fe'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Waldenstrom macroglobulinemia is characterized by:', '["Amyloidosis clinical presentation","Plasma cell leukemia clinical presentation","Lymphoplasmacytic lymphoma with IgM monoclonal gammopathy","IgA myeloma clinical presentation","IgG myeloma clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Lymphoplasmacytic lymphoma with IgM monoclonal gammopathy', 'Waldenstrom: IgM paraprotein, lymphadenopathy, hepatosplenomegaly, hyperviscosity.'),
('33229d2d-9e94-5474-b30e-d9c6c78e3b52'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Acute myeloid leukemia (AML) is characterized by:', '["Lymphoblast proliferation clinical presentation","Myeloblast proliferation in BM (>20%) with Auer rods","Monocyte proliferation clinical presentation","Promyelocyte proliferation clinical presentation","Erythroid hyperplasia clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Myeloblast proliferation in BM (>20%) with Auer rods', 'AML: >20% myeloblasts in BM or blood. Auer rods are pathognomonic for AML.'),
('b8d6c0f3-c7e6-5961-9854-b36168d1b519'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Which AML subtype is associated with t(15;17) PML-RARA?', '["Acute promyelocytic leukemia","AML M2","AML M0","AML M5","AML M4"]'::jsonb, 'Acute promyelocytic leukemia', 'APML: t(15;17) causing PML-RARA fusion, differentiation block at promyelocyte stage. Treated with ATRA.'),
('bfef7fe2-2d04-5ab3-8e0d-4270b008d0f2'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'What is the characteristic finding in APML (M3)?', '["Erythroblasts","Myeloblasts with single Auer rod","Monoblasts","Hypergranular promyelocytes with multiple Auer rods","Megakaryoblasts"]'::jsonb, 'Hypergranular promyelocytes with multiple Auer rods', 'APML: hypergranular promyelocytes with bundles of Auer rods (''faggot cells''). High DIC risk.'),
('852e4ca8-de1f-59fe-8c84-59790b4bc77a'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Which cytogenetic abnormality is common in AML M2?', '["inv","t(8;21) AML1-ETO","t","t","t"]'::jsonb, 't(8;21) AML1-ETO', 't(8;21) is characteristic of AML M2 with maturation, associated with good prognosis.'),
('50877416-e902-55d6-93fd-c8ee031d375f'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Acute lymphoblastic leukemia (ALL) is most common in:', '["Children","Adults","Neonates","Elderly","Adolescents"]'::jsonb, 'Children', 'ALL is the most common childhood cancer (80% of childhood leukemias). Peak age 2-5 years.'),
('827e4a7a-38b3-5f3f-a4ae-9d2dca584f3b'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Which cytogenetic abnormality is associated with good prognosis in childhood ALL?', '["t clinical presentation","t(9;22) Philadelphia clinical presentation","Hypodiploidy clinical presentation","Hyperdiploidy (>50 chromosomes) and t(12;21) TEL-AML1","t clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Hyperdiploidy (>50 chromosomes) and t(12;21) TEL-AML1', 'Hyperdiploidy and t(12;21) have favorable prognosis. t(9;22) and t(4;11) are poor.'),
('5eb5293e-3ff6-57fd-b7d3-b61cca771a3b'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Which immunophenotype is common in childhood B-ALL?', '["CD33+, CD13+ clinical presentation","CD3+, CD7+ clinical presentation","CD10 (CALLA) positive, CD19+, CD34+","CD56+ clinical presentation","CD138+ clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'CD10 (CALLA) positive, CD19+, CD34+', 'Precursor B-ALL: CD19+, CD10+, TdT+. T-ALL: CD3+, CD7+, TdT+.'),
('d2927c60-8830-5220-bbb9-3d07d0746cff'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Lymphoblastic lymphoma differs from ALL by:', '["Different genetics clinical presentation","Different immunophenotype clinical presentation",">25% blasts clinical presentation","Predominant extramedullary mass with <25% blasts in BM","Older age clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Predominant extramedullary mass with <25% blasts in BM', 'LBL: tissue mass with <25% BM blasts. ALL: >25% BM blasts (systemic).'),
('38faafbb-6bad-5c84-b0ad-7bf4e99bfc78'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Hodgkin lymphoma is characterized by:', '["Malignant B cells alone","T cell malignancy","Plasma cell proliferation","Pure histiocytic proliferation","Reed-Sternberg cells in an inflammatory background"]'::jsonb, 'Reed-Sternberg cells in an inflammatory background', 'Hodgkin: RS cells (CD15+, CD30+, CD45-) with non-neoplastic lymphocytes, plasma cells, eosinophils.'),
('95c237c7-591f-5ae2-8ee5-7d8a1c0565a6'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Classic Reed-Sternberg cell has:', '["Bilobed nucleus with large nucleoli","Cleaved nucleus clinical presentation","Single nucleolus clinical presentation","Multilobed nucleus clinical presentation","Small nucleus clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Bilobed nucleus with large nucleoli', 'Diagnostic RS cell: binucleate with prominent inclusion-like nucleoli (owl-eye).'),
('b49d2b3d-e584-5d79-a3ba-f324389ed2f6'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Which subtype of Hodgkin lymphoma is most common?', '["Lymphocyte rich","Lymphocyte depleted","Lymphocyte predominant","Mixed cellularity","Nodular sclerosis"]'::jsonb, 'Nodular sclerosis', 'Nodular sclerosis is the most common subtype (~70%), typically in young adults with mediastinal disease.'),
('3e5a223a-beab-5c62-badb-08595d9ee9f5'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Which virus is associated with Hodgkin lymphoma?', '["HTLV-1","HIV","HPV","EBV","HHV-8"]'::jsonb, 'EBV', 'EBV is found in RS cells of ~40% HL, most commonly in mixed cellularity and HIV-associated.'),
('bc69374b-2051-5083-9b9e-2cb8e9176985'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Non-Hodgkin lymphoma is staged by:', '["Breslow","Clark","Dukes","Ann Arbor staging system","TNM"]'::jsonb, 'Ann Arbor staging system', 'Ann Arbor staging (I-IV) is used for both Hodgkin and NHL. Suffix A/B for symptoms.'),
('8b9b9e96-9e73-5b6f-855e-1ba9cc7cacc2'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Which NHL is most common in adults?', '["Burkitt lymphoma","Diffuse large B-cell lymphoma","Follicular lymphoma","Mantle cell lymphoma","MALT lymphoma"]'::jsonb, 'Diffuse large B-cell lymphoma', 'DLBCL is the most common NHL (~30%), aggressive but potentially curable with R-CHOP.'),
('4e9d0d2a-58bc-5a8b-91d9-bdf1e8342bbb'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Which NHL is indolent but incurable?', '["Follicular lymphoma","Burkitt lymphoma","Anaplastic large cell lymphoma","DLBCL","Lymphoblastic lymphoma"]'::jsonb, 'Follicular lymphoma', 'Follicular lymphoma is indolent (low grade) with relapsing course, often t(14;18) BCL2.'),
('6c14e066-a4d0-50f1-868c-8da529480872'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Follicular lymphoma is associated with:', '["t(14;18) causing BCL-2 overexpression","t(11;14) cyclin D1 clinical presentation","t(8;14) MYC clinical presentation","t(2;5) ALK clinical presentation","t(9;22) BCR-ABL clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 't(14;18) causing BCL-2 overexpression', 't(14;18) places BCL-2 under IgH promoter, preventing apoptosis of germinal center B cells.'),
('38a0d8d9-5d2d-5308-b3c5-3ad4695294b0'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Burkitt lymphoma is associated with:', '["t","t","t","t(8;14) MYC-IgH translocation","t"]'::jsonb, 't(8;14) MYC-IgH translocation', 'Burkitt: MYC on chr 8 translocated to IgH on chr 14, causing MYC overexpression.'),
('e63f4384-e7cb-5ebf-92a5-80e97d8fd54b'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Which subtype of Burkitt lymphoma is endemic in Africa?', '["All types clinical presentation","Endemic (African) associated with EBV and malaria","None clinical presentation","Immunodeficiency clinical presentation","Sporadic clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Endemic (African) associated with EBV and malaria', 'Endemic Burkitt (jaw, abdominal) is EBV-associated in malaria-endemic Africa.'),
('d0af7601-c58f-5121-8cb1-2d0b6afe703e'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Which NHL is associated with t(11;14) cyclin D1?', '["Mantle cell lymphoma","MALT lymphoma","DLBCL","Burkitt lymphoma","Follicular lymphoma"]'::jsonb, 'Mantle cell lymphoma', 'MCL: t(11;14) cyclin D1 overexpression. Aggressive NHL with nodular/diffuse pattern.'),
('95443fb7-8b84-5784-91d2-c6560157ccbd'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'MALT lymphoma is associated with:', '["HHV-8","H. pylori infection","EBV","HTLV-1","CMV"]'::jsonb, 'H. pylori infection', 'Gastric MALT lymphoma is caused by H. pylori. Treatment: H. pylori eradication (antibiotics).'),
('e6922c2b-8dde-5154-b991-fdf98b131717'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Which NHL is associated with HTLV-1?', '["Burkitt","Follicular lymphoma","DLBCL","Adult T-cell leukemia/lymphoma","Mantle cell"]'::jsonb, 'Adult T-cell leukemia/lymphoma', 'ATLL: HTLV-1 endemic in Japan, Caribbean. Hypercalcemia, Lytic bone lesions, and skin involvement.'),
('d6297cd0-382e-542c-a329-6c26baa124a9'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Anaplastic large cell lymphoma (ALCL) is associated with:', '["t","t","t","t","t(2;5) ALK-1 rearrangement"]'::jsonb, 't(2;5) ALK-1 rearrangement', 'ALCL: t(2;5) creates NPM-ALK fusion, expressing ALK-1. CD30 positive T-cell lymphoma.'),
('d807e607-1748-5c75-8ec7-f9f2cb78d471'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Post-transplant lymphoproliferative disorder (PTLD) is associated with:', '["EBV infection","HHV-8","HPV","CMV","HTLV-1"]'::jsonb, 'EBV infection', 'PTLD is usually EBV-driven B cell proliferation in immunosuppressed transplant recipients.'),
('6bab7ea7-11dd-559f-8806-a6f4bb8b1527'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Hairy cell leukemia is characterized by:', '["Smudge cells clinical presentation","Frothy cytoplasm ''hairy cells'' with tartrate-resistant acid phosphatase","Reed-Sternberg cells clinical presentation","Auer rods clinical presentation","Sezary cells clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Frothy cytoplasm ''hairy cells'' with tartrate-resistant acid phosphatase', 'HCL: pancytopenia, splenomegaly, hairy cells in BM (TRAP+). Treatment: cladribine.'),
('a5a981d2-89df-50a8-804a-e52b0f9b6cf6'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Myelodysplastic syndrome (MDS) is characterized by:', '["Reactive changes clinical presentation","Normal BM clinical presentation","Dysplasia in one or more cell lines with cytopenia","Increased Hb clinical presentation","Blast proliferation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Dysplasia in one or more cell lines with cytopenia', 'MDS: ineffective hematopoiesis with dysplastic morphology, cytopenias, risk of AML transformation.'),
('349f5d0a-fd2d-5d8a-b663-6ada48b6b66f'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Which cytopenia is most common in MDS?', '["Neutropenia","No cytopenia","Pancytopenia equally","Thrombocytopenia","Anemia"]'::jsonb, 'Anemia', 'Most MDS patients present with symptomatic anemia (often macrocytic). Transfusion-dependent.'),
('acef4265-aa21-5f16-9764-144025df9cd5'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Myeloproliferative neoplasms (MPN) include all EXCEPT:', '["Polycythemia vera","Primary myelofibrosis","MDS","Essential thrombocythemia","CML"]'::jsonb, 'MDS', 'Classic MPN: PV, ET, PMF, CML. MDS is a separate category (dysplasia).'),
('2690d54d-dc54-508e-b4af-6fa155a845d0'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Essential thrombocythemia (ET) is characterized by:', '["Anemia clinical presentation","Reactive thrombocytosis clinical presentation","Sustained thrombocytosis (>450,000) with JAK2 mutation","Neutropenia clinical presentation","Low platelets clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Sustained thrombocytosis (>450,000) with JAK2 mutation', 'ET: persistent high platelets, normal Hb, splenomegaly, thrombotic/hemorrhagic risk.'),
('b32c4d19-0af5-5fcc-9abf-c1d6ddf27cfd'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Primary myelofibrosis (PMF) is characterized by:', '["Dysplasia clinical presentation","Increased blasts clinical presentation","Lymphocytosis clinical presentation","BM fibrosis, extramedullary hematopoiesis, leukoerythroblastic smear","Ring sideroblasts clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'BM fibrosis, extramedullary hematopoiesis, leukoerythroblastic smear', 'PMF: dry tap, teardrop cells, myeloid metaplasia (splenomegaly), JAK2 or CALR mutation.'),
('e7589f82-70ed-50a4-b9c4-239e7e551a95'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Leukoerythroblastic smear (teardrop cells, nucleated RBCs, immature WBCs) suggests:', '["Hemolytic anemia","Aplastic anemia","Myelophthisic anemia","Iron deficiency","Megaloblastic anemia"]'::jsonb, 'Myelophthisic anemia', 'Leukoerythroblastic: teardrop cells, NRBCs, metamyelocytes. Seen in PMF, metastases, TB.'),
('38639629-9d3c-58d5-92e7-09380381982b'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Which mutation is most common in MPN?', '["BCR-ABL","CALR mutation","MPL mutation","JAK2 V617F","KIT mutation"]'::jsonb, 'JAK2 V617F', 'JAK2 V617F is the most common MPN mutation. CALR mutations occur in JAK2-negative ET/PMF.'),
('d420de9e-5a4e-578c-a5c4-ae5e5cfe7883'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Which is the normal function of the spleen?', '["Red blood cell production clinical presentation","Digestion clinical presentation","Bile production clinical presentation","Hormone production clinical presentation","Filter blood, remove old RBCs, mount immune responses to blood-borne antigens"]'::jsonb, 'Filter blood, remove old RBCs, mount immune responses to blood-borne antigens', 'Spleen: filtration of blood, removal of senescent RBCs and particles, antibody production, clears encapsulated bacteria.'),
('648c42be-146e-53c7-a901-7dc2c1b109b5'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Hypersplenism is defined as:', '["Spleen infarction clinical presentation","Cytopenia without splenomegaly clinical presentation","Splenomegaly without cytopenia clinical presentation","Splenomegaly with cytopenia(s) and normal/hyperplastic BM","Spleen atrophy clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Splenomegaly with cytopenia(s) and normal/hyperplastic BM', 'Hypersplenism: enlarged spleen causing sequestration/destruction of cells, resulting in cytopenia(s).'),
('6a7b3a89-f06b-5e88-b889-7e20371fa01b'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Overwhelming post-splenectomy infection (OPSI) is most commonly caused by:', '["E. coli","Streptococcus pneumoniae","Haemophilus influenzae","Staphylococcus aureus","Neisseria meningitidis"]'::jsonb, 'Streptococcus pneumoniae', 'Spleen clears encapsulated bacteria. OPSI is most often due to S. pneumoniae.'),
('d36c300c-6cb1-51b5-b2d9-c8589958af04'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Which cells are removed by the spleen?', '["Platelets only","WBCs only","All cells equally","Normal RBCs","Old/abnormal RBCs"]'::jsonb, 'Old/abnormal RBCs', 'Spleen ''culls'' (removes) old/damaged RBCs and ''pits'' (removes) inclusions (Howell-Jolly bodies).'),
('872cb7de-0725-5425-977f-5f52dedb4ca6'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Howell-Jolly bodies on RBCs indicate:', '["Hemolysis","Megaloblastic anemia","Splenectomy or hyposplenism","Thalassemia","Iron deficiency"]'::jsonb, 'Splenectomy or hyposplenism', 'Howell-Jolly bodies (DNA remnants) are normally removed by the spleen; present after splenectomy.'),
('1e79508b-a185-5e74-8c81-429885f02d6e'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Which vaccine should be given before elective splenectomy?', '["Only HBV clinical presentation","Pneumococcal, meningococcal, Hib, and influenza vaccines","No vaccination needed clinical presentation","Only influenza clinical presentation","Only tetanus clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Pneumococcal, meningococcal, Hib, and influenza vaccines', 'Patients should receive pneumococcal, meningococcal, H. influenzae type b vaccines 2 weeks before splenectomy.'),
('8f94ae09-00ba-507a-83f0-ebe00f8186fc'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Which is the most common cause of splenomegaly worldwide?', '["Portal hypertension","Infectious mononucleosis","Malaria","Lymphoma","Myeloproliferative diseases"]'::jsonb, 'Malaria', 'Malaria is the most common cause of splenomegaly worldwide due to hyper-reactive splenomegaly.'),
('074e146a-8ec2-5e4b-85b1-c43a509a784e'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Rupture of the spleen is most commonly associated with:', '["Leukemia","Portal hypertension","Trauma","Malaria","Infectious mononucleosis"]'::jsonb, 'Trauma', 'Trauma is the most common cause of splenic rupture. Spontaneous rupture can occur in mononucleosis.'),
('e09629df-1542-50ce-bcc2-3f3a3245a963'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Splenomegaly in portal hypertension is due to:', '["Infiltration","Infection","Infarction","Neoplasm","Congestive splenomegaly"]'::jsonb, 'Congestive splenomegaly', 'Portal hypertension causes passive venous congestion of the spleen (congestive splenomegaly).'),
('22e20788-9796-5e2a-9f53-d833880f0bc1'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Which hematologic condition causes massive splenomegaly?', '["Primary myelofibrosis","Iron deficiency","Hereditary spherocytosis","G6PD deficiency","Thalassemia minor"]'::jsonb, 'Primary myelofibrosis', 'Massive splenomegaly can occur in myelofibrosis, CML, malaria, leishmaniasis, and Gaucher disease.'),
('b5af3e82-52b3-501e-aa34-8b2a9a6f5648'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Hyposplenism can be identified by:', '["Spherocytes clinical presentation","Howell-Jolly bodies, target cells, and pitted RBCs on smear","Acanthocytes clinical presentation","Schistocytes clinical presentation","Sickle cells clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Howell-Jolly bodies, target cells, and pitted RBCs on smear', 'Post-splenectomy: Howell-Jolly bodies, target cells, pitted RBCs, and thrombocytosis.'),
('cac85f3a-880d-57ce-be7a-dad3357b2f24'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Which disorder is associated with functional hyposplenism?', '["Sickle cell disease","Hereditary spherocytosis","CLL","Hemolytic anemia","ITP"]'::jsonb, 'Sickle cell disease', 'Sickle cell disease causes repeated splenic infarcts leading to autosplenectomy.'),
('dc07c6a0-bd32-5974-a436-d1732267fe7a'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Its white pulp contains:', '["RBCs only","Platelets only","Plasma cells only","Lymphoid follicles","Granulocytes only"]'::jsonb, 'Lymphoid follicles', 'White pulp: periarteriolar lymphoid sheaths (T cells) and follicles (B cells). Red pulp: RBC filtration.'),
('fa5f238c-9fba-5f25-adb0-335e6e0c8b95'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Which is the most common benign splenic tumor?', '["Fibroma","Hamartoma","Lymphangioma","Hemangioma","Lipoma"]'::jsonb, 'Hemangioma', 'Splenic hemangioma is the most common benign splenic tumor, often incidental.'),
('473c74d6-c933-5deb-a4d3-df96c7d796c8'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Lymphomatous involvement of the spleen occurs in:', '["Only NHL clinical presentation","Hodgkin and non-Hodgkin lymphoma","Leukemia only clinical presentation","Only Hodgkin clinical presentation","Carcinoma clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Hodgkin and non-Hodgkin lymphoma', 'Both Hodgkin and NHL can involve the spleen, often as part of systemic disease.'),
('1c11561a-c809-52c6-84fd-d1f9bc328bf7'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Splenic rupture in infectious mononucleosis is a risk due to:', '["Neutrophil infiltration clinical presentation","Toxin effect clinical presentation","Bacterial infection clinical presentation","Massive EBV-induced lymphoid proliferation in spleen","Direct viral lysis clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Massive EBV-induced lymphoid proliferation in spleen', 'EBV causes marked lymphoid hyperplasia in spleen, making it fragile and prone to rupture.'),
('582bafc6-fb0b-5a12-917d-916fb0f90947'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Accessory spleen (splenule) is most commonly found in:', '["Splenic hilum, near tail of pancreas","Mesentery clinical presentation","Lung clinical presentation","Kidney clinical presentation","Liver clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Splenic hilum, near tail of pancreas', 'Accessory spleen is common (~10-30%), most often near splenic hilum or pancreatic tail.'),
('ee32d348-4e02-515f-bf14-ed8cefc9bc7e'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Asplenia is associated with congenital heart disease:', '["Heterotaxy syndrome","VSD","PDA","Tetralogy of Fallot","ASD"]'::jsonb, 'Heterotaxy syndrome', 'Congenital asplenia is part of heterotaxy (Ivemark syndrome) with right atrial isomerism and complex CHD.'),
('5a7bf944-af54-5862-b4b1-9411be395e0f'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Which splenic finding is seen in Gaucher disease?', '["Gaucher cells","Hemangiomas","Fibrosis","Granulomas","Amyloid deposits"]'::jsonb, 'Gaucher cells', 'Gaucher cells in spleen/spleen (glucocerebroside-laden macrophages).'),
('f8c37b3f-3d2f-5a84-a2a2-0e97101e6772'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Splenectomy increases susceptibility to infection by:', '["Encapsulated bacteria","Parasites","Viruses","Fungi","All bacteria"]'::jsonb, 'Encapsulated bacteria', 'Spleen is important for opsonization and clearance of encapsulated bacteria. Risk of OPSI.'),
('afb80850-ff72-58e5-9c53-5ee298ce0c0c'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Bleeding time is a test of:', '["Coagulation factors","Platelet function","Vascular integrity","Bone marrow function","Fibrinolysis"]'::jsonb, 'Platelet function', 'Bleeding time measures primary hemostasis (platelet plug formation). Normal: 2-7 min (Ivy method).'),
('c4708f0a-3be4-525f-aa2f-d7d854bc5edd'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Normal platelet count is:', '["50,000-150,000/uL","400,000-600,000/uL",">600,000/uL","<150,000/uL","150,000-400,000/uL"]'::jsonb, '150,000-400,000/uL', 'Normal: 150-400 x10^9/L. Thrombocytopenia <150,000. Risk of spontaneous bleeding when <10,000.'),
('da834867-5a2f-55c0-9c07-cff9c8aac75c'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Thrombocytopenia is defined as platelet count <:', '["100,000/uL","50,000/uL","150,000/uL","250,000/uL","200,000/uL"]'::jsonb, '150,000/uL', 'Thrombocytopenia: <150,000/uL. Moderate: 50-100K. Severe: <20-50K. Spontaneous bleeding: <10K.'),
('76655dc0-cfe7-5db9-bb6d-f198a234c402'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Which is the most common cause of thrombocytopenia in hospitalized patients?', '["ITP","Liver disease","TTP","DIC","Drug-induced thrombocytopenia"]'::jsonb, 'Drug-induced thrombocytopenia', 'Hospitalized patients often have drug-induced thrombocytopenia (especially heparin, chemotherapy).'),
('84e1f6f6-bdb4-5abf-ba4b-2992766aa2bc'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Immune thrombocytopenia (ITP) is characterized by:', '["Isolated thrombocytopenia with increased megakaryocytes in BM","DIC clinical presentation","Leukopenia clinical presentation","Pancytopenia clinical presentation","Hemolytic anemia clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Isolated thrombocytopenia with increased megakaryocytes in BM', 'ITP: isolated low platelets, normal BM with increased megakaryocytes. Autoantibodies against platelet GPIIb/IIIa.'),
('cdb4ea52-4f97-521f-bdca-19e40f3914c4'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Which test is NOT required for ITP diagnosis?', '["HIV, HCV serology","Peripheral smear","Bone marrow biopsy","Platelet antibodies","CBC"]'::jsonb, 'Platelet antibodies', 'ITP is a diagnosis of exclusion. BM exam is needed only for atypical presentation or age >60.'),
('b5fa8573-f4fd-5804-9835-6e2f3094693d'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'First-line treatment for ITP is:', '["Corticosteroids","Splenectomy","Rituximab","IVIG","Platelet transfusion"]'::jsonb, 'Corticosteroids', 'First-line: prednisone 1 mg/kg/day. Second-line: IVIG, rituximab, splenectomy, TPO agonists.'),
('5eacbe97-0642-5c3f-8fa5-edcc187873d4'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Which treatment is reserved for refractory ITP?', '["Splenectomy, rituximab, TPO-RA","Platelet transfusion","Observation","Prednisone","IVIG"]'::jsonb, 'Splenectomy, rituximab, TPO-RA', 'Splenectomy: 60-70% response. TPO-RA: effective for chronic refractory ITP.'),
('a99efbe7-3f4c-58e5-a1c8-2ae55f4ef455'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Heparin-induced thrombocytopenia (HIT) type II is caused by:', '["Pseudothrombocytopenia clinical presentation","Direct marrow suppression clinical presentation","Platelet destruction clinical presentation","Hemodilution clinical presentation","Antibodies against PF4-heparin complexes causing platelet activation"]'::jsonb, 'Antibodies against PF4-heparin complexes causing platelet activation', 'HIT: anti-PF4/heparin IgG antibodies bind FcgammaRIIa on platelets, causing activation and thrombosis.'),
('d5b3e6cd-9c84-5567-8ce1-7bad688d52cd'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Which complication is characteristic of HIT?', '["Anaphylaxis clinical presentation","Allergic reaction clinical presentation","Thrombosis (venous or arterial) rather than bleeding","Severe bleeding clinical presentation","Skin necrosis clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Thrombosis (venous or arterial) rather than bleeding', 'HIT paradoxically causes thrombosis (DVT, PE, stroke) due to platelet activation.'),
('480ab25e-389d-5af1-8dbe-e268c42a0308'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Which test confirms HIT?', '["Platelet count clinical presentation","aPTT clinical presentation","ELISA for PF4-heparin antibodies and serotonin release assay","PT clinical presentation","Bleeding time clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'ELISA for PF4-heparin antibodies and serotonin release assay', 'Screening: PF4-heparin ELISA (high sensitivity). Confirm with SRA (functional, high specificity).'),
('538d37a3-0dad-556e-bbe6-d8dc3165753a'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Treatment of HIT involves:', '["Start aspirin","Give platelet transfusion","Increase heparin dose","Start warfarin","Stop heparin, start alternative anticoagulant"]'::jsonb, 'Stop heparin, start alternative anticoagulant', 'Stop all heparin. Use direct thrombin inhibitor (argatroban) or factor Xa inhibitor (fondaparinux).'),
('7edbc585-033d-576c-b3ce-52c63f99cc66'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura (TTP) pentad includes:', '["Cytopenia, rash, arthritis","Thrombocytopenia, MAHA, Fever, Renal, Neurologic","Anemia, leukopenia, fever","Thrombocytopenia, bleeding, infection","Purpura, petechiae, pallor"]'::jsonb, 'Thrombocytopenia, MAHA, Fever, Renal, Neurologic', 'TTP pentad: thrombocytopenia, microangiopathic hemolytic anemia (schistocytes), fever, renal failure, neurologic symptoms.'),
('37970476-6fe9-5865-8a0b-732487702ee5'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'TTP is caused by:', '["Complement dysregulation","Platelet autoantibodies","DIC","Severe deficiency of ADAMTS13","Coagulation factor deficiency"]'::jsonb, 'Severe deficiency of ADAMTS13', 'ADAMTS13 cleaves ultra-large vWF multimers. Absence leads to platelet microthrombi.'),
('4f4f3171-9a00-5607-91bd-1fbbe0b0e7ba'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Which test confirms TTP?', '["High vWF antigen","Elevated LDH","Thrombocytopenia","Schistocytes on smear","ADAMTS13 activity <10%"]'::jsonb, 'ADAMTS13 activity <10%', 'ADAMTS13 activity <10% confirms TTP. Plasmacytosis and anti-ADAMTS13 antibodies help.'),
('9623221b-8676-5796-b7e6-3201bc1e701c'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'First-line treatment for TTP is:', '["Splenectomy clinical presentation","Plasma exchange (plasmapheresis) and corticosteroids","Platelet transfusion clinical presentation","Rituximab clinical presentation","IVIG clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Plasma exchange (plasmapheresis) and corticosteroids', 'Urgent plasma exchange removes anti-ADAMTS13 antibodies and replenishes ADAMTS13.'),
('8e45cae2-704b-5d88-9550-24cd8f6a424b'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Platelet transfusion is contraindicated in TTP because:', '["It causes infection","It is ineffective","It causes fever","It may exacerbate thrombosis","It causes hemolysis"]'::jsonb, 'It may exacerbate thrombosis', 'Platelet transfusion in TTP (and HIT) is avoided as it may fuel further microvascular thrombosis.'),
('8d45abe5-8aa4-5b5f-894b-edc21672767b'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'For a patient presenting with symptoms, Hemolytic uremic syndrome (HUS) is characterized by:', '["Neurologic symptoms predominant","Fever predominant","Similar to TTP but more neurologic","Thrombocytopenia, MAHA, and acute renal failure","Different ADAMTS13 level"]'::jsonb, 'Thrombocytopenia, MAHA, and acute renal failure', 'HUS: similar to TTP but predominantly renal impairment and less neurologic involvement.'),
('b8f26aa3-a665-5daa-82ba-e892d98d051b'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Typical (D+) HUS is associated with:', '["Pregnancy clinical presentation","ADAMTS13 deficiency clinical presentation","Drug reaction clinical presentation","Shiga toxin-producing E. coli (STEC) O157:H7 infection","Complement mutation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Shiga toxin-producing E. coli (STEC) O157:H7 infection', 'D+ HUS follows STEC gastroenteritis, often in children. Shiga toxin damages microvascular endothelium.'),
('cc67a5a5-466e-5a1c-bd76-ea48cdda4a9e'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Atypical HUS (aHUS) is associated with:', '["E. coli infection clinical presentation","Complement regulatory protein mutations","Pregnancy clinical presentation","Drugs clinical presentation","ADAMTS13 deficiency clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Complement regulatory protein mutations', 'aHUS: dysregulation of alternative complement pathway. Treated with eculizumab (anti-C5).'),
('3518316b-d205-55b5-9fa8-9169b5babd12'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Disseminated intravascular coagulation (DIC) is characterized by:', '["Platelet dysfunction clinical presentation","vWF deficiency clinical presentation","Factor VIII deficiency clinical presentation","Primary hypercoagulable state clinical presentation","Widespread activation of coagulation, consumption of factors, and bleeding"]'::jsonb, 'Widespread activation of coagulation, consumption of factors, and bleeding', 'DIC: excessive thrombin generation, microthrombi, consumption of clotting factors/platelets, bleeding.'),
('97457129-20af-5861-8121-924c4abd693b'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Which lab test is most sensitive for DIC?', '["aPTT","D-dimer","Fibrinogen","Platelet count","PT"]'::jsonb, 'D-dimer', 'D-dimer is sensitive (>95%) but not specific. Platelet count, PT, aPTT, fibrinogen are also used.'),
('02286f7e-18ef-528f-b750-996762a549e3'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Which lab finding in DIC suggests consumption?', '["Low D-dimer","Low fibrinogen","High fibrinogen","High D-dimer only","Normal fibrinogen"]'::jsonb, 'Low fibrinogen', 'Fibrinogen is an acute phase reactant that may be normal initially, but very low levels indicate severe DIC.'),
('d6df7542-65a4-54c1-a1c6-e8f16fbd6008'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Which scoring system is used for DIC diagnosis?', '["CHADS2","SOFA","APACHE","ISTH DIC score","CURB-65"]'::jsonb, 'ISTH DIC score', 'ISTH DIC score: platelet count, PT, D-dimer, fibrinogen. Score >5 = overt DIC.'),
('1d75110a-ed10-5c58-a607-d043a06b6096'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Which condition most commonly causes DIC?', '["All are common","Obstetric complications","Trauma","Malignancy","Sepsis"]'::jsonb, 'All are common', 'Sepsis is the most common cause of DIC. Other causes: trauma, malignancy, obstetrics.'),
('9e4d4811-61a3-5598-8582-c908d9277f24'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Management of DIC primarily involves:', '["Cryoprecipitate","Treating the underlying cause","Platelet transfusion","Heparin","FFP"]'::jsonb, 'Treating the underlying cause', 'Treat the underlying condition. Blood products (platelets, FFP, cryo) given for significant bleeding.'),
('fc96cf24-f41d-59f4-9ab9-12051ad3cdbe'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Which coagulation factors are consumed in DIC?', '["Only factor VII","Only factor VIII","Only factor IX","All factors","Only vitamin K factors"]'::jsonb, 'All factors', 'DIC consumes all coagulation factors and platelets. Factor VIII and fibrinogen are very low.'),
('5950c486-127c-52a0-adc0-b06d0424e8ac'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Chronic DIC is most commonly associated with:', '["Sepsis","Obstetric complications","Trauma","Solid tumors","Liver disease"]'::jsonb, 'Solid tumors', 'Chronic DIC: smoldering consumption, often in pancreatic/breast/prostate cancer with mucin.'),
('ed3642d8-87e5-5376-91b7-77afb8a15e59'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Which pattern of bleeding is seen in DIC?', '["Menorrhagia only","Bleeding from multiple sites","GI bleeding only","Epistaxis only","Joint bleeds"]'::jsonb, 'Bleeding from multiple sites', 'DIC causes widespread microvascular bleeding: petechiae, purpura, bleeding from puncture sites and wounds.'),
('a9e85b1f-5af8-5360-a9fd-3049bded9e7a'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Thrombocytosis is defined as platelet count >:', '["800,000/uL","450,000/uL","600,000/uL","500,000/uL","300,000/uL"]'::jsonb, '450,000/uL', 'Thrombocytosis: >450,000/uL. Reactive: infection, inflammation, iron deficiency. Essential: clonal MPN.'),
('dca7cba0-17d7-5051-9a8d-c1d22df5f520'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Reactive (secondary) thrombocytosis is most commonly caused by:', '["BCR-ABL clinical presentation clinical presentation","JAK2 mutation clinical presentation clinical presentation","MPL mutation clinical presentation clinical presentation","CALR mutation clinical presentation clinical presentation","Infection, inflammation, iron deficiency, malignancy, post-splenectomy"]'::jsonb, 'Infection, inflammation, iron deficiency, malignancy, post-splenectomy', 'Reactive: increased thrombopoietin from inflammation or acute phase response. No thrombotic risk.'),
('6b5d4f9f-561c-5844-b30d-03c6bfdc2bcc'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Essential thrombocythemia (ET) is associated with:', '["Only CALR clinical presentation","JAK2 V617F (50-60%), CALR (20-25%), MPL","BCR-ABL clinical presentation","Only JAK2 clinical presentation","FLT3-ITD clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'JAK2 V617F (50-60%), CALR (20-25%), MPL', 'ET mutations: JAK2 (most common), CALR (especially in JAK2-negative), MPL. Mutation status guides prognosis.'),
('1b60fb93-2648-504c-acb2-d4552806746f'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Which complication distinguishes ET from reactive thrombocytosis?', '["Splenomegaly","Higher platelet count","Abnormal platelet morphology","Thrombosis","Normal WBC"]'::jsonb, 'Thrombosis', 'ET: thrombotic/hemorrhagic complications. Reactive: no significant thrombotic risk.'),
('4aa124aa-a09e-528b-8f00-400338987f69'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'What is the first-line cytoreductive therapy for high-risk ET?', '["Anagrelide","Interferon-alpha","Aspirin","Hydroxyurea","Busulfan"]'::jsonb, 'Hydroxyurea', 'Hydroxyurea reduces platelet count and thrombotic events. Anagrelide second-line. Aspirin for low-risk.'),
('9db93d6e-aa34-5f84-b285-8415e1110569'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Bernard-Soulier syndrome is a platelet disorder caused by:', '["Deficiency of GPIb-IX-V complex","Release defect","ADP receptor defect","Deficiency of GPIIb-IIIa","Storage pool defect"]'::jsonb, 'Deficiency of GPIb-IX-V complex', 'Bernard-Soulier: giant platelets, thrombocytopenia, prolonged bleeding time. Defect in vWF receptor.'),
('d76047be-66bd-593f-b1f1-c22fbb6d11ba'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Glanzmann thrombasthenia is caused by:', '["Storage pool defect","Collagen receptor defect","GPIb-IX deficiency","Release defect","Deficiency of GPIIb-IIIa"]'::jsonb, 'Deficiency of GPIIb-IIIa', 'Glanzmann: normal platelet count, no aggregation to ADP/collagen/epinephrine. Defect in fibrinogen receptor.'),
('1d4143c6-28a4-5344-b51e-34c8c87bd685'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Which test shows abnormal platelet aggregation in Glanzmann thrombasthenia?', '["Platelet count clinical presentation","Aggregation to ADP, collagen, epinephrine, and thrombin","Aggregation to ristocetin clinical presentation","Bleeding time clinical presentation","Clot retraction clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Aggregation to ADP, collagen, epinephrine, and thrombin', 'Glanzmann: absent aggregation to all agonists except ristocetin (GPIb-vWF pathway intact).'),
('627ff2bc-d4c7-5f58-9d03-3b51dfc68ac4'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Which test shows abnormal platelet aggregation in Bernard-Soulier?', '["Aggregation to ADP","All normal","Aggregation to ristocetin","Aggregation to epinephrine","Aggregation to collagen"]'::jsonb, 'Aggregation to ristocetin', 'Bernard-Soulier: absent ristocetin-induced aggregation (corrected by adding normal plasma).'),
('e86f137b-55f1-5375-8073-81faac3f1dcd'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Von Willebrand disease is characterized by:', '["Thrombocytopenia clinical presentation","Low factor VIII only clinical presentation","Prolonged PT clinical presentation","Platelet aggregation defect to ADP clinical presentation","Prolonged bleeding time, abnormal ristocetin aggregation, low vWF"]'::jsonb, 'Prolonged bleeding time, abnormal ristocetin aggregation, low vWF', 'vWD: low vWF (Ag and activity), low FVIII, prolonged BT, abnormal ristocetin aggregation.'),
('1a4393fb-53de-5d45-8536-4e7549264644'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Which is the most common inherited bleeding disorder?', '["Hemophilia B","Factor XI deficiency","Hemophilia A","Platelet function defect","Von Willebrand disease"]'::jsonb, 'Von Willebrand disease', 'vWD is the most common inherited bleeding disorder (up to 1% population).'),
('2dfe8223-b87e-5f66-8f40-5f3549a1dcef'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Which type of vWD is most common?', '["Type 2A","Type 1","Type 2B","Type 2","Type 3"]'::jsonb, 'Type 1', 'Type 1: 70-80% of vWD. Mild to moderate bleeding. Type 3: severe, rare.'),
('502b64fb-ff96-55f4-999d-45289a98bcee'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Which vWF multimer pattern is seen in Type 2A vWD?', '["Supranormal multimers","Loss of low multimers","Loss of high and intermediate multimers","Loss of high multimers only","Normal multimers"]'::jsonb, 'Loss of high and intermediate multimers', 'Type 2A: absence of high and intermediate molecular weight multimers (decreased platelet adhesion).'),
('20222f20-eb58-5d67-9d28-f3dde5465b3e'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Desmopressin (DDAVP) is used in which type of vWD?', '["Type 2B","Type 1","None","Type 3","All types"]'::jsonb, 'Type 1', 'DDAVP releases vWF from endothelial stores. Effective in type 1. Contraindicated in type 2B (thrombocytopenia).'),
('6e8e690a-2b0c-5ad2-a080-b61001772206'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Treatment of type 3 vWD involves:', '["Vitamin K clinical presentation","DDAVP clinical presentation","vWF-containing factor VIII concentrates","Cryoprecipitate clinical presentation","Platelet transfusion clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'vWF-containing factor VIII concentrates', 'Type 3: replace vWF with plasma-derived concentrates (Humate-P). Cryoprecipitate is second-line.'),
('f5f921f0-a5aa-5615-9e38-f3d36d7432ec'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Hemophilia A is deficiency of:', '["Factor VIII","Factor IX","Factor VII","Factor XI","von Willebrand factor"]'::jsonb, 'Factor VIII', 'Hemophilia A (factor VIII deficiency): X-linked, 1:5000 males, severe bleeding into joints.'),
('28d35e05-1bf7-59e3-bba9-e8e407abcca6'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Regarding clinical scenarios, Hemophilia B (Christmas disease) is deficiency of:', '["Factor VII","Factor VIII","Factor IX","Factor X","Factor XI"]'::jsonb, 'Factor IX', 'Hemophilia B (factor IX deficiency): X-linked, clinically identical to hemophilia A.'),
('159cbe4d-dfcb-56d6-94ce-52fa3dc1cc65'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'The characteristic bleeding pattern in hemophilia is:', '["Petechiae clinical presentation","Epistaxis clinical presentation","Deep joint bleeds (hemarthrosis), muscle bleeds","Mucocutaneous bleeding clinical presentation","GI bleeding only clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Deep joint bleeds (hemarthrosis), muscle bleeds', 'Hemophilia: bleeding into weight-bearing joints (knees, ankles, elbows), muscles, and retroperitoneum.'),
('51735e34-26d6-5063-8cff-43a9f6a42fe5'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Prolonged aPTT with normal PT suggests:', '["Factor VII deficiency","Vitamin K deficiency","DIC","Liver disease","Hemophilia A or B"]'::jsonb, 'Hemophilia A or B', 'Prolonged aPTT (intrinsic pathway) + normal PT = hemophilia A/B/XI or vWD.'),
('c5a2a3ec-8748-54d3-95aa-25943666e4ca'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Prolonged PT with normal aPTT suggests:', '["Hemophilia A","vWD","Factor XI deficiency","Factor VII deficiency","Hemophilia B"]'::jsonb, 'Factor VII deficiency', 'PT evaluates factor VII (extrinsic). Isolated PT prolongation = factor VII deficiency or early vitamin K deficiency.'),
('7039209e-f5ed-5ec7-a101-acbba7844b2f'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Which test corrects prolonged aPTT in hemophilia A?', '["Bleeding time clinical presentation","Mixing study with normal plasma","Factor XIII assay clinical presentation","Thrombin time clinical presentation","Platelet count clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Mixing study with normal plasma', 'Mixing study: aPTT corrects immediately with normal plasma (factor deficiency). Does not correct with inhibitor.'),
('bddf06b1-a511-57ae-a205-d88bdba17bd3'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Severity of hemophilia A is classified by:', '["Platelet count","Age of onset","aPTT prolongation","Bleeding frequency","Factor VIII activity level"]'::jsonb, 'Factor VIII activity level', 'Severe: FVIII <1% (spontaneous bleeding). Moderate: 1-5% (bleeding with minor injury). Mild: >5% (surgery/trauma).'),
('b7691109-5af9-51b8-b895-8a1b730e6396'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Treatment of hemophilia A with inhibitors involves:', '["Activated prothrombin complex concentrate (FEIBA) or recombinant factor VIIa","Factor IX clinical presentation","Higher doses of factor VIII clinical presentation","Tranexamic acid clinical presentation","DDAVP clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Activated prothrombin complex concentrate (FEIBA) or recombinant factor VIIa', 'Inhibitors: bypass therapy (FEIBA or rFVIIa). Immune tolerance induction to eradicate inhibitor.'),
('2898df80-d31d-513c-8032-fca64ff2774b'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Which acquired coagulation disorder is most common?', '["Antibody to factor VIII","Factor V deficiency","Liver disease alone","Factor VII deficiency","Vitamin K deficiency"]'::jsonb, 'Vitamin K deficiency', 'Vitamin K deficiency occurs in neonates, biliary obstruction, malnutrition, and warfarin therapy.'),
('d5778ea1-4c88-5b74-91fd-048af20bbbfa'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Which factors are vitamin K-dependent?', '["Factors II, VII, IX, X, Protein C, Protein S","Factors VIII, IX, XI clinical presentation","Factors I, II, III clinical presentation","Factors V, VIII, XIII clinical presentation","All clotting factors clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Factors II, VII, IX, X, Protein C, Protein S', 'Vitamin K is cofactor for gamma-carboxylation of factors II, VII, IX, X and PC, PS.'),
('51d44250-6b4c-5272-bc51-5846182f20b8'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'In a surgical context, Warfarin acts by:', '["Factor Xa inhibition","Antithrombin activation","Direct thrombin inhibition","Inhibiting vitamin K epoxide reductase","Platelet inhibition"]'::jsonb, 'Inhibiting vitamin K epoxide reductase', 'Warfarin blocks VKORC1, preventing recycling of vitamin K and synthesis of functional factors II, VII, IX, X.'),
('957b77ac-6c36-5129-b1b3-2d8ff3b0c7cf'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Which factor has the shortest half-life and decreases first in warfarin therapy?', '["Factor II","Factor X","Factor VII","Factor IX","Protein C"]'::jsonb, 'Factor VII', 'FVII has shortest half-life. PT is sensitive due to FVII. Therapeutic effect requires FII/FX reduction (3-5 days).'),
('6c28adf2-6cb8-53f8-afab-08f245b6191c'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Factor V Leiden mutation causes:', '["Hemophilia B","Factor VIII deficiency","vWF deficiency","Factor V deficiency","Resistance to activated protein C"]'::jsonb, 'Resistance to activated protein C', 'Factor V Leiden: G1691A mutation makes FVa resistant to APC cleavage, causing thrombophilia.'),
('84db5165-a2c7-514b-bad6-0d85f625ab4b'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Which is the most common inherited thrombophilia in Caucasians?', '["Protein S deficiency","Factor V Leiden","Antithrombin deficiency","Prothrombin G20210A","Protein C deficiency"]'::jsonb, 'Factor V Leiden', 'FVL is most common inherited thrombophilia. Heterozygotes have 3-5x increased DVT risk.'),
('e1fe427b-5b5f-5129-86c1-8cbe9d99b06c'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Prothrombin G20210A mutation causes:', '["Anticoagulation","Bleeding","Increased prothrombin levels","No effect","Prothrombin deficiency"]'::jsonb, 'Increased prothrombin levels', '20210A mutation in 3''UTR increases prothrombin mRNA translation, causing elevated FII levels and thrombosis risk.'),
('aff41c14-9d87-5f7b-8e03-cdc3bf138cc5'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Antithrombin (AT) deficiency is associated with:', '["Arterial thrombosis only","Bleeding disorder","Recurrent venous thrombosis","DIC","Thrombocytopenia"]'::jsonb, 'Recurrent venous thrombosis', 'AT deficiency is a strong thrombophilia. 50% of heterozygotes have thrombosis by age 50.'),
('0890124a-0f26-536d-b723-9a5d73d0f67a'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Protein C and Protein S deficiency causes:', '["Both thrombosis and bleeding","Bleeding","No effect","Venous thrombosis","Arterial thrombosis"]'::jsonb, 'Venous thrombosis', 'PC/PS deficiency: 7-10x increased VTE risk. Warfarin skin necrosis due to rapid PC decline.'),
('6974bbdc-747e-5a36-8e3d-c120dc40da0d'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Which thrombophilia is associated with warfarin-induced skin necrosis?', '["Protein C deficiency","Factor V Leiden","Antithrombin deficiency","Prothrombin mutation","MTHFR mutation"]'::jsonb, 'Protein C deficiency', 'Warfarin causes rapid decline of PC (short half-life), causing skin necrosis in PC-deficient patients.'),
('8644893d-e31f-59c8-b4d1-66d168f68f3c'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Which thrombophilia screening test includes tests for lupus anticoagulant?', '["Prothrombin mutation","Factor V Leiden","MTHFR mutation","Antiphospholipid syndrome testing","Protein C activity"]'::jsonb, 'Antiphospholipid syndrome testing', 'APS: lupus anticoagulant (dRVVT), anticardiolipin IgG/IgM, anti-beta2-glycoprotein I antibodies.'),
('9fe68e11-3ac4-54d7-b64e-6742166002a7'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Hyperhomocysteinemia is associated with:', '["Only venous thrombosis","Arterial and venous thrombosis","Only arterial thrombosis","Bleeding disorder","No thrombotic risk"]'::jsonb, 'Arterial and venous thrombosis', 'Elevated homocysteine damages endothelium and increases thrombotic risk. Treat with folic acid, B12, B6.'),
('8876caff-2628-5044-9696-a1a7acdd094b'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'MTHFR C677T mutation is associated with:', '["No effect","Drug resistance","Mild hyperhomocysteinemia","Bleeding","Strong thrombophilia"]'::jsonb, 'Mild hyperhomocysteinemia', 'MTHFR mutation reduces enzyme activity, causing mild hyperhomocysteinemia. Routine thrombophilia testing no longer recommended.'),
('5d0fc0be-417f-5a36-bf09-60e1e234f3a0'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Liver disease causes coagulopathy due to:', '["Vitamin K deficiency alone","Increased consumption","Platelet dysfunction","Inhibitor production","Decreased synthesis of all clotting factors"]'::jsonb, 'Decreased synthesis of all clotting factors', 'Liver synthesizes all clotting factors except vWF. PT prolongation is earliest sign. Factor VIII is normal/high.'),
('5622a594-328c-50cb-8773-f4fc3131b1e2'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Which coagulation factor is elevated in liver disease?', '["Factor II","Factor IX","Factor VII","Factor X","Factor VIII"]'::jsonb, 'Factor VIII', 'FVIII is produced by sinusoidal endothelial cells, not hepatocytes. Normal or high in liver disease.'),
('3e9ec502-1924-5c37-891a-7a074ae8a8ff'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Vitamin K deficiency is characterized by:', '["Normal PT","Prolonged bleeding time","Prolonged PT","Thrombocytopenia","Prolonged aPTT only"]'::jsonb, 'Prolonged PT', 'PT is more sensitive than aPTT. Vitamin K deficiency responds to vitamin K within 24 hours.'),
('86a39d12-cff0-5e04-ae0f-68e8684b8807'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Which factor deficiency causes no bleeding (paradoxical thrombosis)?', '["Factor IX deficiency","Factor VIII deficiency","Factor VII deficiency","Factor X deficiency","Factor XII deficiency"]'::jsonb, 'Factor XII deficiency', 'Factor XII deficiency causes prolonged aPTT but no bleeding (in vitro artifact). Some have thrombosis.'),
('a7c189c4-c6b2-5e58-a19f-4c671ed30a9a'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Factor XIII deficiency is characterized by:', '["Petechiae clinical presentation","Delayed bleeding, poor wound healing, normal PT/aPTT","Hemarthrosis clinical presentation","Mucocutaneous bleeding clinical presentation","Thrombosis clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Delayed bleeding, poor wound healing, normal PT/aPTT', 'FXIII deficiency: impaired cross-linking of fibrin. Screening: clot solubility in 5M urea. Normal PT/aPTT.'),
('9b2f2144-6aaa-5cfa-9ddd-0df93dcfd6ec'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Which test is diagnostic for factor XIII deficiency?', '["Clot solubility test in 5M urea","TT clinical presentation","aPTT clinical presentation","Platelet count clinical presentation","PT clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Clot solubility test in 5M urea', 'FXIII cross-links fibrin. Without it, the clot is soluble in urea or acetic acid.'),
('2110f7bd-1257-552e-9a5b-66baaaf5e331'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Acquired hemophilia A is caused by:', '["von Willebrand disease","Congenital factor VIII deficiency","Platelet disorder","Autoantibodies against factor VIII","Liver disease"]'::jsonb, 'Autoantibodies against factor VIII', 'Acquired hemophilia: sudden bleeding, prolonged aPTT, mixing study does NOT correct (inhibitor). Associated with pregnancy, autoimmune, malignancy.'),
('b4f2c8a9-dff4-53f0-9031-001681c75e2f'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Which test differentiates acquired hemophilia from hemophilia A?', '["APTT mixing study","PT","Platelet count","Factor VIII level","Bleeding time"]'::jsonb, 'APTT mixing study', 'Inhibitor: immediate mixing study shows no correction. Hemophilia: immediate correction.'),
('bf9e0494-d3f5-51e0-9485-5dafd1f8d4d6'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Cryoprecipitate contains:', '["Platelets clinical presentation","Immunoglobulins only clinical presentation","Factor VIII, vWF, fibrinogen, FXIII, fibronectin","Albumin only clinical presentation","All clotting factors clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Factor VIII, vWF, fibrinogen, FXIII, fibronectin', 'Cryoprecipitate: FVIII, vWF, fibrinogen, FXIII. Used for vWD, hemophilia A, hypofibrinogenemia.'),
('519f3e15-c4ce-54fe-8b68-0a8e0aa3553f'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Fresh frozen plasma (FFP) contains:', '["Albumin only","Only vitamin K factors","All coagulation factors","Platelets and factors","Only factor VIII"]'::jsonb, 'All coagulation factors', 'FFP: all clotting factors, anticoagulant proteins (PC, PS, AT). Used for DIC, liver disease, warfarin reversal.'),
('223ede02-7312-504f-a06a-ec9fd4ac6179'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Prothrombin complex concentrate (PCC) contains:', '["All factors clinical presentation","Factor VIII clinical presentation","von Willebrand factor clinical presentation","Factors II, VII, IX, X, Protein C, Protein S","Factor IX only clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Factors II, VII, IX, X, Protein C, Protein S', 'PCC: vitamin K-dependent factors. Used for warfarin reversal (urgent) and hemophilia B.'),
('189280ae-a595-5544-acc9-88eac2fd18f1'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Recombinant factor VIIa (rFVIIa) is used for:', '["Hemophilia A without inhibitors clinical presentation","DIC clinical presentation","ITP clinical presentation","Hemophilia B clinical presentation","Hemophilia with inhibitors, warfarin reversal, surgical bleeding"]'::jsonb, 'Hemophilia with inhibitors, warfarin reversal, surgical bleeding', 'rFVIIa is a bypassing agent for hemophilia with inhibitors and major bleeding.'),
('c91b757a-9487-5eac-8f2a-c593c2baf3a9'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Tranexamic acid (TXA) acts by:', '["Increasing platelet function","Vitamin K replacement","Factor replacement","Inhibiting fibrinolysis","Enhancing coagulation"]'::jsonb, 'Inhibiting fibrinolysis', 'TXA is an antifibrinolytic used in menorrhagia, trauma, and dental procedures in hemophilia.'),
('4f5fc2c6-5635-5bb0-90c0-12e155d691db'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Aminocaproic acid is similar to TXA and is used for:', '["Hemophilia treatment","DIC","Hyperfibrinolysis","Thrombosis","Warfarin reversal"]'::jsonb, 'Hyperfibrinolysis', 'Antifibrinolytics are effective for hyperfibrinolytic states (e.g., post-prostatectomy, liver disease).'),
('05aeb6ca-1b65-5030-9c49-4b6948fe9790'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Which lab test is elevated in hyperfibrinolysis?', '["PT clinical presentation","aPTT clinical presentation","TT clinical presentation","D-dimer and fibrin degradation products","Platelet count clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'D-dimer and fibrin degradation products', 'D-dimer and FDP are elevated in DIC and primary hyperfibrinolysis (though FDPs alone less specific).'),
('f6151962-25e9-59b1-96a2-cbca50324b02'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Euglobulin clot lysis time (ECLT) is a test for:', '["Complement activity","vWF activity","Fibrinolysis","Platelet function","Coagulation factors"]'::jsonb, 'Fibrinolysis', 'Shortened ECLT (<60 min) suggests hyperfibrinolysis. Prolonged in hypofibrinolysis (thrombotic risk).'),
('e2a2e04c-4452-564b-a620-e1bc87ef6176'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Which coagulation factor is the ''thrombin receptor'' on platelets?', '["Thromboxane receptor","GPIb-IX","GPIIb-IIIa","ADP receptor","Protease-activated receptor"]'::jsonb, 'Protease-activated receptor', 'Thrombin activates platelets via PAR-1 (and PAR-4) cleavage. This is the main thrombin receptor on human platelets.'),
('b2b3bd76-987f-5c06-92f9-2ba0ae502d2e'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Which platelet aggregation inhibitor targets the ADP receptor (P2Y12)?', '["Dipyridamole","Abciximab","Clopidogrel","Tirofiban","Aspirin"]'::jsonb, 'Clopidogrel', 'Clopidogrel is a P2Y12 (ADP receptor) inhibitor. Aspirin inhibits cyclooxygenase. Abciximab blocks GPIIb-IIIa.'),
('85d73666-c4ef-5692-8921-6867fbe40f25'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Aspirin inhibits platelet aggregation by:', '["Blocking thromboxane receptor","Blocking GPIIb-IIIa","Blocking ADP receptor","Increasing cAMP","Irreversibly inhibiting cyclooxygenase-1"]'::jsonb, 'Irreversibly inhibiting cyclooxygenase-1', 'Aspirin acetylates COX-1, preventing thromboxane A2 synthesis and platelet aggregation.'),
('43750e03-e006-518d-a002-462c8b7b95d9'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Dipyridamole acts by:', '["Blocking ADP receptor","Blocking GPIIb-IIIa","Blocking COX-1","Inhibiting phosphodiesterase","Antagonizing vitamin K"]'::jsonb, 'Inhibiting phosphodiesterase', 'Dipyridamole inhibits PDE and blocks adenosine reuptake, increasing cAMP in platelets.'),
('505ad3a9-b1a5-5f42-8188-453dcff2549e'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Which platelet count is required before major surgery?', '[">100,000/uL",">20,000/uL",">10,000/uL",">150,000/uL",">50,000/uL"]'::jsonb, '>50,000/uL', 'Platelet count >50K is generally safe for major surgery. Neuraxial anesthesia requires >80K.'),
('2abf2c5c-2b38-5faa-b01f-600e8530593a'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Which blood product is given for thrombocytopenia?', '["PCC","Platelet transfusion","Cryoprecipitate","rFVIIa","FFP"]'::jsonb, 'Platelet transfusion', 'Platelet transfusion is indicated for: <10K stable, <20K with fever, <50K with bleeding/surgery.'),
('0b9e1f84-0017-57d0-b288-11e9d44d7cae'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'One unit of random donor platelets increases platelet count by approximately:', '["100,000/uL","20,000-30,000/uL","1,000-2,000/uL","50,000/uL","5,000-10,000/uL"]'::jsonb, '5,000-10,000/uL', 'One unit raises count by ~5-10K/uL. Pooled (4-6 units) raises by ~30-60K. Apheresis = 1 adult dose.'),
('eca0f5af-3760-57f9-9946-9fb0d28122e9'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Which situation is platelet transfusion contraindicated?', '["TTP and HIT","ITP","Chemotherapy-induced thrombocytopenia","Liver disease","DIC"]'::jsonb, 'TTP and HIT', 'Platelets are avoided in TTP (may worsen thrombosis) and HIT (risk of thrombosis).'),
('25575f9d-e675-5474-aa30-94227cacb216'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Which anticoagulant is used for patients with antiphospholipid syndrome?', '["Fondaparinux","DOACs","Warfarin","Heparin","Aspirin only"]'::jsonb, 'Warfarin', 'Warfarin is standard for thrombotic APS. DOACs (rivaroxaban) are associated with higher recurrence in triple-positive APS.'),
('db56e978-9fe7-5c38-a148-5c6b5a8ed191'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Which DOAC is a direct thrombin inhibitor?', '["Dabigatran","Apixaban","Edoxaban","Rivaroxaban","Fondaparinux"]'::jsonb, 'Dabigatran', 'Dabigatran (Pradaxa) directly inhibits thrombin. Rivaroxaban, apixaban, edoxaban are factor Xa inhibitors.'),
('415b061d-5718-5643-9d5d-7995e3578eb2'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Which agent reverses dabigatran?', '["FFP","Andexanet alfa","Protamine","Vitamin K","Idarucizumab"]'::jsonb, 'Idarucizumab', 'Idarucizumab is the specific reversal agent for dabigatran. Andexanet alfa reverses factor Xa inhibitors.'),
('356d55b3-47fb-5b3d-895b-545f40245be6'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Which factor Xa inhibitor reversal agent exists?', '["Idarucizumab","Tranexamic acid","Andexanet alfa","Protamine","Vitamin K"]'::jsonb, 'Andexanet alfa', 'Andexanet alfa (Andexxa) reverses rivaroxaban, apixaban, and edoxaban.'),
('33fb2fa8-7eff-5403-b3cb-5e0840781f9e'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Which immunoglobulin is elevated in chronic lymphocytic leukemia?', '["IgE","IgA","IgM","IgG","IgD"]'::jsonb, 'IgM', 'CLL: hypogammaglobulinemia with occasional IgM monoclonal gammopathy. Autoimmune hemolysis/thrombocytopenia may occur.'),
('1b0e36bf-54e0-544a-9eea-2dac85c8d3d8'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Which B-cell marker is positive in CLL?', '["CD34","CD138","CD10, CD19","CD5, CD19, CD23","CD20 only"]'::jsonb, 'CD5, CD19, CD23', 'CLL: CD5+ (T-cell marker coexpressed on B cells), CD19+, CD23+, CD20 weak.'),
('83ff2563-0ee2-5065-8e8f-6378a03ae232'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Which is the most common cytogenetic abnormality in CLL?', '["Trisomy 12","11q deletion","17p deletion","13q14 deletion","t"]'::jsonb, '13q14 deletion', '13q14 deletion is the most common (50-60%) and associated with favorable prognosis.'),
('d16b675c-948d-5e11-abea-fae5f47dbd17'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Which leukemia is characterized by positive TdT?', '["AML","CML","Hairy cell leukemia","CLL","ALL"]'::jsonb, 'ALL', 'TdT (terminal deoxynucleotidyl transferase) is expressed in lymphoblasts of ALL.'),
('685f08e2-ca78-569d-8644-106a65f8283b'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Which cytogenetic abnormality is characteristic of ALL-L3?', '["t","t(8;14) MYC-IgH","Hypodiploidy","t","Hyperdiploidy"]'::jsonb, 't(8;14) MYC-IgH', 'Burkitt ALL has MYC rearrangement, typically t(8;14).'),
('9adb0d50-81ae-5b63-8561-87d48d49c846'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Which chromosome abnormality is associated with AML M4Eo?', '["t","inv","t","t","t"]'::jsonb, 'inv', 'inv(16) creates CBFB-MYH11 fusion, characteristic of AML with eosinophilia.'),
('d3068451-2a61-5b14-81f7-a4be1e421888'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Which is the most common AML subtype in adults?', '["AML M5","AML M3","AML M4","AML M0","AML M2"]'::jsonb, 'AML M2', 'AML M2 is the most common subtype.'),
('690b87f6-1e54-562a-84ea-34cd20239e7f'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Which drug specifically targets BCR-ABL in CML?', '["Daunorubicin","Imatinib mesylate","Interferon-alpha","Hydroxyurea","Cytarabine"]'::jsonb, 'Imatinib mesylate', 'Imatinib targets the BCR-ABL oncoprotein, inducing molecular remission in CML.'),
('da7d95ad-61b9-5d78-88b0-a2196a5c4e84'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Which mutation in CML confers resistance to imatinib?', '["V617F clinical presentation","NPM1 mutation clinical presentation","FLT3-ITD clinical presentation","C282Y clinical presentation","T315I mutation in ABL kinase domain"]'::jsonb, 'T315I mutation in ABL kinase domain', 'T315I prevents imatinib binding. Ponatinib is active against T315I.'),
('5f407577-d054-5c5b-ad51-477513c41569'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Which is the blast phase of CML defined as?', '[">5% blasts",">20% blasts in blood or BM",">50% blasts",">30% blasts",">10% blasts"]'::jsonb, '>20% blasts in blood or BM', 'Blast phase: >20% blasts. Chronic <10%, accelerated 10-19%.'),
('e00c9a31-ffd8-52d8-91f5-6606f802c3f5'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Which cytogenetic abnormality is characteristic of CML?', '["t clinical presentation","t(9;22) Philadelphia chromosome","t clinical presentation","t clinical presentation","t clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 't(9;22) Philadelphia chromosome', 'Philadelphia chromosome t(9;22) creates BCR-ABL fusion gene.'),
('5c714c3d-326b-52d7-b12d-83aa1adbd5f2'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'What is the molecular consequence of t(9;22)?', '["BCR-ABL fusion with constitutive tyrosine kinase activity","PML-RARA fusion clinical presentation","BCL2-IgH fusion clinical presentation","MYC-IgH fusion clinical presentation","AML1-ETO fusion clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'BCR-ABL fusion with constitutive tyrosine kinase activity', 'BCR-ABL has uncontrolled tyrosine kinase activity causing proliferation.'),
('e2606557-f608-52f9-9690-b07462f35ce1'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Which is the most common MPN?', '["CML clinical marker","Primary myelofibrosis","CML is not an MPN","Polycythemia vera","Essential thrombocythemia"]'::jsonb, 'CML clinical marker', 'CML is an MPN. Among classic chronic MPNs: PV, ET, PMF.'),
('e277da4a-8d5b-5369-bc32-78f3ac5a2f64'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Which test distinguishes hemophilia A from vWD?', '["vWF antigen and activity","PT","Bleeding time","Factor VIII level","aPTT"]'::jsonb, 'vWF antigen and activity', 'Both have low FVIII, but vWD also has low vWF Ag and activity.'),
('6ad600dd-9be2-530b-a349-c1cec94eb9fa'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'What is the normal vWF antigen to activity ratio?', '["2:1","3:1","0.5-2.0","4:1","1:1"]'::jsonb, '0.5-2.0', 'vWF:RCo/vWF:Ag ratio <0.7 suggests qualitative defect (type 2).'),
('85e371de-63ea-5cc8-bb39-f3aecece8508'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Which is the most common type of hemophilia?', '["Hemophilia A","Factor XI deficiency","Hemophilia C","von Willebrand disease","Hemophilia B"]'::jsonb, 'Hemophilia A', 'Hemophilia A accounts for 80-85% of hemophilia cases.'),
('4be4a670-50ed-56e6-b89c-a023e93ddf25'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Which treatment is used for mild hemophilia A?', '["DDAVP","Tranexamic acid","FFP","Cryoprecipitate","Factor VIII concentrate"]'::jsonb, 'DDAVP', 'DDAVP raises FVIII 2-3x in mild hemophilia A.'),
('03ab4bda-387a-58f5-953b-03ee8b2e3c7e'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Which is the most common site of bleeding in hemophilia?', '["Genitourinary","CNS","GI tract","Knees, elbows, ankles","Skin"]'::jsonb, 'Knees, elbows, ankles', 'Weight-bearing joints are most commonly affected.'),
('8788aeb2-885a-5a55-8883-49da5feac716'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Which is the cause of death in severe hemophilia?', '["Joint disease","Renal failure","Viral infection","Exsanguination from surgery","Intracranial hemorrhage"]'::jsonb, 'Intracranial hemorrhage', 'CNS bleeding is the most common cause of death in hemophilia.'),
('d900b8cc-8d0a-5e87-9849-55d1ef83c40a'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'What is the most common cause of acquired factor VIII inhibitor?', '["Spontaneous","Factor VIII replacement","Infection","Blood transfusion","Medications"]'::jsonb, 'Spontaneous', 'Acquired hemophilia: idiopathic most common, associated with pregnancy, SLE, malignancy.'),
('226da5ef-70ae-5ff2-95ad-6a714c022e37'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Which treatment is used for bleeding in acquired hemophilia?', '["FFP","Cryoprecipitate","DDAVP","Factor VIII concentrate","Bypassing agents"]'::jsonb, 'Bypassing agents', 'Bypass agents manage bleeding. Immunosuppression eradicates inhibitor.'),
('84c749ad-b9c6-5067-bf3d-1e42fa725894'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Which factor deficiency is common in liver disease?', '["Factor XIII deficiency","vWF deficiency","Platelet defect","All except factor VIII","Factor VIII deficiency"]'::jsonb, 'All except factor VIII', 'Liver produces all factors except vWF. PT is earliest to prolong.'),
('79f3c434-c77f-5ee5-b5a2-a6e51bc1a8a4'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'What coagulopathy occurs in nephrotic syndrome?', '["Bleeding tendency","Factor VIII deficiency","Platelet dysfunction","Loss of ATIII and PC in urine","vWF deficiency"]'::jsonb, 'Loss of ATIII and PC in urine', 'Nephrotic: urinary loss of ATIII, PC, PS causes hypercoagulable state.'),
('7d0201dd-4982-50cf-8a20-2142d18801e3'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Which factor is deficient in amyloidosis?', '["Factor VII","Factor X deficiency","Factor VIII","vWF","Factor IX"]'::jsonb, 'Factor X deficiency', 'Amyloid fibrils adsorb factor X causing acquired FX deficiency.'),
('67d04a85-5a82-5ac0-8c07-170b4d5ef294'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Which factor deficiency causes isolated prolonged aPTT?', '["Factor V deficiency","Factor II deficiency","Factor VIII, IX, XI, XII deficiency","Factor VII deficiency","Factor X deficiency"]'::jsonb, 'Factor VIII, IX, XI, XII deficiency', 'Intrinsic pathway (FVIII, IX, XI, XII).'),
('3412ed73-27c5-5f1b-a714-7548c477be84'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Which factor deficiency causes isolated prolonged PT?', '["Factor IX deficiency","Factor XIII deficiency","Factor X deficiency","Factor VIII deficiency","Factor VII deficiency"]'::jsonb, 'Factor VII deficiency', 'Factor VII deficiency (autosomal recessive): prolonged PT, normal aPTT.'),
('55add077-c256-5e88-8f13-03b0875a9daf'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Which test is used for lupus anticoagulant?', '["Platelet count clinical presentation","PT clinical presentation","TT clinical presentation","dRVVT with phospholipid confirmation","aPTT clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'dRVVT with phospholipid confirmation', 'LA: dRVVT is most specific. Mixing and confirmation steps required.'),
('98c2f773-8fc1-527b-9327-fe7e912d37da'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Which is the strongest inherited thrombophilia?', '["Prothrombin mutation","Protein C deficiency","Protein S deficiency","Factor V Leiden","Antithrombin deficiency"]'::jsonb, 'Antithrombin deficiency', 'AT deficiency: 50% thrombotic risk by age 50.'),
('401a673d-30f3-52c9-9baf-ec5ab21cb570'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Which cancers are most associated with thrombosis?', '["Skin clinical presentation","Pancreatic, gastric, lung, ovarian, brain","Breast, prostate clinical presentation","Melanoma clinical presentation","Thyroid clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Pancreatic, gastric, lung, ovarian, brain', 'Trousseau syndrome: migratory thrombophlebitis with mucin-producing adenocarcinomas.'),
('60eacee1-d47d-5b65-9073-8bae367e1a3b'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Which anticoagulant is preferred for cancer-associated thrombosis?', '["Aspirin","Warfarin","Fondaparinux","DOACs","LMWH"]'::jsonb, 'LMWH', 'LMWH is preferred. DOACs increasingly used but caution in GI cancers.'),
('f5f5695e-1c38-5e2b-b699-5deeccdcbfe3'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Which is the most common thrombotic complication of pregnancy?', '["Placental thrombosis","Stroke","PE","DVT","MI"]'::jsonb, 'DVT', 'DVT in pregnancy: left leg affected due to compression of left iliac vein.'),
('e6e4264d-fec1-5c9c-b45d-d5896e7dd38d'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Which thrombophilia is associated with recurrent pregnancy loss?', '["MTHFR","Antiphospholipid syndrome","Prothrombin mutation","Factor V Leiden","Protein S deficiency"]'::jsonb, 'Antiphospholipid syndrome', 'APS: recurrent pregnancy loss. Treatment: LMWH plus aspirin.'),
('715f8303-e48f-56e8-8c2d-8077d6640ad2'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Which hormone therapy increases VTE risk?', '["Progestin-only","HRT low dose","Combined oral contraceptives","IUD","Vaginal estrogen"]'::jsonb, 'Combined oral contraceptives', 'Estrogen increases clotting factors. Third-generation OCPs have higher VTE risk.'),
('52f17f7c-604e-55ad-b96f-c806cbb4d5f4'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Which anticoagulant is most renally excreted?', '["Betrixaban","Rivaroxaban","Apixaban","Dabigatran","Edoxaban"]'::jsonb, 'Dabigatran', 'Dabigatran has the highest renal clearance.'),
('32f0ab9e-660b-5ca8-8eaa-b82d131f16f9'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Which DOAC is contraindicated with mechanical heart valves?', '["Dabigatran","Apixaban","Rivaroxaban","Edoxaban","All DOACs"]'::jsonb, 'Dabigatran', 'Dabigatran showed increased thromboembolic/bleeding events with mechanical valves.'),
('501a9778-58c6-530c-97b6-7dab1bd508df'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Which anticoagulant is safe in pregnancy?', '["Warfarin","Heparin","DOACs","Fondaparinux","Rivaroxaban"]'::jsonb, 'Heparin', 'Heparin/LMWH do not cross placenta. Warfarin is teratogenic.'),
('62908b5e-054d-5dc1-a1a5-b4788f135f3b'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Which test monitors LMWH?', '["Anti-factor Xa activity","aPTT","Platelet count","TT","PT"]'::jsonb, 'Anti-factor Xa activity', 'LMWH monitoring: anti-Xa. Not routinely needed except special situations.'),
('7e19b853-5924-590a-bf1d-2c464f652cf9'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Which is the antidote for heparin?', '["Vitamin K","Idarucizumab","Tranexamic acid","Protamine sulfate","FFP"]'::jsonb, 'Protamine sulfate', 'Protamine completely reverses UFH, partially reverses LMWH.'),
('f038201a-044c-5221-a2ae-bc49b5c78237'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Which is the most common acquired thrombophilia?', '["Oral contraceptives","Immobilization","Cancer","Antiphospholipid syndrome","Pregnancy"]'::jsonb, 'Antiphospholipid syndrome', 'APS is the most common acquired thrombophilia.'),
('8716444e-ed20-56cf-99d2-ec9b65534396'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Which APL antibody confers highest thrombosis risk?', '["All equal","Lupus anticoagulant","Anticardiolipin","Anti-prothrombin","Anti-beta2GPI"]'::jsonb, 'Lupus anticoagulant', 'LA has strongest association. Triple-positive has highest risk.'),
('6fc0583f-4a38-5307-b971-8ba2c13883f3'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Which cell produces vWF?', '["Hepatocytes clinical presentation","Platelets only clinical presentation","Endothelial cells and megakaryocytes","Fibroblasts clinical presentation","Macrophages clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Endothelial cells and megakaryocytes', 'vWF is stored in Weibel-Palade bodies of endothelium and alpha granules of platelets.'),
('7e25b3e6-7947-55d0-b28b-48739819faa3'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'What is the function of vWF?', '["Fibrin polymerization","Platelet adhesion via GPIb-IX","Coagulation activation","Fibrinolysis","Complement activation"]'::jsonb, 'Platelet adhesion via GPIb-IX', 'vWF bridges platelet GPIb-IX to exposed collagen.'),
('5281b71e-4e08-547a-8831-115c5e514363'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Which vWF multimer pattern in type 2B?', '["Normal multimers clinical presentation","All present clinical presentation","Absent clinical presentation","Only low MW clinical presentation","Loss of high MW multimers with thrombocytopenia"]'::jsonb, 'Loss of high MW multimers with thrombocytopenia', 'Type 2B: gain-of-function mutation depletes high MW multimers.'),
('27040554-9863-5afb-b130-3382644fe178'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Which treatment is contraindicated in type 2B vWD?', '["DDAVP clinical marker","Platelet transfusion","vWF concentrate","Cryoprecipitate","Tranexamic acid"]'::jsonb, 'DDAVP clinical marker', 'DDAVP can worsen thrombocytopenia in type 2B.'),
('d357ce1c-985b-5dad-9fd9-502b5a2db65f'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'What is the treatment for vWD type 3?', '["DDAVP","Platelet transfusion","FFP","Cryoprecipitate","vWF-containing FVIII concentrate"]'::jsonb, 'vWF-containing FVIII concentrate', 'Type 3: DDAVP ineffective. Use plasma-derived vWF concentrate.'),
('12503570-9ee8-5399-86db-682766063234'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'What is the half-life of transfused platelets?', '["10-14 days","5-7 days","7-10 days","3-5 days","1-2 days"]'::jsonb, '3-5 days', 'Normal platelet lifespan 7-10 days. Transfused: 3-5 days.'),
('d00ddfc8-d582-55ac-ba84-aa6f5011c35c'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Most common cause of platelet refractoriness?', '["Medications","Underlying disease","Non-immune","Platelet quality","Immune only"]'::jsonb, 'Non-immune', 'Non-immune causes more common than alloimmunization.'),
('7bb83fe1-0331-5117-a3c6-071d8ac0f363'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'How to manage platelet alloimmunization?', '["HLA-matched or crossmatched platelets","IVIG","Higher dose random","Rituximab","Random donor platelets"]'::jsonb, 'HLA-matched or crossmatched platelets', 'Alloimmunized patients need HLA-compatible platelets.'),
('ca7e5aec-0cfa-54f4-abf3-ff13deed1f06'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Most common cause of thrombocytopenia in ICU?', '["TTP","HIT","Sepsis and DIC","ITP","Drug-induced"]'::jsonb, 'Sepsis and DIC', 'Sepsis is the most common cause of thrombocytopenia in ICU.'),
('b5fec03f-b620-514a-b540-94310096029e'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Most common bleeding disorder in women?', '["Platelet disorder","Factor XI deficiency","Thrombocytopenia","von Willebrand disease","Hemophilia carrier"]'::jsonb, 'von Willebrand disease', 'vWD causes menorrhagia and easy bruising.'),
('9d44b785-385b-5bfd-8b25-b9f2a7163661'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Most common cause of menorrhagia in adolescents?', '["Endometriosis","Infection","Malignancy","Coagulation disorder","Uterine fibroids"]'::jsonb, 'Coagulation disorder', 'In adolescents, 10-20% of menorrhagia is due to vWD.'),
('a3c2b563-7c64-500e-ae7d-b8f1ee8725e8'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Which test is most sensitive for vWD type 1?', '["vWF antigen and ristocetin cofactor","aPTT clinical presentation","Factor VIII clinical presentation","PFA-100 clinical presentation","Bleeding time clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'vWF antigen and ristocetin cofactor', 'Both vWF:Ag and vWF:RCo are needed for highest sensitivity.'),
('96e57e86-f94e-5869-ad9f-98dc16987969'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Which FVIII product is for hemophilia with inhibitors?', '["Recombinant FVIIa or aPCC","Cryoprecipitate","High-dose FVIII","FFP","Desmopressin"]'::jsonb, 'Recombinant FVIIa or aPCC', 'Bypassing agents manage acute bleeds with high-titer inhibitors.'),
('6c0d6750-d793-5c9e-9c95-226c02fd60aa'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'What is Bethesda titer used for?', '["Measuring vWF activity","Measuring platelet antibodies","Quantifying protein C","Measuring D-dimer","Quantifying FVIII inhibitor titer"]'::jsonb, 'Quantifying FVIII inhibitor titer', '1 BU neutralizes 50% of FVIII in normal plasma.'),
('4c856b91-f874-5680-ae18-f0675f313c27'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'What is immune tolerance induction (ITI)?', '["Bypass therapy clinical presentation","Porcine FVIII clinical presentation","Regular high-dose FVIII to eradicate inhibitor","Immunosuppression clinical presentation","Gene therapy clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Regular high-dose FVIII to eradicate inhibitor', 'ITI: regular high-dose FVIII over months-years to eradicate inhibitor.'),
('098b4a2c-de96-5d1d-a196-25670fe7a25a'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Most common complication of hemophilia treatment?', '["Bleeding despite treatment","Arthropathy","Viral infection","Catheter infection","Development of inhibitors"]'::jsonb, 'Development of inhibitors', 'Inhibitors develop in ~20-30% of severe hemophilia A.'),
('4ebe5e10-3773-58dd-bfdf-6bfd2470dc5a'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Most common acquired bleeding disorder in elderly?', '["DIC","Acquired hemophilia A","Vitamin K deficiency","Myelodysplasia","Liver disease"]'::jsonb, 'Acquired hemophilia A', 'Acquired hemophilia A presents with spontaneous bleeding in elderly.'),
('2a089716-85e7-5a63-ac7b-a316589c5450'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Which coagulation factor is elevated in hyperthyroidism?', '["Factor VII","Factor X","Factor VIII and vWF","Factor IX","Factor II"]'::jsonb, 'Factor VIII and vWF', 'Hyperthyroidism increases FVIII and vWF causing mild hypercoagulability.'),
('a4e6aae9-9bf5-581f-8734-20fbcd1a4006'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Which condition is associated with acquired protein C deficiency?', '["Pregnancy","Nephrotic syndrome","Warfarin therapy","Liver disease","Infection"]'::jsonb, 'Warfarin therapy', 'Warfarin induces initial hypercoagulable state via rapid PC decline (warfarin necrosis).'),
('8aefb216-5767-5210-adb5-e393d747b792'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'What is the most common thrombophilia in patients with first DVT?', '["Prothrombin mutation","Protein S deficiency","Factor V Leiden","Antithrombin deficiency","Protein C deficiency"]'::jsonb, 'Factor V Leiden', 'FVL is most common. But testing for thrombophilia after first proximal DVT is controversial.'),
('63eaf335-b732-54e6-9600-947d5fd295ae'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Which patient group benefits most from thrombophilia testing?', '["Young (<50) with unprovoked VTE or family history","Catheter-related DVT clinical presentation","Elderly clinical presentation","All first DVT clinical presentation","Post-surgical DVT clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Young (<50) with unprovoked VTE or family history', 'Testing is most useful in young patients with unprovoked VTE, family history, or recurrent VTE.'),
('bf6c04e8-2c1f-5de5-be54-7a2ee937de2a'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Which is the most common site of VTE associated with antiphospholipid syndrome?', '["DVT of lower extremities","PE","Portal vein thrombosis","Renal vein thrombosis","Cerebral venous sinus thrombosis"]'::jsonb, 'DVT of lower extremities', 'DVT (legs) is most common. Unusual sites (cerebral, portal) suggest APS.'),
('3e054bad-1f3d-558e-a4c8-9a37c4638a23'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Which treatment is used for catastrophic APS (CAPS)?', '["Aspirin alone clinical presentation","Anticoagulation, steroids, plasma exchange, rituximab","Heparin alone clinical presentation","Warfarin alone clinical presentation","Steroids alone clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Anticoagulation, steroids, plasma exchange, rituximab', 'CAPS is a life-threatening condition requiring multimodal therapy.'),
('c831f545-a371-59af-8e0d-af5d102c21e0'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Which is the most common cause of DIC in obstetrics?', '["Placental abruption","Amniotic fluid embolism","Retained fetus","Septic abortion","Preeclampsia/eclampsia"]'::jsonb, 'Placental abruption', 'Placental abruption is the most common obstetric cause of DIC.'),
('8c3dae77-0ac5-505c-9d2f-772dccfc26eb'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Which factor deficiency causes hemarthrosis in mild trauma?', '["vWD","Factor XIII deficiency","Platelet disorder","Factor VIII deficiency","Factor VII deficiency"]'::jsonb, 'Factor VIII deficiency', 'Hemophilia A causes deep joint bleeds. vWD causes mucocutaneous bleeding.'),
('e63a3e31-b3d7-5b3d-99cc-23bd984ac237'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Which blood product is richest in fibrinogen?', '["FFP","Cryoprecipitate","rFVIIa","PCC","Platelets"]'::jsonb, 'Cryoprecipitate', 'Cryoprecipitate: >150 mg fibrinogen per unit. Used for hypofibrinogenemia.'),
('006b8adf-dda4-5294-8b47-0a73e8111f58'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Which coagulation factor is most heat-stable?', '["Factor X","Factor V","Factor VII","Factor IX","Factor VIII"]'::jsonb, 'Factor VIII', 'vWF binds and stabilizes FVIII. FVIII is relatively labile without vWF.'),
('5c6f4a59-bd32-546b-bc20-b27c85ed0a90'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Which is the most common cause of perioperative bleeding?', '["Liver disease","vWD","Surgical cause","Platelet dysfunction from drugs","DIC"]'::jsonb, 'Surgical cause', 'Surgical bleeding is most common. Coagulation workup if unexpected or diffuse oozing.'),
('fd465fd7-8942-555c-a929-90797bc897f5'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Which drug interferes with platelet function most commonly?', '["NSAIDs","ACE inhibitors","Statins","Beta blockers","Antibiotics"]'::jsonb, 'NSAIDs', 'NSAIDs inhibit COX-1, impairing thromboxane A2 synthesis and platelet aggregation.'),
('a6b9f922-c0f2-51a8-99fc-36868486337c'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'How long does aspirin effect on platelets last?', '["72 hours","7-10 days","5 days","48 hours","24 hours"]'::jsonb, '7-10 days', 'Aspirin irreversibly acetylates COX-1 for platelet lifespan (7-10 days).'),
('84eee91b-f91b-59f3-b056-07bb32296eb4'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Which test evaluates platelet function?', '["Platelet aggregation studies","PFA-100","All of the above","Bleeding time","Thromboelastography"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Multiple tests available. Aggregometry is gold standard for qualitative platelet disorders.'),
('c3f6b843-c938-5b78-aef7-1d8d79bef187'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Which is the most common type of acute leukemia in adults?', '["Hairy cell leukemia","CLL","ALL","AML","CML"]'::jsonb, 'AML', 'AML is the most common acute leukemia in adults. ALL is most common in children.'),
('3172cf9b-24dc-5001-b1be-1943b8c43aa8'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'Which is the most common type of acute leukemia in children?', '["ALL","AML","CLL","CML","Juvenile myelomonocytic leukemia"]'::jsonb, 'ALL', 'ALL accounts for 80% of childhood leukemias. Peak age 2-5 years.'),
('1a67b8a5-4235-5307-b93b-e25348be686b'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'What is the most common cause of anemia in the elderly?', '["Iron deficiency","Myelodysplasia","Vitamin B12 deficiency","Hemolytic anemia","Anemia of chronic disease"]'::jsonb, 'Anemia of chronic disease', 'ACD is most common in elderly due to chronic inflammatory conditions. B12 deficiency is also common.'),
('2529faa8-1328-5ed1-89f6-01bb4bd85b06'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Hematology', 'The most common cause of iron deficiency anemia in adults worldwide is:', '["Poor dietary intake","Hookworm infection","Malabsorption","Chronic blood loss","Pregnancy"]'::jsonb, 'Chronic blood loss', 'In adults, chronic blood loss is the most common cause. Men: GI source (ulcer, cancer, angiodysplasia). Women: menorrhagia. Hookworm is common in developing countries.'),
('e1766cc4-27f6-5405-b79c-e6dfce9796f5'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Which is the most common type of lymphoma worldwide?', '["Follicular lymphoma","Diffuse large B-cell lymphoma","Hodgkin lymphoma","MALT lymphoma","Burkitt lymphoma"]'::jsonb, 'Diffuse large B-cell lymphoma', 'DLBCL is the most common lymphoma, accounting for 30-40% of all non-Hodgkin lymphomas.'),
('afae83f4-c13e-5daf-90e4-5e3f14b85459'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'What is the hallmark cell of Hodgkin lymphoma?', '["Reed-Sternberg cell","Plasma cell","Small B lymphocyte","Immunoblast","Centroblast"]'::jsonb, 'Reed-Sternberg cell', 'Reed-Sternberg cells are large binucleate cells with prominent nucleoli, pathognomonic for Hodgkin lymphoma.'),
('e0243bcf-745a-5242-a1b6-93efe38142fc'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Which immunophenotype is typical of classical Hodgkin lymphoma?', '["CD15+, CD30+, CD45-","CD3+, CD5+","CD20+, CD45+","CD138+, CD38+","CD34+, TdT+"]'::jsonb, 'CD15+, CD30+, CD45-', 'Classic Hodgkin RS cells are CD15+ and CD30+ but lack CD45 and B/T cell markers.'),
('ce900f06-77ba-5a52-85b7-3ab095b04dd2'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Nodular lymphocyte predominant Hodgkin lymphoma (NLPHL) phenotype:', '["CD34+ clinical presentation","CD20+, CD45+, EMA+, CD15-, CD30-","CD138+ clinical presentation","CD15+, CD30+ clinical presentation","CD3+, CD5+ clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'CD20+, CD45+, EMA+, CD15-, CD30-', 'NLPHL cells (LP cells/popcorn cells) express B-cell markers and lack CD15/CD30.'),
('363360a6-830f-533d-a217-33bb82cea321'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Which subtype of Hodgkin lymphoma has the best prognosis?', '["Lymphocyte rich classic","Nodular sclerosis","Lymphocyte depleted","Lymphocyte predominant","Mixed cellularity"]'::jsonb, 'Lymphocyte predominant', 'Lymphocyte predominant HL has the best prognosis. Lymphocyte depleted has the worst.'),
('b930fab4-d8ca-5a5a-8504-67042401a4bf'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'The most common Hodgkin lymphoma subtype in young adults:', '["Nodular sclerosis","Lymphocyte rich","Mixed cellularity","Lymphocyte depleted","Lymphocyte predominant"]'::jsonb, 'Nodular sclerosis', 'Nodular sclerosis HL is most common in young adults, often with mediastinal involvement.'),
('c496c709-a1e2-5c34-b9a8-0aeb8df21045'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Which virus is strongly associated with Hodgkin lymphoma?', '["HPV","HHV-8","HIV","EBV","HTLV-1"]'::jsonb, 'EBV', 'EBV is found in RS cells of ~40% HL, most often in mixed cellularity and HIV-associated cases.'),
('bfaf33b6-7c47-5bcc-bda2-8f960bbedd68'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Which Bcl-2 overexpression is characteristic of follicular lymphoma?', '["t(14;18) BCL2-IgH","t(2;5) ALK","t(8;14) MYC","t(9;22) BCR-ABL","t(11;14) cyclin D1"]'::jsonb, 't(14;18) BCL2-IgH', 't(14;18) places BCL-2 near Ig heavy chain, causing overexpression and blocking apoptosis.'),
('6c72ac93-9e16-527d-b95a-b8a1b618ac00'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'On diagnostic evaluation, Follicular lymphoma is associated with:', '["t","t","t","t","t"]'::jsonb, 't', 't(14;18) is found in ~85% of follicular lymphomas, causing BCL2 overexpression.'),
('999a1f1a-7a15-50da-aa77-34639cbb6a96'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Which grade of follicular lymphoma has aggressive behavior?', '["Grade 1 worst","Grade 2","Grade 1","Grade 3","All equal"]'::jsonb, 'Grade 3', 'FL Grade 3 has >15 centroblasts/hpf and behaves more aggressively.'),
('0c089998-e93a-5c4b-b9b5-297d37bf9455'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Which translocation is characteristic of mantle cell lymphoma?', '["t","t","t","t","t(11;14) cyclin D1"]'::jsonb, 't(11;14) cyclin D1', 't(11;14) places cyclin D1 under IgH promoter, causing cyclin D1 overexpression.'),
('7077c15d-6799-5264-8fa7-cc56ba9fdad5'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Which immunostain is positive in mantle cell lymphoma?', '["CD138","TdT","Cyclin D1, CD5, CD20","CD10, Bcl-6","CD23, CD5"]'::jsonb, 'Cyclin D1, CD5, CD20', 'MCL: cyclin D1+, CD5+, CD20+, CD23-, CD10-.'),
('d635d658-c765-5d14-9baa-7b4154b3e4ee'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Marginal zone lymphoma (MALT) is associated with:', '["EBV clinical presentation","HTLV-1 clinical presentation","HPV clinical presentation","HHV-8 clinical presentation","H. pylori and chronic inflammation"]'::jsonb, 'H. pylori and chronic inflammation', 'MALT lymphomas arise from chronic inflammation: H. pylori gastritis, Hashimoto thyroiditis.'),
('5437fdc4-33fb-5c60-be5d-cdc131b09051'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Which translocation is associated with MALT lymphoma?', '["t","t","t","t(11;18) API2-MALT1","t"]'::jsonb, 't(11;18) API2-MALT1', 't(11;18) creates API2-MALT1 fusion. Other translocations involve BCL-10 and FOXP1.'),
('8ba972f4-a772-5f09-93bd-2daa96ce451e'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Which treatment can cure early gastric MALT lymphoma?', '["Rituximab","H. pylori eradication","Surgery","Radiation","Chemotherapy"]'::jsonb, 'H. pylori eradication', 'Early gastric MALT (H. pylori+) can be cured with antibiotics alone in ~75%.'),
('7c15eca0-08af-5dfe-8bf8-df60080e4ed5'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Burkitt lymphoma is characterized by:', '["t(9;22) BCR-ABL clinical presentation","t(2;5) ALK clinical presentation","t(11;14) cyclin D1 clinical presentation","t(14;18) BCL2 clinical presentation","t(8;14) MYC, high Ki67, starry-sky pattern"]'::jsonb, 't(8;14) MYC, high Ki67, starry-sky pattern', 'Burkitt: MYC translocation, very high Ki67 (>95%), starry-sky pattern with tingible-body macrophages.'),
('ebd8316c-a0c6-5f9a-9275-af57a1ad19db'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Endemic Burkitt lymphoma is associated with:', '["HTLV-1","EBV and malaria","HHV-8","HIV","HPV"]'::jsonb, 'EBV and malaria', 'Endemic Burkitt: EBV+ in malaria-endemic Africa, typically presents with jaw mass.'),
('1d8a77e1-9838-5dc9-b3a5-509f6c035844'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Adult T-cell leukemia/lymphoma is caused by:', '["HIV","HPV","EBV","HTLV-1","HHV-8"]'::jsonb, 'HTLV-1', 'ATLL is caused by HTLV-1, endemic in Japan and Caribbean. Hypercalcemia is characteristic.'),
('d451c4fa-24c3-5501-aa93-94e459839580'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Primary effusion lymphoma is associated with:', '["HPV","HHV-8","HIV","EBV","HTLV-1"]'::jsonb, 'HHV-8', 'HHV-8 causes primary effusion lymphoma and multicentric Castleman disease.'),
('b026028b-271f-5351-ade4-3a537baa9155'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Enteropathy-associated T-cell lymphoma is associated with:', '["Celiac disease","EBV","H. pylori","HTLV-1","HIV"]'::jsonb, 'Celiac disease', 'EATL arises in patients with refractory celiac disease, often with ulcerative jejunitis.'),
('a5f758fa-034d-51fa-8259-e9be69d8059e'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Mycosis fungoides is a cutaneous T-cell lymphoma presenting with:', '["Leukemia","Erythroderma only","Patches, plaques, then tumors","Lymphadenopathy only","Visceral involvement"]'::jsonb, 'Patches, plaques, then tumors', 'Mycosis fungoides: CD4+ T cells in skin, patch -> plaque -> tumor stage.'),
('6bbf7019-5317-538c-b454-36232242d6da'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Sezary syndrome presents with:', '["Erythroderma, lymphadenopathy, and circulating Sezary cells","Leukemia only clinical presentation","Bone lesions clinical presentation","Skin patches clinical presentation","Lymphoma only clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Erythroderma, lymphadenopathy, and circulating Sezary cells', 'Sezary syndrome: triad of erythroderma, lymphadenopathy, and >1000 Sezary cells/uL.'),
('8256cfe2-42e5-5dd4-ab67-ceea2a1ba3b3'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Anaplastic large cell lymphoma is characterized by:', '["CD20+, CD10+ clinical presentation","CD30+, ALK+ (t(2;5) ALK rearrangement)","CD138+ clinical presentation","CD5+, Cyclin D1+ clinical presentation","CD15+, CD30+ clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'CD30+, ALK+ (t(2;5) ALK rearrangement)', 'ALCL: CD30+ (strong uniform), ALK+ in ~50%. Hallmark cells with horseshoe nuclei.'),
('2383898b-4ba6-5b1d-a2e4-a97ef1ccfa6f'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Post-transplant lymphoproliferative disorder (PTLD) is:', '["Plasma cell neoplasm clinical presentation","Hodgkin lymphoma clinical presentation","EBV-driven B-cell proliferation in immunosuppressed transplant recipients","T-cell lymphoma clinical presentation","Leukemia clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'EBV-driven B-cell proliferation in immunosuppressed transplant recipients', 'PTLD is usually EBV-driven B-cell proliferation. Ranges from polyclonal to monomorphic lymphoma.'),
('d4f3c58a-e14e-5590-a7fb-c76571e8a9ef'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Which lymphoma has poorest prognosis among B-cell NHL?', '["MALT","CLL","Mantle cell lymphoma","Lymphoplasmacytic","Follicular grade 1"]'::jsonb, 'Mantle cell lymphoma', 'MCL is aggressive (median survival 3-5 years). Blastoid variant <2 years.'),
('f5486749-60c8-5592-be4b-adc91a5247d3'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Which lymphoma has best prognosis among NHL?', '["Mantle cell","Burkitt","DLBCL","Peripheral T-cell","MALT lymphoma"]'::jsonb, 'MALT lymphoma', 'MALT is indolent. Early gastric MALT can be cured with H. pylori eradication.'),
('e3ece5eb-915e-5aae-95e0-7ddefbf17bdf'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Which is the most common NHL in children?', '["Lymphoblastic lymphoma","DLBCL","Burkitt lymphoma","Anaplastic large cell","Follicular"]'::jsonb, 'Burkitt lymphoma', 'Burkitt lymphoma is the most common NHL in children, often presenting with abdominal mass.'),
('43982a7a-f4e2-51ce-9282-2c39e0ea767f'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Lymphoblastic lymphoma is most commonly of which lineage?', '["NK-cell","T-cell","Histiocytic","Hodgkin","B-cell"]'::jsonb, 'T-cell', 'T-lymphoblastic lymphoma presents as anterior mediastinal mass in adolescents.'),
('4e26da41-74cd-5c00-9759-7b27aa68316f'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Splenic marginal zone lymphoma presents with:', '["Bone disease clinical presentation","Lymphadenopathy clinical presentation","Skin lesions clinical presentation","Mediastinal mass clinical presentation","Massive splenomegaly with villous lymphocytes"]'::jsonb, 'Massive splenomegaly with villous lymphocytes', 'SMZL: splenomegaly, circulating villous lymphocytes, indolent course.'),
('8c883bf6-5565-52b2-85a0-c4336003d78e'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Which B-cell lymphoma is associated with hepatitis C?', '["Follicular","Lymphoplasmacytic lymphoma","MALT","DLBCL","Burkitt"]'::jsonb, 'Lymphoplasmacytic lymphoma', 'HCV is associated with mixed cryoglobulinemia and lymphoplasmacytic lymphoma.'),
('87c5c6de-fd18-5f08-b55d-718a1e5a4c02'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Which is the most common cancer worldwide?', '["Colorectal cancer","Lung cancer","Breast cancer","Prostate cancer","Stomach cancer"]'::jsonb, 'Lung cancer', 'Lung cancer is the most common and most deadly cancer worldwide.'),
('a5de4232-1aee-5c7d-84bc-235781d15377'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Which is the most common cancer in women worldwide?', '["Lung cancer","Breast cancer","Ovarian cancer","Cervical cancer","Colorectal cancer"]'::jsonb, 'Breast cancer', 'Breast cancer is the most frequently diagnosed cancer and leading cause of cancer death in women.'),
('0c0a6a39-c6f7-5005-81f1-34e5cbbd8e92'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Which is the most common primary brain tumor in adults?', '["Oligodendroglioma","Astrocytoma","Ependymoma","Meningioma","Glioblastoma multiforme"]'::jsonb, 'Glioblastoma multiforme', 'GBM is the most common (50% of glial tumors) and most aggressive primary brain tumor.'),
('9c15002d-a433-5c82-8814-8b7f54d91121'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Which is the most common pediatric brain tumor?', '["Craniopharyngioma","Pilocytic astrocytoma","Medulloblastoma","Ependymoma","Brainstem glioma"]'::jsonb, 'Pilocytic astrocytoma', 'Pilocytic astrocytoma is most common. Medulloblastoma is most common malignant brain tumor in children.'),
('b8eecc89-c3f1-5db2-9cdc-dc6bd8ac1150'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Which lung cancer subtype is most common overall?', '["Small cell","Large cell","Carcinoid","Adenocarcinoma","Squamous cell"]'::jsonb, 'Adenocarcinoma', 'Adenocarcinoma has surpassed squamous as most common lung cancer, especially in non-smokers.'),
('43468775-a3d1-57eb-bdf0-bb57c832ac35'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Which lung cancer is most associated with smoking?', '["Mesothelioma clinical presentation","Adenocarcinoma clinical presentation","Squamous cell and small cell carcinoma","Large cell clinical presentation","Carcinoid clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Squamous cell and small cell carcinoma', 'SCC and SCLC have the strongest smoking association. Adenocarcinoma is most common in non-smokers.'),
('b8e5e6d5-1790-52ec-8d3e-447a754b1cf5'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Which lung cancer is associated with paraneoplastic hypercalcemia?', '["Adenocarcinoma","Squamous cell carcinoma","Small cell","Large cell","Mesothelioma"]'::jsonb, 'Squamous cell carcinoma', 'SCC of lung produces PTHrP causing humoral hypercalcemia of malignancy.'),
('bc9fa98a-21dc-50e2-8bd7-4a9782f9a42b'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Which lung cancer is associated with SIADH and LEMS?', '["Small cell carcinoma","Large cell","Squamous cell","Mesothelioma","Adenocarcinoma"]'::jsonb, 'Small cell carcinoma', 'SCLC is neuroendocrine and produces ADH (SIADH) and anti-VGCC antibodies (LEMS).'),
('393d0496-ba6f-5f2c-8190-6204c264bec8'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Which oncogene mutation is common in lung adenocarcinoma?', '["EGFR, KRAS, ALK, ROS1","KRAS only","ALK only","p53 only","EGFR only"]'::jsonb, 'EGFR, KRAS, ALK, ROS1', 'Lung adenocarcinoma commonly has EGFR, KRAS, ALK, ROS1, and BRAF mutations.'),
('19e367d9-a2e8-5ab2-a433-8e0e3437ab65'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Which is the most common esophageal cancer worldwide?', '["Adenocarcinoma","Melanoma","Squamous cell carcinoma","Small cell","Lymphoma"]'::jsonb, 'Squamous cell carcinoma', 'SCC is most common worldwide, especially in Asia. Adenocarcinoma is increasing in Western countries.'),
('f31efd4c-978d-5bd9-a575-e31e3ed71cf8'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Which histologic type of gastric cancer has worse prognosis?', '["Intestinal type","Tubular","Papillary","Mucinous","Diffuse type"]'::jsonb, 'Diffuse type', 'Diffuse type (poorly cohesive, signet ring) has worse prognosis than intestinal type.'),
('f0ca43dc-ce73-5a30-bb23-c7573855fa25'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Which genetic mutation is associated with hereditary diffuse gastric cancer?', '["KRAS","MLH1","APC","TP53","CDH1 (E-cadherin) mutation"]'::jsonb, 'CDH1 (E-cadherin) mutation', 'CDH1 germline mutation causes hereditary diffuse gastric cancer and lobular breast cancer.'),
('618bdc41-11ec-5806-8cca-256842eb1d62'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Which is the most common pancreatic malignancy?', '["Pancreaticoblastoma","Pancreatic neuroendocrine tumor","Solid pseudopapillary tumor","Acinar cell","Pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma"]'::jsonb, 'Pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma', 'PDAC accounts for >85% of pancreatic cancers. 5-year survival <5%.'),
('891ea479-ec80-5f70-9ccd-21569bf283c0'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Which mutation is most common in pancreatic cancer?', '["CDKN2A","SMAD4","KRAS","BRCA2","TP53"]'::jsonb, 'KRAS', 'KRAS mutation at codon 12 is earliest and most common in PDAC (>90%).'),
('6a408941-8e93-547f-82b2-47971af729c8'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Which is the most common primary liver malignancy?', '["Hepatoblastoma","Cholangiocarcinoma","Metastatic","Hepatocellular carcinoma","Angiosarcoma"]'::jsonb, 'Hepatocellular carcinoma', 'HCC is the most common primary liver cancer, >80% of cases.'),
('acceb7b6-6afd-551c-a991-2fe76e57a256'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Which risk factor is most common for HCC in Pakistan?', '["NAFLD","HBV","Aflatoxin","Chronic HCV","Alcohol"]'::jsonb, 'Chronic HCV', 'HCV is the leading cause of chronic liver disease and HCC in Pakistan.'),
('bea23788-287c-5b1b-a925-27d80fb27dc7'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Which is the most common type of cholangiocarcinoma?', '["Perihilar","Ampullary","Distal extrahepatic","Intrahepatic","Gallbladder"]'::jsonb, 'Perihilar', 'Perihilar CCA at bile duct bifurcation accounts for ~50% of cholangiocarcinomas.'),
('ee9604ee-6131-5c6d-a0bb-1cb8e7dc3a18'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Which is the most common colorectal cancer pathway?', '["All equal","MSI pathway","Serrated pathway","CIMP pathway","Chromosomal instability"]'::jsonb, 'Chromosomal instability', 'CIN pathway accounts for ~85% of sporadic CRC. MSI accounts for ~15%.'),
('e0140c19-e23e-5483-92d0-e6e83ddc6055'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Which hereditary syndrome causes most hereditary CRC?', '["Peutz-Jeghers clinical presentation","Turcot syndrome clinical presentation","Lynch syndrome (HNPCC) - 3-5% of CRC","FAP - <1% clinical presentation","Gardner syndrome clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Lynch syndrome (HNPCC) - 3-5% of CRC', 'Lynch syndrome (mismatch repair deficiency) is the most common hereditary CRC syndrome.'),
('b8455ceb-8760-5a5d-b069-34e36b93917a'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Which is the most common renal cell carcinoma subtype?', '["Oncocytoma","Collecting duct","Clear cell","Chromophobe","Papillary"]'::jsonb, 'Clear cell', 'Clear cell RCC arises from proximal tubular epithelium, rich in lipid/glycogen.'),
('11537b6c-8d97-5d81-a36f-109db0f73b38'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Which gene is inactivated in clear cell RCC?', '["EGFR","c-KIT","APC","KRAS","VHL gene"]'::jsonb, 'VHL gene', 'VHL mutation found in ~60% of sporadic clear cell RCC and von Hippel-Lindau syndrome.'),
('a9218481-30a5-5df9-b471-11e8e3da7e6f'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Which is the most common bladder cancer?', '["Sarcoma clinical presentation","Small cell clinical presentation","Adenocarcinoma clinical presentation","Urothelial (transitional cell) carcinoma","Squamous cell clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Urothelial (transitional cell) carcinoma', 'Urothelial carcinoma accounts for >90% of bladder cancers.'),
('8f1af6fa-dc76-5d3c-970e-90b39215d6a2'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Which is the most common skin cancer?', '["Squamous cell carcinoma","Malignant melanoma","Basal cell carcinoma","Kaposi sarcoma","Merkel cell"]'::jsonb, 'Basal cell carcinoma', 'BCC is most common (80%), slow-growing, rarely metastasizes.'),
('c53e05ab-a8b6-54a2-acb5-a08f04e30f3e'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Which skin cancer has the highest mortality?', '["Malignant melanoma","Squamous cell","Cutaneous lymphoma","Merkel cell","Basal cell"]'::jsonb, 'Malignant melanoma', 'Melanoma accounts for <5% of skin cancers but >75% of deaths.'),
('ca7f524f-560f-5a46-8201-69a15f23b31c'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Which BRAF mutation is common in melanoma?', '["BRAF V600E","GNAQ","c-KIT","NRAS","CTNNB1"]'::jsonb, 'BRAF V600E', 'BRAF V600E is targetable with BRAF + MEK inhibitors.'),
('d981c001-b9eb-5e6e-ba7c-66977b6dfbdd'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Which is the most common thyroid malignancy?', '["Lymphoma","Follicular","Papillary thyroid carcinoma","Medullary","Anaplastic"]'::jsonb, 'Papillary thyroid carcinoma', 'PTC has excellent prognosis (>95% 10-year survival).'),
('e4916bc9-ef59-52bf-aca2-4b2191650858'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Which thyroid cancer has worst prognosis?', '["Medullary","Follicular","Papillary","Hurthle cell","Anaplastic thyroid carcinoma"]'::jsonb, 'Anaplastic thyroid carcinoma', 'Anaplastic thyroid carcinoma (median survival 6 months).'),
('183db603-441e-57dc-8387-a3bc9f797dcb'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Which thyroid cancer is associated with MEN2?', '["Papillary","Follicular","Medullary thyroid carcinoma","Hurthle cell","Anaplastic"]'::jsonb, 'Medullary thyroid carcinoma', 'MTC arises from parafollicular C cells, secretes calcitonin, RET mutation.'),
('4830d678-045e-5f99-b036-c1a2e8fea952'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Which is the most common head and neck cancer?', '["Thyroid cancer","Salivary gland tumors","Nasopharyngeal carcinoma","Lymphoma","Squamous cell carcinoma"]'::jsonb, 'Squamous cell carcinoma', 'Head and neck SCC >90%. Risk: smoking, alcohol, HPV.'),
('9c0374c1-ec49-5acf-9d65-f25ebc86e1b8'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Which virus is associated with oropharyngeal SCC?', '["EBV","HHV-8","HTLV-1","HBV","HPV"]'::jsonb, 'HPV', 'HPV-16 associated with oropharyngeal SCC, especially in non-smokers.'),
('0b4af393-04f4-5f15-8f52-b38e01efea02'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'With respect to management, Which virus is associated with nasopharyngeal carcinoma?', '["HBV","EBV","HTLV-1","HHV-8","HPV"]'::jsonb, 'EBV', 'EBV strongly associated with undifferentiated nasopharyngeal carcinoma, endemic in SE Asia.'),
('12265d55-a90c-5e14-bc4e-0f402e08bc77'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Which is the most common gallbladder cancer subtype?', '["Adenocarcinoma","Adenosquamous","Small cell","Undifferentiated","Squamous cell"]'::jsonb, 'Adenocarcinoma', 'Gallbladder adenocarcinoma is strongly associated with chronic cholecystitis and gallstones.'),
('f007c457-9570-5dcd-afe6-090dc96e835c'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Which is the most common uterine cancer?', '["Carcinosarcoma","Sarcoma","Serous carcinoma","Endometrioid adenocarcinoma","Clear cell"]'::jsonb, 'Endometrioid adenocarcinoma', 'Endometrioid adenocarcinoma >80%. Associated with estrogen excess.'),
('d139b248-175d-5365-a9ff-acf02d1311a5'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Which is the most common ovarian malignancy?', '["Mucinous","Clear cell","Endometrioid","High-grade serous carcinoma","Granulosa cell tumor"]'::jsonb, 'High-grade serous carcinoma', 'HGSC is most common and deadliest, often presents at advanced stage.'),
('268e6ed1-739c-5061-b072-77f9096af1dc'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Which is the most common malignant bone tumor in children?', '["Osteosarcoma","Fibrosarcoma","Giant cell tumor","Chondrosarcoma","Ewing sarcoma"]'::jsonb, 'Osteosarcoma', 'Osteosarcoma is most common primary malignant bone tumor in children (peak 10-20 years).'),
('49189d66-4baa-5f9a-b681-352b2591c2d9'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Which translocation is characteristic of Ewing sarcoma?', '["t","t(11;22) EWSR1-FLI1","t","t","t"]'::jsonb, 't(11;22) EWSR1-FLI1', 'EWSR1-FLI1 in ~85% of Ewing sarcoma.'),
('fda05c19-a734-533b-bd5e-5a5d2a73f659'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Which is the most common soft tissue sarcoma in adults?', '["Leiomyosarcoma","Rhabdomyosarcoma","Synovial sarcoma","Liposarcoma","UPS/MFH"]'::jsonb, 'Liposarcoma', 'Liposarcoma is most common. Rhabdomyosarcoma is most common in children.'),
('dddddbc4-3d30-55e1-80d5-04cb4c143b03'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Which translocation is typical of synovial sarcoma?', '["t(17;22) COL1A1-PDGFB","t(11;22) EWSR1-FLI1","t(12;16) FUS-DDIT3","t(X;18) SS18-SSX","t(2;13) PAX3-FOXO1"]'::jsonb, 't(X;18) SS18-SSX', 't(X;18) in >90% of synovial sarcoma.'),
('ed983279-49ef-5c95-9e63-9f462a577571'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Which is the most common eye malignancy in children?', '["Rhabdomyosarcoma","Melanoma","Retinoblastoma","Medulloepithelioma","Lymphoma"]'::jsonb, 'Retinoblastoma', 'Retinoblastoma caused by RB1 gene mutation.'),
('6506cd00-df39-560a-af1d-dbc27d52b19f'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Which is the most common eye malignancy in adults?', '["SCC of conjunctiva","Lymphoma","Uveal (choroidal) melanoma","Retinoblastoma","BCC of eyelid"]'::jsonb, 'Uveal (choroidal) melanoma', 'Uveal melanoma is most common primary intraocular malignancy in adults.'),
('89516ca3-bc94-54cf-9a80-d6d8459ed6d5'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Paraneoplastic syndrome is defined as:', '["Pain from tumor clinical presentation","Cachexia only clinical presentation","Infection clinical presentation","Metastasis symptoms clinical presentation","Symptoms from tumor products or immune response, not direct invasion"]'::jsonb, 'Symptoms from tumor products or immune response, not direct invasion', 'Paraneoplastic syndromes are indirect effects of cancer via hormones, cytokines, or immune cross-reactivity.'),
('148d16e7-8cf4-5040-8957-95bf17a88c7b'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Hypercalcemia of malignancy is most associated with:', '["Sarcoma clinical presentation","Adenocarcinoma clinical presentation","Small cell clinical presentation","SCC (PTHrP) and bone metastases","Lymphoma clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'SCC (PTHrP) and bone metastases', 'SCC (lung, head/neck) secrete PTHrP. Myeloma and breast cause osteolytic hypercalcemia.'),
('b8730913-db56-5cbc-a787-c19ce1453044'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'SIADH is most associated with:', '["Colon cancer","Breast cancer","Squamous cell","Small cell lung carcinoma","Pancreatic cancer"]'::jsonb, 'Small cell lung carcinoma', 'SCLC produces ADH causing hyponatremia.'),
('a6f2d634-1bb2-5e3f-91ec-cc718f0898cf'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Ectopic Cushing syndrome (ACTH) is most associated with:', '["Carcinoid tumors","Pancreatic NET","MTC","Small cell lung carcinoma","Pheochromocytoma"]'::jsonb, 'Small cell lung carcinoma', 'SCLC is most common cause of ectopic ACTH.'),
('1c7bc800-c5df-5f29-90f6-611b676cf09d'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Carcinoid syndrome requires:', '["Liver metastases","Primary tumor only","Lung carcinoid only","Hindgut carcinoid","No metastases"]'::jsonb, 'Liver metastases', 'Carcinoid syndrome usually requires liver metastases to allow vasoactive substances into systemic circulation.'),
('8f9e41ba-087d-599f-bc10-c67a8df18ce3'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Which urinary metabolite is elevated in carcinoid syndrome?', '["VMA","Metanephrines","5-HIAA","Catecholamines","HVA"]'::jsonb, '5-HIAA', 'Serotonin metabolized to 5-HIAA, measured in 24-hour urine.'),
('b720e3e1-c663-56cc-9800-6e0e0e62f7b5'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Eaton-Lambert syndrome (LEMS) is associated with:', '["Small cell lung cancer","Prostate cancer","Colon cancer","Breast cancer","Thymoma"]'::jsonb, 'Small cell lung cancer', 'LEMS: anti-P/Q VGCC antibodies causing proximal weakness. SCLC is most common association.'),
('aa2eb896-26de-5b07-8139-cdfb335450ba'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Dermatomyositis as paraneoplastic is associated with:', '["Leukemia clinical presentation","Melanoma clinical presentation","Thyroid cancer clinical presentation","Prostate cancer clinical presentation","Ovarian, lung, pancreatic, gastric cancer"]'::jsonb, 'Ovarian, lung, pancreatic, gastric cancer', 'Dermatomyositis with malignancy: ovarian, lung, pancreatic, gastric most common.'),
('20f4e7ae-d8b1-5c5d-a2fb-294e269844d6'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Acanthosis nigricans as paraneoplastic is associated with:', '["Breast cancer","Lymphoma","Lung cancer","Gastric adenocarcinoma","Prostate cancer"]'::jsonb, 'Gastric adenocarcinoma', 'Acanthosis nigricans (velvety hyperpigmentation) associated with gastric adenocarcinoma.'),
('9031e94e-2687-59e5-aa2d-8966c14b46fa'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Trousseau sign (migratory thrombophlebitis) is associated with:', '["Colon cancer clinical presentation","Pancreatic and gastric adenocarcinoma","Prostate cancer clinical presentation","Breast cancer clinical presentation","Lung cancer clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Pancreatic and gastric adenocarcinoma', 'Trousseau syndrome: spontaneous DVT in mucin-producing adenocarcinomas (pancreas, stomach).'),
('543dd9cd-2057-5005-b75f-ab2ff4a54128'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Myasthenia gravis as paraneoplastic is associated with:', '["Small cell lung cancer","Lymphoma","Thymoma","Breast cancer","Thymic carcinoma"]'::jsonb, 'Thymoma', 'MG occurs in 30-50% of thymoma patients. Anti-AChR antibodies.'),
('e57cff5d-8c71-5539-8807-18d591923fed'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Polycythemia as paraneoplastic is associated with:', '["Renal cell carcinoma","None","HCC","Cerebellar hemangioblastoma","All of the above"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'RCC, HCC, and cerebellar hemangioblastoma can produce EPO causing paraneoplastic polycythemia.'),
('066d3093-854e-5ff0-8bbc-633a8332e15e'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Tumor grading is based on:', '["Tumor site clinical presentation","Tumor size clinical presentation","Metastasis clinical presentation","Lymph node involvement clinical presentation","Degree of differentiation and cellular atypia"]'::jsonb, 'Degree of differentiation and cellular atypia', 'Grade: well, moderate, poor, undifferentiated. High grade = aggressive.'),
('08036517-359c-5491-ac5b-bbe29af6fd5a'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Tumor staging is based on:', '["Cellular differentiation","Mitotic count","Nuclear atypia","Tumor markers","Anatomical extent"]'::jsonb, 'Anatomical extent', 'Staging: T (primary), N (nodes), M (metastasis). Prognostic gold standard.'),
('305ee975-56c3-5084-bf4a-ade1f0d5788c'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Which TNM component is most prognostic?', '["Grading","M stage","T stage","N stage","All equally"]'::jsonb, 'M stage', 'Distant metastasis (M1) is the strongest predictor of poor prognosis.'),
('b3e332dc-357a-5fbe-8d04-e6cc2e76bf82'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Gleason grading system is used for:', '["Prostate cancer","Lung cancer","Colon cancer","Bladder cancer","Breast cancer"]'::jsonb, 'Prostate cancer', 'Gleason score based on glandular pattern. 1-5, primary + secondary.'),
('85b424d9-1d49-5389-a2fc-591aa47a2569'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Nottingham (Bloom-Richardson) grading is for:', '["Colon cancer","Lung cancer","Ovarian cancer","Breast cancer","Prostate cancer"]'::jsonb, 'Breast cancer', 'Nottingham: tubule formation, nuclear pleomorphism, mitotic count. Score 3-9.'),
('2b4e9a8c-ba09-5b00-9e12-f59ef45d20ca'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Dukes staging is used for:', '["Breast cancer","Gastric cancer","Colorectal cancer","Prostate cancer","Lung cancer"]'::jsonb, 'Colorectal cancer', 'Dukes A: wall, B: through wall, C: nodes, D: metastasis.'),
('4a6011fa-7cf9-5c82-bde4-038da1f465f2'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'FIGO staging is for:', '["Gynecologic cancers","Prostate cancer","Lung cancer","Breast cancer","Colon cancer"]'::jsonb, 'Gynecologic cancers', 'FIGO staging for cervical, uterine, ovarian, vulvar, vaginal cancers.'),
('c11d256f-41e1-5885-a7dc-d7f6f0fb12f8'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Clark level is for:', '["Prostate cancer","Colon cancer","Lung cancer","Malignant melanoma","Breast cancer"]'::jsonb, 'Malignant melanoma', 'Clark level I-V. Breslow thickness (mm) is more reproducible.'),
('bdb427c2-5947-50be-a7bc-b0a4967bb570'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Breslow thickness is for:', '["Prostate cancer","Colon cancer","Malignant melanoma prognosis","Lung cancer","Breast cancer"]'::jsonb, 'Malignant melanoma prognosis', 'Breslow thickness in mm from granular layer to deepest invasive cell.'),
('2dce620f-9482-509c-8aab-51121b0f128c'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Ann Arbor staging is for:', '["Lymphoma","Solid tumors","Brain tumors","Leukemia","Myeloma"]'::jsonb, 'Lymphoma', 'Ann Arbor stage I-IV for Hodgkin and NHL. A/B suffix for symptoms.'),
('aab9075e-f2c4-5bf7-809f-d9396ebe56fb'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Which IHC marker is positive in carcinomas?', '["Desmin","LCA","Vimentin","S100","Cytokeratin"]'::jsonb, 'Cytokeratin', 'Cytokeratin in epithelial cells and carcinomas.'),
('ce641e51-0b9f-52e2-a6b2-9468749c9b2d'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Which IHC marker is positive in sarcomas?', '["Desmin","LCA","Cytokeratin","Vimentin","S100"]'::jsonb, 'Vimentin', 'Vimentin in mesenchymal cells/sarcomas (non-specific).'),
('3b800ca4-b1bc-575f-8bd0-5134d1d23ee0'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Which IHC marker is positive in lymphomas?', '["Cytokeratin","HMB45","LCA","S100","Vimentin"]'::jsonb, 'LCA', 'LCA/CD45 on most hematopoietic cells (lymphomas, leukemias).'),
('0a3a9ad1-f005-5aa2-81e0-309090e564e6'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Which IHC marker is positive in melanoma?', '["Cytokeratin","S100, HMB45, Melan-A, SOX10","Desmin","CD34","LCA"]'::jsonb, 'S100, HMB45, Melan-A, SOX10', 'S100 sensitive, HMB45 and Melan-A specific for melanoma.'),
('b7bcc614-bf0c-51fa-8fe8-e65ded2eca8f'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Which IHC marker is positive in GIST?', '["Desmin","CD34","CD117 (c-kit) and DOG1","Cytokeratin","S100"]'::jsonb, 'CD117 (c-kit) and DOG1', 'CD117+ in >95% GIST. DOG1 more specific.'),
('9c49d172-88bb-5cb7-8b03-90836433ec7a'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Which IHC marker is positive in prostate cancer?', '["CDX2","GATA3","PSA, NKX3.1, PSAP","TTF1","Thyroglobulin"]'::jsonb, 'PSA, NKX3.1, PSAP', 'PSA and NKX3.1 highly sensitive and specific for prostate origin.'),
('92bbf150-945b-5f81-affa-9b4db6cfb0b2'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Which IHC marker is positive in breast cancer?', '["NKX3.1 clinical presentation","Thyroglobulin clinical presentation","CDX2 clinical presentation","TTF1 clinical presentation","ER, PR, GATA3, mammaglobin, HER2"]'::jsonb, 'ER, PR, GATA3, mammaglobin, HER2', 'GATA3 and mammaglobin breast-specific. ER/PR hormone guidance.'),
('6f92a655-4d66-5ee2-b0bd-99c46afb32ff'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Which IHC marker is positive in lung adenocarcinoma?', '["CK5/6, p63","TTF1, napsin A, CK7","GATA3","CK20, CDX2","PSA"]'::jsonb, 'TTF1, napsin A, CK7', 'TTF1 and napsin A sensitive markers for lung adenocarcinoma.');
insert into fcps_mcq_stage (id, subject_name, chapter_name, question, options, correct_answer, explanation) values
('b6912fb6-1cf4-5c5a-baa5-a5b7ccbb95b5'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Which IHC marker is positive in colorectal cancer?', '["CK20, CDX2, SATB2","S100","NKX3.1","CK7, TTF1","GATA3"]'::jsonb, 'CK20, CDX2, SATB2', 'CRC: CK20+/CK7-, CDX2+, SATB2+.'),
('396ef574-8663-5ac2-aee2-affe9794f2ac'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Which IHC marker is positive in HCC?', '["CK20","HepPar1, Glypican-3, pCEA","CK7","TTF1","CDX2"]'::jsonb, 'HepPar1, Glypican-3, pCEA', 'HepPar1 positive in HCC. Glypican-3 more sensitive for poorly differentiated HCC.'),
('730a5b9e-08a2-57b6-b701-68e9e7c9935e'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Which IHC marker is positive in renal cell carcinoma?', '["GATA3 clinical presentation","CDX2 clinical presentation","TTF1 clinical presentation","PAX8, CD10, vimentin, RCC antigen","NKX3.1 clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'PAX8, CD10, vimentin, RCC antigen', 'Clear cell RCC: PAX8+, CD10+, vimentin+, CK7-.'),
('db97b69f-b800-542e-9cff-86317c61ac09'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Which IHC marker is positive in bladder cancer?', '["TTF1 clinical presentation","Thyroglobulin clinical presentation","GATA3, CK7, CK20, p63, uroplakin","CDX2 clinical presentation","NKX3.1 clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'GATA3, CK7, CK20, p63, uroplakin', 'Urothelial: GATA3+, CK7+, CK20+, p63+.'),
('b24278b6-45c8-5369-a6f9-00d2e8ae3258'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Which IHC marker is positive in ovarian serous carcinoma?', '["CK20","CDX2","TTF1","GATA3","PAX8, WT1, CK7, ER"]'::jsonb, 'PAX8, WT1, CK7, ER', 'Serous ovarian: CK7+/CK20-, PAX8+, WT1+, ER+.'),
('7c9b0bfb-625a-58ed-b840-965b5163392d'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Which IHC marker is positive in neuroendocrine tumors?', '["Cytokeratin clinical presentation","CD45 clinical presentation","Chromogranin, synaptophysin, INSM1","Vimentin clinical presentation","S100 clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Chromogranin, synaptophysin, INSM1', 'Neuroendocrine markers: chromogranin (dense granules), synaptophysin (microvesicles).'),
('9a78f5a5-946d-5557-bfa1-383c549bf952'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Which IHC marker is positive in Ewing sarcoma?', '["S100","Desmin","CD45","CD99 (MIC2) strong membranous","Cytokeratin"]'::jsonb, 'CD99 (MIC2) strong membranous', 'CD99 positive in Ewing sarcoma. Confirm with EWSR1 molecular testing.'),
('7372d684-4f4e-5236-b1da-76b94175ea9a'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Which IHC marker is positive in rhabdomyosarcoma?', '["CD34","LCA","S100","Cytokeratin","Myogenin, MyoD1, desmin"]'::jsonb, 'Myogenin, MyoD1, desmin', 'Myogenin and MyoD1 are nuclear markers specific for skeletal muscle.'),
('8328b7e8-a57c-571d-a444-7b48162b23b6'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Which IHC marker is positive in angiosarcoma?', '["Cytokeratin","CD31, ERG, CD34, Factor VIII","S100","LCA","Desmin"]'::jsonb, 'CD31, ERG, CD34, Factor VIII', 'CD31 and ERG most sensitive/specific endothelial markers.'),
('cd9781dc-89af-576a-a0d7-83c56f1e8651'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Which IHC marker is positive in seminoma?', '["CD30","PLAP, OCT4, SALL4, c-KIT","CEA","AFP","Beta-hCG"]'::jsonb, 'PLAP, OCT4, SALL4, c-KIT', 'Seminoma: OCT4+, PLAP+, c-KIT+. AFP negative.'),
('986d8800-4349-5e88-afb8-db2d517ecbff'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Which IHC marker is positive in embryonal carcinoma?', '["CD30, OCT4, SALL4, SOX2","Beta-hCG","c-KIT","PLAP","AFP"]'::jsonb, 'CD30, OCT4, SALL4, SOX2', 'Embryonal carcinoma: CD30+, OCT4+, SOX2+.'),
('1f7a29bf-0699-515a-85a2-9e0557d62496'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Which technique detects HER2 amplification?', '["Southern blot","FISH or IHC","Sanger sequencing","CGH array","PCR"]'::jsonb, 'FISH or IHC', 'HER2 testing: IHC (0,1+,2+,3+), FISH for IHC 2+.'),
('e23b69dc-5ae7-5b1b-b8ea-5043c5c70809'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Which test detects MSI in colorectal cancer?', '["Sanger sequencing clinical presentation","IHC for MMR proteins or PCR for microsatellites","Karyotype clinical presentation","FISH clinical presentation","CGH array clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'IHC for MMR proteins or PCR for microsatellites', 'MSI: IHC (MLH1, MSH2, MSH6, PMS2) or PCR (BAT-25, BAT-26, etc).'),
('b3a625b1-5550-5149-a890-9b5937582e7a'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Which test is used for lung cancer EGFR mutation?', '["PCR or NGS","FISH","Western blot","IHC","Karyotype"]'::jsonb, 'PCR or NGS', 'EGFR mutation testing in exons 18-21. Most common: exon 19 del, L858R.'),
('b4207d0c-4a9f-5b48-af88-615fc56b0726'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Which test detects ALK rearrangement in lung cancer?', '["FISH (break-apart) or IHC","IHC only","NGS","PCR","Sanger"]'::jsonb, 'FISH (break-apart) or IHC', 'FISH is gold standard for ALK rearrangement. IHC with sensitive antibodies is used for screening.'),
('2c3b70c6-a0ac-5db0-990a-f7b3579498bb'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Which is the most common method for BCR-ABL in CML?', '["Western blot","RT-PCR (quantitative) and FISH","IHC","Sanger","Karyotype"]'::jsonb, 'RT-PCR (quantitative) and FISH', 'RT-PCR for molecular monitoring. FISH for detection. Karyotype for Philadelphia chromosome.'),
('2c44c7cd-756e-5a9a-98ca-769fbd9efd2a'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Which tumor marker is used for colon cancer monitoring?', '["AFP","PSA","CEA","CA 19-9","CA-125"]'::jsonb, 'CEA', 'CEA for colorectal cancer monitoring. Not diagnostic, useful for recurrence detection.'),
('8139c1ce-e1db-5e84-9327-ef384dc002cc'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Which tumor marker is used for pancreatic cancer monitoring?', '["CEA","AFP","CA 15-3","CA-125","CA 19-9"]'::jsonb, 'CA 19-9', 'CA 19-9 for pancreatic cancer monitoring. Non-specific (elevated in cholangitis).'),
('09408739-43ed-5d52-8f8d-342da5e15d86'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Which tumor marker is used for ovarian cancer monitoring?', '["CEA","CA-125","Beta-hCG","AFP","CA 19-9"]'::jsonb, 'CA-125', 'CA-125 for epithelial ovarian cancer monitoring. Also elevated in endometriosis.'),
('8f31d68c-a452-50aa-a42e-600e733d0b95'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Which tumor marker is used for HCC surveillance?', '["CEA","PSA","CA 19-9","AFP","CA-125"]'::jsonb, 'AFP', 'AFP elevated in 60-80% HCC. Used with ultrasound for surveillance in cirrhosis.'),
('f0eb734e-d3bf-5565-b6f4-5ff1339e4ccc'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'AFP and beta-hCG are markers for:', '["Colon cancer","Lung cancer","Breast cancer","Testicular germ cell tumors","Prostate cancer"]'::jsonb, 'Testicular germ cell tumors', 'AFP (yolk sac), beta-hCG (choriocarcinoma) for testicular cancer classification.'),
('9edcce87-45b8-540f-bf7e-0ae7cc49a51d'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Which technique is most common for cancer genomic profiling?', '["Sanger sequencing clinical presentation","Whole exome clinical presentation","RNA sequencing clinical presentation","Next-generation sequencing (NGS) panel","Whole genome clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Next-generation sequencing (NGS) panel', 'Targeted NGS panels (50-500 genes) most common for clinical cancer profiling.'),
('c529d245-4b21-50fc-bb31-69a95465983c'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'PD-L1 IHC predicts response to:', '["Hormone therapy","Immune checkpoint inhibitors","Radiation","Targeted therapy","Chemotherapy"]'::jsonb, 'Immune checkpoint inhibitors', 'PD-L1 expression (TPS, CPS) guides pembrolizumab and nivolumab use.'),
('df269978-4e00-553e-bfc2-6a8a312065b1'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'MSI-H/dMMR is a biomarker for:', '["Immunotherapy response","Chemotherapy","Hormone therapy","Targeted therapy","Radiation"]'::jsonb, 'Immunotherapy response', 'Pembrolizumab approved for MSI-H/dMMR solid tumors regardless of site.'),
('dd0f2181-3027-5013-b564-5f577b6a1f2b'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'For a patient presenting with symptoms, Liquid biopsy detects:', '["Metabolites only clinical presentation","RNA only clinical presentation","Circulating tumor DNA (ctDNA) in blood","Tumor cells only clinical presentation","Proteins only clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Circulating tumor DNA (ctDNA) in blood', 'Liquid biopsy detects ctDNA for mutation profiling and monitoring.'),
('cdc4e7c7-3173-5204-a5b7-ef90db06cb66'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'IHC panel for cancer of unknown primary uses:', '["Electron microscopy only","Single antibody only","CK7/CK20 pattern + lineage markers","Serum markers","H&E only"]'::jsonb, 'CK7/CK20 pattern + lineage markers', 'CK7/CK20 pattern plus TTF1, GATA3, CDX2, NKX3.1, etc.'),
('35734cd6-a29f-5758-8f1e-bfa3cae7a317'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Which cancer has highest somatic mutation burden?', '["Hematologic cancers","Prostate cancer","Melanoma and lung cancer","Pediatric cancers","Thyroid cancer"]'::jsonb, 'Melanoma and lung cancer', 'Melanoma and lung cancer have highest TMB from UV and smoking mutagens.'),
('36c9e660-c98b-587a-b4ba-2226c2cd475b'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Molecular residual disease (MRD) testing uses:', '["Karyotype only clinical presentation","FISH only clinical presentation","ctDNA detection for recurrence monitoring","Microscopy only clinical presentation","IHC only clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'ctDNA detection for recurrence monitoring', 'MRD via ctDNA detects molecular recurrence before clinical relapse.'),
('dcf8ceae-d476-5916-a5b9-9ff7a16a4773'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Which is the most common cancer susceptibility gene?', '["APC","TP53","BRCA1/BRCA2","PTEN","MLH1/MSH2"]'::jsonb, 'BRCA1/BRCA2', 'BRCA1/BRCA2 are most commonly tested hereditary cancer genes.'),
('5356dbfe-dae2-5c84-b6f7-7ad9b9ed7f63'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Tumor mutational burden (TMB) is measured in:', '["Protein expression","Number of genes","Total mutations","Mutations per megabase","Number of chromosomes"]'::jsonb, 'Mutations per megabase', 'TMB: number of somatic mutations per million base pairs. High TMB predicts immunotherapy benefit.'),
('5e722b32-acf5-5652-80c0-d76db9811965'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'HER2-positive breast cancer has:', '["PR positivity clinical presentation","Triple negative clinical presentation","HER2 gene amplification by FISH or IHC 3+","ER positivity clinical presentation","BRCA mutation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'HER2 gene amplification by FISH or IHC 3+', 'HER2+ breast cancer: FISH ratio >2.0 or IHC 3+. Treated with trastuzumab.'),
('a9c18c86-5efd-5d61-9f3d-e812754a14cf'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Which hormone receptor status defines triple-negative breast cancer?', '["ER+, PR+, HER2-","ER-, PR+, HER2+","ER-, PR-, HER2-","ER-, PR-, HER2+","ER+, PR-, HER2+"]'::jsonb, 'ER-, PR-, HER2-', 'Triple-negative breast cancer lacks ER, PR, and HER2. Poor prognosis, chemotherapy mainstay.'),
('cb9a5df0-af2b-5a6c-bbce-4844ae73552d'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Which molecular subtype of breast cancer has the best prognosis?', '["Luminal B","Luminal A","Normal-like","HER2-enriched","Basal-like"]'::jsonb, 'Luminal A', 'Luminal A has the best prognosis. Basal-like has the worst.'),
('58eb781c-253f-5e49-8708-6a2cabbfc07c'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Which is the most common molecular abnormality in GIST?', '["NF1 mutation","PDGFRA mutation","BRAF mutation","c-KIT mutation","SDH deficiency"]'::jsonb, 'c-KIT mutation', 'c-KIT exon 11 mutation in 60-70% of GIST. Imatinib is effective.'),
('a330e193-6fda-5200-ab41-679b7aa5ac25'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Which GIST mutation confers imatinib resistance?', '["c-KIT exon 17 mutation","SDH deficiency","PDGFRA D842V","Exon 9 mutation","Exon 11 mutation"]'::jsonb, 'c-KIT exon 17 mutation', 'KIT exon 17 mutation is resistant to imatinib. PDGFRA D842V also resistant.'),
('72beb311-999a-5463-b598-16ff1f1b3d5b'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Which tumor is associated with WT1 gene mutation?', '["Hepatoblastoma","Rhabdomyosarcoma","Retinoblastoma","Neuroblastoma","Wilms tumor"]'::jsonb, 'Wilms tumor', 'WT1 mutation causes Wilms tumor. Associated with WAGR syndrome.'),
('a714360d-dbbc-5a93-b799-a4730c527725'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'The most common malignant liver tumor in children is:', '["Rhabdomyosarcoma","Hepatocellular carcinoma","Metastatic neuroblastoma","Hepatoblastoma","Angiosarcoma"]'::jsonb, 'Hepatoblastoma', 'Hepatoblastoma is the most common pediatric liver malignancy. Elevated AFP.'),
('6a4e2097-4e50-50ab-91cc-3f22d4c180b9'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Which pediatric tumor shows Homer-Wright rosettes?', '["Rhabdomyosarcoma","Wilms tumor","Neuroblastoma","Retinoblastoma","Hepatoblastoma"]'::jsonb, 'Neuroblastoma', 'Homer-Wright pseudorosettes in neuroblastoma. True rosettes in retinoblastoma (Flexner-Wintersteiner).'),
('6927b9d4-d17d-5072-8065-a9fb2e9b734c'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'N-myc amplification is a poor prognostic factor in:', '["Osteosarcoma","Neuroblastoma","Wilms tumor","Retinoblastoma","Medulloblastoma"]'::jsonb, 'Neuroblastoma', 'N-myc amplification correlates with advanced stage and poor prognosis in neuroblastoma.'),
('6a324bd6-86f4-594c-890e-4b7dcad39e50'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Regarding clinical scenarios, The most common primary brain tumor in adults is:', '["Meningioma","Astrocytoma","Oligodendroglioma","Ependymoma","Glioblastoma"]'::jsonb, 'Glioblastoma', 'GBM is most common primary malignant brain tumor. Meningioma is most common primary brain tumor overall (often benign).'),
('4e7036cc-7dca-5178-8f6f-08dc47dc6e5b'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Which brain tumor shows psammoma bodies?', '["Glioblastoma","Oligodendroglioma","Medulloblastoma","Ependymoma","Meningioma"]'::jsonb, 'Meningioma', 'Psammoma bodies (concentric calcifications) are characteristic of meningioma.'),
('c127c0ef-e5f4-5515-b371-bf9e18e7983e'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'The most common brain tumor in children is:', '["Glioblastoma","Ependymoma","Craniopharyngioma","Medulloblastoma","Pilocytic astrocytoma"]'::jsonb, 'Pilocytic astrocytoma', 'Pilocytic astrocytoma is most common. Medulloblastoma is most common malignant pediatric brain tumor.'),
('218aa4db-929f-5b9f-a593-4bc42c01c010'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Which CNS tumor is associated with chromosome 1p/19q codeletion?', '["Oligodendroglioma","Ependymoma","Glioblastoma","Astrocytoma","Meningioma"]'::jsonb, 'Oligodendroglioma', '1p/19q codeletion is diagnostic of oligodendroglioma and predicts better response to chemo.'),
('93942e58-6211-50d8-a7d1-9ee21eb2ffec'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Medulloblastoma arises from which cells?', '["Meningothelial cells","Ependymal cells","Astrocytes","Cerebellar granule cell progenitors","Oligodendrocytes"]'::jsonb, 'Cerebellar granule cell progenitors', 'Medulloblastoma: primitive neuroectodermal tumor of cerebellum. SHH, WNT, Group 3, Group 4 subtypes.'),
('532be91b-47f5-54e8-9c2f-dfbf9fd0b8f1'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Which tumor is characterized by ''chicken-wire'' vasculature?', '["Rhabdomyosarcoma","Leiomyosarcoma","Liposarcoma","Synovial sarcoma","MPNST"]'::jsonb, 'Liposarcoma', 'Myxoid liposarcoma has prominent ''chicken-wire'' capillary network.'),
('44e44630-a6ca-55c4-9e26-60c625b35b85'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'The most common thyroid malignancy is:', '["Papillary thyroid carcinoma","Follicular thyroid carcinoma","Medullary thyroid carcinoma","Lymphoma","Anaplastic thyroid carcinoma"]'::jsonb, 'Papillary thyroid carcinoma', 'Papillary carcinoma (80%). PTC has BRAF V600E mutation. Psammoma bodies and Orphan Annie nuclei.'),
('b7f6b8b4-4290-5ef3-aa74-d50d00f78adb'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Which thyroid tumor secretes calcitonin?', '["Anaplastic carcinoma","Papillary carcinoma","Hürthle cell carcinoma","Medullary thyroid carcinoma","Follicular carcinoma"]'::jsonb, 'Medullary thyroid carcinoma', 'MTC arises from C-cells. Elevated calcitonin. Associated with MEN 2A/2B (RET mutation).'),
('12ae31cd-3a1e-5460-b37f-bc25cb6441f0'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Follicular thyroid carcinoma invades via:', '["Perineural invasion","Vascular invasion","Lymphatic invasion","Transcoelomic spread","Direct extension"]'::jsonb, 'Vascular invasion', 'Follicular carcinoma: hematogenous spread (bone, lung). No lymph node involvement early.'),
('316a39be-6506-51c7-b37d-3f92df344c69'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Anaplastic thyroid carcinoma is associated with:', '["RET/PTC rearrangement","RAS mutation","TP53 mutation","PAX8/PPARG fusion","BRAF mutation"]'::jsonb, 'TP53 mutation', 'Anaplastic carcinoma has TP53 mutation. Rapidly fatal. No uptake of radioiodine.'),
('f54005bd-6987-5f4a-9c93-e317cfe92eb1'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'RET/PTC rearrangement is characteristic of:', '["Papillary thyroid carcinoma","Follicular carcinoma","Anaplastic carcinoma","Hürthle cell carcinoma","Medullary carcinoma"]'::jsonb, 'Papillary thyroid carcinoma', 'RET/PTC rearrangement found in radiation-induced PTC (e.g., Chernobyl).'),
('a9959265-f119-5b8c-b741-b161a22d2726'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Which syndrome includes parathyroid adenoma?', '["Neurofibromatosis type 1","MEN 2A","Von Hippel-Lindau","MEN 2B","MEN 1"]'::jsonb, 'MEN 1', 'MEN 1: parathyroid adenoma, pituitary adenoma, pancreatic neuroendocrine tumors.'),
('be4ec83c-4202-5e5e-b131-56249fb11cd9'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Pheochromocytoma is associated with which rule?', '["Rule of 7","Rule of 5","Rule of 2","Rule of 3","Rule of 10"]'::jsonb, 'Rule of 10', 'Pheochromocytoma: 10% bilateral, 10% malignant, 10% extra-adrenal, 10% familial.'),
('23a8b302-cb58-53f3-9242-aadc9df1cdd3'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'In a surgical context, Which tumor shows Zellballen pattern?', '["Small cell carcinoma","Pheochromocytoma","Medullary thyroid carcinoma","Carcinoid tumor","Paraganglioma"]'::jsonb, 'Pheochromocytoma', 'Both pheochromocytoma and paraganglioma show Zellballen (nesting) pattern on histology.'),
('d5bcb17b-179a-5988-a497-56994cfd64fc'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'The most common adrenal cortical tumor is:', '["Pheochromocytoma","Adrenal adenoma","Metastasis","Adrenal carcinoma","Myelolipoma"]'::jsonb, 'Adrenal adenoma', 'Non-functioning adrenal adenomas are most common (incidentalomas).'),
('5bc77bcd-7277-5bd8-836c-0fd1a313aed6'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Which malignancy is most commonly metastatic to bone?', '["Thyroid cancer","Lung cancer","Prostate cancer","Kidney cancer","Breast cancer"]'::jsonb, 'Breast cancer', 'Breast most common overall. Prostate in men. Lung, kidney, thyroid also metastasize to bone.'),
('04873dd1-a906-55e6-b05b-919177f008a1'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'The most common skin cancer is:', '["Squamous cell carcinoma","Merkel cell carcinoma","Basal cell carcinoma","Kaposi sarcoma","Malignant melanoma"]'::jsonb, 'Basal cell carcinoma', 'BCC is most common skin cancer. Locally invasive but rarely metastatic.'),
('ccdfbf09-d409-5370-b620-0ebcb4df9881'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Merkel cell carcinoma is associated with:', '["EBV","HTLV-1","HHV-8","Merkel cell polyomavirus","HPV"]'::jsonb, 'Merkel cell polyomavirus', 'MCPyV integrated into genome in 80% of Merkel cell carcinomas. Neuroendocrine marker expression.'),
('99f70aae-a59a-566f-8aee-dc2d4a5b462d'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Which melanoma subtype has poorest prognosis?', '["Acral lentiginous melanoma","Desmoplastic melanoma","Lentigo maligna melanoma","Nodular melanoma","Superficial spreading melanoma"]'::jsonb, 'Nodular melanoma', 'Nodular melanoma: vertical growth phase, rapidly invasive, worse prognosis.'),
('6e83c927-9d96-5d81-a439-bc5290bd1abd'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Acral lentiginous melanoma is most common in:', '["Children","Arabs","Hispanics","Asian/Black patients","Caucasians"]'::jsonb, 'Asian/Black patients', 'Acral lentiginous melanoma: no UV association. More common in darker skin types.'),
('8faf3067-de15-50aa-a198-e84792238d13'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'BRAF V600E mutation in melanoma is targeted by:', '["Erlotinib","Vemurafenib / Dabrafenib","Sunitinib","Trastuzumab","Imatinib"]'::jsonb, 'Vemurafenib / Dabrafenib', 'BRAF inhibitors (vemurafenib, dabrafenib) for BRAF-mutant melanoma.'),
('e73aaabf-e954-5dfc-b82e-d0d48e15dd14'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'SCC in situ is called:', '["Paget disease","Keratoacanthoma","Actinic keratosis","Erythroplasia of Queyrat","Bowen disease"]'::jsonb, 'Bowen disease', 'Bowen disease: SCC in situ (full-thickness dysplasia). Erythroplasia of Queyrat: penile SCC in situ.'),
('a6adfe0e-266b-5dd9-b287-9692422d423b'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Keratoacanthoma is a:', '["Inflammatory lesion","Cyst","Precancerous lesion","Malignant tumor","Benign tumor mimicking SCC"]'::jsonb, 'Benign tumor mimicking SCC', 'Keratoacanthoma: rapidly growing, cup-shaped, central keratin plug. Can regress. But some consider it SCC variant.'),
('71268198-fc81-54c3-bb22-2450c53f0488'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Basal cell carcinoma rarely metastasizes because:', '["It is stroma-dependent","It has no blood supply","It is encapsulated","It is well-differentiated","It is superficial only"]'::jsonb, 'It is stroma-dependent', 'BCC is stroma-dependent. Metastasis rate <0.1%. Treat with surgical excision or Mohs.'),
('31c1d784-8dcf-5602-8123-66a35bb9ecc6'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Which skin cancer arises in scars and chronic ulcers?', '["Merkel cell carcinoma","Melanoma","Dermatofibrosarcoma protuberans","Basal cell carcinoma","Squamous cell carcinoma"]'::jsonb, 'Squamous cell carcinoma', 'Marjolin ulcer: SCC arising in chronic wound, scar, or burn. Aggressive.'),
('36feb913-4b93-59e9-8646-8cbc547c5ac1'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Dermatofibrosarcoma protuberans (DFSP) has:', '["KIT mutation","BRAF mutation","TP53 mutation","COL1A1-PDGFB fusion (t(17;22))","EWSR1 rearrangement"]'::jsonb, 'COL1A1-PDGFB fusion (t(17;22))', 'DFSP: low-grade sarcoma. COL1A1-PDGFB fusion. Treated by imatinib if unresectable.'),
('8521cecb-f0cb-5e8d-9eff-64cf04331c6d'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'On diagnostic evaluation, The most common soft tissue sarcoma in adults is:', '["Undifferentiated pleomorphic sarcoma","Synovial sarcoma clinical presentation","Rhabdomyosarcoma clinical presentation","Liposarcoma clinical presentation","Leiomyosarcoma clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Undifferentiated pleomorphic sarcoma', 'UPS (previously MFH) most common in older adults. Extremities most common site.'),
('85cab8b5-54a1-52f1-9b4e-0bcee659b783'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'The most common sarcoma in children is:', '["Liposarcoma","Ewing sarcoma","Synovial sarcoma","Osteosarcoma","Rhabdomyosarcoma"]'::jsonb, 'Rhabdomyosarcoma', 'Rhabdomyosarcoma: most common pediatric soft tissue sarcoma. Alveolar vs embryonal types.'),
('282b84da-e371-5400-b6d9-decf410e3193'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Ewing sarcoma has which translocation?', '["t(11;14) CCND1-IGH","t(2;13) PAX3-FOXO1","t(11;22)(q24;q12) EWSR1-FLI1","t(X;18) SS18-SSX","t(12;16) FUS-DDIT3"]'::jsonb, 't(11;22)(q24;q12) EWSR1-FLI1', 'EWSR1-FLI1 fusion in 85% of Ewing sarcoma. CD99 (MIC2) positive.'),
('ec6d6815-e6b6-5eb8-9313-00dbcd268315'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Synovial sarcoma has translocation:', '["t(11;22) EWSR1-FLI1","t(7;16) FUS-ATF1","t(12;16) FUS-DDIT3","t(X;18)(p11;q11) SS18-SSX","t(2;13) PAX3-FOXO1"]'::jsonb, 't(X;18)(p11;q11) SS18-SSX', 'SS18-SSX fusion. Biphasic (epithelial + spindle) vs monophasic. SYT-SSX.'),
('0597caa9-8a42-5847-8645-28cc283132e5'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Myxoid liposarcoma has translocation:', '["t(X;18) SS18-SSX","t(9;22) BCR-ABL1","t(12;16)(q13;p11) FUS-DDIT3","t(11;22) EWSR1-FLI1","t(2;13) PAX3-FOXO1"]'::jsonb, 't(12;16)(q13;p11) FUS-DDIT3', 'FUS-DDIT3 (TLS-CHOP) fusion. Myxoid round cell liposarcoma with chicken-wire vessels.'),
('1bbe91a3-d5ed-55e0-b793-253056faa959'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Alveolar rhabdomyosarcoma has:', '["t(1;13) PAX7-FOXO1","t(2;13) PAX3-FOXO1","t(12;16) FUS-DDIT3","t(X;18) SS18-SSX","t(11;22) EWSR1-FLI1"]'::jsonb, 't(2;13) PAX3-FOXO1', 'PAX3-FOXO1 fusion in 60%. Worse prognosis than embryonal rhabdomyosarcoma.'),
('15e79755-dafd-5575-aa14-c877faf0834c'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Which sarcoma is characterized by ''bird''s-eye'' cells?', '["Leiomyosarcoma","Liposarcoma","Myxofibrosarcoma","Angiosarcoma","Synovial sarcoma"]'::jsonb, 'Myxofibrosarcoma', 'Myxofibrosarcoma: pseudolipoblasts (bird''s-eye cells) and curvilinear vessels.'),
('3029dfb4-b4ba-5cd0-ad87-0bef31593f50'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Leiomyosarcoma arises most commonly in:', '["Uterus","GI tract","Extremities","Retroperitoneum","Skin"]'::jsonb, 'Uterus', 'Uterine leiomyosarcoma from myometrium. Also GI tract, retroperitoneum.'),
('7433e39e-46e9-5da2-af20-a1ea07026d0e'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Gastrointestinal stromal tumor (GIST) most commonly arises in:', '["Colon","Rectum","Small intestine","Esophagus","Stomach"]'::jsonb, 'Stomach', 'GIST most common in stomach. KIT or PDGFRA mutation. CD117 (c-KIT) positive.'),
('b3961e96-27c5-5586-9037-baf76afc8343'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Angiosarcoma risk factors include:', '["Radiation exposure","Vinyl chloride exposure","Arsenic exposure","All of these","Chronic lymphedema"]'::jsonb, 'All of these', 'Angiosarcoma: liver (vinyl chloride), breast (Stewart-Treves after mastectomy), skin (radiation).'),
('4c779b40-d793-587e-890a-cc6ae41b805d'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'The most common type of lung cancer worldwide is:', '["Large cell carcinoma","Adenocarcinoma","Squamous cell carcinoma","Small cell carcinoma","Carcinoid tumor"]'::jsonb, 'Adenocarcinoma', 'Adenocarcinoma now most common. Usually peripheral. EGFR/KRAS mutations.'),
('27666f9b-a4f1-589e-bafe-c450612dc3fc'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Small cell lung cancer is associated with:', '["Paraneoplastic syndromes","Clubbing","Gynecomastia","Hypertrophic pulmonary osteoarthropathy","Hypercalcemia"]'::jsonb, 'Paraneoplastic syndromes', 'SCLC: neuroendocrine. SIADH (ADH), Eaton-Lambert (VGCC antibodies), ectopic ACTH (Cushing).'),
('f74d59b3-3212-5ebe-808b-c34a2abf7ab7'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Which molecular abnormality is common in colorectal adenoma-carcinoma sequence?', '["KRAS mutation","MLH1 silencing","TP53 mutation","SMAD4 mutation","APC mutation"]'::jsonb, 'APC mutation', 'APC mutation initiates adenoma. KRAS in intermediate, TP53 in late/carcinoma stage.'),
('294bd8c0-5696-5b45-9cc9-c7e5f8f46322'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Hereditary non-polyposis colorectal cancer (HNPCC/Lynch) is due to:', '["MYH mutation","STK11 mutation","APC mutation","MLH1/MSH2/MSH6 mutations","PTEN mutation"]'::jsonb, 'MLH1/MSH2/MSH6 mutations', 'Lynch syndrome: defective MMR -> microsatellite instability. Right-sided colon cancer.'),
('fe29aa93-b78a-5db8-85a5-34beccafdb08'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma most commonly arises in:', '["Body","Head of pancreas","Uncinate process","Diffuse","Tail"]'::jsonb, 'Head of pancreas', 'Pancreatic head: early obstructive jaundice. KRAS mutation in >90%. Dismal prognosis.'),
('13a6e269-6bb6-5e20-bac3-589a7891ce86'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Which tumor marker is most specific for pancreatic cancer?', '["CA-125","CA 15-3","CEA","AFP","CA 19-9"]'::jsonb, 'CA 19-9', 'CA 19-9 elevated in pancreatic cancer. Also in cholangiocarcinoma. Not diagnostic but useful for monitoring.'),
('e6bacbbf-938e-5e0d-b097-a60770179274'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Prostate adenocarcinoma is graded by:', '["TNM staging","Bone scan","Gleason score","PSA level","Digital rectal exam"]'::jsonb, 'Gleason score', 'Gleason score 2-10. Lower score (3+3=6) well-differentiated. Higher score poorly differentiated.'),
('fad069db-76b1-5769-b9c0-e55fab433a5d'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'The most common histology of bladder cancer is:', '["Sarcoma","Adenocarcinoma","Urothelial (transitional cell) carcinoma","Small cell carcinoma","Squamous cell carcinoma"]'::jsonb, 'Urothelial (transitional cell) carcinoma', 'Urothelial carcinoma (>90%). Risk: smoking, aniline dyes. Papillary vs non-papillary.'),
('8e27f593-b17d-5c36-abf8-bd238a466661'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Clear cell renal cell carcinoma is associated with:', '["VHL mutation","t(6;11) translocation","c-KIT mutation","EWSR1 rearrangement","BRAF mutation"]'::jsonb, 'VHL mutation', 'ccRCC: VHL mutation -> HIF accumulation -> VEGF overproduction. Clear cytoplasm, rich vasculature.'),
('1680abce-59b7-5aa8-98d9-44a4e61fc53a'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Hepatocellular carcinoma (HCC) risk factors:', '["HCV only","Aflatoxin only","HBV only","All of these","Alcohol only"]'::jsonb, 'All of these', 'HCC: cirrhosis main risk factor. HBV (even without cirrhosis). Aflatoxin B1 causes TP53 mutation (R249S).'),
('0cfa6bda-ca8f-5e56-9a79-3773c788d137'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'The most common esophageal malignancy worldwide is:', '["Lymphoma","Small cell carcinoma","Adenocarcinoma","Squamous cell carcinoma","Melanoma"]'::jsonb, 'Squamous cell carcinoma', 'SCC more common worldwide. Adenocarcinoma more common in Western countries (Barrett esophagus).'),
('48effbbe-93fd-5f24-b3a5-198ee7398b3a'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Signet ring cell carcinoma of stomach is associated with:', '["Medullary type clinical presentation","Diffuse type (Lauren classification), poor prognosis","Mucinous type clinical presentation","Papillary type clinical presentation","Intestinal type clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Diffuse type (Lauren classification), poor prognosis', 'Diffuse gastric cancer: CDH1 (E-cadherin) mutation. Linitis plastica appearance.'),
('1d798d3d-bfe9-5a20-9cc0-2aa5f4c034d0'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Prophylactic gastrectomy is indicated in:', '["Hereditary diffuse gastric cancer","H pylori infection clinical presentation","Gastric carcinoid clinical presentation","Intestinal type clinical presentation","Gastric adenoma clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Hereditary diffuse gastric cancer', 'CDH1 mutation carriers: 70% lifetime risk of diffuse gastric cancer. Prophylactic gastrectomy.'),
('474b2e12-f5e0-5294-b538-73e577263b79'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Cervical cancer screening uses:', '["Ultrasound clinical presentation","Biopsy only clinical presentation","CT scan clinical presentation","Colposcopy only clinical presentation","Pap smear (cytology) and HPV DNA testing"]'::jsonb, 'Pap smear (cytology) and HPV DNA testing', 'Pap smear: detects dysplasia before cancer. HPV testing for high-risk subtypes (16, 18).'),
('d933d126-b946-5c28-bc2b-1f5829b91a96'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'The most common ovarian malignancy is:', '["Teratoma","Endometrioid carcinoma","Mucinous carcinoma","Serous epithelial ovarian carcinoma","Clear cell carcinoma"]'::jsonb, 'Serous epithelial ovarian carcinoma', 'High-grade serous carcinoma most common. Associated with TP53 mutation and BRCA mutations.'),
('3de6ec33-593c-5395-a5d3-ae17d010b5a1'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Which testicular tumor secretes hCG?', '["Embryonal carcinoma","Yolk sac tumor","Teratoma","Choriocarcinoma","Seminoma"]'::jsonb, 'Choriocarcinoma', 'Choriocarcinoma: high hCG. Syncytiotrophoblasts. Pure choriocarcinoma very rare, aggressive.'),
('c58bb3a9-722d-5027-afa7-d499908049eb'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Seminoma of testis is sensitive to:', '["Surgery alone","Chemotherapy only","Radiation therapy","Immunotherapy","Hormonal therapy"]'::jsonb, 'Radiation therapy', 'Seminoma: very radiosensitive. Also chemosensitive (cisplatin). Good prognosis.'),
('bb94705c-05e6-50dd-b7ab-f41aeec6714f'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'The most common head and neck cancer is:', '["Lymphoma","Adenocarcinoma","Squamous cell carcinoma","Melanoma","Sarcoma"]'::jsonb, 'Squamous cell carcinoma', 'HNSCC: risk factors tobacco, alcohol, HPV (oropharynx). HPV+ HNSCC has better prognosis.'),
('1443e614-4158-52ad-874e-dc9d73d30545'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'HPV-related oropharyngeal SCC has:', '["Same prognosis","No response to chemotherapy","Better prognosis than HPV-negative","Worse prognosis","No response to radiation"]'::jsonb, 'Better prognosis than HPV-negative', 'HPV+ (p16+) oropharyngeal SCC: responds better to chemo-radiation. Non-smokers, younger patients.'),
('be86031e-1ab1-5179-b8af-dcd7e939004c'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Oral leukoplakia is a:', '["Benign lesion","Inflammatory condition","Malignant lesion","Infectious condition","Premalignant lesion"]'::jsonb, 'Premalignant lesion', 'Leukoplakia: white patch. Risk of malignant transformation 1-5%. Erythroplakia has higher risk (10-15%).'),
('f2e62eed-2931-5626-8531-99c7e47ed8bf'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'With respect to management, AFP (alpha-fetoprotein) is elevated in:', '["Breast cancer clinical presentation","Pancreatic cancer clinical presentation","Colon cancer clinical presentation","Lung cancer clinical presentation","Hepatocellular carcinoma and germ cell tumors"]'::jsonb, 'Hepatocellular carcinoma and germ cell tumors', 'AFP: HCC (70%), yolk sac tumor. Also elevated in pregnancy and hepatitis.'),
('f455fa87-b4cf-5ab9-a47d-775b20d90033'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'CEA (carcinoembryonic antigen) is associated with:', '["Colorectal carcinoma","Ovarian cancer","HCC","Prostate cancer","Breast cancer"]'::jsonb, 'Colorectal carcinoma', 'CEA: colorectal cancer surveillance. Also elevated in smoking, IBD, pancreatitis.'),
('0b6d50c4-1d9e-5166-8d1c-79ab72fbe7c4'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'CA-125 is a marker for:', '["Prostate carcinoma","Ovarian carcinoma","Breast carcinoma","Colorectal carcinoma","Pancreatic carcinoma"]'::jsonb, 'Ovarian carcinoma', 'CA-125 used for monitoring ovarian cancer treatment response and recurrence.'),
('078679fa-d068-5646-8f3d-ea810bb4f8a8'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'CA 15-3 and CA 27-29 are markers for:', '["Pancreatic carcinoma","Lung carcinoma","Colon carcinoma","Ovarian carcinoma","Breast carcinoma"]'::jsonb, 'Breast carcinoma', 'CA 15-3: breast cancer monitoring. Also elevated in other adenocarcinomas.'),
('4aafcb51-de57-5984-aa29-25f5b69fa74c'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'PSA is a marker for:', '["Penile cancer","Renal cancer","Prostate cancer","Bladder cancer","Testicular cancer"]'::jsonb, 'Prostate cancer', 'PSA: prostate-specific antigen. Used for screening (>4 ng/mL) and monitoring recurrence.'),
('8fb01573-eb08-5e56-ae30-8b11025904c8'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'LDH is elevated in which malignancy?', '["Breast cancer","HCC","Testicular germ cell tumors","Pancreatic cancer","Colon cancer"]'::jsonb, 'Testicular germ cell tumors', 'LDH: prognostic factor in testicular GCT, lymphoma, melanoma. Tumor burden marker.'),
('e8747705-8b00-58fe-90d0-cb93d85443ef'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'For a patient presenting with symptoms, Beta-hCG is a marker for:', '["Yolk sac tumor clinical presentation","Embryonal carcinoma clinical presentation","Seminoma clinical presentation","Choriocarcinoma and gestational trophoblastic disease","Teratoma clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Choriocarcinoma and gestational trophoblastic disease', 'hCG: choriocarcinoma, GTD. Also elevated in some germ cell tumors (syncytiotrophoblast).'),
('3009b771-d378-5e2c-a5f5-91e50ffa960f'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Core needle biopsy vs FNA: Core biopsy provides:', '["Tissue architecture and multiple sections","No receptor status clinical presentation","No diagnosis clinical presentation","Only cytology clinical presentation","No grading possible clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Tissue architecture and multiple sections', 'Core biopsy: preserves tissue architecture. FNA: only cytologic features, faster, less invasive.'),
('e53c9258-d1a0-547a-bab1-f59d40083b2a'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Frozen section examination is used for:', '["Genetic testing clinical presentation","Staging only clinical presentation","Screening clinical presentation","Routine diagnosis clinical presentation","Intraoperative margin assessment and diagnosis"]'::jsonb, 'Intraoperative margin assessment and diagnosis', 'Frozen section: 20-30 min. Used for margins, lymph node metastasis, tissue identification.'),
('e22f2495-2aa3-5303-81fe-9014632e60ff'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'TNM staging T stands for:', '["Primary tumor extent","Tumor marker","Treatment response","Tumor type","Tumor grade"]'::jsonb, 'Primary tumor extent', 'T: primary tumor (T0-T4). N: regional lymph nodes (N0-N3). M: distant metastasis (M0/M1).'),
('cf81dce0-7cfa-5b9c-9047-e0e07d0b5b17'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Stage III colon cancer implies:', '["Any T, N1-N2, M0","Any T, any N, M1","T1-T2, N0, M0","T4, N0, M0","T3-T4, N0, M0"]'::jsonb, 'Any T, N1-N2, M0', 'Stage III: lymph node-positive disease. Adjuvant chemo indicated.'),
('91518b58-46e1-5bde-8763-5a27fcc2511f'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Neoadjuvant therapy is given:', '["Without surgery","After surgery","During surgery","Before surgery","For palliation only"]'::jsonb, 'Before surgery', 'Neoadjuvant: before surgery. Goal: reduce tumor size, increase resectability, assess response.'),
('c8faa54c-bbe3-5bb0-8edc-1c36b8ec9879'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Adjuvant therapy is given:', '["For metastatic disease only","Before surgery","At recurrence","Instead of surgery","After surgery"]'::jsonb, 'After surgery', 'Adjuvant: after curative resection. Improves disease-free and overall survival.'),
('32d8de32-4c41-5f8f-b90d-25c673d7b44b'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Metastasectomy is indicated when:', '["Only if primary unknown clinical presentation","Never indicated clinical presentation","Multiple diffuse metastases clinical presentation","All metastatic disease clinical presentation","Limited, resectable metastases with controlled primary"]'::jsonb, 'Limited, resectable metastases with controlled primary', 'Metastasectomy: liver (colorectal), lung (sarcoma). Must have controlled primary and adequate remnant.'),
('1eedcce7-88e9-5229-a11c-b5c629fe7cf8'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Sentinel lymph node biopsy is used for:', '["Only breast cancer","All cancers","Only GI cancers","Breast cancer and melanoma","Only melanoma"]'::jsonb, 'Breast cancer and melanoma', 'Sentinel node: first draining node. If negative, avoids complete lymphadenectomy.'),
('a2dad1f3-2d22-53c1-ba6f-e3342dc13454'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Complete (radical) lymphadenectomy may cause:', '["Pain","Lymphedema","Fever","Hematoma","Seroma"]'::jsonb, 'Lymphedema', 'Lymphedema: complication of axillary/inguinal lymph node dissection. Risk of cellulitis.'),
('a0de20b3-32c0-54cd-a349-64c891fd7134'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Mohs micrographic surgery is indicated for:', '["Brain tumors clinical presentation","Colon cancer clinical presentation","Lung cancer clinical presentation","Skin cancers (BCC, SCC) in cosmetically sensitive areas","Breast cancer clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Skin cancers (BCC, SCC) in cosmetically sensitive areas', 'Mohs: 100% margin examination. Highest cure rate for BCC/SCC. Tissue sparing.'),
('b7ad8f47-4202-5831-81ed-e817aa657b59'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'R0 resection means:', '["Macroscopic residual tumor","Microscopically negative margins","Microscopic residual tumor","Positive margin","Indeterminate margin"]'::jsonb, 'Microscopically negative margins', 'R0: curative resection. R1: microscopic positive. R2: macroscopic residual.'),
('a87e2922-9f27-5406-b779-fbbf97b171e4'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Cytoreductive surgery (debulking) is used in:', '["Ovarian cancer","Pancreatic cancer","Lung cancer","Breast cancer","Colon cancer"]'::jsonb, 'Ovarian cancer', 'Debulking: remove >1 cm residual disease. Improves chemo efficacy. Used in ovarian and some peritoneal cancers.'),
('2bdd393c-2743-5b00-bb83-a0aeabacdefc'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Cell cycle-specific chemotherapeutic agents are most effective against:', '["Solid tumors","Slow-growing tumors","Tumors in G0 phase","Tumors with high growth fraction","All tumors equally"]'::jsonb, 'Tumors with high growth fraction', 'Phase-specific agents (e.g., methotrexate, vinca alkaloids): target dividing cells. High growth fraction -> better response.'),
('a6bc9a93-8e57-55c8-8122-d72c88984b73'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Which chemotherapeutic agent is an alkylating agent?', '["5-Fluorouracil","Methotrexate","Cyclophosphamide","Doxorubicin","Vincristine"]'::jsonb, 'Cyclophosphamide', 'Cyclophosphamide: alkylating agent. Crosslinks DNA. Hemorrhagic cystitis side effect (prevent with mesna).'),
('7d734bf9-016d-5db2-94e4-231968f04e20'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Doxorubicin (Adriamycin) has dose-limiting toxicity:', '["Nephrotoxicity","Cardiomyopathy","Neurotoxicity","Pulmonary fibrosis","Bone marrow suppression"]'::jsonb, 'Cardiomyopathy', 'Anthracycline cardiotoxicity: cumulative >550 mg/m². Dexrazoxane for cardioprotection.'),
('f7589991-65c0-5ef6-898b-29653ff7397c'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Cisplatin is associated with:', '["Nephrotoxicity, ototoxicity, peripheral neuropathy","Anaphylaxis clinical presentation","Hemorrhagic cystitis clinical presentation","Pulmonary fibrosis clinical presentation","Cardiomyopathy clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Nephrotoxicity, ototoxicity, peripheral neuropathy', 'Cisplatin: platinum-based. Nephrotoxicity (hydrate aggressively), ototoxicity, neurotoxicity.'),
('81285d26-e892-54d2-9d85-d4e5e65c780a'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Taxanes (paclitaxel, docetaxel) act by:', '["Stabilizing microtubules","Blocking mitosis","Inhibiting topoisomerase","Inhibiting thymidylate synthase","Crosslinking DNA"]'::jsonb, 'Stabilizing microtubules', 'Taxanes: bind tubulin, stabilize microtubules. M phase arrest. Side effect: peripheral neuropathy.'),
('0393febb-0162-5cc3-89b7-e3368a7976fc'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Regarding clinical scenarios, Methotrexate inhibits:', '["Topoisomerase II clinical presentation","Dihydrofolate reductase (DHFR) -> blocks DNA synthesis","Protein synthesis clinical presentation","Microtubule depolymerization clinical presentation","Thymidylate synthase clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Dihydrofolate reductase (DHFR) -> blocks DNA synthesis', 'Methotrexate: folate analog. Inhibits DHFR. Rescue with leucovorin. S-phase specific.'),
('cb14f743-819d-5686-9314-b3ff2c61e9a2'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', '5-Fluorouracil (5-FU) inhibits:', '["DHFR","Topoisomerase","Thymidylate synthase","Microtubules","DNA crosslinking"]'::jsonb, 'Thymidylate synthase', '5-FU: pyrimidine analog. Inhibits TS. Used in colorectal cancer. Potentiated by leucovorin.'),
('aef186c1-063f-5ce4-a2a5-567785769202'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Irinotecan and Topotecan are:', '["Microtubule poisons","Alkylating agents","Topoisomerase II inhibitors","Topoisomerase I inhibitors","Antimetabolites"]'::jsonb, 'Topoisomerase I inhibitors', 'Topoisomerase I inhibitors: cause DNA single-strand breaks. Irinotecan for colon cancer.'),
('22bb61dc-16a9-53e1-b474-56fd46ecf29d'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Etoposide is:', '["Topoisomerase II inhibitor","Microtubule stabilizer","Antimetabolite","Alkylating agent","Topoisomerase I inhibitor"]'::jsonb, 'Topoisomerase II inhibitor', 'Etoposide: topoisomerase II inhibitor. Used in small cell lung cancer and testicular cancer.'),
('137f1ffd-1dbd-5afa-b58f-4daa1e7144ad'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Imatinib mesylate targets:', '["BCR-ABL (CML) and c-KIT","VEGF","BRAF","HER2","EGFR"]'::jsonb, 'BCR-ABL (CML) and c-KIT', 'Imatinib: tyrosine kinase inhibitor. BCR-ABL in CML, c-KIT/PDGFRA in GIST.'),
('310cc716-2676-594d-90fa-f67dcc75d0e4'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Trastuzumab (Herceptin) targets:', '["CD20","BRAF","EGFR","HER2/neu","VEGF"]'::jsonb, 'HER2/neu', 'Trastuzumab: monoclonal antibody against HER2. Used in HER2+ breast and gastric cancer.'),
('76337869-f3d6-5cca-87f3-3e01b56144ea'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'In a surgical context, Rituximab targets:', '["CD20 on B-cells","CD52","CD22","CD19","CD33"]'::jsonb, 'CD20 on B-cells', 'Rituximab: anti-CD20. Used for NHL, CLL, rheumatoid arthritis. Can cause infusion reactions.'),
('4ea51712-e666-5c92-81ab-290bbadbd522'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Bevacizumab (Avastin) is an:', '["Anti-EGFR antibody","Anti-HER2 antibody","Anti-VEGF monoclonal antibody","Checkpoint inhibitor","TKI"]'::jsonb, 'Anti-VEGF monoclonal antibody', 'Bevacizumab: inhibits VEGF. Used in colorectal, lung, renal, ovarian. Risk: hypertension, bleeding.'),
('aea822ad-d90f-5e31-a489-5581440c7813'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Checkpoint inhibitors target:', '["VEGF","PD-1/PD-L1 or CTLA-4","EGFR","HER2","BRAF"]'::jsonb, 'PD-1/PD-L1 or CTLA-4', 'PD-1 (nivolumab, pembrolizumab), CTLA-4 (ipilimumab). Reactivates anti-tumor T-cells.'),
('d321ec50-4e06-5582-93eb-b6fcdc609c46'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Pembrolizumab is used for tumors with:', '["MSI-high (microsatellite instability) or high TMB","BRAF mutation clinical presentation","EGFR mutation clinical presentation","ALK rearrangement clinical presentation","HER2 amplification clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'MSI-high (microsatellite instability) or high TMB', 'Pembrolizumab approved for MSI-high tumors regardless of site. Also for high TMB, PD-L1+ tumors.'),
('5c7e4d5b-0f66-59d7-8384-b56a8b99241f'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'CAR-T cell therapy involves:', '["Monoclonal antibodies clinical presentation","Vaccine therapy clinical presentation","Checkpoint inhibitors clinical presentation","Cytokine therapy clinical presentation","Engineering patient''s T-cells to express CAR targeting tumor antigen"]'::jsonb, 'Engineering patient''s T-cells to express CAR targeting tumor antigen', 'CAR-T: chimeric antigen receptor. Targets CD19 in B-ALL, DLBCL. Risk: cytokine release syndrome.'),
('1b7bda78-4a69-57d4-bde7-01ac8c064310'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'EGFR tyrosine kinase inhibitors (gefitinib, erlotinib) are effective in:', '["KRAS-mutant lung cancer","Breast cancer","EGFR-mutant lung adenocarcinoma","ALL lung cancer","Colon cancer"]'::jsonb, 'EGFR-mutant lung adenocarcinoma', 'EGFR TKIs sensitive in EGFR-mutant NSCLC. T790M mutation confers resistance (osimertinib).'),
('cd7fa6df-d22b-5124-92be-3a150513d16b'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'ALK inhibitors (crizotinib, alectinib) are used in:', '["All lung cancers","ROS1-rearranged tumors","EGFR-mutated NSCLC","ALK-rearranged NSCLC","KRAS-mutated lung cancer"]'::jsonb, 'ALK-rearranged NSCLC', 'ALK fusion in 3-5% NSCLC. Crizotinib first-line. Alectinib, ceritinib for resistant disease.'),
('53828cd3-c9e2-5d91-9624-db0a322a38e2'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'PARP inhibitors (olaparib) are used in:', '["ALK-rearranged cancer clinical presentation","BRAF-mutated cancer clinical presentation","ALL cancer clinical presentation","EGFR-mutated cancer clinical presentation","BRCA-mutated ovarian and breast cancer"]'::jsonb, 'BRCA-mutated ovarian and breast cancer', 'PARP inhibitors: synthetic lethality with BRCA deficiency. HRD (homologous recombination deficiency).'),
('50c58f6d-9721-5ace-b0f9-d6b987f334c6'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Tumor lysis syndrome is characterized by:', '["Hypophosphatemia clinical presentation","Hypouricemia clinical presentation","Hypercalcemia clinical presentation","Hypokalemia clinical presentation","Hyperuricemia, hyperkalemia, hyperphosphatemia, hypocalcemia"]'::jsonb, 'Hyperuricemia, hyperkalemia, hyperphosphatemia, hypocalcemia', 'TLS: after chemo in high-proliferative tumors (ALL, Burkitt lymphoma). Prevent with allopurinol/rasburicase and hydration.'),
('f1c8bc00-182e-5647-bad6-04724e0b4949'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Radiation therapy acts primarily by:', '["Apoptosis induction clinical presentation","Necrosis clinical presentation","DNA damage (double-strand breaks) leading to cell death","Membrane damage clinical presentation","Protein denaturation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'DNA damage (double-strand breaks) leading to cell death', 'Ionizing radiation: direct DNA damage + ROS production. Cells most sensitive in M and G2 phases.'),
('e910c125-29c2-5958-8a68-02daee5a2204'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Therapeutic ratio in radiotherapy is:', '["Field size","Total dose / Fraction size","TD (tolerance dose) / ED","Number of fractions","Dose rate"]'::jsonb, 'TD (tolerance dose) / ED', 'Therapeutic ratio >1: tumor damage > normal tissue. Fractionation improves normal tissue tolerance.'),
('f88f5998-b1c5-5574-af69-f68b55c892e6'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Linear accelerator (LINAC) generates:', '["Neutrons","Gamma rays from cobalt-60","Alpha particles","Protons","High-energy photons (X-rays) and electrons"]'::jsonb, 'High-energy photons (X-rays) and electrons', 'LINAC: most common radiotherapy machine. Produces megavoltage X-rays and electrons.'),
('77105d5c-950d-5032-80c4-587104d6f005'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Brachytherapy involves:', '["Systemic radiopharmaceutical","External beam radiation","Gamma knife","Radioactive source placed inside or near tumor","Proton beam"]'::jsonb, 'Radioactive source placed inside or near tumor', 'Brachytherapy: I-125 seeds (prostate), Ir-192 (cervix). High dose to tumor, low to surrounding tissue.'),
('0fa5502e-f13c-565b-b083-1608e7629a3a'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Acute radiation toxicity to skin (dermatitis) occurs:', '["During or within weeks of radiation","Never","Months to years after","Only with chemo-radiation","Only after high dose"]'::jsonb, 'During or within weeks of radiation', 'Acute: erythema, dry/moist desquamation. Chronic: fibrosis, telangiectasias, atrophy.'),
('b0640d6b-78f0-5243-a2e7-efb79dc7d582'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Radiation-induced fibrosis is a:', '["Reversible effect","Immediate effect","Inflammatory response","Chronic (late) effect of radiation","Acute effect"]'::jsonb, 'Chronic (late) effect of radiation', 'Late effects: fibrosis, vascular damage, necrosis. Occur months to years after RT.'),
('e2a8fa55-d960-5ac4-a894-f624766b1100'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Stereotactic radiosurgery (SRS) is used for:', '["Spinal tumors","Small intracranial tumors","Large brain tumors","All brain tumors","Lung tumors"]'::jsonb, 'Small intracranial tumors', 'SRS (Gamma Knife, CyberKnife): high precision, single fraction. For small (<3 cm) intracranial lesions.'),
('b61474b2-d767-5ced-9b24-7675c20e5d1d'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Stereotactic body radiotherapy (SBRT) is used for:', '["Breast cancer only clinical presentation","Prostate cancer only clinical presentation","Brain tumors only clinical presentation","All cancers clinical presentation","Early-stage lung cancer, liver metastases"]'::jsonb, 'Early-stage lung cancer, liver metastases', 'SBRT: high-dose, few fractions. For stage I NSCLC, oligometastases. Excellent local control.'),
('ed022484-27d4-57ef-b41e-34f8c5af3130'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Radiosensitizers enhance:', '["Effects of surgery","Effects of chemotherapy","Normal tissue protection","Effects of immunotherapy","Effects of radiation"]'::jsonb, 'Effects of radiation', 'Radiosensitizers: chemo agents that increase tumor radiosensitivity. Concurrent chemo-RT.'),
('c1b660af-4391-5edf-909b-c01b2491305c'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Radioprotectors (e.g., amifostine) protect:', '["Normal tissues (salivary glands, mucosa) from radiation","Bone marrow only clinical presentation","Kidneys only clinical presentation","Lungs only clinical presentation","Tumor from radiation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Normal tissues (salivary glands, mucosa) from radiation', 'Amifostine: free radical scavenger. Protects salivary glands in head & neck RT.'),
('d6b47133-0293-53ae-ab57-43d3551db7d3'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'The most common cancer worldwide in males is:', '["Prostate cancer","Stomach cancer","Liver cancer","Colorectal cancer","Lung cancer"]'::jsonb, 'Lung cancer', 'Lung cancer incidence #1 in men. Breast cancer #1 in women. Lung #1 in mortality overall.'),
('2f57ed29-5756-5048-98b3-a9184594760d'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'The most common cancer worldwide in females is:', '["Lung cancer","Cervical cancer","Thyroid cancer","Breast cancer","Colorectal cancer"]'::jsonb, 'Breast cancer', 'Breast cancer most common in women globally. Cervical cancer in developing countries.'),
('3a6b1a34-dca5-57ab-b35b-fff081f2d6a4'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'The leading cause of cancer death worldwide is:', '["Breast cancer","Stomach cancer","Colorectal cancer","Lung cancer","Liver cancer"]'::jsonb, 'Lung cancer', 'Lung cancer #1 in both incidence and mortality in men. #1 in mortality in women in developed countries.'),
('93891223-49aa-5038-baf0-fe82a7919275'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'The most common cancer in Pakistan is:', '["Liver","Lung","Oral cavity","Breast","Colorectal"]'::jsonb, 'Oral cavity', 'Oral cancer incidence high in Pakistan due to gutka, paan, naswar. Also breast cancer in women.'),
('4fde2655-25e8-5995-8f80-b8d8ce73f92b'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Colorectal cancer screening is done by:', '["Ultrasound","Colonoscopy","X-ray","CT scan","MRI"]'::jsonb, 'Colonoscopy', 'Colonoscopy: gold standard. Also FIT/fecal occult blood test. Starting age: 45 (US) or 50.'),
('0f80344f-3300-5757-9362-ff3c1a5b67c2'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Mammography screening for breast cancer starts at age:', '["No screening needed","30","60","40-50 years","20"]'::jsonb, '40-50 years', 'USPSTF: 50-74 biennially. ACS: 45-54 annually. High-risk (BRCA): earlier, with MRI.'),
('0f10a908-cbb5-58fd-aa57-fc9b98226b22'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Pap smear screening for cervical cancer should begin at:', '["Age 16","Age 40","Age 21","Age 30","At menopause"]'::jsonb, 'Age 21', 'Pap smear every 3 years. HPV co-testing every 5 years after age 30.'),
('2e1fb3c1-46b0-51b1-8bce-6e087a00f6e2'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Low-dose CT screening for lung cancer is recommended for:', '["Only previous lung cancer","Non-smokers","All smokers","All adults","Heavy smokers"]'::jsonb, 'Heavy smokers', 'LDCT reduces lung cancer mortality by 20% in high-risk smokers. Annual screening.'),
('a5672611-0f28-5349-9e9d-daaee2ced327'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Cancer screening tests must demonstrate:', '["High sensitivity clinical presentation","Reduction in cancer-specific mortality","Lower cost clinical presentation","Better detection only clinical presentation","Patient preference clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Reduction in cancer-specific mortality', 'Screening effectiveness measured by mortality reduction (e.g., mammography, colonoscopy, Pap).'),
('e7e2996a-fb52-5856-acbf-a49f4c02c701'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Lead time bias in screening refers to:', '["Delay in diagnosis clinical presentation","Apparent improved survival due to earlier detection, not actual prolongation","Overdiagnosis of indolent cancers clinical presentation","False positive results clinical presentation","Selection of healthier patients clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Apparent improved survival due to earlier detection, not actual prolongation', 'Lead time bias: screening seems to improve survival even with same death date.'),
('07b78c6d-1d92-5a4b-83f0-a3597f8fbf20'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Li-Fraumeni syndrome is caused by:', '["NF1 mutation","TP53 germline mutation","APC mutation","BRCA1 mutation","RB1 mutation"]'::jsonb, 'TP53 germline mutation', 'Li-Fraumeni: TP53. Multiple primary cancers: sarcoma, breast, brain, adrenal, leukemia.'),
('e6810191-841e-50c9-a7fa-4915376db1fa'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Familial adenomatous polyposis (FAP) is caused by:', '["MYH mutation","MSH2 mutation","MLH1 mutation","APC gene mutation","STK11 mutation"]'::jsonb, 'APC gene mutation', 'FAP: hundreds of colorectal adenomas. APC mutation. Prophylactic colectomy by age 20-25.'),
('54ba1cb4-f0ae-5145-9add-67a4f06e47c5'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Hereditary breast and ovarian cancer syndrome is due to:', '["BRCA1/BRCA2 mutations","TP53 mutation","CDH1 mutation","STK11 mutation","PTEN mutation"]'::jsonb, 'BRCA1/BRCA2 mutations', 'BRCA1/2: homologous recombination repair. Breast, ovary, pancreas, prostate cancer risk.'),
('a0c46f74-0edc-58dd-8c5c-8d8a1dab3b69'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Cowden syndrome is caused by:', '["PTEN mutation","BRCA1","STK11","TP53","APC"]'::jsonb, 'PTEN mutation', 'Cowden: PTEN. Trichilemmomas, oral papillomas, macrocephaly. Breast and thyroid cancer.'),
('850d34f0-4c16-597c-9369-4418bd7a0b24'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Peutz-Jeghers syndrome has:', '["CDH1 mutation","STK11/LKB1 mutation","PTEN mutation","APC mutation","MLH1 mutation"]'::jsonb, 'STK11/LKB1 mutation', 'PJS: hamartomatous polyps, lip pigmentation. Increased risk of GI, breast, pancreas, ovarian cancer.'),
('1c954cc7-b3b4-5b4d-8b89-137403f924d9'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Von Hippel-Lindau disease is caused by:', '["TSC1 mutation","RET mutation","VHL gene mutation","MENIN mutation","NF1 mutation"]'::jsonb, 'VHL gene mutation', 'VHL: hemangioblastoma (CNS, retina), RCC, pheochromocytoma. Clear cell RCC characteristic.'),
('8c1127b2-ab46-5f49-b473-30d44f54c4d9'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'MEN 2A and 2B are caused by:', '["MENIN mutation","NF1 mutation","RET proto-oncogene mutation","TP53 mutation","VHL mutation"]'::jsonb, 'RET proto-oncogene mutation', 'MEN 2A: MTC, pheochromocytoma, hyperparathyroidism. MEN 2B: MTC, pheochromocytoma, mucosal neuromas.'),
('3f157969-9401-57d0-b03c-bcaf3ebffa00'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'On diagnostic evaluation, Neurofibromatosis type 1 (NF1) is caused by:', '["RB1 mutation clinical presentation","NF1 gene mutation (17q11), neurofibromin deficiency","TSC1 mutation clinical presentation","VHL mutation clinical presentation","NF2 mutation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'NF1 gene mutation (17q11), neurofibromin deficiency', 'NF1 (von Recklinghausen): café-au-lait spots, neurofibromas, Lisch nodules, optic glioma.'),
('b15a1f9f-be2b-547f-8e09-a4479356dc35'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Retinoblastoma is caused by:', '["MYC amplification","RAS mutation","BCL2 translocation","TP53 mutation","RB1 gene mutation"]'::jsonb, 'RB1 gene mutation', 'Knudson two-hit: both RB1 alleles inactivated. Hereditary (bilateral) vs sporadic (unilateral).'),
('641dea96-0145-5ca5-95c4-9bb643a5d359'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Two-hit hypothesis in tumor suppressor genes means:', '["Tumorigenesis requires 2 mutations in oncogenes","Two hits from environmental factors","Two different genes must be mutated","Both alleles must be inactivated for tumorigenesis","One allele sufficient"]'::jsonb, 'Both alleles must be inactivated for tumorigenesis', 'Knudson''s hypothesis: first hit (inherited/somatic), second hit (somatic). RB1 paradigm.'),
('b4a36e50-f03e-5379-89aa-850bbdbe553e'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Superior vena cava (SVC) syndrome is most commonly caused by:', '["Goiter","Lymphoma","Metastatic breast cancer","Thymoma","Lung cancer"]'::jsonb, 'Lung cancer', 'SVC syndrome: obstruction of SVC. Lung cancer (SCLC) most common cause. Emergency: stent + RT/chemo.'),
('9e3e60b1-334b-5bed-8964-a5c48ed1d721'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Malignant spinal cord compression emergency requires:', '["Physical therapy clinical presentation","Observation clinical presentation","Antibiotics clinical presentation","Urgent MRI and steroids (dexamethasone) + RT/surgery","Analgesics only clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Urgent MRI and steroids (dexamethasone) + RT/surgery', 'Spinal cord compression: back pain, motor/sensory loss. MRI within 24h. Dexamethasone + RT.'),
('17d2696a-6b71-5afb-a3af-4cfcf6a347f8'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Hypercalcemia of malignancy is most commonly caused by:', '["Multiple myeloma","PTHrP secretion","Vitamin D excess","Bone metastases","Primary hyperparathyroidism"]'::jsonb, 'PTHrP secretion', 'PTHrP: most common mechanism (SCC lung, HNSCC, renal, breast). Treat: bisphosphonates (zoledronic acid).'),
('4518eefa-fd27-5023-9305-411a8fa6329a'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Febrile neutropenia (ANC <500) requires:', '["Observation clinical presentation","Immediate broad-spectrum IV antibiotics","Antipyretics only clinical presentation","Growth factors only clinical presentation","Oral antibiotics clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Immediate broad-spectrum IV antibiotics', 'Febrile neutropenia: oncologic emergency. High mortality. Empirical piperacillin-tazobactam or cefepime.'),
('75d0caf3-fd37-50fe-90dd-04674db66a6c'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Tumor lysis syndrome prophylaxis includes:', '["No prophylaxis needed clinical presentation","All of these: hydration, allopurinol/rasburicase, monitoring","Monitoring only clinical presentation","Allopurinol only clinical presentation","Hydration only clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'All of these: hydration, allopurinol/rasburicase, monitoring', 'TLS: high-risk in ALL, Burkitt. Prevent with aggressive hydration, rasburicase for high uric acid.'),
('068bbab6-7a7f-5fbe-bf39-abc4077de77f'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Hemorrhagic cystitis from cyclophosphamide prevented by:', '["Hydration only","Foley catheter","Allopurinol","Antibiotics","Mesna"]'::jsonb, 'Mesna', 'Mesna: binds acrolein (toxic metabolite of cyclophosphamide) in bladder. Also aggressive hydration.'),
('7739686e-57db-576b-95c5-8d33525e9f64'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Chemotherapy extravasation management:', '["Apply pressure clinical presentation","Massage area clinical presentation","Inject saline to dilute clinical presentation","Continue infusion clinical presentation","Stop infusion, aspirate, apply cold/warm compress, specific antidote"]'::jsonb, 'Stop infusion, aspirate, apply cold/warm compress, specific antidote', 'Extravasation: stop immediately. Vesicants (doxorubicin, vinca): specific antidotes. Plastic surgery consult.'),
('872c7252-a00c-578d-860e-8bf4559c79b1'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Palmar-plantar erythrodysesthesia (hand-foot syndrome) is caused by:', '["Cisplatin clinical presentation","Bevacizumab clinical presentation","Cyclophosphamide clinical presentation","Imatinib clinical presentation","Capecitabine, 5-FU, liposomal doxorubicin"]'::jsonb, 'Capecitabine, 5-FU, liposomal doxorubicin', 'HFS: redness, desquamation of palms/soles. D/C capecitabine temporarily, supportive care.'),
('76ed8c9b-4b2b-58be-91c4-14fbe44de682'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Radiation recall dermatitis:', '["Allergic reaction clinical presentation","Chronic radiation effect clinical presentation","Inflammatory reaction in previously irradiated field triggered by chemotherapy","Acute radiation effect clinical presentation","Infection clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Inflammatory reaction in previously irradiated field triggered by chemotherapy', 'Radiation recall: occurs days after chemo (doxorubicin, taxanes, gemcitabine) in prior RT field.'),
('e5ac42d6-c168-5e78-aad9-9d878a3dfede'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Chemotherapy-induced peripheral neuropathy is classic for:', '["Methotrexate clinical presentation","Vinca alkaloids, taxanes, cisplatin, oxaliplatin","Cyclophosphamide clinical presentation","5-FU clinical presentation","Bleomycin clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Vinca alkaloids, taxanes, cisplatin, oxaliplatin', 'CIPN: vincristine (early), taxanes (painful), cisplatin (chronic), oxaliplatin (cold-induced).'),
('402f01c1-f03c-5512-acb6-105b0335d4e2'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'HPV vaccine prevents:', '["All cancers clinical presentation","Lung cancer clinical presentation","Breast cancer clinical presentation","Colon cancer clinical presentation","Cervical cancer (HPV 16/18) and genital warts"]'::jsonb, 'Cervical cancer (HPV 16/18) and genital warts', 'Quadrivalent (6,11,16,18) and nonavalent vaccine. Given at age 11-12 years. Prevents 70-90% cervical cancer.'),
('eae41b45-7b3b-5ce3-b2c9-c26285c953dc'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'HBV vaccination prevents:', '["Hepatitis only","Hepatocellular carcinoma","Liver cancer only","No cancer prevention","Cirrhosis only"]'::jsonb, 'Hepatocellular carcinoma', 'HBV vaccine: first anti-cancer vaccine. Reduces HCC risk by preventing chronic HBV infection.'),
('974e3fd3-26ce-5f31-a8d2-bae82cb1db2f'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Chemoprevention for breast cancer:', '["Aspirin clinical presentation","Statins clinical presentation","Tamoxifen or raloxifene in high-risk women","Metformin clinical presentation","Vitamin D clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Tamoxifen or raloxifene in high-risk women', 'Tamoxifen (SERM): reduces ER+ breast cancer risk by 50% in high-risk women. Side effects: DVT, endometrial cancer.'),
('3d7afab0-f4b3-5292-9481-273469740091'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Aspirin chemoprevention reduces risk of:', '["Colorectal cancer","Breast cancer","All cancers","Prostate cancer","Lung cancer"]'::jsonb, 'Colorectal cancer', 'Aspirin: reduces colorectal adenoma and cancer incidence. Proposed mechanism: COX-2 inhibition.'),
('e0375ee4-2d37-553a-851b-2d0d4a6477e4'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Cancer cachexia is characterized by:', '["Weight loss, muscle wasting, anorexia, systemic inflammation","Increased appetite clinical presentation","Normal nutrition clinical presentation","Edema clinical presentation","Weight gain clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Weight loss, muscle wasting, anorexia, systemic inflammation', 'Cancer cachexia: multifactorial. Cytokines (TNF-alpha, IL-6). Poor prognosis. Treat with nutrition and anti-inflammatory.'),
('6f91fa43-197c-5bbe-9b2f-d6b570d9684b'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Paraneoplastic syndromes are:', '["Direct metastasis clinical presentation","Psychiatric disorders clinical presentation","Infection complications clinical presentation","Symptom complexes caused by tumor-derived mediators, not direct invasion","Treatment side effects clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Symptom complexes caused by tumor-derived mediators, not direct invasion', 'Paraneoplastic: ectopic hormones, autoimmune. SCLC (SIADH, Eaton-Lambert). Renal cell (erythropoietin).'),
('080c9415-087e-547d-806f-66359bf9e926'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Eaton-Lambert syndrome (paraneoplastic) is associated with:', '["Prostate cancer","Small cell lung cancer","Breast cancer","Lymphoma","Colon cancer"]'::jsonb, 'Small cell lung cancer', 'Eaton-Lambert: proximal muscle weakness, improves with repeated effort. VGCC antibodies. SCLC most common.'),
('a7263849-ec41-5c80-8a5b-def3777b5f16'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Acanthosis nigricans (paraneoplastic) is associated with:', '["Breast cancer","Lung cancer","Gastric adenocarcinoma","Colon cancer","Pancreatic cancer"]'::jsonb, 'Gastric adenocarcinoma', 'Acanthosis nigricans: velvety hyperpigmentation in axilla/neck. Associated with GI adenocarcinoma.'),
('96253318-53d5-5bce-8620-435362505b6e'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'The most common primary site for metastatic carcinoma of unknown primary (CUP) is:', '["Liver","Colon","Lung","Pancreas","Breast"]'::jsonb, 'Lung', 'CUP: lung, pancreas, and GI tract most common primaries. Immunohistochemistry helps identify origin.'),
('70b503e4-86ec-55cc-a186-94dccef6fe20'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'IHC panel for carcinoma unknown primary: CK7+/CK20- suggests:', '["Prostate origin clinical presentation","Lung, breast, thyroid, endometrial origin","Renal origin clinical presentation","Hepatocellular origin clinical presentation","Colorectal origin clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Lung, breast, thyroid, endometrial origin', 'CK7+/CK20-: lung, breast, thyroid, ovarian, endometrial. CK7-/CK20+: colorectal, Merkel cell.'),
('7cac21cd-c55d-5110-9316-059dc144e64e'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Wide local excision of soft tissue sarcoma means:', '["Excisional biopsy clinical presentation","Marginal excision clinical presentation","Curettage clinical presentation","Simple enucleation clinical presentation","Resection with 1-2 cm margin of normal tissue"]'::jsonb, 'Resection with 1-2 cm margin of normal tissue', 'Wide excision: en bloc removal with normal tissue cuff. Biopsy tract included in specimen.'),
('3805c73d-7605-5c7b-8b22-50fc963a499d'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Compartmental resection for sarcoma:', '["Amputation","Biopsy","Removal of tumor only","Removal of entire anatomical compartment","Removal with 1 mm margin"]'::jsonb, 'Removal of entire anatomical compartment', 'Compartmental resection: removes entire muscle group from origin to insertion. Lowest local recurrence.'),
('e7389f81-d34d-5af3-a050-3a9ceb74f44d'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Amputation is indicated when:', '["All sarcomas clinical presentation clinical presentation","Patient preference clinical presentation clinical presentation","All bone tumors clinical presentation clinical presentation","Large tumors only clinical presentation clinical presentation","Tumor cannot be resected with negative margins or limb function compromised"]'::jsonb, 'Tumor cannot be resected with negative margins or limb function compromised', 'Amputation: for extensive tumors with neurovascular involvement. Limb salvage preferred if safe margins.'),
('374d41b6-ef47-59b6-be6e-c01109030cd0'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Primary vs secondary cancer prevention: Primary prevents:', '["Recurrence","Development of cancer","Metastases","Spread of cancer","Detection of early cancer"]'::jsonb, 'Development of cancer', 'Primary: prevent cancer initiation. Secondary: early detection (screening). Tertiary: reduce recurrence.'),
('1a6a8d9d-c763-554f-96b7-a34e0147b960'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Cancer surveillance after curative treatment includes:', '["No follow-up needed clinical presentation","Regular follow-up with imaging, markers, and clinical exam","Only one visit clinical presentation","Only blood tests clinical presentation","Only imaging clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Regular follow-up with imaging, markers, and clinical exam', 'Surveillance: detect recurrence early. Frequency depends on cancer type, stage, and risk.'),
('3093866c-5f1c-523c-842d-e6084469843c'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Multidisciplinary tumor board discussion involves:', '["Only surgeons clinical presentation","Only pathologists clinical presentation","Surgeon, medical oncologist, radiation oncologist, pathologist, radiologist","Only oncologists clinical presentation","Only the primary physician clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Surgeon, medical oncologist, radiation oncologist, pathologist, radiologist', 'MDT: all disciplines collaborate to plan optimal treatment. Standard of cancer care.'),
('57205753-e3a4-5b2b-96e9-9be72b03bcda'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Palliative cancer care focuses on:', '["Only nutrition clinical presentation","Cure clinical presentation","Prevention clinical presentation","Only pain relief clinical presentation","Symptom relief and quality of life"]'::jsonb, 'Symptom relief and quality of life', 'Palliative: symptom management (pain, nausea, dyspnea). Can be combined with anticancer therapy.'),
('eed7bbd1-5b9f-5267-aeef-9f6843a715ef'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Hospice care is for:', '["Symptom management during chemo","Curative treatment only","Patients with terminal cancer","Post-surgery recovery","All cancer patients"]'::jsonb, 'Patients with terminal cancer', 'Hospice: end-of-life care. No active anticancer treatment. Focus on comfort and dignity.'),
('ea00f9a3-acca-5d0e-8a38-560b187ab7a9'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'End-of-life discussions should include:', '["Only treatment options clinical presentation","Patient''s goals, advance directives, code status, proxy decision-maker","Only prognosis clinical presentation","Only pain management clinical presentation","Only code status clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Patient''s goals, advance directives, code status, proxy decision-maker', 'Advanced care planning: discuss goals, values, CPR, DNR, living will, healthcare proxy.'),
('b781bc3e-bb9f-5f93-9815-fc9fc39dfdd9'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Quality of life (QOL) in cancer is assessed using:', '["Only imaging","Only pain scores","Only lab tests","Validated QOL instruments","Only physical exam"]'::jsonb, 'Validated QOL instruments', 'QOL: patient-reported outcomes. Multidimensional (physical, emotional, social, functional).'),
('8d9c4163-ff68-5f53-9746-6ba92c56c00f'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Precision oncology (personalized medicine) relies on:', '["Chemotherapy only clinical presentation","Molecular profiling of tumor (NGS, IHC, FISH) for targeted therapy","Standard treatment for all clinical presentation","Radiation only clinical presentation","Surgery only clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Molecular profiling of tumor (NGS, IHC, FISH) for targeted therapy', 'Precision oncology: match treatment to genomic alterations. Example: EGFR/ALK in lung, HER2 in breast.'),
('9c0c507f-b8be-54a0-bb48-1f86b57b1170'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Liquid biopsy (ctDNA) detects:', '["Circulating tumor DNA in blood","Metabolites","Exosomes","Protein markers","Circulating tumor cells"]'::jsonb, 'Circulating tumor DNA in blood', 'Liquid biopsy: detect minimal residual disease, resistance mutations, monitor response. ctDNA.'),
('c7fb3708-297c-55d5-9e93-94e56b197db3'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Tumor heterogeneity means:', '["All cells identical","Benign cells","Different subclones within the same tumor","Same cells throughout tumor","No mutations"]'::jsonb, 'Different subclones within the same tumor', 'Intratumoral heterogeneity: different mutations in different regions. Leads to treatment resistance.'),
('ef836017-2035-5dfa-b8d6-80937adb3f2e'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'Cancer stem cells are:', '["Immune cells in tumor clinical presentation","All cancer cells clinical presentation","Differentiated cancer cells clinical presentation","Stromal cells clinical presentation","Tumor-initiating cells with self-renewal capacity"]'::jsonb, 'Tumor-initiating cells with self-renewal capacity', 'CSCs: small subset. Self-renewal, differentiation, resistance to chemo/RT. Target for novel therapies.'),
('4cf05874-4689-5599-845d-802336ec4105'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'The hallmarks of cancer (Hanahan & Weinberg) include:', '["Sustaining proliferative signaling, evading growth suppressors, resisting cell death","Only angiogenesis","Only proliferation","All of these and more","Only metastasis"]'::jsonb, 'All of these and more', '10 hallmarks: sustaining proliferative signaling, evading growth suppressors, resisting cell death, enabling replicative immortality, inducing angiogenesis, activating invasion/metastasis, deregulating cellular energetics, avoiding immune destruction, genome instability, tumor-promoting inflammation.'),
('da3920c0-53e1-5ecf-b4ae-a4f292ccd09e'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Oncology', 'With respect to management, The most common cancer worldwide in males is:', '["Colorectal cancer","Stomach cancer","Lung cancer","Prostate cancer","Liver cancer"]'::jsonb, 'Lung cancer', 'Lung cancer is the most commonly diagnosed cancer globally (2022). Leading cause of cancer death in both sexes combined. Prostate #2 incidence in males, lung #1 in mortality.'),
('dc3427da-fd75-5e4f-aa3b-118c989a1036'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Which phase of wound healing lasts from day 1 to day 5?', '["Remodeling phase","Hemostasis phase","Proliferative phase","Inflammatory phase","Maturation phase"]'::jsonb, 'Inflammatory phase', 'Inflammatory phase: hemostasis, neutrophil/macrophage infiltration. Days 1-5.'),
('577c1b4f-e20d-5b3f-bcfc-a89bfc9bf316'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'The proliferative phase of wound healing is characterized by:', '["Neutrophil infiltration clinical presentation","Scar remodeling clinical presentation","Fibroblast proliferation and collagen deposition","Hemostasis clinical presentation","Vasoconstriction clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Fibroblast proliferation and collagen deposition', 'Proliferative phase (day 5 to 3 weeks): fibroblasts produce collagen, angiogenesis, epithelialization.'),
('f58b13b4-1343-5048-b492-9f60959f04ea'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'The maturation (remodeling) phase of wound healing lasts:', '["5 days to 3 weeks","24-48 hours","3 weeks to 12-24 months","1-2 years","1-5 days"]'::jsonb, '3 weeks to 12-24 months', 'Maturation: collagen remodeling, scar strength increases (max 80% of original at 3 months).'),
('ebc49b3f-836d-564f-9c39-2d109ceec6b3'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Which growth factor is most important for wound healing?', '["EGF","PDGF","TGF-beta","FGF","VEGF"]'::jsonb, 'PDGF', 'PDGF: chemotactic for fibroblasts and macrophages. First growth factor released from platelets.'),
('e6fed1e6-3ea9-5c2e-bbd0-fff63521a5fe'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Vitamin C is essential for wound healing because:', '["It reduces inflammation clinical presentation","It is a cofactor for prolyl hydroxylase in collagen synthesis","It prevents scarring clinical presentation","It improves blood flow clinical presentation","It prevents infection clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'It is a cofactor for prolyl hydroxylase in collagen synthesis', 'Vitamin C: hydroxylation of proline and lysine in procollagen. Deficiency causes impaired healing (scurvy).'),
('b1a58e59-a126-5ca6-be55-84488be616ee'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Epithelialization of a wound occurs by:', '["Keratinization clinical presentation","Fibrosis clinical presentation","Granulation tissue formation clinical presentation","Migration of basal epithelial cells across the wound surface","Angiogenesis clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Migration of basal epithelial cells across the wound surface', 'Epithelialization: basal cells migrate from wound edges. Requires moist environment.'),
('6c74e97a-a785-50ad-813b-89618c2a0162'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Granulation tissue consists of:', '["Fat tissue clinical presentation","Nerve tissue clinical presentation","Epithelial cells only clinical presentation","Fibroblasts, capillaries, and inflammatory cells","Collagen only clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Fibroblasts, capillaries, and inflammatory cells', 'Granulation tissue: new capillaries (angiogenesis), fibroblasts, and mixed inflammatory cells.'),
('c993aed6-ef27-58b7-9aa3-4f05df0c3391'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Wound contraction is mediated by:', '["Macrophages","Neutrophils","Lymphocytes","Epithelial cells","Myofibroblasts"]'::jsonb, 'Myofibroblasts', 'Myofibroblasts: contractile cells that pull wound edges together. Express alpha-SMA.'),
('119df77b-3a95-5b42-bb8c-0b1ae0d97361'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Healing by primary intention involves:', '["Skin grafting","Wound left open","Delayed closure","Clean, apposed wound edges with minimal tissue loss","Large gap requiring granulation"]'::jsonb, 'Clean, apposed wound edges with minimal tissue loss', 'Primary intention: surgical incision, sutured. Minimal scar.'),
('22c78e24-ff9c-5be2-b360-94c2c3acfaf0'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Healing by secondary intention involves:', '["Clean surgical incision clinical presentation","Sutured wound edges clinical presentation","Open wound that fills with granulation tissue and contracts","Minimal scarring clinical presentation","No infection risk clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Open wound that fills with granulation tissue and contracts', 'Secondary intention: wounds with tissue loss, left open. More granulation, more scarring.'),
('1419ac60-19ea-5318-8438-d1442dd15fa1'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Healing by tertiary (delayed primary) intention involves:', '["Flap coverage clinical presentation","Wound left open, later closed surgically after 3-5 days","Immediate closure clinical presentation","No closure ever clinical presentation","Skin grafting only clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Wound left open, later closed surgically after 3-5 days', 'Tertiary intention: for contaminated wounds. Debride, wait, then close when clean.'),
('c2ed60bd-a2f7-5bc3-8da6-e872f89dea1b'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'A wound is weakest at which postoperative day?', '["Day 3","Day 30","Day 5-7","Day 14","Day 1"]'::jsonb, 'Day 5-7', 'Wound strength lowest at day 5-7: collagenolysis exceeds synthesis. Risk of dehiscence.'),
('0619a665-38ec-5750-8173-eeff792a2245'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Maximum wound strength reaches what percentage of original?', '["100% at 6 weeks","50% at 6 months","100% at 2 weeks","30% at 1 month","80% at 3 months"]'::jsonb, '80% at 3 months', 'Wound never regains 100% strength. ~80% at 3 months. Collagen remodeling continues for months.'),
('1ad8aba8-c078-56b2-98a0-c509c18c52dc'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Tensile strength of a wound at 1 week is approximately:', '["100%","10% of normal skin","80%","5%","50%"]'::jsonb, '10% of normal skin', 'At 1 week: ~10% strength. At 2 weeks: ~20%. At 3 weeks: ~30%. Sutures provide most strength early.'),
('8356e240-bc5b-5d75-acc4-0b3ff3e0e76e'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Angiogenesis in wound healing is stimulated by:', '["FGF","VEGF","EGF","TGF-beta","PDGF"]'::jsonb, 'VEGF', 'VEGF: main angiogenic factor. Secreted by macrophages and fibroblasts under hypoxic conditions.'),
('c95c366e-60fe-589e-9b59-61cfdb93e62f'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Which systemic factor impairs wound healing?', '["Diabetes mellitus","Good nutrition","Young age","Normal BMI","Well-controlled blood pressure"]'::jsonb, 'Diabetes mellitus', 'Diabetes: impaired immune function, microvascular disease, neuropathy, hyperglycemia impairs collagen.'),
('9893d1e9-f459-547f-8a3e-54f921a04968'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'For a patient presenting with symptoms, Corticosteroids impair wound healing by:', '["Enhancing immunity clinical presentation","Promoting angiogenesis clinical presentation","Inhibiting inflammation, fibroblast proliferation, and collagen synthesis","Increasing collagen clinical presentation","Improving epithelization clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Inhibiting inflammation, fibroblast proliferation, and collagen synthesis', 'Steroids: anti-inflammatory (decrease macrophage migration), inhibit fibroblast function, decrease TGF-beta.'),
('944d2e1c-0a31-55e1-bc39-47ef31c66ae3'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Radiation therapy impairs wound healing by:', '["Increased angiogenesis clinical presentation","Endarteritis obliterans (microvascular damage) and fibrosis","Direct skin damage only clinical presentation","Immune suppression only clinical presentation","Collagen overproduction clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Endarteritis obliterans (microvascular damage) and fibrosis', 'Radiation: progressive endarteritis -> tissue hypoxia -> impaired healing. Chronic radiation fibrosis.'),
('32556aac-5c1c-5322-94db-e5ccf2eaf714'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Malnutrition impairs wound healing through:', '["High protein intake","Vitamin A excess","Mineral excess","Excess calories","Protein deficiency"]'::jsonb, 'Protein deficiency', 'Albumin <3: impaired healing. Protein, vitamin C, zinc, copper deficiencies all impair healing.'),
('565255bd-963b-5bf6-b333-ed74578ecdf0'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Zinc deficiency impairs wound healing because:', '["Zinc prevents infection clinical presentation","Zinc is a cofactor for collagen synthesis and cell proliferation","Zinc is an antioxidant clinical presentation","Zinc improves immunity clinical presentation","Zinc increases blood flow clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Zinc is a cofactor for collagen synthesis and cell proliferation', 'Zinc: required for fibroblast proliferation, collagen synthesis, and epithelialization.'),
('4dae1ab5-8336-5ac4-96ce-af5d78ed7861'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Which of the following is a local factor impairing wound healing?', '["All of these","Normal perfusion","Young age","Good nutrition","No infection"]'::jsonb, 'All of these', 'Local: infection (most important), ischemia, foreign body, hematoma, edema, radiation.'),
('cb6505dd-e68f-5d35-ab6f-fbc81f580b36'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Wound infection impairs healing by:', '["Reducing edema clinical presentation","Promoting angiogenesis clinical presentation","Bacterial toxins and enzymes degrade collagen and prolong inflammation","Increasing collagen clinical presentation","Improving oxygenation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Bacterial toxins and enzymes degrade collagen and prolong inflammation', 'Infection: bacteria produce collagenase, proteases. Prolong inflammatory phase. Most common cause of delayed healing.'),
('55808218-9a24-5f08-97ba-7c96abe8b5f0'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Tissue hypoxia impairs wound healing because:', '["Oxygen prevents infection clinical presentation","Oxygen is required for hydroxylation of proline and lysine in collagen","Oxygen increases inflammation clinical presentation","Oxygen reduces edema clinical presentation","Oxygen promotes scarring clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Oxygen is required for hydroxylation of proline and lysine in collagen', 'Oxygen: critical for collagen synthesis (prolyl hydroxylase). PO2 <40 mmHg impairs healing.'),
('dd90a84e-e42a-51f1-a42a-1c3ab9fee638'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Smoking impairs wound healing due to:', '["Bleeding tendency clinical presentation","Nicotine-induced vasoconstriction and CO-induced hypoxia","Direct toxicity clinical presentation","Infection clinical presentation","Immunosuppression clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Nicotine-induced vasoconstriction and CO-induced hypoxia', 'Smoking: nicotine (vasoconstriction), CO (decreased oxygen delivery). Increased wound complications.'),
('04b02d0e-757a-5fd9-9f2b-a1aaa599de6c'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Jaundice (obstructive) impairs wound healing due to:', '["Infection clinical presentation","Impaired absorption of vitamin K and fat-soluble vitamins","Direct toxicity clinical presentation","Bleeding only clinical presentation","Pruritus clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Impaired absorption of vitamin K and fat-soluble vitamins', 'Obstructive jaundice: vitamin K deficiency -> coagulation defects. Bilirubin impairs fibroblast function.'),
('c1cba68f-e616-5816-b8bc-9d4a67eac704'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Uremia impairs wound healing through:', '["Improved nutrition clinical presentation","Increased collagen clinical presentation","Impaired immune function and fibroblast proliferation","Reduced edema clinical presentation","Enhanced angiogenesis clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Impaired immune function and fibroblast proliferation', 'Uremia: neutrophil dysfunction, impaired fibroblast proliferation. Dialysis improves healing.'),
('52b7b71e-6bb1-53de-8df2-79085dc567d8'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Obesity impairs wound healing due to:', '["Decreased vascularity of adipose tissue and increased tension","Improved nutrition clinical presentation","Enhanced immune function clinical presentation","Increased collagen synthesis clinical presentation","Reduced infection risk clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Decreased vascularity of adipose tissue and increased tension', 'Obesity: poor blood supply to fat, increased wound tension, higher infection risk.'),
('bb014b84-7d09-54cf-bbf5-7d7f96840d4b'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Age-related impaired wound healing is due to:', '["Diminished inflammatory response and slower cell proliferation","Increased collagen synthesis clinical presentation","Better immune function clinical presentation","Faster epithelialization clinical presentation","Enhanced angiogenesis clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Diminished inflammatory response and slower cell proliferation', 'Elderly: delayed healing but ultimately similar result if no comorbidities.'),
('d941fec8-4902-530b-a0c3-288a44bde465'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Vitamin A supplementation can reverse:', '["Steroid-induced impaired wound healing","Malnutrition","Diabetes-related impairment","Smoking-related impairment","Radiation-induced impairment"]'::jsonb, 'Steroid-induced impaired wound healing', 'Vitamin A (retinol): reverses steroid effect on wound healing. Promotes epithelialization and collagen.'),
('d5546b41-f965-5c7c-99f5-5ce9af27993a'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Local anesthetics with epinephrine should be avoided in:', '["Wounds in end-artery areas","Scalp","Back","Trunk","Thigh"]'::jsonb, 'Wounds in end-artery areas', 'Epinephrine causes vasoconstriction -> ischemia in end-artery areas. Risk of necrosis.'),
('30632135-1585-52d1-93bb-614574ba9da4'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'A hypertrophic scar differs from keloid in that:', '["Hypertrophic extends beyond clinical presentation","Hypertrophic scar remains within wound boundaries; keloid extends beyond","Keloid stays within clinical presentation","Neither is pathological clinical presentation","Both are same clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Hypertrophic scar remains within wound boundaries; keloid extends beyond', 'Hypertrophic: within original wound, regresses over time. Keloid: extends beyond, continues growing.'),
('15950ae7-b614-5204-a6c3-03dc93aceb71'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Keloid formation is most common in:', '["Elderly","Caucasians","Equal in all races","Dark-skinned individuals","Children"]'::jsonb, 'Dark-skinned individuals', 'Keloid: 15x more common in dark skin. Genetic predisposition. High recurrence after excision.'),
('430f901f-c217-50d9-9f81-ba55734e9490'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Which area is most prone to keloid formation?', '["Scalp clinical presentation","Face clinical presentation","Palms clinical presentation","Earlobe, sternum, shoulder, upper back","Soles clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Earlobe, sternum, shoulder, upper back', 'Common keloid sites: earlobe (piercing), sternum, deltoid, upper back. Rare on palms/soles.'),
('1db6b992-d791-5f98-8a95-41073f3e9f93'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Treatment of keloid includes:', '["All of these: intralesional steroids, excision + RT, silicone sheets, pressure","Steroids only clinical presentation clinical presentation","Excision only clinical presentation clinical presentation","Radiation only clinical presentation clinical presentation","Observation only clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'All of these: intralesional steroids, excision + RT, silicone sheets, pressure', 'Multimodal: triamcinolone injections, excision + RT, silicone gel, pressure earrings.'),
('4b257ae8-2f7b-5dbd-a7c2-70a39181bad2'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Pressure therapy for scar prevention (e.g., after burns):', '["Promotes infection clinical presentation","Causes necrosis clinical presentation","Increases blood flow clinical presentation","Has no effect clinical presentation","Reduces scar thickness by decreasing blood flow and collagen"]'::jsonb, 'Reduces scar thickness by decreasing blood flow and collagen', 'Pressure garments: 25-30 mmHg for 23h/day. Mechanism: reduce capillary blood flow, collagen synthesis.'),
('f271b88c-6392-5f90-8658-aeb2918f96b7'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Silicone gel sheeting for scars works by:', '["Hydration and occlusion","Chemical effect","Temperature effect","Direct pharmacological effect","Pressure"]'::jsonb, 'Hydration and occlusion', 'Silicone: increases hydration, reduces transepidermal water loss. Flattens and softens scars.'),
('595563bc-33e5-53b0-96e2-4ae35dd1143a'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'A contracture is:', '["Keloid clinical presentation","Scar hypertrophy clinical presentation","Permanent shortening of skin/muscle/tendon due to scar tissue","Normal scar clinical presentation","Wound dehiscence clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Permanent shortening of skin/muscle/tendon due to scar tissue', 'Contracture: scar tissue shortens, restricts movement. Burn scars commonly cause contractures.'),
('53d23350-5e4e-5caa-9a41-966b1d2d1b72'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Z-plasty is used to:', '["Lengthen and realign scar contractures","Prevent infection clinical presentation","Reduce pain clinical presentation","Promote healing clinical presentation","Remove scars clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Lengthen and realign scar contractures', 'Z-plasty: transposition flaps. Lengthens scar, changes direction to follow relaxed skin tension lines.'),
('4d518041-cb5d-5304-a061-1f648d1a4d1c'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Atrophic scar is characterized by:', '["Thin, depressed scar","Raised scar","Keloid","Normal scar","Hypertrophic scar"]'::jsonb, 'Thin, depressed scar', 'Atrophic scar: dermal atrophy. Examples: acne scars, striae distensae, steroid-induced.'),
('f358b550-78ff-553e-9bde-b5ec5bd71160'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Which scar type follows a typical surgical incision?', '["Atrophic scar","Keloid","Hypertrophic scar","Contracture","Fine line scar"]'::jsonb, 'Fine line scar', 'Fine line scar: thin, flat, gradually fades. Normal maturation over 6-12 months.'),
('285c1e6e-d829-550d-93bb-8396e3fd3723'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Scar maturation (fading and softening) typically takes:', '["5 years","1 week","1 month","6-12 months","3 months"]'::jsonb, '6-12 months', 'Scar remodeling: 6 months to 2 years. Redness fades, scar softens and flattens.'),
('42f89c7c-d6e2-5005-8f2c-bc430a25c3db'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Which suture technique causes the least scarring?', '["Subcuticular (intradermal) closure","Horizontal mattress","Vertical mattress","Continuous running","Simple interrupted"]'::jsonb, 'Subcuticular (intradermal) closure', 'Subcuticular: absorbable suture in dermis. Minimal surface scarring. No suture marks.'),
('fcbba9dd-b601-58ef-b2ba-898ef2f8559b'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Dog-ear (standing cone) deformity after wound closure is caused by:', '["Poor knot technique","Infection","Suture reaction","Hematoma","Excess skin at wound ends"]'::jsonb, 'Excess skin at wound ends', 'Dog-ear: unequal wound edges or too much skin at ends. Excise triangular extension.'),
('646daf94-6ee0-5eca-ba38-1da41679a2d1'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Wound dehiscence (burst abdomen) most commonly occurs:', '["Day 3-4","Postoperative day 5-8","After 3 weeks","Day 10-14","Day 1-2"]'::jsonb, 'Postoperative day 5-8', 'Dehiscence: fascial disruption. Risk factors: infection, obesity, malnutrition, steroids, cough/straining.'),
('75b2f294-944e-53a0-bb73-23d2911b7519'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Risk factors for wound dehiscence include:', '["Non-smoker clinical presentation clinical presentation","Well-nourished state clinical presentation clinical presentation","Female gender clinical presentation clinical presentation","All of these: infection, obesity, malnutrition, coughing, ascites, steroid use","Young age only clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'All of these: infection, obesity, malnutrition, coughing, ascites, steroid use', 'Dehiscence: most common POD 5-8. Emergency repair needed. Evisceration if bowel protrudes.'),
('a70c9dc6-e526-5161-8b28-418dea5f3ef2'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Which suture material is absorbable and monofilament?', '["Prolene","Nylon","PDS","Silk","Vicryl"]'::jsonb, 'PDS', 'PDS: monofilament, absorbable (6-8 weeks tensile strength). Good for fascia. Less tissue reaction.'),
('0c1b30b1-34f7-5cd9-9629-7df657976cf0'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Which suture material is non-absorbable and monofilament?', '["Chromic catgut","Prolene","Silk","PDS","Vicryl"]'::jsonb, 'Prolene', 'Prolene: non-absorbable, monofilament. Minimal tissue reaction. Used for skin, vascular anastomosis.'),
('0cc945d3-e17f-5f24-9ef9-79092dd0e034'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Silk suture is:', '["Non-absorbable, braided","Synthetic","Premade loop","Monofilament","Absorbable"]'::jsonb, 'Non-absorbable, braided', 'Silk: braided, handles well. But tissue reaction and infection risk (braided). Not for infected wounds.'),
('1fdc7a68-1191-5768-9877-e9a78afda8f5'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Which suture has the least tissue reaction?', '["Chromic catgut","Prolene","Nylon","Vicryl","Silk"]'::jsonb, 'Prolene', 'Prolene: inert, minimal tissue reaction. Nylon also minimal. Monofilaments cause less reaction than braided.'),
('c1d44065-482e-536a-af30-11f8dcfb9d2a'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Chromic catgut is:', '["Braided synthetic","Metal","Non-absorbable","Absorbable","Monofilament synthetic"]'::jsonb, 'Absorbable', 'Chromic catgut: treated to resist enzymatic digestion. Absorbs in 10-21 days. Used for ligation.'),
('72af3cda-e9cb-532d-94d3-219734457456'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Vicryl (polyglactin 910) is:', '["Monofilament, non-absorbable","Braided, absorbable synthetic suture","Metal wire","Non-absorbable braided","Natural absorbable"]'::jsonb, 'Braided, absorbable synthetic suture', 'Vicryl: braided, synthetic absorbable. Tensile strength ~3 weeks. Complete absorption in 60-90 days.'),
('e833ebcc-5381-5033-8ecf-b9e56dc766da'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Nylon (Ethilon) suture is:', '["Coated clinical presentation","Absorbable clinical presentation","Natural clinical presentation","Non-absorbable, monofilament synthetic polyamide","Braided clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Non-absorbable, monofilament synthetic polyamide', 'Nylon: monofilament, elastic. Good for skin closure. Must be removed.'),
('08fed1bc-994c-5319-b5bc-ade2ff9124a1'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'The strongest knot is:', '["Granny knot","Surgeon''s knot","Half hitch","Single throw","Slip knot"]'::jsonb, 'Surgeon''s knot', 'Surgeon''s knot: first throw double-wrapped to prevent slipping. Then square knot.'),
('fd149df5-9411-50ec-8999-633bde97d855'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'An ideal surgical knot should be:', '["Loose","Oblique","Flat, square, snug, and not too tight","Round","Multiple granny knots"]'::jsonb, 'Flat, square, snug, and not too tight', 'Square knot: flat, secure. Granny knot: slips. Avoid excessive tension (strangulation).'),
('f34f9baa-6127-53bc-b652-4d02339214fa'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Tissue adhesive (skin glue) is:', '["Fibrin glue","Octylcyanoacrylate","None of these","Vicryl","Surgical tape"]'::jsonb, 'Octylcyanoacrylate', 'Dermabond: cyanoacrylate. For superficial, low-tension wounds. Moisture-resistant.'),
('6c663f10-e942-55ae-ac24-b0614e1239e1'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Absorbable sutures lose most tensile strength by:', '["1 year","1 day","3-4 weeks","1 week","6 months"]'::jsonb, '3-4 weeks', 'Absorbable sutures: Vicryl (3 weeks), PDS (6-8 weeks), Monocryl (2-3 weeks). Absorb by hydrolysis.'),
('ea8e61d5-3415-56e4-b608-ced324ba8903'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Monocryl (poliglecaprone) is:', '["Braided absorbable clinical presentation","Natural absorbable clinical presentation","Monofilament, absorbable, minimal tissue reaction","Non-absorbable monofilament clinical presentation","Metal clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Monofilament, absorbable, minimal tissue reaction', 'Monocryl: monofilament absorbable. Excellent handling, minimal reaction. Used for subcuticular closure.'),
('94d7301b-6c3f-532f-99d2-05bdc555f8da'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Which needle type is used for skin closure?', '["Reverse cutting needle","Round body (taper) needle","Blunt needle","Spatula needle","Cutting needle"]'::jsonb, 'Cutting needle', 'Cutting needle (conventional cutting) for tough tissue (skin). Reverse cutting for cosmetic skin closure.'),
('2250ff02-ca7f-540c-9fe5-66f39a4af7ed'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Round body (taper) needle is used for:', '["Bone","Cartilage","Calcified tissue","Tough tissue","Soft, easily penetrable tissue"]'::jsonb, 'Soft, easily penetrable tissue', 'Taper needle: penetrates without cutting. Used for intestine, vessel, muscle, fascia.'),
('897a6643-94b7-5221-a063-1f9ae300fe46'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'A swaged (atraumatic) needle has:', '["Blunt tip clinical presentation","Multiple needles clinical presentation","Separate needle and thread clinical presentation","Reverse cutting clinical presentation","Suture swaged (attached) to needle; single pass, less tissue trauma"]'::jsonb, 'Suture swaged (attached) to needle; single pass, less tissue trauma', 'Swaged needle: suture is continuous with needle. Minimizes tissue trauma. Used for most surgery.'),
('cfd3b9e9-8873-5d47-a86b-ab86f176a047'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Incisional biopsy means:', '["Removal of entire lesion clinical presentation","No biopsy clinical presentation","Removal of a portion of the lesion for diagnosis","Core needle biopsy clinical presentation","Frozen section clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Removal of a portion of the lesion for diagnosis', 'Incisional biopsy: wedge of tissue from lesion. For large lesions (>2 cm) when excisional would be deforming.'),
('31b6071e-d4b0-5f40-86c2-7e3d145a6c76'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Excisional biopsy means:', '["Shave biopsy clinical presentation","Punch biopsy clinical presentation","Removal of part clinical presentation","FNA clinical presentation","Complete removal of the entire lesion with margin of normal tissue"]'::jsonb, 'Complete removal of the entire lesion with margin of normal tissue', 'Excisional biopsy: entire lesion removed. Both diagnostic and therapeutic. For small lesions.'),
('5f3ff4fd-ed73-5de7-a731-177eb1f71664'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Regarding clinical scenarios, Punch biopsy is used for:', '["Deep tumors","Small skin lesions","Bone","Visceral organs","Large tumors"]'::jsonb, 'Small skin lesions', 'Punch biopsy: circular blade, full-thickness skin. Single stitch often needed.'),
('1fb19a91-dcac-5d22-8c31-5ea88ad4e68c'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Shave biopsy is:', '["Deep excision clinical presentation","Full thickness clinical presentation","Core biopsy clinical presentation","Horizontal excision of superficial lesion","Needle aspiration clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Horizontal excision of superficial lesion', 'Shave biopsy: superficial. For raised lesions. May not get deep margin. Scarring minimal.'),
('9372b428-a0b6-5520-983a-7fb0353175f5'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Core needle biopsy (Tru-Cut) provides:', '["Fluid only","Cytology only","Cells in suspension","No tissue architecture","Core of tissue preserving architecture"]'::jsonb, 'Core of tissue preserving architecture', 'Core biopsy: 14-18G needle. Tissue architecture preserved. For breast, liver, prostate, kidney.'),
('25909d80-7c52-5f6b-878c-368d3eb92407'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Fine-needle aspiration (FNA) provides:', '["Full tissue core","Cytologic specimens","Histologic sections","Frozen section","Excisional specimen"]'::jsonb, 'Cytologic specimens', 'FNA: 22-25G needle. Cells only. Quick, less invasive. Used for thyroid, lymph node, pancreas.'),
('4440daf7-f4ca-5723-a476-0935560004e4'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Which biopsy technique is preferred for suspected melanoma?', '["Excisional biopsy","Incisional biopsy","Shave biopsy","Punch biopsy","FNA"]'::jsonb, 'Excisional biopsy', 'Melanoma: excisional with narrow margin to allow microstaging (Breslow thickness).'),
('74620713-5944-55a7-ac94-30b4ee6e534a'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Frozen section (intraoperative consultation) provides:', '["Genetic analysis","Permanent paraffin section","Cytology","Immunohistochemistry","Rapid diagnosis (20-30 min) during surgery"]'::jsonb, 'Rapid diagnosis (20-30 min) during surgery', 'Frozen section: flash-freeze, cut, stain. Used for margin status, lymph node metastasis, tissue ID.'),
('25d84066-a98d-5f13-a3ea-d2785aa2bf87'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Permanent (paraffin-embedded) section takes:', '["1 hour","24-48 hours","5 min","1 week","Same day"]'::jsonb, '24-48 hours', 'Permanent section: formalin fixation (12-24h), processing, paraffin embedding, sectioning, H&E staining.'),
('03d7d95f-77ba-5a43-be76-aba877f85832'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'The fixative of choice for routine histology is:', '["10% neutral buffered formalin","Bouin solution","Alcohol","Zenker solution","Glutaraldehyde"]'::jsonb, '10% neutral buffered formalin', '10% NBF: preserves tissue, good for H&E, IHC. Formalin crosslinks proteins.'),
('56a417f9-c028-50f6-8062-e1a92668e6a5'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Which biopsy is contraindicated for suspected vascular tumors?', '["FNA","Shave biopsy","Punch biopsy","Incisional or core biopsy","Excisional biopsy"]'::jsonb, 'Incisional or core biopsy', 'Vascular tumors: risk of hemorrhage. Excisional or FNA may be safer. Angiography sometimes needed.'),
('f928a030-1e82-5b9a-bd39-68de2e6a1c1b'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Biopsy of ulcer should include:', '["Normal tissue only","Necrotic tissue","Base","Edge","Center of ulcer"]'::jsonb, 'Edge', 'Biopsy edge: includes viable tumor and normal tissue. Center often necrotic.'),
('faab15f8-4536-55c4-a2ac-8299241cc5f4'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Stereotactic biopsy is used for:', '["Lymph nodes","Thyroid nodules","Skin lesions","Palpable breast lumps","Non-palpable breast lesions"]'::jsonb, 'Non-palpable breast lesions', 'Stereotactic: mammographic guidance. Core needle biopsy. For microcalcifications or subtle masses.'),
('ee5ae689-f040-5a68-87af-799c2dbf0283'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Endoscopic biopsy is performed during:', '["External exam clinical presentation","Endoscopy/colonoscopy/gastroscopy","Ultrasound clinical presentation","CT scan clinical presentation","X-ray clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Endoscopy/colonoscopy/gastroscopy', 'Endoscopic biopsy: through endoscope. For GI, bronchial, bladder mucosal lesions.'),
('93eeadac-c1b4-5565-abba-d1feac1c5443'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Open biopsy (surgical incisional/excisional) is indicated when:', '["Small lesions only clinical presentation","All lesions clinical presentation","Needle biopsy inadequate or high-risk of complication","No lesions clinical presentation","Superficial lesions only clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Needle biopsy inadequate or high-risk of complication', 'Open biopsy: when FNA/core insufficient or risky. Gold standard for many tumors.'),
('4ae8a3fe-0a5f-5583-a90a-5d399205ce70'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'A passive (open) drain works by:', '["Active pumping","Suction","Electric pump","Capillary action and gravity","Negative pressure"]'::jsonb, 'Capillary action and gravity', 'Passive drain: e.g., Penrose drain. Relies on gravity, capillary action. Risk of ascending infection.'),
('f7b3b22f-078f-59d4-9db6-49b2ced265fa'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'An active (closed) drain works by:', '["Wick","Siphoning","Gravity","Suction","Capillary action"]'::jsonb, 'Suction', 'Active drain: e.g., Jackson-Pratt, Hemovac. Closed system reduces infection risk. Negative pressure.'),
('332fb02b-cc17-5c4e-8dbf-a612c52fc188'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Penrose drain is:', '["Active drain","Sump drain","Closed suction drain","Passive, open drain","Chest tube"]'::jsonb, 'Passive, open drain', 'Penrose: soft latex tube placed in wound. Drains by capillary action. Used for superficial wounds.'),
('3fcc45eb-803a-559c-bf2c-1d5f7158d152'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Jackson-Pratt (JP) drain is:', '["Closed suction drain","Chest tube","Open passive drain","Gravity drain","Wick drain"]'::jsonb, 'Closed suction drain', 'JP: round/flat perforated tube with bulb. Soft, commonly used for subcutaneous drainage.'),
('497ae12e-9e0f-5c04-be17-26b561cebb52'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Chest tube (intercostal drain) is used for:', '["Bladder drainage","Abdominal drainage","Subcutaneous drainage","Joint drainage","Drainage of pleural space"]'::jsonb, 'Drainage of pleural space', 'Chest tube: in pleural cavity. Underwater seal. For air/fluid drainage. Connected to suction.'),
('47a5ccf7-1d1b-5160-8ce1-c2c2f6f588ff'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Sump drain (e.g., Salem sump) has:', '["Active suction only","Single lumen","No vent","Passive drainage only","Double lumen"]'::jsonb, 'Double lumen', 'Sump: outer vent tube equalizes pressure, prevents mucosal occlusion. Used in upper GI surgery.'),
('55c29553-8a96-5ecd-91a5-f94f3b35b825'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Corrugated drain is:', '["Closed suction","Chest tube","Passive open drain","Active","Sump"]'::jsonb, 'Passive open drain', 'Corrugated: flat, grooved rubber. Passive. Often used in abdominal surgery. Short-term.'),
('118869be-64f8-55cc-b5eb-5c2105520811'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'The most common complication of surgical drains is:', '["Pain","Hemorrhage","Dislodgement","Infection","Blockage"]'::jsonb, 'Infection', 'Drain infection: risk increases with duration. Remove as soon as drainage <30 mL/day.'),
('25963962-54f2-5b07-aee1-a1162aa0535f'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'A drain should be removed when daily output is:', '["<30 mL/day","Zero","100-200 mL/day",">100 mL/day","No specific criteria"]'::jsonb, '<30 mL/day', 'Drain removal: low output (<30 mL), serous fluid, no evidence of leak or infection.'),
('a18f69df-5d35-52da-84bc-274cc808464c'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Negative pressure wound therapy (NPWT/VAC) uses:', '["Positive pressure clinical presentation","Controlled negative pressure (125 mmHg intermittent) to promote granulation","Electric stimulation clinical presentation","Ultrasound clinical presentation","Heat clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Controlled negative pressure (125 mmHg intermittent) to promote granulation', 'VAC: foam dressing + seal + vacuum. Increases blood flow, decreases edema, promotes granulation.'),
('cd6854e7-2bc0-5f1b-8682-66b7686f9beb'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'NPWT is contraindicated in:', '["Chronic wounds clinical presentation","Malignant wounds, untreated osteomyelitis, exposed vessels/nerves","Pressure ulcers clinical presentation","Acute wounds clinical presentation","Diabetic foot ulcers clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Malignant wounds, untreated osteomyelitis, exposed vessels/nerves', 'NPWT contraindications: malignancy in wound, exposed vital structures, necrotic tissue, untreated infection.'),
('fbcd266d-76b2-5b8b-8c3b-8e2799ec97db'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Wound packing with wet-to-dry dressings:', '["Prevents infection","Reduces pain","Mechanical debridement","Keeps wound moist","Promotes epithelialization"]'::jsonb, 'Mechanical debridement', 'Wet-to-dry: wet gauze placed, dries, removed with adherent necrotic tissue. Mechanical debridement.'),
('789d4cf1-9a33-59fb-bbdc-601ab96a3944'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Moist wound healing (occlusive dressings):', '["Causes maceration clinical presentation","Increases infection clinical presentation","Slows healing clinical presentation","Promotes epithelialization faster than dry wound","Should not be used clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Promotes epithelialization faster than dry wound', 'Moist environment: epithelial migration faster, less pain, less scar. Hydrocolloids, hydrogels.'),
('00091f67-5b7e-52bc-ab63-b285b978d375'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Alginate dressings are indicated for:', '["Dry wounds","Exudative wounds","Burn eschar","Necrotic wounds","Superficial abrasions"]'::jsonb, 'Exudative wounds', 'Alginates: derived from seaweed. Highly absorbent. Forms gel. For moderate-heavy exudate.'),
('3ad304e3-978d-5343-9c55-09f8f1f3d41d'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Hydrocolloid dressings are:', '["Non-occlusive clinical presentation","Antimicrobial clinical presentation","Drying clinical presentation","Occlusive, waterproof, absorbent","Non-adherent clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Occlusive, waterproof, absorbent', 'Hydrocolloid: contains gel-forming agents (pectin, gelatin). Adhesive. For pressure ulcers, partial thickness.'),
('3d25ddd4-221e-5b1b-b295-1d2d011ec179'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Surgical site infection (SSI) is defined as:', '["Infection at any site","Only deep infections","Infection at surgical site within 30 days","Infection within 1 week","Only wound infections"]'::jsonb, 'Infection at surgical site within 30 days', 'SSI: superficial (skin/SC), deep (fascia/muscle), organ/space. Within 30 days (90 days with implant).'),
('8a5c2c79-a467-5c44-af18-138bc33e2840'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'The most common organism in SSI is:', '["Streptococcus pyogenes","Bacteroides fragilis","Pseudomonas aeruginosa","E. coli","Staphylococcus aureus"]'::jsonb, 'Staphylococcus aureus', 'S. aureus: most common cause of SSI. MRSA increasingly prevalent.'),
('24740a40-740c-5540-8926-4507463aba15'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Which antibiotic is given for surgical prophylaxis?', '["Vancomycin clinical presentation","Gentamicin clinical presentation","Clindamycin clinical presentation","Cefazolin (first-generation cephalosporin) most common","Metronidazole clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Cefazolin (first-generation cephalosporin) most common', 'Cefazolin: covers skin flora (S. aureus, Strep). For clean surgery. Redose if >4h or blood loss >1500 mL.'),
('3e1ee19e-f360-55c7-a87e-3db2dbcab4d1'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Prophylactic antibiotic should be given:', '["After surgery clinical presentation","24 hours before clinical presentation","After incision clinical presentation","At wound closure clinical presentation","Within 60 minutes before incision"]'::jsonb, 'Within 60 minutes before incision', 'Timing: 0-60 min before incision. Redose if prolonged surgery or major blood loss.'),
('b4fcc1d9-335c-592d-b7be-bcdd79efef01'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Duration of antibiotic prophylaxis for clean surgery:', '["10 days","5 days","7 days","Single dose","Until drain removal"]'::jsonb, 'Single dose', 'A single dose is sufficient for most clean surgeries. Extended use increases resistance and C. diff.'),
('d4bd6b21-d78a-5c68-b5c1-bb44a12e6d25'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Carbuncle is:', '["Deep staphylococcal infection of multiple hair follicles with necrosis","Viral infection clinical presentation","Bacterial cellulitis clinical presentation","Superficial abscess clinical presentation","Single hair follicle infection clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Deep staphylococcal infection of multiple hair follicles with necrosis', 'Carbuncle: coalescing furuncles. Common on neck/back. Often in diabetics. May require I&D.'),
('3c00d88f-12ef-5dc9-a3bd-f588c8905fa5'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Furuncle (boil) is:', '["Acute infection of a single hair follicle","Impetigo","Cellulitis","Deep abscess","Multiple follicle infection"]'::jsonb, 'Acute infection of a single hair follicle', 'Furuncle: hair follicle infection. Common on neck, face, axilla, buttocks. Treat with I&D + antibiotics.'),
('30a2212b-4f24-533b-9244-752c34e8dbbf'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'An abscess is defined as:', '["Localized collection of pus within a cavity","Solid mass clinical presentation","Necrotic tissue clinical presentation","Cyst with fluid clinical presentation","Diffuse inflammation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Localized collection of pus within a cavity', 'Abscess: walled-off pus. Requires drainage (I&D). Antibiotics alone insufficient due to poor penetration.'),
('ef700969-2e76-55b6-8ed1-315e8b155951'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'The classic sign of abscess formation is:', '["Erythema","Fluctuance","Swelling","Warmth","Pain"]'::jsonb, 'Fluctuance', 'Fluctuance: indicates liquid pus. Early abscess may not be fluctuant (cellulitis phase).'),
('cea8ad8f-a1af-5947-a484-80d55e9120ec'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Principles of abscess management:', '["Incision and drainage (I&D), break loculations, culture, pack wound","Heat only clinical presentation","Observation clinical presentation","Steroids clinical presentation","Antibiotics alone clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Incision and drainage (I&D), break loculations, culture, pack wound', 'Abscess: I&D is mainstay. Antibiotics adjunct if systemic signs/cellulitis. Leave open to drain.'),
('be13a356-c39b-551d-9f6f-e6fde8e9ebc2'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Psoas abscess most commonly arises from:', '["Hematogenous spread clinical presentation","Penetrating trauma clinical presentation","Post-surgical clinical presentation","Diverticulitis clinical presentation","Pott disease (tuberculosis of spine) - direct extension"]'::jsonb, 'Pott disease (tuberculosis of spine) - direct extension', 'Psoas abscess: primary (hematogenous) or secondary (from spine/GI). TB is common cause in endemic areas.'),
('8b219139-eb56-55d1-ae08-8b61312c247f'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Subphrenic abscess is commonly located:', '["Below pelvis clinical presentation","In retroperitoneum clinical presentation","In subcutaneous plane clinical presentation","In pleural space clinical presentation","Between diaphragm and liver (right) or spleen"]'::jsonb, 'Between diaphragm and liver (right) or spleen', 'Subphrenic abscess: after abdominal surgery (especially upper GI). Fever, shoulder pain. CT/US guided drainage.'),
('94023999-ff6c-5f7e-8275-86c2badefdbe'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Pelvic abscess after surgery presents with:', '["Lower abdominal pain, fever, diarrhea","Back pain clinical presentation","Chest pain clinical presentation","Headache clinical presentation","Upper abdominal pain clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Lower abdominal pain, fever, diarrhea', 'Pelvic abscess: collection in pouch of Douglas. Tenesmus, diarrhea. Drain via rectum or vagina.'),
('0bc37314-723f-594e-a410-4eec7eaacbf4'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Hidradenitis suppurativa is:', '["Impetigo","Folliculitis","Chronic infection of apocrine sweat glands","Erysipelas","Sebaceous cyst infection"]'::jsonb, 'Chronic infection of apocrine sweat glands', 'Hidradenitis: recurrent abscesses/sinuses in axilla, groin, inframammary. Chronic disease. Excision of involved skin.'),
('91648661-5a7d-5548-a77c-69bfd12aebf9'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Necrotizing fasciitis is:', '["Impetigo clinical presentation","Myositis clinical presentation","Rapidly progressive infection of fascia with tissue necrosis","Superficial cellulitis clinical presentation","Abscess clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Rapidly progressive infection of fascia with tissue necrosis', 'Necrotizing fasciitis: gas in tissue (Crepitus), systemic toxicity. Requires radical debridement. Polymicrobial (Type I) or Group A Strep (Type II).'),
('a5ca1f34-5773-50a8-ada1-419dfe9cb398'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Clean surgical wound (Class I) has:', '["Gross contamination clinical presentation","Entry into GI tract clinical presentation","Minor contamination clinical presentation","Established infection clinical presentation","No infection, no inflammation, no entry into respiratory/GI/GU tract"]'::jsonb, 'No infection, no inflammation, no entry into respiratory/GI/GU tract', 'Class I: elective, closed primarily. Infection rate <2%.'),
('be8c22f6-7387-554b-b6c8-88e03b4f337a'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Clean-contaminated wound (Class II) is:', '["Traumatic wound clinical presentation","Contaminated field clinical presentation","Dirty wound clinical presentation","No infection clinical presentation","Entry into respiratory, GI, or GU tract under controlled conditions"]'::jsonb, 'Entry into respiratory, GI, or GU tract under controlled conditions', 'Class II: controlled entry into viscera. Infection rate <5-10%.'),
('fd550d27-0284-5dfe-a0cb-871f92521259'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Contaminated wound (Class III) includes:', '["Clean incision clinical presentation","Controlled enterotomy clinical presentation","Open traumatic wound, major break in sterility, spillage from GI tract","Healed wound clinical presentation","No contamination clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Open traumatic wound, major break in sterility, spillage from GI tract', 'Class III: significant contamination. Infection rate 10-20%.'),
('f07407f8-53cc-53b9-81b9-8e7089e60153'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Dirty-infected wound (Class IV) includes:', '["Clean wound clinical presentation","Established infection at operative site","Elective surgery clinical presentation","No infection clinical presentation","Minor contamination clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Established infection at operative site', 'Class IV: frank infection. Infection rate >30%. Therapeutic (not prophylactic) antibiotics needed.'),
('a0fcecf1-b66f-557e-9ae4-8f50f5686ad0'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Abrasions are wounds involving:', '["Full thickness skin","Underlying muscle","Bone","Only epidermis","Tendon"]'::jsonb, 'Only epidermis', 'Abrasion: superficial friction injury. Heals by epithelialization. Clean gently.'),
('071b995e-1483-5b8c-86c1-5a678bf58e4a'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Laceration is:', '["Abrasion","Avulsion","Puncture wound","Torn or jagged wound","Clean cut wound"]'::jsonb, 'Torn or jagged wound', 'Laceration: blunt trauma tears tissue. Edges irregular. Debride irregular edges before closure.'),
('05453fe6-556c-53f0-a1a2-ef826a03b1f3'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Incised wound is:', '["Bite","Crush injury","Burn","Clean cut by sharp instrument","Torn wound"]'::jsonb, 'Clean cut by sharp instrument', 'Incised: surgical incision or knife wound. Minimal tissue damage. Good healing if clean.'),
('6c901e54-d651-5737-8cf0-6a08dc126904'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Puncture wound is:', '["Superficial, wide","Narrow, deep wound","Burn","Clean cut","Abrasion"]'::jsonb, 'Narrow, deep wound', 'Puncture: small entrance, deep. Clostridium tetani risk. Debride, leave open, tetanus prophylaxis.'),
('c7c9de49-aaa3-552e-a8db-460a0843f3c6'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Avulsion injury involves:', '["Burn","Only epidermis","Puncture","Tearing away of tissue","Clean cut"]'::jsonb, 'Tearing away of tissue', 'Avulsion: flap of tissue torn off. May have compromised blood supply. Assess viability.'),
('34acb274-5a82-52c8-8ed4-c12ccf5ca6a0'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Crush injury has:', '["No infection risk clinical presentation","Clean edges clinical presentation","Minimal damage clinical presentation","Extensive tissue damage with devitalized tissue","Good healing clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Extensive tissue damage with devitalized tissue', 'Crush: internal injury may be severe despite intact skin. Compartment syndrome, rhabdomyolysis risk.'),
('7224e80f-3f80-55bf-a46f-20aaf7bf73e0'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Tetanus prophylaxis for clean minor wound:', '["No prophylaxis needed","Td booster if >10 years since last dose","Td booster if >5 years","TIG always","Tdap only"]'::jsonb, 'Td booster if >10 years since last dose', 'Clean/minor: booster if >10 years. Dirty wounds: booster if >5 years. TIG for high-risk, unvaccinated.'),
('3b8257f3-3a60-570d-8ed6-0daf50cbee7c'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Human bite wounds are high risk for:', '["Strep only","Tetanus only","Gas gangrene","Staph only","Polymicrobial infection"]'::jsonb, 'Polymicrobial infection', 'Human bite: high infection rate. Eikenella corrodens characteristic. Prophylactic antibiotics. Do not close primarily.'),
('b03672e5-cae8-57e0-b611-4055f1e6bd28'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Animal (dog/cat) bite prophylaxis includes:', '["Closure clinical presentation","No treatment clinical presentation","Antibiotics only clinical presentation","Rabies and tetanus assessment, antibiotics, wound irrigation","Rabies vaccine only clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Rabies and tetanus assessment, antibiotics, wound irrigation', 'Dog/cat bites: copious irrigation, debridement, amoxicillin-clavulanate. Rabies if suspect. Cat bites: deep puncture, high infection.'),
('09655dbd-274f-5aad-88b2-85ebded65219'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Tetanus immunoglobulin (TIG) is indicated:', '["All wounds clinical presentation","No wounds clinical presentation","High-risk wound with unknown/<3 doses tetanus vaccine","Clean wounds clinical presentation","Only in children clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'High-risk wound with unknown/<3 doses tetanus vaccine', 'TIG: 250-500 IU IM. For tetanus-prone wounds (puncture, crush, burn) with inadequate immunization.'),
('b101b2b4-49bd-5cc4-9738-b89fcc532eeb'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Gas gangrene (clostridial myonecrosis) is caused by:', '["Bacteroides","Pseudomonas","S. aureus","Clostridium perfringens","E. coli"]'::jsonb, 'Clostridium perfringens', 'Gas gangrene: crepitus, pain, toxemia. Surgical emergency: radical debridement + antibiotics (penicillin) + hyperbaric O2.'),
('d7f4d9b7-1669-54c9-963e-fbb3ca8eb432'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Ideal wound dressing should:', '["Cause pain clinical presentation clinical presentation","Be non-absorbent clinical presentation clinical presentation","Be occlusive always clinical presentation clinical presentation","Be dry clinical presentation clinical presentation","Maintain moist environment, absorb exudate, allow gas exchange, be non-adherent"]'::jsonb, 'Maintain moist environment, absorb exudate, allow gas exchange, be non-adherent', 'Ideal: moist, absorbent, non-adherent, allow gas exchange, protective, pain-free removal.'),
('229fa952-d39d-5d66-b2d0-7177d3885e74'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Tulle gras (paraffin gauze) dressing:', '["Non-adherent, allows drainage","Compressive","Antimicrobial","Occlusive","Absorbent"]'::jsonb, 'Non-adherent, allows drainage', 'Tulle gras: wide-mesh cotton gauze impregnated with paraffin. Non-adherent. For donor sites, burns.'),
('36e3b827-09e6-5eff-807d-0a30b6f5585a'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Foam dressings are indicated for:', '["Intact skin","Superficial abrasions","Moderate to heavily exuding wounds","Necrotic wounds","Dry wounds"]'::jsonb, 'Moderate to heavily exuding wounds', 'Foam: polyurethane. Absorbs exudate, maintains moisture. For pressure ulcers, leg ulcers.'),
('1bf9196a-f593-5869-b9c9-59b015214ceb'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Hydrogel dressings are used for:', '["Third-degree burns","Exudative wounds","Bleeding wounds","Infected wounds","Dry, necrotic, or painful wounds"]'::jsonb, 'Dry, necrotic, or painful wounds', 'Hydrogel: water/glycerin base. Rehydrates dry wound, pain relief. For necrotic wounds (autolytic debridement).'),
('18ba5159-beba-58e2-a9ad-a6e773947394'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Iodine-based dressings (povidone-iodine, cadexomer iodine):', '["Non-antimicrobial","Broad-spectrum antimicrobial","For dry wounds only","Moisturizing","For intact skin only"]'::jsonb, 'Broad-spectrum antimicrobial', 'Iodine: bactericidal, fungicidal. Cadexomer iodine: slowly releases iodine, absorbs exudate.'),
('8b2a46a8-6b54-52cc-9e72-cf569b8dfffa'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Silver sulfadiazine cream (Silvadene) is used for:', '["Dry wounds","Abrasions","Burn wounds","Clean surgical wounds","Blisters"]'::jsonb, 'Burn wounds', 'Silver sulfadiazine: for partial-thickness burns. Bactericidal. Avoid on face (stains).'),
('7594c606-f402-5a94-ac98-2dd939b2c283'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Honey as a wound dressing:', '["Has no effect clinical presentation","Increases infection clinical presentation","Dries wound clinical presentation","Antimicrobial, debriding, anti-inflammatory, promotes healing","Causes necrosis clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Antimicrobial, debriding, anti-inflammatory, promotes healing', 'Medical honey (Manuka): osmotic effect draws fluid, H2O2 production, antibacterial. For chronic wounds.'),
('d242bbf4-e164-56f2-b3fa-896d7480d0ff'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Maggot debridement therapy (MDT):', '["Are not used clinical presentation","Cause pain clinical presentation","Sterile larvae (Lucilia sericata) eat necrotic tissue, disinfect wound","Increase infection clinical presentation","Are for clean wounds clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Sterile larvae (Lucilia sericata) eat necrotic tissue, disinfect wound', 'MDT: biosurgery. Larvae secrete enzymes that debride necrotic tissue and kill bacteria.'),
('23e0f285-224e-511a-b784-a5dd591f9a20'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Growth factor therapy (becaplermin - PDGF) is used for:', '["Burns","Acute wounds","Pressure ulcers","Clean surgical wounds","Diabetic foot ulcers"]'::jsonb, 'Diabetic foot ulcers', 'Becaplermin: recombinant PDGF. Promotes granulation in diabetic foot ulcers.'),
('a452ca54-a5b2-5d31-bb84-62f507508e7b'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Skin grafts (split-thickness vs full-thickness):', '["STSG contains epidermis + partial dermis; FTSG contains full dermis","Both contain muscle clinical presentation","STSG is full thickness clinical presentation","FTSG has no dermis clinical presentation","Neither has dermis clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'STSG contains epidermis + partial dermis; FTSG contains full dermis', 'STSG: thin, contracts more, less sensitive. FTSG: better cosmesis, less contraction, requires well-vascularized bed.'),
('12e772e4-ab91-5746-8415-99668fe2149e'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Split-thickness skin graft (STSG) donor site:', '["Requires closure clinical presentation","Scarless clinical presentation","Heals by epithelialization from skin appendages","Does not heal clinical presentation","Needs flap clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Heals by epithelialization from skin appendages', 'STSG donor site: re-epithelializes in 10-14 days from hair follicles, sweat glands. Staged healing.'),
('febe4321-d0e3-521d-9def-5a78e21dd9ca'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Meshed skin graft:', '["Is thicker clinical presentation","Slows healing clinical presentation","Is not used clinical presentation","Has no advantages clinical presentation","Allows expansion (ratio 1:1 to 1:6), permits fluid drainage"]'::jsonb, 'Allows expansion (ratio 1:1 to 1:6), permits fluid drainage', 'Meshed graft: cuts slits to expand area. 1:3 ratio = 3x coverage. Allows drainage, conforms to irregular surfaces.'),
('7dc950ce-5d41-500d-8e45-6909413f301a'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Full-thickness skin graft (FTSG) is indicated for:', '["Large wounds clinical presentation clinical presentation","Contaminated wounds clinical presentation clinical presentation","Donor site healing clinical presentation clinical presentation","Infected wounds clinical presentation clinical presentation","Cosmetically sensitive areas (face, hands) - better color match, less contraction"]'::jsonb, 'Cosmetically sensitive areas (face, hands) - better color match, less contraction', 'FTSG: includes epidermis + full dermis. Better cosmetic result. Donor site closed primarily.'),
('d231cf65-f925-5b5b-8b03-b0b38a3e9b8a'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Which flap has its own blood supply (pedicled)?', '["FTSG","STSG","Local flap","Free flap","Allograft"]'::jsonb, 'Local flap', 'Pedicled flap: remains attached by vascular pedicle. Free flap: vessels divided and reanastomosed.'),
('19fcdf0f-916d-5875-8d09-870b58ad1a8d'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Random pattern flap relies on:', '["Granulation tissue","Adhesion","Named axial vessel","Subdermal plexus","Microvascular anastomosis"]'::jsonb, 'Subdermal plexus', 'Random flap: limited by blood supply. Axial flap: includes named artery (longer, more reliable).'),
('e071d11d-5bd6-5d8f-a011-1343179a918f'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Burns are classified by depth: First-degree (superficial) involves:', '["Only epidermis","Epidermis + dermis","Muscle","Full thickness","Subcutaneous tissue"]'::jsonb, 'Only epidermis', 'First-degree: sunburn. Heals in 3-5 days. No scar.'),
('b09153f2-0715-59e5-8428-6e528c851c88'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Second-degree (partial-thickness) burn involves:', '["Muscle","Full thickness","Epidermis + part of dermis","Subcutaneous","Only epidermis"]'::jsonb, 'Epidermis + part of dermis', 'Superficial partial (superficial dermis): painful, blanch. Deep partial (deep dermis): less painful, no blanch.'),
('336b7639-8c46-5c65-9785-9f1e9519c6ad'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Third-degree (full-thickness) burn involves:', '["Muscle","Subcutaneous only","Partial dermis","Only epidermis","Entire epidermis + dermis"]'::jsonb, 'Entire epidermis + dermis', 'Third-degree: destroys skin appendages. Requires excision and grafting. Eschar may require escharotomy.'),
('b900d1cb-e783-5680-8ac9-22337ce06b70'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Rule of nines for adult burn estimation: Anterior trunk is:', '["9%","4.5%","1%","36%","18%"]'::jsonb, '18%', 'Anterior trunk 18%, posterior trunk 18%, each arm 9%, each leg 18%, head & neck 9%, perineum 1%.'),
('881c4b0f-1183-5014-b186-49d47114e6bc'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Parkland formula for fluid resuscitation in first 24h:', '["4 mL x weight (kg) x %TBSA","2 mL/kg/%","1 mL/kg/%","No fluids","10 mL/kg/%"]'::jsonb, '4 mL x weight (kg) x %TBSA', 'Parkland: Ringer lactate 4 mL/kg/%TBSA. First half in 8h from burn time. Urine output target 0.5-1 mL/kg/h.'),
('4c6cb62d-3d82-519f-b67a-7fc06bab6439'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Inhalation injury in burns is suggested by:', '["Leg burns only clinical presentation","No symptoms clinical presentation","Facial burns, carbonaceous sputum, hoarseness, elevated COHb","Arm burns only clinical presentation","Clear sputum clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Facial burns, carbonaceous sputum, hoarseness, elevated COHb', 'Inhalation injury: major cause of burn mortality. COHb >10%. Bronchoscopy for diagnosis.'),
('143a805e-bfe1-5329-bf0f-7b7d7b4aa707'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Escharotomy is indicated for:', '["Any burn clinical presentation","Superficial burns clinical presentation","Small burns clinical presentation","Circumferential full-thickness burns","Infected burns clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Circumferential full-thickness burns', 'Escharotomy: incise eschar to release pressure. Prevents neurovascular compromise. For limbs, chest, digits.'),
('5882458c-3558-5674-a987-169a06faa42e'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Electrical burn injury has:', '["Small entry wound but deep internal damage","No muscle damage clinical presentation","Large entry wound only clinical presentation","Only skin damage clinical presentation","No cardiac effect clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Small entry wound but deep internal damage', 'Electrical burns: tissue damage along current path. Myoglobinuria -> renal failure. Cardiac monitoring needed.'),
('20612bc9-cb3b-57ba-a33e-67617f7b1bc4'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Chemical burn treatment:', '["Copious irrigation (water lavage) for at least 20 minutes","Neutralizing agents clinical presentation","Dry dressings clinical presentation","Oil clinical presentation","No treatment clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Copious irrigation (water lavage) for at least 20 minutes', 'Chemical burn: irrigate immediately. Remove contaminated clothing. Specific antidotes for some (HF: calcium gluconate).'),
('d5441d33-fcbf-53a8-8cb8-b751b197e9cc'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Burn wound sepsis (most common cause of death in burns):', '["Topical antimicrobials","Hydrotherapy","Surgery","No treatment","Systemic antibiotics only"]'::jsonb, 'Topical antimicrobials', 'Topical antimicrobials: decrease bacterial colonization, prevent conversion of partial to full thickness.'),
('93ec3573-975f-599e-a326-2a865582fc13'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Early excision and grafting of burn wounds:', '["Not recommended clinical presentation","Increases infection clinical presentation","Causes more scarring clinical presentation","Removes necrotic tissue, reduces infection, decreases hospital stay","Delays healing clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Removes necrotic tissue, reduces infection, decreases hospital stay', 'Early excision (within 72h): debridement of eschar, immediate or staged grafting. Standard of care.'),
('84b43677-3bdc-582b-8018-a0afdd276573'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Cyanide poisoning in smoke inhalation is treated with:', '["Oxygen only clinical presentation","Atropine clinical presentation","Hydroxocobalamin (Cyanokit) or sodium thiosulfate","Flumazenil clinical presentation","Bicarbonate clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Hydroxocobalamin (Cyanokit) or sodium thiosulfate', 'Cyanide: from burning synthetic materials (polyurethane). Hydroxocobalamin is first-line antidote.'),
('135f8cb2-c53c-575a-8885-cd936426d9d4'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Carbon monoxide poisoning from fires: treatment:', '["Beta-blockers","100% oxygen","Room air","50% oxygen","No oxygen"]'::jsonb, '100% oxygen', 'CO: >200x affinity for Hb. Pulse oximeter falsely normal. Carboxyhemoglobin level measured. Hyperbaric O2 for severe.'),
('e97d5944-d009-5b3f-a2e0-878da92219cf'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Burn shock (first 24-48h) is primarily:', '["Cardiogenic shock","Hypovolemic shock","Obstructive shock","Distributive shock","Neurogenic shock"]'::jsonb, 'Hypovolemic shock', 'Burn shock: massive capillary leak, decreased plasma volume. Fluid resuscitation key. After 24h, fluid mobilizes.'),
('ac749f17-7f27-5ed8-b942-e7f4ae3eee35'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Major burn criteria (ABA):', '["Superficial burns clinical presentation clinical presentation",">10% TBSA in children, >15% in adults, >25% TBSA, or any full-thickness, or involving special areas","Any burn clinical presentation clinical presentation","Only >90% clinical presentation clinical presentation","Only >50% clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, '>10% TBSA in children, >15% in adults, >25% TBSA, or any full-thickness, or involving special areas', 'Major burns: transfer to burn center. Criteria: >10% child, >15% adult, face/hands/genitals/feet/perineum/joints.'),
('a16af4da-c59b-5f08-8a43-46ab1fe4f85e'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Pressure ulcer staging: Stage 1 is:', '["Non-blanchable erythema","Partial-thickness skin loss","Full-thickness skin loss","Unstageable","Bone/tendon exposure"]'::jsonb, 'Non-blanchable erythema', 'Stage 1: intact skin with persistent redness. Reversible with pressure relief.'),
('0fb3c48f-8d53-594a-bec4-2a4e99dda7ae'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Stage 2 pressure ulcer:', '["Partial-thickness skin loss","Eschar covered","Full-thickness skin loss","Bone exposure","Non-blanchable erythema"]'::jsonb, 'Partial-thickness skin loss', 'Stage 2: epidermal/dermal loss. Shallow, pink/red wound bed. No slough.'),
('2498caf0-88ac-5257-92ec-0a1d16eac328'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Stage 3 pressure ulcer:', '["Non-blanchable erythema","Bone exposure","Epidermal loss","Full-thickness skin loss","Superficial"]'::jsonb, 'Full-thickness skin loss', 'Stage 3: full-thickness loss down to but not through fascia. Fat visible. Undermining possible.'),
('d97cdcb9-58c4-5541-a373-a0717fad76fe'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Stage 4 pressure ulcer:', '["Non-blanchable clinical presentation","Superficial clinical presentation","Full-thickness loss with exposed bone, tendon, or muscle","Fat visible only clinical presentation","Epidermal loss only clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Full-thickness loss with exposed bone, tendon, or muscle', 'Stage 4: deep tissue loss with exposed deep structures. Osteomyelitis risk.'),
('fac5d8da-d278-52d8-b767-3aa876210141'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Unstageable pressure ulcer:', '["Healed clinical presentation clinical presentation","Full-thickness tissue loss with base covered by slough/eschar","Stage 1 clinical presentation clinical presentation","No ulcer clinical presentation clinical presentation","Stage 2 clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Full-thickness tissue loss with base covered by slough/eschar', 'Unstageable: until slough/eschar removed (debridement), depth cannot be assessed.'),
('2849116c-7d11-5f42-a44e-31ed1cc64e47'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Deep tissue injury (DTI):', '["Stage 2 clinical presentation clinical presentation","Stage 3 clinical presentation clinical presentation","Purple/maroon intact skin or blood-filled blister due to pressure damage","Stage 1 clinical presentation clinical presentation","Stage 4 clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Purple/maroon intact skin or blood-filled blister due to pressure damage', 'DTI: deep damage under intact skin. May evolve to Stage 3/4. Reversible if pressure relieved.'),
('5e4cf217-0ab4-5fc5-b677-23c328fdf13c'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Most common site for pressure ulcer:', '["Trochanter","Ischial tuberosity","Occiput","Sacrum/coccyx","Heel"]'::jsonb, 'Sacrum/coccyx', 'Sacrum: most common in supine position. Heel: second most common. Ischial: sitting.'),
('37fef5e1-0987-56be-9685-c37b6fdeaa60'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Which pressure ulcer cannot be reversed without intervention?', '["Stage 1 and 2 reversible","Stage 2","No stage","Stage 3 and 4","Stage 1"]'::jsonb, 'No stage', 'Stage 1-2: reversible with pressure relief. Stage 3-4: tissue loss requires reconstruction.'),
('b2505ecf-4053-5f27-8e12-a0d41461f589'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Braden scale assesses:', '["Risk of pressure ulcer development","Ulcer stage clinical presentation","Wound infection clinical presentation","Burn depth clinical presentation","Wound size clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Risk of pressure ulcer development', 'Braden: 6 subscales. Score ≤18 at risk. ≤9 very high risk. Interventions: repositioning, support surfaces.'),
('4b1f5248-b6fc-5485-9d36-9abf28608779'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Pressure relief for prevention:', '["Every 6 hours","Reposition every 2 hours","No repositioning","Continuous position","Once daily"]'::jsonb, 'Reposition every 2 hours', 'Repositioning q2h: gold standard. Specialized mattresses (low-air-loss, alternating pressure) reduce incidence.'),
('ac29739c-85b0-5f84-8d4c-75e3fa86031c'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Support surfaces for pressure ulcer prevention:', '["All of these: low-air-loss beds, alternating pressure mattresses, gel overlays","No mattress needed clinical presentation","Any surface clinical presentation","Standard mattress only clinical presentation","Water bed clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'All of these: low-air-loss beds, alternating pressure mattresses, gel overlays', 'Support surfaces redistribute pressure. Static (foam, gel) for low risk. Dynamic (alternating) for high risk.'),
('ba0286c4-33ac-5fb5-9489-a8dacd494a71'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Surgical debridement of pressure ulcer removes:', '["Necrotic tissue, eschar, slough, and biofilm","No debridement clinical presentation","Only granulation clinical presentation","Only superficial tissue clinical presentation","Healthy tissue clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Necrotic tissue, eschar, slough, and biofilm', 'Sharp/surgical debridement: fastest method. Removes all devitalized tissue. Must see healthy bleeding base.'),
('75250b1c-17f3-5a01-8d39-b3df1e966a60'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Flap reconstruction for pressure ulcers uses:', '["No closure","Healing by secondary intention","Skin grafts only","Primary closure","Muscle or musculocutaneous flaps"]'::jsonb, 'Muscle or musculocutaneous flaps', 'Pressure ulcer flaps: gluteus rotation for sacrum, tensor fascia lata for trochanter, hamstring for ischial.'),
('6a1e2309-1497-535c-bb0c-5e810c50adda'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Recurrence rate after pressure ulcer flap surgery:', '["Unknown","100%","High","Zero","Low"]'::jsonb, 'High', 'High recurrence: due to ongoing pressure, poor nutrition, incontinence. Prevention is key.'),
('2d9dbda6-3080-5102-a155-f313b03ac214'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Wound healing by secondary intention in pressure ulcers:', '["For all ulcers clinical presentation","Only Stage 4 clinical presentation","Only infected clinical presentation","Allowed to granulate and contract","Never clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Allowed to granulate and contract', 'Secondary intention: for smaller Stage 2-3 ulcers. Takes weeks to months. Proper wound care essential.'),
('f3fd7f70-0167-53e4-8cab-73389eb85f4b'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Dry gangrene is characterized by:', '["Gas in tissue clinical presentation","Coagulative necrosis (dark, dry, mummified) due to arterial occlusion","Yellow-green color clinical presentation","Moist, infected necrosis clinical presentation","Putrid odor clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Coagulative necrosis (dark, dry, mummified) due to arterial occlusion', 'Dry gangrene: arterial insufficiency (atherosclerosis, diabetes). No infection. Line of demarcation visible.'),
('e5d1515b-c120-5a6a-9dd3-43438d231793'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'In a surgical context, Wet gangrene is characterized by:', '["Sharp line of demarcation clinical presentation","Coagulative necrosis + liquefactive necrosis due to bacterial infection","No odor clinical presentation","No infection clinical presentation","Dry, mummified clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Coagulative necrosis + liquefactive necrosis due to bacterial infection', 'Wet gangrene: arterial + venous occlusion with infection. Rapid spread. Foul odor. Sepsis risk.'),
('03c570bb-863f-5a1e-9d92-5a2ef074bd6e'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Gas gangrene is caused by:', '["E. coli","Clostridium perfringens","B. fragilis","Strep pyogenes","S. aureus"]'::jsonb, 'Clostridium perfringens', 'Gas gangrene: clostridial myonecrosis. Exotoxins (alpha toxin - lecithinase). Crepitus, severe pain, toxemia.'),
('acc68891-5393-59ff-becf-f63f98b15ed0'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Fournier gangrene is:', '["Gas gangrene of leg clinical presentation","Venous gangrene clinical presentation","Dry gangrene of foot clinical presentation","Wet gangrene of arm clinical presentation","Necrotizing fasciitis of perineum, scrotum, penis"]'::jsonb, 'Necrotizing fasciitis of perineum, scrotum, penis', 'Fournier: polymicrobial (aerobic + anaerobic). Rapidly progressive. Radical debridement + antibiotics.'),
('dce33b16-700f-5333-a7b5-52536261e6c7'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Melanoma vs melena (bowel gangrene): Intestinal gangrene presents with:', '["Chronic constipation clinical presentation","Severe abdominal pain, peritonitis, shock, bloody diarrhea","Painless clinical presentation","Dysuria clinical presentation","Dysphagia clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Severe abdominal pain, peritonitis, shock, bloody diarrhea', 'Intestinal gangrene: strangulation, mesenteric ischemia. Emergency laparotomy. Resect non-viable bowel.'),
('5b84ff86-13e7-5c50-8801-0078f3b9b2c2'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Coagulative necrosis is seen in:', '["Brain infarct clinical presentation clinical presentation","Fibrinoid necrosis clinical presentation clinical presentation","Fat necrosis clinical presentation clinical presentation","Ischemic tissue (except brain) - e.g., myocardial infarct, splenic/kidney infarct","Caseous necrosis clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Ischemic tissue (except brain) - e.g., myocardial infarct, splenic/kidney infarct', 'Coagulative: architecture preserved despite cell death. Most common type after ischemia.'),
('02c713c1-2d8f-5351-8447-6497c8e1e403'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Liquefactive necrosis is characteristic of:', '["Any infarct","Kidney infarction","Brain infarction","Myocardial infarction","Spleen infarction"]'::jsonb, 'Brain infarction', 'Brain: liquefactive necrosis (cystic spaces). Also abscesses (pus = liquefied dead tissue + neutrophils).'),
('873fe727-7daa-59e8-8ca0-f2ddf80f67fb'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Caseous necrosis is characteristic of:', '["Abscess","Tuberculosis","Infarct","Fat necrosis","Gangrene"]'::jsonb, 'Tuberculosis', 'Caseous: TB granulomas. Macrophages with epithelioid change + Langhans giant cells. Amorphous eosinophilic material.'),
('d2388330-69d9-5d23-b614-89c87b801ed9'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Fat necrosis is seen in:', '["Infarct","Tuberculosis","Acute pancreatitis","Myocardial infarction","Trauma to breast"]'::jsonb, 'Acute pancreatitis', 'Fat necrosis: chalky white areas (calcium soaps). Acute pancreatitis: omental/retroperitoneal fat.'),
('4368790e-2869-51ae-9748-2d0ea5b9727c'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Fibrinoid necrosis is seen in:', '["Infarct","Fat necrosis","Tuberculosis","Infection","Vasculitis"]'::jsonb, 'Vasculitis', 'Fibrinoid: vessel wall damage, fibrin deposits. PAS-positive. Seen in malignant hypertension, PAN.'),
('b8a9499b-fad5-54e7-ac5d-43ad5d9e72d9'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Traumatic fat necrosis of breast:', '["Infectious clinical presentation","Presents as painless mass, may mimic carcinoma","Always malignant clinical presentation","Requires mastectomy clinical presentation","No treatment needed clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Presents as painless mass, may mimic carcinoma', 'Breast fat necrosis: history of trauma. May need biopsy to rule out cancer. Self-limiting.'),
('cf8e80be-b133-5ec1-afea-aca96ebc5733'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Apoptosis is:', '["Autophagy","Necrosis","Programmed cell death","Uncontrolled cell death","Gangrene"]'::jsonb, 'Programmed cell death', 'Apoptosis: coordinated, energy-dependent. Caspases activated. No inflammatory response. Single cells affected.'),
('6e46512d-56df-5bca-b5f1-ff4e92e6162b'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Necrosis is:', '["Without inflammation","Unprogrammed cell death due to injury","Controlled cell death","Physiologic","Clean removal"]'::jsonb, 'Unprogrammed cell death due to injury', 'Necrosis: pathologic. Cell and organelle swelling, plasma membrane rupture, inflammation. Always pathologic.'),
('20272fd4-0254-52cc-859b-a26dede0c40d'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Steatonecrosis (fat necrosis) in acute pancreatitis is due to:', '["Amylase activity clinical presentation","Phospholipase activity clinical presentation","Elastase activity clinical presentation","Trypsin activity clinical presentation","Lipase-mediated breakdown of triglycerides into FFA that complex with calcium"]'::jsonb, 'Lipase-mediated breakdown of triglycerides into FFA that complex with calcium', 'Fat necrosis: lipase digests triglycerides -> free fatty acids + calcium -> calcium soaps (saponification).'),
('9e832a79-139f-5f3c-b0d8-61dde24c168c'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Regarding the pathological aspect, On diagnostic evaluation, Wallerian degeneration refers to:', '["Skin necrosis clinical presentation","Muscle atrophy clinical presentation","Fat necrosis clinical presentation","Degeneration of distal nerve segment after axonal transection","Myocardial necrosis clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Degeneration of distal nerve segment after axonal transection', 'Wallerian: axon and myelin degenerate distal to nerve injury. Schwann cells proliferate. Proximal segment regenerates.'),
('a8a7931f-722a-528b-a626-ec0e7963029b'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'The metabolic response to surgery (stress response) includes:', '["Anabolic state clinical presentation","Decreased cortisol clinical presentation","Decreased heart rate clinical presentation","Increased catecholamines, cortisol, glucagon","Decreased metabolism clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Increased catecholamines, cortisol, glucagon', 'Stress response: hypermetabolism, protein catabolism, insulin resistance, sodium/water retention.'),
('1de78ef9-9f59-5091-9169-60e47854ea88'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'The ebb phase of injury response:', '["Recovery phase clinical presentation clinical presentation","Immediate (first 24-48h): decreased metabolic rate, hypovolemia, shock","Late phase clinical presentation clinical presentation","Catabolic phase clinical presentation clinical presentation","Anabolic phase clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Immediate (first 24-48h): decreased metabolic rate, hypovolemia, shock', 'Ebb: hypovolemia, decreased tissue perfusion, metabolic rate down. Treatment: resuscitation.'),
('a33f6d58-9161-562e-9918-46fdec55bb8f'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'The flow phase of injury response:', '["Hypometabolic","Immediate phase","Catabolic","Anabolic","Reversal of catabolism"]'::jsonb, 'Catabolic', 'Flow: hypermetabolism, proteolysis, lipolysis, gluconeogenesis, insulin resistance. Can last days to weeks.'),
('1fc0dd4c-fe7d-51af-a788-e88e2e695e4f'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Nitrogen balance after major surgery is:', '["Equilibrium","Negative","Positive","Neutral","No change"]'::jsonb, 'Negative', 'Negative nitrogen balance: muscle protein breakdown. Wound healing and recovery require protein. Resolves in recovery phase.'),
('971d9e91-2f87-529c-a228-6baed3ab6e9d'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Nutritional assessment: Serum albumin <3.5 g/dL indicates:', '["Over-nutrition","Normal","Malnutrition","Good nutrition","Acute starvation"]'::jsonb, 'Malnutrition', 'Albumin: poor acute marker (long half-life, affected by inflammation). Prealbumin (transthyretin) better for acute changes.'),
('a6b0193e-62ab-55af-99b6-c240da845cad'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Indications for enteral nutrition (EN):', '["Bowel obstruction clinical presentation","Functional GI tract but unable to meet nutritional needs orally","Peritonitis clinical presentation","Severe diarrhea clinical presentation","High-output fistula clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Functional GI tract but unable to meet nutritional needs orally', 'EN: preferred over parenteral. Maintains gut barrier, reduces infection. NG, gastrostomy, jejunostomy.'),
('297c8185-06cc-5827-938a-b419716d8fd4'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Contraindications to enteral nutrition:', '["Malnutrition clinical presentation clinical presentation","Surgery clinical presentation clinical presentation","All of these: bowel obstruction, peritonitis, severe ileus, high-output fistula","Diabetes clinical presentation clinical presentation","Old age clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'All of these: bowel obstruction, peritonitis, severe ileus, high-output fistula', 'EN contraindicated: non-functioning gut. Consider parenteral nutrition (PN) in these cases.'),
('442ea1bc-c33f-5b1a-9d39-8f883a0d273f'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Nasogastric (NG) tube feeding:', '["Long-term solution clinical presentation","For all patients clinical presentation","No aspiration risk clinical presentation","Temporary access for short-term EN (<4-6 weeks) - risk of aspiration","Permanent access clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Temporary access for short-term EN (<4-6 weeks) - risk of aspiration', 'NG: risk of aspiration (check placement). Verify pH <5.5 or CXR before use.'),
('f3184c3c-1032-59f1-8e99-4d76a05bc8c0'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Percutaneous endoscopic gastrostomy (PEG) is for:', '["Only cancer patients clinical presentation","Only children clinical presentation","Long-term enteral feeding (>4-6 weeks) when oral intake inadequate","All patients clinical presentation","Short-term feeding clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Long-term enteral feeding (>4-6 weeks) when oral intake inadequate', 'PEG: endoscopically placed. Safe. Use after 2 weeks for wound maturation. Replace by bumper.'),
('36ecce40-fb5f-56b2-ad28-5ae5bbd7c722'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Jejunostomy feeding (J-tube) is indicated when:', '["Colon resection","No GI function","Obstruction distal to jejunum","Gastric feeding not possible","Short-term feeding"]'::jsonb, 'Gastric feeding not possible', 'J-tube: placed surgically or radiologically. Continuous drip feeds. Less reflux/aspiration risk.'),
('2563b31c-204f-5be2-8242-688c79d09fd8'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Complications of enteral feeding include:', '["Only infection clinical presentation clinical presentation","Only pain clinical presentation clinical presentation","All of these: diarrhea, aspiration, tube displacement, blockage, refeeding syndrome","None clinical presentation clinical presentation","Only bleeding clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'All of these: diarrhea, aspiration, tube displacement, blockage, refeeding syndrome', 'EN complications: mechanical (blockage), GI (diarrhea, bloating), metabolic (hyperglycemia), infectious (aspiration pneumonia).'),
('2b055755-423a-5a6d-90c8-3891db8fd722'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Parenteral nutrition (PN) indications:', '["All surgical patients","Malnourished only","Non-functioning GI tract","Short term only","Functional GI tract"]'::jsonb, 'Non-functioning GI tract', 'PN: central venous access. Total PN (TPN) for complete nutritional support. Peripheral PN (PPN) for short term.'),
('3e691163-5ac7-5525-b013-32988bbed894'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Refeeding syndrome is characterized by:', '["No electrolyte changes clinical presentation","Hyperglycemia only clinical presentation","Hyperkalemia clinical presentation","Hypophosphatemia, hypokalemia, hypomagnesemia","Hyperphosphatemia clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Hypophosphatemia, hypokalemia, hypomagnesemia', 'Refeeding syndrome: insulin surge drives phosphate, K+, Mg++ into cells. Can cause cardiac failure. Prevent by slow refeeding, electrolyte monitoring.'),
('62829aab-1ead-59c6-bc54-658380ef2aca'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Refeeding syndrome prevention:', '["Slow caloric initiation (20-25 kcal/kg/day), monitor electrolytes, supplement phosphate/K+/Mg++","No supplements clinical presentation clinical presentation","Fast 100% calories clinical presentation clinical presentation","No monitoring clinical presentation clinical presentation","Full feeding immediately clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Slow caloric initiation (20-25 kcal/kg/day), monitor electrolytes, supplement phosphate/K+/Mg++', 'Prevention: start at 50% target calories, check electrolytes pre-refeeding and q12-24h, replace aggressively.'),
('ddfa0e94-b1ae-5dcd-909b-c9bc6643608b'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Enteral vs Parenteral nutrition: EN advantage over PN:', '["No benefit clinical presentation clinical presentation","Preserves gut barrier, fewer infections, lower cost, more physiologic","More infections clinical presentation clinical presentation","More expensive clinical presentation clinical presentation","Slower clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Preserves gut barrier, fewer infections, lower cost, more physiologic', 'EN: reduces bacterial translocation, maintains gut-associated lymphoid tissue (GALT). EN preferred when possible.'),
('e4088dd8-1be6-58b9-b350-4723d79533eb'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Post-operative fluid management: Maintenance fluid in adults:', '["50 mL/kg/day","100 mL/kg/day","200 mL/kg/day","30 mL/kg/day","10 mL/kg/day"]'::jsonb, '30 mL/kg/day', 'Maintenance: 30 mL/kg/day. Hartmann''s (Ringer lactate) or dextrose-saline. Adjust for losses.'),
('0ab5059d-82c6-5fed-9267-a55d87b9e69e'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Third-space fluid loss after surgery:', '["Fluid shifts from intravascular to interstitial space","No fluid shifts","Increased intravascular volume","Hemodilution","Increased urine output"]'::jsonb, 'Fluid shifts from intravascular to interstitial space', 'Third-space: due to capillary leak and inflammation. Accounts for need for >maintenance fluids post-op.'),
('96216b3a-c8e2-5daf-9be7-b46af0989a5d'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Post-operative oliguria: most common cause is:', '["Renal failure clinical presentation","Post-renal obstruction clinical presentation","SIADH clinical presentation","Hypovolemia (prerenal azotemia) - inadequate fluid resuscitation","UTI clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Hypovolemia (prerenal azotemia) - inadequate fluid resuscitation', 'Post-op oliguria: check vitals, urine output, BUN/Cr, urine Na. Tx: fluid bolus if hypovolemia.'),
('de4c7485-5420-595c-9826-3237a158890e'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'SIADH after surgery leads to:', '["Hypernatremia clinical presentation","Hypokalemia clinical presentation","Metabolic acidosis clinical presentation","Hyponatremia (dilutional) due to inappropriate ADH secretion","Hypervolemia clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Hyponatremia (dilutional) due to inappropriate ADH secretion', 'SIADH: common after surgery, pain, nausea. Free water retention. Hypo-osmolality, urine Na >20. Fluid restrict.'),
('ea821ad1-3f37-5f61-aa2f-d9ed43978326'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Stress-dose steroids are needed for:', '["Patients on chronic steroids (>5 mg prednisone >3 weeks) undergoing surgery","No patients clinical presentation","Only cardiac patients clinical presentation","All patients clinical presentation","Only diabetic patients clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Patients on chronic steroids (>5 mg prednisone >3 weeks) undergoing surgery', 'Stress-dose: prevent adrenal crisis. Give hydrocortisone 50-100 mg q8h perioperatively, taper.'),
('dc6b37ab-4f9d-55a8-8f32-68c3fcb88978'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Perioperative beta-blockers:', '["Stop before surgery clinical presentation","No benefit clinical presentation","Continue chronic beta-blockers; consider for high-risk cardiac patients","Start all patients clinical presentation","Only for hypertension clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Continue chronic beta-blockers; consider for high-risk cardiac patients', 'Beta-blockers: reduce cardiac events in intermediate/high-risk patients on beta-blockers. Do not start before major surgery.'),
('e4629033-99ea-5ee9-84e6-01ebfd04bbfc'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Preoperative antibiotics: redosing interval for cefazolin:', '["Once only","Every 4 hours during surgery","Every 2 hours","Every 12 hours","Every 8 hours"]'::jsonb, 'Every 4 hours during surgery', 'Redose cefazolin q4h. For blood loss >1500 mL, also redose. Discontinue within 24h.'),
('cae1a78e-d12c-59f5-9d08-9ed0433ffd45'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'VTE prophylaxis in surgery:', '["Warfarin clinical presentation clinical presentation","LMWH (enoxaparin) or unfractionated heparin + mechanical prophylaxis","Only ambulation clinical presentation clinical presentation","Aspirin only clinical presentation clinical presentation","No prophylaxis clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'LMWH (enoxaparin) or unfractionated heparin + mechanical prophylaxis', 'VTE prophylaxis: LMWH + sequential compression devices. Caprini risk score. Continue until discharge (extended for high-risk).'),
('bf940eb7-f4ab-52ae-a239-f1bd3e291a39'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Enhanced Recovery After Surgery (ERAS) includes:', '["Only early mobilization clinical presentation","Only early feeding clinical presentation","Only pain control clinical presentation","Only fasting clinical presentation","All of these: preoperative optimization, minimal fasting, carbohydrate loading"]'::jsonb, 'All of these: preoperative optimization, minimal fasting, carbohydrate loading', 'ERAS: evidence-based perioperative care. Reduces length of stay, complications. Multimodal approach.'),
('7337edaa-81c9-5c29-b73a-53b54062f592'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Preoperative carbohydrate loading (ERAS):', '["Glucose IV","Nothing by mouth after midnight","Clear carbohydrate drink 2h before surgery","Only water","Only food"]'::jsonb, 'Clear carbohydrate drink 2h before surgery', 'CHO loading: 50g carbohydrate 2-4h pre-op. Reduces postoperative insulin resistance. Do not use in gastroparesis/obstruction.'),
('6754c477-bdef-55ce-83e0-4dfc7dc4e866'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Hypothermia in the OR leads to:', '["Better outcomes clinical presentation","Faster recovery clinical presentation","Increased surgical site infection, coagulopathy, cardiac events","Reduced bleeding clinical presentation","No complications clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Increased surgical site infection, coagulopathy, cardiac events', 'Perioperative hypothermia: impairs immune function, platelet function, drug metabolism. Active warming essential.'),
('fbde71e2-318f-5cc9-8652-7fb9d3be9022'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Wolf''s law relates to:', '["Burn resuscitation clinical presentation","Wound healing clinical presentation","Fluid balance clinical presentation","Bone remodeling in response to mechanical stress","Nutrition clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Bone remodeling in response to mechanical stress', 'Wolf''s law: bone adapts to loads. Not directly surgical pathology but relevant to fracture healing.'),
('a7e96040-1d9c-5ae0-aa97-b59b60f7db32'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Halsted principles of surgery include:', '["Only hemostasis clinical presentation clinical presentation","Only gentle handling clinical presentation clinical presentation","All of these: gentle tissue handling, meticulous hemostasis, aseptic technique","Only closure clinical presentation clinical presentation","Only asepsis clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'All of these: gentle tissue handling, meticulous hemostasis, aseptic technique', 'Halsted: core surgical principles. Minimize tissue trauma, infection, and wound complications.'),
('3dc026e8-579c-5cb7-9a94-c16b28d626b5'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Good surgical handwashing (scrub) duration:', '["10 seconds","Minimum 2-5 minutes","30 seconds","15 seconds","10 minutes"]'::jsonb, 'Minimum 2-5 minutes', 'Scrub: hands to elbows. Alcohol-based hand rub acceptable if hands not visibly soiled.'),
('4cca5a2a-e132-54a5-8222-463623a4fd94'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Surgical gowning: sterile area is:', '["Only gloves clinical presentation clinical presentation","Back of gown clinical presentation clinical presentation","Only hands clinical presentation clinical presentation","Entire gown clinical presentation clinical presentation","Front of gown from chest to sterile field level (waist), sleeves to elbows"]'::jsonb, 'Front of gown from chest to sterile field level (waist), sleeves to elbows', 'Sterile field: front from chest to waist, sleeves (except axilla). Back is not sterile.'),
('1981d618-26a5-5a75-9447-ebd6284ebfa7'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Surgical specimen labeling must include:', '["Patient name, ID, date, type of specimen, anatomical site, orientation sutures","Date only clinical presentation clinical presentation","Only surgeon name clinical presentation clinical presentation","Name only clinical presentation clinical presentation","No labeling needed clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Patient name, ID, date, type of specimen, anatomical site, orientation sutures', 'Labeling: critical for correct diagnosis. All specimens must be labeled at time of collection.'),
('2d5e6fc5-5c4d-559d-9b26-1882ace32b72'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Orientation sutures on surgical specimens:', '["Are not used clinical presentation","No standard meaning clinical presentation","Confuse pathologist clinical presentation","Short suture = margin (e.g., superior), long = another margin","Are decorative clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Short suture = margin (e.g., superior), long = another margin', 'Orientation: surgeon marks margins with sutures. Pathologist inks and sections accordingly.'),
('e38bad56-d4bf-53c5-b2ef-e0f5e29088e8'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Margins inked by pathologist:', '["Not important clinical presentation","One color only clinical presentation","H&E only clinical presentation","No ink needed clinical presentation","Different colors for different margins"]'::jsonb, 'Different colors for different margins', 'Ink: identifies margin surfaces. Tumor at inked margin = positive margin (R1).'),
('6d1c13f6-fdaa-5c93-a80e-a7d6a001be9d'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Specimen radiography (specimen X-ray) is used for:', '["Confirming excision of target lesion","No specimens clinical presentation","Bone specimens clinical presentation","All specimens clinical presentation","Lymph nodes only clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Confirming excision of target lesion', 'Specimen X-ray: for non-palpable breast lesions, lung nodules. Confirm lesion within specimen.'),
('415a0144-a61b-5cfd-b156-6de1e6ecb09a'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Frozen section: specimen sent to pathology:', '["Dry container clinical presentation clinical presentation","Fresh (unfixed) specimen transported immediately on saline-moistened gauze","In alcohol clinical presentation clinical presentation","In saline clinical presentation clinical presentation","In formalin clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Fresh (unfixed) specimen transported immediately on saline-moistened gauze', 'Frozen section: fresh, no fixative. Transport immediately. Formalin ruins frozen section (freezing artifact).'),
('f2f54559-ebf8-5e91-be24-585bc743cf22'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Specimen for flow cytometry (lymphoma workup):', '["Dry clinical presentation","Fresh specimen in sterile saline or RPMI medium","In formalin clinical presentation","In alcohol clinical presentation","Paraffin block clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Fresh specimen in sterile saline or RPMI medium', 'Flow cytometry: requires viable cells. Send fresh/refrigerated. Formalin kills cells -> uninterpretable.'),
('846f0c47-885d-522e-ae75-ae2bc106baf0'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Specimen for electron microscopy:', '["Saline clinical presentation clinical presentation","Glutaraldehyde (not formalin) - preserves ultrastructure","Formalin clinical presentation clinical presentation","Alcohol clinical presentation clinical presentation","Dry clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Glutaraldehyde (not formalin) - preserves ultrastructure', 'Glutaraldehyde: crosslinks proteins better, preserves fine structure. For renal biopsies, cilia disorders.'),
('5ad7c2f1-3379-5262-9fa0-16744585eb19'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Specimen for microbiological culture:', '["Paraffin clinical presentation","Alcohol preserved clinical presentation","Sterile container, fresh tissue/fluid","Formalin fixed clinical presentation","Dry swab clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Sterile container, fresh tissue/fluid', 'Culture: must be fresh. Transport in sterile container. Add saline to keep moist. Refrigerate if delay.'),
('6d6e8192-9043-5911-b12e-27a8e2c15742'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Breast specimen orientation: surgeon marks:', '["Medial margin clinical presentation","Posterior margin clinical presentation","Inferior margin clinical presentation","No markings clinical presentation","Superior margin (short suture) and lateral margin"]'::jsonb, 'Superior margin (short suture) and lateral margin', 'Breast: short suture = superior, long = lateral. Pathologist orients and sections for margins.'),
('c91d9a27-7148-503e-b5ee-e1bea3363b3e'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Shrinkage of surgical specimen in formalin:', '["Expands","Doubles in size","Tissue shrinks up to 10-30%","No shrinkage","No change"]'::jsonb, 'Tissue shrinks up to 10-30%', 'Formalin fixation: causes tissue shrinkage. Surgeon should account for this when assessing margins intraop.'),
('ee7bc26c-e10d-5e49-a7c5-c4ed89a8b445'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'A pathological complete response (pCR) means:', '["Stable disease clinical presentation clinical presentation","Minimal residual clinical presentation clinical presentation","No residual invasive tumor in resected specimen after neoadjuvant therapy","Progression clinical presentation clinical presentation","Partial response clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'No residual invasive tumor in resected specimen after neoadjuvant therapy', 'pCR: no invasive cancer in breast and lymph nodes (breast cancer). Predicts good long-term outcome.'),
('14544d5e-be2c-5d8b-8cd9-f6f94bb2a7a5'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Surgical pathology report essential elements:', '["Diagnosis only clinical presentation clinical presentation","Size only clinical presentation clinical presentation","Margin only clinical presentation clinical presentation","Grade only clinical presentation clinical presentation","All of these: gross description, diagnosis, margin status, stage, grade, biomarker status"]'::jsonb, 'All of these: gross description, diagnosis, margin status, stage, grade, biomarker status', 'Synoptic reporting: standardized format with all prognostic elements. CAP protocol.'),
('e699192f-964c-53ac-b9dc-f7fb06ee0e3f'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Synoptic reporting in pathology:', '["Incomplete data clinical presentation","No structure clinical presentation","Verbal report clinical presentation","Structured, standardized report with key data elements","Narrative paragraph clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Structured, standardized report with key data elements', 'Synoptic: ensures all essential elements included. Easier for clinicians to extract data. CAP templates.'),
('cfb41f09-a70a-5938-94d2-0cdb65dfa5d8'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Second opinion review of pathology:', '["Always wrong clinical presentation","Only for medicolegal clinical presentation","Re-review of slides by another pathologist","Not needed clinical presentation","Never recommended clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Re-review of slides by another pathologist', 'Second opinion: important for rare/complex tumors. May change treatment significantly.'),
('514fffa9-a194-5d7f-8345-2bab32d75030'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Tumor banking requires:', '["No storage clinical presentation clinical presentation","No consent clinical presentation clinical presentation","Without consent clinical presentation clinical presentation","Formalin fixed tissue only clinical presentation clinical presentation","Informed consent, institutional approval, fresh tissue, de-identification, proper storage"]'::jsonb, 'Informed consent, institutional approval, fresh tissue, de-identification, proper storage', 'Tumor bank: fresh tissue frozen at -80°C or liquid N2. Anonymized. Research use. Ethical approvals required.'),
('b684534d-4162-59aa-894d-b056f4df5394'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'ASA (American Society of Anesthesiologists) classification:', '["Cardiac risk clinical presentation clinical presentation","ASA 1: healthy; ASA 2: mild systemic disease; ASA 3: severe systemic disease","Age only clinical presentation clinical presentation","Surgical site clinical presentation clinical presentation","Blood loss clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'ASA 1: healthy; ASA 2: mild systemic disease; ASA 3: severe systemic disease', 'ASA: predicts perioperative mortality. ASA 1: <0.1%, ASA 4: >25% mortality.'),
('5ea1946e-24d1-5286-9456-db6f7197cb19'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Cardiac risk assessment: Revised Cardiac Risk Index (RCRI) includes:', '["Anesthesia type clinical presentation","Duration clinical presentation","All: high-risk surgery, CAD, CHF, stroke, DM on insulin, CKD","Age only clinical presentation","Gender only clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'All: high-risk surgery, CAD, CHF, stroke, DM on insulin, CKD', 'RCRI: 6 factors. 0 predictors: ~0.5% MACE. ≥3: >10% MACE. Guides need for cardiac testing.'),
('99625c59-25cc-5a8c-96bb-6cc7676d30a5'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'High-risk surgery per RCRI:', '["Intraperitoneal, intrathoracic, or suprainguinal vascular surgery","Eye surgery clinical presentation","All surgery clinical presentation","Dental surgery clinical presentation","Superficial surgery clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Intraperitoneal, intrathoracic, or suprainguinal vascular surgery', 'RCRI: these surgeries have >5% risk of cardiac complications. May need preoperative cardiac evaluation.'),
('e9016184-fea9-5f11-bda8-92490fecf6be'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'METS (metabolic equivalents) assessment: Unable to climb 2 flights:', '["Excellent clinical presentation clinical presentation","Poor functional capacity (<4 METS) - increased perioperative risk","Low risk clinical presentation clinical presentation","Good function clinical presentation clinical presentation","Normal clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Poor functional capacity (<4 METS) - increased perioperative risk', '<4 METS: cannot climb 2 flights or walk uphill. Increased risk of cardiac complications.'),
('9d97efb7-18dc-5ed9-a18e-d5c3402041d8'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Preoperative ECG is indicated for:', '["All patients clinical presentation clinical presentation","Only >80 years clinical presentation clinical presentation","No patients clinical presentation clinical presentation","Only cardiac patients clinical presentation clinical presentation","All patients with cardiac risk factors, age >65, or undergoing intermediate/high-risk surgery"]'::jsonb, 'All patients with cardiac risk factors, age >65, or undergoing intermediate/high-risk surgery', 'ECG: baseline for comparison. Screen for occult CAD, arrhythmias, LVH.'),
('bae2aee2-3de0-5e9d-98d7-9db7a86f9119'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Smoking cessation before elective surgery:', '["Not needed clinical presentation","At least 4-8 weeks before surgery","24 hours clinical presentation","After surgery clinical presentation","1 week clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'At least 4-8 weeks before surgery', 'Smoking: 4-8 weeks to reduce complications. Even 24-48h can reduce CO levels, improve O2 delivery.'),
('e4c55937-eda2-555c-b0c0-e69c02412581'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Preoperative nutritional support:', '["Short starvation good clinical presentation","7-14 days of EN/PN for severely malnourished","No benefit clinical presentation","PN only clinical presentation","No support needed clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, '7-14 days of EN/PN for severely malnourished', 'Preop nutrition: 7-14 days before major surgery if severely malnourished. Reduces complications.'),
('69becf29-ff33-5a96-8250-97eb1534a6aa'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Preoperative bowel preparation for colorectal surgery:', '["No prep needed clinical presentation clinical presentation","Antibiotics only clinical presentation clinical presentation","Mechanical bowel prep + oral antibiotics (neomycin + metronidazole) reduces SSI","Enema only clinical presentation clinical presentation","Mechanical prep only clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Mechanical bowel prep + oral antibiotics (neomycin + metronidazole) reduces SSI', 'Prep: mechanical (PEG/GoLYTELY) + oral antibiotics. Reduces surgical site infection. Not for all (left colon/rectal).'),
('29353dc0-bc5d-557c-a3fb-76c268fffb1f'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Preoperative fasting guidelines:', '["6h for solids, 2h for clear liquids","12h fasting for all clinical presentation","Only liquids 24h clinical presentation","NPO after midnight clinical presentation","No fasting clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, '6h for solids, 2h for clear liquids', 'Fasting: 6h solid food, 2h clear liquids. Clear CHO drinks 2h pre-op (ERAS).'),
('83839f89-d47b-5b5c-8520-edfce4945a30'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Wound classification for SSI risk:', '["All same clinical presentation","Clean (1-2%), Clean-contaminated (5-10%), Contaminated (10-20%), Dirty","Clean clinical presentation","No classification clinical presentation","Dirty clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Clean (1-2%), Clean-contaminated (5-10%), Contaminated (10-20%), Dirty', 'SSI risk correlates with wound class. Clean: <2%. Dirty: >30%. Prophylactic antibiotics only for clean if implant.'),
('8a6a1a01-a8e3-5f48-af8f-ee8509a66ceb'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'NNIS (National Nosocomial Infection Surveillance) risk index:', '["Wound class + ASA + duration (score 0-3) predicts SSI risk","Only age clinical presentation","Only ASA clinical presentation","Only duration clinical presentation","Only wound class clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Wound class + ASA + duration (score 0-3) predicts SSI risk', 'NNIS: 1 point each for contaminated/dirty, ASA 3+, surgery >75th percentile duration. Score 0: 1.5% SSI; Score 3: 13%.'),
('89d74e11-5558-5d06-92d7-ccca81b7eb9c'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Caprini risk score for VTE:', '["Mortality risk clinical presentation clinical presentation","Cardiac risk clinical presentation clinical presentation","Bleeding risk clinical presentation clinical presentation","Assesses risk of thromboembolism (score 0-10+) to guide prophylaxis intensity","SSI risk clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Assesses risk of thromboembolism (score 0-10+) to guide prophylaxis intensity', 'Caprini: factors like age, obesity, cancer, prior VTE, surgery type. Score ≥5: high risk -> LMWH + mechanical.'),
('2ca21b42-31fa-5f6f-a736-002bfdf9bcb4'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Preoperative skin preparation with chlorhexidine-alcohol:', '["Not used clinical presentation","Worse than iodine clinical presentation","Equal efficacy clinical presentation","Reduces SSI compared to povidone-iodine","No difference clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Reduces SSI compared to povidone-iodine', 'Chlorhexidine-alcohol: >povidone-iodine for SSI reduction. Also allow adequate drying time.'),
('133e0eee-2de7-59a0-b08d-bb93c2a93623'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Hair removal before surgery:', '["Depilatory cream clinical presentation clinical presentation","Wax clinical presentation clinical presentation","Do not shave (razor causes microabrasions, increases SSI). Use clipper if needed","Shave always clinical presentation clinical presentation","No removal clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Do not shave (razor causes microabrasions, increases SSI). Use clipper if needed', 'Hair removal: no removal ideal. If needed, use clippers immediately before surgery. No razors.'),
('ff44d886-5a3a-55e7-94a8-bb3751b5b617'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Normothermia in the OR (maintaining temperature >36°C):', '["Increases infection clinical presentation","Better to be cold clinical presentation","Increases bleeding clinical presentation","Reduces SSI, cardiac events, and blood loss","No effect clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Reduces SSI, cardiac events, and blood loss', 'Warming: forced-air warmers, warmed IV fluids. Maintain core temp >36°C throughout surgery.'),
('6a91be33-3d6e-54ef-8d50-cff51754f2ed'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Postoperative fever: most common cause in first 48h:', '["Atelectasis","DVT","Pneumonia","Wound infection","UTI"]'::jsonb, 'Atelectasis', 'Atelectasis: most common cause of early post-op fever (<48h). Treat with incentive spirometry, ambulation.'),
('d313d84d-665b-5583-b542-00c8be8dbaa9'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Fever on POD 2-3 is commonly due to:', '["DVT clinical presentation","Wound infection (SSI) or IV line infection","UTI clinical presentation","Pneumonia clinical presentation","Drug fever clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Wound infection (SSI) or IV line infection', 'POD 2-3: wound infection most likely (erythema, purulence, wound tenderness). Also IV phlebitis.'),
('f0a80151-4d9a-5253-a4e3-1aebd00daaee'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Fever on POD 4-7:', '["Wound infection early clinical presentation","Atelectasis clinical presentation","UTI (especially if catheterized), pneumonia, or IV line infection","DVT clinical presentation","Drug fever clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'UTI (especially if catheterized), pneumonia, or IV line infection', 'POD 4-7: UTI (catheter), pneumonia (aspiration), central line infection.'),
('09035536-b8ec-511d-99ce-4e76bf23f65b'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Fever on POD 7-10:', '["Wound infection","UTI","Viral infection","Atelectasis","DVT/pulmonary embolism"]'::jsonb, 'DVT/pulmonary embolism', 'DVT: pain, swelling, Homan sign (unreliable). Duplex ultrasound. Treat with anticoagulation.'),
('d1bfb099-3cfb-598c-8d71-d17d9b5c12c1'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Fever >2 weeks post-op:', '["Atelectasis clinical presentation","Drug fever clinical presentation","Intra-abdominal abscess, anastomotic leak, or infected prosthesis","Viral infection clinical presentation","Simple UTI clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Intra-abdominal abscess, anastomotic leak, or infected prosthesis', 'Late fever: abscess (subphrenic, pelvic, psoas), leak, or implant infection. CT scan for diagnosis.'),
('9f6ebb7c-31ac-5e8a-9632-1598aa3ebe8f'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Drug fever: diagnosis of exclusion:', '["High WBC clinical presentation clinical presentation","Hypoxia clinical presentation clinical presentation","Bradycardia clinical presentation clinical presentation","Hypotension clinical presentation clinical presentation","Fever with no infectious cause, eosinophilia, resolves after stopping drug"]'::jsonb, 'Fever with no infectious cause, eosinophilia, resolves after stopping drug', 'Drug fever: antibiotics (beta-lactams), anesthetics, blood products. Stop offending agent.'),
('e108c1f1-72a0-56e5-a5fa-5a7f1ae08cdf'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Postoperative pneumonia risk factors:', '["Short surgery clinical presentation clinical presentation","All of these: smoking, COPD, aspiration, prolonged surgery, NG tube, immunosuppression","Nonsmoker clinical presentation clinical presentation","No NG tube clinical presentation clinical presentation","Young age clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'All of these: smoking, COPD, aspiration, prolonged surgery, NG tube, immunosuppression', 'Prevention: incentive spirometry, early ambulation, oral care, elevation of head of bed.'),
('4064f008-5041-5cfe-9a11-660c9590151a'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Postoperative urinary retention:', '["Normal","Self-limited","Inability to void after surgery","No treatment needed","Always needs catheter"]'::jsonb, 'Inability to void after surgery', 'Urinary retention: incidence 5-40%. Risk factors: age >50, BPH, pelvic surgery, spinal anesthesia.'),
('18ebd07b-120b-5ba8-8afd-d139eaed3541'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Paralytic ileus after abdominal surgery:', '["Always obstruction clinical presentation","Requires laparotomy clinical presentation","Surgical emergency clinical presentation","Never resolves clinical presentation","Transient bowel motility dysfunction"]'::jsonb, 'Transient bowel motility dysfunction', 'Ileus: absent bowel sounds, distention. Management: NPO, NG tube if vomiting, electrolyte correction.'),
('eab10fae-e540-5178-8990-b6d3b5b944ee'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Early postoperative small bowel obstruction vs ileus:', '["Both need surgery clinical presentation clinical presentation","Same thing clinical presentation clinical presentation","Ileus: colicky pain clinical presentation clinical presentation","Obstruction: colicky pain, high-pitched BS, sometimes with history of adhesion; ileus: diffuse","Obstruction: no pain clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Obstruction: colicky pain, high-pitched BS, sometimes with history of adhesion; ileus: diffuse', 'Obstruction: mechanical. CT usually diagnostic. Ileus: non-mechanical. Differentiate clinically.'),
('2c9348a5-a1e9-5340-89ee-79b254ba1639'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Postoperative hemorrhage: early (<24h) vs late (>24h):', '["Early: infection clinical presentation","Both same clinical presentation","No distinction clinical presentation","Late: poor hemostasis clinical presentation","Early: inadequate hemostasis; Late: vessel erosion, infection, coagulopathy"]'::jsonb, 'Early: inadequate hemostasis; Late: vessel erosion, infection, coagulopathy', 'Early: technical (slipped ligature). Late: leakage from vessel due to infection, anastomotic erosion.'),
('26253992-1898-5598-9dfe-189584279ca2'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Postoperative wound hematoma presents with:', '["Purulent drainage clinical presentation","No symptoms clinical presentation","Fever clinical presentation","Swelling, pain, bruising, firm mass","Bleeding from drain clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Swelling, pain, bruising, firm mass', 'Hematoma: if large/expanding, return to OR for evacuation. May become infected. Also compresses local vessels.'),
('bebcf7ba-3100-58ed-9a1e-075f520ae1fe'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Postoperative pancreatitis (after upper abdominal surgery):', '["No enzymes","Hypocalcemia","Normal","Elevated serum amylase/lipase","Cholangitis"]'::jsonb, 'Elevated serum amylase/lipase', 'Post-op pancreatitis: after gastrectomy, splenectomy, ERCP. Usually mild, self-limited.'),
('26f3ee3f-cece-5524-a067-abe4015044f2'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Postoperative jaundice causes:', '["Only hemolysis clinical presentation clinical presentation","All of these: hemolysis (transfusion), bilirubin overload, hepatitis, cholestasis, drug-induced","Only hepatitis clinical presentation clinical presentation","Only drugs clinical presentation clinical presentation","Only obstruction clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'All of these: hemolysis (transfusion), bilirubin overload, hepatitis, cholestasis, drug-induced', 'Post-op jaundice: multifactorial. Check direct/indirect bilirubin, LFTs, ultrasound if obstructive.'),
('023ac418-aad7-5252-80ff-566575181b33'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Postoperative delirium is most common in:', '["Elderly patients (>65), with pre-existing cognitive impairment, after major surgery","No risk factors clinical presentation clinical presentation","Children clinical presentation clinical presentation","Young adults clinical presentation clinical presentation","Middle-aged clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Elderly patients (>65), with pre-existing cognitive impairment, after major surgery', 'Delirium: acute confusion. Assess: infection, electrolytes, hypoxia, drugs, pain, sleep deprivation.'),
('96416b29-5ba7-57b8-82ec-be5cd3f3e856'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Indirect inguinal hernia passes:', '["Through deep inguinal ring","Directly through posterior wall","Medial to epigastric vessels","Through femoral canal","Obturator foramen"]'::jsonb, 'Through deep inguinal ring', 'Indirect: congenital (patent processus vaginalis). Can descend into scrotum. Higher risk of strangulation.'),
('71059e0e-136f-59db-9e8d-6713f462de30'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Direct inguinal hernia passes:', '["Into scrotum","Through Hesselbach triangle","Through deep ring","Femoral canal","Umbilicus"]'::jsonb, 'Through Hesselbach triangle', 'Direct: acquired (posterior wall weakness). Less likely to strangulate than indirect. Usually in elderly.'),
('f69ff5fb-b485-5d7a-858f-c0dba5f027b7'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Femoral hernia is located:', '["Above inguinal ligament clinical presentation","Through umbilical ring clinical presentation","Below inguinal ligament, medial to femoral vein","Obturator clinical presentation","Through deep inguinal ring clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Below inguinal ligament, medial to femoral vein', 'Femoral hernia: more common in women (but inguinal hernia still more common overall). High strangulation risk -> surgery.'),
('3831105e-e6d5-5ac6-8a52-83291d4009b2'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Incarcerated hernia means:', '["Traumatic","Irreducible hernia","Obstructed","Strangulated","Infected"]'::jsonb, 'Irreducible hernia', 'Incarceration: hernia stuck. Strangulation: blood supply compromised. Emergency surgery if strangulated.'),
('59ffbd4e-4e61-592f-9fe1-a74b4b512bce'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Strangulated hernia has:', '["Ischemia/necrosis of contents","Reducible","Irreducible only","No pain","No obstruction"]'::jsonb, 'Ischemia/necrosis of contents', 'Strangulation: pain, tenderness, obstruction, signs of peritonitis. Immediate exploration and resection if necrotic.'),
('2881e42b-256d-5236-9e74-c4e17ce50260'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Richter hernia involves:', '["Only part of bowel circumference incarcerated","Entire bowel circumference","Omentum only","Bladder only","Full thickness"]'::jsonb, 'Only part of bowel circumference incarcerated', 'Richter: partial enterocele. May not cause obstruction but risk of necrosis. Commonly femoral hernia.'),
('cd3e0d69-65d0-5893-982d-c48659174cd7'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Littre hernia contains:', '["Bowel","Appendix","Omentum","Fat","Meckel diverticulum"]'::jsonb, 'Meckel diverticulum', 'Littre: Meckel''s diverticulum in hernia sac. Rare.'),
('25237b86-6ca1-52dd-9534-dc2b2c93ddeb'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Maydl hernia (hernia-in-W):', '["Omentum only clinical presentation clinical presentation","Two loops of bowel in sac with intra-abdominal connecting segment","Cecum clinical presentation clinical presentation","One loop clinical presentation clinical presentation","Bladder clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Two loops of bowel in sac with intra-abdominal connecting segment', 'Maydl: strangulated intermediate segment (inside abdomen) may be missed. High mortality.'),
('0eb5aae2-acd7-592a-b653-82d9b26e1409'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Amyand hernia contains:', '["Omentum","Ovary","Meckel diverticulum","Bladder","Appendix in inguinal hernia sac"]'::jsonb, 'Appendix in inguinal hernia sac', 'Amyand: appendix in hernia. If inflamed -> appendicitis within hernia sac.'),
('013aa690-6d40-524f-b52b-45619232fe5b'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Spigelian hernia occurs:', '["At semilunar line","At umbilicus","In groin","In lumbar region","At obturator foramen"]'::jsonb, 'At semilunar line', 'Spigelian: through transversus abdominis aponeurosis at semilunar line. Often interparietal. US/CT to diagnose.'),
('cee1981e-7b9a-50b5-bc14-dc40e0d7f6a6'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Umbilical hernia in adults:', '["Congenital","Only in children","Rare","Acquired","Always closes spontaneously"]'::jsonb, 'Acquired', 'Adult umbilical: acquired through umbilical scar. Higher strangulation risk than pediatric. Repair indicated.'),
('20a66ed4-aa7d-5a4c-ae87-e8c5bd82eb35'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Incisional hernia occurs at:', '["Previous surgical incision site","Only subcostal","Only inguinal","Only laparoscopic port sites","Any site"]'::jsonb, 'Previous surgical incision site', 'Incisional: after abdominal surgery. Risk: infection, obesity, malnutrition, COPD, steroids. Repair with mesh.'),
('e464cf6a-c81a-5e99-9b59-e0e10a94a7a6'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Laparoscopic hernia repair vs open:', '["Open always better clinical presentation","Slower recovery clinical presentation","No difference clinical presentation","More pain clinical presentation","Less pain, faster recovery, but higher recurrence"]'::jsonb, 'Less pain, faster recovery, but higher recurrence', 'Laparoscopic (TEP/TAPP): advantages in bilateral/recurrent. Mesh tension-free repair is gold standard.'),
('23c27a84-130b-5bf7-9d2c-e3199bddfbda'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Bassini repair (open inguinal hernia):', '["Laparoscopic repair","Primary tissue repair","Shouldice repair","McVay repair","Mesh repair"]'::jsonb, 'Primary tissue repair', 'Bassini: first modern repair. High recurrence without mesh. Lichtenstein (tension-free mesh) is now standard.'),
('9f3dcd41-2e2b-5128-b35a-854930dda238'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Lichtenstein tension-free hernia repair:', '["Tissue repair clinical presentation","Laparoscopic clinical presentation","Plug clinical presentation","No mesh clinical presentation","Mesh placed on posterior wall of inguinal canal"]'::jsonb, 'Mesh placed on posterior wall of inguinal canal', 'Lichtenstein: mesh over defect. Low recurrence (<1%). Can be under local anesthesia. Gold standard for primary hernia.'),
('fdfd7a47-80da-5fab-bfa2-1cbf9cf2f029'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Fistula is:', '["Abnormal communication between two epithelial-lined surfaces","Blind track from surface","Abscess cavity","Track connecting abscess to surface","Necrotic track"]'::jsonb, 'Abnormal communication between two epithelial-lined surfaces', 'Fistula: e.g., enterocutaneous, rectovaginal, tracheoesophageal. Epithelialized track.'),
('51c4b75e-8217-5aa0-93ed-94b0f0ab6935'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Sinus is:', '["Abscess clinical presentation","Ulcer clinical presentation","Blind-ending track from an epithelial surface into deeper tissue","Same as fistula clinical presentation","Two-way connection clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Blind-ending track from an epithelial surface into deeper tissue', 'Sinus: one open end. Example: pilonidal sinus. May be congenital or acquired.'),
('a5fb2e85-2a0e-552e-9202-a14c0d6d111c'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Enterocutaneous fistula management:', '["No nutrition clinical presentation clinical presentation","Immediate surgery clinical presentation clinical presentation","Resuscitation, control sepsis, nutrition (TPN/EN), skin protection, definitive surgery","Antibiotics only clinical presentation clinical presentation","Observation clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Resuscitation, control sepsis, nutrition (TPN/EN), skin protection, definitive surgery', 'ECF: high output (>500 mL/day) -> fluid/electrolyte loss. Spontaneous closure possible if low output. Surgery if no closure.'),
('03aebd7c-9dd1-55ca-9234-e04e08ef13e6'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Fistula-in-ano: Goodsall rule:', '["No rule clinical presentation","All tracks straight clinical presentation","If external opening anterior to transverse anal line -> internal opening is radial (direct track","Internal opening always midline clinical presentation","External opening always posterior clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'If external opening anterior to transverse anal line -> internal opening is radial (direct track', 'Goodsall rule: anterior EOs -> straight to dentate line. Posterior EOs -> track curves to posterior midline.'),
('3e9fe521-6d87-5d8a-81d1-253fd0907a36'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Types of fistula-in-ano: intersphincteric (most common):', '["Through external sphincter","Above levator ani","Horseshoe","Track between internal and external sphincter","Subcutaneous"]'::jsonb, 'Track between internal and external sphincter', 'Park classification: intersphincteric (70%), transsphincteric (25%), suprasphincteric, extrasphincteric.'),
('ef4518ca-31ae-59a7-ae62-1a8a6e41f0c3'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Pilonidal sinus is located:', '["Groin clinical presentation clinical presentation","Umbilicus clinical presentation clinical presentation","Perineum clinical presentation clinical presentation","Axilla clinical presentation clinical presentation","In sacrococcygeal region (natal cleft), containing hair shafts"]'::jsonb, 'In sacrococcygeal region (natal cleft), containing hair shafts', 'Pilonidal: acquired (hair insertion). Young, hairy males. Treat: excision with/without closure.'),
('04881ab0-5209-5763-9c3e-db5f74a73442'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Treatment of pilonidal sinus:', '["No treatment clinical presentation","Antibiotics only clinical presentation","Laser only clinical presentation","Excision and either open healing or flap closure","I&D only clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Excision and either open healing or flap closure', 'Pilonidal: open healing (secondary intention) has lower recurrence but longer healing. Flap (Limberg) for large/recurrent.'),
('df2bb527-54e8-55c6-9008-f2cb47510133'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Lymphedema after groin dissection:', '["Lipedema clinical presentation","Renal edema clinical presentation","Chronic swelling of leg due to disrupted lymphatic channels","Cardiac edema clinical presentation","Venous edema clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Chronic swelling of leg due to disrupted lymphatic channels', 'Lymphedema: protein-rich fluid. Non-pitting initially, later fibrotic. Treat: compression, physiotherapy, lymphatic bypass.'),
('c0b0ea58-5529-576f-a57f-9610ec52f7aa'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Lymphocele after pelvic surgery:', '["Lymphatic fluid collection","Hematoma","Cyst","Seroma","Abscess"]'::jsonb, 'Lymphatic fluid collection', 'Lymphocele: after lymphadenectomy (pelvic, axillary). May need percutaneous drainage if symptomatic or infected.'),
('082a86e3-48aa-5aca-890e-e6b350ef168e'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Seroma after mastectomy:', '["Infected always clinical presentation","Hematoma clinical presentation","Requires surgery clinical presentation","Serous fluid collection under flap","Abscess clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Serous fluid collection under flap', 'Seroma: common after mastectomy + ALND. Aspirate if symptomatic. Usually self-limited.'),
('51495ae3-4961-50de-b288-efce8a57be95'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Cyst vs abscess: Cyst has:', '["No wall clinical presentation","Granulation tissue clinical presentation","Epithelial lining (secretory), sterile fluid","Infection clinical presentation","Pus, no lining clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Epithelial lining (secretory), sterile fluid', 'Cyst: lined epithelium. Examples: sebaceous, dermoid, epidermal inclusion. Abscess: pus cavity, no epithelial lining.'),
('fcfbf687-522e-597d-9317-94037ba4801f'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Epidermal inclusion cyst is:', '["Sebaceous cyst clinical presentation clinical presentation","Pilar cyst clinical presentation clinical presentation","Ganglion cyst clinical presentation clinical presentation","Dermoid cyst clinical presentation clinical presentation","Benign cyst lined by stratified squamous epithelium, filled with keratin"]'::jsonb, 'Benign cyst lined by stratified squamous epithelium, filled with keratin', 'Epidermal: common on face, neck, trunk. Rupture causes inflammation. Excision.'),
('f147531d-8e3b-5c47-ac7a-dd017acc27e5'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Pilar (trichilemmal) cyst:', '["Feet clinical presentation clinical presentation","Extremities clinical presentation clinical presentation","Trunk clinical presentation clinical presentation","Face clinical presentation clinical presentation","On scalp, lined by outer root sheath, no granular layer, firm"]'::jsonb, 'On scalp, lined by outer root sheath, no granular layer, firm', 'Pilar: scalp, autosomal dominant inheritance. No granular layer on histology. Less inflamed than epidermal.'),
('2b86c02b-d48d-561e-9114-8f394c69e0b2'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Ganglion cyst arises from:', '["Nerve sheath clinical presentation","Ganglion cells clinical presentation","Artery clinical presentation","Bone clinical presentation","Mucinous degeneration of joint capsule or tendon sheath"]'::jsonb, 'Mucinous degeneration of joint capsule or tendon sheath', 'Ganglion: clear, gelatinous fluid. Not a true cyst (no epithelial lining). May resolve spontaneously. Aspiration or excision.'),
('e664be11-bc3f-5c98-a2ee-dc7f79b55308'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Baker cyst (popliteal cyst):', '["Anterior knee clinical presentation","Fluid-filled bursa in popliteal fossa","Elbow clinical presentation","Hip clinical presentation","Ankle clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Fluid-filled bursa in popliteal fossa', 'Baker''s: usually secondary to knee osteoarthritis or meniscal tear. May rupture -> calf pseudothrombophlebitis.'),
('b04d8adf-2302-54a0-b2e8-03c6d944b20e'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Melanotic myelopathy (malignant melanoma): sentinel node biopsy:', '["No biopsy clinical presentation","First draining lymph node; if negative, avoids complete lymphadenectomy","Biopsy of all nodes clinical presentation","Complete node dissection clinical presentation","FNA clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'First draining lymph node; if negative, avoids complete lymphadenectomy', 'Sentinel node biopsy: standard for melanoma >1 mm Breslow thickness. Blue dye + radioisotope.'),
('6e28b7d3-297f-5dc2-be3f-8e1231a8909d'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Duke classification revision for colorectal cancer:', '["Only lymph nodes clinical presentation clinical presentation","Dukes A (T1-T2, N0), Dukes B (T3-T4, N0, no obstruction/perf = B1, with = B2), Dukes C","No classification clinical presentation clinical presentation","TNM only clinical presentation clinical presentation","Only tumor size clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Dukes A (T1-T2, N0), Dukes B (T3-T4, N0, no obstruction/perf = B1, with = B2), Dukes C', 'Dukes A: good prognosis (90% 5-yr). Dukes C: worse (40-60% with chemo). Now AJCC/TNM is standard.'),
('f5cd055b-c39f-56a9-b507-34d025aad588'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Law of the gut (peristalsis after surgery):', '["Small bowel recovers first (hours), then stomach (24-48h), then colon","Colon first clinical presentation clinical presentation","No order clinical presentation clinical presentation","All at once clinical presentation clinical presentation","Stomach first clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Small bowel recovers first (hours), then stomach (24-48h), then colon', 'Post-op GI recovery: small bowel (6-12h), stomach (12-24h), colon (48-72h). Ileus management accordingly.'),
('3bc89431-507a-598d-b976-ccb06ccb6734'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Nasogastric tube decompression after abdominal surgery:', '["Not routine (selective use) - early feeding is beneficial","Never needed clinical presentation","Only for vomiting clinical presentation","Always needed clinical presentation","For 7 days minimum clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Not routine (selective use) - early feeding is beneficial', 'Routine NG decompression: not recommended. Increases pneumonia, delays recovery. Selective if distention/vomiting.'),
('cce52f5f-ac92-5059-9b14-df0bbf5fd43d'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Postoperative nausea and vomiting (PONV) prophylaxis:', '["Single agent always clinical presentation clinical presentation","No prophylaxis clinical presentation clinical presentation","Only metoclopramide clinical presentation clinical presentation","Multimodal (5-HT3 antagonist + dexamethasone + droperidol) for high-risk patients","Only ondansetron clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Multimodal (5-HT3 antagonist + dexamethasone + droperidol) for high-risk patients', 'PONV: female, non-smoker, hx PONV/motion sickness, opioids. Risk score. Multimodal prevention.'),
('cb24cdde-41ed-55e5-88ba-aa198d78888e'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Abdominal compartment syndrome:', '["Observation clinical presentation","Normal after surgery clinical presentation","Self-limited clinical presentation","No treatment clinical presentation","Intra-abdominal pressure >20 mmHg with organ dysfunction"]'::jsonb, 'Intra-abdominal pressure >20 mmHg with organ dysfunction', 'ACS: tense abdomen, decreased urine output, respiratory compromise. Measure bladder pressure (IAP). Decompressive laparotomy.'),
('1b78cc08-16be-5b3b-92ae-3082e4a50421'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Compartment syndrome of limb (Volkmann ischemia):', '["Superficial swelling clinical presentation clinical presentation","Neuropathy clinical presentation clinical presentation","Increased pressure in closed osteofascial compartment -> muscle/nerve ischemia","Venous insufficiency clinical presentation clinical presentation","Arterial occlusion clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Increased pressure in closed osteofascial compartment -> muscle/nerve ischemia', 'Compartment syndrome: pain out of proportion, paresthesias, pulselessness (late). Emergency fasciotomy.'),
('695bbdaa-e05f-5bf7-b326-d76c24cf0ce2'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Volkmann ischemic contracture:', '["Extension contracture clinical presentation clinical presentation","Normal outcome clinical presentation clinical presentation","Untreated forearm compartment syndrome -> muscle necrosis -> flexion contracture","Recovers fully clinical presentation clinical presentation","Painless clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Untreated forearm compartment syndrome -> muscle necrosis -> flexion contracture', 'Volkmann: after supracondylar fracture (most common). Claw hand deformity. Prevent by early fasciotomy.'),
('af2c9879-b020-5b4d-b4b3-20a0d08123d7'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Gas gangrene vs necrotizing fasciitis diagnosis:', '["X-ray only clinical presentation clinical presentation","Ultrasound clinical presentation clinical presentation","CT shows gas in tissue. Surgical exploration reveals fascial necrosis. Gram stain, culture","Clinical diagnosis only clinical presentation clinical presentation","MRI clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'CT shows gas in tissue. Surgical exploration reveals fascial necrosis. Gram stain, culture', 'Differentiation: NF has fascial involvement without myonecrosis initially. Gas gangrene: muscle necrosis, severe toxemia.'),
('4eabff95-0f4e-55c9-a37c-e0867fabffbf'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Hyperbaric oxygen therapy (HBOT) for gas gangrene:', '["Cure without surgery clinical presentation clinical presentation","Increases tissue O2, inhibits Clostridial exotoxin production, improves leukocyte function","Not effective clinical presentation clinical presentation","First-line treatment clinical presentation clinical presentation","Conservative management clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Increases tissue O2, inhibits Clostridial exotoxin production, improves leukocyte function', 'HBOT: adjunct to surgery + antibiotics. Not replacement for debridement.'),
('d4cd896f-1d5f-5581-bf7b-59bda3910ff8'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Rhabdomyolysis after crush injury:', '["Always self-limited clinical presentation","No treatment needed clinical presentation","No renal risk clinical presentation","Muscle breakdown -> myoglobinuria -> acute kidney injury","Only hydration clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Muscle breakdown -> myoglobinuria -> acute kidney injury', 'Rhabdomyolysis: CK >5000, dark urine. Tx: aggressive IV fluids, bicarbonate, mannitol. Dialysis if needed.'),
('695d4e25-207b-5f80-a0ff-aa9be942a26b'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Intestinal anastomotic leak presents with:', '["Just fever clinical presentation","No symptoms clinical presentation","Bradycardia clinical presentation","Mild discomfort clinical presentation","Abdominal pain, fever, peritonitis, leukocytosis, tachycardia"]'::jsonb, 'Abdominal pain, fever, peritonitis, leukocytosis, tachycardia', 'Leak: most common after colorectal (LAR). CT with oral contrast. Treatment: antibiotics, drainage, or reoperation.'),
('602b2612-2027-5257-9c80-b84bb380e08d'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Anastomotic bleeding (early, within 24-48h):', '["No treatment","Transfusion only","Self-limited always","Observation","From staple line/vessel"]'::jsonb, 'From staple line/vessel', 'Anastomotic bleed: after GI surgery. Usually self-limited. If persistent, endoscopic cautery/clip or reoperation.'),
('2aab7065-e1b7-56f1-835e-9c0175553afc'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Anastomotic stricture (late complication):', '["Early complication","Never resolves","Always needs reoperation","Malignant","Fibrotic narrowing at anastomosis"]'::jsonb, 'Fibrotic narrowing at anastomosis', 'Stricture: treat with endoscopic dilation. Recurrent cases may need surgical revision.'),
('dc68017d-b17b-5d42-8702-ac12a20e3311'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Surgical Pathology', 'Principles of surgical safety checklist (WHO):', '["Only time-out clinical presentation clinical presentation","Sign-in (before anesthesia), Time-out (before incision), Sign-out","Only sign-out clinical presentation clinical presentation","No checklist clinical presentation clinical presentation","Only sign-in clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Sign-in (before anesthesia), Time-out (before incision), Sign-out', 'WHO checklist: reduces mortality and complications by 30-50%. Team communication, verification of patient/procedure/site.'),
('aa0107e5-4768-5363-985d-54bc4066396e'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'The single most important measure to prevent healthcare-associated infections is:', '["Environmental cleaning","Hand hygiene","Antibiotic prophylaxis","Wearing gloves","Isolation precautions"]'::jsonb, 'Hand hygiene', 'Hand hygiene: most effective. WHO 5 Moments. Alcohol hand rub for routine use.'),
('327a2391-ba61-50ad-8045-9ffab45e37fb'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'WHO ''My 5 Moments for Hand Hygiene'' includes:', '["After leaving hospital clinical presentation clinical presentation","Before medication clinical presentation clinical presentation","Before entering hospital clinical presentation clinical presentation","Before patient, before procedure, after body fluid exposure, after patient, after environment","After eating clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Before patient, before procedure, after body fluid exposure, after patient, after environment', '5 Moments: before touching patient, before clean/aseptic procedure, after body fluid exposure risk, after touching patient, after touching patient surroundings.'),
('c542725f-5fa4-573d-8f2e-614ea420baab'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Alcohol-based hand rub is preferred over soap and water unless:', '["Always use soap clinical presentation","Never use alcohol clinical presentation","For all situations clinical presentation","Only in ICU clinical presentation","Hands visibly soiled, or suspected C. difficile/norovirus spores"]'::jsonb, 'Hands visibly soiled, or suspected C. difficile/norovirus spores', 'Alcohol: more effective, faster. But not sporicidal (C. diff, norovirus -> wash).'),
('1efc9dde-f923-5882-be94-2ea3fa01f2fb'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Duration of hand washing with soap and water (WHO):', '["10 seconds","40-60 seconds","5 minutes","5 seconds","2 minutes"]'::jsonb, '40-60 seconds', 'WHO: rub hands with soap for 40-60 sec. For alcohol rub: 20-30 sec until dry.'),
('c7faf019-46fa-58ea-b572-283c10b34f8c'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Surgical hand scrub with alcohol-based product:', '["No scrub clinical presentation","10 minutes clinical presentation","30 seconds clinical presentation","1 minute clinical presentation","3-5 minutes (first scrub of day), 2-3 min"]'::jsonb, '3-5 minutes (first scrub of day), 2-3 min', 'Surgical scrub: removes transient flora, reduces resident flora. Must dry before gloving.'),
('2f057ca5-ecb5-5652-8fc1-e8016c338a63'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Standard precautions apply to:', '["Only infected patients","Only surgical patients","All patients regardless of diagnosis","Only ICU patients","Only known HIV/HBV patients"]'::jsonb, 'All patients regardless of diagnosis', 'Standard precautions: baseline for all patient care. Includes hand hygiene, PPE, safe injection.'),
('22334b92-e399-53df-9882-5e134e6f89b8'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Transmission-based precautions are for:', '["Only respiratory patients clinical presentation","All patients clinical presentation","Only surgical wounds clinical presentation","Only immunocompromised clinical presentation","Patients with known or suspected transmissible pathogens"]'::jsonb, 'Patients with known or suspected transmissible pathogens', 'Transmission-based: contact, droplet, airborne categories. Additional to standard precautions.'),
('d187a0be-c0ae-5b67-b7fd-ac5f07c562ec'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Contact precautions are indicated for:', '["Measles clinical presentation","Influenza clinical presentation","TB clinical presentation","Meningococcal meningitis clinical presentation","MRSA, VRE, C. difficile, wound infections, scabies, norovirus"]'::jsonb, 'MRSA, VRE, C. difficile, wound infections, scabies, norovirus', 'Contact: private room, gloves, gown. Dedicated equipment.'),
('50540166-f3c4-583b-a459-b814616f0a01'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Droplet precautions are indicated for:', '["Varicella clinical presentation clinical presentation","Influenza, meningococcus, pertussis, mumps, rubella, adenovirus","Measles clinical presentation clinical presentation","MRSA clinical presentation clinical presentation","TB clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Influenza, meningococcus, pertussis, mumps, rubella, adenovirus', 'Droplet: surgical mask within 6 feet. Private room or cohort.'),
('ae55078d-98bf-5216-aef9-d8a09b54182d'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Airborne precautions are indicated for:', '["VRE clinical presentation","Influenza clinical presentation","C. difficile clinical presentation","TB (pulmonary), measles, varicella, disseminated zoster","MRSA clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'TB (pulmonary), measles, varicella, disseminated zoster', 'Airborne: negative pressure room (>=12 ACH), N95/PAPR.'),
('43588bda-91e5-562b-a47d-1d50bb69c839'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'N95 respirator must be:', '["Fit-tested","For all patients","Same as surgical mask","Worn loosely","Reused indefinitely"]'::jsonb, 'Fit-tested', 'N95: filters >=95% of airborne particles. Fit test annually.'),
('e3d63dba-09e3-5532-9938-69a138e3e56f'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'PPE removal order (doffing):', '["Gown first clinical presentation","Mask first clinical presentation","Gloves -> goggles/face shield -> gown -> mask","Goggles first clinical presentation","Any order clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Gloves -> goggles/face shield -> gown -> mask', 'Doffing: gloves (most contaminated) first, then goggles, gown, mask. Hand hygiene after each.'),
('2969f880-9868-54a6-8bbb-3139f685326b'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Safe injection practices include:', '["Not needed clinical presentation","Multi-dose vials for all clinical presentation","Sterile needle/syringe for each injection, single-dose vials preferred","Reuse needles for same patient clinical presentation","No hand hygiene clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Sterile needle/syringe for each injection, single-dose vials preferred', 'Unsafe injection: HCV transmission major risk. Never administer from same syringe to multiple.'),
('5435ad02-8bd5-5d9d-8e11-cece24eea047'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Respiratory hygiene/cough etiquette:', '["Only for known TB clinical presentation clinical presentation","No precautions clinical presentation clinical presentation","Only children clinical presentation clinical presentation","Cover cough with tissue, mask if coughing, hand hygiene, spatial separation","Only in winter clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Cover cough with tissue, mask if coughing, hand hygiene, spatial separation', 'Respiratory hygiene: at first point of contact. Masks for coughing patients.'),
('154578db-563b-5120-b5d6-639317165516'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Transmission of HBV in healthcare settings:', '["Percutaneous (needlestick) most common - HBV survives on surfaces for 7 days","Airborne clinical presentation clinical presentation","Fecal-oral clinical presentation clinical presentation","Vector-borne clinical presentation clinical presentation","Droplet clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Percutaneous (needlestick) most common - HBV survives on surfaces for 7 days', 'HBV: most infectious bloodborne pathogen. 30% risk after needlestick. Vaccine available.'),
('d7b28136-664e-53e4-a6e3-8a317de0de2d'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Sterilization means:', '["Elimination of pathogens only clinical presentation","Cleaning only clinical presentation","Complete elimination of all microorganisms including bacterial spores","Disinfection clinical presentation","Reduction of microorganisms clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Complete elimination of all microorganisms including bacterial spores', 'Sterilization: destroys all microbial life. Methods: steam, ETO, gamma radiation.'),
('82d3a286-2963-57cd-8837-233bb04d6a96'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Disinfection means:', '["No effect on viruses clinical presentation","Elimination of most pathogenic microorganisms","Only for skin clinical presentation","Complete sterilization clinical presentation","Cleaning only clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Elimination of most pathogenic microorganisms', 'Disinfection: high-level (kills some spores), intermediate, low-level.'),
('c7aa28e3-6c59-52fe-a1c9-9f19887c5d46'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Cleaning is:', '["Physical removal of organic debris","Same as sterilization","Not needed","Same as disinfection","Optional"]'::jsonb, 'Physical removal of organic debris', 'Cleaning: reduces bioburden by 99%. Must precede all disinfection/sterilization.'),
('0a1f73f4-54c1-59df-a2dc-428cb2f11238'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Spaulding classification: Critical items:', '["Do not need sterilization","Contact mucous membranes","Contact intact skin","Items entering sterile tissue/vascular system","Cleaning only"]'::jsonb, 'Items entering sterile tissue/vascular system', 'Critical: high risk. Require sterilization. Examples: scalpel, forceps, implant, needle.'),
('d3a024ca-c27a-59b8-8287-2deeb855cd9a'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Semi-critical items (Spaulding):', '["Must be sterilized clinical presentation","Low-level disinfection clinical presentation","Items contacting mucous membranes/non-intact skin","No treatment clinical presentation","Cleaning only clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Items contacting mucous membranes/non-intact skin', 'Semi-critical: require high-level disinfection. Examples: endoscopes.'),
('f091662e-27d3-5270-9c9c-dd70ded910a4'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Non-critical items (Spaulding):', '["Needle","Items in contact with intact skin","No cleaning","Must be sterilized","High-level disinfection"]'::jsonb, 'Items in contact with intact skin', 'Non-critical: lowest risk. Clean with detergent or low-level disinfectant. Examples: BP cuff.'),
('df4ec88b-4cc6-5f71-8e6c-fef7f166849f'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Steam sterilization (autoclave) parameters:', '["150 C for 1 min","200 C for 30 sec","121 C at 15 psi for 15-30 min","100 C for 10 min","90 C for 60 min"]'::jsonb, '121 C at 15 psi for 15-30 min', 'Autoclave: most reliable, non-toxic. Biological indicator: G. stearothermophilus.'),
('88461596-ff17-5021-a62d-331d69e33e27'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Dry heat sterilization requires:', '["100 C for 10 min","200 C for 5 min","170 C for 1 hour","150 C for 30 min","121 C for 15 min"]'::jsonb, '170 C for 1 hour', 'Dry heat: for items that tolerate high heat but not moisture. Longer than steam.'),
('1e51326f-007e-5e64-b863-ddca91deb342'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Ethylene oxide (ETO) sterilization:', '["Gas sterilant for heat/moisture-sensitive items","Rapid method clinical presentation","No aeration needed clinical presentation","Same as autoclave clinical presentation","For metal only clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Gas sterilant for heat/moisture-sensitive items', 'ETO: alkylates DNA. Toxic, carcinogenic. Requires 12-48h cycle + aeration.'),
('b3d786da-a21f-5551-a4b9-c973c1881472'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Hydrogen peroxide gas plasma sterilization:', '["Same as ETO clinical presentation","Not sporicidal clinical presentation","High heat required clinical presentation","H2O2 vapor + radiofrequency -> plasma","Only for liquids clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'H2O2 vapor + radiofrequency -> plasma', 'Plasma: fast (28-75 min), no toxic residue. For heat-sensitive instruments.'),
('274135e6-e806-507f-852f-4952aef5ce30'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Peracetic acid sterilization:', '["For skin only","Low level","Not sporicidal","Liquid chemical sterilant","Gas"]'::jsonb, 'Liquid chemical sterilant', 'Peracetic acid: for immersible instruments. Automated endoscope reprocessor.'),
('3f80b6d8-90e4-55ee-af6c-3fd549c2038a'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Glutaraldehyde (2%) for high-level disinfection:', '["1 minute","No time","5 minutes","Requires 20-30 minutes immersion","1 hour for disinfection"]'::jsonb, 'Requires 20-30 minutes immersion', 'Glutaraldehyde: high-level disinfectant. Rinse with sterile water. Respiratory irritant.'),
('7534435f-582d-5772-aa84-c7e228d7a8b7'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Ortho-phthalaldehyde (OPA) 0.55%:', '["No advantage","More irritating","Alternative to glutaraldehyde","Slower than glutaraldehyde","Same as alcohol"]'::jsonb, 'Alternative to glutaraldehyde', 'OPA: superior mycobactericidal. No activation needed. Stains proteins gray.'),
('a1090e55-3a55-5552-b25e-2be6a5199361'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Chlorine-based disinfectants (sodium hypochlorite):', '["No effect on viruses clinical presentation","Not for surfaces clinical presentation","Not sporicidal clinical presentation","Only for water clinical presentation","Broad-spectrum, inexpensive, sporicidal"]'::jsonb, 'Broad-spectrum, inexpensive, sporicidal', 'Bleach: 1:10 dilution (5000 ppm) for C. diff. 1:100 (500 ppm) for general surfaces.'),
('e7c09b85-252b-5f7e-a13a-31dcc9675bb2'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Alcohol (70% isopropyl/ethyl) as disinfectant:', '["Sterilant clinical presentation","Intermediate-level disinfectant (not sporicidal). Rapid action","Sporicidal clinical presentation","High-level clinical presentation","No effect on viruses clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Intermediate-level disinfectant (not sporicidal). Rapid action', 'Alcohol: good for skin prep, small surfaces. Not for instruments. Flammable.'),
('eee26041-7f28-5015-a12f-f3efc167fd20'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Surgical asepsis (sterile technique) aims to:', '["No special measures clinical presentation","Prevent introduction of microorganisms into surgical wound","Wash hands only clinical presentation","Use antibiotics clinical presentation","Clean the room clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Prevent introduction of microorganisms into surgical wound', 'Surgical asepsis: sterile gown, gloves, drapes, instruments throughout procedure.'),
('6059847a-9b68-57d7-a609-0ffe34e2bc57'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'The sterile field is created by:', '["No barrier","Sterile drapes","Clean drapes only","No drapes needed","Plastic sheets"]'::jsonb, 'Sterile drapes', 'Sterile field: only top of draped table is sterile. Below waist level is not.'),
('fdf955ac-4844-5820-a796-d24a79587363'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Items on the sterile field must be:', '["Re-sterilized after 1h","Sterile for 24h","Not monitored","Sterile until opened","Constantly monitored"]'::jsonb, 'Constantly monitored', 'Sterile field: must be constantly observed. Discard questionable items.'),
('186d273a-f231-510f-a263-4d503f3b0f9c'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'A sterile package is considered contaminated if:', '["Sealed clinical presentation","Within expiration date clinical presentation","Intact clinical presentation","All of these: wet, torn, expired, dropped, improperly sealed","Dry clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'All of these: wet, torn, expired, dropped, improperly sealed', 'Sterile package: moisture wicks bacteria (wet = contaminated).'),
('6574ae6a-5121-535b-aa94-dd6af6fce3c5'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Sterile gloves should be put on:', '["After sterile gown","No order","Before gown","Without gown","At any time"]'::jsonb, 'After sterile gown', 'Gloving: after gowning. Cuff covers gown cuff.'),
('833b82b6-0185-508b-9e82-7def1fb7ae8d'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Opening sterile supplies: wrapper edges:', '["All sterile clinical presentation","No rule clinical presentation","The first 1 inch of wrapper edge is considered non-sterile","Any part touched clinical presentation","Entire wrapper sterile clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'The first 1 inch of wrapper edge is considered non-sterile', 'Outer 1-inch margin of sterile wrapper is not sterile.'),
('bd38d109-ce25-5d0b-92dc-8093c8df685a'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'The sterile zone of a sterile gown:', '["Front from chest to waist level, sleeves from cuff to 2 inches above elbow","Entire gown including back clinical presentation","Only gloves clinical presentation","Only hands clinical presentation","Only left side clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Front from chest to waist level, sleeves from cuff to 2 inches above elbow', 'Gown: anterior chest to waist, sleeves to 2 in above elbow. Axilla/back/neck NOT sterile.'),
('e48b60b6-544e-5c9f-a85d-74ad22de50d0'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'During surgery, the scrub nurse passes instruments:', '["Throw them clinical presentation","Pass behind back clinical presentation","Pass over patient clinical presentation","On Mayo stand or directly into surgeon''s hand","Any method clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'On Mayo stand or directly into surgeon''s hand', 'Instrument handling: direct hand-to-hand. Sharp instruments announced.'),
('11d905ff-696c-51a5-95a6-9125d384ba91'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'A break in sterile technique requires:', '["No action","Continue - no effect","Immediate correction","Complete next day","Ignore it"]'::jsonb, 'Immediate correction', 'Break: stop, correct immediately. Do not proceed with contaminated items.'),
('b51102c0-3de5-5ee0-a681-9bcc50d1472c'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Sterile draping: the area prepared should be:', '["Large enough to allow extension of incision and placement of drains","No draping needed clinical presentation","Smallest possible area clinical presentation","Only 2 inches around incision clinical presentation","Only incision site clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Large enough to allow extension of incision and placement of drains', 'Skin prep: from incision outward. Draping: wide enough for unexpected extension.'),
('303fe709-3bec-5548-9821-ca109a724813'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'The circulating nurse:', '["Unsterile role: opens supplies, monitors field, documents, fetches items","Scrubbed and sterile clinical presentation","Not needed clinical presentation","Same as scrub nurse clinical presentation","Sterile role clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Unsterile role: opens supplies, monitors field, documents, fetches items', 'Circulating nurse: non-sterile. Manages OR flow, documentation, counts.'),
('1a91e2b9-d886-581d-a4fc-8179d9f03eb2'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'The scrub nurse (scrubbed person):', '["Sterile gown/gloves. Sets up table, passes instruments, maintains sterility","Doctor clinical presentation clinical presentation","Unsterile clinical presentation clinical presentation","Same as circulator clinical presentation clinical presentation","Not needed clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Sterile gown/gloves. Sets up table, passes instruments, maintains sterility', 'Scrub: instrument table setup, blade loading, suture preparation, counting.'),
('122e06e6-1713-5d49-ba9d-bd6c549ba44a'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Surgical hand scrub with chlorhexidine/iodine:', '["10 min for every case","Only first case","Not needed before surgery","Same as patient hand hygiene","Removes transient flora, reduces resident flora"]'::jsonb, 'Removes transient flora, reduces resident flora', 'Surgical scrub: reduce resident flora. Use antimicrobial agent.'),
('a0f7202e-aa9d-5d26-8324-f2ec56866727'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Double gloving (two pairs of gloves):', '["Uncomfortable clinical presentation","No benefit clinical presentation","Not used clinical presentation","Increases infection clinical presentation","Reduces risk of inner glove perforation exposure by 70%"]'::jsonb, 'Reduces risk of inner glove perforation exposure by 70%', 'Double gloving: recommended for high-risk or long procedures.'),
('2754d245-79ae-5005-b7f5-1ba21e039870'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Indicator for glove perforation:', '["Needle prick only","Colored inner glove","Double gloves always","Check visually","No indicator"]'::jsonb, 'Colored inner glove', 'Perforation indicator: green/purple inner glove shows dark spot when wet.'),
('017b9260-42a9-555b-a165-b459bc415ce9'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Povidone-iodine (Betadine) mechanism:', '["No effect clinical presentation clinical presentation","Barrier only clinical presentation clinical presentation","Iodine released -> oxidizes microbial proteins, DNA, membranes","Mechanical clinical presentation clinical presentation","Drying only clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Iodine released -> oxidizes microbial proteins, DNA, membranes', 'PVP-I: broad-spectrum. Inactivated by organic material. Requires 2 min contact.'),
('87223365-b4df-5de7-a683-838aef3cef1f'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Chlorhexidine gluconate mechanism:', '["Disrupts cell membranes, protein precipitation","Barrier clinical presentation","Not antimicrobial clinical presentation","Drying clinical presentation","Oxidation only clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Disrupts cell membranes, protein precipitation', 'Chlorhexidine: broad-spectrum, persistent (residual activity 6h). Not sporicidal.'),
('e40c2cc8-c865-5ad1-80d5-1feab680b7f8'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Advantage of chlorhexidine over povidone-iodine:', '["Persistent (residual) antimicrobial activity for hours","Cheaper clinical presentation","Better sporicidal clinical presentation","Better in organic matter clinical presentation","Faster clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Persistent (residual) antimicrobial activity for hours', 'Chlorhexidine: residual activity. Better for preoperative skin prep (SSI reduction).'),
('407fd1b0-366e-5564-b997-2c66fb829a20'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Alcohol (70% isopropyl) as antiseptic:', '["Denatures proteins","Slow acting","For skin only","No effect on viruses","Best sporicidal"]'::jsonb, 'Denatures proteins', 'Alcohol: 70% more effective than 90% (longer contact). Used with chlorhexidine/iodine.'),
('94aedf21-7da8-5924-af8b-71d87cbe0e3e'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Hydrogen peroxide (3%):', '["Oxidizing agent, effervescent","Best antiseptic","Sterilant","For intact skin","Systemic"]'::jsonb, 'Oxidizing agent, effervescent', 'H2O2: limited antimicrobial. Can cause O2 embolism in closed cavities.'),
('550715ff-0f46-5e73-acbe-91383eb01ec2'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Which antiseptic is safe for mucous membranes?', '["Hydrogen peroxide clinical presentation","Bleach clinical presentation","Chlorhexidine 2% clinical presentation","Alcohol 70% clinical presentation","Povidone-iodine (diluted) or chlorhexidine (0.05%) - not alcohol"]'::jsonb, 'Povidone-iodine (diluted) or chlorhexidine (0.05%) - not alcohol', 'Mucous membranes: diluted PVP-I (1:10) or diluted chlorhexidine (0.05%).'),
('abddfb6a-aa4a-5ba9-a31f-9d207188af84'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Hexachlorophene (pHisoHex):', '["For neonates","Not available","First choice","Safe for all","Not recommended"]'::jsonb, 'Not recommended', 'Hexachlorophene: neurotoxic -> not for broken skin, mucous membranes, or infants.'),
('af3e3d5a-9a65-5d50-844f-5e6216447695'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Histopathological examination reveals instability. Triclosan:', '["Antibacterial in soaps and sutures","Toxic clinical presentation","For sterilization clinical presentation","Sporicidal clinical presentation","Not antimicrobial clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Antibacterial in soaps and sutures', 'Triclosan: coated sutures (Vicryl Plus) reduce SSI.'),
('f6d2a4a8-5c97-5e1e-a0a5-d70d454f0cef'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Silver nitrate:', '["Systemic","Caustic, antimicrobial","For routine antisepsis","Sporicidal","Not used"]'::jsonb, 'Caustic, antimicrobial', 'Silver nitrate: 0.5% for burns, 1% for ophthalmia neonatorum. Stains black.'),
('f1402cf6-db20-5164-9590-4cb099afd2c2'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Mafenide acetate (Sulfamylon):', '["For clean wounds clinical presentation","No side effects clinical presentation","Topical antimicrobial for burns","Not painful clinical presentation","Oral clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Topical antimicrobial for burns', 'Mafenide: penetrates burn eschar. Side effects: pain, metabolic acidosis.'),
('abcfa7da-5c63-5443-b751-7a2204ec2a7a'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Which antiseptic is inactivated by organic matter?', '["Hydrogen peroxide","Povidone-iodine","Silver","Alcohol","Chlorhexidine"]'::jsonb, 'Povidone-iodine', 'Organic matter: blood, pus inactivates iodine. Clean wound before applying.'),
('996784a0-0b16-5b89-aecb-7f7ccb27ab7d'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Preoperative skin preparation agent of choice:', '["Chlorhexidine-alcohol (2% CHG + 70% IPA) - most effective SSI reduction","Water only clinical presentation","Soap only clinical presentation","Alcohol alone clinical presentation","Povidone-iodine alone clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Chlorhexidine-alcohol (2% CHG + 70% IPA) - most effective SSI reduction', 'CHG-alcohol > PVP-I for SSI. Allow full drying. Avoid eyes/ears/mucous membranes.'),
('d8939900-0d8b-540d-82e4-bcdb25802a62'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Povidone-iodine surgical scrub contact time:', '["10 minutes clinical presentation","No contact time clinical presentation","2-5 minutes (must be wet for full effect) before drying","30 seconds clinical presentation","Immediate clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, '2-5 minutes (must be wet for full effect) before drying', 'Iodine: requires adequate wet contact time. Lather 2-5 min.'),
('a7b88d37-3173-59e5-bc93-a874d5514cc2'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Chlorhexidine-alcohol application for skin prep:', '["Apply with friction, concentric circles outward from incision, dry completely","Apply once no friction clinical presentation","No drying clinical presentation","Rinse off clinical presentation","Wipe periphery to center clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Apply with friction, concentric circles outward from incision, dry completely', 'Skin prep: start at incision, outward circles. Do not pool (chemical burn).'),
('9974b00b-c31f-58b8-82db-871a9a62478d'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Antiseptic vs disinfectant:', '["Disinfectant = skin clinical presentation","Antiseptic: for living tissue. Disinfectant: for inanimate surfaces","No difference clinical presentation","Same thing clinical presentation","Antiseptic = surfaces clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Antiseptic: for living tissue. Disinfectant: for inanimate surfaces', 'Antiseptic: skin/mucous membrane. Disinfectant: surfaces (may be toxic to tissues).'),
('2b1262a1-1c54-5e8f-96d5-514ac8f96b31'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Hospital waste classification: Infectious waste:', '["Packaging clinical presentation","Food waste clinical presentation","Office waste clinical presentation","Waste contaminated with blood/body fluids","Paper waste clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Waste contaminated with blood/body fluids', 'Infectious waste: 15-25% of hospital waste. Yellow bags, incineration.'),
('635fec46-cb77-5a4e-a257-d081f4f384eb'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Sharps waste includes:', '["Needles, syringes, scalpels, blades, broken glass, trocars","Gloves clinical presentation","Only scalpels clinical presentation","Paper only clinical presentation","Only needles clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Needles, syringes, scalpels, blades, broken glass, trocars', 'Sharps: puncture-resistant, leak-proof containers. Do not recap needles.'),
('91188037-3275-571c-8ae5-02e8ebd3810d'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Color-coding for waste disposal:', '["Black: sharps clinical presentation clinical presentation","Red: infectious clinical presentation clinical presentation","Yellow: general clinical presentation clinical presentation","Blue: clinical clinical presentation clinical presentation","Yellow: infectious/clinical. Red: sharps. Black: general. Blue: recyclable"]'::jsonb, 'Yellow: infectious/clinical. Red: sharps. Black: general. Blue: recyclable', 'Color codes: yellow=clinical (incinerate), red=sharps, black=general, blue=glass/plastic.'),
('90073d24-2d74-5390-8b37-9d8a58057668'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Placenta and pathological waste:', '["Blue bag clinical presentation","Black bag clinical presentation","General waste clinical presentation","Yellow bag (infectious clinical waste) -> incineration","Recycle clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Yellow bag (infectious clinical waste) -> incineration', 'Pathological waste: tissues, organs, placentas. Incinerated.'),
('fc33549e-084f-53bb-910c-caf171802c0c'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Pharmaceutical waste (expired drugs):', '["General waste clinical presentation","Sewer clinical presentation","Recycle clinical presentation","Sharps container clinical presentation","Return to pharmacy for proper disposal. Some need incineration"]'::jsonb, 'Return to pharmacy for proper disposal. Some need incineration', 'Pharmaceutical: cytotoxic/controlled substances need special handling. Not down drain.'),
('1d35966c-3a63-513d-bbb8-865321887ea7'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Cytotoxic waste (chemotherapy agents):', '["Recycle clinical presentation","General waste clinical presentation","Sharps container clinical presentation","Purple/yellow bags with cytotoxic symbol -> incineration >1000 C","Sewer clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Purple/yellow bags with cytotoxic symbol -> incineration >1000 C', 'Cytotoxic waste: separate, labeled ''CYTOTOXIC''. Incinerate at >1000C.'),
('31868eb5-ee65-545f-9167-3744c74008b6'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Needle-stick injury: immediate management:', '["Squeeze wound clinical presentation clinical presentation","No action clinical presentation clinical presentation","Wash with soap and water, report, test source and worker, start PEP if indicated","Oral antibiotics clinical presentation clinical presentation","Apply bleach clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Wash with soap and water, report, test source and worker, start PEP if indicated', 'Needlestick: wash, report within 1h. Test source (HIV, HBV, HCV). Start PEP.'),
('1c9c74ae-2989-5c1b-9b7b-e371f47dc982'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Post-exposure prophylaxis (PEP) for HIV:', '["Only monitor clinical presentation","No treatment clinical presentation","Start within 72h (ideally within 2h) - 3-drug ART for 28 days","Single drug clinical presentation","Start within 1 week clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Start within 72h (ideally within 2h) - 3-drug ART for 28 days', 'HIV PEP: 3 drug regimen. Risk: 0.3% percutaneous. Follow up at 1,3,6 months.'),
('5b3c1a4a-de2a-510d-8881-921f0c5168c9'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'HBV post-exposure prophylaxis:', '["Antivirals","Vaccine only","No treatment","HBIG only","HBIG + HBV vaccine"]'::jsonb, 'HBIG + HBV vaccine', 'HBV: 30% seroconversion risk. If anti-HBs >10, no prophylaxis needed.'),
('23278d79-3a1d-5aeb-8cb0-1744830ba62e'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'HCV post-exposure prophylaxis:', '["Ribavirin","No monitoring","No PEP available","Interferon","Direct-acting antivirals"]'::jsonb, 'No PEP available', 'HCV: 1.8% seroconversion. No effective PEP. Early DAA for acute infection.'),
('d3659876-83cb-5690-a375-0087c745fe40'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Sharps disposal containers should:', '["Be puncture-resistant, leak-proof, closable, labeled. Replace when 3/4 full","Use cardboard box clinical presentation clinical presentation","No label needed clinical presentation clinical presentation","Replace when full clinical presentation clinical presentation","Not closable clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Be puncture-resistant, leak-proof, closable, labeled. Replace when 3/4 full', 'Sharps container: FDA-approved. Never overfill. Locate at point of use.'),
('d092edef-00e0-53e9-8130-757817dc7359'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Do not recap used needles:', '["Bend needle clinical presentation","Remove by hand clinical presentation","Always recap clinical presentation","Recapping is leading cause of needlestick","Recap two hands clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Recapping is leading cause of needlestick', 'Recapping: prohibited. Use safety-engineered devices.'),
('0834157b-8e3c-58e8-860c-b3c18ffeaea0'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Safety-engineered devices:', '["Same as standard clinical presentation","More dangerous clinical presentation","Needles that retract, sheath, or blunt after use","Not available clinical presentation","Not effective clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Needles that retract, sheath, or blunt after use', 'Safety devices: OSHA requirement. Retractable lancets, blunt-tip suture needles.'),
('663f5bba-0492-59f6-b30b-296b9681ac49'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Spill management for blood/body fluids:', '["Just wipe clinical presentation clinical presentation","Water only clinical presentation clinical presentation","No PPE needed clinical presentation clinical presentation","Let it dry clinical presentation clinical presentation","Contain, wear PPE, absorbent material, clean with detergent, disinfect bleach 1:10"]'::jsonb, 'Contain, wear PPE, absorbent material, clean with detergent, disinfect bleach 1:10', 'Spill: small (<10 cm): clean/disinfect. Large: contain, absorbent, bleach.'),
('0ea63ec4-8492-577c-ad6d-8fa338f2f4da'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Laundry management: contaminated linens:', '["Cold water wash clinical presentation","Sort at bedside clinical presentation","No special handling clinical presentation","Bleach always clinical presentation","Handle with gloves, labeled leak-proof bag"]'::jsonb, 'Handle with gloves, labeled leak-proof bag', 'Linen: handle minimally. Color-coded bags. Wash with hot water and detergent.'),
('fccd2ede-a5cf-5e29-a2f1-094417bd1c7c'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Healthcare-associated infection (HAI) definition:', '["Always severe clinical presentation","Asymptomatic clinical presentation","Infection acquired in hospital not present at admission","Infection present at admission clinical presentation","Community-acquired clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Infection acquired in hospital not present at admission', 'HAI: onset >=48h after admission. Most common: UTI, SSI, pneumonia, BSI.'),
('1082e7a2-801d-571e-bc33-23e49a07101e'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Most common HAI is:', '["CLABSI","Catheter","C. diff","VAP","SSI"]'::jsonb, 'Catheter', 'CAUTI: most common HAI (30-40%). Avoid unnecessary catheterization.'),
('a58698b3-b3f9-5125-83c3-775a38d322ee'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Central line-associated bloodstream infection (CLABSI):', '["Not preventable clinical presentation","Always present clinical presentation","Only peripheral line clinical presentation","Primary BSI with central line within 48h of insertion","From any IV line clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Primary BSI with central line within 48h of insertion', 'CLABSI prevention bundle: hand hygiene, maximal barrier, CHG prep, femoral avoidance.'),
('38dc3c17-69ca-5a8a-aba1-be60e48f62da'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Ventilator-associated pneumonia (VAP):', '["Pneumonia >48h after intubation/mechanical ventilation","Community pneumonia clinical presentation","Non-invasive only clinical presentation","Day 1 pneumonia clinical presentation","Not ventilator-related clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Pneumonia >48h after intubation/mechanical ventilation', 'VAP prevention: HOB 30-45, oral CHG, subglottic suctioning, sedation vacation.'),
('23b80f71-7a22-5632-a01d-dce516ac5586'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'C. difficile infection risk factors:', '["All: antibiotics (clindamycin, cephalosporins, FQs), hospitalization, PPIs, age >65","Only age clinical presentation clinical presentation","Only hospitalization clinical presentation clinical presentation","Only PPIs clinical presentation clinical presentation","Only clindamycin clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'All: antibiotics (clindamycin, cephalosporins, FQs), hospitalization, PPIs, age >65', 'C. diff: toxin-mediated diarrhea. Contact + hand washing (soap/water). Treat with vancomycin.'),
('8442f720-57b0-52fa-bd2a-abfaeb86c841'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'SSI prevention measures include:', '["Only hand washing clinical presentation clinical presentation","Only normothermia clinical presentation clinical presentation","Only glucose control clinical presentation clinical presentation","All: antibiotic prophylaxis, normothermia, glucose control, skin prep, hair clipping","Only antibiotics clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'All: antibiotic prophylaxis, normothermia, glucose control, skin prep, hair clipping', 'SSI bundle: antibiotic timing, normothermia >36C, glucose <180, CHG prep, oxygenation.'),
('d9243769-64ae-5874-81f6-0026d7f63749'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Preoperative blood glucose control to prevent SSI:', '["Only in diabetics clinical presentation","Only oral agents clinical presentation","Maintain glucose <180-200 mg/dL in diabetic and non-diabetic patients","No need clinical presentation","Glucose >200 clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Maintain glucose <180-200 mg/dL in diabetic and non-diabetic patients', 'Hyperglycemia impairs neutrophil function. Insulin infusion if needed.'),
('7753aac8-2d93-5ab9-8ef1-3b58cfa5763c'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Perioperative oxygen supplementation to prevent SSI:', '["No benefit clinical presentation","Administer FiO2 80% during and 2-6h after surgery","Only in COPD clinical presentation","Only after extubation clinical presentation","Room air clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Administer FiO2 80% during and 2-6h after surgery', 'High FiO2: increases tissue O2, improves neutrophil killing. Reduces SSI by 25%.'),
('8f757ecb-6b3f-5545-8140-e5fc6dcc980b'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Surveillance of SSI: post-discharge follow-up:', '["1 week only clinical presentation","No follow-up clinical presentation","Only in-hospital clinical presentation","Only in ICU clinical presentation","30 days (90 days if implant) - SSI may appear after discharge"]'::jsonb, '30 days (90 days if implant) - SSI may appear after discharge', 'SSI surveillance: 50-70% detected post-discharge. Phone or clinic.'),
('bc454d02-53b0-5427-aa33-5bcd7edd1107'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'NHSN risk index:', '["Only procedure type","Only ASA","Only wound class","Only surgeon","Wound class + ASA + duration"]'::jsonb, 'Wound class + ASA + duration', 'NHSN: 0-3 points. Benchmarking SSI rates. Compare observed vs expected.'),
('c641d9cf-8e58-57ca-825f-727954270766'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'CAUTI prevention:', '["No catheter needed clinical presentation","Antibiotic irrigations clinical presentation","Insert only if indicated, remove ASAP, aseptic technique, closed drainage","Daily change clinical presentation","Silver catheters clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Insert only if indicated, remove ASAP, aseptic technique, closed drainage', 'CAUTI prevention: avoid/reduce catheter use. Nurse-initiated removal.'),
('3f33cfec-8f5a-56bb-a982-29d6a755da5a'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Urinary catheter indications:', '["Patient request clinical presentation","Convenience clinical presentation","All patients clinical presentation","Incontinence without retention clinical presentation","Retention, strict I/O, surgery/prostate, perineal wounds, hospice"]'::jsonb, 'Retention, strict I/O, surgery/prostate, perineal wounds, hospice', 'Catheter: valid indications only. Remove within 24h post-op.'),
('cb92095a-71d6-599c-9206-cfc8bf8bcbd7'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'CLABSI prevention: Maximal sterile barrier:', '["Only sterile field clinical presentation","Sterile gown, gloves, cap, mask, full-body drape during insertion","Only gown clinical presentation","Clean gloves only clinical presentation","No barrier clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Sterile gown, gloves, cap, mask, full-body drape during insertion', 'Maximal barrier: 6-fold reduction in CLABSI vs standard.'),
('90245396-0280-5469-80a0-833f4ad613de'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'CLABSI: preferred insertion site:', '["PICC","Femoral","IJ","All same","Subclavian"]'::jsonb, 'Subclavian', 'Subclavian: lowest CLABSI risk. Femoral: highest (avoid).'),
('8db99e06-3b09-545d-904f-48584039a568'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'CHG-impregnated dressings for central lines:', '["No effect","Avoid use","Increase infection","Reduce CLABSI","Only for femoral"]'::jsonb, 'Reduce CLABSI', 'CHG dressing: Biopatch. Reduces catheter colonization.'),
('0586c6ac-fa9a-525b-bf40-a44ef5cab22a'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Operating room ventilation standard:', '["No ventilation","Positive pressure","Room air","Negative pressure","Recirculated only"]'::jsonb, 'Positive pressure', 'OR ventilation: positive pressure (air flows OUT). 15-20 ACH. HEPA filters.'),
('fb283c75-c70c-59c5-8ed6-bedf6baa032d'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Laminar airflow in OR:', '["No benefit clinical presentation","HEPA-filtered air flows uniformly over operating field","Same as standard clinical presentation","Not for orthopedics clinical presentation","For all ORs clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'HEPA-filtered air flows uniformly over operating field', 'Laminar flow: reduces airborne particles. Controversial for SSI reduction.'),
('06b92afd-00e9-5704-ac7f-529131c0f154'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'OR temperature and humidity:', '["Low temperature","20-24 C, humidity 30-60%","Any","High humidity","No standards"]'::jsonb, '20-24 C, humidity 30-60%', 'OR environment: balance bacterial growth vs patient hypothermia.'),
('463bc2a6-32ee-5fd1-91e5-7365d01b8eee'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Traffic in OR should be:', '["Minimized (each door opening increases airborne particles). Doors closed","Unrestricted clinical presentation clinical presentation","High traffic fine clinical presentation clinical presentation","No restrictions clinical presentation clinical presentation","Always open clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Minimized (each door opening increases airborne particles). Doors closed', 'OR traffic: increases wound contamination. Minimize personnel.'),
('2da5cabf-cc2e-513a-bc44-87f99414a17e'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Surgical attire in restricted zone:', '["Scrub suit, cap (cover all hair), mask, shoe covers","No mask needed clinical presentation","Street clothes clinical presentation","Scrub suit only clinical presentation","No cap needed clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Scrub suit, cap (cover all hair), mask, shoe covers', 'Attire: cover head hair. Masks for sterile team. No jewelry/nail polish.'),
('bd642cfc-b783-546e-b53f-e970a585da45'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Mask use in OR:', '["Not needed clinical presentation","Reuse multiple clinical presentation","Change between cases if wet/soiled. Wear entire case","Dangle during case clinical presentation","Change hourly clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Change between cases if wet/soiled. Wear entire case', 'Masks: fluid-resistant. Remove by straps, not front. Do not wear outside OR.'),
('88324fde-aa51-51ec-af3d-10fa35ed4285'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Proper OR gown removal:', '["Remove gloves first, then gown","Remove by front","No order","Both together","Gown first"]'::jsonb, 'Remove gloves first, then gown', 'Doffing: gloves first. Gown: unfasten, pull from inside sleeves, discard.'),
('4a198a8b-624b-52d9-a933-b5efe73dc12d'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Cleaning OR between cases:', '["No cleaning clinical presentation","Only floor mop clinical presentation","Wet-dust horizontal surfaces, clean visible soiling, change table cover","Full terminal clean clinical presentation","Spray only clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Wet-dust horizontal surfaces, clean visible soiling, change table cover', 'Between cases: limited clean. Terminal cleaning at end of day.'),
('4326e71c-ef02-5e36-8e9a-96c2c892ed75'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Terminal cleaning of OR:', '["All surfaces (walls, floors, lights, equipment) disinfected","Only floor clinical presentation","No cleaning clinical presentation","Weekly clinical presentation","Only table clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'All surfaces (walls, floors, lights, equipment) disinfected', 'Terminal: thorough disinfection all horizontal/vertical surfaces.'),
('12441d5a-b109-5401-81e5-42c69a4b835e'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Flash sterilization (immediate-use):', '["Preferred method","For storage","For wrapped packs","All instruments","For urgent dropped instruments"]'::jsonb, 'For urgent dropped instruments', 'Flash: 134 C for 3 min. No wrap. Transport immediately. Not for implants.'),
('39ded605-15e3-53e8-9a54-5eec1a4aa7be'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Biological indicator for steam sterilization:', '["E. coli clinical presentation","B. atrophaeus clinical presentation","S. aureus clinical presentation","No indicator clinical presentation","Geobacillus stearothermophilus spores"]'::jsonb, 'Geobacillus stearothermophilus spores', 'Biological indicator: weekly testing. Spores killed = cycle valid.'),
('d60bcff4-5936-5d80-ad72-97f55417ddba'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Chemical indicator for ETO sterilization:', '["G. stearothermophilus clinical presentation","E. coli clinical presentation","Bacillus atrophaeus (formerly B. subtilis) spores","S. aureus clinical presentation","No indicator clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Bacillus atrophaeus (formerly B. subtilis) spores', 'B. atrophaeus: indicator for ETO, dry heat, vaporized H2O2.'),
('6503581e-c9d9-5c48-bf15-5c60caf890e4'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Bowie-Dick test for:', '["Pre-vacuum autoclave","Gravity autoclave","ETO","Plasma sterilizer","Dry heat"]'::jsonb, 'Pre-vacuum autoclave', 'Bowie-Dick: chemical indicator in test pack. Uniform color = adequate air removal.'),
('200f0ece-24a9-52ab-aa06-7c749003c934'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Sterilization monitoring: 3 levels:', '["Chemical only clinical presentation","Biological only clinical presentation","Physical (temp/time/pressure), chemical (strips/tape), biological","No monitoring clinical presentation","Physical only clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Physical (temp/time/pressure), chemical (strips/tape), biological', 'Three levels: mechanical, chemical, biological. All used together.'),
('c361721d-f6bc-5591-a015-91e38b3709cd'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Sterile storage: shelf life depends on:', '["Event-related factors","Fixed expiration only","Manufacturer","Sterilizer type","Instrument type"]'::jsonb, 'Event-related factors', 'Event-related sterility: if packaging intact/dry/clean, item remains sterile.'),
('9a7cbe67-d609-5f4e-98e5-e6fbc0ed02d4'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Environmental cleaning frequency for patient rooms:', '["Daily (terminal at discharge). High-touch surfaces frequently","Only on discharge clinical presentation","Monthly clinical presentation","Weekly clinical presentation","No cleaning clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Daily (terminal at discharge). High-touch surfaces frequently', 'High-touch surfaces: bed rails, call bell, table, door handles. Disinfect daily.'),
('b090e778-850d-57ad-a5dd-e806cc5b78f7'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Cleaning of isolation rooms:', '["Only for C. diff clinical presentation","No special cleaning clinical presentation","After patient discharge (terminal cleaning) per pathogen","Same as non-isolation clinical presentation","Only weekly clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'After patient discharge (terminal cleaning) per pathogen', 'Terminal clean: all surfaces, curtains, mattress. Sporicidal for C. diff.'),
('b3ecb643-6b83-5d6b-9086-bcdff8c823f4'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'No-touch disinfection methods:', '["Manual cleaning only clinical presentation","For OR only clinical presentation","UV-C light and hydrogen peroxide vapor","Not effective clinical presentation","For equipment only clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'UV-C light and hydrogen peroxide vapor', 'UV-C: reduces C. diff, MRSA, VRE. H2O2 vapor: sporicidal. Adjunct to cleaning.'),
('e51331e0-e9cc-52d5-9ab7-c1cab84d0f9d'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Disinfectant for C. difficile:', '["Chlorhexidine clinical presentation","Phenolic clinical presentation","Quaternary ammonium clinical presentation","Alcohol clinical presentation","Sodium hypochlorite (bleach) 1000-5000 ppm"]'::jsonb, 'Sodium hypochlorite (bleach) 1000-5000 ppm', 'C. diff spores resistant to alcohol, quats, phenolics. Bleach 1:10.'),
('c196b6ef-382b-515d-b754-244b181b7f69'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Quaternary ammonium compounds (Quats):', '["Low-level disinfectant for general surfaces","High-level clinical presentation","For instruments clinical presentation","Sporicidal clinical presentation","Sterilant clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Low-level disinfectant for general surfaces', 'Quats: bacteriostatic, limited gram-negative, no spores. For non-critical surfaces.'),
('3757fa52-7a3a-5e2d-9a3e-b6822c31f2b7'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Phenolic disinfectants:', '["Sterilant clinical presentation","Intermediate-level (tuberculocidal, virucidal). Not for infant areas","Safe for infants clinical presentation","High-level clinical presentation","Sporicidal clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Intermediate-level (tuberculocidal, virucidal). Not for infant areas', 'Phenolics: can cause hyperbilirubinemia in neonates. Not for bassinets.'),
('c3a3b282-1a12-5f16-ab32-4666db04a82f'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Formaldehyde as disinfectant:', '["Safe clinical presentation clinical presentation","High-level disinfectant/sterilant (gas or liquid). Carcinogenic.","Low-level clinical presentation clinical presentation","Not used clinical presentation clinical presentation","For skin clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'High-level disinfectant/sterilant (gas or liquid). Carcinogenic.', 'Formaldehyde: carcinogenic. Limited use. Glutaraldehyde/OPA preferred.'),
