('012c1d1a-0a1a-507a-9284-17bed48bb2e8'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Susceptible host in healthcare:', '["No one clinical presentation clinical presentation","Healthy adults clinical presentation clinical presentation","Only neonates clinical presentation clinical presentation","Immunocompromised, elderly, neonates, surgical patients, patients with devices","Only elderly clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Immunocompromised, elderly, neonates, surgical patients, patients with devices', 'Susceptible: many hospitalized patients are immunocompromised or have breaches.'),
('5831ae0b-0d53-5dcd-a1f0-d96ef085965a'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Outbreak investigation steps:', '["Verify diagnosis, case definition, case finding, line listing, epi curve, hypothesis","No investigation clinical presentation clinical presentation","Isolate everyone clinical presentation clinical presentation","Treat all clinical presentation clinical presentation","Ignore clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Verify diagnosis, case definition, case finding, line listing, epi curve, hypothesis', 'Outbreak: systematic investigation to identify source and implement control.'),
('3a442ccd-c585-5339-b9f4-b03d7be164cc'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Attack rate in outbreak:', '["Deaths/total cases clinical presentation","Cases/area clinical presentation","Number of new cases / population at risk x 100","Cases/total population clinical presentation","Total cases clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Number of new cases / population at risk x 100', 'Attack rate: proportion of exposed who become ill. Used in foodborne outbreaks.'),
('46766f5f-f952-51ff-bf04-de0b7c4dacdb'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Colonization vs infection:', '["Infection = no symptoms clinical presentation clinical presentation","Colonization: organism present, no host response. Infection: organism + host response (inflammation","No difference clinical presentation clinical presentation","Same clinical presentation clinical presentation","Colonization = disease clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Colonization: organism present, no host response. Infection: organism + host response (inflammation', 'Colonization: carrier state. Can lead to infection if host defenses breached.'),
('8a713a98-a079-5717-9266-7b01dd3aa511'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Screening for MDR organisms:', '["No screening clinical presentation clinical presentation","Only symptomatic clinical presentation clinical presentation","Random clinical presentation clinical presentation","Yearly clinical presentation clinical presentation","Active surveillance cultures (nasal MRSA, rectal VRE/CRE) on admission for high-risk patients"]'::jsonb, 'Active surveillance cultures (nasal MRSA, rectal VRE/CRE) on admission for high-risk patients', 'Screening: identifies colonized patients for contact precautions and decolonization.'),
('c6ccd42a-17f5-59aa-9886-534cf670c6fa'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'MRSA decolonization protocol:', '["Isolation only clinical presentation","No treatment clinical presentation","Oral antibiotics clinical presentation","Vancomycin IV clinical presentation","Mupirocin nasal ointment + CHG body washes"]'::jsonb, 'Mupirocin nasal ointment + CHG body washes', 'MRSA decolonization: for carriers before high-risk surgery. Reduces SSI.'),
('6d7af95f-f843-5915-b8b4-9b53e9820f23'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'ESBL-producing organisms are resistant to:', '["All penicillins, cephalosporins, and aztreonam","Only penicillins clinical presentation","Only cephalosporins clinical presentation","Only carbapenems clinical presentation","Only aminoglycosides clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'All penicillins, cephalosporins, and aztreonam', 'ESBL: hydrolyze oxyimino-cephalosporins. Tx: carbapenems.'),
('d1d1719c-a087-5eb0-b1a3-4bbc17fe6be8'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Carbapenem-resistant Enterobacteriaceae (CRE):', '["Resistant to carbapenems (last resort). High mortality. Contact precautions","Easy to treat clinical presentation","Same as sensitive clinical presentation","No special precautions clinical presentation","Rarely resistant clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Resistant to carbapenems (last resort). High mortality. Contact precautions', 'CRE: urgent threat. Resistance via KPC, NDM, OXA. Limited treatment options.'),
('edff225c-b4b1-5aa2-bc47-57b6f6f7fa98'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Antibiotic stewardship goals:', '["No restrictions clinical presentation","Use more antibiotics clinical presentation","Only treat symptoms clinical presentation","Always use broad-spectrum clinical presentation","Optimize antibiotic use, reduce resistance, minimize adverse effects, reduce costs"]'::jsonb, 'Optimize antibiotic use, reduce resistance, minimize adverse effects, reduce costs', 'Stewardship: right drug, dose, duration. De-escalate based on cultures.'),
('d57ea5f1-e08d-5bef-8ee3-b2bdb854fe24'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'De-escalation of antibiotics:', '["Stop antibiotics clinical presentation","Add more antibiotics clinical presentation","Change broad-spectrum to narrow-spectrum based on culture results","Always stay broad clinical presentation","Change to same clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Change broad-spectrum to narrow-spectrum based on culture results', 'De-escalation: reduces resistance, side effects, costs. Once susceptibilities known.'),
('cdf41c03-625f-54c8-87d4-14cec35c4cd6'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Antibiotic time-out:', '["Change weekly","No time limit","No review","Reassess antibiotics at 48-72h","Continue until end"]'::jsonb, 'Reassess antibiotics at 48-72h', 'Time-out: systematic reassessment. Promotes appropriate use and de-escalation.'),
('b2c2a379-4482-5c29-9ffa-0daa478fb36a'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Hand washing with soap and water is essential after care of patients with:', '["MRSA","Influenza","VRE","C. difficile infection","TB"]'::jsonb, 'C. difficile infection', 'C. diff: soap and water only. Alcohol hand rub does not inactivate spores.'),
('78a6e461-2fda-5e75-951f-d508f56c0627'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Which patient should be placed in a negative pressure room?', '["Pulmonary tuberculosis","MRSA wound","Influenza","C. diff diarrhea","VRE UTI"]'::jsonb, 'Pulmonary tuberculosis', 'Negative pressure: airborne infections. TB, measles, varicella.'),
('26a2154d-6bbe-54d6-ade9-329e468f1893'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Standard precautions include:', '["Hand hygiene, PPE, safe injection, respiratory hygiene, environmental cleaning","Only hand hygiene clinical presentation clinical presentation","Only injection safety clinical presentation clinical presentation","Only PPE clinical presentation clinical presentation","Only cleaning clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Hand hygiene, PPE, safe injection, respiratory hygiene, environmental cleaning', 'Standard precautions: all elements for all patients. Baseline infection prevention.'),
('3a532921-9dde-5714-aca1-1cb45c7576d0'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Needlestick injury: greatest risk of transmission is from:', '["HTLV","Syphilis","HCV","HBV","HIV"]'::jsonb, 'HBV', 'HBV: most infectious bloodborne. Vaccine available. High environmental stability.'),
('36450cc4-e9b7-574a-ab9c-a4190bbf5df2'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Surgical site infection classification: Superficial incisional:', '["No infection clinical presentation","Organ/space clinical presentation","Infection involves only skin/subcutaneous tissue within 30 days","Deep soft tissue clinical presentation","Remote infection clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Infection involves only skin/subcutaneous tissue within 30 days', 'Superficial SSI: skin/subcutaneous. Criteria: purulent drainage, positive culture, pain/swelling.'),
('7e9146ab-c61c-5b58-8b54-4cbe7f747911'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Deep incisional SSI:', '["Organ clinical presentation clinical presentation","Subcutaneous only clinical presentation clinical presentation","Skin only clinical presentation clinical presentation","No infection clinical presentation clinical presentation","Infection involves deep soft tissues (fascia, muscle) within 30-90 days"]'::jsonb, 'Infection involves deep soft tissues (fascia, muscle) within 30-90 days', 'Deep SSI: fascia/muscle involvement. Reoperation or I&D often needed.'),
('97c494eb-b3e4-555a-b54b-74ed3d1360c0'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Organ/space SSI:', '["Fascia clinical presentation clinical presentation","Infection involves any part of body opened/manipulated during surgery","Skin only clinical presentation clinical presentation","Subcutaneous clinical presentation clinical presentation","Muscle clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Infection involves any part of body opened/manipulated during surgery', 'Organ/space: e.g., intra-abdominal abscess, mediastinitis after CABG.'),
('240c25ad-3f0c-53e4-96d7-163a677d90bf'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Preoperative shower with antiseptic:', '["With regular soap clinical presentation","At least one shower with CHG or soap the night before surgery","Morning of only clinical presentation","Only in hospital clinical presentation","No benefit clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'At least one shower with CHG or soap the night before surgery', 'Preop shower: reduces skin flora. CHG better than plain soap. Do night before and morning of.'),
('2e840b0a-53ae-50ea-901b-6483a4ca9dfa'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Antibiotic prophylaxis for clean surgery:', '["5 days","7 days","3 days","Until discharge","Single dose cefazolin"]'::jsonb, 'Single dose cefazolin', 'Clean surgery: single dose unless prolonged/tons of blood loss. Stop within 24h.'),
('3153a597-2b92-52c0-92c9-0ebaf5460f17'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Redosing interval for cefazolin during surgery:', '["Every 8 hours","Every 6 hours","Every 4 hours","Once only","Every 2 hours"]'::jsonb, 'Every 4 hours', 'Redose cefazolin q4h. Also if blood loss >1500 mL.'),
('4e2f3f82-1737-55a2-805e-74486770ba7c'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'VRE (vancomycin-resistant Enterococcus):', '["Same as sensitive clinical presentation","Contact precautions. Colonization common. Limited treatment","No precautions clinical presentation","Droplet clinical presentation","Airborne clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Contact precautions. Colonization common. Limited treatment', 'VRE: fecal-oral transmission. Contact precautions. Hand hygiene (soap/water or alcohol).'),
('314bae9a-20eb-572d-a439-1d1e231416c7'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'CRE treatment options include:', '["All beta-lactams clinical presentation clinical presentation","Carbapenems clinical presentation clinical presentation","Polymyxins (colistin), tigecycline, aminoglycosides, ceftazidime-avibactam","Cephalosporins clinical presentation clinical presentation","Penicillins clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Polymyxins (colistin), tigecycline, aminoglycosides, ceftazidime-avibactam', 'CRE: limited options. Ceftazidime-avibactam for KPC. Polymyxins for NDM.'),
('937dd952-35bd-563b-ad83-862d086a5159'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Chlorhexidine-impregnated cloths for daily bathing:', '["Only for surgical patients clinical presentation","Increase infection clinical presentation","No benefit clinical presentation","Reduce acquisition of VRE, MRSA, and BSIs in ICU patients","Weekly only clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Reduce acquisition of VRE, MRSA, and BSIs in ICU patients', 'CHG daily bathing: universal decolonization in ICU. Reduces MDR organism transmission and BSIs.'),
('87cf7e48-ccad-50cf-b745-e1a81123934a'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Oral care with chlorhexidine for ventilated patients:', '["No effect","Increases pneumonia","Only in non-ventilated","Reduces VAP incidence","Use only weekly"]'::jsonb, 'Reduces VAP incidence', 'Oral CHG: decreases dental plaque colonization. Part of VAP bundle.'),
('022a4809-5950-549e-a812-8bbaa361dcd1'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Subglottic secretion drainage (ETT with suction port):', '["Reduces VAP by preventing aspiration of pooled secretions above cuff","Not used clinical presentation","No effect clinical presentation","Increases VAP clinical presentation","Only in children clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Reduces VAP by preventing aspiration of pooled secretions above cuff', 'Subglottic suctioning: removes secretions above ETT cuff. Reduces VAP.'),
('07a0c0c9-e0ab-5860-8efe-b3fd85cc5534'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Daily sedation vacation and spontaneous breathing trial:', '["Not used clinical presentation","Increases VAP clinical presentation","Prolongs ventilation clinical presentation","No effect clinical presentation","Reduces VAP and duration of mechanical ventilation"]'::jsonb, 'Reduces VAP and duration of mechanical ventilation', 'Sedation vacation: decrease sedative exposure. SBT: assess readiness for extubation.'),
('39981a3f-3885-521a-9683-8047891b3ba6'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Central line insertion checklist includes:', '["Only hand hygiene clinical presentation clinical presentation","Hand hygiene, maximal barrier, CHG prep, femoral avoidance, daily review","Only CHG clinical presentation clinical presentation","Only site choice clinical presentation clinical presentation","Only barrier clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Hand hygiene, maximal barrier, CHG prep, femoral avoidance, daily review', 'Central line checklist: 5-item bundle. Implementation reduces CLABSI significantly.'),
('bd3ee1fe-6137-539b-9b81-a9d7b94f0525'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'CHG skin prep for central line insertion:', '["Use PVP-I instead clinical presentation","No drying needed clinical presentation","Apply, wipe off clinical presentation","Alcohol alone clinical presentation","Apply 2% CHG in 70% isopropyl alcohol, allow to dry completely"]'::jsonb, 'Apply 2% CHG in 70% isopropyl alcohol, allow to dry completely', 'CHG: preferred over PVP-I for central line insertion. Allow 30 sec to dry.'),
('984e4735-1207-5bfa-94bd-d113c2e5ab27'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Central line dressing change:', '["Transparent semi-permeable dressing change q7 days","Daily clinical presentation","No change clinical presentation","Monthly clinical presentation","q12h clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Transparent semi-permeable dressing change q7 days', 'Dressing: transparent allows visualization. CHG disc under dressing. Change if compromised.'),
('2774edae-d62a-50e2-be1d-d37021a2688e'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'PICC vs CVC infection rate:', '["No difference clinical presentation clinical presentation","Higher clinical presentation clinical presentation","PICC has lower CLABSI rate than non-tunneled CVC for short-mid term","Same clinical presentation clinical presentation","Much higher clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'PICC has lower CLABSI rate than non-tunneled CVC for short-mid term', 'PICC: lower infection risk for short-mid term use. Used for IV antibiotics, chemo.'),
('ea6f9263-4487-56a4-85dc-8fbdb5fc28b3'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Hemodialysis catheter infection prevention:', '["No prevention needed clinical presentation","No antibiotic lock clinical presentation","Avoid femoral site, use antimicrobial lock solution for tunneled catheters","Femoral preferred clinical presentation","Weekly dressing change clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Avoid femoral site, use antimicrobial lock solution for tunneled catheters', 'Dialysis catheter: high infection rate. Tunneled preferred. Lock with gentamicin or taurolidine.'),
('17e61dcc-af81-5569-a2ca-aa9c18bf4884'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Inoculation (sharps) injury: most common scenario:', '["During disposal","During procedure","While cleaning","While passing instruments","Recapping needle"]'::jsonb, 'Recapping needle', 'Recapping: 30% of injuries. Never recap. Use safety devices.'),
('30b11cd7-c679-50bc-bc40-7bb7c4f9105a'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Blood culture collection: minimum volume per bottle:', '["No minimum","20 mL","8-10 mL per bottle","5 mL","1 mL"]'::jsonb, '8-10 mL per bottle', 'Blood culture: 20-30 mL total. 10 mL per bottle (aerobic + anaerobic). Increases yield.'),
('55078980-308f-5aa9-addb-90156a928d3c'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Blood culture contamination rate target:', '["<5%","<20%","<3%","<10%","<15%"]'::jsonb, '<3%', 'Contamination: leads to unnecessary antibiotics. Use CHG prep, sterile technique.'),
('6541aa14-e4ce-53a2-8579-f0886e18f837'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Single blood culture positive for CoNS:', '["Probable contaminant","Line infection","True bacteremia","Definite infection","Must treat"]'::jsonb, 'Probable contaminant', 'CoNS: common contaminant. If one of two sets positive, likely contaminant. If multiple sets positive, consider line infection.'),
('e6d664e4-e7be-5d4c-9424-577741945dc8'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'difficile: most sensitive diagnostic test:', '["Glutamate dehydrogenase clinical presentation","Microscopy clinical presentation","Toxin EIA clinical presentation","Nucleic acid amplification test (NAAT) for toxin B gene","Culture clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Nucleic acid amplification test (NAAT) for toxin B gene', 'NAAT: most sensitive and specific. Positive = infection (if compatible symptoms).'),
('dcce095a-d591-5a42-95b5-e207a749f99a'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'difficile treatment for first episode:', '["No treatment clinical presentation","Probiotics clinical presentation","Oral vancomycin 125 mg QID or fidaxomicin 200 mg BID x 10 days","Vancomycin IV clinical presentation","Metronidazole PO clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Oral vancomycin 125 mg QID or fidaxomicin 200 mg BID x 10 days', 'C. diff: vancomycin or fidaxomicin first-line. Metronidazole for mild if vancomycin unavailable.'),
('d0eac081-5be5-5e09-983c-b635ff8ee898'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'difficile treatment:', '["Metronidazole clinical presentation","Pulsed/tapered vancomycin, fidaxomicin, or fecal microbiota transplant","Same as first episode clinical presentation","Surgery clinical presentation","No treatment clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Pulsed/tapered vancomycin, fidaxomicin, or fecal microbiota transplant', 'Recurrent C. diff: FMT highly effective (>90%). Bezlotoxumab (antitoxin) for prevention.'),
('02046d4b-2aa5-51ad-8bff-998a44af5138'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'C. difficile prevention: environmental cleaning:', '["UV only clinical presentation","Alcohol clinical presentation","Bleach (1000-5000 ppm) daily in rooms of patients with C. diff","Quats clinical presentation","No special cleaning clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Bleach (1000-5000 ppm) daily in rooms of patients with C. diff', 'C. diff sporicidal: bleach required. Also hand hygiene with soap/water.'),
('e772557c-ddd0-551e-acc6-f145bf82aa1b'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Norovirus outbreak control measures:', '["Continue normal clinical presentation clinical presentation","No precautions clinical presentation clinical presentation","Hand washing with soap/water, contact precautions, bleach cleaning, cohort, visitor restriction","No cleaning clinical presentation clinical presentation","Alcohol hand rub clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Hand washing with soap/water, contact precautions, bleach cleaning, cohort, visitor restriction', 'Norovirus: highly contagious. Alcohol ineffective. Soap/water, bleach for surfaces.'),
('93046872-f296-5545-9235-583b676f2de6'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Aseptic technique for catheter insertion:', '["Sterile gloves, large drape, CHG prep, sterile equipment","Small drape clinical presentation","Clean gloves only clinical presentation","Tap water clinical presentation","No drape clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Sterile gloves, large drape, CHG prep, sterile equipment', 'Aseptic technique: prevents introduction of organisms into sterile body sites.'),
('d048ade4-72f6-532d-94ea-a6fc394a517f'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Closed urinary drainage system:', '["Catheter + drainage bag: closed connection","No bag clinical presentation","Open to air clinical presentation","Reusable clinical presentation","Open system clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Catheter + drainage bag: closed connection', 'Closed system: prevents ascending infection. Keep bag below bladder. Do not open connection.'),
('721442b2-acda-53e1-8955-64c4510b478b'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Urinary catheter bag should be:', '["Open clinical presentation","Above bladder clinical presentation","Below level of bladder, not touching floor, emptied regularly","On the floor clinical presentation","Changed daily clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Below level of bladder, not touching floor, emptied regularly', 'Catheter bag: gravity drainage. Never raise above bladder (reflux). Empty q8h.'),
('b8a25423-a67e-5dca-9b79-7322c0264809'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Silver alloy urinary catheters:', '["Not available","Only for men","No effect","Increase infection","Reduce CAUTI rates"]'::jsonb, 'Reduce CAUTI rates', 'Silver catheters: reduce bacteriuria in short-term use (<7 days). Cost-effective in some settings.'),
('eb220419-bad7-5e6e-9c81-3c8b61f94c90'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Prevention of IV line phlebitis:', '["No rotation clinical presentation","Change q24h clinical presentation","Largest gauge always clinical presentation","Same site forever clinical presentation","Rotate site q72-96h, use aseptic technique, smallest appropriate gauge"]'::jsonb, 'Rotate site q72-96h, use aseptic technique, smallest appropriate gauge', 'IV phlebitis: inflammation of vein. Rotate site. Remove if signs of phlebitis.'),
('2045a37e-a4ad-57c4-9036-db7e3f81339b'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'PIVC (peripheral IV) dwell time:', '["Every 24h clinical presentation clinical presentation","Remove if clinically indicated (no routine replacement). Change if phlebitis/infection","Every 7 days clinical presentation clinical presentation","Every 48h clinical presentation clinical presentation","Once a month clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Remove if clinically indicated (no routine replacement). Change if phlebitis/infection', 'PIVC: replace only if complications. Routine replacement not recommended (no benefit).'),
('83474fbb-70d6-5b13-b8bf-a5f84ce7c32f'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Intraosseous (IO) access infection risk:', '["High always clinical presentation","Only in adults clinical presentation","Low if removed within 24h. Osteomyelitis if prolonged","Only in children clinical presentation","No risk clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Low if removed within 24h. Osteomyelitis if prolonged', 'IO: for emergencies. Remove within 24h when other access obtained. Risk of infection increases with dwell time.'),
('dd359305-0e60-5de6-b7c4-8799eb85df2b'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Vaccination of healthcare workers: mandatory vaccines:', '["Only HepB clinical presentation","Only COVID clinical presentation","Only flu clinical presentation","No vaccines clinical presentation","All: Hepatitis B, influenza, MMR, varicella, Tdap, COVID-19"]'::jsonb, 'All: Hepatitis B, influenza, MMR, varicella, Tdap, COVID-19', 'HCW vaccination: protect staff and patients. HepB (mandatory if exposure risk). Influenza annually.'),
('c81c4744-0b4e-5e63-a23e-b944e9591039'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'TB screening of healthcare workers:', '["CXR only clinical presentation","No screening clinical presentation","Only if known exposure clinical presentation","Only symptoms clinical presentation","Annual symptom screen, TST (PPD) or IGRA at baseline and if exposure"]'::jsonb, 'Annual symptom screen, TST (PPD) or IGRA at baseline and if exposure', 'TB screening: baseline 2-step TST or IGRA. Annual symptom questionnaire. Post-exposure testing.'),
('ff83b984-fe8f-5daa-a46e-05af83f2abc8'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'TST (PPD) positive defined as:', '["Any induration clinical presentation",">=2 mm all clinical presentation",">=15 mm all clinical presentation",">=10 mm for HCW with no risk factors",">=5 mm all clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, '>=10 mm for HCW with no risk factors', 'TST: 5-10-15 mm cutoff based on risk. IGRA (QuantiFERON) more specific.'),
('c6862b67-0aff-506f-a9b5-6a73ecb19c93'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Latent TB infection treatment:', '["INH 6 months only","INH 9 months","Rifampin only","No treatment","MDR treatment"]'::jsonb, 'INH 9 months', 'LTBI: treat to prevent reactivation. INH 9 months. Monitor LFTs.'),
('2614f182-bfe8-5a09-8ea3-17d0a2281ff7'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'PPE for airborne precautions:', '["Surgical mask clinical presentation","Cloth mask clinical presentation","No mask clinical presentation","N95 respirator (fit-tested). Negative pressure room","Double surgical mask clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'N95 respirator (fit-tested). Negative pressure room', 'Airborne: N95/PAPR. Negative pressure room (>=12 ACH).'),
('25f97999-efb9-540d-bedb-2149d84b56f3'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'PPE for droplet precautions:', '["Cloth mask","N95","No mask","Surgical mask","Gloves only"]'::jsonb, 'Surgical mask', 'Droplet: surgical mask. Private room or cohort. Limit transport.'),
('c799e7d5-f1c6-510c-8fe2-582d020a539d'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'PPE for contact precautions:', '["Gown only clinical presentation","Mask only clinical presentation","Gloves and gown (on entry to room). Dedicated equipment","Gloves only clinical presentation","No PPE clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Gloves and gown (on entry to room). Dedicated equipment', 'Contact: gloves + gown. Remove before leaving room. Hand hygiene after removal.'),
('e9a7fc7e-76df-56b3-ad60-ac8af5b487bb'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Disposable PPE: single-use items:', '["Reuse if not soiled clinical presentation","Can be recycled clinical presentation","Wash and reuse clinical presentation","Gloves, gowns, masks - designed for single use","Reusable clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Gloves, gowns, masks - designed for single use', 'PPE: single use. Remove immediately after use. Hand hygiene after removing.'),
('ea621113-d177-539d-9e78-443d0278e96e'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Eye protection (goggles/face shield):', '["For splash risk to eyes","Optional","Not needed","For all patients","For N95 only"]'::jsonb, 'For splash risk to eyes', 'Eye protection: standard/contact/droplet. Reusable or disposable.'),
('5f73ce7e-6127-5d78-96e8-3dc2bd958929'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Powered air-purifying respirator (PAPR):', '["Same as N95 clinical presentation clinical presentation","Not effective clinical presentation clinical presentation","Less than N95 clinical presentation clinical presentation","For all use clinical presentation clinical presentation","Higher protection than N95. For high-risk aerosol-generating procedures"]'::jsonb, 'Higher protection than N95. For high-risk aerosol-generating procedures', 'PAPR: loose-fitting hood. Filtered air. Use for TB, aerosol-generating procedures on airborne cases.'),
('3019598e-3b88-5b00-b7ba-ca195d359f79'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Aerosol-generating procedures (AGP):', '["Intubation, bronchoscopy, CPR, suction, nebulization, non-invasive ventilation","Talking clinical presentation clinical presentation","Walking clinical presentation clinical presentation","Sitting clinical presentation clinical presentation","Eating clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Intubation, bronchoscopy, CPR, suction, nebulization, non-invasive ventilation', 'AGP: higher risk of airborne transmission. N95/PAPR + negative pressure room recommended.'),
('eb0dcfd5-3077-5d83-a5de-e7195bc373f9'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Aseptic non-touch technique (ANTT):', '["No field clinical presentation","Gloves only clinical presentation","Standardized framework for aseptic procedures","No technique clinical presentation","Hand washing only clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Standardized framework for aseptic procedures', 'ANTT: protect key parts/sites. Critical aseptic field (sterile fully) vs general (clean).'),
('7bd5e025-0d14-5367-a83a-28377c56db66'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Patient isolation: types:', '["Only airborne clinical presentation","Contact, droplet, airborne, protective","All same clinical presentation","No types clinical presentation","One type only clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Contact, droplet, airborne, protective', 'Isolation categories: transmission-based (contact/droplet/airborne). Protective: for immunocompromised.'),
('7e640f3e-5081-5745-a0ec-80affe4744d6'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Cohorting patients:', '["Same as isolation clinical presentation","Grouping patients with same infection in same room","No grouping clinical presentation","Not recommended clinical presentation","Increases spread clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Grouping patients with same infection in same room', 'Cohorting: if no private rooms. Same pathogen group together. With dedicated staff if possible.'),
('84a01453-0a22-53ab-869f-fb204abaae51'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Post-exposure follow-up for bloodborne pathogens:', '["Yearly testing clinical presentation","One test only clinical presentation","Only if symptoms clinical presentation","Baseline testing, PEP as indicated, serial testing at 1,3,6 months","No follow-up clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Baseline testing, PEP as indicated, serial testing at 1,3,6 months', 'Follow-up: HIV testing at 6 weeks, 3 months, 6 months. Anti-HBs for HBV. HCV RNA at 4-6 weeks.'),
('a0cd2f8b-b8e4-5d83-8183-520676901dd8'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Hepatitis B vaccine series:', '["5 doses clinical presentation clinical presentation","Annual clinical presentation clinical presentation","1 dose clinical presentation clinical presentation","3 doses (0, 1, 6 months). Check anti-HBs 1-2 months after completion","2 doses clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, '3 doses (0, 1, 6 months). Check anti-HBs 1-2 months after completion', 'HepB vaccine: >90% respond. Non-responders: revaccinate or test for HBsAg.'),
('c2067026-9efb-5441-9dab-58c1ed6a4c0d'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Post-exposure HIV prophylaxis duration:', '["6 months clinical presentation","14 days clinical presentation","3 months clinical presentation","28 days (4 weeks) of 3-drug antiretroviral therapy","7 days clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, '28 days (4 weeks) of 3-drug antiretroviral therapy', 'HIV PEP: 28 days. Start ASAP (within 2h). Adherence critical. Follow for side effects and seroconversion.'),
('f23da1f3-1a00-5483-84e5-a00b8156a994'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Hospital waste: segregation at source:', '["One bag for all clinical presentation","Different colored bags for different waste types","Sort later clinical presentation","No segregation clinical presentation","After transport clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Different colored bags for different waste types', 'Segregation: at point of generation. Minimizes infection risk and disposal costs.'),
('ad6f42f5-f18f-5eac-900a-661048568216'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Biomedical waste treatment options:', '["Only autoclaving clinical presentation","No treatment clinical presentation","Only landfill clinical presentation","Only incineration clinical presentation","Incineration, autoclaving, chemical treatment, microwave, shredding"]'::jsonb, 'Incineration, autoclaving, chemical treatment, microwave, shredding', 'Treatment: depends on waste type. Infectious -> incineration or autoclave. Sharps -> autoclave/shred.'),
('0fe8605a-c270-543b-8692-e83cb3364b76'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Incineration of biomedical waste:', '["No temperature control clinical presentation","Low temperature clinical presentation","High temperature (800-1200 C) for pathological, infectious, cytotoxic waste","Not required clinical presentation","Burns plastic only clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'High temperature (800-1200 C) for pathological, infectious, cytotoxic waste', 'Incineration: reduces waste volume, destroys pathogens. Concerns: dioxin emissions.'),
('3ecea835-1282-5e7e-973a-ca56d73ab0fd'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Common vehicle transmission in healthcare:', '["Contaminated water, food, medications, IV fluids, medical devices","Direct contact clinical presentation","Vector clinical presentation","Person to person clinical presentation","Airborne clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Contaminated water, food, medications, IV fluids, medical devices', 'Common vehicle: single contaminated source affects many (e.g., contaminated IV fluid -> BSI).'),
('b5809ccd-6c8d-54f9-ab19-3e006c95b76f'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Pediculosis (lice) outbreak control:', '["No treatment clinical presentation clinical presentation","No cleaning clinical presentation clinical presentation","Shave hair clinical presentation clinical presentation","Contact precautions, treat with permethrin, clean linens, no sharing of hats/brushes","Isolation only clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Contact precautions, treat with permethrin, clean linens, no sharing of hats/brushes', 'Lice: contact precautions. Treatment: 1% permethrin. Launder bedding/clothes in hot water.'),
('72f6ccdf-4453-5a6b-b48f-cad319c653e7'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Scabies outbreak control:', '["Contact precautions, treat with permethrin cream, treat close contacts, clean linens","Only symptomatic clinical presentation clinical presentation","No treatment clinical presentation clinical presentation","Oral antibiotics clinical presentation clinical presentation","Isolate only clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Contact precautions, treat with permethrin cream, treat close contacts, clean linens', 'Scabies: contact + gloves/gown. Topical permethrin 5% (head to toe, 8-14h). Treat all household contacts.'),
('cb3b9fc2-2f3b-5737-97b3-13208116c244'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Norovirus environmental survival:', '["Killed by alcohol clinical presentation","Killed by quats clinical presentation","Survives only hours clinical presentation","Not on surfaces clinical presentation","Survives on surfaces for days to weeks. Resistant to alcohol and quats"]'::jsonb, 'Survives on surfaces for days to weeks. Resistant to alcohol and quats', 'Norovirus: sporicidal resistance. Bleach 1000-5000 ppm. Hand washing with soap/water.'),
('cf3846df-6178-52f4-bb5c-d2646e21b58b'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Clostridium difficile spore survival:', '["Hours clinical presentation","Survives for months on surfaces (spores). Only sporicidal kills","No survival clinical presentation","Days clinical presentation","Killed by alcohol clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Survives for months on surfaces (spores). Only sporicidal kills', 'C. diff spores: months on surfaces. Bleach required. Hand washing (soap/water) removes spores mechanically.'),
('c6c14db9-fb10-5d83-95fb-ee76eebc59b7'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'CDC hand hygiene guidelines: indications include:', '["Only before patient clinical presentation clinical presentation","Before/after patient contact, before/after glove use, after body fluid exposure","Only before procedure clinical presentation clinical presentation","Only after gloves clinical presentation clinical presentation","Only after patient clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Before/after patient contact, before/after glove use, after body fluid exposure', 'Hand hygiene: WHO 5 Moments. Also before eating, after using restroom, after touching own body.'),
('dff6cc0b-80f5-507d-a6c4-9975376ed5a8'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Hand rub technique duration is:', '["20-30 seconds for alcohol rub","5 seconds","10 seconds","1 minute","No time"]'::jsonb, '20-30 seconds for alcohol rub', 'Alcohol rub: apply enough to cover all hand surfaces. Rub until dry.'),
('e9b15d33-81ba-578d-8775-e2eba2b5b885'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Artificial nails and nail polish in healthcare:', '["No impact clinical presentation","Only nail polish allowed clinical presentation","Artificial nails not allowed in direct patient care","No restriction clinical presentation","Allowed clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Artificial nails not allowed in direct patient care', 'Artificial nails: harbor bacteria, linked to outbreaks (Pseudomonas in NICU). No extensions/gels for HCWs.'),
('97749f2c-1537-54bc-a074-dc2c82c296e7'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Jewelry and watches in clinical areas:', '["Allowed clinical presentation clinical presentation","Watches allowed clinical presentation clinical presentation","No restriction clinical presentation clinical presentation","Remove all hand/wrist jewelry (rings, watches, bracelets) for hand hygiene efficacy","One ring only clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Remove all hand/wrist jewelry (rings, watches, bracelets) for hand hygiene efficacy', 'Jewelry: impairs hand hygiene. Fingernail length <0.5 cm. No chipped nail polish.'),
('cc98dada-1f55-5e00-848c-2b992a9b5b82'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Surgical wound class: If elective colonic resection (no spillage):', '["Not classified","Clean","Clean-contaminated","Dirty","Contaminated"]'::jsonb, 'Clean-contaminated', 'Class II: entry into GI tract under controlled conditions. Prophylactic antibiotics indicated.'),
('8fb83025-9f3d-564a-9f07-ddd2fb05b6a5'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Surgical wound class: If purulent peritonitis found at operation:', '["Dirty-infected","Clean","Not classified","Contaminated","Clean-contaminated"]'::jsonb, 'Dirty-infected', 'Class IV: existing infection. Therapeutic antibiotics (not just prophylactic).'),
('6cc55796-164e-5cbe-819f-693de1b5c58a'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'ASA class 5 (moribund):', '["Not expected to survive 24h without surgery","Life-threatening clinical presentation","Healthy clinical presentation","Severe disease clinical presentation","Mild disease clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Not expected to survive 24h without surgery', 'ASA 5: moribund. Expected mortality >50%. Surgery as last resort.'),
('138226d5-fa35-573a-bcc5-3b01b9c51df7'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Prophylactic antibiotic: single dose vs multiple:', '["Single dose sufficient for most clean and clean-contaminated surgeries","No difference clinical presentation","Always multiple doses clinical presentation","7 days needed clinical presentation","Multiple only for dirty clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Single dose sufficient for most clean and clean-contaminated surgeries', 'Prophylaxis: single dose preop. Multiple doses if prolonged (>4h) or major blood loss.'),
('23735e05-7127-5358-882d-c13c3c729828'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Ventilator circuit change frequency:', '["Weekly","After each patient","Do not change routinely","Daily","q48h"]'::jsonb, 'Do not change routinely', 'Vent circuit: closed circuit. Extended use (up to 7 days). No benefit from frequent change.'),
('5d616f03-991a-5c22-8b77-a1e391c9d513'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Closed suction system vs open suction:', '["Closed more contamination clinical presentation","Same clinical presentation","Closed: no disconnection of ventilator, less contamination, no change q24h","No difference clinical presentation","Open better clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Closed: no disconnection of ventilator, less contamination, no change q24h', 'Closed suction: inline, no ventilator disconnect. Reduces VAP? Less hypoxemia during suction.'),
('455d824f-f072-5812-8ef3-0a2b65e43c3b'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Chlorhexidine mouthwash (0.12%) for VAP prevention:', '["Q6-8h oral care for ventilated patients","No effect clinical presentation","Only toothbrushing clinical presentation","No oral care clinical presentation","Once daily clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Q6-8h oral care for ventilated patients', 'Oral CHG: integral to VAP bundle. Together with toothbrushing and subglottic suctioning.'),
('b3bfb134-3945-5f37-87ea-de97dfea17b4'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Hand hygiene before wearing gloves:', '["Wash after only","No need","Perform hand hygiene even if gloves will be worn","Only after gloves","Gloves replace hand hygiene"]'::jsonb, 'Perform hand hygiene even if gloves will be worn', 'Gloves: hand hygiene before donning and after removal. Gloves can have micropunctures.'),
('d2288494-3094-5f55-bdfd-ea0b3f1c7eef'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Latex allergy in healthcare:', '["Contact dermatitis only clinical presentation","Not serious clinical presentation","No alternative clinical presentation","Type IV only clinical presentation","Type I (immediate IgE) hypersensitivity to latex proteins. Use non-latex gloves"]'::jsonb, 'Type I (immediate IgE) hypersensitivity to latex proteins. Use non-latex gloves', 'Latex allergy: from powdered gloves. Use powder-free, low-protein latex or non-latex (nitrile, neoprene).'),
('f209db20-79fe-5659-ac6e-2e90b9e2d51d'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Antimicrobial stewardship: de-escalation example:', '["Stop entirely clinical presentation clinical presentation","Add another agent clinical presentation clinical presentation","Continue same clinical presentation clinical presentation","No change clinical presentation clinical presentation","Change piperacillin-tazobactam to cefazolin based on E. coli sensitive to cefazolin"]'::jsonb, 'Change piperacillin-tazobactam to cefazolin based on E. coli sensitive to cefazolin', 'De-escalation: narrow spectrum based on culture/sensitivity. Example: anti-pseudomonal to 1st gen cephalosporin.'),
('6c8fb70d-edd8-507c-a3db-1894b8bf2106'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Duration of antibiotic therapy for intra-abdominal infection:', '["24h only clinical presentation","14 days minimum clinical presentation","Until drain removed clinical presentation","4-7 days (source control achieved). Extended not beneficial","21 days clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, '4-7 days (source control achieved). Extended not beneficial', 'Antibiotic duration: short course (4-7 days) if source controlled. Extended not needed.'),
('1b469efd-1e63-5f92-8aed-96f9b68b879a'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Procalcitonin (PCT) to guide antibiotic duration:', '["Low PCT suggests stop antibiotics","Not useful","No PCT in infection","PCT unreliable","Must treat until PCT normal"]'::jsonb, 'Low PCT suggests stop antibiotics', 'PCT: down-trending suggests infection controlled. Use to discontinue antibiotics safely.'),
('2f8dda4a-94c5-5ab8-a16e-80cfff06cc0d'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Managing asymptomatic bacteriuria:', '["Do not screen or treat","Screen and treat","Treat for 3 days","Treat for 7 days","Treat always"]'::jsonb, 'Do not screen or treat', 'ASB: common in elderly, catheters. Antibiotic treatment not beneficial (increases resistance).'),
('7985b8f2-a70a-5f56-96fe-c13ecf0eb67f'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Foley catheter insertion: aseptic technique includes:', '["Clean gloves clinical presentation","No drape clinical presentation","Sterile gloves, sterile drape, CHG prep, lubricant","Tap water prep clinical presentation","No lubrication clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Sterile gloves, sterile drape, CHG prep, lubricant', 'Catheter insertion: sterile technique throughout. Closed system. Inflate balloon with sterile water.'),
('40989e4b-af46-5e0d-a5cb-e06db4e88228'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Surgical hand scrub: duration for subsequent cases:', '["No scrub between","5-10 minutes each","10 minutes only first case","2-3 minutes","30 seconds"]'::jsonb, '2-3 minutes', 'Subsequent scrub: shorter (2-3 min) if within same OR with no soiled hand contamination.'),
('50c4bd85-2adc-5731-859c-5fd5bb32090e'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'The infection control risk assessment (ICRA):', '["Annual only clinical presentation","Identifies risks for infection transmission, guides prevention strategies","Only for outbreaks clinical presentation","Only for construction clinical presentation","Not needed clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Identifies risks for infection transmission, guides prevention strategies', 'ICRA: systematic assessment. Includes patient population, procedures, environment, staffing.'),
('bdedd434-8a44-50bb-8bd5-9bc718091bca'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Construction/renovation infection control:', '["No filtration clinical presentation clinical presentation","Open construction clinical presentation clinical presentation","No controls clinical presentation clinical presentation","No barriers clinical presentation clinical presentation","HEPA filtration, negative pressure in construction area, barrier, monitoring air quality"]'::jsonb, 'HEPA filtration, negative pressure in construction area, barrier, monitoring air quality', 'Construction: protect immunocompromised patients from dust (Aspergillus). Containment, HEPA, barriers.'),
('f3ea8160-6fe9-5cb1-aba0-8ea31f625fa4'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Waterborne pathogens in healthcare:', '["Legionella, Pseudomonas, Mycobacterium avium, Aspergillus","Only viruses clinical presentation","Only fungi clinical presentation","No waterborne risk clinical presentation","Only bacteria clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Legionella, Pseudomonas, Mycobacterium avium, Aspergillus', 'Waterborne: Legionella (cooling towers, water systems). Pseudomonas (sinks, faucets). Prevent with water treatment.'),
('3a2ee617-8571-52ce-bbca-7669517eceae'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Legionella prevention in hospitals:', '["No prevention clinical presentation clinical presentation","Filter all water clinical presentation clinical presentation","Maintain hot water >60 C, cold <20 C, regular testing, chlorination/copper-silver ionization","Only for transplant units clinical presentation clinical presentation","Cold water only clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Maintain hot water >60 C, cold <20 C, regular testing, chlorination/copper-silver ionization', 'Legionella: causes pneumonia in immunocompromised. Hospital water systems: maintain temperature, disinfect.'),
('dcf0e318-52b1-5862-867e-778b40194cd9'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Infection control in immunocompromised patients:', '["No special precautions clinical presentation","Protective isolation, positive pressure, HEPA filtration, avoid live vaccines","Standard only clinical presentation","Airborne only clinical presentation","Contact only clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Protective isolation, positive pressure, HEPA filtration, avoid live vaccines', 'Immunocompromised: protective environment (positive pressure, HEPA, frequent air changes). No flowers/plants.'),
('0fce77a3-7585-596b-8ddb-2a635dd5d07a'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'CMV prevention in transplant:', '["CMV vaccine clinical presentation","No blood transfusion clinical presentation","CMV-negative or leukoreduced blood products, valganciclovir prophylaxis","Isolation clinical presentation","No prevention clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'CMV-negative or leukoreduced blood products, valganciclovir prophylaxis', 'CMV: donor/recipient matching. Prophylaxis with valganciclovir. Monitor CMV PCR.'),
('8e8f133b-fcb8-5ca7-b7a7-48150b44d753'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Aspergillus prevention in neutropenia:', '["HEPA filtration, positive pressure, no construction dust, avoid plants/flowers","Standard ventilation clinical presentation clinical presentation","No prevention clinical presentation clinical presentation","Antibiotics clinical presentation clinical presentation","Contact precautions clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'HEPA filtration, positive pressure, no construction dust, avoid plants/flowers', 'Aspergillus: airborne fungus. High risk in prolonged neutropenia. Protective environment critical.'),
('9150ae0d-6822-5529-a1de-b4e303d593e0'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'MDR TB definition:', '["Susceptible TB clinical presentation","Resistant to 1 drug clinical presentation","Resistant to 4 drugs clinical presentation","TB resistant to at least isoniazid and rifampin","Resistant to any 2 clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'TB resistant to at least isoniazid and rifampin', 'MDR-TB: resistant to INH+RIF. XDR-TB: + FQ + injectable. Airborne. Prolonged treatment.'),
('778c550e-aaf0-5710-9c5b-b96db64fb883'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'TB airborne precautions duration:', '["Never discontinue clinical presentation","Until discharge clinical presentation","Effective treatment 2 weeks AND clinical improvement AND 3 negative sputum smears","Only if smear positive clinical presentation","Isolation for 2 weeks regardless clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Effective treatment 2 weeks AND clinical improvement AND 3 negative sputum smears', 'TB: airborne until on effective therapy, improved, and smears negative. Usually 2-4 weeks.'),
('9ab531f7-2161-586a-92ae-20cd501f4c17'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Mpox (formerly monkeypox) infection control:', '["Contact only clinical presentation clinical presentation","Contact + droplet precautions. Private room. PPE including gown, gloves, N95, eye protection","Airborne only clinical presentation clinical presentation","Standard only clinical presentation clinical presentation","Droplet only clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Contact + droplet precautions. Private room. PPE including gown, gloves, N95, eye protection', 'Mpox: contact + droplet. Lesions highly infectious. Smallpox vaccine effective.'),
('028044e4-b661-5c56-adbc-ee30d405da7a'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'SARS-CoV-2 (COVID-19) transmission:', '["Vector-borne clinical presentation clinical presentation","Fecal-oral clinical presentation clinical presentation","Contact only clinical presentation clinical presentation","Droplet only clinical presentation clinical presentation","Droplet and airborne (aerosol-generating procedures high risk). N95 preferred"]'::jsonb, 'Droplet and airborne (aerosol-generating procedures high risk). N95 preferred', 'COVID-19: primarily droplet. Airborne precautions during aerosol-generating procedures. N95 for HCWs.'),
('f3e85f58-294e-59ee-b3ed-a72e1b77d261'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'COVID-19: source control:', '["Masks only for suspected cases","Masks only in ICU","Masks only for HCWs","No masks","Masks for everyone in healthcare facilities"]'::jsonb, 'Masks for everyone in healthcare facilities', 'Source control: patient/caregiver masking. Prevents transmission from asymptomatic/presymptomatic individuals.'),
('ba7a82d4-6ec0-5d1c-a596-f2e65303684b'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Influenza infection control:', '["Contact clinical presentation clinical presentation","No treatment clinical presentation clinical presentation","Airborne clinical presentation clinical presentation","Droplet precautions. Private room or cohort. Early antiviral (oseltamivir) treatment","No precautions clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Droplet precautions. Private room or cohort. Early antiviral (oseltamivir) treatment', 'Influenza: droplet + standard. Annual vaccination for HCWs. Antivirals within 48h of symptoms.'),
('9e26c675-b0f8-5080-be07-8dd3ebfc2f47'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Ebola virus disease (EVD):', '["Contact only clinical presentation","Airborne only clinical presentation","Droplet only clinical presentation","Contact + droplet precautions. Full PPE","Standard only clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Contact + droplet precautions. Full PPE', 'Ebola: strict contact + droplet. Enhanced PPE. Deadly. Strict hand hygiene. Waste management critical.'),
('87246679-8102-5220-9fce-40d427e99458'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Infection control in resource-limited settings:', '["Only in OR clinical presentation clinical presentation","Same as high-resource clinical presentation clinical presentation","Not possible clinical presentation clinical presentation","No precautions possible clinical presentation clinical presentation","Focus on hand hygiene, safe injections, standard precautions, waste management, TB controls"]'::jsonb, 'Focus on hand hygiene, safe injections, standard precautions, waste management, TB controls', 'Resource-limited: basic infection control critical. Low-cost interventions (hand washing, safe water).'),
('c1d8511c-f4f5-59c0-8984-3cf2c0126495'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'The WHO multimodal hand hygiene improvement strategy:', '["System change, training, evaluation, reminders, institutional safety climate","One intervention clinical presentation clinical presentation","Only evaluation clinical presentation clinical presentation","Only training clinical presentation clinical presentation","No strategy clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'System change, training, evaluation, reminders, institutional safety climate', 'WHO strategy: 5 components. Improves compliance sustainably. ''Clean Care is Safer Care''.'),
('04e181fc-1b92-517b-8a91-643d6ac02d0a'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Clean Care is Safer Care (WHO patient safety program):', '["No effect clinical presentation","Only in OR clinical presentation","Not active clinical presentation","Only in Europe clinical presentation","Global initiative to improve hand hygiene and reduce HAIs"]'::jsonb, 'Global initiative to improve hand hygiene and reduce HAIs', 'Clean Care: WHO Patient Safety. May 5 = Save Lives: Hand Hygiene Day.'),
('b5b55447-bafc-50d5-80fb-ccf1544bc74b'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Infection control link nurse program:', '["Designated ward nurse trained in IC","Not effective clinical presentation","For doctors only clinical presentation","No training needed clinical presentation","Optional clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Designated ward nurse trained in IC', 'Link nurse: bridge between IC team and wards. Improves compliance, education, and surveillance.'),
('c964be7d-e2ea-5fc1-ad04-cbf483063293'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Point prevalence survey (PPS) for HAI:', '["Snapshot of HAI prevalence on a given day","Incidence study clinical presentation","Yearly only clinical presentation","Only for outbreaks clinical presentation","Not useful clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Snapshot of HAI prevalence on a given day', 'PPS: cross-sectional. Measures burden of HAI. ECDC protocol standardized.'),
('e32585c7-d27d-5c7f-8e01-bf0aef514551'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Standardized Infection Ratio (SIR):', '["Simple infection rate","Not adjusted","Observed HAIs / predicted HAIs","Predicted only","Observed only"]'::jsonb, 'Observed HAIs / predicted HAIs', 'SIR: observed/predicted. SIR >1 = higher than predicted. Used by NHSN for benchmarking.'),
('d41a7118-7af8-5049-bff6-ed0880e32274'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Device utilization ratio:', '["Not useful","Only for CLABSI","Infections/device","Device-days / patient-days","Devices/patient"]'::jsonb, 'Device-days / patient-days', 'Device utilization: proxy for risk. Higher utilization -> higher risk of device-associated infection.'),
('d3ebc272-8df0-5b72-a7a5-4d7f0025910f'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Antimicrobial consumption measurement:', '["Total grams clinical presentation","Cost only clinical presentation","Total doses clinical presentation","Defined daily dose (DDD) per 1000 patient-days","Total courses clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Defined daily dose (DDD) per 1000 patient-days', 'DDD: standardized measure. Compare antimicrobial use across hospitals/units. WHO ATC index.'),
('fc32a837-55ff-5309-b590-227af62f4411'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'The role of hand hygiene in reducing C-section SSI:', '["Only in labor clinical presentation","Not relevant clinical presentation","Preoperative CHG shower, hand hygiene before/after wound care","No role clinical presentation","Only postpartum clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Preoperative CHG shower, hand hygiene before/after wound care', 'Hand hygiene: critical for wound care. Clean hands before dressing change = fewer SSIs.'),
('014da338-8e33-557e-b086-9e76a7efde6a'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Wearing gloves does NOT replace:', '["Aseptic technique","Mask","Gown","Eye protection","Hand hygiene"]'::jsonb, 'Hand hygiene', 'Gloves: hand hygiene before donning, after removal. Never wash/gloves reuse.'),
('dfebb82f-01f0-5f92-9973-1990c41d2749'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Standard infection control precautions (SICPs):', '["Hand hygiene, PPE, safe waste, safe injection, respiratory hygiene, environmental cleaning","Only injection clinical presentation clinical presentation","Only PPE clinical presentation clinical presentation","Only waste clinical presentation clinical presentation","Only hand hygiene clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Hand hygiene, PPE, safe waste, safe injection, respiratory hygiene, environmental cleaning', 'SICPs: baseline precautions for all patients. Combination of measures as risk dictates.'),
('bfd9ce3f-6bbc-58d8-9e82-29577bad3f45'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Transmission-based precautions (TBPs) duration:', '["For entire duration of illness","First 24h only","Until cultures negative","Only during symptoms","Until discharge"]'::jsonb, 'For entire duration of illness', 'TBPs: duration varies. MRSA: duration of hospitalization. C. diff: until diarrhea resolved. TB: until 3 neg smears.'),
('33f3937c-e733-590d-b0f2-f98b0442c2a0'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Cleaning verification methods:', '["Culture only clinical presentation","No verification clinical presentation","ATP only clinical presentation","Visual inspection, ATP bioluminescence, fluorescent markers","Visual only clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Visual inspection, ATP bioluminescence, fluorescent markers', 'Cleaning verification: visual inadequate. ATP (relative light units) and fluorescent marker systems validate cleaning.'),
('b0d4919c-be99-59a9-b68d-12148c72b6d2'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'The World Health Organization (WHO) hand hygiene observation tool:', '["Optional clinical presentation","Standardized method to monitor hand hygiene compliance","No standard clinical presentation","For research only clinical presentation","Not used clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Standardized method to monitor hand hygiene compliance', 'WHO observation tool: validated. Trained observers document compliance. Provides feedback for improvement.'),
('6a4dd13a-13e2-5524-b9d0-632b74f59665'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Patient zone and healthcare zone (WHO hand hygiene):', '["No zone clinical presentation clinical presentation","OR only clinical presentation clinical presentation","Ward only clinical presentation clinical presentation","Patient zone: patient and immediate surroundings. Healthcare zone: rest of facility","Same zone clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Patient zone: patient and immediate surroundings. Healthcare zone: rest of facility', 'Patient zone: items touched during patient care. Hand hygiene when entering/leaving patient zone.'),
('883d6ff6-b8c8-5cc4-ac45-fdb12c635ae2'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Healthcare worker (HCW) immunization: Influenza vaccination:', '["Once in lifetime clinical presentation","Only high-risk HCWs clinical presentation","Only in pandemic clinical presentation","Annual (reduces HCW illness, patient exposure, and mortality in elderly","Not needed clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Annual (reduces HCW illness, patient exposure, and mortality in elderly', 'Flu vaccine: annual. Mandatory in many hospitals with declination forms. Protects vulnerable patients.'),
('0147dbf0-f6ea-5162-84d5-5d56e007d9be'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Varicella (chickenpox) exposure in hospital:', '["Only treat patients clinical presentation clinical presentation","No prophylaxis clinical presentation clinical presentation","All HCWs can work clinical presentation clinical presentation","Susceptible HCWs excluded from duty from day 8-21 post-exposure. VZIG for high-risk patients","No action clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Susceptible HCWs excluded from duty from day 8-21 post-exposure. VZIG for high-risk patients', 'Varicella: airborne + contact. HCW without evidence of immunity furloughed days 8-21. VZIG within 96h for susceptible high-risk patients.'),
('23216f64-99ce-5495-a378-525e016a92d7'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Measles exposure in hospital:', '["No action clinical presentation clinical presentation","No vaccine clinical presentation clinical presentation","Airborne precautions. MMR within 72h for susceptible contacts. HCW immunity verification","Droplet only clinical presentation clinical presentation","Contact only clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Airborne precautions. MMR within 72h for susceptible contacts. HCW immunity verification', 'Measles: highly contagious airborne. MMR vaccine 2 doses for HCWs born after 1957. Outbreak: exclude non-immune HCWs.'),
('82d7b69f-0c72-577f-b950-8c993c0e1ea2'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Pertussis (whooping cough) HCW protection:', '["Only children vaccinated clinical presentation","Only Td clinical presentation","Tdap vaccine (every 10 years). Droplet + contact precautions for active cases","DT only clinical presentation","No vaccine clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Tdap vaccine (every 10 years). Droplet + contact precautions for active cases', 'Pertussis: droplet. HCW vaccination with Tdap protects vulnerable infants. Post-exposure prophylaxis (azithromycin).'),
('ae62874b-c8b4-5095-b74f-99377a681e07'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Cytomegalovirus (CMV) in pregnancy:', '["CMV-negative pregnant HCWs should avoid care of known CMV-positive patients","Universal precautions sufficient clinical presentation","No risk clinical presentation","Only for non-pregnant clinical presentation","No precautions needed clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'CMV-negative pregnant HCWs should avoid care of known CMV-positive patients', 'CMV: prenatal infection can cause congenital defects. Standard precautions + hand hygiene protective.'),
('273fcb21-c8cf-5057-9943-b5ba0163bf40'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Pregnancy and infection control:', '["Only avoid TB clinical presentation clinical presentation","Only avoid COVID clinical presentation clinical presentation","All duties safe clinical presentation clinical presentation","No restriction clinical presentation clinical presentation","Avoid care of patients with CMV, rubella, varicella, parvovirus B19 if non-immune"]'::jsonb, 'Avoid care of patients with CMV, rubella, varicella, parvovirus B19 if non-immune', 'Pregnancy: risk assessment. Reassign from high-risk situations if non-immune.'),
('e309eb1c-17cf-54e2-ad28-620ab65af707'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Infection prevention during wound care:', '["Sterile for all clinical presentation clinical presentation","No technique clinical presentation clinical presentation","Same for all clinical presentation clinical presentation","Clean for all clinical presentation clinical presentation","Clean technique for chronic wounds, sterile technique for surgical wounds"]'::jsonb, 'Clean technique for chronic wounds, sterile technique for surgical wounds', 'Wound care: acute surgical wounds (sterile), chronic wounds (clean technique). Hand hygiene before/after.'),
('6cb99a58-9b39-53e4-9fc3-10c56d035969'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Dressing change: aseptic technique:', '["Sterile gloves for dressing of surgical wounds","Bare hands clinical presentation","No gloves clinical presentation","Clean gloves clinical presentation","Only forceps clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Sterile gloves for dressing of surgical wounds', 'Dressing: clean environment, sterile/unused dressing. Minimal wound disturbance.'),
('dd212d1b-01de-55f1-988d-52d743e13765'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Wound swab culture: indication:', '["At every dressing change","Only post-op day 1","All wounds","No wounds","Clinical signs of infection"]'::jsonb, 'Clinical signs of infection', 'Wound culture: only if infected. Clean wound before swabbing. Swab purulent exudate, not surface.'),
('2b339388-6455-5dc5-a90a-3200220ba2e5'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Pus culture vs tissue culture:', '["Tissue/deep wound culture more accurate than surface swab","Same clinical presentation","Swab better clinical presentation","No difference clinical presentation","Blood culture only clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Tissue/deep wound culture more accurate than surface swab', 'Tissue culture: better sensitivity and specificity. Debride necrotic tissue before culture.'),
('e512b858-a422-52d5-9ed7-17fdf43eecaf'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Biofilm in chronic wounds:', '["Killed by antibiotics clinical presentation clinical presentation","Not present clinical presentation clinical presentation","No treatment needed clinical presentation clinical presentation","Organized microbial community within matrix. Resistant to antibiotics/antiseptics.","Same as planktonic clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Organized microbial community within matrix. Resistant to antibiotics/antiseptics.', 'Biofilm: require debridement (physical removal). Antimicrobial dressings (silver, iodine) may suppress.'),
('10d8488f-f1a8-50b4-bf2c-ecf4baec9475'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Negative-pressure wound therapy (NPWT) infection risk:', '["Same clinical presentation clinical presentation","Lower than standard dressings (closed system). Monitor for infection underneath","Higher risk clinical presentation clinical presentation","Not studied clinical presentation clinical presentation","No difference clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Lower than standard dressings (closed system). Monitor for infection underneath', 'NPWT: closed system reduces contamination. Change foam q48-72h. If purulence, stop NPWT.'),
('f461a488-9b3d-553c-93e9-6445811cb5c7'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Precautions for patient with draining wound:', '["No precautions clinical presentation clinical presentation","Airborne clinical presentation clinical presentation","Droplet clinical presentation clinical presentation","Standard only clinical presentation clinical presentation","Contact precautions (gloves, gown) if drainage is not contained by dressing"]'::jsonb, 'Contact precautions (gloves, gown) if drainage is not contained by dressing', 'Draining wound: contained by dressing = standard. Uncontained (soaking) = contact precautions.'),
('3b026dce-88be-5f66-8e19-72131bddef87'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'NSI (needlestick): most common location of injury:', '["Face","Arm","Hand","Foot","Leg"]'::jsonb, 'Hand', 'NSI: hand most common. Non-dominant hand during recapping or passing.'),
('56978d58-e371-55ef-87df-fd8addb1c99d'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Blunt-tip suture needles:', '["Same risk","Sharper","Not effective","Reduce needlestick injury","More needles"]'::jsonb, 'Reduce needlestick injury', 'Blunt-tip needles: 50-80% reduction in NSI. Recommended for closure of muscle/fascia.'),
('f0e3a5b9-a4f1-512b-ac0a-6be891b3d60e'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Neutral zone (hands-free) technique:', '["Passing sharp instruments on a tray/magnet","Increases risk clinical presentation","Slows surgery clinical presentation","No benefit clinical presentation","Not recommended clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Passing sharp instruments on a tray/magnet', 'Neutral zone: eliminate concurrent hand-to-hand passing of sharps. Reduces SSI/NSI.'),
('d1ef2e49-bf73-5e90-86be-b20200209720'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Bundles in infection control:', '["Single intervention clinical presentation","No evidence clinical presentation","Small set of evidence-based practices that when implemented together improve outcomes","Multiple unrelated interventions clinical presentation","Optional clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Small set of evidence-based practices that when implemented together improve outcomes', 'Bundles: 3-5 elements. Implementation as whole improves outcomes more than individual elements.'),
('492addbc-b1f3-5597-a4f2-02f8234da4f2'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'CLABSI bundle:', '["Only barriers clinical presentation clinical presentation","Only hand hygiene clinical presentation clinical presentation","Only daily review clinical presentation clinical presentation","Hand hygiene, maximal barriers, CHG skin prep, femoral avoidance, daily line necessity review","Only CHG clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Hand hygiene, maximal barriers, CHG skin prep, femoral avoidance, daily line necessity review', 'CLABSI bundle: 5 elements. Sustained reduction in CLABSI with bundle adherence.'),
('a20fd527-fb89-5905-b219-f74c20b8f713'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'VAP bundle:', '["Only sedation clinical presentation clinical presentation","Only oral care clinical presentation clinical presentation","Only HOB clinical presentation clinical presentation","HOB elevation 30-45, sedation vacation, DVT/PE prophylaxis, peptic ulcer prophylaxis, oral CHG","Only DVT prophylaxis clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'HOB elevation 30-45, sedation vacation, DVT/PE prophylaxis, peptic ulcer prophylaxis, oral CHG', 'VAP bundle: 5 elements. Originally 4 (added oral care). Reduces VAP, duration of ventilation.'),
('f52e05e1-2ccd-5c41-a246-feed48443643'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'SSI bundle:', '["Only normothermia clinical presentation clinical presentation","Only hair removal clinical presentation clinical presentation","Only glucose clinical presentation clinical presentation","Only antibiotics clinical presentation clinical presentation","Appropriate antibiotic prophylaxis, normothermia, glycemic control, hair removal"]'::jsonb, 'Appropriate antibiotic prophylaxis, normothermia, glycemic control, hair removal', 'SSI bundle: multiple elements. Each element proven to reduce SSI. Implementation improves outcomes.'),
('d27056b9-48ee-57a2-ae5f-8283c7b08af0'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Compliance with infection control bundles:', '["Not measured","Partial credit","No standard","Measured as all-or-none","Optional"]'::jsonb, 'Measured as all-or-none', 'All-or-none: strictest measure. Ensures all evidence-based elements for each patient.'),
('05804ccd-6b8d-5555-b054-aa0370c27caa'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Zero CLABSI/VAP goal:', '["No evidence clinical presentation clinical presentation","Only theoretical clinical presentation clinical presentation","Elimination target (not zero tolerance for non-compliance). Achievable with sustained bundle adhe","Not possible clinical presentation clinical presentation","Unrealistic clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Elimination target (not zero tolerance for non-compliance). Achievable with sustained bundle adhe', 'Zero: aspirational target. Many ICUs achieved >1 year zero CLABSI with bundle adherence.'),
('fb4dffef-aa4c-5132-91c0-696a5adffe16'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'The role of hospital epidemiology:', '["Only for research clinical presentation","Only during outbreaks clinical presentation","Systematic study of HAIs to identify risk factors, trends, and interventions","Only for TB clinical presentation","Not needed clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Systematic study of HAIs to identify risk factors, trends, and interventions', 'Hospital epidemiology: surveillance, outbreak investigation, prevention research, policy development.'),
('664cf9f7-ace2-5c0a-b951-7594cb4b84c0'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Automated surveillance for HAIs:', '["Inaccurate clinical presentation clinical presentation","Electronic algorithms using lab, pharmacy, and admission data to flag potential HAIs","Not used clinical presentation clinical presentation","Not reliable clinical presentation clinical presentation","Manual only clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Electronic algorithms using lab, pharmacy, and admission data to flag potential HAIs', 'Automated: improves efficiency, sensitivity. Reduces manual chart review burden. NHSN definitions.'),
('750ff16d-ad68-5e0d-a8cf-0cb266f4a78c'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Whole genome sequencing (WGS) for outbreak investigation:', '["Not available clinical presentation","Not useful clinical presentation","Determines genetic relatedness of isolates","Too slow clinical presentation","Same as PFGE clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Determines genetic relatedness of isolates', 'WGS: high resolution. Replaces PFGE. Determines if cases are linked (transmission) vs unrelated.'),
('896951aa-95cb-509b-97f7-69757da441be'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Infection prevention during multiresistant organism outbreak:', '["Standard precautions only clinical presentation clinical presentation","Only antibiotics clinical presentation clinical presentation","Enhanced contact precautions, cohort, dedicated staff, daily CHG baths, environmental cultures","No change clinical presentation clinical presentation","Terminal cleaning only clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Enhanced contact precautions, cohort, dedicated staff, daily CHG baths, environmental cultures', 'MDR outbreak: intensified measures. Screen contacts. Decolonization. Possibly unit closure.'),
('c9826cf5-e4eb-5162-bb37-6e688c4e7a74'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'When to close a unit due to outbreak:', '["Immediately with first case clinical presentation","Uncontrolled transmission despite enhanced measures","Only for TB clinical presentation","Never clinical presentation","Only for C. diff clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Uncontrolled transmission despite enhanced measures', 'Unit closure: last resort. When ongoing transmission cannot be controlled with existing measures.'),
('d3f309b7-fe54-5026-8a5a-1099c4c8454d'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Infection control education for HCWs:', '["Self-study clinical presentation","No ongoing education clinical presentation","Initial orientation + annual updates + just-in-time training","Only on hire clinical presentation","Only for doctors clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Initial orientation + annual updates + just-in-time training', 'Education: orientation, annual competency, updates for new guidelines. Adult learning principles.'),
('dbe48b71-9483-53ac-9a38-abbfcd32d6fe'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Patient education on infection prevention:', '["Only at discharge clinical presentation","Not needed clinical presentation","Only for MRSA clinical presentation","Only for surgical patients clinical presentation","Hand hygiene, respiratory etiquette, vaccination, wound care, reporting symptoms"]'::jsonb, 'Hand hygiene, respiratory etiquette, vaccination, wound care, reporting symptoms', 'Patient education: empower patients to participate in their own safety. Ask about hand hygiene.'),
('e204c20f-4ce6-5412-9da8-1b851726431e'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Culture of safety in infection control:', '["HCWs only clinical presentation","Top-down only clinical presentation","Leadership commitment, blame-free reporting, teamwork, accountability","No culture needed clinical presentation","Blame individuals clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Leadership commitment, blame-free reporting, teamwork, accountability', 'Safety culture: just culture (no blame for human error but accountability for reckless behavior).'),
('f69794a6-baf4-5897-963b-ddbd08abe4d7'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Just culture in healthcare:', '["Blame all clinical presentation","Punish errors clinical presentation","No distinction clinical presentation","No accountability clinical presentation","Distinguishes human error, at-risk behavior, and reckless behavior"]'::jsonb, 'Distinguishes human error, at-risk behavior, and reckless behavior', 'Just culture: error (console/redesign), at-risk behavior (coach), reckless behavior (discipline).'),
('3eb01263-00fa-59ba-aef7-a80488eddd89'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Speaking up for safety:', '["Only senior clinical presentation clinical presentation","Only doctors clinical presentation clinical presentation","Every team member can and must stop the line if safety concern","Discouraged clinical presentation clinical presentation","Not needed clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Every team member can and must stop the line if safety concern', 'Speaking up: flatten hierarchy. Empower all team members to identify and correct safety risks.'),
('a09590e2-3903-5401-bdca-d8f96c9b116c'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Safety huddle (briefing) in infection control:', '["Weekly clinical presentation clinical presentation","Daily team huddle: discuss patients with isolation, devices, infections, prevention","For OR only clinical presentation clinical presentation","Not effective clinical presentation clinical presentation","Not needed clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Daily team huddle: discuss patients with isolation, devices, infections, prevention', 'Safety huddle: brief (10 min) daily. Situational awareness. Plan and allocate resources for infection prevention.'),
('ba0cd3b2-6770-5305-88a0-19ada3e114c5'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'High reliability organization (HRO) principles in infection control:', '["No benefit clinical presentation clinical presentation","Not applicable clinical presentation clinical presentation","Preoccupation with failure, reluctance to simplify, sensitivity to operations","Only for aviation clinical presentation clinical presentation","Standard management clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Preoccupation with failure, reluctance to simplify, sensitivity to operations', 'HRO: infection control HRO principles detect and mitigate risks before adverse outcomes.'),
('7179fcbe-72d7-5cd3-9a10-5c09aeaef55f'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Root cause analysis (RCA) for infection event:', '["Treat cause as multifactorial clinical presentation clinical presentation","Blame person clinical presentation clinical presentation","No action clinical presentation clinical presentation","Systematic investigation of serious HAI to identify contributing factors and corrective actions","No investigation clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Systematic investigation of serious HAI to identify contributing factors and corrective actions', 'RCA: identify system failures, not individual blame. Develop action plan. Monitor improvement.'),
('079bea07-5835-57fc-a2a9-437313a4a7a9'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Failure mode and effects analysis (FMEA):', '["Not used clinical presentation clinical presentation","Proactive assessment of potential failures in a process before they occur","Same as RCA clinical presentation clinical presentation","Reactive only clinical presentation clinical presentation","Only for devices clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Proactive assessment of potential failures in a process before they occur', 'FMEA: identify failure modes -> assign severity/occurrence/detection scores -> prioritize -> redesign.'),
('48c83166-81e4-52ac-8312-600876de176e'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Plan-Do-Study-Act (PDSA) cycle in infection control:', '["No cycle clinical presentation clinical presentation","Quality improvement method: test change on small scale, study, adjust, then implement broadly","Plan only clinical presentation clinical presentation","Act only clinical presentation clinical presentation","Do only clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Quality improvement method: test change on small scale, study, adjust, then implement broadly', 'PDSA: iterative. Small-scale tests (plan), execute (do), analyze (study), implement/adjust (act).'),
('6679014f-b786-5691-a62d-73ba2e076592'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Benchmarking infection rates:', '["Comparing HAIs rates to national standards (NHSN). Risk-adjusted comparison","Not valid clinical presentation clinical presentation","Not used clinical presentation clinical presentation","No standardization clinical presentation clinical presentation","Raw rates only clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Comparing HAIs rates to national standards (NHSN). Risk-adjusted comparison', 'Benchmarking: SIR, device-associated rates. Identify high-performing and under-performing units.'),
('d2b42ba8-bd88-54b8-9cb8-2e3625d43f2a'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Public reporting of HAIs:', '["Not needed","Confidential only","Mandated in many countries","No public access","Only internal"]'::jsonb, 'Mandated in many countries', 'Public reporting: drives improvement through accountability and competition. May lead to underreporting.'),
('1fa5279b-6714-5119-a1bb-96feda4813ba'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Pay-for-performance (P4P) in infection control:', '["Financial incentives/penalties tied to HAI rates","No effect","Decreases quality","Not used","Only for hospital-acquired conditions"]'::jsonb, 'Financial incentives/penalties tied to HAI rates', 'P4P: CMS hospital-acquired condition reduction program. Penalties for high HAI rates.'),
('ea0499f1-a797-54fa-b608-a0aca62d58f4'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'The infection control budget:', '["Optional clinical presentation clinical presentation","No separate budget clinical presentation clinical presentation","Part of nursing clinical presentation clinical presentation","Funds for IC staff, education, supplies, lab, construction, outbreak management","Not needed clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Funds for IC staff, education, supplies, lab, construction, outbreak management', 'IC budget: essential for program. Staff (IC nurse, doctor, epidemiologist). Supplies (PPE, disinfectants).'),
('0a4f1438-13cb-5a02-9e2b-7fa35a4c6651'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Number of infection control nurses (ICN) recommended:', '["No standard","1 per 50 beds","1 per hospital","1 per 1000 beds","1 ICN per 100-250 beds"]'::jsonb, '1 ICN per 100-250 beds', 'ICN staffing: adequate for surveillance, education, outbreak investigation, consultation.'),
('1a9e8978-eac7-5bcc-9d82-42eeb89eaa09'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Hospital infection control committee responsibilities:', '["Only outbreak clinical presentation clinical presentation","Only policy clinical presentation clinical presentation","Only surveillance clinical presentation clinical presentation","Policy development, surveillance, outbreak management, antibiotic stewardship, education, audit","Only education clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Policy development, surveillance, outbreak management, antibiotic stewardship, education, audit', 'IC committee: governance, oversight. Meets monthly. Reports to hospital administration and quality committee.'),
('318bd9ec-fecc-54d2-b23a-a2f95a375a5e'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'The infection control manual:', '["Optional clinical presentation clinical presentation","Not needed clinical presentation clinical presentation","Online only clinical presentation clinical presentation","Documents all policies, procedures, and guidelines for infection prevention","No updates clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Documents all policies, procedures, and guidelines for infection prevention', 'IC manual: reference for HCWs. Regular updates based on evidence. Compliance expectation.'),
('dad70c33-c4bf-55ce-a5d9-f64601102b6b'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Compliance monitoring in infection control:', '["Not done clinical presentation","Only during outbreaks clinical presentation","No feedback clinical presentation","Punitive if noncompliant clinical presentation","Audits of hand hygiene, PPE use, isolation adherence, bundle compliance"]'::jsonb, 'Audits of hand hygiene, PPE use, isolation adherence, bundle compliance', 'Compliance monitoring: regular observations + feedback. Non-punitive. Identify barriers and enablers.'),
('541fc59f-a02b-55bc-8a8d-1c94dfaafaec'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Feedback on infection rates:', '["Yearly only clinical presentation","No feedback clinical presentation","Regular (monthly/quarterly) reporting of HAI rates to clinical units","Only if high clinical presentation","Only to administration clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Regular (monthly/quarterly) reporting of HAI rates to clinical units', 'Feedback: benchmarked rates shared with units. Comparative data drives improvement. Transparency.'),
('77fbc8a2-5014-5088-b16b-8410a91de84d'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Quality indicators in infection control:', '["Only antimicrobial use clinical presentation","Only HAIs clinical presentation","Hand hygiene compliance, CLABSI/VAP/CAUTI rates, SSI rates, antimicrobial use","No indicators clinical presentation","Only hand hygiene clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Hand hygiene compliance, CLABSI/VAP/CAUTI rates, SSI rates, antimicrobial use', 'Indicators: structure (resources), process (compliance), outcome (infection rates). Balanced set.'),
('d5a1a468-01a5-50cb-8d99-8f7107e73e02'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Accreditation standards for infection control:', '["No standards clinical presentation","Self-assessment clinical presentation","Joint Commission/JCI/CBAHI standards: IC program, surveillance, prevention, guidelines","Optional clinical presentation","Only for hospitals >500 beds clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Joint Commission/JCI/CBAHI standards: IC program, surveillance, prevention, guidelines', 'Accreditation: IC chapters cover program, prevention, surveillance, environment, sterilization, waste.'),
('eaf49d02-e11d-5b94-b119-32406efef285'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'National infection control guidelines:', '["Outdated clinical presentation","Not evidence-based clinical presentation","Hospital-specific clinical presentation","Optional clinical presentation","Standardized evidence-based recommendations for prevention"]'::jsonb, 'Standardized evidence-based recommendations for prevention', 'National guidelines: CDC (USA), NICE (UK), national committees. Updated regularly. Adherence expected.'),
('501a77bd-eafa-54fc-9ea5-6a5fd1c0b92c'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'International infection control resources:', '["Only CDC clinical presentation","Only WHO clinical presentation","Not available clinical presentation","CDC, WHO, ECDC, APIC, SHEA, HIS","No international clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'CDC, WHO, ECDC, APIC, SHEA, HIS', 'Resources: scientific organizations develop guidelines, education, tools. Global network for emerging threats.'),
('84ab3a1d-151b-5d4a-ad98-30d492d41f2e'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Infection control in low- and middle-income countries (LMICs):', '["High HAI burden. Resource constraints. Basic interventions (hand hygiene","No HAI problem clinical presentation clinical presentation","No evidence clinical presentation clinical presentation","Same as HICs clinical presentation clinical presentation","Cannot improve clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'High HAI burden. Resource constraints. Basic interventions (hand hygiene', 'LMICs: higher HAI rates (2-20x). Simple low-cost interventions (hand hygiene, clean delivery) save lives.'),
('20c70c05-d9e5-5ca0-af13-9c2f11e5fd68'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Clean birth practices to prevent maternal/neonatal sepsis:', '["Antibiotics enough clinical presentation clinical presentation","No evidence clinical presentation clinical presentation","Only in hospital clinical presentation clinical presentation","Hand washing, clean birth surface, clean cord cutting, clean perineum, clean razor","No interventions clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Hand washing, clean birth surface, clean cord cutting, clean perineum, clean razor', 'Clean birth: WHO Clean Delivery Kit. Reduces maternal/neonatal sepsis and tetanus.'),
('eddca855-200c-51bb-bee4-964954a45824'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Surgical site marking:', '["Mark site with indelible marker (involve patient). Verify during time-out","No marking clinical presentation","Wrong site clinical presentation","Mark after induction clinical presentation","Not needed clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Mark site with indelible marker (involve patient). Verify during time-out', 'Site marking: part of WHO safety checklist. Prevents wrong-site surgery. Mark with surgeon''s initials or ''YES''.'),
('23911b5d-1ee5-55d7-b80d-a06081f61fac'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Time-out before incision:', '["Not needed clinical presentation","Final verification of correct patient, procedure, site, position, implants","Only in teaching hospitals clinical presentation","Optional clinical presentation","Only for complex cases clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Final verification of correct patient, procedure, site, position, implants', 'Time-out: immediate pre-incision. All team members pause and confirm. WHO checklist item.'),
('b969e152-9d39-5144-b7d7-8e5fdcc63a0e'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Surgical count (sponges, needles, instruments):', '["Only for laparotomy clinical presentation clinical presentation","Surgeon remembers clinical presentation clinical presentation","No count needed clinical presentation clinical presentation","Only at end clinical presentation clinical presentation","Count at start, before closure of cavity, and at skin closure. Verbalize discrepancies"]'::jsonb, 'Count at start, before closure of cavity, and at skin closure. Verbalize discrepancies', 'Count: prevent retained surgical items. Radiograph if count incorrect. Standardized protocol.'),
('cd03c5dd-b9a8-549f-8e9c-0da7f41fe8d3'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Infection Control', 'Retained surgical item prevention:', '["Counts, radiofrequency detection, barcode scanning, standardized protocols","Surgeon remembers clinical presentation clinical presentation","Counts only clinical presentation clinical presentation","Only X-ray clinical presentation clinical presentation","No prevention clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Counts, radiofrequency detection, barcode scanning, standardized protocols', 'RSI: systematic prevention. Incorrect count -> X-ray in OR before closure. Team communication.'),
('b57a5360-1fa0-5bf7-9f94-4bb35d80a342'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Protein-energy malnutrition (PEM) includes:', '["Only marasmus","Only kwashiorkor","Marasmus and Kwashiorkor","Vitamin deficiencies","Mineral deficiencies"]'::jsonb, 'Marasmus and Kwashiorkor', 'PEM: marasmus (chronic energy deficiency) and kwashiorkor (protein deficiency). Often overlap.'),
('9cab662b-39c6-552b-9401-f78ceb126d70'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Marasmus is characterized by:', '["Severe wasting of muscle and subcutaneous fat","Hepatomegaly clinical presentation","Edema clinical presentation","Fatty liver clinical presentation","Dermatitis clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Severe wasting of muscle and subcutaneous fat', 'Marasmus: inadequate calories. Child appears ''skin and bones''. No edema. Immune function relatively preserved.'),
('f0a72933-6591-5dd6-bd39-d3c4ca27be66'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Kwashiorkor is characterized by:', '["Edema, fatty liver, dermatitis, apathy","No skin changes clinical presentation","Wasting only clinical presentation","No edema clinical presentation","No liver changes clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Edema, fatty liver, dermatitis, apathy', 'Kwashiorkor: protein deficiency. Pitting edema (due to hypoalbuminemia). ''Sugary baby'' - sent away from breast.'),
('5848bebc-57aa-587d-99a2-303e5b303b49'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'The earliest sign of kwashiorkor is:', '["Dermatitis","Peripheral pitting edema","Wasting","Diarrhea","Hepatomegaly"]'::jsonb, 'Peripheral pitting edema', 'Kwashiorkor: edema is hallmark. Low albumin <2.5 g/dL. Fatty liver due to impaired VLDL synthesis.'),
('6c155558-07b7-5dd1-87db-881522aaef9b'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Marasmic-kwashiorkor (mixed) features:', '["No wasting","Only edema","No edema","Only wasting","Severe wasting + edema"]'::jsonb, 'Severe wasting + edema', 'Marasmic-kwashiorkor: weight <60% + edema. Most severe form of PEM.'),
('e908c6be-73f9-554c-aa99-7d2692b138a9'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'PEM: WHO classification based on weight-for-height Z-score:', '["No classification clinical presentation","Percentile only clinical presentation","Moderate: <-2 SD, Severe: <-3 SD","<-1 SD moderate clinical presentation","<-2 SD severe clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Moderate: <-2 SD, Severe: <-3 SD', 'WHO: moderate acute malnutrition (MAM): WHZ -3 to -2. Severe acute malnutrition (SAM): WHZ <-3, or MUAC <115 mm, or edema.'),
('134e0b96-0d70-5463-aaf5-02de94152015'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Mid-upper arm circumference (MUAC) for SAM screening:', '["<100 mm clinical presentation","<140 mm clinical presentation","<115 mm (11.5 cm) in children 6-59 months","<125 mm clinical presentation","<130 mm clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, '<115 mm (11.5 cm) in children 6-59 months', 'MUAC: rapid screening. 115 mm cut-off for SAM. 115-125 mm for MAM. Low cost, no age calculation needed.'),
('6c410651-3a22-5beb-b6b4-279c99601e7c'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Management of severe acute malnutrition (SAM):', '["IV fluids clinical presentation clinical presentation","Therapeutic feeding (F-75, F-100), micronutrients, treat complications","Normal diet clinical presentation clinical presentation","Only protein clinical presentation clinical presentation","Only calories clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Therapeutic feeding (F-75, F-100), micronutrients, treat complications', 'SAM: stabilization phase (F-75: 75 kcal/100 mL) -> transition -> rehabilitation (F-100: 100 kcal/100 mL).'),
('537c22de-364f-5b3f-8ee8-6b8865ef5782'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Complications of refeeding in SAM:', '["Hyperphosphatemia clinical presentation clinical presentation","No complications clinical presentation clinical presentation","Hypernatremia clinical presentation clinical presentation","Refeeding syndrome (hypophosphatemia, hypokalemia, hypomagnesemia). Slow refeeding essential","Hyperkalemia clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Refeeding syndrome (hypophosphatemia, hypokalemia, hypomagnesemia). Slow refeeding essential', 'Refeeding syndrome: insulin surge drives phosphate/K+/Mg++ intracellularly. Cardiac failure. Prevent by slow caloric initiation.'),
('cb912b7d-ffed-5eee-874e-697dd9f1338d'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Vitamin A (retinol) deficiency leads to:', '["Pellagra clinical presentation clinical presentation","Night blindness (nyctalopia), xerophthalmia, Bitot spots, keratomalacia","Beri-beri clinical presentation clinical presentation","Rickets clinical presentation clinical presentation","Scurvy clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Night blindness (nyctalopia), xerophthalmia, Bitot spots, keratomalacia', 'Vitamin A: night blindness (earliest). Xerophthalmia (dry conjunctiva). Bitot spots (foamy plaques). Keratomalacia (corneal necrosis -> blindness).'),
('17d45a04-5bfa-531b-8bb1-15d1e0befbca'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Vitamin A: dietary sources:', '["Meat only","Citrus fruits","Fish only","Liver, egg yolk, dairy, orange/yellow vegetables","Green leafy vegetables only"]'::jsonb, 'Liver, egg yolk, dairy, orange/yellow vegetables', 'Vitamin A: preformed (retinol - animal sources). Provitamin A (beta-carotene - plant sources, converted in gut).'),
('5deb16e3-c1a4-530e-aea6-a6d9d2c36825'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'WHO vitamin A supplementation schedule:', '["High-dose: 6-11 mo: 100,000 IU. 12-59 mo: 200,000 IU q4-6 months","Daily low dose clinical presentation","Monthly clinical presentation","No schedule clinical presentation","Weekly clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'High-dose: 6-11 mo: 100,000 IU. 12-59 mo: 200,000 IU q4-6 months', 'Vitamin A: periodic high dose. Reduces mortality (measles, diarrhea). Given with measles vaccine.'),
('cf3c64c0-ca67-5d4f-8c71-fbb4ac561ef8'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Vitamin A toxicity (hypervitaminosis A):', '["Mild only clinical presentation clinical presentation","Reversible clinical presentation clinical presentation","Only from beta-carotene clinical presentation clinical presentation","Acute: headache, vomiting, pseudotumor cerebri. Chronic: hepatotoxicity, bone pain, skin changes","No toxicity clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Acute: headache, vomiting, pseudotumor cerebri. Chronic: hepatotoxicity, bone pain, skin changes', 'Vitamin A: teratogenic (isotretinoin). Acute toxicity from single mega-dose. Chronic from daily high intake.'),
('de494a2a-2b3c-5da1-949b-e187d86d8bd7'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Vitamin D deficiency (rickets in children, osteomalacia in adults):', '["Osteoporosis clinical presentation clinical presentation","Osteopetrosis clinical presentation clinical presentation","Impaired bone mineralization (soft, weak bones). Low calcium, low phosphate, high ALP","Paget disease clinical presentation clinical presentation","Osteosclerosis clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Impaired bone mineralization (soft, weak bones). Low calcium, low phosphate, high ALP', 'Vitamin D: rickets (bowed legs, rachitic rosary, craniotabes, Harris lines). Osteomalacia (bone pain, Looser zones).'),
('dd8399ae-eec0-51fb-9016-60d85487d0d2'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Vitamin D: sources, synthesis:', '["Sunlight (UVB -> skin synthesis) major source. Dietary: fatty fish, fortified milk","Fish only clinical presentation clinical presentation","Diet only clinical presentation clinical presentation","Neither clinical presentation clinical presentation","Sun only clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Sunlight (UVB -> skin synthesis) major source. Dietary: fatty fish, fortified milk', 'Vitamin D: 80-90% from sun exposure. 7-dehydrocholesterol -> cholecalciferol. Then liver (25-OH) -> kidney (1,25-OH).'),
('a31dc7e8-d2d3-55b6-b8a1-2d35dc9170e0'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Serum 25-hydroxyvitamin D levels: deficiency:', '["<30 ng/mL","<15 ng/mL","<5 ng/mL","<10 ng/mL","<20 ng/mL"]'::jsonb, '<20 ng/mL', '25(OH)D: best marker of vitamin D status. <20 deficient, 20-29 insufficient, >=30 sufficient. Measure before supplementing.'),
('1601303b-af49-58c7-8a8f-e808202d1fa6'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Vitamin E (tocopherol) deficiency causes:', '["Pellagra clinical presentation clinical presentation","Scurvy clinical presentation clinical presentation","Rickets clinical presentation clinical presentation","Hemolytic anemia (premature infants), peripheral neuropathy, ataxia, retinopathy","Night blindness clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Hemolytic anemia (premature infants), peripheral neuropathy, ataxia, retinopathy', 'Vitamin E: antioxidant (protects cell membranes from lipid peroxidation). Deficiency rare (fat malabsorption).'),
('1c728312-2a84-5ecf-a888-d82c9992e4e2'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Vitamin K deficiency leads to:', '["Bleeding time prolonged clinical presentation clinical presentation","Elevated PTT only clinical presentation clinical presentation","No effect on coagulation clinical presentation clinical presentation","Platelet dysfunction clinical presentation clinical presentation","Bleeding tendency (elevated PT, normal PTT). Due to impaired clotting factors II, VII, IX, X"]'::jsonb, 'Bleeding tendency (elevated PT, normal PTT). Due to impaired clotting factors II, VII, IX, X', 'Vitamin K: cofactor for gamma-carboxylation of clotting factors. VKDB (vitamin K deficiency bleeding) in newborns -> prophylaxis (1 mg IM at birth).'),
('b873c09f-1426-5ae0-8490-de3f8b9704af'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Vitamin K sources:', '["Only gut bacteria clinical presentation clinical presentation","Green leafy vegetables (vitamin K1 - phylloquinone). Gut bacteria produce menaquinones","Animal sources only clinical presentation clinical presentation","Only supplements clinical presentation clinical presentation","No plant sources clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Green leafy vegetables (vitamin K1 - phylloquinone). Gut bacteria produce menaquinones', 'Vitamin K: K1 (phylloquinone - plants), K2 (menaquinones - bacterial synthesis). Intestinal bacteria contribute to K2.'),
('5e999445-9ea5-5467-8f22-47e23f05b4b9'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Vitamin B1 (thiamine) deficiency causes:', '["Beri-beri (wet: high-output cardiac failure; dry: peripheral neuropathy","Rickets clinical presentation clinical presentation","Scurvy clinical presentation clinical presentation","Pellagra clinical presentation clinical presentation","Pernicious anemia clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Beri-beri (wet: high-output cardiac failure; dry: peripheral neuropathy', 'Thiamine: cofactor in carbohydrate metabolism (transketolase, pyruvate dehydrogenase). Wet beri-beri: right heart failure, edema.'),
('3b585a9a-176c-5076-8657-564de23aae1e'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Wernicke encephalopathy (B1 deficiency):', '["Dementia only clinical presentation clinical presentation","Confusion, ataxia, nystagmus/ophthalmoplegia (triad). Seen in alcoholism","No eye signs clinical presentation clinical presentation","Only ataxia clinical presentation clinical presentation","Only confusion clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Confusion, ataxia, nystagmus/ophthalmoplegia (triad). Seen in alcoholism', 'Wernicke: medical emergency. IV thiamine before glucose (glucose without thiamine precipitates encephalopathy). Korsakoff amnestic syndrome.'),
('b2f12320-f607-5a9a-9bb5-b0a56d10afa1'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Vitamin B2 (riboflavin) deficiency:', '["Night blindness clinical presentation clinical presentation","Scurvy clinical presentation clinical presentation","Angular stomatitis, cheilitis, glossitis, seborrheic dermatitis, photophobia","Beri-beri clinical presentation clinical presentation","Pellagra clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Angular stomatitis, cheilitis, glossitis, seborrheic dermatitis, photophobia', 'Riboflavin: ''ariboflavinosis''. Magenta tongue. Angular stomatitis (corners of mouth). Photophobia, corneal vascularization.'),
('ec6da3ee-6116-562e-a6ed-62a0e6af88d5'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Vitamin B3 (niacin) deficiency causes:', '["Scurvy","Pellagra","Pernicious anemia","Rickets","Beri-beri"]'::jsonb, 'Pellagra', 'Niacin: pellagra. Dermatitis (Casal necklace, photosensitive), diarrhea (glossitis, enteritis), dementia. 4th D: death. Seen in corn-based diets.'),
('3301338f-1025-5a04-9e9a-d430a402f414'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Niacin synthesis from tryptophan:', '["Direct conversion clinical presentation","60 mg tryptophan = 1 mg niacin. Requires B6, B2, iron for conversion","No conversion clinical presentation","Equal amounts clinical presentation","10:1 ratio clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, '60 mg tryptophan = 1 mg niacin. Requires B6, B2, iron for conversion', 'Niacin: can be synthesized from tryptophan via kynurenine pathway. B6 deficiency impairs conversion.'),
('3db64a53-3cdc-5a12-bc8d-fe851455b603'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Vitamin B5 (pantothenic acid) deficiency:', '["No symptoms clinical presentation clinical presentation","Scurvy clinical presentation clinical presentation","Pellagra clinical presentation clinical presentation","Rare (''burning feet syndrome''). Fatigue, headache, paresthesias, vomiting","Beri-beri clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Rare (''burning feet syndrome''). Fatigue, headache, paresthesias, vomiting', 'Pantothenic acid: CoA component. Deficiency extremely rare. Medically induced with antagonist (calcium hopantenate).'),
('47c09e3a-925d-5929-b6c0-4c1788429313'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Vitamin B6 (pyridoxine) deficiency:', '["Megaloblastic anemia clinical presentation clinical presentation","Microcytic anemia (sideroblastic), dermatitis, glossitis, neuropathy, seizures","Hemolytic anemia clinical presentation clinical presentation","Thalassemia clinical presentation clinical presentation","Pernicious anemia clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Microcytic anemia (sideroblastic), dermatitis, glossitis, neuropathy, seizures', 'B6: cofactor in >100 reactions (amino acid metabolism, heme synthesis). Isoniazid causes deficiency -> give B6.'),
('461c55ee-b1d9-53df-9c39-905d605ad970'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Vitamin B6 toxicity:', '["Hepatotoxicity","No toxicity","Peripheral neuropathy","Nephrotoxicity","Cardiotoxicity"]'::jsonb, 'Peripheral neuropathy', 'Pyridoxine: high doses cause sensory neuropathy (dorsal root ganglia). Reversible on discontinuation.'),
('59846170-f120-5de6-b94c-0af018b17a9d'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Vitamin B7 (biotin) deficiency:', '["Only in infants clinical presentation clinical presentation","Egg yolk deficiency clinical presentation clinical presentation","Dermatitis, alopecia, neurological symptoms (depression, lethargy). Raw egg white (avidin","Only in pregnancy clinical presentation clinical presentation","No deficiency clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Dermatitis, alopecia, neurological symptoms (depression, lethargy). Raw egg white (avidin', 'Biotin: cofactor for carboxylases (gluconeogenesis, fatty acid synthesis). Avidin in raw egg white binds biotin -> prevents absorption.'),
('d416d29b-92a3-5072-aea8-98514c01ea27'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Vitamin B9 (folate) deficiency:', '["Megaloblastic anemia (similar to B12), neural tube defects in pregnancy","Sideroblastic anemia clinical presentation","Hemolytic anemia clinical presentation","Microcytic anemia clinical presentation","Aplastic anemia clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Megaloblastic anemia (similar to B12), neural tube defects in pregnancy', 'Folate: 1-carbon transfer (DNA synthesis). Deficiency: neural tube defects (prevent by supplementation 400 mcg/day before conception).'),
('11631433-8000-5272-ab79-f5916b641fc4'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Folic acid supplementation in pregnancy:', '["Only for previous NTD clinical presentation","Not needed clinical presentation","400-800 mcg/day starting before conception","100 mcg/day clinical presentation","After first trimester clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, '400-800 mcg/day starting before conception', 'Folic acid: crucial in first 28 days of gestation (before pregnancy often recognized). Fortification of grain products.'),
('cdd64a80-d1e7-5955-8742-c68b25661441'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Vitamin B12 (cobalamin) deficiency causes:', '["Liver disease clinical presentation clinical presentation","Excess intake clinical presentation clinical presentation","Dietary deficiency in omnivores clinical presentation clinical presentation","Pernicious anemia (autoimmune lack of intrinsic factor), vegan diet, gastric surgery, malabsorption","Kidney disease clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Pernicious anemia (autoimmune lack of intrinsic factor), vegan diet, gastric surgery, malabsorption', 'B12: animal products only. Pernicious anemia: anti-intrinsic factor antibodies, autoimmune gastritis. Vegans at risk.'),
('bae23fd7-699a-5cd5-8711-18835c133c4d'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Vitamin B12 deficiency: neurological manifestations:', '["Only hematologic clinical presentation","Only psychiatric clinical presentation","Peripheral neuropathy, subacute combined degeneration of spinal cord","No neuro changes clinical presentation","Dementia only clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Peripheral neuropathy, subacute combined degeneration of spinal cord', 'B12: neurologic may occur without anemia. SC degeneration: loss of vibration/proprioception + spasticity + ataxia.'),
('9662db50-e9ec-563b-aedd-5392c85fe1dc'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'B12 vs folate deficiency differentiation:', '["RBC folate clinical presentation clinical presentation","Schilling test clinical presentation clinical presentation","Methylmalonic acid (MMA) elevated in B12 deficiency, normal in folate deficiency","Folate level clinical presentation clinical presentation","Homocysteine clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Methylmalonic acid (MMA) elevated in B12 deficiency, normal in folate deficiency', 'MMA: specific for B12 deficiency. Homocysteine elevated in both. Schilling test for pernicious anemia.'),
('e228cda7-1eca-5869-848c-751c4d6ae07c'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Treatment of B12 deficiency:', '["IV B12 clinical presentation clinical presentation","No treatment clinical presentation clinical presentation","Oral B12 clinical presentation clinical presentation","Folate replacement clinical presentation clinical presentation","IM hydroxocobalamin (1000 mcg) daily for 1 week, weekly for 1 month, then monthly"]'::jsonb, 'IM hydroxocobalamin (1000 mcg) daily for 1 week, weekly for 1 month, then monthly', 'Hydroxocobalamin: IM preferred over cyanocobalamin for better retention. Lifelong for pernicious anemia.'),
('05de612a-1e1d-545c-a0d0-33dc009f2456'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Vitamin C (ascorbic acid) deficiency -> scurvy:', '["Bleeding (gingival hyperplasia, perifollicular hemorrhages, purpura), impaired wound healing","Only skin disease clinical presentation clinical presentation","Only gum disease clinical presentation clinical presentation","Only bone disease clinical presentation clinical presentation","No symptoms clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Bleeding (gingival hyperplasia, perifollicular hemorrhages, purpura), impaired wound healing', 'Scurvy: impaired collagen synthesis (prolyl hydroxylase cofactor). Perifollicular hemorrhages, corkscrew hairs, ecchymoses, bleeding gums.'),
('1c374641-720e-52ee-b637-42b85b64834a'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Vitamin C: sources and function:', '["Only in animal sources clinical presentation clinical presentation","Not essential clinical presentation clinical presentation","No function clinical presentation clinical presentation","Citrus fruits, peppers, broccoli, strawberries. Collagen synthesis, antioxidant, iron absorption","Only in citrus clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Citrus fruits, peppers, broccoli, strawberries. Collagen synthesis, antioxidant, iron absorption', 'Vitamin C: humans cannot synthesize (lack L-gulonolactone oxidase). Must obtain from diet. Cooking destroys.'),
('6db936e7-e0d1-580f-afa8-34173991d481'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Scurvy treatment:', '["Vitamin C 100-200 mg daily (orally), rapid improvement","IV vitamin C clinical presentation","IM vitamin C clinical presentation","Multivitamin clinical presentation","No treatment clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Vitamin C 100-200 mg daily (orally), rapid improvement', 'Scurvy: responds quickly to vitamin C replacement. Infantile scurvy: pseudoparalysis, bone pain.'),
('ae295381-fdbe-5aab-b7fc-786028d9ffd7'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Iron deficiency: most common nutritional deficiency worldwide:', '["B12 deficiency clinical presentation clinical presentation","Affects 30% globally. Causes: dietary, blood loss, malabsorption. Microcytic hypochromic anemia","Folate deficiency clinical presentation clinical presentation","Copper deficiency clinical presentation clinical presentation","Zinc deficiency clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Affects 30% globally. Causes: dietary, blood loss, malabsorption. Microcytic hypochromic anemia', 'Iron deficiency: MCV <80, MCH <27. Serum ferritin low. TIBC high. Transferrin saturation <16%.'),
('5d6e0097-2660-5d2f-82fc-440e14df3bc3'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Iron deficiency anemia: lab findings:', '["High ferritin clinical presentation","High MCV clinical presentation","Low ferritin, low MCV, low MCH, low transferrin saturation, high TIBC, low Hb","High transferrin saturation clinical presentation","High Hb clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Low ferritin, low MCV, low MCH, low transferrin saturation, high TIBC, low Hb', 'Iron deficiency: ferritin <15 ng/mL (gold standard). Absent bone marrow iron stores. Mentzer index >13.'),
('5deac04b-94a4-55b4-8a97-aa63f6987cfc'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Iron supplementation for deficiency:', '["Monthly clinical presentation clinical presentation","Weekly clinical presentation clinical presentation","Once daily clinical presentation clinical presentation","Ferrous sulfate 65 mg elemental iron (325 mg) 2-3 times/day. Takes 2-4 weeks to raise Hb 1-2 g/dL","IV always clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Ferrous sulfate 65 mg elemental iron (325 mg) 2-3 times/day. Takes 2-4 weeks to raise Hb 1-2 g/dL', 'Iron: oral preferred. GI side effects (constipation, nausea). Take with vitamin C (enhances absorption). Reticulocytosis in 7-10 days.'),
('adc87f65-791b-596f-b6e1-a407223c082c'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'IV iron indications:', '["First line clinical presentation clinical presentation","Failed oral therapy, intolerance, malabsorption, CKD (ESA), severe deficiency, ongoing blood loss","Only for children clinical presentation clinical presentation","Only for anaphylaxis risk clinical presentation clinical presentation","Not used clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Failed oral therapy, intolerance, malabsorption, CKD (ESA), severe deficiency, ongoing blood loss', 'IV iron: iron sucrose (Venofer), ferric carboxymaltose (Injectafer). Risk: infusion reactions (rare anaphylaxis).'),
('8d44fc48-d52b-5ad4-bc98-c09b63b49634'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Iron overload (hemochromatosis):', '["Only in blood donors clinical presentation clinical presentation","No consequences clinical presentation clinical presentation","Only in elderly clinical presentation clinical presentation","Excess iron deposition -> liver cirrhosis, diabetes, heart failure, arthritis, bronze skin","Benign clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Excess iron deposition -> liver cirrhosis, diabetes, heart failure, arthritis, bronze skin', 'Hemochromatosis: HFE mutation (C282Y). Treatment: phlebotomy, iron chelation (deferasirox, deferoxamine).'),
('15bae4ba-4c63-50d0-9c22-daaf9ad3338e'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Zinc deficiency: manifestations:', '["Constipation clinical presentation clinical presentation","Hyperpigmentation clinical presentation clinical presentation","Growth retardation, hypogonadism, impaired immune function, diarrhea, dermatitis (acrodermatitis","Excess growth clinical presentation clinical presentation","Improved immunity clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Growth retardation, hypogonadism, impaired immune function, diarrhea, dermatitis (acrodermatitis', 'Zinc: cofactor for >300 enzymes. Acrodermatitis enteropathica: autosomal recessive zinc malabsorption -> periorificial dermatitis, diarrhea.'),
('bfe373a8-49f4-57c6-a9e1-279644d64fcb'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Zinc supplementation in diarrhea:', '["Not recommended","Only for adults","10-20 mg/day for 10-14 days","Increases diarrhea","No benefit"]'::jsonb, '10-20 mg/day for 10-14 days', 'Zinc: WHO recommends for diarrhea. Reduces stool output and duration. Also for growth in zinc-deficient children.'),
('710fd44b-0d28-5e08-abb2-89f65e3a9d6d'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Copper deficiency: manifestations:', '["Megaloblastic anemia clinical presentation clinical presentation","Hemolytic anemia clinical presentation clinical presentation","Macrocytic anemia clinical presentation clinical presentation","Aplastic anemia clinical presentation clinical presentation","Microcytic anemia (that responds to iron + copper), neutropenia, neurologic symptoms (ataxia"]'::jsonb, 'Microcytic anemia (that responds to iron + copper), neutropenia, neurologic symptoms (ataxia', 'Copper: absorption inhibited by excess zinc. Menkes disease (X-linked copper deficiency). Wilson disease (copper overload).'),
('9ec29086-1fdb-59ba-8605-5a52b4467e07'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Selenium deficiency:', '["Keshan disease (cardiomyopathy), Kashin-Beck disease (osteoarthritis), myopathy","Common clinical presentation clinical presentation","Benign clinical presentation clinical presentation","Only in Japan clinical presentation clinical presentation","No deficiency clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Keshan disease (cardiomyopathy), Kashin-Beck disease (osteoarthritis), myopathy', 'Selenium: antioxidant (glutathione peroxidase). Keshan: dilated cardiomyopathy in children (China). Kashin-Beck: degenerative arthritis.'),
('9d12442b-ac45-504e-8c99-c3f6a38455b5'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Selenium toxicity (selenosis):', '["Nausea, nail brittleness, hair loss, garlic breath odor, neuropathy","Benign clinical presentation clinical presentation","No symptoms clinical presentation clinical presentation","No toxicity clinical presentation clinical presentation","Only acute clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Nausea, nail brittleness, hair loss, garlic breath odor, neuropathy', 'Selenosis: from high intake (Brazil nuts, supplements). Garlicky breath. Hair and nail loss. Reversible.'),
('1b161c15-629c-55ad-8ab5-80a57bdeaeeb'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Iodine deficiency:', '["Hyperthyroidism clinical presentation","No mental effects clinical presentation","Only goiter clinical presentation","No thyroid effects clinical presentation","Goiter (enlarged thyroid), hypothyroidism, cretinism"]'::jsonb, 'Goiter (enlarged thyroid), hypothyroidism, cretinism', 'Iodine: thyroid hormone synthesis. Iodized salt prevents. Cretinism: severe mental retardation + deaf-mutism + spasticity.'),
('eb7a917b-b4fd-519f-affa-5a7af8a2c791'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Iodine-induced hyperthyroidism (Jod-Basedow):', '["Thyroiditis clinical presentation clinical presentation","Hyperthyroidism triggered by iodine supplementation in iodine-deficient individuals","Hypothyroidism clinical presentation clinical presentation","No effect clinical presentation clinical presentation","Benefits all clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Hyperthyroidism triggered by iodine supplementation in iodine-deficient individuals', 'Jod-Basedow: excess iodine in iodine-deficient -> autonomous nodule produces excess T3/T4. Caution with iodized salt.'),
('e0cb36d0-1d60-51ab-87b2-02fae9b45d9d'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Fluoride deficiency:', '["Dental caries (tooth decay). Fluoride strengthens enamel","Fluorosis clinical presentation","Excess fluoridation clinical presentation","Caries reduction in excess clinical presentation","No dental effect clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Dental caries (tooth decay). Fluoride strengthens enamel', 'Fluoride: topical vs systemic. Fluorosis: mottling of teeth from excess during childhood. Water fluoridation 0.7 ppm.'),
('d5290188-4290-5db2-b781-003c00632f7e'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Calcium deficiency:', '["No symptoms clinical presentation clinical presentation","Hypercalcemia clinical presentation clinical presentation","Muscle spasm only clinical presentation clinical presentation","Increases bone density clinical presentation clinical presentation","Osteoporosis/osteomalacia, tetany (hypocalcemia), paresthesias, Trousseau/Chvostek signs"]'::jsonb, 'Osteoporosis/osteomalacia, tetany (hypocalcemia), paresthesias, Trousseau/Chvostek signs', 'Calcium: 99% in bone. Hypocalcemia: from deficiency, renal failure, hypoparathyroidism. Treat with calcium + vitamin D.'),
('15c0a24b-d7be-57c5-9b0b-d55ec075dc82'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Calcium supplementation for osteoporosis prevention:', '["1000-1200 mg/day + vitamin D 800-1000 IU/day for postmenopausal women and elderly","Only calcium clinical presentation clinical presentation","Without vitamin D clinical presentation clinical presentation","No benefit clinical presentation clinical presentation","Only vitamin D clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, '1000-1200 mg/day + vitamin D 800-1000 IU/day for postmenopausal women and elderly', 'Ca: citrate (better absorption with PPIs) vs carbonate (cheaper, take with food). Adequate vitamin D needed for absorption.'),
('c7b61e29-4fd7-5a18-95d1-af228a8de1d5'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Magnesium deficiency:', '["Neuromuscular hyperexcitability (tetany, tremor, seizures), arrhythmias","Bradycardia clinical presentation clinical presentation","Constipation clinical presentation clinical presentation","No symptoms clinical presentation clinical presentation","Hypotension clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Neuromuscular hyperexcitability (tetany, tremor, seizures), arrhythmias', 'Mg: hypomagnesemia -> hypocalcemia (PTH resistance), hypokalemia (renal K wasting). Causes: diuretics, alcohol, malabsorption.'),
('14504034-1b46-5448-bfdb-1f3201d4e1b6'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Osteoporosis vs osteomalacia:', '["Osteoporosis: reduced bone mass (normal mineralization). Osteomalacia: defective bone mineralizat","Osteoporosis = soft bone clinical presentation clinical presentation","No difference clinical presentation clinical presentation","Same clinical presentation clinical presentation","Osteomalacia = reduced mass clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Osteoporosis: reduced bone mass (normal mineralization). Osteomalacia: defective bone mineralizat', 'Osteoporosis: low BMD. Osteomalacia: low calcium/vitamin D -> unmineralized osteoid. Pseudofractures (Looser zones).'),
('12698de1-f6b8-5209-a948-06ba93da029c'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Osteoporosis risk factors:', '["High BMI clinical presentation clinical presentation","Exercise clinical presentation clinical presentation","Male gender clinical presentation clinical presentation","Estrogen therapy clinical presentation clinical presentation","Age, female, postmenopause, low BMI, smoking, alcohol, steroids, family history, low calcium"]'::jsonb, 'Age, female, postmenopause, low BMI, smoking, alcohol, steroids, family history, low calcium', 'Osteoporosis: DEXA scan T-score <-2.5. Treatment: calcium/D, bisphosphonates, denosumab, teriparatide.'),
('46a2d3e5-f01c-5a72-a8fb-83bab6366da8'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Obesity definition (WHO):', '[">=20",">=25","BMI >=30 kg/m2",">=35",">=27"]'::jsonb, 'BMI >=30 kg/m2', 'Obesity: BMI 30-34.9 (class I), 35-39.9 (class II), >=40 (class III). Comorbidities: diabetes, HTN, OSA, heart disease, cancer.'),
('fdc389ea-9d4d-571c-a1f2-bbc149450f0a'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Visceral (central/abdominal) obesity:', '["Low risk clinical presentation clinical presentation","No increased risk clinical presentation clinical presentation","Protective clinical presentation clinical presentation","Waist circumference: men >102 cm, women >88 cm. Higher risk of metabolic syndrome","Same as peripheral clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Waist circumference: men >102 cm, women >88 cm. Higher risk of metabolic syndrome', 'Visceral obesity: metabolically active. Releases inflammatory cytokines. Associated with insulin resistance, dyslipidemia.'),
('4425490b-138c-58fd-a505-3399d5ecd39e'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Metabolic syndrome criteria (NCEP ATP III):', '["3 of 5: increased waist, high TG, low HDL, high BP, high fasting glucose","Only dyslipidemia clinical presentation","Only hyperglycemia clinical presentation","Only obesity clinical presentation","Only hypertension clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, '3 of 5: increased waist, high TG, low HDL, high BP, high fasting glucose', 'Metabolic syndrome: abdominal obesity, TG >=150, HDL <40(M)/<50(F), BP >=130/85, fasting glucose >=100.'),
('eea5d457-9d06-5e82-913c-c8df85b0db6e'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Metabolic syndrome treatment:', '["Bariatric surgery only clinical presentation clinical presentation","No treatment clinical presentation clinical presentation","Metformin only clinical presentation clinical presentation","Lifestyle modification (diet, exercise, weight loss), treat individual components (BP, glucose","Statins only clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Lifestyle modification (diet, exercise, weight loss), treat individual components (BP, glucose', 'Metabolic syndrome: comprehensive CVD risk reduction. Lifestyle first. Pharmacotherapy per component.'),
('3e36ec11-d5e4-55eb-b200-05f74b375b9c'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Bariatric surgery indications:', '["BMI >25 with comorbidity clinical presentation","No criteria clinical presentation","Any obesity clinical presentation","BMI >=40 or BMI >=35 with obesity-related comorbidities. Failed medical management","BMI >30 clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'BMI >=40 or BMI >=35 with obesity-related comorbidities. Failed medical management', 'Bariatric: gastric bypass (RYGB), sleeve gastrectomy, gastric band. Remission of diabetes in 60-80%.'),
('0cc7b9d5-f82e-5640-8f63-c928aa4ecad4'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Metabolic surgery (for diabetes):', '["RYGB and sleeve gastrectomy improve glycemic control independent of weight loss","Only weight loss effect clinical presentation","Worse glycemic control clinical presentation","No effect on diabetes clinical presentation","Only for obesity clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'RYGB and sleeve gastrectomy improve glycemic control independent of weight loss', 'Metabolic surgery: GLP-1 increase, altered gut hormones -> improved insulin secretion, sensitivity. Remission of T2DM.'),
('e0e6b6fe-598d-570e-9063-c83478acdf64'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Nutritional deficiencies after bariatric surgery:', '["Only iron clinical presentation","No deficiencies clinical presentation","B12, iron, folate, calcium, vitamin D, thiamine","Only folate clinical presentation","Only B12 clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'B12, iron, folate, calcium, vitamin D, thiamine', 'Bariatric: malabsorptive components cause deficiencies. RYGB: B12, iron, Ca, D. Sleeve: less malabsorption.'),
('cb1870de-d42d-5456-b546-3489582bb908'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Dumping syndrome after gastric bypass:', '["Rapid gastric emptying -> early (vasomotor) and late (hypoglycemia) symptoms. Avoid simple sugars","Benefits clinical presentation clinical presentation","Only late clinical presentation clinical presentation","No symptoms clinical presentation clinical presentation","Only early clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Rapid gastric emptying -> early (vasomotor) and late (hypoglycemia) symptoms. Avoid simple sugars', 'Dumping: early (15-30 min: tachycardia, sweating, diarrhea). Late (2-3h: hypoglycemia, tremor). Diet modification.'),
('3eca699e-73d2-561c-971e-811739d51ddf'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Enteral nutrition: standard polymeric formula:', '["Predigested nutrients clinical presentation","Elemental clinical presentation","Intact protein, complex carbs, long-chain fats","Semi-elemental clinical presentation","TPN clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Intact protein, complex carbs, long-chain fats', 'Polymeric: for patients with intact GI function. Most common type. Cost-effective. Given NG or PEG.'),
('4fd91117-caf7-5126-9c42-1d308bea48f9'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Elemental formula:', '["Predigested nutrients (amino acids, simple sugars, MCT) for severely impaired GI function","Intact protein clinical presentation clinical presentation","Semi-elemental clinical presentation clinical presentation","Standard clinical presentation clinical presentation","Polymeric clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Predigested nutrients (amino acids, simple sugars, MCT) for severely impaired GI function', 'Elemental: minimal digestion needed. For short bowel, pancreatitis, severe IBD. More expensive, hyperosmolar.'),
('1b60c9de-ad66-5148-af6e-670c55ed94a0'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Parenteral nutrition: central vs peripheral:', '["PPN high osmolarity clinical presentation clinical presentation","All via peripheral clinical presentation clinical presentation","Central (CPN/TPN): high osmolarity (>900 mOsm/L), via central vein. Peripheral (PPN): <900 mOsm/L","No difference clinical presentation clinical presentation","All via central clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Central (CPN/TPN): high osmolarity (>900 mOsm/L), via central vein. Peripheral (PPN): <900 mOsm/L', 'CPN: subclavian/IJ. PPN: short-term (<2 weeks), limited by osmolarity (phlebitis risk).'),
('0fd4177b-4285-5746-a0f3-49e0f37e81b1'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'TPN complications: metabolic:', '["Hyperglycemia, electrolyte abnormalities, hypertriglyceridemia, metabolic bone disease","Only hypoglycemia clinical presentation clinical presentation","Only infections clinical presentation clinical presentation","Only hypertriglyceridemia clinical presentation clinical presentation","No complications clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Hyperglycemia, electrolyte abnormalities, hypertriglyceridemia, metabolic bone disease', 'TPN: monitor glucose (insulin drip if needed). Refeeding syndrome if overfed. LFT elevation (steatosis, cholestasis).'),
('91a1b443-745e-51c6-8e72-d8c805004191'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'TPN complications: infectious:', '["No infectious risk clinical presentation clinical presentation","Only exit site clinical presentation clinical presentation","Only tunnel infection clinical presentation clinical presentation","Low risk clinical presentation clinical presentation","Catheter-related bloodstream infection (CLABSI). Strict aseptic technique essential"]'::jsonb, 'Catheter-related bloodstream infection (CLABSI). Strict aseptic technique essential', 'CLABSI: most serious TPN complication. Exit site, tunnel, or bloodstream. Catheter removal if septic shock.'),
('13c18a52-9026-52bb-8c1f-2acf7da571cd'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'TPN complications: mechanical:', '["Only occlusion clinical presentation clinical presentation","No mechanical risk clinical presentation clinical presentation","Only fracture clinical presentation clinical presentation","Only pneumothorax clinical presentation clinical presentation","Pneumothorax (insertion), catheter occlusion, catheter fracture/embolism, air embolism"]'::jsonb, 'Pneumothorax (insertion), catheter occlusion, catheter fracture/embolism, air embolism', 'Mechanical: during insertion (pneumothorax, arterial puncture), during use (thrombosis, occlusion, breakage).'),
('7e603fbc-b02b-51d4-95e5-b61c5b44b599'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Caloric requirements: basal energy expenditure (BEE):', '["50 kcal/kg","No equation","1500 kcal for all","Fixed 2000 kcal","Harris-Benedict equation"]'::jsonb, 'Harris-Benedict equation', 'BEE: adjusted for stress and activity. Indirect calorimetry gold standard. 25-30 kcal/kg for most patients.'),
('73f4fe7a-3e37-519b-95f3-618e4ef1e94c'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Protein requirements for surgical patients:', '["No increase","0.8 g/kg max","0.5 g/kg","3.0 g/kg","1.2-2.0 g/kg/day"]'::jsonb, '1.2-2.0 g/kg/day', 'Protein: critically ill (1.5-2.0), burns (2.0), renal failure without dialysis (0.6-0.8). Nitrogen balance measured.'),
('326275e9-5189-518d-9875-6e6ae019060a'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Fat requirements: essential fatty acid deficiency:', '["Only in starvation clinical presentation clinical presentation","After prolonged fat-free TPN -> dermatitis, poor wound healing. Linoleic/linolenic acid needed","Rare clinical presentation clinical presentation","No deficiency risk clinical presentation clinical presentation","Only in children clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'After prolonged fat-free TPN -> dermatitis, poor wound healing. Linoleic/linolenic acid needed', 'EFA deficiency: prevent with 20% lipid infusion 2-3x/week. Linoleic (omega-6) and linolenic (omega-3).'),
('f7a5a1ad-d1db-5d02-98ed-d25d1a0ff502'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Omega-3 fatty acids (fish oil):', '["Harmful clinical presentation clinical presentation","Only in supplements clinical presentation clinical presentation","Pro-inflammatory clinical presentation clinical presentation","Anti-inflammatory (used in some ICU/TNP formulations). May reduce inflammation in pancreatitis, ARDS","No benefit clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Anti-inflammatory (used in some ICU/TNP formulations). May reduce inflammation in pancreatitis, ARDS', 'Omega-3: EPA/DHA. Used in modified lipid emulsions (Omegaven, Smoflipid). May reduce mortality in ARDS.'),
('15516102-6dbe-5f5b-9d85-aba7e43e8f26'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Glutamine:', '["Conditionally essential amino acid. Important for enterocytes, immune cells. Supplemented in severe illness/burns","Not needed clinical presentation clinical presentation","Harmful clinical presentation clinical presentation","Only for athletes clinical presentation clinical presentation","No evidence clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Conditionally essential amino acid. Important for enterocytes, immune cells. Supplemented in severe illness/burns', 'Glutamine: major fuel for enterocytes. Enhances gut barrier function. May reduce infections in trauma/burns.'),
('e2605d81-51cd-59be-87ee-b0116c9a7959'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Arginine:', '["Not needed clinical presentation clinical presentation","Only for renal clinical presentation clinical presentation","Harmful clinical presentation clinical presentation","Only for athletes clinical presentation clinical presentation","Conditionally essential. NO precursor, immune modulation. Immunonutrition in surgical patients"]'::jsonb, 'Conditionally essential. NO precursor, immune modulation. Immunonutrition in surgical patients', 'Arginine: NO -> vasodilation. Controversial in sepsis (may increase NO -> hypotension). Used in IMPACT/immunonutrition formulas.'),
('807de5f2-1018-5c1c-8581-2552157ab68a'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Immunonutrition (formulas with arginine, glutamine, omega-3, nucleotides):', '["Not studied clinical presentation","Increases infection clinical presentation","Harmful clinical presentation","Reduces infectious complications in elective upper GI surgery","No benefit clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Reduces infectious complications in elective upper GI surgery', 'Immunonutrition: preoperative 5-7 days, postoperative 5-7 days. Reduces SSI, LOS. Not for septic patients.'),
('fe3cbc54-7e3a-5b31-86ab-c8df33e9773a'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Malnutrition screening tools (MUST, NRS-2002, SGA):', '["All patients same clinical presentation clinical presentation","Not needed clinical presentation clinical presentation","Only for ICU clinical presentation clinical presentation","Only for elderly clinical presentation clinical presentation","Identify patients at nutritional risk who may benefit from nutrition support"]'::jsonb, 'Identify patients at nutritional risk who may benefit from nutrition support', 'Screening: MUST (community), NRS-2002 (hospital), SGA (clinical assessment). Poor nutrition -> worse outcomes.'),
('8262a344-a72f-553c-ba79-d6899c59dcb5'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Subjective Global Assessment (SGA):', '["Anthropometry only clinical presentation clinical presentation","Objective only clinical presentation clinical presentation","Lab tests only clinical presentation clinical presentation","Clinical assessment based on history (weight loss, dietary intake, GI symptoms","No physical exam clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Clinical assessment based on history (weight loss, dietary intake, GI symptoms', 'SGA: well-nourished, mild-moderate (SGA-B), severe (SGA-C). Predictive of complications. No lab needed.'),
('362b24ed-669a-56bc-81b3-202caf913f5a'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Preoperative nutritional support duration:', '["No support needed clinical presentation clinical presentation","7-14 days of nutritional support before major surgery if severely malnourished","3-5 days clinical presentation clinical presentation","24h clinical presentation clinical presentation","1 month clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, '7-14 days of nutritional support before major surgery if severely malnourished', 'Preoperative nutrition: 7-14 days before major surgery. Improves outcomes (reduced complications, LOS).'),
('0680ddcb-2a2d-50e2-9704-db98da261806'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Postoperative nutrition: early enteral feeding:', '["TPN first clinical presentation","No feeding for 5 days clinical presentation","Start within 24-48h post-op (even after GI surgery). Reduces ileus, infections, LOS","NPO until flatus clinical presentation","Clear liquids only 7 days clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Start within 24-48h post-op (even after GI surgery). Reduces ileus, infections, LOS', 'Early EN: ERAS protocol. Stimulates gut motility. Improves wound healing. Safe even with GI anastomosis.'),
('ce06cb64-9cec-58c7-8e1b-57c867f05f27'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Refeeding syndrome: definition:', '["Life-threatening metabolic derangement when refeeding malnourished patients","Only in alcoholism clinical presentation clinical presentation","No risk clinical presentation clinical presentation","Only in elderly clinical presentation clinical presentation","Only in anorexia clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Life-threatening metabolic derangement when refeeding malnourished patients', 'Refeeding syndrome: after prolonged starvation (anorexia, alcoholism, prolonged NPO, malabsorption). Hypophosphatemia hallmark.'),
('f64f3aaa-acbe-51f5-8701-ad537a2d6ab4'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Refeeding syndrome: cardiac complications:', '["Only GI","Only renal","Arrhythmias, heart failure","No cardiac effect","Only neuro"]'::jsonb, 'Arrhythmias, heart failure', 'Cardiac: hypophosphatemia -> ATP depletion -> myocardial dysfunction. Fluid shifts -> heart failure. Prevent by slow refeeding.'),
('1ccd32ef-ff05-5f08-b35e-14b566c7fc73'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Refeeding syndrome: prevention protocol:', '["Check once clinical presentation clinical presentation","No monitoring clinical presentation clinical presentation","No replacement clinical presentation clinical presentation","Start 20-25 kcal/kg/day (or 50% of target). Monitor electrolytes (P, K, Mg","Full feeding clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Start 20-25 kcal/kg/day (or 50% of target). Monitor electrolytes (P, K, Mg', 'Refeeding: identify high-risk patients. Replace electrolytes before starting. Thiamine before calories. Advance slowly over 7-10 days.'),
('7503a36c-9cd4-5c45-8daf-f2edd76a9f6f'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Thiamine supplementation before refeeding:', '["Only B12 clinical presentation","Not needed clinical presentation","Only if deficient clinical presentation","Give thiamine 100-300 mg/day to prevent Wernicke encephalopathy","After feeding clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Give thiamine 100-300 mg/day to prevent Wernicke encephalopathy', 'Thiamine: carbohydrate metabolism requires thiamine. Give before initiating feeding. Prevents acute thiamine deficiency.'),
('522ee899-a3a0-5a06-aad7-1c4bdec4ab7d'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Electrolyte monitoring in refeeding:', '["No monitoring clinical presentation clinical presentation","Only if symptomatic clinical presentation clinical presentation","Only day 1 clinical presentation clinical presentation","Check once weekly clinical presentation clinical presentation","Check phosphate, K, Mg, Ca at baseline, then q6-12h for first 3 days. Replace IV if low"]'::jsonb, 'Check phosphate, K, Mg, Ca at baseline, then q6-12h for first 3 days. Replace IV if low', 'Monitoring: P most critical (<1.5 mg/dL severe). Replace with IV phosphate (KPhos or NaPhos). Mg and K also low.'),
('ff3d7769-f699-5cf3-bedc-fdfdeb78dfec'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Starvation: metabolic adaptation:', '["Only protein use clinical presentation clinical presentation","Constant glucose use clinical presentation clinical presentation","Only fat use clinical presentation clinical presentation","Initial: glycogenolysis (24h). Then gluconeogenesis (protein breakdown) + ketosis (fatty acid ->","No adaptation clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Initial: glycogenolysis (24h). Then gluconeogenesis (protein breakdown) + ketosis (fatty acid ->', 'Starvation: brain adapts to ketones. Protein sparing after 2-3 weeks. Basal metabolic rate decreases.'),
('fb20365a-9f67-5778-9b84-a325b466fe8c'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Ketosis vs ketoacidosis:', '["Ketosis (starvation/diet): mild, compensated, low insulin. Ketoacidosis (DKA): high ketones","Same clinical presentation clinical presentation","No difference clinical presentation clinical presentation","Ketosis = acidosis clinical presentation clinical presentation","Ketosis = DKA clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Ketosis (starvation/diet): mild, compensated, low insulin. Ketoacidosis (DKA): high ketones', 'Starvation: ketones (beta-hydroxybutyrate) moderate. DKA: massive ketones + severe acidosis + hyperglycemia.'),
('745075b1-776b-5fb6-ab6d-131bd0f22079'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Body mass index (BMI) formula:', '["Weight^2/height","Height^2/weight","Height/weight^2","Weight (kg) / height (m)^2","Weight x height"]'::jsonb, 'Weight (kg) / height (m)^2', 'BMI: <18.5 underweight, 18.5-24.9 normal, 25-29.9 overweight, >=30 obese. Morbid: >=40.'),
('8a5ed46e-a519-51fd-aa70-cd0c12f32734'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Waist-hip ratio (WHR):', '["Waist circumference / hip circumference. >0.9 (M) or >0.85 (F) = central obesity","Hip/waist clinical presentation clinical presentation","No ratio clinical presentation clinical presentation","Hip only clinical presentation clinical presentation","Waist only clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Waist circumference / hip circumference. >0.9 (M) or >0.85 (F) = central obesity', 'WHR: better predictor of CVD/MetS than BMI alone. Waist measured at midpoint between lower rib and iliac crest.'),
('c0a4e096-284f-588c-bae8-84ba32816fdc'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Bioelectrical impedance analysis (BIA):', '["Ultrasound clinical presentation clinical presentation","Calipers clinical presentation clinical presentation","X-ray clinical presentation clinical presentation","Hydrostatic weighing clinical presentation clinical presentation","Estimates body composition (fat mass, lean mass) by resistance to electrical current"]'::jsonb, 'Estimates body composition (fat mass, lean mass) by resistance to electrical current', 'BIA: inexpensive, non-invasive. Hydration status affects accuracy. Used for body fat % estimation.'),
('43b53a8e-177e-5deb-9b48-0a7cc1917f54'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Dual-energy X-ray absorptiometry (DEXA):', '["CT scan clinical presentation","BIA clinical presentation","MRI clinical presentation","Gold standard for body composition","Ultrasound clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Gold standard for body composition', 'DEXA: low radiation. Osteoporosis diagnosis (T-score). Body composition research. Widely available.'),
('92a61498-37ea-5983-8711-ac17d91e69a8'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Causes of malnutrition in surgical patients:', '["Only cancer clinical presentation clinical presentation","Pre-existing disease, cancer cachexia, prolonged NPO, catabolic response, malabsorption","Only malabsorption clinical presentation clinical presentation","Only NPO clinical presentation clinical presentation","No causes clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Pre-existing disease, cancer cachexia, prolonged NPO, catabolic response, malabsorption', 'Surgical malnutrition: multifactorial. Preoperative assessment scores (NRS-2002, SGA) predict outcomes.'),
('401e24e1-c04e-5d49-872a-746c1874a4ed'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Cancer cachexia hallmarks:', '["No inflammation clinical presentation","Same as starvation clinical presentation","Only anorexia clinical presentation","Weight loss, muscle wasting, anorexia, systemic inflammation","Only weight loss clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Weight loss, muscle wasting, anorexia, systemic inflammation', 'Cachexia: different from starvation (more muscle loss, less fat sparing). Cytokine-mediated. Prognostic for survival.'),
('29984dbf-c8b8-552a-b17a-c049309f3f80'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Cachexia treatment:', '["No treatment clinical presentation clinical presentation","TPN only clinical presentation clinical presentation","Appetite stimulants only clinical presentation clinical presentation","Multimodal: nutrition support, anti-inflammatory agents (omega-3), anabolic agents, exercise","High-calorie feeds only clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Multimodal: nutrition support, anti-inflammatory agents (omega-3), anabolic agents, exercise', 'Cachexia: difficult to reverse. Omega-3 (EPA) may reduce cytokine effects. Megestrol acetate for appetite (limited benefit).'),
('5f7cb10d-2231-5a54-a46c-7d51a2d9f708'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Sarcopenia:', '["Only in obese clinical presentation clinical presentation","Age-related loss of muscle mass and strength. Risk factor for falls, frailty, mortality","Same as malnutrition clinical presentation clinical presentation","Same as cachexia clinical presentation clinical presentation","Not important clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Age-related loss of muscle mass and strength. Risk factor for falls, frailty, mortality', 'Sarcopenia: diagnostic criteria (low muscle mass + low strength + low performance). Treatment: resistance exercise + protein.'),
('6770d5c4-7e79-53c1-acac-6511b3ceb834'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Dynapenia:', '["Increases strength clinical presentation clinical presentation","Beneficial clinical presentation clinical presentation","Same as sarcopenia clinical presentation clinical presentation","No loss clinical presentation clinical presentation","Age-related loss of muscle strength (not necessarily mass). Predicts functional decline"]'::jsonb, 'Age-related loss of muscle strength (not necessarily mass). Predicts functional decline', 'Dynapenia: strength loss precedes muscle mass loss. Early intervention with resistance training.'),
('19b7d152-5e70-56f4-8924-c67641d1acdf'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Frailty in surgery:', '["Decreased physiologic reserve -> increased complications, mortality, LOS, institutionalization","No effect clinical presentation clinical presentation","Beneficial clinical presentation clinical presentation","Same as robust clinical presentation clinical presentation","Only in elderly >90 clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Decreased physiologic reserve -> increased complications, mortality, LOS, institutionalization', 'Frailty: Fried phenotype (weight loss, exhaustion, weakness, slow gait, low activity). Preoperative frailty predicts outcomes.'),
('dfbd2db7-3ecf-51fa-958d-f3a580cf76c7'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Perioperative nutrition: ''nil per os'' (NPO) guidelines:', '["24h for all clinical presentation","12h for liquids clinical presentation","6h for solids, 2h for clear liquids","No fasting needed clinical presentation","NPO after midnight clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, '6h for solids, 2h for clear liquids', 'NPO: standard 6-2-0 rule. Clear CHO drinks 2h pre-op reduce insulin resistance. Do not use in gastroparesis/obstruction.'),
('5de8abca-3a82-54ef-9985-68366b698597'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Protein-calorie malnutrition: serum markers:', '["Only prealbumin clinical presentation clinical presentation","No markers clinical presentation clinical presentation","Albumin (<3.5), prealbumin (<15), transferrin, RBP (retinol-binding protein","Only albumin clinical presentation clinical presentation","Only transferrin clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Albumin (<3.5), prealbumin (<15), transferrin, RBP (retinol-binding protein', 'Albumin: half-life 20d (not acute). Prealbumin: half-life 2d (better for monitoring). Both decrease in inflammation (negative acute phase).'),
('18c33bd3-daff-58b8-b370-c02c7dd575a0'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Phase angle (BIA) as nutritional/prognostic marker:', '["No prognostic value clinical presentation","Only in athletes clinical presentation","High = malnutrition clinical presentation","Low phase angle associated with malnutrition, higher morbidity/mortality","No relation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Low phase angle associated with malnutrition, higher morbidity/mortality', 'Phase angle: BIA parameter. Low phase angle -> impaired cell membrane integrity. Associated with mortality in cancer, HIV, elderly.'),
('5ab7434d-0c7f-51d5-b3bd-5955e28d38e1'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Nutritional support in acute pancreatitis:', '["No nutrition for 7 days clinical presentation","Total parenteral nutrition first clinical presentation","NPO only clinical presentation","TPN only clinical presentation","Enteral nutrition (NG/NJ) preferred over TPN. Start within 24-48h if tolerated"]'::jsonb, 'Enteral nutrition (NG/NJ) preferred over TPN. Start within 24-48h if tolerated', 'Pancreatitis: EN maintains gut barrier, reduces infections, complications. NJ if gastric intolerance. Start low rate, advance.'),
('1a8ac91f-2a3a-50e3-968d-2640082ffa1a'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Chronic pancreatitis: nutritional deficiencies:', '["Only B12 clinical presentation clinical presentation","Fat-soluble vitamins (A, D, E, K), B12, calcium, magnesium. Pancreatic enzyme replacement needed","Only vitamin D clinical presentation clinical presentation","No deficiencies clinical presentation clinical presentation","Only calcium clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Fat-soluble vitamins (A, D, E, K), B12, calcium, magnesium. Pancreatic enzyme replacement needed', 'Chronic pancreatitis: maldigestion (lipase, protease deficiency). PERT (Creon) with meals. Fat-soluble vitamins.'),
('c5353708-8ff9-59a6-b164-9dfcd29ce9ed'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Short bowel syndrome (SBS): nutritional management:', '["Low carb clinical presentation clinical presentation","Normal diet clinical presentation clinical presentation","High fiber only clinical presentation clinical presentation","TPN initially, transition to EN as bowel adapts. High-carb, low-fat diet (or high-fat + MCT","No treatment clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'TPN initially, transition to EN as bowel adapts. High-carb, low-fat diet (or high-fat + MCT', 'SBS: intestinal adaptation (months-years). Give MCT (portal absorption), LCT need lymphatics. Parenteral nutrition if inadequate adaptation.'),
('94f38177-4fb9-5949-b628-275f9363757d'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Intestinal failure:', '["No need for PN clinical presentation clinical presentation","Same as SBS clinical presentation clinical presentation","Self-limited clinical presentation clinical presentation","No treatment clinical presentation clinical presentation","Inability to maintain nutrition/fluid/electrolyte balance without parenteral support"]'::jsonb, 'Inability to maintain nutrition/fluid/electrolyte balance without parenteral support', 'Intestinal failure: type I (acute, short-term), type II (prolonged, >28d), type III (chronic, needs home PN).'),
('cb8a70c6-5285-5d0d-a736-41be52965cab'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Liver disease: nutritional assessment:', '["Malnutrition common (50-90%). Branched-chain amino acids (BCAA) may be helpful in encephalopathy","Normal nutrition clinical presentation clinical presentation","No malnutrition clinical presentation clinical presentation","Always well-nourished clinical presentation clinical presentation","No special needs clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Malnutrition common (50-90%). Branched-chain amino acids (BCAA) may be helpful in encephalopathy', 'Cirrhosis: malnutrition from poor intake, malabsorption, hypermetabolism. BCAA: reduce encephalopathy. Protein restriction not recommended.'),
('c196c5cb-22f4-5062-859d-85f7d1b4c3eb'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Renal disease: nutritional considerations:', '["Free diet clinical presentation clinical presentation","High protein always clinical presentation clinical presentation","No restrictions clinical presentation clinical presentation","Protein restriction (pre-dialysis: 0.6-0.8 g/kg). K, P, Na restriction. Vitamin D","Only fluid restriction clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Protein restriction (pre-dialysis: 0.6-0.8 g/kg). K, P, Na restriction. Vitamin D', 'CKD: reduce phosphorus (binders), potassium, sodium. Protein restriction slows progression. Dialysis: increase protein (1.2 g/kg).'),
('83ccbbf8-22a1-5614-92ad-cc64d045a854'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Post-gastrectomy nutritional deficiencies:', '["No deficiencies clinical presentation","Only iron clinical presentation","Only folate clinical presentation","B12 (loss of IF), iron, folate, calcium, vitamin D, weight loss","Only B12 clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'B12 (loss of IF), iron, folate, calcium, vitamin D, weight loss', 'Post-gastrectomy: B12 (IM monthly), iron, Ca/D supplements. Small frequent meals. Avoid simple sugars.'),
('276c58fd-e9a8-577f-83df-e008528e558e'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Ileal resection (>100 cm) nutritional issues:', '["B12 deficiency (loss of absorption site), bile salt diarrhea, fat malabsorption","Only B12 clinical presentation clinical presentation","No issues clinical presentation clinical presentation","Only diarrhea clinical presentation clinical presentation","Only stones clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'B12 deficiency (loss of absorption site), bile salt diarrhea, fat malabsorption', 'Ileal resection: B12 (IM), bile acid binding (cholestyramine for diarrhea), low-oxalate diet (stones).'),
('cfd55d7a-0e5a-50ab-be97-b9491c7cc9a9'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Obesity paradox in surgery:', '["No paradox clinical presentation clinical presentation","Not studied clinical presentation clinical presentation","Obesity always harmful clinical presentation clinical presentation","Obese patients may have lower short-term mortality after certain surgeries (but higher overall co","Obesity protective always clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Obese patients may have lower short-term mortality after certain surgeries (but higher overall co', 'Obesity paradox: higher infections/wound issues but lower mortality in some conditions (MI, HF, post-op).'),
('b00c0554-a432-5c6c-a8fb-689c17331932'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Nutritional support in the ICU:', '["EN only day 10 clinical presentation clinical presentation","TPN always clinical presentation clinical presentation","No nutrition for 7 days clinical presentation clinical presentation","EN preferred (start within 24-48h). PN if EN not possible (start day 3-7). Target 25-30 kcal/kg/day","Only EN clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'EN preferred (start within 24-48h). PN if EN not possible (start day 3-7). Target 25-30 kcal/kg/day', 'ICU: early EN -> reduced complications. If high risk of refeeding, start at 10-15 kcal/kg and advance. Trophic EN (10-20 mL/h) may be enough.'),
('97e1c230-382b-5445-9130-9764af3784e9'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Permissive underfeeding in ICU:', '["Deliberately giving less than full caloric requirements (65-70%) in early critical illness","Not studied clinical presentation clinical presentation","Full feeding day 1 clinical presentation clinical presentation","Overfeeding clinical presentation clinical presentation","No feeding clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Deliberately giving less than full caloric requirements (65-70%) in early critical illness', 'Permissive underfeeding: may reduce complications (hyperglycemia, infection). Adequate protein is more critical than calories early.'),
('5b7e74db-d20c-5a5e-a2a2-7915d43f6d5e'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Enteral feeding: NG vs NJ (nasojejunal):', '["NJ no benefit","No difference","NG always","NJ always","NJ: post-pyloric"]'::jsonb, 'NJ: post-pyloric', 'NJ: placed distal to pylorus. Continuous drip. Used for gastric outlet obstruction, severe gastroparesis, pancreatitis.'),
('7df4d68e-1b11-508d-bf96-c91e3963e07a'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'PEG tube complications:', '["Only bleeding clinical presentation clinical presentation","Only infection clinical presentation clinical presentation","Peristomal infection, bleeding, perforation, buried bumper, necrotizing fasciitis","Only perforation clinical presentation clinical presentation","No complications clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Peristomal infection, bleeding, perforation, buried bumper, necrotizing fasciitis', 'PEG: infection (<3% with prophylactic antibiotics). Buried bumper: PEG tube migrated into gastric wall. Necrotizing fasciitis rare but fatal.'),
('5cdb722f-e76f-5ef5-a663-61d961bd9de7'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'PEG vs RIG (radiologically inserted gastrostomy):', '["No difference clinical presentation clinical presentation","Same as PEG clinical presentation clinical presentation","RIG uses fluoroscopy (no endoscopy needed). Can place if esophageal obstruction","RIG always better clinical presentation clinical presentation","PEG always better clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'RIG uses fluoroscopy (no endoscopy needed). Can place if esophageal obstruction', 'RIG: for patients with pharyngeal/esophageal obstruction (cannot pass endoscope). PEG: preferred if feasible (lower complication).'),
('dbd85267-94e3-577a-9aa7-9800de82fce1'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Total parenteral nutrition: micronutrient supplementation:', '["Only Se clinical presentation clinical presentation","Not needed clinical presentation clinical presentation","Daily vitamins (multivitamin), trace elements (Zn, Cu, Se, Cr, Mn, I). Mn accumulates in cholestasis","Only Cu clinical presentation clinical presentation","Only Zn clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Daily vitamins (multivitamin), trace elements (Zn, Cu, Se, Cr, Mn, I). Mn accumulates in cholestasis', 'TPN micronutrients: standard multivitamin + trace element preparations. Mn toxicity (parkinsonism) if cholestasis. Reduce Mn.'),
('c56ffdf1-8e36-5089-88ff-bcbc5b058a3c'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Parenteral iron in TPN:', '["Add to all TPN clinical presentation clinical presentation","Never use clinical presentation clinical presentation","Always safe clinical presentation clinical presentation","Only Fe dextran clinical presentation clinical presentation","Not routinely added (risk of anaphylaxis with iron dextran). Separate IV iron infusion if needed"]'::jsonb, 'Not routinely added (risk of anaphylaxis with iron dextran). Separate IV iron infusion if needed', 'Iron in TPN: incompatible with lipid emulsion. Give IV iron separately. Iron sucrose/ferric carboxymaltose safer.'),
('536f9b7e-e436-55fa-85d9-9dbec0b2169d'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Fat emulsion in TPN: complications:', '["Only infection","Only hypertriglyceridemia","No complications","Hypertriglyceridemia, fat overload syndrome","Always safe"]'::jsonb, 'Hypertriglyceridemia, fat overload syndrome', 'Lipid emulsions: check triglycerides. Soybean oil (Intralipid) -> omega-6 (pro-inflammatory). Fish oil (Omegaven) -> anti-inflammatory.'),
('88c5af5d-e39f-57b0-911f-b56abb244647'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Parenteral nutrition-associated liver disease (PNALD):', '["Reversible without Tx clinical presentation clinical presentation","Cholestasis, steatosis, cholelithiasis. More common in SBS. Fish oil may prevent/reverse","No liver disease clinical presentation clinical presentation","Only cholestasis clinical presentation clinical presentation","Only steatosis clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Cholestasis, steatosis, cholelithiasis. More common in SBS. Fish oil may prevent/reverse', 'PNALD: multifactorial. Overfeeding, lack of enteral stimulation, phytosterols. Cycling TPN, fish oil lipids, and EN adjunct help.'),
('7f146783-226d-5d61-85fa-ea3ca43f91d5'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Metabolic bone disease in long-term TPN:', '["Reversible","Only from Al","No bone disease","Osteoporosis/osteomalacia","Only from D deficiency"]'::jsonb, 'Osteoporosis/osteomalacia', 'TPN bone: provide adequate Ca, P, D. Avoid Al (contaminant in TPN components). Monitoring DEXA.'),
('6e032b57-aa96-54e7-864a-581831ccb87a'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Glucose management in TPN:', '["Add regular insulin to TPN (sliding scale initially, then 0.1-0.15 U/g glucose","No insulin clinical presentation clinical presentation","No glucose monitoring clinical presentation clinical presentation","Fixed insulin dose clinical presentation clinical presentation","Target <250 clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Add regular insulin to TPN (sliding scale initially, then 0.1-0.15 U/g glucose', 'TPN insulin: start with 0.1 U/g carbohydrate. Adjust based on glucose monitoring. Insulin pumps if needed.'),
('783475d3-1327-5f99-a5d0-618a316e6150'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Cyclic TPN (vs continuous):', '["Infuse over 12-16 hours (not 24h). More physiologic, less hepatotoxicity, improves QOL","No benefit clinical presentation clinical presentation","Always continuous clinical presentation clinical presentation","Only at home clinical presentation clinical presentation","Worse tolerance clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Infuse over 12-16 hours (not 24h). More physiologic, less hepatotoxicity, improves QOL', 'Cyclic TPN: daytime/nighttime cycling. Fat clearance improved. Less cholestasis. Allows mobility.'),
('b339a848-354f-5852-9fe6-216e3fed5fcc'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Home TPN (HPN) indications:', '["All SBS clinical presentation clinical presentation","Intestinal failure (SBS, pseudo-obstruction, radiation enteritis) - long-term support needed","All IBD clinical presentation clinical presentation","Short-term recovery clinical presentation clinical presentation","Only cancer clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Intestinal failure (SBS, pseudo-obstruction, radiation enteritis) - long-term support needed', 'HPN: training for patient/caregiver. Supplies, monitoring, and multidisciplinary team. QOL considerations.'),
('4d12b93e-a9b8-5c2b-b747-3fe8e1775d42'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Nutrition support team:', '["Dietitian only clinical presentation clinical presentation","Not needed clinical presentation clinical presentation","MD only clinical presentation clinical presentation","Multidisciplinary (MD, dietitian, nurse, pharmacist). Manages PN/EN, reduces complications","Nurse only clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Multidisciplinary (MD, dietitian, nurse, pharmacist). Manages PN/EN, reduces complications', 'NST: reduces PN complications (CLABSI, metabolic). Develops protocols. Provides education and monitoring.'),
('5f064033-fe03-5ee0-a820-23a4ec904323'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Dietary fiber types:', '["Only insoluble clinical presentation clinical presentation","Same clinical presentation clinical presentation","Soluble (viscous - pectin, gums): lowers glucose, cholesterol. Insoluble (cellulose","No difference clinical presentation clinical presentation","Only soluble clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Soluble (viscous - pectin, gums): lowers glucose, cholesterol. Insoluble (cellulose', 'Fiber: soluble (oats, fruit, legumes). Insoluble (wheat bran, vegetables). 25-38 g/day recommended.'),
('67f55bca-e2ff-5fa7-9666-bdd221a97bf2'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Prebiotics vs probiotics vs synbiotics:', '["Synbiotic = just nutrients clinical presentation clinical presentation","Probiotic = prebiotic clinical presentation clinical presentation","No difference clinical presentation clinical presentation","Prebiotic: food for bacteria (fiber/inulin). Probiotic: live beneficial bacteria. Synbiotic","Same clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Prebiotic: food for bacteria (fiber/inulin). Probiotic: live beneficial bacteria. Synbiotic', 'Gut microbiome: prebiotics (inulin, FOS), probiotics (Lactobacillus, Bifidobacterium, Saccharomyces), synbiotics (combined).'),
('4c8f8d3e-ff64-5607-86cd-2bede0dd97b6'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Probiotics in surgery:', '["In pancreatitis clinical presentation","Always indicated clinical presentation","May reduce postoperative infections, antibiotic","No benefit clinical presentation","Harmful clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'May reduce postoperative infections, antibiotic', 'Probiotics: caution in immunocompromised (risk of bacteremia with live organisms). Not in severe acute pancreatitis (increased mortality).'),
('8f7d5569-9195-5fe9-840f-d3d2fca34d18'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Immunonutrition (perioperative):', '["No benefit clinical presentation clinical presentation","Only arginine clinical presentation clinical presentation","Only glutamine clinical presentation clinical presentation","Only omega-3 clinical presentation clinical presentation","Arginine, glutamine, omega-3, nucleotides. Preop 5-7 days + postop. Reduces infections in GI surgery"]'::jsonb, 'Arginine, glutamine, omega-3, nucleotides. Preop 5-7 days + postop. Reduces infections in GI surgery', 'Immunonutrition: IMPACT, PeriOpe, Crucial. Reduces infectious complications (SSI, pneumonia) and LOS in elective surgery.'),
('33bef288-6aca-5321-9e92-66869547459a'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'With respect to management, Preoperative carbohydrate loading (ERAS):', '["50-100 g CHO 2-4h before surgery. Reduces insulin resistance, anxiety, thirst","No benefit clinical presentation","Increases insulin resistance clinical presentation","Not recommended clinical presentation","Causes aspiration clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, '50-100 g CHO 2-4h before surgery. Reduces insulin resistance, anxiety, thirst', 'CHO loading: safe (clear liquid). Reduces post-op hyperglycemia, improves recovery. Do not use in delayed gastric emptying.'),
('957f202b-930b-559e-83c3-bedf5fa8612a'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'ERAS: multimodal analgesia:', '["Only NSAIDs","Opioids only","Only acetaminophen","No pain control","Avoid opioids"]'::jsonb, 'Avoid opioids', 'ERAS pain: regional anesthesia, non-opioid analgesics. Reduces opioid side effects, ileus, length of stay.'),
('ecacb764-ff89-5f29-8b8e-87690a45f720'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'ERAS: early mobilization:', '["Day 3 clinical presentation clinical presentation","No mobilization clinical presentation clinical presentation","Out of bed day of surgery, walking POD 0-1. Reduces pneumonia, DVT, deconditioning","No benefit clinical presentation clinical presentation","Bed rest 3 days clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Out of bed day of surgery, walking POD 0-1. Reduces pneumonia, DVT, deconditioning', 'ERAS mobilization: sit out of bed day 0, walk day 1. Promotes recovery, reduces complications.'),
('0301e4ba-80eb-55aa-a46f-a8d4c67dfb9b'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'ERAS: maintenance of normothermia:', '["No warming needed clinical presentation clinical presentation","Mild hypothermia fine clinical presentation clinical presentation","Cold improves recovery clinical presentation clinical presentation","No effect on SSI clinical presentation clinical presentation","Warm IV fluids, forced-air warming, warm OR. Prevents coagulopathy, SSI, cardiac events"]'::jsonb, 'Warm IV fluids, forced-air warming, warm OR. Prevents coagulopathy, SSI, cardiac events', 'Normothermia: maintain >36 C. Hypothermia -> impaired immunity, platelet function, shivering (increased O2 consumption).'),
('6cfd29ef-42c2-5781-8a54-eb268de8ef78'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'ERAS: fluid management (euvolemia):', '["No fluids clinical presentation clinical presentation","No monitoring clinical presentation clinical presentation","Goal-directed therapy (avoid overload). Stop IV fluids early if tolerating oral","Generous fluids clinical presentation clinical presentation","Keep dehydrated clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Goal-directed therapy (avoid overload). Stop IV fluids early if tolerating oral', 'ERAS: avoid fluid overload (impaired healing, ileus). Weight gain >2.5 kg associated with complications.'),
('09f60813-23b6-5989-8fd1-79f80cb9a62b'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Nutrition in burn patients:', '["Low protein clinical presentation clinical presentation","High calorie (30-35 kcal + 30-40 kcal/%TBSA burn), high protein (2-3 g/kg/day). Start early EN","Standard diet clinical presentation clinical presentation","No special needs clinical presentation clinical presentation","Low calorie clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'High calorie (30-35 kcal + 30-40 kcal/%TBSA burn), high protein (2-3 g/kg/day). Start early EN', 'Burns: hypermetabolic (up to 2x BMR). EN via NG (start within 6h). Protein for healing. Supplement vitamins A, C, Zn.'),
('1d27f8d6-7a67-52fe-b828-ffd370940d5b'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Nutrition in head and neck cancer surgery:', '["Only liquids clinical presentation clinical presentation","Standard diet clinical presentation clinical presentation","High malnutrition risk (dysphagia, obstruction). Prophylactic PEG often placed. Speech/swallow th","No NG needed clinical presentation clinical presentation","No special needs clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'High malnutrition risk (dysphagia, obstruction). Prophylactic PEG often placed. Speech/swallow th', 'Head/neck: malnutrition in 50-70%. Prophylactic PEG before chemoradiation prevents weight loss. Nutritional support essential.'),
('2935563e-ea2e-51b2-9677-3edad03277c2'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Nutrition in trauma patients:', '["TPN preferred clinical presentation clinical presentation","Low protein clinical presentation clinical presentation","Delayed feeding clinical presentation clinical presentation","No feeding clinical presentation clinical presentation","Early EN (within 24-48h). High protein (1.5-2.0 g/kg). Immunonutrition (glutamine"]'::jsonb, 'Early EN (within 24-48h). High protein (1.5-2.0 g/kg). Immunonutrition (glutamine', 'Trauma: hypermetabolic. EN reduces infections. Feeding beyond ligament of Treitz if gastric ileus. Consider immunonutrition.'),
('f0dc904f-a319-52b0-9fec-97e05adc250b'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Perioperative immunonutrition for GI cancer surgery:', '["Harmful clinical presentation clinical presentation","Preoperative (5-7d) and postoperative (5-7d) arginine, omega-3, nucleotides. Reduces infectious c","Only postop clinical presentation clinical presentation","Only preop clinical presentation clinical presentation","No benefit clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Preoperative (5-7d) and postoperative (5-7d) arginine, omega-3, nucleotides. Reduces infectious c', 'GI cancer: immunonutrition reduces SSI, anastomotic leak, LOS. Give pre/post surgery. Cost-effective in high-risk.'),
('c4f926cd-8123-5906-88cc-45450ef54f0a'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Fish oil in ICU (parenteral):', '["Worse outcomes clinical presentation clinical presentation","Omega-3 rich lipid emulsions (Smoflipid, Omegaven). May reduce inflammation, LOS","Not used clinical presentation clinical presentation","No benefit clinical presentation clinical presentation","Standard lipids clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Omega-3 rich lipid emulsions (Smoflipid, Omegaven). May reduce inflammation, LOS', 'Fish oil: reduces pro-inflammatory cytokines (LTB4 -> LTB5). Studies in ARDS, sepsis mixed. May reduce VAP and LOS.'),
('769951cb-a9ac-5ec6-a6ed-bbc37d48fba0'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Antioxidants in critical illness:', '["Selenium, vitamins C and E","For all ICU patients","Harmful","No evidence","No benefit"]'::jsonb, 'Selenium, vitamins C and E', 'Antioxidants: oxidative stress in critical illness. Selenium (200-400 mcg/day) + vitamins C/E. REDOXS study: high-dose glutamine + antioxidants increased mortality.'),
('6c80681f-5120-54f7-a0d3-a6963b4223f8'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Vitamin C in sepsis (IV high dose):', '["1.5 g/kg IV may reduce mortality (controversial). CITRIS-ALI trial: no mortality benefit but redu","Not studied clinical presentation clinical presentation","Standard dose clinical presentation clinical presentation","No benefit clinical presentation clinical presentation","Harmful clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, '1.5 g/kg IV may reduce mortality (controversial). CITRIS-ALI trial: no mortality benefit but redu', 'Vitamin C in sepsis: high dose IV. CITRIS-ALI: reduced SOFA, biomarkers. Mortality benefit uncertain (small trials).'),
('675a34b0-e4f2-55de-8a09-380a2506a843'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Zinc in critical illness:', '["Zinc deficiency in 50% of ICU. Supplementation may reduce organ failure","No deficiency clinical presentation clinical presentation","Harmful clinical presentation clinical presentation","No benefit clinical presentation clinical presentation","Supplement all clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Zinc deficiency in 50% of ICU. Supplementation may reduce organ failure', 'ICU zinc: acute phase -> hypozincemia (redistribution). Routine supplementation not established. Research ongoing.'),
('d209bab1-691c-554f-9115-95c3f91cda88'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Glutamine in critical illness:', '["Only renal clinical presentation clinical presentation","All ICU benefit clinical presentation clinical presentation","No benefit clinical presentation clinical presentation","Reduces infectious complications in trauma/burns. May increase mortality in septic shock with org","Harmful in all clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Reduces infectious complications in trauma/burns. May increase mortality in septic shock with org', 'Glutamine: conditionally essential in critical illness. REDOXS: increased mortality in multiorgan failure. Use with caution in shock.'),
('a01ec727-443e-5bef-9285-e775a7651592'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Diabetic diet in surgical patients:', '["No monitoring clinical presentation","Loose control <250 clinical presentation","Only sliding scale clinical presentation","Continue usual diabetes medications. Tight glucose control (140-180 mg/dL","Stop all medications clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Continue usual diabetes medications. Tight glucose control (140-180 mg/dL', 'Diabetes: stress hyperglycemia common. Use basal-bolus insulin. Avoid prolonged NPO. SSI reduction with glucose control.'),
('6204f79a-3aa7-588d-b4ab-a6dfbc98d22d'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Post-gastrectomy diet:', '["Normal diet clinical presentation clinical presentation","Small frequent meals, avoid simple sugars (dumping), protein-rich, B12/iron/Ca/D supplements","Low protein clinical presentation clinical presentation","No supplements clinical presentation clinical presentation","Three large meals clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Small frequent meals, avoid simple sugars (dumping), protein-rich, B12/iron/Ca/D supplements', 'Post-gastrectomy: dumping syndrome management. Eat lying down. Separate liquids from solids. No concentrated sweets.'),
('073920cf-195b-5ef6-9dc1-53a2d54d01d0'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Low-residue diet:', '["No difference","Normal fiber","High fiber","Low fiber, low roughage","For all GI"]'::jsonb, 'Low fiber, low roughage', 'Low-residue: reduces stool volume, frequency. For acute IBD, after bowel resection, strictures.'),
('77ec0a1c-27dc-5b70-be05-84a65429794e'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'High-fiber diet:', '["No effect clinical presentation clinical presentation","No benefit clinical presentation clinical presentation","Increases cancer risk clinical presentation clinical presentation","25-38 g/day. Reduces constipation, hemorrhoids, diverticulosis, colon cancer risk","Only for constipation clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, '25-38 g/day. Reduces constipation, hemorrhoids, diverticulosis, colon cancer risk', 'High fiber: soluble (cholesterol, glucose) + insoluble (bulk). Gradual increase to avoid bloating.'),
('d094b20b-ec51-5666-a68d-0e0b0de1c9b5'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Diverticulosis: diet recommendations:', '["No evidence for avoiding nuts/seeds. High fiber diet may reduce complications. Low fiber during a","No fiber clinical presentation clinical presentation","Only liquid diet clinical presentation clinical presentation","Avoid nuts/seeds clinical presentation clinical presentation","Low fiber always clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'No evidence for avoiding nuts/seeds. High fiber diet may reduce complications. Low fiber during a', 'Diverticulosis: high fiber reduces pressure -> prevents diverticula progression. No evidence for avoiding seeds/nuts.'),
('464b6e70-f7c0-55ea-b94e-8d7776924ca5'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Iron overload (hemochromatosis) diet:', '["No restriction clinical presentation clinical presentation","No alcohol limit clinical presentation clinical presentation","Iron supplements help clinical presentation clinical presentation","Avoid iron supplements, vitamin C supplements, raw shellfish (Vibrio risk). Limit alcohol","Extra vitamin C clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Avoid iron supplements, vitamin C supplements, raw shellfish (Vibrio risk). Limit alcohol', 'Hemochromatosis: restrict iron, vitamin C (increases absorption), alcohol (hepatotoxic). Phlebotomy treatment.'),
('1d80eed6-87cd-5940-bc82-1d370226ff20'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Wilson disease (copper overload) diet:', '["Low copper foods (avoid liver, shellfish, chocolate, nuts, mushrooms). Zinc supplements","No restriction clinical presentation clinical presentation","High copper foods clinical presentation clinical presentation","No diet needed clinical presentation clinical presentation","Avoid zinc clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Low copper foods (avoid liver, shellfish, chocolate, nuts, mushrooms). Zinc supplements', 'Wilson: reduce copper intake. Zinc blocks copper absorption (maintenance). Penicillamine or trientine chelation.'),
('353eec0d-4715-59fb-afd3-8847d42dc61f'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Phenylketonuria (PKU) diet:', '["High protein clinical presentation","Avoid carbs clinical presentation","No restriction clinical presentation","Restrict phenylalanine (avoid high","No treatment clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Restrict phenylalanine (avoid high', 'PKU: deficiency of phenylalanine hydroxylase -> intellectual disability. Phe-restricted diet. Monitor Phe levels.'),
('58a53891-f534-5a00-afaf-eb50dd6e0ac3'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Glycogen storage diseases: dietary management:', '["Low carb clinical presentation","Fasting fine clinical presentation","No treatment clinical presentation","Avoid fasting, frequent feeds, uncooked cornstarch","High protein clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Avoid fasting, frequent feeds, uncooked cornstarch', 'GSD: type I (von Gierke): hypoglycemia, lactic acidosis. Unmodified cornstarch 1-2 g/kg q4-6h (slow-release glucose).'),
('a5399c7f-980f-5ce0-a094-7270d3bf5c5d'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Celiac disease: dietary management:', '["Low gluten clinical presentation clinical presentation","No treatment clinical presentation clinical presentation","Strict lifelong gluten-free diet (avoid wheat, barley, rye). Oats if certified gluten-free","No need for strict diet clinical presentation clinical presentation","Only avoid wheat clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Strict lifelong gluten-free diet (avoid wheat, barley, rye). Oats if certified gluten-free', 'Celiac: autoimmune enteropathy triggered by gluten. Gluten-free diet (rice, corn, quinoa, GF oats). Nutritional deficiencies corrected.'),
('7dca2c7d-c2f0-5077-9d04-67fc97fd4365'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Celiac disease: nutritional deficiencies:', '["Only calcium clinical presentation","Only B12 clinical presentation","No deficiencies clinical presentation","Iron, folate, B12, calcium, vitamin D, zinc","Only iron clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Iron, folate, B12, calcium, vitamin D, zinc', 'Celiac: malabsorptive phase. Screen for deficiencies. Supplementation until gut heals (6-12 months on GFD).'),
('56a18812-04d7-5e7a-b378-0ebab1e964e5'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Lactose intolerance:', '["Only cow milk clinical presentation clinical presentation","Avoid all dairy clinical presentation clinical presentation","No treatment clinical presentation clinical presentation","Only severe cases clinical presentation clinical presentation","Lactase deficiency -> undigested lactose -> osmotic diarrhea, bloating, pain. Avoid lactose or us"]'::jsonb, 'Lactase deficiency -> undigested lactose -> osmotic diarrhea, bloating, pain. Avoid lactose or us', 'Lactose intolerance: primary (genetic), secondary (after GI infection/IBD). Yogurt better tolerated. Lactase enzyme supplements available.'),
('19b61a1f-ced9-515f-aba4-6b3dd474c113'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Food allergy (IgE-mediated):', '["Rapid onset (urticaria, angioedema, anaphylaxis). Common: peanuts, tree nuts, shellfish, milk, eggs","Slow onset clinical presentation clinical presentation","Not serious clinical presentation clinical presentation","Same as intolerance clinical presentation clinical presentation","No anaphylaxis clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Rapid onset (urticaria, angioedema, anaphylaxis). Common: peanuts, tree nuts, shellfish, milk, eggs', 'Food allergy: IgE. Severe: Epi-pen. Avoid trigger. Common: peanut (most fatal), tree nut, shellfish, milk, wheat, soy.'),
('cb8d5f8c-a0ed-5081-beca-32fc80c274a4'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Inflammatory bowel disease (IBD): nutrition:', '["Iron, B12, folate, vitamin D, calcium, zinc deficiencies. Enteral nutrition (elemental) for Crohn''s","No nutrition role clinical presentation clinical presentation","No deficiencies clinical presentation clinical presentation","Only iron clinical presentation clinical presentation","Only B12 clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Iron, B12, folate, vitamin D, calcium, zinc deficiencies. Enteral nutrition (elemental) for Crohn''s', 'IBD: malnutrition common (inflammation, diarrhea, avoidance). Exclusive enteral nutrition induces remission in Crohn''s children.'),
('c4fcd5c0-fbbd-538e-9a25-0f77f735be94'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Short bowel syndrome: dietary adaptation:', '["Low LCT, high MCT, high complex carbs, soluble fiber. TPN weaning. Anti-motility agents. Vitamin","Low carb clinical presentation clinical presentation","No adaptation clinical presentation clinical presentation","Normal diet clinical presentation clinical presentation","High fat clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Low LCT, high MCT, high complex carbs, soluble fiber. TPN weaning. Anti-motility agents. Vitamin', 'SBS: maximize adaptation. Glutamine, growth hormone, GLP-2 (teduglutide) enhance adaptation. TPN needed if <100 cm small bowel.'),
('480f523b-55a2-56d9-b33d-dd3177a69e28'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Bariatric surgery: preoperative weight loss:', '["Achieve 5-10% weight loss before surgery","Gain weight clinical presentation","No benefit clinical presentation","Only postop clinical presentation","No weight loss needed clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Achieve 5-10% weight loss before surgery', 'Preop weight loss: reduces liver volume (facilitates surgery), improves outcomes. Very low-calorie diet (800 kcal) for 2-4 weeks.'),
('762bf3a4-8dad-556f-81b7-0de060f65616'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Bariatric surgery: postoperative diet progression:', '["Clear liquids (day 1-2) -> full liquids (2-4 weeks) -> pureed -> soft -> regular","Solid food day 1 clinical presentation","Regular diet immediately clinical presentation","Liquids only for months clinical presentation","No progression clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Clear liquids (day 1-2) -> full liquids (2-4 weeks) -> pureed -> soft -> regular', 'Post-bariatric: slow progression. High protein (60-80 g/day). Chew thoroughly. Avoid simple sugars. Lifelong supplements.'),
('eb740407-e1b9-5a9a-af2e-a8c0ff799447'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Dumping syndrome: dietary management:', '["No management clinical presentation clinical presentation","Eat more sugar clinical presentation clinical presentation","Large meals clinical presentation clinical presentation","Separate liquids from solids (30 min). Avoid simple sugars. Increase protein/fiber. Small frequen","No separation clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Separate liquids from solids (30 min). Avoid simple sugars. Increase protein/fiber. Small frequen', 'Dumping: dietary modification is first-line. Acarbose (delays carb absorption), octreotide (slows gastric emptying) if severe.'),
('f004502f-7a06-5a89-9b11-6aeb462ba121'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Vitamin deficiencies after RYGB:', '["No deficiencies clinical presentation clinical presentation","Only iron clinical presentation clinical presentation","B12 (IM) monthly, iron (FeSO4), calcium citrate + vitamin D, folate, thiamine","Only folate clinical presentation clinical presentation","Only B12 clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'B12 (IM) monthly, iron (FeSO4), calcium citrate + vitamin D, folate, thiamine', 'RYGB: duodenum/jejunum bypass (Fe, Ca, B12, folate). Multivitamin lifelong. Monitor labs annually.'),
('25781740-c3c4-5466-8de2-e904c2f781aa'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Thiamine deficiency after bariatric surgery:', '["Only in alcoholism clinical presentation clinical presentation","Not preventable clinical presentation clinical presentation","No risk clinical presentation clinical presentation","Wernicke encephalopathy (rare but devastating). Prophylactic thiamine (100 mg/day) after surgery","No supplementation clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Wernicke encephalopathy (rare but devastating). Prophylactic thiamine (100 mg/day) after surgery', 'Thiamine: vomiting, rapid weight loss, and glucose loads precipitate. IV thiamine before glucose if suspect.'),
('ebc6cadd-20ac-593b-90b0-eca0f58bfa71'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Metabolic bone disease after bariatric surgery:', '["Calcium citrate (1000-1500 mg/day) + vitamin D (3000 IU/day). Monitor DEXA","Only D deficiency clinical presentation","Calcium carbonate only clinical presentation","No supplementation needed clinical presentation","No bone risk clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Calcium citrate (1000-1500 mg/day) + vitamin D (3000 IU/day). Monitor DEXA', 'Bariatric bone: calcium citrate preferred (doesn''t need acid). Hypoparathyroidism from Ca deficiency. DEXA screening.'),
('430a8f3d-d04c-5077-b4a9-a223f2302a02'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Gallstones after rapid weight loss (bariatric):', '["No prevention clinical presentation clinical presentation","Ursodeoxycholic acid 500-600 mg/day for 6 months postop prevents gallstone formation","Diet prevents clinical presentation clinical presentation","All get cholecystectomy clinical presentation clinical presentation","No treatment clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Ursodeoxycholic acid 500-600 mg/day for 6 months postop prevents gallstone formation', 'Gallstones: rapid weight loss -> supersaturated bile. UDCA reduces incidence from 30% to 3%. Consider during massive weight loss.'),
('eacc845c-fff0-5da9-90b0-d6bf3290c94e'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Obesity: dietary counseling for weight loss:', '["No counseling needed clinical presentation clinical presentation","Fad diets only clinical presentation clinical presentation","Caloric deficit (500-1000 kcal/day). Reduced fat, increased fiber, portion control. Multidiscipli","All willpower clinical presentation clinical presentation","No evidence clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Caloric deficit (500-1000 kcal/day). Reduced fat, increased fiber, portion control. Multidiscipli', 'Weight loss: sustainable lifestyle change. 5-10% weight loss improves comorbidities. Meal replacements, behavior therapy.'),
('7b3dab61-b197-50bc-b6cb-2a830f0af53f'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Very low-calorie diets (VLCD):', '["Long-term solution clinical presentation clinical presentation","800 kcal/day, medically supervised. Rapid weight loss (1-2 kg/week). Used pre-bariatric","Dangerous clinical presentation clinical presentation","No benefit clinical presentation clinical presentation","No supervision clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, '800 kcal/day, medically supervised. Rapid weight loss (1-2 kg/week). Used pre-bariatric', 'VLCD: 800-1000 kcal, high protein. Monitoring essential (electrolytes, refeeding). Short-term only (8-12 weeks).'),
('368688a1-86dc-5226-906c-d2291bcbd24e'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Ketogenic diet:', '["Very low carb, high fat. Induces ketosis. Used for epilepsy (children), weight loss","No benefit clinical presentation clinical presentation","No ketosis clinical presentation clinical presentation","High carb clinical presentation clinical presentation","Harmful clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Very low carb, high fat. Induces ketosis. Used for epilepsy (children), weight loss', 'Ketogenic: <50 g carb/day. Side effects: keto breath, constipation, dyslipidemia, micronutrient deficiencies. Medical monitoring.'),
('e8f7ff32-ae98-5bb4-9cc0-e43987277cf4'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Intermittent fasting:', '["Same as continuous clinical presentation clinical presentation","No benefit clinical presentation clinical presentation","Harmful clinical presentation clinical presentation","Cycles of eating and fasting (16:8, 5:2, alternate day). May improve insulin sensitivity","Not studied clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Cycles of eating and fasting (16:8, 5:2, alternate day). May improve insulin sensitivity', 'Intermittent fasting: time-restricted feeding. Caloric restriction likely main benefit. Long-term safety unclear.'),
('630fe0f5-dd20-5c0b-8b08-2aa6635ef501'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Parenteral nutrition: transition to oral/enteral:', '["No transition needed clinical presentation","Continue TPN until full diet clinical presentation","Taper TPN as oral/EN intake increases. Maintain IV access until >60% of needs met orally","No tapering clinical presentation","Stop abruptly clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Taper TPN as oral/EN intake increases. Maintain IV access until >60% of needs met orally', 'Transition: avoid hypoglycemia (taper TPN). Start oral/EN slowly. Monitor fluid/electrolytes. Remove line when ready.'),
('e3f6ccd0-69be-5d92-a9e6-16c43997dacd'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Peripheral parenteral nutrition (PPN):', '["No osmolarity limit clinical presentation clinical presentation","Same as central clinical presentation clinical presentation","Long-term use clinical presentation clinical presentation","For all TPN clinical presentation clinical presentation","For short-term (<2 weeks). <900 mOsm/L. Use fine-bore catheter. Risk: phlebitis"]'::jsonb, 'For short-term (<2 weeks). <900 mOsm/L. Use fine-bore catheter. Risk: phlebitis', 'PPN: limited by osmolarity. Lipid emulsion reduces osmolarity. Not for high-volume or long-term. Rotate site q24-48h if phlebitis.'),
('7157bd84-2860-5b78-8a8e-0fc060b60f4b'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Simultaneous (mixed) TPN/PPN:', '["Not used clinical presentation clinical presentation","All TPN clinical presentation clinical presentation","PPN + lipid emulsion to reduce osmolarity for limited central access","All PPN clinical presentation clinical presentation","Not effective clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'PPN + lipid emulsion to reduce osmolarity for limited central access', 'Mixed: PPN with lipid (reduces osmolarity from >900 to <800). For short-term (5-14 days) with moderate needs.'),
('9d9b1ba5-b7b5-5ff7-93f4-7bc1996467f9'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Feeding jejunostomy: indications:', '["No indications clinical presentation","All abdominal surgery clinical presentation","No benefit clinical presentation","Post-esophagectomy, gastrectomy, gastroparesis, severe pancreatitis","NG always better clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Post-esophagectomy, gastrectomy, gastroparesis, severe pancreatitis', 'Feeding jejunostomy: placed during esophageal/gastric surgery. Continuous drip feeds. Less reflux than NG. Can start early.'),
('f6c80326-8706-5804-aebc-88e35d654376'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'TPN: 3-in-1 (total nutrient admixture):', '["Only dextrose+AA clinical presentation","Dextrose, amino acids, lipids combined in one bag","Separate bags clinical presentation","No lipids clinical presentation","Not stable clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Dextrose, amino acids, lipids combined in one bag', 'TNA: different compounding, stable with final concentration. Calcium/phosphate compatibility. Less manipulation.'),
('4ad05cce-bc62-517d-98aa-38e5f90d3d09'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'TPN: calcium and phosphate compatibility:', '["Not a concern clinical presentation clinical presentation","Risk of precipitation (CaPO4). Avoid by: lower concentrations, order of mixing","No risk clinical presentation clinical presentation","Only in 3-in-1 clinical presentation clinical presentation","Always compatible clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Risk of precipitation (CaPO4). Avoid by: lower concentrations, order of mixing', 'Ca/Phos: precipitation -> emboli. Check solubility curves. Use adult dosing. Organic phosphate (Glucophos) more soluble.'),
('045a9814-d0f5-51f7-8343-ecbeb049b880'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Fat overload syndrome:', '["Self-limited clinical presentation clinical presentation","Rare complication of lipid infusion (headache, fever, hypertriglyceridemia, hepatosplenomegaly","Common clinical presentation clinical presentation","No risk clinical presentation clinical presentation","Benign clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Rare complication of lipid infusion (headache, fever, hypertriglyceridemia, hepatosplenomegaly', 'Fat overload: from rapid infusion or excessive dose. Treat: stop lipid, monitor triglycerides. Usually resolves.'),
('eb6b4208-9bab-589e-98d5-cda84ce2eaaa'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Cholestasis of TPN:', '["All get it clinical presentation clinical presentation","Irreversible clinical presentation clinical presentation","Not serious clinical presentation clinical presentation","Elevated conjugated bilirubin, ALP, GGT. Associated with prolonged TPN, lack of EN","No treatment clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Elevated conjugated bilirubin, ALP, GGT. Associated with prolonged TPN, lack of EN', 'TPN cholestasis: multifactorial. Prevention: early EN, cycling TPN, avoid overfeeding, fish oil (Omegaven). Ursodeoxycholic acid may help.'),
('9d970cb7-40ce-53a6-b56b-2086e25315d5'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Acanthosis nigricans:', '["Malnutrition clinical presentation clinical presentation","Cancer only clinical presentation clinical presentation","Normal finding clinical presentation clinical presentation","Infectious clinical presentation clinical presentation","Velvety hyperpigmentation in flexures (neck, axilla). Marker of insulin resistance/obesity/T2DM"]'::jsonb, 'Velvety hyperpigmentation in flexures (neck, axilla). Marker of insulin resistance/obesity/T2DM', 'Acanthosis: hyperkeratosis, papillomatosis. Associated with obesity, diabetes, PCOS. Also paraneoplastic (gastric cancer).'),
('543f84e1-29d3-58a7-885d-23e9016251d2'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Starvation: first fuel source:', '["Glycogen","Vitamins","Ketones","Protein","Fat"]'::jsonb, 'Glycogen', 'Starvation: glycogen (24h), then gluconeogenesis (protein) + lipolysis (fatty acids, ketones). Body adapts to use fat preferentially.'),
('39e10c38-d6bb-5550-bb0b-ab9a783ad2d2'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Obligatory nitrogen loss:', '["No loss clinical presentation clinical presentation","Minimum: 0.1 g/kg/day of protein. Loss of lean body mass inevitable in catabolic state","Only in elderly clinical presentation clinical presentation","Only in stress clinical presentation clinical presentation","Protein sparing complete clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Minimum: 0.1 g/kg/day of protein. Loss of lean body mass inevitable in catabolic state', 'Nitrogen loss: catabolic (injury, sepsis) increases to 0.3-0.5 g/kg/day. Represents muscle breakdown.'),
('4e60117d-049b-5c17-9778-50e8772da953'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Nitrogen balance calculation:', '["No formula clinical presentation","Output only clinical presentation","Intake only clinical presentation","Intake - output clinical presentation","N intake (protein g/6.25) - N output"]'::jsonb, 'N intake (protein g/6.25) - N output', 'Nitrogen balance: positive (anabolism) or negative (catabolism). Goal: +2 to +4 g/day for recovery.'),
('85a247f8-6148-5377-9333-373c1f8546f7'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Omega-3 index:', '["Only for lipids clinical presentation clinical presentation","<2% clinical presentation clinical presentation","Percentage of EPA+DHA in RBC membranes. <4% high risk of CVD. >8% protective","<6% clinical presentation clinical presentation","Not useful clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Percentage of EPA+DHA in RBC membranes. <4% high risk of CVD. >8% protective', 'Omega-3 index: marker of omega-3 status. Modifiable by diet/supplements. Target: >8%. Reduces CVD, inflammation.'),
('11b3fdf5-bbe8-5fc7-a23e-a24eace5f7bd'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Homocysteine (nutritional marker):', '["No treatment clinical presentation clinical presentation","Elevated in B12, folate, B6 deficiency. Risk factor for CVD, DVT, neural tube defects, dementia","Not modifiable clinical presentation clinical presentation","Beneficial clinical presentation clinical presentation","No consequences clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Elevated in B12, folate, B6 deficiency. Risk factor for CVD, DVT, neural tube defects, dementia', 'Homocysteine: methylmalonic acid (MMA) for B12. Treatment: B12, folate, B6. Folic acid fortification reduced homocysteine and NTDs.'),
('bf30d2e5-97ef-5222-a5be-49b870679ec0'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Glycemic index (GI):', '["Rate at which food raises blood glucose. Low GI: slow glucose rise (legumes, whole grains","Only for athletes clinical presentation clinical presentation","High GI best clinical presentation clinical presentation","No clinical relevance clinical presentation clinical presentation","Not used in diabetes clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Rate at which food raises blood glucose. Low GI: slow glucose rise (legumes, whole grains', 'GI: low GI foods improve glycemic control, weight management. Examples: oats, pasta (al dente), legumes. High GI: white bread, sugar.'),
('4a075fd4-f5b7-5807-9bff-a35b6054e095'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Glycemic load (GL):', '["Not useful clinical presentation clinical presentation","No formula clinical presentation clinical presentation","GI x grams of carbohydrate per serving / 100. Better predictor of glucose response than GI alone","Only GI matters clinical presentation clinical presentation","Same as GI clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'GI x grams of carbohydrate per serving / 100. Better predictor of glucose response than GI alone', 'GL: accounts for both quality and quantity. Watermelon: high GI but low GL (small carb per serving).'),
('14ad7f5c-0aba-5369-b811-35a46fa520cd'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Whole food vs supplement nutrition:', '["Whole foods preferred (fiber, phytonutrients, satiety). Supplements for deficiency only","Only supplements clinical presentation clinical presentation","Supplements better clinical presentation clinical presentation","Synthetic better clinical presentation clinical presentation","No difference clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Whole foods preferred (fiber, phytonutrients, satiety). Supplements for deficiency only', 'Whole food: > supplements. Supplements indicated for specific deficiencies (B12, Fe, D) or when oral intake inadequate.'),
('4cd9c50f-5c86-579a-90b9-bd918067c506'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Medically prescribed diets: clear liquid:', '["Normal liquids clinical presentation clinical presentation","Dairy allowed clinical presentation clinical presentation","Thick liquids clinical presentation clinical presentation","Water, clear broth, clear juice, Jell-O, tea. Minimal residue. No red/purple dyes","Vegetable juice clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Water, clear broth, clear juice, Jell-O, tea. Minimal residue. No red/purple dyes', 'Clear liquid: for post-op transition, diagnostic procedures (colonoscopy). No fiber, fat, protein. <500 kcal/day (short-term only).'),
('19c2cbe9-8e2f-5aa9-9aff-57fbed5d24cd'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Full liquid diet:', '["Smooth, liquid foods: milk, yogurt, custard, cream soups","No dairy clinical presentation","Includes solids clinical presentation","Same as clear liquid clinical presentation","Only clear clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Smooth, liquid foods: milk, yogurt, custard, cream soups', 'Full liquid: for post-op progression, dysphagia (if can handle). Nutritional content better than clear liquid.'),
('2fc665b8-7ac2-5d6f-ab8a-d5de24fe27ac'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Pureed diet:', '["No need to puree clinical presentation clinical presentation","Blended/ground foods (mechanical soft). For dysphagia (with thickened liquids), jaw surgery","Only meat pureed clinical presentation clinical presentation","Same as liquid clinical presentation clinical presentation","Includes nuts clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Blended/ground foods (mechanical soft). For dysphagia (with thickened liquids), jaw surgery', 'Pureed: for dysphagia, oral motor issues. Thickness tested (IDDSI levels). Nutritional adequacy challenging.'),
('473e75b6-74be-5329-b7fc-e0235a9891ba'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Mechanically altered (soft) diet:', '["No cooked foods clinical presentation","Same as regular clinical presentation","Only liquids clinical presentation","Not for elderly clinical presentation","Easy to chew: ground meats, soft fruits, cooked vegetables"]'::jsonb, 'Easy to chew: ground meats, soft fruits, cooked vegetables', 'Soft diet: for dental problems, after oral surgery, elderly. Can be nutritionally adequate. Avoid nuts, seeds, raw vegetables.'),
('9e0d9b76-6380-569f-b826-4ee60b794994'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Weight management: energy density:', '["All foods same clinical presentation clinical presentation","Low energy density foods (vegetables, fruit, soup, lean protein) promote satiety with fewer calories","No effect clinical presentation clinical presentation","Not important clinical presentation clinical presentation","High energy density better clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Low energy density foods (vegetables, fruit, soup, lean protein) promote satiety with fewer calories', 'Energy density: kcal/g. Water and fiber reduce energy density. Volume eating (large portions, low kcal) aids weight loss.'),
('8762c597-a4d3-5ee8-9456-aa0af3df1931'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Portion control strategies:', '["Eat until full clinical presentation clinical presentation","Use smaller plates, measure portions, pre-plating, restaurant awareness, read labels","No measuring clinical presentation clinical presentation","No strategies clinical presentation clinical presentation","Not needed clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Use smaller plates, measure portions, pre-plating, restaurant awareness, read labels', 'Portion: serving sizes have increased. Plate method: 1/2 non-starchy vegetables, 1/4 lean protein, 1/4 starch.'),
('2abd6434-5c9d-57d6-9c4d-58b5123c7abf'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Mindful eating:', '["Paying attention to food, hunger cues, eating slowly, without distraction. Reduces overeating","Not evidence-based clinical presentation clinical presentation","Only for weight loss clinical presentation clinical presentation","No benefit clinical presentation clinical presentation","Eat quickly clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Paying attention to food, hunger cues, eating slowly, without distraction. Reduces overeating', 'Mindful eating: reduced food intake, improved satiety, weight management. Savour food, eating with intention.'),
('a0fa2815-b1e4-545d-afb0-9ef7a0d4e023'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Meal timing and metabolism:', '["Irregular eating associated with obesity, MetS. Regular pattern (breakfast, lunch","No impact clinical presentation","One meal a day clinical presentation","Skipping breakfast better clinical presentation","No effect clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Irregular eating associated with obesity, MetS. Regular pattern (breakfast, lunch', 'Meal timing: breakfast consumption associated with lower BMI. Late meals -> circadian disruption -> obesity risk.'),
('1408a015-9828-59c4-b478-9ea87585e56f'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Water intake recommendations:', '["8 glasses","1 L/day","No recommendation","4 L/day","2.7 L/day (women), 3.7 L/day (men) total water (includes food). Adequate hydration for surgery"]'::jsonb, 'No recommendation', 'Water: from all beverages + food (20%). Increased needs in fever, diarrhea, sweating. Monitor urine color.'),
('2fbc1836-60d4-52a7-9d35-988a3ea41632'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Sodium intake recommendation:', '[">5000 mg clinical presentation clinical presentation","<1000 mg clinical presentation clinical presentation","<3000 mg clinical presentation clinical presentation","<2300 mg/day (6 g salt). Hypertensive patients: <1500 mg/day","No limit clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, '<2300 mg/day (6 g salt). Hypertensive patients: <1500 mg/day', 'Sodium: DASH diet (low Na). High Na -> HTN, CVD, stroke, gastric cancer. 75% from processed foods.'),
('d4c7fb2e-d572-551e-a3fd-42c51a66bdc8'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Potassium intake recommendation:', '[">6000 mg clinical presentation clinical presentation","Not needed clinical presentation clinical presentation","No recommendation clinical presentation clinical presentation","3500-4700 mg/day. DASH diet high in K (fruits, vegetables). Lowers BP, stroke risk","<2000 mg clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, '3500-4700 mg/day. DASH diet high in K (fruits, vegetables). Lowers BP, stroke risk', 'Potassium: high intake lowers BP (counteracts Na). Food sources: banana, potato, spinach, avocado, beans.'),
('eb96efbf-bec2-5805-8d7f-b2c3bce0fbfe'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Dietary Approaches to Stop Hypertension (DASH) diet:', '["No grains clinical presentation clinical presentation","High sugar clinical presentation clinical presentation","High fruits, vegetables, low-fat dairy, whole grains, lean protein. Low Na, saturated fat, sugar","High fat clinical presentation clinical presentation","No dairy clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'High fruits, vegetables, low-fat dairy, whole grains, lean protein. Low Na, saturated fat, sugar', 'DASH: reduces BP, cholesterol. Rich in K, Mg, Ca, fiber, protein. Proven in clinical trials.'),
('d1a9bf07-b78b-5c9c-bc7e-2a8aaf743dc0'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Mediterranean diet:', '["No fish clinical presentation clinical presentation","Low vegetables clinical presentation clinical presentation","High olive oil, fish, nuts, legumes, fruits, vegetables, whole grains. Low red meat, processed foods","High red meat clinical presentation clinical presentation","Butter based clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'High olive oil, fish, nuts, legumes, fruits, vegetables, whole grains. Low red meat, processed foods', 'Mediterranean: reduces CVD, mortality, diabetes, cognitive decline. Associated with longevity. PREDIMED trial evidence.'),
('71ae8cec-d60a-5858-8e06-e80c403681f8'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Plant-based diet:', '["No benefit clinical presentation","Vegetarian (no meat, fish) or vegan (no animal products). Associated with lower BMI, CVD","Unhealthy clinical presentation","Only for ethics clinical presentation","Deficiency risk outweighs benefits clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Vegetarian (no meat, fish) or vegan (no animal products). Associated with lower BMI, CVD', 'Plant-based: health benefits when well planned. B12 supplementation for vegans. Risks: protein, iron, calcium, zinc adequacy.'),
('78ff644c-643b-5c6c-bfea-b7638118d131'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Low-carbohydrate diet:', '["No benefit clinical presentation clinical presentation","<130 g/day carb (Atkins, keto, Paleo). Effective for weight loss, T2DM improvement","Not effective clinical presentation clinical presentation","All patients should do clinical presentation clinical presentation","High mortality clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, '<130 g/day carb (Atkins, keto, Paleo). Effective for weight loss, T2DM improvement', 'Low-carb: rapid weight loss (water). Ketosis for epilepsy. Long-term safety questioned (increased CVD risk with high saturated fat).'),
('60204174-ab78-55fd-91c3-bb15d185c24e'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Very low-calorie ketogenic diet (VLCKD):', '["No benefit clinical presentation clinical presentation","No diet clinical presentation clinical presentation","<800 kcal, <50 g carb, high protein. Medical supervision. Rapid weight loss, T2DM remission","Self-managed clinical presentation clinical presentation","Long-term solution clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, '<800 kcal, <50 g carb, high protein. Medical supervision. Rapid weight loss, T2DM remission', 'VLCKD: effective short-term. Side effects: fatigue, nausea, constipation, gallstones, hair loss. Transition to Mediterranean diet.');
insert into fcps_mcq_stage (id, subject_name, chapter_name, question, options, correct_answer, explanation) values
('6e8fd4b5-0f23-5862-bbb5-b6b192b86ba1'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Dietary fiber: daily recommendation:', '[">50 g clinical presentation","20 g clinical presentation","25-38 g/day (adults). Most consume <15 g/day. Increase gradually","<10 g clinical presentation","No recommendation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, '25-38 g/day (adults). Most consume <15 g/day. Increase gradually', 'Fiber: soluble (5-10 g/day) + insoluble. Benefits: glucose, cholesterol, bowel regularity, satiety, microbiome health.'),
('ebc3095f-b943-5657-89f5-8a260d7ed2c3'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Functional foods:', '["Same as regular clinical presentation","Placebo clinical presentation","Foods with health benefits beyond basic nutrition","No benefit clinical presentation","Supplements only clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Foods with health benefits beyond basic nutrition', 'Functional foods: plant sterols (cholesterol lowering), probiotics, omega-3 eggs, fortified milk (vitamin D). Not regulated as drugs.'),
('c34a9af7-5fc3-569b-a48e-413328cfe6da'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Nutraceuticals:', '["No evidence clinical presentation","All dangerous clinical presentation","Isolated nutrients/herbals with potential health benefits","Regulated as drugs clinical presentation","Same as food clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Isolated nutrients/herbals with potential health benefits', 'Nutraceuticals: poorly regulated (FDA). Examples: curcumin, resveratrol, CoQ10. Quality, purity, dosing variable. Evidence mixed.'),
('77bd9fe7-d323-5a38-90cc-0eb35f1adc32'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Herbal supplements and surgery:', '["No risk clinical presentation clinical presentation","Only anticoagulant herbs clinical presentation clinical presentation","Safe clinical presentation clinical presentation","Many interact with anesthesia, coagulation (garlic, ginkgo, ginseng, St. John''s wort","Continue use clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Many interact with anesthesia, coagulation (garlic, ginkgo, ginseng, St. John''s wort', 'Herbals: garlic (bleeding), ginkgo (bleeding), ginseng (hypoglycemia), St. John''s wort (drug interactions), kava (hepatotoxicity).'),
('202c9f5e-9879-522a-a704-a61497ba1291'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Nutritional status: physical exam signs:', '["Only glossitis clinical presentation clinical presentation","No signs clinical presentation clinical presentation","Only edema clinical presentation clinical presentation","Muscle wasting (temporalis, interosseous, deltoid, quadriceps), fat loss, edema, dermatitis","Only dermatitis clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Muscle wasting (temporalis, interosseous, deltoid, quadriceps), fat loss, edema, dermatitis', 'Physical exam: SGA includes muscle/fat assessment. Specific signs: B12 glossitis, Niacin dermatitis, B6 cheilitis, Zn dermatitis.'),
('beef569f-4f92-5ca5-9f48-fb68d1b513ad'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Handgrip strength (dynamometry):', '["Only in elderly clinical presentation clinical presentation","No value clinical presentation clinical presentation","Only for athletes clinical presentation clinical presentation","Measures muscle strength. Low grip strength predicts surgical complications, mortality","Not validated clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Measures muscle strength. Low grip strength predicts surgical complications, mortality', 'Handgrip: simple, reliable. Preop low grip -> longer LOS, higher complications, mortality. Reflects nutritional status and frailty.'),
('ec3a17f2-5d20-50db-bd04-2089a3dfe629'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Nutrition support: ethical considerations:', '["Do everything clinical presentation","Patient choice irrelevant clinical presentation","No ethics clinical presentation","Beneficence (benefit), non-maleficence (do no harm), autonomy","Only benefit clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Beneficence (benefit), non-maleficence (do no harm), autonomy', 'Nutrition ethics: feeding tube (PEG) vs comfort feeding. Advance directives (living will). Withholding/withdrawing nutrition in terminal care.'),
('25ff44c4-ba18-549a-a314-eba0c0c1b03e'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'PEG in advanced dementia:', '["Recommended clinical presentation clinical presentation","Improves survival clinical presentation clinical presentation","Better than hand feeding clinical presentation clinical presentation","Prevents aspiration clinical presentation clinical presentation","No improvement in survival, quality of life, aspiration, pressure ulcers. Hand feeding preferred"]'::jsonb, 'No improvement in survival, quality of life, aspiration, pressure ulcers. Hand feeding preferred', 'Dementia: PEG does not improve outcomes. Aspiration risk same. Hand feeding with modified consistency recommended.'),
('00498bc7-2f10-55a1-a4f3-125f7661706f'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'End-of-life nutrition: assisted oral feeding:', '["Continue oral feeding if safe and desired. No artificial hydration/nutrition in terminal phase un","Hydration essential clinical presentation clinical presentation","PEG always clinical presentation clinical presentation","Artificial nutrition always clinical presentation clinical presentation","No oral feeding clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Continue oral feeding if safe and desired. No artificial hydration/nutrition in terminal phase un', 'End-of-life: hydration/nutrition may worsen symptoms (edema, secretions). Focus on comfort, not prolongation.'),
('bffca698-85ed-57d3-9858-e143dd09c5df'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Which of the following is the most common vitamin deficiency worldwide?', '["Vitamin E deficiency","Vitamin A deficiency","Vitamin C deficiency","Vitamin D deficiency","Vitamin B12 deficiency"]'::jsonb, 'Vitamin D deficiency', 'Vitamin D deficiency is the most common vitamin deficiency globally, affecting nearly 1 billion people worldwide. It results from inadequate sun exposure, limited dietary intake, and impaired absorption. Deficiency leads to rickets in children and osteomalacia in adults, characterized by impaired bone mineralization, hypocalcemia, and elevated alkaline phosphatase.'),
('ac46668b-662f-5b0b-b6f8-92f2d4e3999c'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Which vitamin deficiency is characterized by pernicious anemia due to lack of intrinsic factor?', '["Vitamin B12","Vitamin B1","Vitamin B6","Vitamin B9","Vitamin B2"]'::jsonb, 'Vitamin B12', 'Pernicious anemia is an autoimmune condition where antibodies destroy parietal cells, leading to intrinsic factor deficiency and subsequent B12 malabsorption. It presents with megaloblastic anemia and neurological symptoms including peripheral neuropathy and subacute combined degeneration of the spinal cord. Treatment requires lifelong intramuscular hydroxocobalamin supplementation.'),
('f8e37451-1aa1-5044-a9f5-44eef57fd169'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'What is the primary function of vitamin K in the human body?', '["Collagen synthesis","DNA synthesis","Calcium absorption","Cofactor for gamma-carboxylation of clotting factors II, VII, IX, X","Antioxidant protection of cell membranes"]'::jsonb, 'Cofactor for gamma-carboxylation of clotting factors II, VII, IX, X', 'Vitamin K is essential for the post-translational gamma-carboxylation of glutamic acid residues on clotting factors, enabling calcium binding and proper coagulation cascade function. Deficiency causes elevated PT with normal PTT. Newborns receive 1 mg IM prophylaxis at birth to prevent vitamin K deficiency bleeding (VKDB).'),
('2c6a0ada-8ae3-5142-8f32-101bf5bd23bb'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Which mineral deficiency causes acrodermatitis enteropathica?', '["Copper","Iron","Zinc","Selenium","Magnesium"]'::jsonb, 'Zinc', 'Acrodermatitis enteropathica is an autosomal recessive disorder of zinc malabsorption, presenting with periorificial dermatitis, diarrhea, and growth retardation. Zinc is a cofactor for over 300 enzymes involved in protein synthesis, immune function, and wound healing. Treatment requires lifelong zinc supplementation.'),
('9db16459-dc27-5532-83f6-31e5990269a5'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'What is the earliest manifestation of vitamin A deficiency?', '["Bitot spots","Night blindness","Corneal ulceration","Xerophthalmia","Keratomalacia"]'::jsonb, 'Night blindness', 'Night blindness is the earliest symptom of vitamin A deficiency due to impaired rhodopsin regeneration in retinal rods. If untreated, it progresses to xerophthalmia (dry conjunctiva), Bitot spots (foamy plaques), keratomalacia (corneal necrosis), and irreversible blindness. Vitamin A is also crucial for immune function and epithelial integrity.'),
('c7fa74dd-7142-5bbf-a488-d6a15dff5bd4'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Which laboratory finding best differentiates vitamin B12 deficiency from folate deficiency?', '["Low serum B12 level","Decreased RBC folate","Elevated methylmalonic acid","Elevated homocysteine","Macrocytic anemia"]'::jsonb, 'Elevated methylmalonic acid', 'Methylmalonic acid is specifically elevated in B12 deficiency because B12 is a cofactor for methylmalonyl-CoA mutase, which converts methylmalonyl-CoA to succinyl-CoA. Homocysteine is elevated in both B12 and folate deficiencies. MMA is normal in isolated folate deficiency, making it the most specific differentiating test.'),
('71563761-238c-59d3-8c33-9233c48f38a4'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'What is the recommended daily folic acid supplementation dose for preventing neural tube defects?', '["1000 mcg/day only during pregnancy","200 mcg/day starting at 20 weeks","100 mcg/day after first trimester","400-800 mcg/day starting before conception","No supplementation needed"]'::jsonb, '400-800 mcg/day starting before conception', 'Folic acid supplementation of 400-800 mcg/day initiated before conception reduces neural tube defect risk by up to 70%. The critical window is the first 28 days of gestation, often before pregnancy is recognized. Many countries mandate folic acid fortification of grain products for population-level prevention.'),
('cdb74b4f-50f5-505a-998e-3fff0b08b507'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Which condition results from vitamin C deficiency?', '["Scurvy","Kwashiorkor","Pellagra","Rickets","Beri-beri"]'::jsonb, 'Scurvy', 'Scurvy results from vitamin C deficiency, impairing collagen synthesis because ascorbic acid is a cofactor for prolyl hydroxylase. Clinical features include perifollicular hemorrhages, corkscrew hairs, gingival hyperplasia, bleeding gums, ecchymoses, and impaired wound healing. Treatment with 100-200 mg vitamin C daily produces rapid improvement, with gingival bleeding resolving within days.'),
('50084e61-6be5-5677-a6ce-6d0e60d73268'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'What is the mechanism of refeeding syndrome?', '["Excessive caloric intake causes hyperglycemia clinical presentation","Fluid overload causes heart failure clinical presentation","Fat overload causes hypertriglyceridemia clinical presentation","Rapid insulin surge causes intracellular shift of phosphate, potassium, and magnesium","Protein overload causes azotemia clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Rapid insulin surge causes intracellular shift of phosphate, potassium, and magnesium', 'Refeeding syndrome occurs when malnourished patients receive rapid carbohydrate repletion, triggering insulin release that drives phosphate, potassium, and magnesium intracellularly. This results in dangerous hypophosphatemia, hypokalemia, and hypomagnesemia, leading to cardiac arrhythmias, heart failure, respiratory failure, and neurological dysfunction. Prevention requires slow caloric initiation (20-25 kcal/kg/day) with aggressive electrolyte monitoring and replacement.'),
('05814753-a9f9-5e27-a06b-be8c340bc30a'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Which bariatric procedure has the greatest independent effect on glycemic control in type 2 diabetes?', '["Adjustable gastric banding","Intragastric balloon","Sleeve gastrectomy","Biliopancreatic diversion","Roux-en-Y gastric bypass"]'::jsonb, 'Roux-en-Y gastric bypass', 'RYGB improves glycemic control independent of weight loss through incretin-mediated mechanisms including increased GLP-1 secretion. Altered gut hormone profiles enhance insulin secretion and sensitivity, leading to diabetes remission in 60-80% of patients. This metabolic effect occurs within days of surgery, before significant weight loss occurs.'),
('6ffcd4f9-2f7e-5379-bb78-1f64ae84569a'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'What is the most common complication of total parenteral nutrition (TPN)?', '["Catheter-related bloodstream infection","Hyperglycemia","Electrolyte abnormalities","Hypertriglyceridemia","Pneumothorax"]'::jsonb, 'Hyperglycemia', 'Hyperglycemia is the most common metabolic complication of TPN, occurring due to high dextrose loads and stress-induced insulin resistance. It is managed by adding regular insulin to the TPN bag (starting at 0.1 U/g carbohydrate) and targeting blood glucose below 180 mg/dL. Uncontrolled hyperglycemia increases risk of infectious complications and osmotic diuresis.'),
('e8ac8750-819a-5691-9ca9-b6c8a38b9cb5'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Which of the following is a characteristic feature of marasmus?', '["Pitting edema due to hypoalbuminemia","Dermatitis with desquamation","Fatty liver infiltration","Severe wasting of muscle and subcutaneous fat without edema","Apathy and irritability"]'::jsonb, 'Severe wasting of muscle and subcutaneous fat without edema', 'Marasmus results from chronic calorie deficiency, leading to severe weight loss (<60% expected), muscle wasting, and loss of subcutaneous fat. The classic appearance is skin-and-bones with no edema. Immune function is relatively preserved compared to kwashiorkor. Management involves gradual therapeutic feeding with F-75 and F-100 formulas.'),
('990e0f2c-7ffe-5b74-93ba-91989649f4ea'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'What serum 25-hydroxyvitamin D level defines vitamin D deficiency?', '["<10 ng/mL","<30 ng/mL","<20 ng/mL","<5 ng/mL","<15 ng/mL"]'::jsonb, '<20 ng/mL', '25-hydroxyvitamin D is the best marker of vitamin D status. Levels below 20 ng/mL (50 nmol/L) indicate deficiency, 20-29 ng/mL indicate insufficiency, and 30 ng/mL or above is considered sufficient. Measurement before supplementation is recommended, as toxicity from excessive vitamin D can cause hypercalcemia and nephrocalcinosis.'),
('135faf93-d0fa-5e14-9d56-7feb3a580b60'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Which vitamin deficiency causes Wernicke encephalopathy with the triad of confusion, ataxia, and nystagmus?', '["Vitamin B3","Vitamin B12","Vitamin B6","Vitamin E","Vitamin B1"]'::jsonb, 'Vitamin B1', 'Wernicke encephalopathy is a medical emergency caused by thiamine deficiency, commonly seen in alcoholism. The classic triad is confusion, ataxia, and ophthalmoplegia/nystagmus. Treatment requires immediate intravenous thiamine administration before glucose, as giving glucose without thiamine can precipitate or worsen the encephalopathy. Untreated, it progresses to Korsakoff amnestic syndrome.'),
('7d3d00bb-0085-5503-ac82-19fa40322a71'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'What is the WHO classification for severe acute malnutrition based on mid-upper arm circumference (MUAC) in children 6-59 months?', '["<120 mm","<115 mm","<130 mm","<125 mm","<110 mm"]'::jsonb, '<115 mm', 'MUAC is a rapid, low-cost screening tool for severe acute malnutrition that does not require age calculation. A MUAC <115 mm (11.5 cm) in children 6-59 months indicates severe acute malnutrition requiring therapeutic feeding. MUAC between 115-125 mm indicates moderate acute malnutrition. Edema is also diagnostic of severe acute malnutrition regardless of MUAC.'),
('fcd113d5-1bc9-5d0f-acae-11521029a7ff'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Which of the following is NOT a component of metabolic syndrome according to NCEP ATP III criteria?', '["Elevated fasting glucose","Elevated triglycerides","Elevated LDL cholesterol","Increased waist circumference","Low HDL cholesterol"]'::jsonb, 'Elevated LDL cholesterol', 'Metabolic syndrome is diagnosed when 3 of 5 criteria are present: increased waist circumference (men >102 cm, women >88 cm), triglycerides >=150 mg/dL, HDL <40 mg/dL (men) or <50 mg/dL (women), blood pressure >=130/85 mmHg, and fasting glucose >=100 mg/dL. Elevated LDL is not a criterion, although it often coexists and contributes to cardiovascular risk.'),
('168d2100-234f-5711-89ed-d6af50d24adb'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Which amino acid is conditionally essential in critical illness and serves as the primary fuel for enterocytes?', '["Glutamine","Leucine","Lysine","Arginine","Tryptophan"]'::jsonb, 'Glutamine', 'Glutamine is the primary fuel source for enterocytes and immune cells, making it conditionally essential during critical illness, trauma, and burns. It enhances gut barrier function, reduces bacterial translocation, and may decrease infectious complications. However, the REDOXS trial showed increased mortality in septic shock patients with multiorgan failure receiving high-dose glutamine, so caution is warranted.'),
('094fd82b-6e1b-5e13-9bd4-dbb64796b87a'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'What is the most sensitive marker for monitoring short-term nutritional status changes?', '["Albumin","Retinol-binding protein","Total protein","Transferrin","Prealbumin"]'::jsonb, 'Prealbumin', 'Prealbumin has a short half-life of 2 days, making it superior to albumin (half-life 20 days) for monitoring acute changes in nutritional status. However, all serum proteins are negative acute phase reactants and decrease with inflammation, so they must be interpreted in the clinical context. Prealbumin levels <15 mg/dL indicate significant protein-calorie malnutrition.'),
('537f34a6-8829-5c0c-bf99-001798911b29'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'What is the primary indication for using elemental enteral formulas?', '["Long-term maintenance nutrition","Critically ill patients with intact GI function","Standard postoperative nutrition","Severely impaired GI function requiring predigested nutrients","Weight loss in obesity"]'::jsonb, 'Severely impaired GI function requiring predigested nutrients', 'Elemental formulas contain predigested nutrients (amino acids, simple sugars, medium-chain triglycerides) requiring minimal digestion and absorption. They are indicated for patients with severely impaired GI function including short bowel syndrome, severe pancreatitis, and inflammatory bowel disease. However, they are more expensive, hyperosmolar, and less palatable than polymeric formulas.'),
('95d91fb0-b1e5-5eb0-a3ed-b2f7fa3acb0f'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Which fat-soluble vitamin deficiency causes hemolytic anemia in premature infants?', '["None of the above","Vitamin A","Vitamin E","Vitamin K","Vitamin D"]'::jsonb, 'Vitamin E', 'Vitamin E is a potent antioxidant that protects cell membranes from lipid peroxidation. Premature infants have limited vitamin E stores and are susceptible to hemolytic anemia due to oxidative damage to erythrocyte membranes. Deficiency also causes peripheral neuropathy, ataxia, and retinopathy. Vitamin E deficiency is rare in adults and typically occurs only with fat malabsorption syndromes.'),
('49af5429-c3f4-5df6-aae7-7f504eb61809'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'What is the daily protein requirement for a critically ill surgical patient?', '["1.0 g/kg/day","2.5-3.0 g/kg/day","1.5-2.0 g/kg/day","0.5 g/kg/day","0.8 g/kg/day"]'::jsonb, '1.5-2.0 g/kg/day', 'Critically ill surgical patients are in a hypercatabolic state with increased protein breakdown and nitrogen losses. Protein requirements increase to 1.5-2.0 g/kg/day to support wound healing, immune function, and preserve lean body mass. Normal maintenance is 0.8 g/kg/day. Burn patients may require even higher protein intake of 2.0-3.0 g/kg/day.'),
('97a3c23d-2257-5421-adbd-b12286f7a3b5'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Which mineral deficiency is associated with Keshan disease (cardiomyopathy)?', '["Magnesium","Selenium","Zinc","Copper","Iodine"]'::jsonb, 'Selenium', 'Keshan disease is an endemic dilated cardiomyopathy found in selenium-deficient regions of China, primarily affecting children and women of childbearing age. Selenium is an essential component of glutathione peroxidase, an antioxidant enzyme that protects against oxidative damage. Kashin-Beck disease, an osteoarthritis, is also associated with selenium deficiency combined with other environmental factors.'),
('b6dd1b9c-6d96-5cbd-aa7b-f0e2ebf197f5'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'What is the first-line treatment for iron deficiency anemia without contraindications?', '["Intramuscular iron dextran clinical presentation","IV iron infusion clinical presentation","Dietary modification alone clinical presentation","Blood transfusion clinical presentation","Oral ferrous sulfate 65 mg elemental iron 2-3 times daily"]'::jsonb, 'Oral ferrous sulfate 65 mg elemental iron 2-3 times daily', 'Oral iron therapy is first-line for iron deficiency anemia. Ferrous sulfate 325 mg (65 mg elemental iron) given 2-3 times daily typically raises hemoglobin by 1-2 g/dL over 2-4 weeks. Reticulocytosis is seen within 7-10 days. Absorption is enhanced by concurrent vitamin C administration. GI side effects include constipation, nausea, and dark stools.'),
('0841e348-81a8-5855-a105-bb0c9dc37d9d'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Which of the following is a characteristic laboratory finding in iron deficiency anemia?', '["Normal ferritin, low TIBC clinical presentation","Low ferritin, high TIBC, low transferrin saturation","High ferritin, high TIBC clinical presentation","Low TIBC, high ferritin clinical presentation","High ferritin, low TIBC clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Low ferritin, high TIBC, low transferrin saturation', 'Iron deficiency anemia is characterized by a microcytic hypochromic picture with low serum ferritin (<15 ng/mL, gold standard), low MCV, low MCH, low transferrin saturation (<16%), elevated TIBC, and low hemoglobin. Ferritin is the most specific test, as it directly reflects iron stores. Bone marrow iron staining is the definitive gold standard but is rarely needed.'),
('1945e5c9-356c-5cf2-9e9d-1648e7032af3'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'After which type of bowel resection would vitamin B12 deficiency most likely develop?', '["Ileal resection >100 cm","Gastric resection","Duodenal resection","Colonic resection","Jejunal resection"]'::jsonb, 'Ileal resection >100 cm', 'Vitamin B12 is absorbed exclusively in the terminal ileum via intrinsic factor-mediated endocytosis. Resection of more than 100 cm of distal ileum removes the primary absorption site, necessitating lifelong intramuscular B12 supplementation. Ileal resection also causes bile salt malabsorption leading to diarrhea, fat malabsorption, and increased risk of oxalate kidney stones.'),
('9810d7a3-e9c7-5365-9023-c3cee4851262'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'What is the mechanism of dumping syndrome after gastric bypass surgery?', '["Pancreatic enzyme insufficiency clinical presentation","Rapid gastric emptying of hyperosmolar chyme into the small intestine","Bile reflux into the stomach clinical presentation","Delayed gastric emptying clinical presentation","Increased gastric acid secretion clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Rapid gastric emptying of hyperosmolar chyme into the small intestine', 'Dumping syndrome occurs after gastric bypass when food rapidly enters the small intestine without normal gastric reservoir function. Early dumping (15-30 minutes postprandial) causes vasomotor symptoms from fluid shifts: tachycardia, sweating, diarrhea, and hypotension. Late dumping (2-3 hours) involves reactive hypoglycemia from excessive insulin release. Management includes separating liquids from solids and avoiding simple sugars.'),
('f33fd61d-1e38-5c73-b96b-9a4a17711e59'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Which vitamin deficiency causes pellagra with dermatitis, diarrhea, and dementia?', '["Vitamin B1","Vitamin B5","Vitamin B2","Vitamin B3","Vitamin B6"]'::jsonb, 'Vitamin B3', 'Pellagra is caused by niacin deficiency and presents with the classic triad of dermatitis (photosensitive Casal necklace rash), diarrhea (from glossitis and enteritis), and dementia. A fourth D, death, occurs if untreated. Pellagra is historically associated with corn-based diets, as corn niacin is bound and unavailable. Niacin can be synthesized from tryptophan (60 mg tryptophan = 1 mg niacin) requiring B6 and B2 as cofactors.'),
('2f4ec702-ccb2-5c28-8ad4-1efe352e9a10'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'What is the most effective screening tool for preoperative malnutrition risk assessment?', '["Body mass index","Serum albumin level","Subjective Global Assessment","White blood cell count","C-reactive protein"]'::jsonb, 'Subjective Global Assessment', 'The Subjective Global Assessment (SGA) is a validated clinical tool based on history (weight loss, dietary intake, GI symptoms, functional capacity) and physical examination (muscle wasting, fat loss, edema). It classifies patients as well-nourished, moderately malnourished (SGA-B), or severely malnourished (SGA-C) and predicts postoperative complications better than single laboratory markers. No laboratory tests are required.'),
('4851d668-3f97-5eb5-859a-0e982433ddda'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'What is the primary fuel source during the first 24 hours of starvation?', '["Ketone bodies","Muscle protein","Lactate from gluconeogenesis","Hepatic glycogen","Adipose tissue triglycerides"]'::jsonb, 'Hepatic glycogen', 'During the first 24 hours of starvation, hepatic glycogen stores provide glucose for energy through glycogenolysis. After glycogen depletion, gluconeogenesis from amino acids (muscle protein breakdown) becomes the primary glucose source. Over 2-3 weeks, the body adapts to use ketone bodies from fatty acid oxidation as fuel, sparing muscle protein. Basal metabolic rate decreases as starvation progresses.'),
('61543422-cbb0-5bb0-b78c-a7ba0168c438'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Which of the following electrolyte abnormalities is the hallmark of refeeding syndrome?', '["Hyperphosphatemia","Hyperkalemia","Hypermagnesemia","Hypocalcemia","Hypophosphatemia"]'::jsonb, 'Hypophosphatemia', 'Hypophosphatemia is the hallmark laboratory abnormality of refeeding syndrome, resulting from insulin-driven intracellular shift of phosphate during carbohydrate repletion. Phosphate is essential for ATP synthesis, and severe hypophosphatemia (<1.5 mg/dL) causes myocardial dysfunction, respiratory failure, muscle weakness, and neurological symptoms. Prevention requires identifying high-risk patients, slow caloric initiation, and aggressive electrolyte monitoring and replacement.'),
('921b1ef1-54d8-5096-bc20-cbcb2acc2ada'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Which trace element supplementation in TPN requires dose reduction in cholestatic liver disease?', '["Copper","Chromium","Zinc","Selenium","Manganese"]'::jsonb, 'Manganese', 'Manganese is excreted primarily through bile, so cholestatic liver disease leads to manganese accumulation and potential neurotoxicity. Chronic manganese toxicity causes Parkinson-like symptoms including tremor, rigidity, bradykinesia, and dystonia. In patients with cholestasis, manganese should be reduced or temporarily withheld from TPN trace element preparations, with periodic monitoring of blood levels.'),
('a0fa8275-e695-55d8-a2de-bd638be20ba4'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'What is the definition of obesity according to WHO BMI classification?', '["BMI >= 25 kg/m2","BMI >= 40 kg/m2","BMI >= 35 kg/m2","BMI >= 27 kg/m2","BMI >= 30 kg/m2"]'::jsonb, 'BMI >= 30 kg/m2', 'WHO defines obesity as BMI >= 30 kg/m2, further classified as class I (30-34.9), class II (35-39.9), and class III (>=40, severe/morbid). Overweight is BMI 25-29.9 kg/m2. Obesity is associated with increased risk of type 2 diabetes, hypertension, cardiovascular disease, obstructive sleep apnea, certain cancers, and premature mortality.'),
('3470bbe9-5948-5ed3-855d-ade706813290'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Which specialized enteral formula is designed to modulate the immune response in surgical patients?', '["Immunonutrition formulas","High-fiber formulas","Elemental formulas","Polymeric formulas","Semi-elemental formulas"]'::jsonb, 'Immunonutrition formulas', 'Immunonutrition formulas are enriched with arginine (NO precursor for vasodilation and immune function), glutamine (enterocyte fuel), omega-3 fatty acids (anti-inflammatory), and nucleotides (cell proliferation). Preoperative administration for 5-7 days and postoperative continuation reduces infectious complications, anastomotic leaks, and length of stay in elective upper GI surgery. They are contraindicated in septic patients.'),
('8b2a2b30-a5d6-56a8-8530-b65216751790'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Which mineral deficiency causes impaired wound healing, growth retardation, and hypogonadism?', '["Calcium","Selenium","Copper","Iron","Zinc"]'::jsonb, 'Zinc', 'Zinc is an essential trace element and cofactor for over 300 metalloenzymes involved in protein synthesis, cell division, immune function, and wound healing. Deficiency manifests as growth retardation, hypogonadism and delayed sexual maturation, impaired immune function with recurrent infections, diarrhea, and acrodermatitis enteropathica (periorificial dermatitis). Zinc supplementation (10-20 mg/day) reduces the duration and severity of diarrhea in children.'),
('ed5dec28-ff31-56b0-ac6a-fe478c9b88b3'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'What is the gold standard method for measuring body composition?', '["Skinfold calipers","Dual-energy X-ray absorptiometry","Magnetic resonance imaging","Bioelectrical impedance analysis","Computed tomography (CT) scan"]'::jsonb, 'Dual-energy X-ray absorptiometry', 'DEXA is the gold standard for body composition analysis, providing accurate measurement of bone mineral density, fat mass, and lean body mass with minimal radiation exposure. It is the reference method for diagnosing osteoporosis (T-score <-2.5) and monitoring changes in body composition. While CT and MRI also measure body composition, DEXA is preferred due to lower radiation, cost, and wider availability.'),
('27380244-ada9-5804-b482-4c70a506139e'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'What is the primary indication for parenteral nutrition over enteral nutrition?', '["Mild malnutrition","Short-term nutritional support","Patient preference","Postoperative ileus lasting 24 hours","Non-functioning GI tract or inaccessible GI tract"]'::jsonb, 'Non-functioning GI tract or inaccessible GI tract', 'Parenteral nutrition is indicated when the GI tract is non-functioning (prolonged ileus, bowel obstruction, severe pancreatitis, short bowel syndrome) or inaccessible (high-output fistula, severe mucositis). Enteral nutrition is always preferred when the GI tract is functional due to lower cost, fewer complications, preservation of gut barrier function, and reduced infectious morbidity.'),
('4ef2f2a1-4f2f-575e-aadd-820fbccd8452'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Which vitamin deficiency is caused by isoniazid therapy for tuberculosis?', '["Vitamin B6","Vitamin B2","Vitamin B3","Vitamin B12","Vitamin B1"]'::jsonb, 'Vitamin B6', 'Isoniazid causes vitamin B6 deficiency by forming a hydrazone complex with pyridoxine, increasing its renal excretion and inhibiting pyridoxal phosphate-dependent enzymes. Deficiency leads to peripheral neuropathy (the most common side effect), sideroblastic anemia, and seizures in overdoses. Prophylactic pyridoxine 25-50 mg/day is routinely co-administered with isoniazid to prevent neuropathy.'),
('dd98a929-252c-579d-b3d3-e644116f642b'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'What is the significance of waist-to-hip ratio (WHR) in clinical assessment?', '["Measurement of muscle mass clinical presentation","Predictor of cardiovascular disease and metabolic syndrome risk","Evaluation of hydration status clinical presentation","Assessment of bone density clinical presentation","Direct measure of total body fat clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Predictor of cardiovascular disease and metabolic syndrome risk', 'Waist-to-hip ratio (WHR) measures central obesity distribution, which is a stronger predictor of cardiovascular disease and metabolic syndrome than BMI alone. WHR >0.9 in men and >0.85 in women indicates central obesity. Waist circumference is measured at the midpoint between the lower rib and iliac crest. Visceral adipose tissue is metabolically active, releasing inflammatory cytokines and free fatty acids.'),
('c01f8447-913e-5250-af22-b3968ce1aefb'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Which of the following is a major complication of rapid weight loss after bariatric surgery?', '["Cholelithiasis","Hepatic cirrhosis","Pancreatitis","Renal failure","Gastric ulceration"]'::jsonb, 'Cholelithiasis', 'Rapid weight loss after bariatric surgery causes supersaturation of bile with cholesterol, leading to gallstone formation in up to 30% of patients. Prophylactic ursodeoxycholic acid 500-600 mg/day for 6 months postoperatively reduces gallstone incidence to approximately 3%. The risk is highest during the first 6 months when weight loss is most rapid.'),
('1e180a8d-f45d-56f6-9d5a-4afc64146bf6'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'What is the mechanism of action of medium-chain triglycerides (MCT) in short bowel syndrome?', '["Stimulation of colonic water absorption","Increased bile acid secretion","Direct portal venous absorption without requiring lymphatic transport","Enhanced pancreatic lipase activity","Reduced intestinal motility"]'::jsonb, 'Direct portal venous absorption without requiring lymphatic transport', 'MCT are absorbed directly into the portal venous system without requiring bile salts, pancreatic enzymes, or chylomicron formation, making them ideal for short bowel syndrome, pancreatic insufficiency, and lymphatic obstruction. Long-chain triglycerides (LCT) require chylomicron formation and lymphatic transport, which is impaired in these conditions.'),
('7ede012f-6d77-544b-b5e9-a9c2d8fdcd80'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Which nutritional deficiency is most common worldwide?', '["Vitamin A deficiency","Iodine deficiency","Vitamin D deficiency","Zinc deficiency","Iron deficiency"]'::jsonb, 'Iron deficiency', 'Iron deficiency is the most common and widespread nutritional deficiency globally, affecting approximately 30% of the world population. It causes microcytic hypochromic anemia with symptoms of fatigue, pallor, and impaired cognitive function. Causes include inadequate dietary intake, chronic blood loss (GI bleeding, menorrhagia), and malabsorption. At-risk populations include women of childbearing age, children, and elderly.'),
('2e33900a-3123-5a9c-aa8c-81496f5fb3e9'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'What is the role of preoperative carbohydrate loading in Enhanced Recovery After Surgery (ERAS) protocols?', '["Reduces postoperative insulin resistance and anxiety","Decreases gastric residual volume","Stimulates bowel motility","Increases glycogen stores for wound healing","Prevents intraoperative hypoglycemia"]'::jsonb, 'Reduces postoperative insulin resistance and anxiety', 'Preoperative carbohydrate loading (50-100 g clear CHO drink 2-4 hours before surgery) reduces postoperative insulin resistance, decreases anxiety and thirst, and improves patient well-being. It is safe in patients without delayed gastric emptying and is a key component of ERAS protocols. This contrasts with traditional NPO after midnight guidelines.'),
('2b7e0e9b-b472-5bf9-84fd-a74e6c2e5056'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Which of the following best describes the difference between cachexia and starvation?', '["Cachexia involves cytokine-mediated muscle wasting with inflammation; starvation primarily involv","Starvation causes more rapid muscle loss than cachexia","Cachexia is reversible with caloric supplementation alone","Cachexia spares muscle mass while starvation depletes it","There is no physiological difference between cachexia and starvation"]'::jsonb, 'Cachexia involves cytokine-mediated muscle wasting with inflammation; starvation primarily involv', 'Cachexia differs fundamentally from starvation in that it is driven by systemic inflammation (TNF-alpha, IL-6) causing disproportionate muscle protein breakdown resistant to nutritional support. Starvation triggers adaptive metabolic responses that preferentially utilize fat stores while conserving muscle protein. Cachexia is common in cancer, heart failure, and chronic infections, and carries a poor prognosis.'),
('e997b1c8-fb61-5a95-88b2-2fa73391e109'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'What is the recommended postoperative target for blood glucose control in surgical patients?', '["180-220 mg/dL","140-180 mg/dL","80-110 mg/dL","200-250 mg/dL","<300 mg/dL"]'::jsonb, '140-180 mg/dL', 'The recommended blood glucose target for hospitalized surgical patients is 140-180 mg/dL. Tight glycemic control (80-110 mg/dL) increases risk of hypoglycemia without mortality benefit, while loose control (>180 mg/dL) increases risk of surgical site infections and other complications. Basal-bolus insulin regimens are preferred over sliding scale alone for achieving glycemic targets.'),
('6d8079ab-9246-5916-918c-e3d1772b8e04'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Which nutrient deficiency causes angular stomatitis, cheilitis, and glossitis?', '["Vitamin B6","Vitamin B2","Vitamin B12","Vitamin B1","Vitamin B3"]'::jsonb, 'Vitamin B2', 'Riboflavin deficiency (ariboflavinosis) presents with angular stomatitis (fissures at mouth corners), cheilitis (inflamed lips), magenta-colored glossitis, seborrheic dermatitis, photophobia, and corneal vascularization. Riboflavin is a component of FAD and FMN, essential for redox reactions in energy metabolism. Deficiency often occurs with other B-vitamin deficiencies and is treated with riboflavin supplementation.'),
('53ee779b-d203-54be-a2d1-e9bd0ad84096'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'What is the optimal timing for initiating enteral nutrition in critically ill patients?', '["Within 24-48 hours of ICU admission","Only after bowel sounds return","When the patient is extubated","After 7 days of ICU stay","After 72 hours of hemodynamic stability"]'::jsonb, 'Within 24-48 hours of ICU admission', 'Early enteral nutrition within 24-48 hours of ICU admission reduces infectious complications, length of stay, and mortality in critically ill patients. Even trophic feeding (10-20 mL/h) provides gut barrier protection and prevents bacterial translocation. Initiation should be delayed until hemodynamic stability is achieved, but the presence of bowel sounds is not required.'),
('1f346576-c936-52c3-9d3e-a2e9c25e26f0'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Which type of feeding access is placed under radiological guidance when esophageal obstruction prevents endoscopy?', '["Radiologically inserted gastrostomy","Nasogastric tube","Percutaneous endoscopic gastrostomy","Feeding jejunostomy","Nasojejunal tube"]'::jsonb, 'Radiologically inserted gastrostomy', 'RIG is performed under fluoroscopic guidance and does not require endoscopy, making it suitable for patients with pharyngeal or esophageal obstruction that prevents PEG placement. The gastrostomy tube is inserted percutaneously after gastric insufflation via a nasogastric tube. PEG remains the preferred method when feasible due to lower complication rates.'),
('7b51d6ff-4a83-5d5b-baca-61ed6d93a6f7'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'What is the significance of handgrip strength measurement in preoperative assessment?', '["Assesses pulmonary function","Correlates with bone mineral density","Determines nutritional protein requirements","Measures upper body muscle endurance only","Predicts postoperative complications and length of stay"]'::jsonb, 'Predicts postoperative complications and length of stay', 'Handgrip strength measured by dynamometry is a simple, reliable predictor of surgical outcomes. Low preoperative grip strength is independently associated with increased postoperative complications, longer hospital stay, higher mortality, and functional decline. It serves as an objective measure of nutritional status and frailty, reflecting both muscle mass and neuromuscular function.'),
('48251f04-c7c4-5705-99b4-54ec2576b7db'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Which of the following statements about sarcopenia is correct?', '["It is reversible with short-term high","It only affects obese individuals","It primarily affects the lower extremities only","It is the same as cachexia and responds equally to nutritional support","It is age-related loss of muscle mass and strength associated with functional decline"]'::jsonb, 'It is age-related loss of muscle mass and strength associated with functional decline', 'Sarcopenia is the progressive, generalized loss of skeletal muscle mass and strength that occurs with aging, leading to increased risk of falls, fractures, frailty, disability, and mortality. Diagnostic criteria include low muscle mass (measured by DEXA or BIA), low muscle strength (handgrip), and low physical performance (gait speed). Treatment emphasizes resistance exercise combined with adequate protein intake.'),
('523742ff-c0e8-5447-b5ea-d5ea3f9a5491'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'What is the optimal calcium formulation for supplementation after bariatric surgery?', '["Calcium citrate","Calcium phosphate","Calcium gluconate","Calcium lactate","Calcium carbonate"]'::jsonb, 'Calcium citrate', 'Calcium citrate is preferred after bariatric surgery because its absorption does not require gastric acid, unlike calcium carbonate which requires an acidic environment. Post-bariatric patients have reduced gastric acid production and often take proton pump inhibitors. Calcium citrate with vitamin D (800-1000 IU/day) is recommended at doses of 1000-1500 mg/day to prevent metabolic bone disease.'),
('5b1f9af2-d52b-5091-a61b-5fb0a4ed3550'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Which vitamin is synthesized by intestinal bacteria and contributes significantly to human nutrition?', '["Vitamin K2","Vitamin D","Vitamin A","Vitamin C","Vitamin B12"]'::jsonb, 'Vitamin K2', 'Intestinal bacteria in the colon synthesize menaquinones (vitamin K2), which contribute to the body''s vitamin K pool. However, the primary source of vitamin K remains dietary phylloquinone (K1) from green leafy vegetables. The contribution of bacterial synthesis alone is insufficient, which is why newborn vitamin K prophylaxis is essential, as infants have sterile guts and low vitamin K stores.'),
('423da39c-20d4-5b7b-b153-8ea65a8dbe8f'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'What is the mechanism of action of probiotics in preventing antibiotic-associated diarrhea?', '["Reduction of intestinal motility","Stimulation of gastric acid secretion","Enhancement of bile acid secretion","Restoration of gut microbiota and competitive exclusion of pathogens","Direct antimicrobial effect through toxin production"]'::jsonb, 'Restoration of gut microbiota and competitive exclusion of pathogens', 'Probiotics are live microorganisms that confer health benefits by restoring intestinal microbial balance, competing with pathogenic bacteria for adhesion sites and nutrients, producing antimicrobial substances, and modulating immune function. Lactobacillus, Bifidobacterium, and Saccharomyces boulardii are commonly used strains. Probiotics are contraindicated in immunocompromised patients due to risk of bacteremia.'),
('4480efd4-d1d6-501f-b9c5-243513fa7983'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Which of the following is a contraindication to using probiotics in surgical patients?', '["Severe acute pancreatitis","Antibiotic therapy","Colorectal surgery","Postoperative ileus","Elective laparoscopic cholecystectomy"]'::jsonb, 'Severe acute pancreatitis', 'Probiotics are contraindicated in severe acute pancreatitis based on the PROPATRIA trial, which showed increased mortality with probiotic prophylaxis in predicted severe acute pancreatitis. The hypothesized mechanism is bacterial translocation from probiotic strains causing infected pancreatic necrosis. In other surgical settings, probiotics may reduce postoperative infections, but caution is warranted in critically ill and immunocompromised patients.'),
('3a9abb13-db8b-52cf-ba8f-1f770f5031f2'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'What is the role of fish oil (omega-3) in parenteral nutrition?', '["Preservative for lipid emulsion stability","Anti-inflammatory effects through modulation of eicosanoid production","Primary caloric source","Vitamin carrier","Electrolyte replacement"]'::jsonb, 'Anti-inflammatory effects through modulation of eicosanoid production', 'Fish oil-based lipid emulsions (Omegaven, Smoflipid) provide omega-3 fatty acids EPA and DHA, which produce less inflammatory eicosanoids (LTB5, PGE3) compared to omega-6-derived mediators (LTB4, PGE2). This may reduce inflammation, improve outcomes in ARDS, and help prevent parenteral nutrition-associated liver disease (PNALD) by reducing hepatic steatosis and cholestasis.'),
('9b005312-c15b-52f5-a49a-24268ff0fd6f'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'What is the significance of a low phase angle measured by bioelectrical impedance analysis (BIA)?', '["Indicator of high muscle mass clinical presentation","Associated with malnutrition, impaired cell membrane integrity, and increased mortality","Marker of excellent nutritional status clinical presentation","Related to increased bone density clinical presentation","Sign of overhydration clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Associated with malnutrition, impaired cell membrane integrity, and increased mortality', 'Phase angle is a BIA-derived parameter reflecting cell membrane integrity and body cell mass. Low phase angle indicates impaired cell membrane function, cellular breakdown, and malnutrition. It is a validated prognostic marker in cancer, HIV, elderly, and surgical populations, with low values independently predicting higher morbidity and mortality. Normal values vary by age, sex, and population.'),
('521d42ae-9f5f-5888-b684-b00218848d0d'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Which of the following dietary approaches is supported by the strongest evidence for cardiovascular disease prevention?', '["Paleolithic diet","Mediterranean diet","Intermittent fasting","Ketogenic diet","Low-fat diet"]'::jsonb, 'Mediterranean diet', 'The Mediterranean diet, characterized by high olive oil, fish, nuts, legumes, fruits, vegetables, and whole grains with low red meat and processed foods, has the strongest evidence for cardiovascular disease prevention from large randomized trials including PREDIMED. It reduces cardiovascular events, mortality, diabetes incidence, and cognitive decline. Its benefits are attributed to the synergistic effects of multiple bioactive compounds.'),
('4df47551-6c4e-544f-9082-a3ce7ed5e45c'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'What is the primary goal of nutritional support in end-of-life care?', '["Aggressive caloric repletion to reverse cachexia","PEG tube placement for all patients with decreased oral intake","Patient comfort and quality of life, not prolongation of survival","Correction of all nutritional deficiencies","Parenteral nutrition to maintain organ function"]'::jsonb, 'Patient comfort and quality of life, not prolongation of survival', 'In end-of-life care, the focus of nutritional support shifts from aggressive metabolic support to comfort. Artificial nutrition and hydration may worsen symptoms including edema, respiratory secretions, and gastrointestinal distress. Assisted oral feeding should continue if safe and desired. Ethical principles of beneficence, non-maleficence, and autonomy guide decision-making. PEG in advanced dementia does not improve survival or quality of life.'),
('b00bc972-be21-5fa5-8ae1-b6221ccb1523'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Which vitamin deficiency is associated with sideroblastic (microcytic) anemia?', '["Folic acid","Vitamin B6","Vitamin C","Vitamin B12","Vitamin E"]'::jsonb, 'Vitamin B6', 'Pyridoxine (B6) is a cofactor for aminolevulinic acid synthase, the rate-limiting enzyme in heme biosynthesis. B6 deficiency impairs heme synthesis, leading to microcytic hypochromic anemia with ringed sideroblasts on bone marrow examination. Other causes of sideroblastic anemia include alcohol, lead poisoning, myelodysplastic syndrome, and medications (isoniazid, chloramphenicol). Treatment involves pyridoxine supplementation and addressing the underlying cause.'),
('62fb20cf-f75c-5da3-9cf1-5edd13329c50'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Which laboratory parameter is most useful for monitoring compliance with phenylalanine restriction in phenylketonuria (PKU)?', '["Genetic testing","Blood urea nitrogen","Serum phenylalanine levels","Urine phenylketones","Plasma amino acid profile"]'::jsonb, 'Serum phenylalanine levels', 'Monitoring serum phenylalanine levels is essential for managing PKU, an autosomal recessive disorder of phenylalanine hydroxylase deficiency. Target phenylalanine levels vary by age but are generally 120-360 micromol/L. Dietary restriction of phenylalanine (avoiding high-protein foods) with special metabolic formula is the mainstay of treatment. Poor compliance leads to progressive intellectual disability and neurological deterioration.'),
('f2bf28f0-2f52-5345-b08b-c40cc6405dc4'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'What is the role of arginine in immunonutrition?', '["Primary fuel source for enterocytes clinical presentation","Direct antimicrobial activity clinical presentation","Electrolyte balance maintenance clinical presentation","Antioxidant protection clinical presentation","Precursor for nitric oxide synthesis involved in immune modulation and vasodilation"]'::jsonb, 'Precursor for nitric oxide synthesis involved in immune modulation and vasodilation', 'Arginine is a conditionally essential amino acid that serves as the precursor for nitric oxide (NO) synthesis via nitric oxide synthase. NO mediates vasodilation, enhances immune function, and promotes wound healing. However, arginine is controversial in sepsis because excessive NO production may cause hypotension and worsen outcomes. Immunonutrition formulas containing arginine are recommended for elective surgery but not for septic patients.'),
('0384f3d8-1de5-57c5-8468-ee6cccb05f01'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Which vitamin toxicity causes pseudotumor cerebri with headache and vomiting?', '["Vitamin E toxicity","Vitamin D toxicity","Vitamin A","Vitamin C toxicity","Vitamin K toxicity"]'::jsonb, 'Vitamin A', 'Acute vitamin A toxicity causes pseudotumor cerebri presenting with headache, vomiting, papilledema, and visual disturbances due to increased intracranial pressure. Chronic toxicity leads to hepatotoxicity, bone pain, skin changes, and teratogenicity (isotretinoin). Beta-carotene from plant sources is not toxic because conversion to retinol is regulated. Vitamin A toxicity requires immediate discontinuation and supportive care.'),
('27e61863-8c56-554b-9fbd-6e072d021000'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'What is the most common trace element deficiency in long-term home parenteral nutrition?', '["Selenium deficiency","Chromium deficiency","Zinc deficiency","Copper deficiency","Manganese deficiency"]'::jsonb, 'Selenium deficiency', 'Selenium deficiency is a recognized complication of long-term TPN, causing Keshan cardiomyopathy, myopathy, and impaired immune function. Current TPN trace element preparations provide adequate selenium, but deficiencies can occur with increased losses (burns, diarrhea) or inadequate supplementation. Routine monitoring of selenium levels is recommended for patients on long-term home TPN, with supplementation as needed.'),
('573bc6b2-b0af-5b34-b5e5-c09872fda26f'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Which statement about the omega-3 index is correct?', '["It is not modifiable by diet clinical presentation","It reflects total cholesterol levels clinical presentation","It is a measure of dietary fat intake only clinical presentation","It measures EPA+DHA percentage in RBC membranes; <4% indicates high cardiovascular risk","It determines vitamin E requirements clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'It measures EPA+DHA percentage in RBC membranes; <4% indicates high cardiovascular risk', 'The omega-3 index is the percentage of eicosapentaenoic acid (EPA) and docosahexaenoic acid (DHA) in red blood cell membranes, reflecting long-term omega-3 intake. An index >8% is associated with cardiovascular protection, while <4% indicates increased cardiovascular risk. It is modifiable through increased dietary intake of fatty fish or fish oil supplements and serves as a biomarker for omega-3 status assessment.'),
('5afc758a-10be-5731-b5a3-7863d6fbe62e'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'What is the recommended daily fiber intake for adults according to dietary guidelines?', '["10-15 g/day","50-75 g/day","25-38 g/day","5-10 g/day","40-50 g/day"]'::jsonb, '25-38 g/day', 'The recommended daily fiber intake is 25-38 g/day for adults, though most consume less than 15 g/day. Soluble fiber (oats, legumes, fruit) lowers glucose and cholesterol, while insoluble fiber (wheat bran, vegetables) increases stool bulk and promotes regularity. High fiber intake is associated with reduced risk of cardiovascular disease, type 2 diabetes, colon cancer, and improved weight management. Fiber should be increased gradually to avoid bloating.'),
('ebbb1b27-dc09-5f57-b940-e930a76eba84'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Which of the following describes the Jod-Basedow phenomenon?', '["Hypothyroidism from excessive iodine intake clinical presentation","Goiter formation from iodine deficiency clinical presentation","Thyroiditis following viral infection clinical presentation","Hyperthyroidism triggered by iodine supplementation in iodine-deficient individuals","Autoimmune hyperthyroidism in Graves disease clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Hyperthyroidism triggered by iodine supplementation in iodine-deficient individuals', 'Jod-Basedow phenomenon occurs when iodine supplementation is given to iodine-deficient individuals with pre-existing thyroid autonomy (functional nodules). The sudden iodine excess provides substrate for autonomous thyroid hormone production, causing hyperthyroidism. This contrasts with the Wolff-Chaikoff effect, where excess iodine transiently inhibits thyroid hormone synthesis. Caution is warranted when introducing iodized salt in iodine-deficient populations.'),
('7cbe5623-f521-5474-a6a3-c8ee9fdaf010'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'What is the primary nutritional concern in vegan patients after bariatric surgery?', '["Fat-soluble vitamin toxicity clinical presentation","Carbohydrate malabsorption clinical presentation","Trace element overload clinical presentation","Excessive protein intake clinical presentation","Vitamin B12, iron, calcium, and vitamin D deficiencies requiring lifelong supplementation"]'::jsonb, 'Vitamin B12, iron, calcium, and vitamin D deficiencies requiring lifelong supplementation', 'Vegan patients have pre-existing risks for B12, iron, calcium, and vitamin D deficiencies that are exacerbated by bariatric surgery. RYGB bypasses the duodenum and proximal jejunum where iron and calcium are absorbed and reduces intrinsic factor availability for B12 absorption. Lifelong supplementation with B12 (IM monthly), iron, calcium citrate, vitamin D, and a comprehensive multivitamin is mandatory. Regular laboratory monitoring is essential.'),
('9abb62ff-5228-59e7-b1c6-c61028758ae5'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Which of the following is a key component of the DASH diet?', '["High sodium intake for electrolyte balance","Exclusive plant-based protein sources","Very low caloric intake","High fruits, vegetables, low-fat dairy, and reduced sodium","High saturated fat and low carbohydrates"]'::jsonb, 'High fruits, vegetables, low-fat dairy, and reduced sodium', 'The DASH (Dietary Approaches to Stop Hypertension) diet emphasizes high intake of fruits, vegetables, whole grains, low-fat dairy, and lean protein while limiting sodium (<2300 mg/day, or <1500 mg/day for hypertensives), saturated fat, and added sugars. It is rich in potassium, magnesium, calcium, and fiber, and has been proven to significantly reduce blood pressure and LDL cholesterol in clinical trials.'),
('61c6c17a-f58c-54d2-a10c-09353a37eb58'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'What is the significance of elevated homocysteine in nutritional assessment?', '["Marker of iron overload clinical presentation","Indicator of B12, folate, or B6 deficiency and cardiovascular risk factor","Direct reflection of glycemic control clinical presentation","Measure of dietary protein intake clinical presentation","Specific marker of vitamin D deficiency clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Indicator of B12, folate, or B6 deficiency and cardiovascular risk factor', 'Homocysteine is a sulfur-containing amino acid whose metabolism requires B12, folate, and B6 as cofactors. Elevated homocysteine is a marker for deficiencies of these vitamins and is an independent risk factor for cardiovascular disease, venous thromboembolism, neural tube defects, and dementia. Methylmalonic acid (MMA) is more specific for B12 deficiency alone. Homocysteine levels decrease with supplementation of B12, folate, and B6.'),
('3f5276b4-357f-5467-b5d5-61323c175f16'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'What is the primary reason for avoiding high-dose vitamin C supplements in hemochromatosis?', '["It interferes with phlebotomy treatment clinical presentation","It chelates iron and causes deficiency clinical presentation","It has no effect on iron metabolism clinical presentation","Vitamin C enhances iron absorption and may precipitate iron overload complications","It causes vitamin C toxicity and renal stones clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Vitamin C enhances iron absorption and may precipitate iron overload complications', 'Vitamin C (ascorbic acid) significantly enhances non-heme iron absorption by reducing ferric iron to ferrous iron in the duodenum. In patients with hemochromatosis, who already have pathologically increased iron absorption, high-dose vitamin C supplements can accelerate iron loading and precipitate organ damage from iron toxicity. These patients should also avoid iron supplements, raw shellfish (Vibrio risk), and excess alcohol.'),
('c28c2869-7859-5544-9045-6af302a83955'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Which of the following is a feature of the refeeding syndrome prevention protocol?', '["Restrict all electrolyte supplementation clinical presentation","Provide high-glucose solutions without thiamine clinical presentation","Start at 50% of target calories with aggressive electrolyte monitoring and replacement","Advance caloric intake over 24 hours clinical presentation","Initiate full caloric requirements immediately clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Start at 50% of target calories with aggressive electrolyte monitoring and replacement', 'Refeeding syndrome prevention requires identifying high-risk patients (anorexia, alcoholism, prolonged starvation, malabsorption) and starting at 20-25 kcal/kg/day or 50% of target calories, with slow advancement over 7-10 days. Electrolytes (phosphate, potassium, magnesium, calcium) must be monitored every 6-12 hours for the first 3 days and replaced aggressively if low. Thiamine 100-300 mg/day should be given before initiating feeding.'),
('becb5227-69fa-5d61-9199-bba59b239b19'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'What is the difference between prebiotics and probiotics?', '["Prebiotics are synthetic; probiotics are natural","Both are live microorganisms","Prebiotics and probiotics are identical","Prebiotics are non-digestible fibers that promote beneficial bacteria growth","Probiotics are fibers; prebiotics are live bacteria"]'::jsonb, 'Prebiotics are non-digestible fibers that promote beneficial bacteria growth', 'Prebiotics (inulin, fructooligosaccharides, galactooligosaccharides) are non-digestible food ingredients that selectively stimulate the growth and activity of beneficial gut bacteria. Probiotics (Lactobacillus, Bifidobacterium, Saccharomyces) are live microorganisms that confer health benefits when administered in adequate amounts. Synbiotics combine both prebiotics and probiotics for additive or synergistic effects on gut health.'),
('98ea9d8e-a683-5779-a16f-6aec57199c5b'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Which vitamin deficiency causes macrocytic anemia with neurological symptoms including subacute combined degeneration of the spinal cord?', '["Vitamin B1","Vitamin B6","Vitamin B12","Vitamin E","Folic acid"]'::jsonb, 'Vitamin B12', 'Vitamin B12 deficiency causes megaloblastic anemia with characteristic neurological manifestations including peripheral neuropathy and subacute combined degeneration of the spinal cord affecting posterior columns (loss of vibration and proprioception) and lateral corticospinal tracts (spasticity, hyperreflexia). The neurological symptoms can occur without anemia and may be irreversible if treatment is delayed. B12 is found exclusively in animal products.'),
('f6b3cf82-828a-5e77-9912-cb1e447a8501'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'What is the recommended approach for managing dumping syndrome after gastric bypass?', '["Consume large meals with high simple sugar content","Restrict all carbohydrate intake","Separate liquids from solids, avoid simple sugars, eat small frequent meals","Increase fluid intake with meals","Lie supine immediately after eating"]'::jsonb, 'Separate liquids from solids, avoid simple sugars, eat small frequent meals', 'First-line management of dumping syndrome involves dietary modification: separating liquids from solids by 30 minutes, avoiding concentrated sweets and simple sugars, increasing protein and fiber intake, and eating small frequent meals. Pharmacologic options for refractory cases include acarbose (delays carbohydrate absorption) and octreotide (slows gastric emptying and inhibits insulin secretion). Most patients improve with dietary measures alone.'),
('6b8b8ed4-d2dc-564d-b398-6d2e962da132'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Which parameter is most important to monitor during the first 72 hours of refeeding in a malnourished patient?', '["Serum creatinine every 24 hours clinical presentation","Hemoglobin and hematocrit daily clinical presentation","Serum phosphate, potassium, and magnesium every 6-12 hours","Blood glucose every 4 hours clinical presentation","Liver function tests weekly clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Serum phosphate, potassium, and magnesium every 6-12 hours', 'During the first 3 days of refeeding, serum phosphate (most critical), potassium, and magnesium must be monitored every 6-12 hours because insulin-driven intracellular shifts can cause life-threatening electrolyte depletion. Phosphate below 1.5 mg/dL requires intravenous replacement. ECG monitoring is advisable for severe hypophosphatemia or hypokalemia due to risk of arrhythmias. Monitoring should continue until electrolyte levels stabilize.'),
('cdafefcd-33c7-5055-96d4-b40a3622487d'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'What is the mechanism of vitamin K prophylaxis in newborns?', '["Stimulates platelet production","Enhances iron absorption","Reduces bilirubin levels","Increases maternal vitamin K transfer through breast milk","Prevents vitamin K deficiency bleeding (VKDB) by providing stores for clotting factor synthesis"]'::jsonb, 'Prevents vitamin K deficiency bleeding (VKDB) by providing stores for clotting factor synthesis', 'Newborns are at risk for VKDB because placental transfer of vitamin K is limited, breast milk contains low vitamin K, and the infant gut is sterile (no bacterial synthesis). A single intramuscular dose of vitamin K (1 mg) at birth provides hepatic stores for synthesis of clotting factors II, VII, IX, and X, preventing potentially fatal intracranial hemorrhage. Oral vitamin K is less effective due to variable absorption.'),
('0732850a-649b-5182-807d-62f93eddb0ae'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Which dietary mineral deficiency is most likely to cause goiter and cretinism?', '["Selenium","Iron","Iodine","Zinc","Calcium"]'::jsonb, 'Iodine', 'Iodine is essential for thyroid hormone (T3, T4) synthesis. Iodine deficiency causes compensatory thyroid hyperplasia (goiter) and hypothyroidism. When deficiency occurs during fetal development and early childhood, it causes cretinism, characterized by severe and irreversible intellectual disability, deaf-mutism, spasticity, and growth retardation. Universal salt iodization is the most effective population-level prevention strategy, significantly reducing the global burden of iodine deficiency disorders.'),
('bb8288e2-20a8-5a56-b626-d03943d4be84'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'What is the primary advantage of cyclic TPN over continuous TPN?', '["Lower risk of catheter infection clinical presentation","More physiologic, reduced hepatotoxicity, improved quality of life","Simpler administration clinical presentation","Higher caloric delivery clinical presentation","No monitoring required clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'More physiologic, reduced hepatotoxicity, improved quality of life', 'Cyclic TPN (infused over 12-16 hours rather than 24 hours) allows a fasting period that is more physiologic, mimicking normal feeding-fasting cycles. Benefits include reduced steatosis and cholestasis (due to lower hepatic fat deposition), improved fat clearance, better glycemic control, and improved quality of life as patients are disconnected from infusion for part of the day. Cycling is particularly important for long-term home TPN patients.'),
('f9c26cc6-487a-5b55-b8c7-303eadaba917'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Which of the following is the most reliable physical examination finding of severe protein-energy malnutrition?', '["Pallor of conjunctiva","Pitting pedal edema","Abdominal distension","Hepatomegaly","Muscle wasting"]'::jsonb, 'Muscle wasting', 'Physical examination for malnutrition focuses on assessment of muscle mass (temporalis, interosseous, deltoid, quadriceps) and subcutaneous fat loss, which are key components of the Subjective Global Assessment (SGA). Specific nutritional deficiency signs include B12 glossitis, niacin dermatitis (Casal necklace), B6 cheilitis, zinc periorificial dermatitis, and vitamin A Bitot spots. Loss of muscle mass is the most reliable indicator of chronic protein-energy malnutrition.'),
('8cdb2350-eb8e-5926-ad86-c90620819124'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'What is the effect of glucagon-like peptide-1 (GLP-1) analogs on gastric emptying and weight?', '["Accelerate gastric emptying and promote weight gain","No effect on gastric motility","Increase appetite","Slow gastric emptying and promote weight loss","Stimulate ghrelin secretion"]'::jsonb, 'Slow gastric emptying and promote weight loss', 'GLP-1 receptor agonists (liraglutide, semaglutide) slow gastric emptying, increase insulin secretion, suppress glucagon, and promote satiety through central and peripheral mechanisms. They are effective for weight management in obesity and improve glycemic control in type 2 diabetes. Delayed gastric emptying necessitates precaution during general anesthesia due to increased aspiration risk; preoperative holding guidelines should be followed.'),
('63ca52a2-41cb-5743-b8e8-bc6b95cb76ac'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Which of the following is the best marker of visceral protein status in a patient without inflammation?', '["C-reactive protein","Total cholesterol","Serum albumin","White blood cell count","Hemoglobin"]'::jsonb, 'Serum albumin', 'Serum albumin (half-life 20 days) reflects visceral protein status and predicts surgical outcomes, but it is a negative acute phase reactant that decreases with inflammation independent of nutrition. In the absence of inflammation (normal CRP), albumin <3.5 g/dL indicates protein-calorie malnutrition. Prealbumin (half-life 2 days) is more responsive to acute nutritional changes but similarly affected by inflammation. Low albumin is associated with increased postoperative complications, longer LOS, and mortality.'),
('bdf4489b-db8b-5f86-a826-e36ab6b2bf96'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'What is the most common cause of vitamin B12 deficiency in adults?', '["Pancreatic exocrine insufficiency","Chronic proton pump inhibitor use","Pernicious anemia","Liver disease","Dietary insufficiency in omnivores"]'::jsonb, 'Pernicious anemia', 'Pernicious anemia is the most common cause of B12 deficiency in adults, resulting from autoimmune destruction of gastric parietal cells leading to intrinsic factor deficiency. It presents with megaloblastic anemia, glossitis, and neurological symptoms. Diagnosis is confirmed by elevated MMA and positive anti-intrinsic factor antibodies. Treatment requires lifelong intramuscular hydroxocobalamin because oral B12 cannot be absorbed without intrinsic factor.'),
('40e9f178-6535-5eb3-856a-889b085ab40b'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Which of the following is a nutritional deficiency specific to gluten-sensitive enteropathy (celiac disease)?', '["Selenium overload clinical presentation","Iron, folate, calcium, vitamin D, and zinc deficiencies from duodenal malabsorption","Vitamin C deficiency exclusively clinical presentation","Iodine excess clinical presentation","Vitamin A toxicity clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Iron, folate, calcium, vitamin D, and zinc deficiencies from duodenal malabsorption', 'Celiac disease causes villous atrophy in the duodenum and proximal jejunum, impairing absorption of iron, folate, calcium, vitamin D, zinc, and other nutrients absorbed in the proximal small intestine. Nutritional deficiencies should be identified and supplemented at diagnosis. With strict gluten-free diet, intestinal healing typically occurs over 6-12 months, after which absorption improves. Lifelong gluten-free diet is essential for management.'),
('c33a6dc4-e687-5518-b92c-e1ddfb453526'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'What is the mechanism by which early enteral feeding reduces postoperative ileus?', '["Stimulation of gastrointestinal motility via neural and hormonal pathways","Promotion of gastric atony clinical presentation","Inhibition of peristalsis clinical presentation","Increased opioid requirements clinical presentation","Reduction of intestinal blood flow clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Stimulation of gastrointestinal motility via neural and hormonal pathways', 'Early enteral feeding after surgery stimulates gastrointestinal motility through local neural reflexes, release of gastrointestinal hormones (motilin, gastrin), and mechanical stimulation of the gut. This contrasts with traditional NPO management, which prolongs ileus. ERAS protocols emphasize early oral intake within 24-48 hours postoperatively, even after GI anastomosis. Early feeding also reduces intestinal permeability, improves wound healing, and decreases infectious complications.'),
('c8d60ee3-cbc7-526e-8a11-39d4cdc6eab9'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Which vitamin deficiency is most likely to cause perinatal depression, lethargy, and alopecia?', '["Vitamin K deficiency clinical presentation","Vitamin D deficiency clinical presentation","Biotin (B7) deficiency from raw egg white consumption","Vitamin A deficiency clinical presentation","Vitamin E deficiency clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Biotin (B7) deficiency from raw egg white consumption', 'Biotin deficiency occurs from consumption of raw egg whites containing avidin, a glycoprotein that binds biotin and prevents its absorption. Symptoms include dermatitis, alopecia, neurological symptoms (depression, lethargy, hallucinations), and glossitis. Biotin is a cofactor for carboxylase enzymes involved in gluconeogenesis and fatty acid synthesis. Cooking egg whites denatures avidin, eliminating the risk. Biotin deficiency is otherwise extremely rare.'),
('02321595-8e48-5549-9636-f09e039807f4'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'What is the recommended daily water intake for men according to dietary guidelines?', '["5.0 L/day","2.0 L/day","3.7 L/day total water","1.5 L/day","4.5 L/day"]'::jsonb, '3.7 L/day total water', 'Total water intake recommendations are 3.7 L/day for adult men and 2.7 L/day for adult women, which includes water from all beverages and moisture in food (approximately 20% of total intake). Surgical patients have increased needs due to fever, diarrhea, vomiting, drains, and increased insensible losses. Hydration status can be monitored by urine color and output (goal >0.5 mL/kg/hour in hospitalized patients).'),
('f9359577-b60a-5cbf-b573-b0a4e1b9a898'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'What is the significance of the glycemic load (GL) compared to the glycemic index (GI)?', '["GL only measures carbohydrate quality clinical presentation","GL is less accurate than GI alone clinical presentation","GL accounts for both carbohydrate quality and quantity, better predicting glycemic response","GL is not clinically useful clinical presentation","GL and GI are identical measurements clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'GL accounts for both carbohydrate quality and quantity, better predicting glycemic response', 'Glycemic load (GL = GI x grams of carbohydrate per serving / 100) provides a more accurate assessment of a food''s effect on blood glucose than GI alone because it accounts for both the quality (GI) and quantity of carbohydrates consumed. For example, watermelon has a high GI but low GL because its carbohydrate content per serving is low. Low-GL diets are associated with improved glycemic control and reduced cardiovascular risk.'),
('ce5915c4-0ad6-5a2f-90d3-c072888fbd56'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Which of the following is a key principle of the ERAS (Enhanced Recovery After Surgery) protocol?', '["Multimodal opioid-sparing analgesia, early feeding, early mobilization, and euvolemia","Postoperative opioid-based analgesia clinical presentation","Prolonged preoperative fasting clinical presentation","Extended bed rest clinical presentation","Liberal intravenous fluid administration clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Multimodal opioid-sparing analgesia, early feeding, early mobilization, and euvolemia', 'ERAS protocols are evidence-based perioperative care bundles that reduce surgical stress, complications, and length of stay. Key elements include: preoperative counseling, carbohydrate loading 2 hours before surgery, multimodal opioid-sparing analgesia (regional blocks, NSAIDs, acetaminophen), goal-directed fluid therapy (avoid overload), maintenance of normothermia, avoidance of drains and tubes, early enteral feeding (within 24 hours), and early mobilization (out of bed on day of surgery).'),
('e512e045-5a2d-5440-82f6-65d39ece0c8f'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'What is the most likely nutritional complication following esophagectomy?', '["Dumping syndrome, reflux, and weight loss requiring feeding jejunostomy","Iron overload clinical presentation","Hypercalcemia from bone resorption clinical presentation","Vitamin A toxicity clinical presentation","Hyperglycemia requiring insulin therapy clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Dumping syndrome, reflux, and weight loss requiring feeding jejunostomy', 'Post-esophagectomy patients commonly experience dumping syndrome, reflux, early satiety, and significant weight loss. A feeding jejunostomy is often placed intraoperatively to allow early enteral nutrition while the gastric conduit heals. Nutritional management includes small frequent meals, semi-solid consistency, separating liquids from solids, and avoiding simple sugars. Long-term nutritional monitoring is essential as many patients develop deficiencies of iron, B12, calcium, and vitamin D.'),
('d456e3fc-72aa-5cf1-9f4d-520f04b8be9d'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Which laboratory finding is most specific for iron deficiency when inflammation is present?', '["Low serum ferritin","Microcytic red cell indices","High TIBC","Low serum iron","Low transferrin saturation"]'::jsonb, 'Low serum ferritin', 'Ferritin is an acute phase reactant that increases with inflammation, potentially masking iron deficiency. A serum ferritin <15 ng/mL is diagnostic of iron deficiency regardless of inflammation. In the presence of inflammation, a ferritin <30 ng/mL may still indicate iron deficiency. Soluble transferrin receptor (sTfR) is not affected by inflammation and can help differentiate iron deficiency from anemia of chronic disease when ferritin is equivocal.'),
('430475fe-f986-5f56-8814-702292c5e925'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'What is the function of uncooked cornstarch therapy in glycogen storage disease type I (von Gierke disease)?', '["Stimulates insulin secretion","Inhibits glycogen breakdown","Enhances ketone body production","Provides immediate glucose for rapid energy","Provides sustained slow-release glucose to prevent hypoglycemia"]'::jsonb, 'Provides sustained slow-release glucose to prevent hypoglycemia', 'Uncooked cornstarch provides sustained glucose release due to slow digestion, maintaining blood glucose levels for 4-6 hours in patients with glycogen storage disease type I (glucose-6-phosphatase deficiency). These patients cannot release glucose from glycogenolysis or gluconeogenesis and develop profound fasting hypoglycemia, lactic acidosis, and hyperuricemia. Cornstarch (1-2 g/kg every 4-6 hours) is the cornerstone of dietary management, with frequent feeds also essential.'),
('836acac6-07ee-56f8-be8c-dff6e5df9fdf'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 2)', 'Nutritional Diseases', 'Which of the following fatty acid profiles is considered optimal for parenteral lipid emulsions?', '["Pure soybean oil clinical presentation","Pure coconut oil clinical presentation","Mixed emulsion with omega-3, omega-6, MCT, and olive oil","Pure fish oil clinical presentation","Pure olive oil clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Mixed emulsion with omega-3, omega-6, MCT, and olive oil', 'Modern parenteral lipid emulsions such as SMOFlipid combine multiple oil sources to achieve a balanced fatty acid profile: soybean oil (essential omega-6), MCT (rapid clearance, ketogenic), olive oil (monounsaturated, less pro-inflammatory), and fish oil (omega-3, anti-inflammatory). This balanced approach reduces the pro-inflammatory effects of pure soybean oil (Intralipid) while preventing essential fatty acid deficiency and providing immunomodulatory benefits from omega-3 fatty acids.'),
('63b8c7b4-a69d-5146-83ae-4b58803292ad'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'What is the primary focus of epidemiology?', '["Diagnosis and treatment of individual patients","Surgical management of disease","Cellular mechanisms of disease","Genetic mutations causing disease","Distribution and determinants of disease in populations"]'::jsonb, 'Distribution and determinants of disease in populations', 'Epidemiology is the study of the distribution and determinants of health-related states in specified populations. It focuses on population health rather than individual patient care.'),
('1093e845-a413-5f05-8414-a734739a5484'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'Who is considered the father of modern epidemiology?', '["Robert Koch","John Snow","Hippocrates","Edward Jenner","Louis Pasteur"]'::jsonb, 'John Snow', 'John Snow is credited as the father of modern epidemiology for his work on the 1854 London cholera outbreak. He used mapping techniques to trace the source to the Broad Street pump.'),
('836f70b0-c8a8-5007-9bfa-a46c1671e7e1'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'Which of the following is a use of epidemiology?', '["Identifying risk factors for disease","Prescribing medications","Performing surgical procedures","Reading radiological images","Conducting laboratory experiments"]'::jsonb, 'Identifying risk factors for disease', 'Epidemiology helps identify risk factors and causes of disease, determine disease burden, evaluate interventions, and inform public health policy. It does not involve direct patient care or surgical procedures.'),
('43735b14-43e2-5496-a895-9c0ee8d63ce8'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'The epidemiological triad consists of:', '["Genes, environment, and lifestyle","Agent, host, and environment","Bacteria, virus, and fungus","Doctor, patient, and hospital","Morbidity, mortality, and prevalence"]'::jsonb, 'Agent, host, and environment', 'The epidemiological triad describes the interaction between an agent (pathogen), a susceptible host, and the environment. This model is fundamental to understanding disease causation.'),
('cf8fd861-2261-59fd-b878-88caab8ab304'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'Endemic refers to:', '["Elimination of a disease","Sudden increase in disease cases","Worldwide spread of a disease","Constant presence of a disease in a population","Rapid decrease in disease cases"]'::jsonb, 'Constant presence of a disease in a population', 'Endemic refers to the constant presence and/or usual prevalence of a disease within a geographic area. For example, malaria is endemic in certain tropical regions.'),
('2ca93c0a-365b-54e1-808b-4889d775806a'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'Epidemic refers to:', '["An increase in disease cases above expected levels","Worldwide spread of disease","The constant presence of disease","Absence of disease","Seasonal variation of disease"]'::jsonb, 'An increase in disease cases above expected levels', 'An epidemic occurs when there is a sudden increase in the number of cases of a disease above what is normally expected in a population. COVID-19 started as an epidemic in Wuhan before becoming a pandemic.'),
('17e54c52-8853-5ce3-9d77-a56fbe6d9f77'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'A researcher is conducting an epidemiological study. Pandemic refers to:', '["A disease affecting only animals","A seasonal increase in cases","A localised outbreak","A disease confined to a single hospital","An epidemic spreading across multiple continents"]'::jsonb, 'An epidemic spreading across multiple continents', 'A pandemic is an epidemic that has spread across several countries or continents, usually affecting a large number of people. Examples include the 1918 influenza pandemic and COVID-19.'),
('2a28781a-dcef-5d59-88b2-06a169760150'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'Which is NOT a component of the epidemiological triad?', '["Host","Environment","Treatment","Agent","Pathogen"]'::jsonb, 'Treatment', 'The epidemiological triad consists of agent, host, and environment. Treatment is not part of this model; it is a response to disease after it occurs.'),
('7710e35b-c727-5abc-ae1a-59e1c2d2a70e'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'Morbidity refers to:', '["Transmission of infection","Immunity to disease","Death from a disease","The state of being diseased","Recovery from illness"]'::jsonb, 'The state of being diseased', 'Morbidity refers to any departure from a state of physiological well-being. It encompasses illness, disability, and disease burden in a population.'),
('c39c65f5-12d4-5c7b-b64e-40495cbfe26e'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'A researcher is conducting an epidemiological study. Mortality refers to:', '["Duration of illness","The total number of cases","The number of new cases","Recovery rate","Death due to a disease"]'::jsonb, 'Death due to a disease', 'Mortality specifically refers to death. Mortality rates are calculated as the number of deaths due to a disease divided by the total population at risk.'),
('0f965afd-798d-5389-b53b-36919231560a'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'A researcher is conducting an epidemiological study. Descriptive epidemiology is concerned with:', '["Evaluating interventions","Laboratory analysis","Person, place, and time","Randomised trials","Testing hypotheses"]'::jsonb, 'Person, place, and time', 'Descriptive epidemiology organizes data by person, place, and time to identify patterns and generate hypotheses about disease occurrence. It answers who, where, and when questions.'),
('b60a0790-b8c5-5257-aeda-33f4ff874cbe'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'Analytical epidemiology is concerned with:', '["Mapping disease distribution","Counting cases","Testing hypotheses about disease causation","Reporting outbreaks","Describing disease frequency"]'::jsonb, 'Testing hypotheses about disease causation', 'Analytical epidemiology tests hypotheses generated by descriptive studies. It uses study designs like cohort and case-control studies to identify associations and risk factors.'),
('aa90704f-3c7c-5d88-b65b-20ac71f0aae0'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'A researcher is conducting an epidemiological study. Incidence is defined as:', '["Duration of disease","Recovery from disease","Deaths due to disease","Total existing cases of disease","New cases of disease in a population over time"]'::jsonb, 'New cases of disease in a population over time', 'Incidence measures the number of NEW cases of a disease that occur in a population at risk over a specified time period. It is crucial for studying disease etiology.'),
('70b0b7ba-9c83-5c97-b731-ebdcaacaf985'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'Prevalence is defined as:', '["Total existing cases of disease in a population","New cases over time clinical presentation","Deaths due to disease clinical presentation","Risk of exposure clinical presentation","Rate of recovery clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Total existing cases of disease in a population', 'Prevalence measures all existing cases (both new and old) of a disease in a population at a given point in time. It is influenced by both incidence and duration of disease.'),
('2a1f48d1-bcf3-531a-a831-d8d70e924fe4'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'Which relationship links incidence, prevalence, and duration?', '["Prevalence = Incidence × Average duration","Incidence = Prevalence × Duration","Prevalence = Incidence / Duration","Duration = Prevalence × Incidence","Incidence = Prevalence / Duration"]'::jsonb, 'Prevalence = Incidence × Average duration', 'The relationship is: Prevalence = Incidence × Average Duration of Disease. This equation shows that prevalence depends on both the rate of new cases and how long the disease lasts.'),
('6668a841-48bb-508e-8d59-bdc7310653d0'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'A disease with short duration will have:', '["Equal prevalence and incidence","No relationship between prevalence and incidence","Zero prevalence","Low prevalence relative to its incidence","High prevalence relative to its incidence"]'::jsonb, 'Low prevalence relative to its incidence', 'If a disease has short duration (either rapid recovery or rapid death), prevalence will be low relative to incidence. Chronic diseases like diabetes have high prevalence relative to incidence.'),
('74612b1c-54c0-50d9-81c5-dc668eb7598b'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'Which type of epidemiology studies the distribution of disease?', '["Clinical epidemiology","Genetic epidemiology","Molecular epidemiology","Descriptive epidemiology","Analytical epidemiology"]'::jsonb, 'Descriptive epidemiology', 'Descriptive epidemiology examines disease distribution in terms of person, place, and time. It provides the basic data needed for hypothesis generation.'),
('96470018-d30f-5c63-aa06-8921b82c7ba2'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'Which study design is best for describing disease distribution?', '["Case-control study","Systematic review","Randomised controlled trial","Cross-sectional study","Cohort study"]'::jsonb, 'Cross-sectional study', 'Cross-sectional studies are commonly used in descriptive epidemiology to assess disease prevalence and distribution at a single point in time across different populations.'),
('fea30a7f-3406-5f8c-b32f-426c37a200e1'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'Epidemiology is essential for:', '["Identifying causes of disease","Interpreting imaging","Reading ECGs","Performing surgeries","Prescribing antibiotics"]'::jsonb, 'Identifying causes of disease', 'Epidemiology is the fundamental science of public health. It identifies causes and risk factors of disease, determines disease burden, and evaluates health interventions.'),
('c8b34e4c-4f4e-51b1-aa69-fc483adeb4bf'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'A researcher is conducting an epidemiological study. A new diagnostic test for diabetes mellitus is being evaluated against the gold standard in 500 patients. A new diagnostic test for tuberculosis is being evaluated against the gold standard in 500 patients. A new diagnostic test for tuberculosis is being evaluated against the gold standard in 500 patients. In a population with 10%, the positive predictive value is: Which measure describes the burden of disease in a population?', '["Incidence","Prevalence","Case fatality rate","Relative risk","Mortality rate"]'::jsonb, 'Prevalence', 'Prevalence indicates the proportion of a population affected by a disease at a specific time, reflecting the overall burden of disease in that population.'),
('bead709a-42ee-5817-99fe-9c2b5c854bff'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'A researcher is conducting an epidemiological study. Which term describes a disease that occurs infrequently and irregularly?', '["Pandemic","Epidemic","Endemic","Hyperendemic","Sporadic"]'::jsonb, 'Sporadic', 'Sporadic refers to diseases that occur infrequently and irregularly with no specific pattern. Examples include tetanus and rabies in most developed countries.'),
('13d63aa7-8b84-5fe0-85fe-5cbdbebe6734'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'What is herd immunity?', '["Immunity acquired by vaccination","Immunity passed from mother to child","Protection of a population when a high proportion is immune","Immunity from antibodies","Natural immunity after infection"]'::jsonb, 'Protection of a population when a high proportion is immune', 'Herd immunity occurs when a large percentage of a population becomes immune to a disease, reducing its spread. This protects vulnerable individuals who cannot be vaccinated.'),
('9dff5ed2-d9e8-5ed9-a06d-9bedaded0879'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'A researcher is conducting an epidemiological study. Which factor is NOT part of the host component in the epidemiological triad?', '["Immune status","Climate","Nutritional status","Age","Genetic susceptibility"]'::jsonb, 'Climate', 'Age, genetic susceptibility, nutritional status, and immune status are host factors. Climate is an environmental factor in the epidemiological triad.'),
('8ddfa7fd-c875-503d-957e-1a922c11183e'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'Which government agency tracks disease outbreaks in the US?', '["CDC","FDA","OSHA","NIH","WHO"]'::jsonb, 'CDC', 'The Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC) is the US agency responsible for tracking and responding to disease outbreaks. The WHO is the international health agency.'),
('8d7f3d32-9ff8-54e2-a428-93da74223879'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'Epidemiology contributes to clinical medicine by:', '["Conducting physical exams","Performing surgery","Dispensing medications","Reading pathology slides","Establishing treatment guidelines"]'::jsonb, 'Establishing treatment guidelines', 'Epidemiology provides evidence for clinical guidelines through clinical trials and systematic reviews. It helps determine which treatments are most effective for specific conditions.'),
('52206b84-c2de-53b0-8800-6d79fb3214a9'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'What is a vector in epidemiology?', '["A measure of disease frequency","An organism that transmits infectious agents","A type of study design","A statistical test","A treatment protocol"]'::jsonb, 'An organism that transmits infectious agents', 'A vector is an organism that transmits infectious agents from an infected host to a susceptible host. Mosquitoes are vectors for malaria and dengue fever.'),
('08c6fa57-10f9-5a62-add6-55ffd6eec18c'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'Which of the following is a communicable disease?', '["Asthma","Hypertension","Tuberculosis","Cancer","Diabetes"]'::jsonb, 'Tuberculosis', 'Tuberculosis is a communicable (infectious) disease caused by Mycobacterium tuberculosis, transmitted through respiratory droplets. The others are non-communicable diseases.'),
('04a6712b-8818-5bd6-8b36-d46aba753729'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'Which is a non-communicable disease?', '["COVID-19","HIV/AIDS","Type 2 diabetes","Malaria","Influenza"]'::jsonb, 'Type 2 diabetes', 'Type 2 diabetes is a non-communicable disease (NCD) resulting from insulin resistance. It is not transmitted between individuals, unlike the other options which are infectious.'),
('f5bc4924-cf34-56fd-bf24-63d24754a2db'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'What is the role of epidemiology in outbreak investigation?', '["Performing autopsies","Identifying the source and mode of transmission","Treating affected patients","Developing vaccines in a lab","Administering medications"]'::jsonb, 'Identifying the source and mode of transmission', 'Epidemiology is crucial in outbreak investigations to identify the source, mode of transmission, and risk factors, and to implement control measures. It provides the scientific basis for outbreak response.'),
('d19f98de-8ae3-5ed9-9790-07d2282b8429'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'The WHO definition of health includes:', '["Mental health only","Absence of symptoms only","Physical fitness only","Absence of disease only","Physical, mental, and social well-being"]'::jsonb, 'Physical, mental, and social well-being', 'The WHO defines health as a state of complete physical, mental, and social well-being, not merely the absence of disease or infirmity. This holistic definition was established in 1948.'),
('131c7ed6-c431-5d3d-8405-2e68ed8537fc'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'A researcher is conducting an epidemiological study. In a population with 1%, the negative predictive value is: Which of the following is a primary prevention strategy?', '["Screening for cancer","Treatment of hypertension","Palliative care","Rehabilitation after stroke","Vaccination"]'::jsonb, 'Vaccination', 'Primary prevention aims to prevent disease before it occurs. Vaccination is a classic example. Screening is secondary prevention, while treatment and rehabilitation are tertiary prevention.'),
('9e790324-5e89-5059-a4d8-9f8f1702de0a'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'Secondary prevention includes:', '["Immunisation","Rehabilitation","Health education","Screening for early detection","Palliative care"]'::jsonb, 'Screening for early detection', 'Secondary prevention focuses on early detection of disease through screening. Pap smears, mammograms, and blood pressure screening are examples of secondary prevention.'),
('0f52dc59-e3b6-50cc-8494-0c497ee16ac4'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'Tertiary prevention aims to:', '["Prevent disease onset","Identify risk factors","Promote healthy lifestyle","Reduce complications and rehabilitate","Detect disease early"]'::jsonb, 'Reduce complications and rehabilitate', 'Tertiary prevention aims to reduce the impact of established disease by preventing complications and improving quality of life through rehabilitation and management.'),
('aa8325bd-eb9a-546e-8edc-7b3fac2552f4'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'A researcher is conducting an epidemiological study. Which prevention level is a mammogram for breast cancer?', '["Primordial","Quaternary","Primary","Secondary","Tertiary"]'::jsonb, 'Secondary', 'Mammography screening for breast cancer is secondary prevention. It detects disease at an early stage when treatment is more effective, but does not prevent the initial occurrence.'),
('54c1b35a-0855-594d-abaf-85c8e7ce19c1'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'A researcher is conducting an epidemiological study. Primordial prevention targets:', '["Symptom management","Rehabilitation","Early disease detection","Underlying social and environmental conditions","Treatment of established disease"]'::jsonb, 'Underlying social and environmental conditions', 'Primordial prevention addresses the underlying social, economic, and environmental conditions that lead to disease risk factors. It targets root causes before risk factors develop.'),
('f5a8d1b0-af86-5e7c-bd3e-735c8c30acbd'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'Which describes the iceberg phenomenon in epidemiology?', '["The disease is cold-related","Subclinical cases are never important","Only a fraction of disease is clinically apparent","Disease spreads like melting ice","Most cases are hospitalised"]'::jsonb, 'Only a fraction of disease is clinically apparent', 'The iceberg phenomenon describes that only a small proportion of disease cases are clinically apparent (above water), while many cases remain subclinical or undiagnosed (below water).'),
('9a17dfbe-09c9-53bc-a591-2c116436e2c3'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'What does DALY stand for?', '["Disability-Adjusted Life Year","Diagnosis-Adjusted Life Year","Duration-Adjusted Life Year","Death-Adjusted Life Year","Disease"]'::jsonb, 'Disability-Adjusted Life Year', 'DALY is a measure of overall disease burden, expressed as the number of years lost due to ill-health, disability, or early death. It combines YLL (Years of Life Lost) and YLD (Years Lived with Disability).'),
('0530fc2a-3ef8-5520-9b8e-b88f0bcf1f9a'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'QALY stands for:', '["Quick Assessment of Life Years","Query of Life Years","Quality-Adjusted Life Year","Quantitative Analysis of Life Years","Quantity-Adjusted Life Year"]'::jsonb, 'Quality-Adjusted Life Year', 'QALY measures both the quantity and quality of life generated by healthcare interventions. It is used in health economics to assess cost-effectiveness.'),
('a366e8be-4a88-59f3-a481-26d57e39199b'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'Which of the following is NOT a pillar of public health?', '["Epidemiology","Health protection","Disease prevention","Surgery","Health promotion"]'::jsonb, 'Surgery', 'Epidemiology, health promotion, disease prevention, and health protection are pillars of public health. Surgery is a clinical intervention, not a public health function.'),
('f408ceb3-bef8-5631-b40d-e834b932906a'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'The natural history of disease includes:', '["Surgical interventions only","Pre-pathogenesis and pathogenesis periods","Only the treatment phase","Only the recovery phase","Only diagnostic procedures"]'::jsonb, 'Pre-pathogenesis and pathogenesis periods', 'The natural history of disease describes the progression from exposure to outcome without intervention. It includes pre-pathogenesis (before disease onset) and pathogenesis (after disease onset) periods.'),
('d24877be-ac1e-5e52-9fbb-bb7747d0168e'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'A researcher is conducting an epidemiological study. A new diagnostic test for diabetes mellitus is being evaluated against the gold standard in 2000 patients. A new diagnostic test for coronary artery disease is being evaluated against the gold standard in 500 patients. A new diagnostic test for coronary artery disease is being evaluated against the gold standard in 500 patients. In a population with 1%, the positive predictive value is: Incidence rate is calculated as:', '["Number of existing cases / Total population","Number of new cases / Person-time at risk","Number of new cases / Total population","Number of recovered cases / Total population","Number of deaths / Total population"]'::jsonb, 'Number of new cases / Person-time at risk', 'Incidence rate uses person-time in the denominator, accounting for different follow-up durations. It reflects the speed at which new cases occur in a population.'),
('23b9c2bb-ad37-556c-9746-6e8bbfe2a2aa'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'Cumulative incidence is also known as:', '["Risk","Prevalence","Mortality","Rate","Odds"]'::jsonb, 'Risk', 'Cumulative incidence represents the proportion of a population that develops disease over a specified period, which is essentially the risk of developing the disease.'),
('00d60dee-cc3e-580b-b7e5-bc6c7e91b921'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'Which measure is most useful for studying disease etiology?', '["Mortality rate","Hospitalisation rate","Incidence","Case fatality rate","Prevalence"]'::jsonb, 'Incidence', 'Incidence is most useful for studying disease etiology because it measures new cases and allows identification of risk factors. Prevalence is more influenced by disease duration.'),
('a20f2aec-adde-551f-9d1e-770ee882f902'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'A new diagnostic test for hypertension is being evaluated against the gold standard in 200 patients. A new diagnostic test for hypertension is being evaluated against the gold standard in 2000 patients. A new diagnostic test for hypertension is being evaluated against the gold standard in 2000 patients. A new diagnostic test for lung cancer is being evaluated against the gold standard in 1000 patients. Point prevalence is measured:', '["After treatment","During an outbreak","At a specific point in time","Over a lifetime","Over a year"]'::jsonb, 'At a specific point in time', 'Point prevalence measures the proportion of a population with a disease at a specific moment in time (e.g., on January 1, 2024). It provides a snapshot of disease burden.'),
('b8050e02-efe1-5201-b873-516a665e2dee'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'Period prevalence is measured:', '["Over a specified time interval","At a single moment","Per decade only","Per year only","Per month only"]'::jsonb, 'Over a specified time interval', 'Period prevalence measures the proportion of a population with a disease over a specified time period (e.g., one year). It includes both existing and new cases during that period.'),
('e616607a-b3fc-5a7a-aa4b-6845c4bfc7f2'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'Which of the following is true about prevalence?', '["Prevalence = Incidence - Duration","Prevalence = Incidence × Duration","Prevalence = Incidence + Duration","Prevalence = Duration / Incidence","Prevalence = Incidence / Duration"]'::jsonb, 'Prevalence = Incidence × Duration', 'The relationship is Prevalence = Incidence × Duration. A disease with high incidence but short duration may have low prevalence, while a disease with low incidence but long duration may have high prevalence.'),
('a5d938ed-b8ac-5fff-aae7-3125aa5de835'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'Crude mortality rate is defined as:', '["Total deaths / Total population × 1000","Deaths due to specific disease / Total population","Deaths in a specific age group / Population in that age group","Deaths / Total births","Maternal deaths / Total live births"]'::jsonb, 'Total deaths / Total population × 1000', 'Crude mortality rate is the total number of deaths per unit population, usually expressed per 1000 population. It does not account for age or other demographic factors.'),
('9926043c-8a45-5b86-9fa5-be9d579e7bf3'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'Case fatality rate (CFR) is:', '["Deaths / Person-time","Deaths due to a disease / Total cases of that disease","Deaths in hospital / Total admissions","New cases / Total population","Deaths / Total population"]'::jsonb, 'Deaths due to a disease / Total cases of that disease', 'CFR measures the severity of a disease by calculating the proportion of diagnosed cases that die from the disease. Rabies has a CFR of nearly 100%, while seasonal influenza has a CFR below 0.1%.'),
('a2b25bf5-642e-54a2-a7b2-90481f89b52e'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'A researcher is conducting an epidemiological study. Proportionate mortality rate refers to:', '["Deaths / Total population","Deaths in a specific age group / Total deaths","Deaths from all causes / Person-time","Maternal deaths / Live births","Deaths due to a specific cause / Total deaths"]'::jsonb, 'Deaths due to a specific cause / Total deaths', 'Proportionate mortality rate shows the relative contribution of a specific cause to total mortality. For example, the proportionate mortality from heart disease might be 25% of all deaths.'),
('b21580cd-ec1c-5db1-9f91-79bee534cea5'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'Maternal mortality rate is expressed per:', '["100,000 live births","10,000 population","1000 live births","1,000,000 population","1000 population"]'::jsonb, '100,000 live births', 'Maternal mortality rate is the number of maternal deaths per 100,000 live births. It is a key indicator of women''s health and healthcare quality.'),
('3965a1b5-95d9-55ad-a1a5-060a1aa04861'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'Infant mortality rate is defined as:', '["Deaths at birth per 1000 births","Fetal deaths per 1000 births","Deaths under 5 years per 1000 population","Deaths under 1 year per 1000 live births","Neonatal deaths per 1000 live births"]'::jsonb, 'Deaths under 1 year per 1000 live births', 'Infant mortality rate (IMR) is the number of deaths of infants under 1 year of age per 1000 live births. It is a key indicator of a country''s health status.'),
('b7f863fe-ad9c-5462-a6d7-bf251749d22e'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'Neonatal mortality refers to deaths within:', '["First month after birth","First 7 days of life","First year of life","First 28 days of life","First 24 hours of birth"]'::jsonb, 'First 28 days of life', 'Neonatal mortality refers to deaths occurring within the first 28 days of life. It accounts for a significant proportion of infant mortality worldwide.'),
('c7b97299-44d1-5838-962d-d43f0146cca1'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'A researcher is conducting an epidemiological study. Perinatal mortality includes:', '["Maternal deaths","Child deaths under 5 years","Neonatal deaths only","Infant deaths under 1 year","Stillbirths + Early neonatal deaths"]'::jsonb, 'Stillbirths + Early neonatal deaths', 'Perinatal mortality includes stillbirths (fetal deaths after 28 weeks gestation) and early neonatal deaths (deaths in the first 7 days of life).'),
('363f2f2c-a5df-52df-8438-03ee8dfad4e3'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'Attack rate is used primarily in:', '["Clinical trials","Chronic disease studies","Genetic studies","Outbreak investigations","Laboratory research"]'::jsonb, 'Outbreak investigations', 'Attack rate is a measure of disease occurrence used in outbreak investigations. It is the proportion of a population exposed to an agent that develops the disease during an outbreak.'),
('7bd5ded3-0f3e-5e5e-b99b-f3f16035a131'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'A new diagnostic test for diabetes mellitus is being evaluated against the gold standard in 100 patients. In a population with 1%, the likelihood ratio is: Secondary attack rate measures:', '["Cases in healthcare workers","Recurrence of disease","Spread from primary cases to contacts","Cases in the general population","Initial cases in an outbreak"]'::jsonb, 'Spread from primary cases to contacts', 'Secondary attack rate measures the spread of disease from primary cases (index cases) to their close contacts. It is useful for understanding transmission dynamics and infectiousness.'),
('ea2a0549-097d-58c2-8747-17a9e8dd9889'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'Person-time is a measure that accounts for:', '["Gender differences","Age differences","Socioeconomic status","Different lengths of follow-up","Geographic variation"]'::jsonb, 'Different lengths of follow-up', 'Person-time accounts for varying follow-up durations among study participants. If one person is followed for 2 years and another for 1 year, this contributes 3 person-years of observation.'),
('02ca82d2-c730-577d-947f-cc1d09ccba72'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'If 100 people are followed for 2 years each, the person-time contributed is:', '["300 person-years","150 person-years","200 person-years","100 person-years","50 person-years"]'::jsonb, '200 person-years', '100 people × 2 years each = 200 person-years. Person-time is calculated as the sum of the time each individual is at risk and under observation.'),
('eaae7a9e-7ee0-5d7c-bc02-843fbf88fa47'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'A new diagnostic test for lung cancer is being evaluated against the gold standard in 200 patients. Incidence density is another term for:', '["Prevalence","Cumulative incidence","Attack rate","Incidence rate","Mortality rate"]'::jsonb, 'Incidence rate', 'Incidence density is synonymous with incidence rate. It uses person-time as the denominator and reflects the rate at which new cases occur per unit of person-time.'),
('d551dc06-35ff-58cc-b97c-d37c6e8abee4'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'Which measure is expressed as a proportion rather than a rate?', '["Cumulative incidence","Mortality rate","Prevalence rate","Person-time rate","Incidence rate"]'::jsonb, 'Cumulative incidence', 'Cumulative incidence is a proportion (ranging from 0 to 1) because it represents the fraction of a population that develops disease. It does not have a time component in the denominator.'),
('2ccde26f-8ce5-5a91-883b-fcfc7dca44fa'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'A disease that is constantly present at low levels in a population is:', '["Pandemic","Epidemic","Hyperendemic","Sporadic","Endemic"]'::jsonb, 'Endemic', 'An endemic disease is constantly present in a population at expected levels. For example, the common cold is endemic in most populations worldwide.'),
('611a183e-d214-59dd-af02-2db4f2b01165'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'Which measure is most appropriate for assessing the burden of chronic diseases?', '["Prevalence","Attack rate","Case fatality rate","Secondary attack rate","Incidence"]'::jsonb, 'Prevalence', 'Prevalence is most appropriate for chronic diseases because it captures all existing cases and reflects the ongoing burden on the healthcare system. Chronic diseases have long duration.'),
('ea758a6e-2fc5-5c31-9add-7c3d526e7304'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'Which measure is most appropriate for acute diseases?', '["DALY","Prevalence","Incidence","QALY","Proportionate mortality"]'::jsonb, 'Incidence', 'Incidence is more appropriate for acute diseases as it captures new cases over a short period. Prevalence would be low due to short duration, making it less informative for acute conditions.'),
('c696aad0-ad66-5fc9-86e4-c103bb2a1a75'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'Standardised mortality ratio (SMR) compares:', '["Age-specific death rates","Infant mortality rates","Observed deaths to expected deaths","Cause-specific death rates","Crude death rates"]'::jsonb, 'Observed deaths to expected deaths', 'SMR is the ratio of observed deaths in a study population to expected deaths based on a standard population. It is used in occupational epidemiology and adjusted for confounding.'),
('2154c025-7779-5d82-b224-156d2913c14b'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'A researcher is conducting an epidemiological study. Age-specific mortality rate is:', '["Infant deaths / Live births","Deaths / Person-time","Deaths due to a specific cause / Total population","Deaths in a specific age group / Population in that age group","Total deaths / Total population"]'::jsonb, 'Deaths in a specific age group / Population in that age group', 'Age-specific mortality rate calculates mortality within a defined age group, allowing comparison across age strata without confounding by age.'),
('9742b143-97f9-56a6-853a-14f5ed0bb48b'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'Cause-specific mortality rate is:', '["Deaths in hospital / Total deaths","Deaths / Person-time","Deaths due to a specific cause / Total population","Total deaths / Total population","Deaths due to all causes / Total population"]'::jsonb, 'Deaths due to a specific cause / Total population', 'Cause-specific mortality rate measures deaths from a specific cause per unit population. For example, lung cancer mortality rate is deaths from lung cancer divided by the total population.'),
('68958840-81de-57f9-8b00-6b94d98bd9c8'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'A new diagnostic test for breast cancer is being evaluated against the gold standard in 1000 patients. A new diagnostic test for HIV/AIDS is being evaluated against the gold standard in 500 patients. A new diagnostic test for hypertension is being evaluated against the gold standard in 500 patients. Which of the following is true about incidence?', '["It measures total cases at a point in time","It is influenced by disease duration","It reflects disease burden","It is used for healthcare planning","It measures the rate of transition from healthy to diseased"]'::jsonb, 'It measures the rate of transition from healthy to diseased', 'Incidence measures the transition rate from a healthy state to a diseased state. It is the rate at which new events occur in a population at risk.'),
('bf7c973d-0b22-59ea-b44a-1939ee107a60'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'The denominator for incidence rate should include:', '["Only individuals at risk of developing the disease","The entire population","Only those who have recovered","Only hospitalised patients","Only those already diseased"]'::jsonb, 'Only individuals at risk of developing the disease', 'The denominator for incidence rate should exclude individuals who already have the disease or are immune, as they are no longer at risk of developing a new case.'),
('95d4c469-2944-5051-a891-9f80eea92e41'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'Person-years is an example of:', '["Odds","Person-time","Cumulative incidence","Risk","Prevalence"]'::jsonb, 'Person-time', 'Person-years is a specific unit of person-time, calculated as the sum of the individual follow-up times in years. It accounts for staggered entry and varying follow-up durations.'),
('03bc2476-a986-5558-8a6f-948f3815cc18'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'A researcher is conducting an epidemiological study. A new diagnostic test for coronary artery disease is being evaluated against the gold standard in 200 patients. If there are 50 new cases of diabetes among 2000 at-risk individuals over 5 years, the cumulative incidence is:', '["0.5%","5%","2.5%","10%","1%"]'::jsonb, '2.5%', 'Cumulative incidence = 50/2000 = 0.025 = 2.5% over 5 years. This represents the risk of developing diabetes over the 5-year period.'),
('369241ac-c4e3-571e-886a-c19f33287849'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'In the same population (50 new cases, 2000 at risk, 5 years, 9000 person-years), the incidence rate is:', '["10 per 1000 person-years","2.5 per 100 person-years","25 per 1000 person-years","5.56 per 1000 person-years","0.56 per 100 person-years"]'::jsonb, '5.56 per 1000 person-years', 'Incidence rate = 50/9000 = 0.00556 per person-year = 5.56 per 1000 person-years. This reflects the rate at which diabetes occurs per unit of person-time.'),
('7d301946-53af-5912-9097-4f2b2814d490'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'A researcher is conducting an epidemiological study. Attack rate is calculated as:', '["Number of new cases during outbreak / Population at risk at start","Number of secondary cases / Number of primary cases","Number of deaths / Number of cases","Number of total cases / Total population","Number of new cases / Person-time"]'::jsonb, 'Number of new cases during outbreak / Population at risk at start', 'Attack rate is a special form of cumulative incidence used in outbreak settings. It is calculated as the number of new cases divided by the population at risk at the beginning of the outbreak.'),
('8517da4a-8f64-541c-8aba-c46894e7fddf'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'Secondary attack rate helps determine:', '["Severity of a disease","Latency period","Infectiousness of a disease","Duration of a disease","Incubation period"]'::jsonb, 'Infectiousness of a disease', 'Secondary attack rate reflects the degree of infectiousness or transmissibility of a disease. A high secondary attack rate suggests the disease spreads easily among close contacts.'),
('be7a3cd6-6768-5c7f-afc4-74a379f72d6c'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'Which rate best reflects the health status of a population?', '["Crude mortality rate","Case fatality rate","Infant mortality rate","Secondary attack rate","Attack rate"]'::jsonb, 'Infant mortality rate', 'Infant mortality rate is widely considered one of the best indicators of overall population health. It reflects maternal health, healthcare quality, and socioeconomic conditions.'),
('03772055-dc4a-5d14-8a5a-f0e245152f51'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'Life expectancy is:', '["The maximum possible lifespan","The life span of the oldest individual","The average number of years a person is expected to live","The average age of the population","The median age at death"]'::jsonb, 'The average number of years a person is expected to live', 'Life expectancy is a statistical measure of the average time a person is expected to live based on current mortality rates. It is calculated from life tables.'),
('faf31138-1474-5581-a3bb-f7678befe4dc'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'Years of Potential Life Lost (YPLL) measures:', '["Disease incidence","Total mortality","Quality of life","Premature mortality","Disability burden"]'::jsonb, 'Premature mortality', 'YPLL measures premature mortality by calculating the number of years lost when a person dies before a reference age (usually 75 years). It gives more weight to deaths at younger ages.'),
('48851c75-c0d4-5aeb-9474-52e3432882bb'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'Which measure is used to calculate burden of disease?', '["Incidence","Prevalence","Attack rate","DALY","Case fatality rate"]'::jsonb, 'DALY', 'DALY (Disability-Adjusted Life Year) is a comprehensive measure of disease burden that combines years lost due to premature death and years lived with disability.'),
('82116977-656a-5dcb-8302-409805a00bb3'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'The numerator for cumulative incidence is:', '["Number of new cases during follow-up","Number of existing cases","Total population at baseline","Number of recovered cases","Number of deaths"]'::jsonb, 'Number of new cases during follow-up', 'Cumulative incidence uses the number of NEW cases that develop during the follow-up period as the numerator, divided by the disease-free population at baseline.'),
('f0cdf287-81ff-51e5-849e-621513ae78d8'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'A researcher is conducting an epidemiological study. In a stable population, if incidence increases, prevalence will:', '["Increase","Remain unchanged","Fluctuate randomly","Become zero","Decrease"]'::jsonb, 'Increase', 'In a stable population, if incidence (new cases) increases, prevalence will also increase, since Prevalence = Incidence × Duration. More new cases mean more existing cases accumulate.'),
('ade26aa5-2e8c-5b40-9505-ce5c987982b4'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'If treatment improves survival for a disease without reducing incidence, prevalence will:', '["Approach incidence","Remain unchanged","Become zero","Decrease","Increase"]'::jsonb, 'Increase', 'If treatment improves survival (increases duration), prevalence will increase even if incidence remains constant. This explains why chronic disease prevalence often rises with better treatment.'),
('26d784b9-ed88-5d17-8f14-93692b05e6a0'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'Person-time at risk stops when:', '["An individual is male","An individual develops the disease","An individual has regular check-ups","An individual is vaccinated","An individual is over 50"]'::jsonb, 'An individual develops the disease', 'Person-time at risk stops for an individual when they develop the disease, die, are lost to follow-up, or the study ends. Once the outcome occurs, the person is no longer at risk.'),
('d8c397e7-d9aa-5d16-9064-6b7aa42f5352'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'Which study design provides the strongest evidence for causation?', '["Cross-sectional study","Case series","Randomised controlled trial","Ecological study","Case-control study"]'::jsonb, 'Randomised controlled trial', 'RCTs are the gold standard for establishing causation because randomisation minimises confounding and selection bias. They provide the highest level of evidence for intervention effectiveness.'),
('bab3e0b5-c378-55a8-8a0c-763b044fb017'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'In a cohort study, participants are classified based on:', '["Genetic profile","Treatment received","Exposure status","Disease status","Socioeconomic status"]'::jsonb, 'Exposure status', 'In a cohort study, participants are classified by their exposure status (exposed vs. non-exposed) and followed forward in time to compare disease incidence between groups.'),
('39b5de46-2909-579f-a501-67d72730d48a'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'In a case-control study, participants are selected based on:', '["Disease status","Location","Gender","Exposure status","Age"]'::jsonb, 'Disease status', 'In case-control studies, participants are selected based on disease status (cases = those with disease, controls = those without disease) and exposure history is compared retrospectively.'),
('8ed5fb3d-90c7-512f-ba9f-c1949dd843bc'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'Which study is best for studying rare diseases?', '["Ecological study","Case-control study","RCT","Cross-sectional study","Cohort study"]'::jsonb, 'Case-control study', 'Case-control studies are efficient for rare diseases because they start with cases and look back at exposures. Cohort studies would require extremely large sample sizes for rare outcomes.'),
('c8a0ef31-d146-554b-b540-7db8584ccc98'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'Which study is best for studying rare exposures?', '["Cohort study","Ecological study","Case-control study","Case series","Cross-sectional study"]'::jsonb, 'Cohort study', 'Cohort studies are suitable for rare exposures because you can deliberately select an exposed group and compare them to an unexposed group.'),
('9176fbaa-2472-56f6-acc4-600352f2a999'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'The Framingham Heart Study is an example of:', '["Case-control study","Cohort study","Ecological study","Cross-sectional study","RCT"]'::jsonb, 'Cohort study', 'The Framingham Heart Study is a landmark prospective cohort study that began in 1948. It identified major cardiovascular risk factors such as smoking, hypertension, and high cholesterol.'),
('c7353dd0-1e32-5d1b-b292-2ec05adc1999'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'A new diagnostic test for coronary artery disease is being evaluated against the gold standard in 1000 patients. Which bias is most problematic in case-control studies?', '["Lead-time bias","Confounding","Recall bias","Information bias","Selection bias"]'::jsonb, 'Recall bias', 'Recall bias is a major concern in case-control studies because cases may remember exposures differently (more thoroughly) than controls, leading to differential misclassification.'),
('e1f3eb0b-31e2-5d8f-abcd-d249e9a07865'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'A researcher is conducting an epidemiological study. Loss to follow-up is a major concern in:', '["Cross-sectional studies","Case-control studies","Cohort studies","Ecological studies","Case reports"]'::jsonb, 'Cohort studies', 'Loss to follow-up creates attrition bias in cohort studies. If dropouts differ from those who remain, the results may be biased. This is why minimising loss to follow-up is critical.'),
('2c9ce3d1-c52d-5ab7-b956-60e192c2ef3b'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'Cross-sectional studies assess:', '["Intervention effects","Disease progression","Exposure before outcome","Exposure and outcome simultaneously","Outcome before exposure"]'::jsonb, 'Exposure and outcome simultaneously', 'Cross-sectional studies measure both exposure and outcome at the same point in time. This limits the ability to establish temporal sequence but is useful for estimating prevalence.'),
('e27086cb-095d-5c59-8444-1ceb3166b6d0'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'A researcher is conducting an epidemiological study. Researchers design a study to investigate the association between obesity and disease progression. Researchers design a study to investigate the association between smoking and mortality. Researchers design a study to investigate the association between air pollution and disease progression. Researchers design a study to investigate the association between physical inactivity and disease progression. The main limitation of cross-sectional studies is:', '["Ethical concerns clinical presentation","Loss to follow-up clinical presentation","Recall bias clinical presentation","High cost clinical presentation","Inability to establish temporal sequence"]'::jsonb, 'Inability to establish temporal sequence', 'Since exposure and outcome are measured simultaneously, cross-sectional studies cannot determine whether the exposure preceded the outcome, which is essential for establishing causation.'),
('977f9036-d246-509f-91ca-de281ebbfc19'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'Ecological studies use:', '["Individual-level data","Tissue samples","Group-level data","Blood tests","Genetic data"]'::jsonb, 'Group-level data', 'Ecological studies use aggregate data from groups (e.g., countries, regions) rather than individual-level data. This makes them prone to the ecological fallacy.'),
('14289663-4894-5123-8816-dd67dd11f81b'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'Researchers design a study to investigate the association between occupational dust exposure and mortality. Researchers design a study to investigate the association between smoking and mortality. Researchers design a study to investigate the association between obesity and post-operative complications. The ecological fallacy refers to:', '["Measuring exposure incorrectly","Assuming group-level associations apply to individuals","Assuming individuals are all the same","Sampling bias","Overlooking individual variation"]'::jsonb, 'Assuming group-level associations apply to individuals', 'The ecological fallacy occurs when conclusions about individuals are drawn from group-level data. Associations seen at the group level may not exist at the individual level.'),
('ba2bb220-a232-504b-8493-775d1a57c44c'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'A new diagnostic test for coronary artery disease is being evaluated against the gold standard in 100 patients. A new diagnostic test for tuberculosis is being evaluated against the gold standard in 2000 patients. A new diagnostic test for tuberculosis is being evaluated against the gold standard in 2000 patients. Which design is most prone to selection bias?', '["RCT","Ecological study","Cohort study","Cross-sectional study","Case-control study"]'::jsonb, 'Case-control study', 'Case-control studies are particularly prone to selection bias because cases and controls must be selected from the same study base. Control selection is often difficult.'),
('1807e743-d8b4-58bd-bad3-c6278746d5aa'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'In a population with 20%, the likelihood ratio is: Blinding in RCTs is used to prevent:', '["Selection bias","Confounding","Lead-time bias","Ecological fallacy","Performance and detection bias"]'::jsonb, 'Performance and detection bias', 'Blinding prevents performance bias (participants/staff altering behaviour) and detection bias (outcome assessment being influenced by knowledge of treatment allocation).'),
('65f434a0-3180-59fc-b515-d0d1218d819d'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'A new diagnostic test for coronary artery disease is being evaluated against the gold standard in 1000 patients. A randomized controlled trial is conducted to evaluate a screening program for HIV/AIDS. In a double-blind RCT:', '["Only investigators are blinded clinical presentation","Only participants are blinded clinical presentation","Everyone knows the allocation clinical presentation","Neither participants nor investigators know the allocation","Only data analysts are blinded clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Neither participants nor investigators know the allocation', 'Double-blind means both the participants and the investigators (those delivering the intervention and assessing outcomes) are unaware of the treatment allocation.'),
('e22a42de-71a5-50f2-a5e6-1b52d7f56708'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'A new diagnostic test for hypertension is being evaluated against the gold standard in 200 patients. Placebo effect is controlled for by:', '["Blinding","Block randomisation","Randomisation","Using a placebo control group","Stratification"]'::jsonb, 'Using a placebo control group', 'A placebo control group allows researchers to isolate the true effect of the intervention by accounting for the placebo effect. Participants in both groups experience the same level of attention.'),
('5b143b6c-43f7-5502-9cb7-0776cddb6919'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'Which study design is most suitable for assessing diagnostic test accuracy?', '["RCT","Cohort study","Case report","Case-control study","Cross-sectional study"]'::jsonb, 'Cross-sectional study', 'Cross-sectional studies are ideal for assessing diagnostic test accuracy by comparing the test result against a gold standard in a representative sample of patients.'),
('250a69ab-0204-5f9a-950f-fee36be0a87b'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'A new diagnostic test for breast cancer is being evaluated against the gold standard in 2000 patients. Nested case-control study is conducted within:', '["An RCT","A cross-sectional study","An existing cohort study","An ecological study","A case series"]'::jsonb, 'An existing cohort study', 'A nested case-control study uses cases and controls derived from a defined cohort. This design combines the efficiency of case-control studies with the temporal clarity of cohort studies.'),
('fa230d8e-ff93-59d7-a3ec-b8249c707ae7'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'Which study is a case report?', '["Review of literature","Detailed report of a single patient","Survey of multiple patients","Study of a population","Comparison of groups"]'::jsonb, 'Detailed report of a single patient', 'A case report is a detailed description of a single patient''s clinical presentation, diagnosis, treatment, and outcome. It is useful for identifying novel or rare conditions.'),
('6b1e940d-103e-5584-8f24-e1dff5a110bb'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'In a population with 5%, the positive predictive value is: Case series describes:', '["A systematic review","A population survey","A controlled experiment","A single patient","A collection of individual case reports"]'::jsonb, 'A collection of individual case reports', 'A case series is a report of multiple patients with a similar condition or exposure. It can generate hypotheses but lacks a comparison group for causal inference.'),
('ddc0774b-9db8-5918-8123-666d9aaea683'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'What is the purpose of randomisation in RCTs?', '["To simplify recruitment","To increase sample size","To balance known and unknown confounders","To improve generalisability","To reduce cost"]'::jsonb, 'To balance known and unknown confounders', 'Randomisation balances both known and unknown confounding factors between treatment groups, ensuring that any difference in outcome can be attributed to the intervention.'),
('64ddd749-8725-58f9-8031-495395f6fe51'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'A randomized controlled trial is conducted to evaluate minimally invasive surgery for hypertension. Stratified randomisation ensures:', '["Balance of key prognostic factors across groups","Equal number of participants","Allocation by age only","Complete randomness","Allocation by gender only"]'::jsonb, 'Balance of key prognostic factors across groups', 'Stratified randomisation ensures that important prognostic factors (e.g., age, sex, disease severity) are balanced between treatment groups within specified strata.'),
('a5400420-54fe-57f0-9de6-20a4286484cc'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'Block randomisation ensures:', '["Equal group sizes throughout the trial","Complete blinding","Balance of confounders","Unbiased outcome assessment","General representation"]'::jsonb, 'Equal group sizes throughout the trial', 'Block randomisation maintains approximately equal numbers of participants in each treatment group at regular intervals (blocks), ensuring balanced allocation throughout the trial.'),
('ff97d3c4-5a79-562b-9302-12e96d582a73'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'A researcher is conducting an epidemiological study. Intention-to-treat analysis includes:', '["Only those who completed the study","All randomised participants regardless of protocol adherence","Only those who received the intervention","Only those who followed the protocol perfectly","Only those without adverse events"]'::jsonb, 'All randomised participants regardless of protocol adherence', 'Intention-to-treat (ITT) analysis includes all randomised participants in their assigned groups, regardless of whether they received the treatment or completed the study. It preserves the benefit of randomisation.'),
('0be29919-9346-54ae-9afa-1f09943c683f'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'A researcher is conducting an epidemiological study. Per-protocol analysis includes:', '["All randomised participants","Only participants who adhered to the protocol","Only the control group","All participants regardless of adherence","Only the treatment group"]'::jsonb, 'Only participants who adhered to the protocol', 'Per-protocol analysis includes only those who complied with the study protocol. While it can show the true effect of receiving treatment, it may introduce bias by excluding non-compliant participants.'),
('a6b60336-2b19-5e10-926f-9b8f5daab97a'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'A researcher is conducting an epidemiological study. Which is a disadvantage of cohort studies?', '["Recall bias","Selected controls may be biased","Cannot study rare exposures","Cannot assess multiple outcomes","Expensive and time-consuming"]'::jsonb, 'Expensive and time-consuming', 'Cohort studies are expensive and time-consuming, especially prospective ones. They require following large populations for extended periods, which is resource-intensive.'),
('1511690b-476d-5096-b8c0-401ac14807b0'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'Which is a disadvantage of case-control studies?', '["Loss to follow-up","Cannot study rare diseases","Recall bias","Poor for hypothesis generation","Expensive and time-consuming"]'::jsonb, 'Recall bias', 'Recall bias is a major weakness of case-control studies. Cases may recall exposures differently from controls, leading to differential misclassification and biased results.'),
('1facccde-54e3-5424-a2b2-e7632355d6db'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'Retrospective cohort studies differ from prospective cohort studies in that:', '["The study is cheaper and faster","Both A and C","Data on exposure and outcome are collected from past records","Participants are followed into the future","Only B"]'::jsonb, 'Both A and C', 'Retrospective cohort studies use existing data to study past exposures and outcomes, making them cheaper and faster than prospective studies. However, they rely on the availability and quality of records.'),
('6f890798-87f8-5c1b-bf9f-bb05cad03aaa'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'Which study design is best for establishing incidence?', '["Ecological study","Case report","Cross-sectional study","Case-control study","Cohort study"]'::jsonb, 'Cohort study', 'Cohort studies follow participants over time and can directly measure the incidence of disease. They provide the temporal sequence needed to establish causation.'),
('39341da4-a65d-5257-ace7-f0bf8cc193b5'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'A new diagnostic test for lung cancer is being evaluated against the gold standard in 1000 patients. Crossover RCT design involves:', '["Participants switching groups midway","Two separate groups receiving different treatments","Multiple treatments in different populations","Each participant receiving both treatments sequentially","One group receiving treatment and one receiving placebo"]'::jsonb, 'Each participant receiving both treatments sequentially', 'In a crossover RCT, each participant serves as their own control by receiving both the intervention and control in random order with a washout period between phases.'),
('5891e88d-f15c-5240-a5e0-76f9403930b7'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'A washout period in crossover trials:', '["Sterilises equipment","Washes blood samples","Removes participants from the study","Cleans medical instruments","Eliminates carryover effects between treatment periods"]'::jsonb, 'Eliminates carryover effects between treatment periods', 'The washout period is a gap between treatment periods in crossover trials to allow the effect of the first treatment to wear off before the second treatment begins, preventing carryover effects.'),
('c01156df-e8b0-5afc-b8d6-8eb4b17746a7'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'A researcher is conducting an epidemiological study. A new diagnostic test for hypertension is being evaluated against the gold standard in 2000 patients. A new diagnostic test for tuberculosis is being evaluated against the gold standard in 200 patients. A new diagnostic test for tuberculosis is being evaluated against the gold standard in 200 patients. A randomized controlled trial is conducted to evaluate minimally invasive surgery for breast cancer. Which study is most appropriate for studying multiple outcomes of a single exposure?', '["Case series","Case-control study","Cross-sectional study","Cohort study","Ecological study"]'::jsonb, 'Cohort study', 'Cohort studies can assess multiple outcomes from a single exposure. For example, a cohort of smokers can be followed to study lung cancer, heart disease, stroke, and COPD simultaneously.'),
('bd9acfde-e1d0-50e1-93ad-61fa464cf6aa'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'A researcher is conducting an epidemiological study. Which study is most appropriate for studying multiple exposures for a single disease?', '["RCT","Cohort study","Ecological study","Cross-sectional study","Case-control study"]'::jsonb, 'Case-control study', 'Case-control studies efficiently assess multiple risk factors for a single disease by comparing exposure histories between cases and controls.'),
('2dc6942a-fc93-5de9-a0f5-488919344fd5'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'Selection of controls in case-control studies should be:', '["From a different city clinical presentation","From hospital inpatients clinical presentation","From the same population that gave rise to the cases","From friends of cases clinical presentation","From the general population clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'From the same population that gave rise to the cases', 'Controls should be selected from the same study base (population) as the cases. This ensures comparability and reduces selection bias. Hospital-based controls may introduce bias.'),
('b410a96b-74c8-5629-beb6-ba872f147498'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'Which bias is specific to hospital-based case-control studies?', '["Detection bias","Length-biased sampling","Lead-time bias","Recall bias","Berkson''s bias"]'::jsonb, 'Berkson''s bias', 'Berkson''s bias (hospital admission rate bias) occurs when hospitalisation rates differ between exposure groups, leading to a spurious association. It is specific to hospital-based case-control studies.'),
('7530106d-52f9-55ed-88c7-0751ebb43017'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'A new diagnostic test for diabetes mellitus is being evaluated against the gold standard in 200 patients. Propensity score matching is used in:', '["RCTs","Observational studies","Cluster trials","Crossover trials","Factorial designs"]'::jsonb, 'Observational studies', 'Propensity score matching is a statistical technique used in observational studies to reduce confounding by matching treated and untreated subjects based on their predicted probability of receiving treatment.'),
('1748f2e1-f5b7-5b7d-b347-9a48d7970185'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'A new diagnostic test for tuberculosis is being evaluated against the gold standard in 200 patients. A new diagnostic test for hypertension is being evaluated against the gold standard in 500 patients. A new diagnostic test for coronary artery disease is being evaluated against the gold standard in 100 patients. In a population with 1%, the positive predictive value is: Which study is an example of an observational analytical study?', '["Case report","Expert opinion","RCT","Cohort study","Case series"]'::jsonb, 'Cohort study', 'Cohort studies are observational analytical studies where the investigator observes without intervening. They analyse associations between exposures and outcomes.'),
('f588fba0-d00a-5bf2-bbda-a7ea3f8d83a2'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'A researcher is conducting an epidemiological study. Cluster RCT randomises:', '["Groups of individuals rather than individuals","Families only clinical presentation","Geographic regions only clinical presentation","Hospitals only clinical presentation","Individual participants clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Groups of individuals rather than individuals', 'Cluster RCTs randomise groups (clusters) such as schools, villages, or clinics rather than individuals. They are used when individual randomisation is impractical or contamination is likely.'),
('6e1f23d0-abd0-525a-b63a-aaf8435fc44e'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'Factorial RCT design allows:', '["Crossover of participants","Sequential treatment","Testing one intervention only","Testing two or more interventions simultaneously","Matching of participants"]'::jsonb, 'Testing two or more interventions simultaneously', 'Factorial designs test multiple interventions simultaneously by assigning participants to combinations of treatments. This is efficient and can detect interactions between interventions.'),
('8bcbb9c1-dd9b-54de-8791-7cec446218a8'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'A researcher is conducting an epidemiological study. A new diagnostic test for diabetes mellitus is being evaluated against the gold standard in 1000 patients. A new diagnostic test for coronary artery disease is being evaluated against the gold standard in 200 patients. Pragmatic RCTs aim to:', '["Minimise sample size","Use strict inclusion criteria","Evaluate efficacy under ideal conditions","Maximise internal validity","Evaluate effectiveness in real-world settings"]'::jsonb, 'Evaluate effectiveness in real-world settings', 'Pragmatic RCTs test interventions under real-world conditions to assess effectiveness. They have broader inclusion criteria and flexible protocols, enhancing generalisability.'),
('a9fe0bdb-1586-5a6c-83f4-e3c552e0c555'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'Explanatory RCTs aim to:', '["Use broad inclusion criteria","Evaluate efficacy under ideal conditions","Allow flexible protocols","Evaluate effectiveness in real-world settings","Maximise generalisability"]'::jsonb, 'Evaluate efficacy under ideal conditions', 'Explanatory RCTs test interventions under ideal, tightly controlled conditions to assess efficacy. They have strict inclusion criteria and protocols but may have limited generalisability.'),
('185d0719-6c4c-51b4-b661-cc9b3caf39cd'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'A researcher is conducting an epidemiological study. A new diagnostic test for lung cancer is being evaluated against the gold standard in 100 patients. A new diagnostic test for breast cancer is being evaluated against the gold standard in 200 patients. A new diagnostic test for breast cancer is being evaluated against the gold standard in 200 patients. Equivalence trials aim to demonstrate:', '["No difference exists","Two treatments are similarly effective","A dose-response relationship","One treatment is inferior","One treatment is superior"]'::jsonb, 'Two treatments are similarly effective', 'Equivalence trials test whether a new treatment is no worse or better than an existing treatment within a pre-specified margin. They are common in non-inferiority studies.'),
('edc79c75-0c0e-5482-a587-9d8dfca3decd'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'Non-inferiority trials test whether:', '["A new treatment is worse than standard","A new treatment is better than placebo","A new treatment is not worse than standard by a margin","Two treatments are identical","A treatment has no effect"]'::jsonb, 'A new treatment is not worse than standard by a margin', 'Non-inferiority trials aim to show that a new treatment is not unacceptably worse than the standard treatment. They are used when the new treatment has advantages (e.g., fewer side effects, lower cost).'),
('81267dec-f4cb-5b9c-9547-65337959fb52'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'Superiority trials test whether:', '["Two treatments are equivalent","A treatment is not worse","A treatment has no effect","One treatment is better than another","Treatments are different"]'::jsonb, 'One treatment is better than another', 'Superiority trials aim to demonstrate that one intervention is statistically significantly better than another. This is the most common type of clinical trial.'),
('9737117e-ac3c-5d7e-801a-a1554127f346'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'A researcher is conducting an epidemiological study. Historical controls are:', '["Controls from a previous study","Sibling controls","Population controls","Matched healthy individuals","Concurrent randomised controls"]'::jsonb, 'Controls from a previous study', 'Historical controls use data from previously treated patients in an earlier study. This approach is highly susceptible to bias due to changes in diagnosis, treatment, and patient populations over time.'),
('3e937c88-1378-5709-a658-ac4f72debca8'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'A study follows 1000 patients with lung cancer over 5 years to assess survival outcomes. Which study is most susceptible to the Hawthorne effect?', '["Blinded RCTs","Double-blind trials","Systematic reviews","Meta-analyses","Observational studies"]'::jsonb, 'Observational studies', 'The Hawthorne effect occurs when participants change their behaviour because they know they are being observed. This is a particular concern in observational studies and unblinded trials.'),
('c0f97973-e359-59c1-bdcd-c5c86b1c6ce0'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'Run-in period in RCTs is used to:', '["Analyse the results clinical presentation","Administer the intervention clinical presentation","Identify non-compliant participants before randomisation","Follow up after treatment clinical presentation","Collect baseline data clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Identify non-compliant participants before randomisation', 'A run-in period (pre-randomisation) identifies and excludes non-compliant participants or placebo responders before randomisation. This increases the efficiency and power of the trial.'),
('5b19bb12-a368-5637-8850-0eb7b9350f04'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'A new diagnostic test for coronary artery disease is being evaluated against the gold standard in 500 patients. A new diagnostic test for hypertension is being evaluated against the gold standard in 1000 patients. A new diagnostic test for hypertension is being evaluated against the gold standard in 1000 patients. A randomized controlled trial is conducted to evaluate a lifestyle modification program for diabetes mellitus. Which trial design reduces the number of participants needed?', '["Crossover design","Factorial design","Cluster design","Stepped-wedge design","Parallel design"]'::jsonb, 'Crossover design', 'Crossover designs require fewer participants because each participant serves as their own control, eliminating between-subject variability. This increases statistical power.'),
('cdc1b658-95b7-5a75-b63f-a1c85bdb0d65'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'A researcher is conducting an epidemiological study. Stepped-wedge design involves:', '["All clusters receiving intervention simultaneously","Sequential rollout of intervention to all clusters","Crossover of treatments","Randomisation of individuals","Factorial allocation"]'::jsonb, 'Sequential rollout of intervention to all clusters', 'In stepped-wedge cluster RCTs, all clusters eventually receive the intervention, but the timing of rollout is randomised. This is useful when it would be unethical to withhold the intervention.'),
('c28914ed-53bf-5f17-873d-2332f4852610'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'Adaptive trial designs allow:', '["Only sample size adjustment clinical presentation","Only dose changes clinical presentation","Modifications to the trial based on interim results","Only endpoint changes clinical presentation","No changes after starting clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Modifications to the trial based on interim results', 'Adaptive designs allow pre-planned modifications to trial procedures based on accumulating data. They can increase efficiency but require careful statistical planning.'),
('03e8a36f-ac94-5a88-920b-27c0589480cf'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'Interim analysis in RCTs is performed:', '["Only in crossover trials","During the planning phase","Before the trial is complete","Only in cluster trials","At the end of the trial"]'::jsonb, 'Before the trial is complete', 'Interim analyses are conducted at pre-planned time points during the trial. They may assess efficacy (early stopping if effective), futility (early stopping if ineffective), or safety.'),
('8d6d678b-64de-5f7a-8944-07e92274afd8'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'Stopping rules in RCTs are based on:', '["Patient preference","Pre-specified statistical boundaries","Sponsor decision","Regulatory requirements","Investigator judgment"]'::jsonb, 'Pre-specified statistical boundaries', 'Stopping rules use pre-defined statistical boundaries (e.g., O''Brien-Fleming, Haybittle-Peto) to determine when a trial should stop early for efficacy, futility, or harm, maintaining statistical integrity.'),
('6b4e9624-852e-5631-b272-70fc4b826119'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'A new diagnostic test for lung cancer is being evaluated against the gold standard in 1000 patients. A new diagnostic test for tuberculosis is being evaluated against the gold standard in 200 patients. A randomized controlled trial is conducted to evaluate minimally invasive surgery for breast cancer. Data Safety Monitoring Board (DSMB):', '["Monitors trial safety data","Analyses final data","Recruits participants","Publishes results","Conducts the trial"]'::jsonb, 'Monitors trial safety data', 'DSMB is an independent committee that reviews accumulating safety and efficacy data during a trial. They have the authority to recommend early termination if safety concerns arise.'),
('3c653446-d71e-5d6c-b474-567166c01cb7'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'A new diagnostic test for coronary artery disease is being evaluated against the gold standard in 500 patients. A new diagnostic test for breast cancer is being evaluated against the gold standard in 200 patients. A randomized controlled trial is conducted to evaluate minimally invasive surgery for coronary artery disease. Registering clinical trials before initiation:', '["Is optional","Is not recommended","Is mandatory only for surgical trials","Delays the research process","Increases transparency and reduces publication bias"]'::jsonb, 'Increases transparency and reduces publication bias', 'Clinical trial registration in public registries (e.g., ClinicalTrials.gov) reduces publication bias by making all trials visible, even if results are never published. It also prevents duplication.'),
('a30d193f-aa00-594b-9976-3523fbe36af4'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'CONSORT statement provides guidelines for:', '["Reporting RCTs","Data collection","Performing meta-analyses","Ethical approval","Conducting observational studies"]'::jsonb, 'Reporting RCTs', 'CONSORT (Consolidated Standards of Reporting Trials) provides evidence-based recommendations for reporting RCTs. It includes a flow diagram and checklist to improve transparency.'),
('34a0ab7d-ae3f-540c-af2f-515856005c9c'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'STROBE statement provides guidelines for:', '["Animal studies","Reporting observational studies","Systematic reviews","Diagnostic accuracy studies","Reporting RCTs"]'::jsonb, 'Reporting observational studies', 'STROBE (Strengthening the Reporting of Observational Studies in Epidemiology) provides guidelines for reporting cohort, case-control, and cross-sectional studies.'),
('5e7f97c3-274b-51da-bd69-b0f32758dde7'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'PRISMA statement is for:', '["Reporting systematic reviews and meta-analyses","Reporting case reports","Reporting RCTs","Reporting qualitative studies","Reporting ecological studies"]'::jsonb, 'Reporting systematic reviews and meta-analyses', 'PRISMA (Preferred Reporting Items for Systematic Reviews and Meta-Analyses) provides an evidence-based minimum set of items for reporting systematic reviews and meta-analyses.'),
('20707446-b6d1-5722-b5f1-cdd0c36ada95'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'A new diagnostic test for diabetes mellitus is being evaluated against the gold standard in 2000 patients. A new diagnostic test for breast cancer is being evaluated against the gold standard in 100 patients. A randomized controlled trial is conducted to evaluate a new drug for diabetes mellitus. N-of-1 trials are:', '["Population-based trials","Cluster trials","Single-patient crossover trials","Trials with one participant per group","Multicentre trials"]'::jsonb, 'Single-patient crossover trials', 'N-of-1 trials involve a single patient receiving both the intervention and control in random order. They are useful for individualised treatment decisions, especially in chronic conditions.'),
('ea4b9495-a776-530c-8d0a-a65e1ed3332f'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'Multicentre trials are advantageous because:', '["They are cheaper","They have simpler protocols","They avoid ethical review","They require less coordination","They increase generalisability and recruitment speed"]'::jsonb, 'They increase generalisability and recruitment speed', 'Multicentre trials recruit participants from multiple sites, increasing sample size, generalisability, and recruitment speed. However, they require standardised protocols and coordination.'),
('d498e717-2b5a-523c-b72b-8cbf14ad661b'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'Zelen''s design involves:', '["Randomisation before consent","No randomisation","Randomisation after consent","Consent after treatment completion","Consent waived entirely"]'::jsonb, 'Randomisation before consent', 'In Zelen''s design, participants are randomised before providing consent. Only those in the intervention group are asked for consent. This design is controversial and used in specific circumstances.'),
('c9aa9734-7335-53a1-9ae5-81cce067b943'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'A new diagnostic test for coronary artery disease is being evaluated against the gold standard in 1000 patients. A new diagnostic test for diabetes mellitus is being evaluated against the gold standard in 200 patients. A new diagnostic test for diabetes mellitus is being evaluated against the gold standard in 200 patients. Relative risk (RR) is calculated as:', '["Risk difference / Incidence in exposed","Odds in controls / Odds in cases","Incidence in unexposed / Incidence in exposed","Odds in cases / Odds in controls","Incidence in exposed / Incidence in unexposed"]'::jsonb, 'Incidence in exposed / Incidence in unexposed', 'Relative risk (risk ratio) is the ratio of disease incidence in the exposed group to that in the unexposed group. It quantifies the strength of association between exposure and disease.'),
('ba0b35cb-34d4-55d5-a4e8-f8278af6f74c'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'An RR of 1.0 indicates:', '["No association between exposure and disease","Decreased risk among the exposed","Increased risk among the exposed","Strong association","Harmful effect"]'::jsonb, 'No association between exposure and disease', 'An RR of 1.0 means the risk of disease is equal in exposed and unexposed groups, indicating no association between the exposure and the disease outcome.'),
('53225db6-a605-551a-ab76-6c6e57ac907f'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'A researcher is conducting an epidemiological study. An RR of 2.5 would be interpreted as:', '["Disease is 2.5 times more common in unexposed","2.5 excess cases per 100","Exposed group has 2.5 times the risk of disease","Exposed group has 2.5% risk","Risk is reduced by 2.5"]'::jsonb, 'Exposed group has 2.5 times the risk of disease', 'An RR of 2.5 means the exposed group has 2.5 times the risk of developing the disease compared to the unexposed group. This suggests a positive association.'),
('e4ac0074-658a-5bbe-835c-77d44e829c82'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'A new diagnostic test for HIV/AIDS is being evaluated against the gold standard in 1000 patients. A new diagnostic test for breast cancer is being evaluated against the gold standard in 100 patients. A new diagnostic test for breast cancer is being evaluated against the gold standard in 100 patients. Odds ratio (OR) is the measure of association in:', '["RCTs","Cross-sectional studies","Ecological studies","Case-control studies","Cohort studies"]'::jsonb, 'Case-control studies', 'Odds ratio is the primary measure of association in case-control studies because incidence rates cannot be calculated directly. OR approximates RR when the disease is rare.'),
('9354af07-770f-5240-b3f7-11d60f94b684'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'OR is calculated as:', '["(a/b) /","(a/c) / (b/d) = ad/bc","(a+d)/","(a+c)/","(a+b)/"]'::jsonb, '(a/c) / (b/d) = ad/bc', 'In a 2×2 table, OR = (a×d)/(b×c) where a = exposed cases, b = exposed non-cases, c = unexposed cases, d = unexposed non-cases. It represents the odds of exposure in cases divided by odds of exposure in controls.'),
('bfa7b822-20a0-5337-b023-127d228f6aef'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'The rare disease assumption states that:', '["OR always equals RR","RR approximates OR","OR approximates RR when disease prevalence is low","OR is always larger than RR","OR cannot be calculated for common diseases"]'::jsonb, 'OR approximates RR when disease prevalence is low', 'When the disease is rare (<10% prevalence), the odds ratio closely approximates the relative risk. This is because the odds become similar to risk when events are uncommon.'),
('27aeee84-c5f7-5064-91d4-0aca1295a8ce'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'Risk difference (RD) is:', '["Attributable risk percent","(Incidence in exposed - Incidence in unexposed) / Incidence in exposed","Odds in exposed - Odds in unexposed","Incidence in exposed - Incidence in unexposed","Incidence in exposed / Incidence in unexposed"]'::jsonb, 'Incidence in exposed - Incidence in unexposed', 'Risk difference (also called attributable risk) is the absolute difference in incidence between exposed and unexposed groups. It indicates the excess risk attributable to the exposure.'),
('48a9495b-08de-5825-8eba-d5bbba1b2b32'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'A researcher is conducting an epidemiological study. Researchers design a study to investigate the association between smoking and cardiovascular events. Number Needed to Treat (NNT) is calculated as:', '["1 / Prevalence","1 / Relative risk","1 / Odds ratio","1 / Incidence","1 / Risk difference"]'::jsonb, '1 / Risk difference', 'NNT = 1/RD. It represents the number of patients who need to be treated to prevent one adverse outcome. A lower NNT indicates a more effective intervention.'),
('8f5ca724-e603-5420-8af0-e419bf961cd8'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'A new diagnostic test for diabetes mellitus is being evaluated against the gold standard in 500 patients. Number Needed to Harm (NNH) is:', '["1 / Prevalence","1 / Attributable risk in the exposed","1 / Odds ratio","Number of patients needed to cause one adverse event","1 / Relative risk"]'::jsonb, '1 / Attributable risk in the exposed', 'NNH = 1/Attributable Risk. It represents the number of patients who need to be exposed to cause one additional harmful event. A lower NNH indicates higher risk of harm.'),
('9b095ac1-b7ba-53c0-8ee7-08c6ae2dc4e5'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'Population Attributable Risk (PAR) is:', '["Risk difference in the exposed group","Odds ratio in the population","Prevalence in the population","Risk difference in the total population","Relative risk in the total population"]'::jsonb, 'Risk difference in the total population', 'PAR = Incidence in total population - Incidence in unexposed. It estimates the disease burden in the entire population that could be eliminated if the exposure were removed.'),
('5f468cc0-0d62-57c7-b03a-ef078d3c1e30'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'Attributable fraction (AF) in the exposed is:', '["RR - 1","1 - RR","RR /","1 /","(RR - 1) / RR"]'::jsonb, '(RR - 1) / RR', 'Attributable fraction in the exposed = (RR - 1)/RR. It represents the proportion of disease in the exposed group that can be attributed to the exposure.'),
('97eea2bc-c0a3-517a-8949-40211a7b0c96'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'A researcher is conducting an epidemiological study. If RR = 2.0, the attributable fraction in the exposed is:', '["25%","75%","50%","200%","100%"]'::jsonb, '50%', 'AF in exposed = (2.0 - 1)/2.0 = 0.5 = 50%. This means 50% of cases in the exposed group are due to the exposure.'),
('b5ad95b9-d97c-51c6-824f-ebf70d3487a4'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'Population attributable fraction depends on:', '["Study design","Only prevalence of exposure","Only RR","Sample size","Both RR and prevalence of exposure"]'::jsonb, 'Both RR and prevalence of exposure', 'Population attributable fraction = [Pe × (RR - 1)] / [1 + Pe × (RR - 1)], where Pe is the prevalence of exposure in the population. It depends on both the strength of association and exposure prevalence.'),
('99dbedad-f659-59b7-a1c3-619b24673446'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'A 95% confidence interval for RR that does not include 1.0 indicates:', '["The sample size is inadequate","The result is biased","No statistical significance","The RR is not precise","Statistical significance at p < 0.05"]'::jsonb, 'Statistical significance at p < 0.05', 'If the 95% CI for RR excludes 1.0 (the null value), the association is statistically significant at the 0.05 level. This means the p-value is less than 0.05.'),
('04edfd3c-b361-5bbe-850a-980aaff5732c'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'A researcher is conducting an epidemiological study. Which measure is most appropriate for quantifying the public health impact of an exposure?', '["Number needed to treat","Odds ratio","Population attributable risk","Risk difference","Relative risk"]'::jsonb, 'Population attributable risk', 'PAR estimates the number of cases in the population attributable to the exposure, reflecting the potential benefit of removing the exposure. It combines both the strength of association and exposure prevalence.'),
('c088dd99-1cf2-567e-9918-542201846c0b'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'Which measure is most appropriate for assessing the biological strength of an association?', '["NNT","Risk difference","PAR","Relative risk","Population attributable fraction"]'::jsonb, 'Relative risk', 'Relative risk (or odds ratio) quantifies the strength of the association between exposure and disease. A higher RR indicates a stronger biological association, though causation requires additional criteria.'),
('1a82f255-848e-5730-8e37-77604187b68b'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'A new diagnostic test for diabetes mellitus is being evaluated against the gold standard in 2000 patients. Attributable risk percent in the population is calculated as:', '["1 / Risk difference","(OR - 1)/OR × 100","(Incidence in exposed - Incidence in unexposed) / Incidence in exposed","(Incidence in population - Incidence in unexposed) / Incidence in population","(RR - 1)/RR × 100"]'::jsonb, '(Incidence in population - Incidence in unexposed) / Incidence in population', 'Population attributable risk percent = (Incidence in population - Incidence in unexposed) / Incidence in population × 100. It indicates the percentage of disease in the population attributable to the exposure.'),
('4ac86762-9dcf-5d61-856d-dc3886f0086c'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'In a 2×2 table, the formula for RR is:', '["(a/(a+b)) / (c/(c+d))","(a+c)/","ad/bc","(a/(a+c)) / (b/(b+d))","(a+b)/"]'::jsonb, '(a/(a+b)) / (c/(c+d))', 'RR = [a/(a+b)] / [c/(c+d)], where a/(a+b) is incidence in exposed and c/(c+d) is incidence in unexposed. This is the ratio of two cumulative incidences.'),
('2c2b1c48-d7cb-56ea-9f1a-65d91da21b67'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'A researcher is conducting an epidemiological study. Which measure is used in cohort studies?', '["All of the above","Only A and C","Relative risk","Risk difference","Odds ratio"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Cohort studies can calculate RR, RD, and OR (though RR and RD are more commonly reported). OR can also be calculated from cohort data using the same 2×2 table.'),
('6ce914f6-640c-5362-ac51-3f13f9a58315'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'Symmetric measure of association that can be used in any design:', '["Relative risk","Risk difference","Attributable risk","NNT","Odds ratio"]'::jsonb, 'Odds ratio', 'Odds ratio is a symmetric measure that can be calculated from case-control, cohort, cross-sectional, and RCT data. It has desirable mathematical properties for logistic regression.'),
('b6da4668-4226-5300-af3e-8250cca07a7a'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'If RR = 0.4, the exposure is:', '["Neutral","Inconclusive","Causative","Protective","Harmful"]'::jsonb, 'Protective', 'An RR less than 1.0 indicates a protective effect. An RR of 0.4 means the exposed group has 40% of the risk (60% risk reduction) compared to the unexposed group.'),
('0bf0e60d-e01e-5660-971d-32ba6990f7cc'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'If OR > 1.0 in a case-control study, the exposure is:', '["Confounded clinical presentation","Inversely associated clinical presentation","Protective clinical presentation","Unrelated clinical presentation","Positively associated with the disease"]'::jsonb, 'Positively associated with the disease', 'An OR > 1.0 suggests a positive association, meaning the exposure may be a risk factor for the disease. However, this does not prove causation without considering other evidence.'),
('f23085d2-2c77-5f93-8dc5-75d5ce1a6274'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'A researcher is conducting an epidemiological study. Which of the following is NOT a measure of association?', '["OR","RR","Risk difference","Attributable fraction","Prevalence"]'::jsonb, 'Prevalence', 'Prevalence is a measure of disease frequency, not association. RR, OR, RD, and attributable fraction are all measures of association between exposure and outcome.'),
('be2a28fc-e953-58c5-a931-6cf8ef0ebd32'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'Standardised morbidity ratio (SMR) is:', '["Observed - Expected cases","Expected - Observed","Observed/Expected cases","Expected/Observed cases","(Observed - Expected)/Expected"]'::jsonb, 'Observed/Expected cases', 'SMR is the ratio of observed number of cases to expected number of cases based on a standard population. An SMR > 1 indicates more cases than expected.'),
('34a9780b-c1af-588b-af6c-aa534bf6b8a5'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'Researchers design a study to investigate the association between smoking and cardiovascular events. In a cohort study with 10 cases among 100 exposed and 5 cases among 200 unexposed, what is the RR?', '["0.25","2.0","4.0","10.0","0.5"]'::jsonb, '4.0', 'Incidence in exposed = 10/100 = 0.1. Incidence in unexposed = 5/200 = 0.025. RR = 0.1/0.025 = 4.0.'),
('71991646-f355-5de1-aac9-259ec82311c6'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'In the same cohort (10 exposed cases/100 exposed, 5 unexposed cases/200 unexposed), what is the RD?', '["1 per 100","7.5 per 100","10 per 100","5 per 100","2.5 per 100"]'::jsonb, '7.5 per 100', 'RD = 0.10 - 0.025 = 0.075 = 7.5 per 100. This means there are 7.5 excess cases per 100 exposed individuals attributable to the exposure.'),
('3b9592cb-eeec-578e-82f3-12e3580c94f2'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'A researcher is conducting an epidemiological study. In the same cohort, what is the NNT for a protective exposure?', '["50 clinical presentation","10 clinical presentation","Cannot calculate from these data","100 clinical presentation","200 clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Cannot calculate from these data', 'NNT = 1/RD. But since the exposure appears harmful (RR > 1), we would calculate NNH instead. NNH = 1/0.075 = 13.3, meaning about 13 people need to be exposed for one excess case.'),
('55ce84f8-05d4-5f55-b18b-fe8094101910'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'Which measure is preferred for communicating absolute risk to patients?', '["Population attributable risk","Risk difference","Standardised ratio","Odds ratio","Relative risk"]'::jsonb, 'Risk difference', 'Risk difference is most useful for clinical decision-making and communicating absolute risk. A patient wants to know their actual risk, not just the relative comparison.'),
('61fc5e4e-3d33-5cda-88a2-eef16e60df22'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'A researcher is conducting an epidemiological study. A new diagnostic test for tuberculosis is being evaluated against the gold standard in 2000 patients. The term ''attributable risk'' is synonymous with:', '["Attributable fraction","Odds ratio","Relative risk","Population attributable risk","Risk difference"]'::jsonb, 'Risk difference', 'Attributable risk is the same as risk difference. It quantifies the excess risk in the exposed group compared to the unexposed group.'),
('3c66c5b1-7860-5a75-889b-f9afdbb3ddc7'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'A researcher is conducting an epidemiological study. If a disease has an RR of 3.0 with smoking and 30% of the population smokes, the PAR% is approximately:', '["37.5%","50%","66.7%","75%","25%"]'::jsonb, '37.5%', 'PAR% = [Pe(RR-1)]/[1+Pe(RR-1)] = [0.3(2)]/[1+0.3(2)] = 0.6/1.6 = 37.5%. About 37.5% of cases are attributable to smoking.'),
('caaaf6e2-e77c-5e0d-b4f6-3227d68fc194'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'A researcher is conducting an epidemiological study. In case-control studies, the disease odds ratio is equivalent to:', '["Relative risk","Risk difference","Standardised ratio","Exposure odds ratio","Attributable risk"]'::jsonb, 'Exposure odds ratio', 'In case-control studies, the odds ratio can be calculated as (odds of exposure in cases)/(odds of exposure in controls), which equals ad/bc - the same as the disease odds ratio.'),
('0771b8e6-e5c3-5d68-bdd6-b04aa72502ba'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'A researcher is conducting an epidemiological study. In a population with 1%, the accuracy is: Mantel-Haenszel OR is used for:', '["Calculating crude OR","Adjusting for selection bias","Assessing interaction","Pooling OR across strata","Testing linear trend"]'::jsonb, 'Pooling OR across strata', 'Mantel-Haenszel OR provides a weighted average of stratum-specific ORs. It is used to adjust for confounding by producing a pooled estimate across strata.'),
('4f40499f-406a-5c15-b0c4-851c2b12562d'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'Which measure can be directly calculated in a cross-sectional study?', '["Person-time rate","Cumulative incidence","Attack rate","Prevalence ratio","Incidence rate"]'::jsonb, 'Prevalence ratio', 'Cross-sectional studies can calculate prevalence ratio (PR) and prevalence odds ratio (POR), but not incidence-based measures since they do not follow participants over time.'),
('36fc443a-13b6-5f9d-bb5a-1e6c9cc8b1cd'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'If the 95% CI for an OR is 1.2 to 3.4, the result is:', '["Biased","Not statistically significant","Inconclusive","Underpowered","Statistically significant"]'::jsonb, 'Statistically significant', 'Since the CI (1.2 to 3.4) does not include 1.0, the association is statistically significant at p < 0.05. The OR is significantly greater than 1.0.'),
('a91070e5-5aad-5057-8d0b-712a15d6d36d'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'If the 95% CI for an RR is 0.8 to 1.6, the result is:', '["Statistically significant","Highly significant","Meaningful association","Not statistically significant","Inconclusive"]'::jsonb, 'Not statistically significant', 'Since the CI (0.8 to 1.6) includes 1.0, the association is not statistically significant at the 0.05 level. We cannot conclude there is a true association.'),
('4192134c-96f1-58ba-a8fc-d50745d34333'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'A researcher is conducting an epidemiological study. An unmeasured confounder would most likely affect:', '["Precision","Reliability","External validity","Generalisability","Internal validity"]'::jsonb, 'Internal validity', 'Confounding affects internal validity - the ability to draw accurate causal conclusions. It introduces systematic error by distorting the true association between exposure and outcome.'),
('05f74087-fcd2-5814-afa8-e69d75778379'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'The strength of an association is measured by the magnitude of:', '["RR or OR","Sample size","Chi-square statistic","Confidence interval width","p-value"]'::jsonb, 'RR or OR', 'The strength of association is quantified by the magnitude of RR or OR. A larger RR/OR indicates a stronger association, which is one criterion for causation.'),
('bac4d5ec-fa69-580a-a138-0bece04b9929'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'Dose-response relationship supports causation when:', '["Increasing exposure increases risk","The study is large","Disease is rare","Exposure is rare","Any exposure is harmful"]'::jsonb, 'Increasing exposure increases risk', 'A dose-response (biological gradient) relationship, where increasing levels of exposure are associated with increasing risk, strengthens the case for causation. This is one of Bradford Hill''s criteria.'),
('64e30592-9d39-5be3-a632-58525bdac423'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'Consistency of association across different studies supports:', '["Confounding","Causality","Bias","Chance","Random error"]'::jsonb, 'Causality', 'When multiple independent studies in different populations with different designs show similar results, it strengthens the causal interpretation. Consistency is one of Bradford Hill''s criteria.'),
('12e2d0ce-cf60-5df3-b33a-f183b95f79a4'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'Temporality in causation means:', '["Outcome must precede exposure","Time is irrelevant","The study must be longitudinal","Exposure and outcome occur simultaneously","Exposure must precede the outcome"]'::jsonb, 'Exposure must precede the outcome', 'Temporality is the most important Bradford Hill criterion - the cause must precede the effect. This is why cohort studies and RCTs provide stronger causal evidence than cross-sectional studies.'),
('ef915092-4442-52a4-b305-9a7d792a279d'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'A new diagnostic test for diabetes mellitus is being evaluated against the gold standard in 1000 patients. Specificity of association means:', '["One exposure leads to one disease","Many exposures lead to one disease","Any exposure can cause any disease","The association is weak","One exposure leads to many diseases"]'::jsonb, 'One exposure leads to one disease', 'Specificity suggests that one cause leads to one effect. However, this criterion is not essential (e.g., smoking causes many diseases) and is considered a weak criterion for causation.'),
('419a2620-ece6-52bd-92b7-791af2e35a27'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'Plausibility in causal inference requires:', '["Control for all confounders","Large sample size","Prospective design","The association makes biological sense","Statistical significance"]'::jsonb, 'The association makes biological sense', 'Plausibility means the observed association is consistent with existing biological knowledge. While important, lack of plausibility may simply reflect incomplete scientific knowledge.'),
('229fba76-35be-503e-931f-bceea14a743d'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'Analogy in Bradford Hill criteria refers to:', '["Comparing to placebo","Using analogous statistical tests","Similar exposures causing similar effects","Analogy with laboratory experiments","Analogy with animal studies"]'::jsonb, 'Similar exposures causing similar effects', 'Analogy suggests that if a similar exposure is known to cause a similar disease, it strengthens the case for causation. For example, if drug A causes birth defects, a similar drug B might also.'),
('916d5fb4-460d-5540-9198-1e4c76a911b8'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'Experiment criterion in Bradford Hill refers to:', '["Clinical experience clinical presentation","In vitro studies clinical presentation","Laboratory experiments only clinical presentation","Evidence from randomised experiments or natural experiments","Animal experiments clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Evidence from randomised experiments or natural experiments', 'Experimental evidence, particularly from randomised controlled trials or natural experiments, provides strong support for causation. This is considered the most rigorous evidence.'),
('fc81ee5e-72d5-5737-938b-5fa898a72453'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'Coherence criterion requires:', '["The association does not conflict with known facts","One cause leads to one effect","The association is consistent across studies","Biological gradient exists","The cause precedes the effect"]'::jsonb, 'The association does not conflict with known facts', 'Coherence means the cause-and-effect interpretation should not seriously conflict with what is already known about the natural history and biology of the disease.'),
('84216678-1d9c-567b-af75-34d37dc84ed1'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'A new diagnostic test for coronary artery disease is being evaluated against the gold standard in 500 patients. A new diagnostic test for lung cancer is being evaluated against the gold standard in 500 patients. A new diagnostic test for lung cancer is being evaluated against the gold standard in 500 patients. Which measure is least affected by the prevalence of the disease?', '["Relative risk","Attributable risk","Risk difference","Population attributable risk","Odds ratio"]'::jsonb, 'Odds ratio', 'Odds ratio is relatively stable across disease prevalence (except for very common diseases). RR, RD, and attributable risk vary with baseline risk, which changes with disease prevalence.'),
('8f0ef68a-3305-56fd-bc0c-05ba720030ed'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'Standardised mortality ratio (SMR) of 120 means:', '["20% more deaths observed than expected","20% fewer deaths","120% more deaths","120 more deaths observed","Deaths are 120 times expected"]'::jsonb, '20% more deaths observed than expected', 'SMR = 120 = 120/100 = 1.2. This means there are 20% more deaths observed than expected based on the standard population rates.'),
('d42fcbc1-7726-557a-8c3a-094afb3757be'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'A new diagnostic test for HIV/AIDS is being evaluated against the gold standard in 200 patients. Rate ratio is another term for:', '["Risk difference clinical presentation","Standardised ratio clinical presentation","Odds ratio clinical presentation","Relative risk based on incidence rates","Prevalence ratio clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Relative risk based on incidence rates', 'Rate ratio is a relative risk calculated from incidence rates (with person-time denominators). It compares rates between exposed and unexposed groups.'),
('6233fb9f-60f6-5a37-95d3-df977a574a15'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'A researcher is conducting an epidemiological study. In a 2×2 table with a=40, b=60, c=10, d=90, what is the odds ratio?', '["0.17","1.5","4.0","0.67","6.0"]'::jsonb, '6.0', 'OR = ad/bc = (40×90)/(60×10) = 3600/600 = 6.0. The odds of exposure among cases is 6 times the odds of exposure among controls.'),
('261f6e33-b8ff-58b0-a325-d1496e8a9706'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'In the same 2×2 table (a=40, b=60, c=10, d=90), what is the RR?', '["6.0","4.5","0.5","3.0","2.0"]'::jsonb, '2.0', 'RR = [40/(40+60)]/[10/(10+90)] = (40/100)/(10/100) = 0.4/0.1 = 4.0. However, wait - let me recalculate: Incidence in exposed = 40/100 = 0.4, Incidence in unexposed = 10/100 = 0.1. RR = 0.4/0.1 = 4.0.'),
('31021a73-6ad4-5acc-9899-b6cc1f9c0f60'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'Chance (random error) in a study is assessed using:', '["Positive predictive value","Sensitivity and specificity","Kappa statistic","RR and OR","p-values and confidence intervals"]'::jsonb, 'p-values and confidence intervals', 'Chance is assessed through statistical inference using p-values (significance tests) and confidence intervals. These quantify random variation and the role of sampling error.'),
('9691f01b-351d-5f25-8f07-aad10b0a5adf'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'A researcher is conducting an epidemiological study. Type I error (alpha) is:', '["Rejecting a false null hypothesis","Rejecting a true null hypothesis","Accepting a true null hypothesis","Accepting a false null hypothesis","Failing to reject a false null hypothesis"]'::jsonb, 'Rejecting a true null hypothesis', 'Type I error occurs when we conclude there is an effect when none exists (false positive). Alpha is the probability of making a Type I error, typically set at 0.05.'),
('b33aa851-2683-501d-82c5-509e55fb30d4'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'A researcher is conducting an epidemiological study. Type II error (beta) is:', '["Accepting a true null hypothesis","Rejecting a false alternative","Failing to reject a false null hypothesis","Rejecting a true null hypothesis","Accepting a false alternative"]'::jsonb, 'Failing to reject a false null hypothesis', 'Type II error occurs when we fail to detect a true effect (false negative). Beta is the probability of Type II error. Power = 1 - Beta.'),
('5dc4c110-5b65-536d-a5da-69530fc555f6'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'Study power is defined as:', '["1 - Beta","Alpha","1 - Alpha","1/Alpha","Beta"]'::jsonb, '1 - Beta', 'Power = 1 - Beta, which is the probability of correctly rejecting a false null hypothesis (detecting a true effect). Power should be at least 80%.'),
('014f11f5-8712-5791-811c-63511cddb4db'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'Increasing sample size:', '["Increases Type I error","Decreases power","Increases power","Has no effect on power","Increases Beta"]'::jsonb, 'Increases power', 'Larger sample sizes reduce standard errors, making it easier to detect true effects. This increases statistical power. Power = f(sample size, effect size, alpha).'),
('8abf36a4-f157-5ad3-bac3-91d1cbcc7b88'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'A researcher is conducting an epidemiological study. A new diagnostic test for hypertension is being evaluated against the gold standard in 100 patients. A new diagnostic test for coronary artery disease is being evaluated against the gold standard in 500 patients. A new diagnostic test for tuberculosis is being evaluated against the gold standard in 500 patients. Bias is defined as:', '["Chance variation clinical presentation","Random error clinical presentation","Systematic error in study design or conduct","Sampling variation clinical presentation","Measurement imprecision clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Systematic error in study design or conduct', 'Bias is systematic error that leads to an incorrect estimate of the association between exposure and outcome. Unlike random error, bias cannot be reduced by increasing sample size.'),
('60da427a-7673-5de6-ad62-48ef6aa5d899'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'Selection bias occurs when:', '["Measurement of exposure is inaccurate clinical presentation","Confounders are not controlled clinical presentation","The sample size is too small clinical presentation","The association between exposure and disease differs between those who participate and those who","The data are missing clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'The association between exposure and disease differs between those who participate and those who', 'Selection bias arises from procedures used to select participants. It occurs when the exposure-disease association differs between participants and non-participants.'),
('a4d98c37-7c95-5208-8824-4fe217a70305'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'Information bias results from:', '["Confounding","Errors in measuring exposure or outcome","Errors in selecting participants","Random variation","Missing data"]'::jsonb, 'Errors in measuring exposure or outcome', 'Information bias (measurement bias) occurs when the data collected about exposure or outcome is systematically inaccurate. This can lead to misclassification.'),
('87e2bc77-1bf2-5d6b-8ab2-f807c49f3de2'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'A researcher is conducting an epidemiological study. A new diagnostic test for diabetes mellitus is being evaluated against the gold standard in 2000 patients. Recall bias is a type of:', '["Chance","Information bias","Ecological fallacy","Confounding","Selection bias"]'::jsonb, 'Information bias', 'Recall bias is a form of information bias where cases remember past exposures differently than controls. It is common in case-control studies.'),
('1a1b8ae3-e831-5546-b682-3207f217e239'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'Misclassification bias can be:', '["Always systematic","Only differential","Always random","Only non-differential","Differential or non-differential"]'::jsonb, 'Differential or non-differential', 'Misclassification can be differential (differs between comparison groups) or non-differential (similar across groups). Differential misclassification can bias in either direction; non-differential usually biases toward the null.'),
('fed8123e-03b5-599e-a71b-7df881639c49'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'Non-differential misclassification typically biases toward:', '["Away from the null","Either direction","No effect","The true value","The null value"]'::jsonb, 'The null value', 'Non-differential misclassification (error equally likely in all groups) typically biases the association toward the null (no effect), making it harder to detect a true association.'),
('303d0df4-82be-5318-bf8b-0d5b9ba4ca89'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'A new diagnostic test for coronary artery disease is being evaluated against the gold standard in 1000 patients. Differential misclassification can bias:', '["Only toward infinity","Only away from the null","Only toward zero","In either direction","Only toward the null"]'::jsonb, 'In either direction', 'Differential misclassification (error differs between groups) can bias the association in either direction - toward or away from the null. This makes it particularly problematic.'),
('b1b11070-087b-5717-8806-71cf073c9300'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'A new diagnostic test for breast cancer is being evaluated against the gold standard in 100 patients. A new diagnostic test for hypertension is being evaluated against the gold standard in 1000 patients. A new diagnostic test for hypertension is being evaluated against the gold standard in 1000 patients. Confounding occurs when:', '["Measurements are inaccurate clinical presentation","The study is not blinded clinical presentation","The sample is not representative clinical presentation","The exposure of interest is mixed with another factor that affects the outcome","Participants drop out clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'The exposure of interest is mixed with another factor that affects the outcome', 'Confounding is a mixing of effects where a third variable (confounder) is associated with both the exposure and the outcome, distorting the true association between them.'),
('3d7ba2c4-6fbe-5781-a5f7-df7ebba433fb'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'In a population with 20%, the Youden index is: A confounder must:', '["Be caused by the outcome","Be associated only with outcome","Be associated only with exposure","Be associated with both exposure and outcome","Be caused by the exposure"]'::jsonb, 'Be associated with both exposure and outcome', 'A confounder must be (1) associated with the exposure, (2) independently associated with the outcome, and (3) not be an intermediate variable in the causal pathway.'),
('89077cce-3ffb-5028-89b3-e498e97e6285'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'Which is NOT a method to control for confounding?', '["Randomisation","Stratification","Restriction","Matching","Selection bias"]'::jsonb, 'Selection bias', 'Randomisation, restriction, matching, and stratification are methods to control confounding. Selection bias is a different type of error, not a method to control confounding.'),
('55b88489-17f2-5a8c-a4c7-769b77428072'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'A new diagnostic test for diabetes mellitus is being evaluated against the gold standard in 1000 patients. Restriction as a method to control confounding involves:', '["Stratifying the analysis clinical presentation","Limiting the study to a single category of the confounder","Matching cases and controls clinical presentation","Randomly assigning participants clinical presentation","Using multivariable models clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Limiting the study to a single category of the confounder', 'Restriction limits the study to participants with a specific value of the confounder (e.g., only non-smokers). This eliminates confounding by that variable but limits generalisability.'),
('8d2511da-d7cc-5d2b-8f06-254e9e88f402'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'Matching in case-control studies involves:', '["Selecting all available controls clinical presentation","Randomly selecting controls clinical presentation","Selecting controls similar to cases on potential confounders","Using population controls clinical presentation","Using hospital controls clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Selecting controls similar to cases on potential confounders', 'Matching selects controls that are comparable to cases on key confounding variables (e.g., age, sex). This eliminates confounding by matched variables but requires matched analysis.'),
('9a9abbec-0839-5788-a04c-83be3780d209'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'Stratification in data analysis:', '["Ignores the confounder clinical presentation","Corrects information bias clinical presentation","Adjusts for selection bias clinical presentation","Examines associations within strata of the confounder","Increases sample size clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Examines associations within strata of the confounder', 'Stratification involves analysing the exposure-disease association within separate categories (strata) of the confounding variable, then pooling results. This yields a confounder-adjusted estimate.'),
('c0afd428-25b2-5c0a-baa0-34e908f6a2ad'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'Multivariable regression analysis controls for confounding by:', '["Including confounders as covariates in the model","Matching participants clinical presentation","Restricting the sample clinical presentation","Stratifying the data clinical presentation","Randomising participants clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Including confounders as covariates in the model', 'Multivariable regression models (e.g., logistic regression, Cox regression) can include multiple confounders as independent variables, providing an adjusted estimate of the exposure effect.'),
('b404ab0e-8d16-592d-94ae-434ebd106622'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'A new diagnostic test for breast cancer is being evaluated against the gold standard in 100 patients. A new diagnostic test for lung cancer is being evaluated against the gold standard in 1000 patients. A new diagnostic test for lung cancer is being evaluated against the gold standard in 1000 patients. Which study design eliminates confounding by known and unknown factors?', '["Case-control study","Cross-sectional study","RCT with randomisation","Cohort study","Ecological study"]'::jsonb, 'RCT with randomisation', 'Randomisation is the only method that can theoretically control for both known and unknown confounding factors by randomly distributing them across treatment groups.'),
('93f67a19-83c9-58a6-86e0-bac4c263dff0'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'A researcher is conducting an epidemiological study. Confounding by indication occurs when:', '["Blinding is broken","The indication for treatment is ignored","The indication for treatment is associated with the outcome","Participants are lost to follow-up","Treatment is randomised"]'::jsonb, 'The indication for treatment is associated with the outcome', 'Confounding by indication occurs in observational studies when the reason a patient receives a treatment is associated with their prognosis. This is a major challenge in pharmacoepidemiology.'),
('722c56a5-d034-53fb-803f-43742ea89d60'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'Hawthorne effect is a form of:', '["Publication bias","Selection bias","Performance bias","Confounding","Recall bias"]'::jsonb, 'Performance bias', 'Hawthorne effect (behaviour change due to being observed) is a type of performance bias. It can affect outcomes in unblinded studies by altering participant behaviour.'),
('615a14e3-2315-539c-b03b-7aa330a780d3'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'A researcher is conducting an epidemiological study. Lead-time bias affects studies of:', '["Treatment efficacy","Disease etiology","Screening programmes","Disease prevalence","Genetic association"]'::jsonb, 'Screening programmes', 'Lead-time bias occurs in screening studies when earlier diagnosis makes survival appear longer even if mortality is unchanged. It artificially inflates survival time without true benefit.'),
('d9ef4e09-9b66-5bbb-9479-380dab410c14'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'Length-biased sampling occurs when:', '["All diseases are detected equally","Treatment is more effective","Screening preferentially detects less aggressive disease","Screening misses all cases","Aggressive disease is detected earlier"]'::jsonb, 'Screening preferentially detects less aggressive disease', 'Length-biased sampling occurs because slower-growing, less aggressive cancers are detectable for longer periods and are more likely to be detected by screening. This overestimates screening benefit.'),
('9656669f-85de-584d-9b1b-63a360f802d2'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'Publication bias refers to:', '["Only RCTs being published","Only large studies being published","All studies being published regardless of results","Studies with positive results being more likely to be published","Studies with negative results being more likely to be published"]'::jsonb, 'Studies with positive results being more likely to be published', 'Publication bias arises when studies with statistically significant (positive) results are more likely to be published than those with null or negative results. This distorts the literature.'),
('b971f3fc-aaf3-530d-9ab4-9d5fa0b798f1'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'Funnel plots are used to detect:', '["Measurement error","Confounding","Selection bias","Publication bias","Information bias"]'::jsonb, 'Publication bias', 'Funnel plots plot effect size against study size (or precision). Asymmetry in the funnel plot suggests publication bias, as smaller studies with negative results are missing.'),
('2a841327-dc11-56a2-8071-2b52cc676c4f'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'A new diagnostic test for coronary artery disease is being evaluated against the gold standard in 200 patients. Sensitivity analysis assesses:', '["The prevalence of disease","The sensitivity of a diagnostic test","How robust results are to assumptions or decisions","The specificity of a diagnostic test","The accuracy of measurement"]'::jsonb, 'How robust results are to assumptions or decisions', 'Sensitivity analysis examines how changing assumptions, inclusion criteria, or analytical methods affects the conclusions. If results change substantially, they are sensitive (not robust).'),
('2f6f8b29-6cf0-52a3-94f5-7e637b658094'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'A new diagnostic test for HIV/AIDS is being evaluated against the gold standard in 1000 patients. A new diagnostic test for coronary artery disease is being evaluated against the gold standard in 1000 patients. Detection bias occurs when:', '["Sample size is inadequate","Confounders are imbalanced","Outcome ascertainment differs between groups","Loss to follow-up occurs","Exposure differs between groups"]'::jsonb, 'Outcome ascertainment differs between groups', 'Detection bias arises when the way outcomes are detected or measured differs between comparison groups. Blinding of outcome assessors can prevent detection bias.'),
('f5570aca-1367-5c61-b826-e12eb0e886ff'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'Attention bias in clinical trials occurs when:', '["The control group receives no attention","The treatment is ineffective","The sample is too small","The study is single-blind","Extra attention given to one group affects outcomes"]'::jsonb, 'Extra attention given to one group affects outcomes', 'Attention bias occurs when participants receiving extra attention (e.g., more frequent follow-up) show better outcomes regardless of the treatment itself. This is controlled by equal attention to all groups.'),
('4721fc24-6ca7-59fa-8ae1-58a18a9ec4f5'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'A researcher is conducting an epidemiological study. Surveillance bias occurs when:', '["No surveillance is performed clinical presentation","Surveillance is equal across groups clinical presentation","More intensive surveillance in one group leads to more diagnoses","The study is observational clinical presentation","Participants refuse surveillance clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'More intensive surveillance in one group leads to more diagnoses', 'Surveillance bias (detection bias) occurs when one group is monitored more closely, leading to more diagnoses in that group. This can falsely suggest a higher disease incidence.'),
('7e2c7ad8-c1c9-54d1-932d-4b6e4bc97f80'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'A study follows 1000 patients with lung cancer over 5 years to assess survival outcomes. Healthy worker effect is a type of:', '["Publication bias","Lead-time bias","Confounding","Selection bias","Information bias"]'::jsonb, 'Selection bias', 'The healthy worker effect occurs because employed individuals are generally healthier than the general population. This selection bias affects occupational epidemiology studies.'),
('487ec5c7-b466-5e2f-9cf0-fe29e7cff988'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'Confounding by severity occurs when:', '["Disease severity affects treatment choice and outcome","Treatment is standardised","Outcome assessment is blinded","Treatment is randomised","All patients have the same severity"]'::jsonb, 'Disease severity affects treatment choice and outcome', 'Confounding by severity occurs when patients with more severe disease receive different treatments than those with mild disease, and severity independently affects outcomes.'),
('4834b54e-f937-555c-8781-94fb932e6538'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'In a population with 20%, the Youden index is: Which method of confounding control is used AFTER data collection?', '["Matching","Blinding","Randomisation","Restriction","Stratification"]'::jsonb, 'Stratification', 'Stratification and multivariable regression are analytical methods applied after data collection. Randomisation, restriction, matching, and blinding are implemented during study design.'),
('ead33dfb-9b9a-566a-980b-8af9d52e11e7'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'Residual confounding occurs when:', '["No confounding exists","Confounders are not measured","Confounding persists despite attempts to control it","The study is randomised","Confounding is perfectly controlled"]'::jsonb, 'Confounding persists despite attempts to control it', 'Residual confounding occurs when the methods used to control confounding are imperfect (e.g., measured with error, wrong categorisation, or unmeasured confounders).'),
('634e4796-8073-5f58-8bd5-fcaafbb94483'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'Instrumental variable analysis is used to:', '["Address unmeasured confounding","Improve measurement","Address selection bias","Address information bias","Increase sample size"]'::jsonb, 'Address unmeasured confounding', 'Instrumental variable (IV) analysis uses a variable that is associated with the exposure but not directly with the outcome (except through the exposure) to control for unmeasured confounding.'),
('888ce370-ea00-5617-83cc-b81016cd4526'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'Simpson''s paradox refers to:', '["Lead-time bias clinical presentation","Large sample paradox clinical presentation","An association reversal when data are stratified","Small sample problems clinical presentation","Ecological fallacy clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'An association reversal when data are stratified', 'Simpson''s paradox occurs when a trend appears in different groups of data but disappears or reverses when these groups are combined. It illustrates the importance of controlling for confounding.'),
('e432f534-954b-5e1b-8b14-26175fcc10da'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'Immortal time bias occurs when:', '["There is a period where the outcome cannot occur and it is misclassified","The outcome always occurs clinical presentation","Participants cannot die clinical presentation","Time stops during the study clinical presentation","The study is immortal clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'There is a period where the outcome cannot occur and it is misclassified', 'Immortal time bias arises when there is a period of follow-up during which the outcome (e.g., death) cannot occur, but this time is misclassified as exposed person-time. Common in pharmacoepidemiology.'),
('5d00e9fc-d8c7-5e69-80eb-343d8c813bb1'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'A researcher is conducting an epidemiological study. A new diagnostic test for breast cancer is being evaluated against the gold standard in 1000 patients. A new diagnostic test for diabetes mellitus is being evaluated against the gold standard in 2000 patients. A new diagnostic test for breast cancer is being evaluated against the gold standard in 2000 patients. Confounding can be assessed by comparing:', '["Pre and post intervention values","Cases and controls","Exposed and unexposed groups","Crude and adjusted estimates","Baseline characteristics"]'::jsonb, 'Crude and adjusted estimates', 'Confounding is present if the crude (unadjusted) estimate of association differs substantially from the estimate adjusted for potential confounders.'),
('5ebc641e-3646-51dc-8ff4-8aa22c099ed2'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'In a population with 5%, the likelihood ratio is: Which is NOT a source of random error?', '["Sampling variation","Small sample size","Random measurement error","Within-subject variability","Systematic measurement error"]'::jsonb, 'Systematic measurement error', 'Systematic measurement error is bias, not random error. Sampling variation, small sample sizes, random measurement error, and within-subject variability all contribute to random error.'),
('c9300fb0-cce0-51a6-a2f9-485c044e0155'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'A researcher is conducting an epidemiological study. A p-value of 0.03 means:', '["There is a 3% chance the null hypothesis is true clinical presentation","The null hypothesis is definitely false clinical presentation","The result is not significant clinical presentation","There is a 97% chance the alternative is true clinical presentation","There is a 3% chance of observing the result (or more extreme) if the null hypothesis is true"]'::jsonb, 'There is a 3% chance of observing the result (or more extreme) if the null hypothesis is true', 'A p-value is the probability of obtaining results as extreme as or more extreme than those observed, assuming the null hypothesis is true. It is NOT the probability that the null hypothesis is true.'),
('85320054-3a66-5423-bda3-6f3be264f190'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'Multiple comparisons increase the risk of:', '["Selection bias","Type II error","Confounding","Type I error","Information bias"]'::jsonb, 'Type I error', 'When many statistical tests are performed, the probability of at least one false positive (Type I error) increases. Corrections like Bonferroni adjust for multiple comparisons.'),
('bc17e9dc-50ce-57c1-b064-4d6fa6cf081d'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'Bonferroni correction divides alpha by:', '["The number of comparisons","The number of groups","2","The sample size","10"]'::jsonb, 'The number of comparisons', 'Bonferroni correction adjusts the significance level by dividing alpha (e.g., 0.05) by the number of comparisons. For 10 tests, the new threshold = 0.05/10 = 0.005.'),
('025f1044-82bc-5297-8886-3db7d1ea4cbd'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'Propensity score is:', '["A type of matching variable clinical presentation","The probability of receiving treatment given covariates","The exposure probability clinical presentation","The probability of the outcome clinical presentation","The odds of treatment clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'The probability of receiving treatment given covariates', 'Propensity score is the probability of a participant receiving a specific treatment based on their observed covariates. It is used to reduce confounding in observational studies.'),
('4f238c51-65b4-54be-8ace-d4c13dd18566'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'E-value in confounding analysis quantifies:', '["The sample size needed clinical presentation","The strength of the observed association clinical presentation","The minimum strength of association an unmeasured confounder would need to explain away the obser","The power of the study clinical presentation","The p-value clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'The minimum strength of association an unmeasured confounder would need to explain away the obser', 'E-value is the minimum strength of association that an unmeasured confounder would need to have with both exposure and outcome to fully explain the observed association. A large E-value suggests robustness.'),
('b4f3d397-05a7-5656-a9e4-0197d1a4b19a'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'A researcher is conducting an epidemiological study. Bradford Hill criteria do NOT include:', '["Consistency","Strength","Generalisability","Plausibility","Temporality"]'::jsonb, 'Generalisability', 'The Bradford Hill criteria are: strength, consistency, specificity, temporality, biological gradient, plausibility, coherence, experiment, and analogy. Generalisability is not a criterion.'),
('05ff99ef-88aa-5bf5-84e8-c206eb044ce9'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'A new diagnostic test for tuberculosis is being evaluated against the gold standard in 100 patients. Which bias can occur in meta-analyses?', '["Confounding","Recall bias","Lead-time bias","Selection bias","Publication bias"]'::jsonb, 'Publication bias', 'Publication bias is a particular concern in meta-analyses because the literature search may miss unpublished negative studies, leading to an overestimate of the treatment effect.'),
('d4c03fd5-570d-5a7f-b4e0-a861316111e0'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'In a population with 10%, the Youden index is: Small study effect refers to:', '["Small studies showing no effect","Small studies showing larger effects than large studies","All studies showing the same effect","Large studies showing larger effects","Sample size has no effect on results"]'::jsonb, 'Small studies showing larger effects than large studies', 'Small study effect describes the tendency for smaller studies to show larger treatment effects. This can be due to publication bias, lower methodological quality, or true heterogeneity.'),
('4dbf4157-2713-5983-bdec-71bc63aae169'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'A new diagnostic test for diabetes mellitus is being evaluated against the gold standard in 2000 patients. A new diagnostic test for hypertension is being evaluated against the gold standard in 100 patients. Which method assesses confounding by comparing crude and adjusted results?', '["Stepwise regression","Statistical significance of confounder","Change-in-estimate criterion","Forward selection","Backward elimination"]'::jsonb, 'Change-in-estimate criterion', 'The change-in-estimate criterion considers a variable a confounder if its inclusion changes the effect estimate by more than a specified amount (e.g., 10%).'),
('e39f1493-6a63-5dd8-9240-f9f3ad7433f5'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'Directed Acyclic Graphs (DAGs) are used to:', '["Perform meta-analysis","Assess publication bias","Identify confounders and causal pathways","Calculate sample size","Conduct sensitivity analysis"]'::jsonb, 'Identify confounders and causal pathways', 'DAGs are visual tools that represent causal relationships between variables. They help identify confounders, mediators, and colliders, guiding the selection of variables for adjustment.'),
('796f1294-7f3b-5491-8f23-36a1d061839f'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'A new diagnostic test for coronary artery disease is being evaluated against the gold standard in 2000 patients. A new diagnostic test for breast cancer is being evaluated against the gold standard in 100 patients. A new diagnostic test for breast cancer is being evaluated against the gold standard in 100 patients. Collider bias occurs when:', '["Sample size is too small","Information is missing","Stratifying on a variable caused by both exposure and outcome","Selection bias in control selection","A confounder is not measured"]'::jsonb, 'Stratifying on a variable caused by both exposure and outcome', 'Collider bias (Berkson''s bias) occurs when a variable (collider) is influenced by both exposure and outcome. Conditioning on a collider creates a spurious association between exposure and outcome.'),
('bcbf0389-8323-5909-a41e-527d4bb8a251'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'Which bias is most likely in a case-control study where controls are hospitalised patients?', '["Berkson''s bias","Recall bias","Lead-time bias","Publication bias","Immortal time bias"]'::jsonb, 'Berkson''s bias', 'Berkson''s bias is a type of selection bias that occurs when hospitalised controls have different exposure patterns than the general population, creating a spurious association.'),
('45ced12c-d6f9-515a-9267-d16237418a73'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'Loss to follow-up bias is minimized by:', '["Using matching","Using blinded assessment","Maximising participant retention","Restricting the sample","Increasing sample size"]'::jsonb, 'Maximising participant retention', 'Loss to follow-up (attrition) bias is minimised by strategies that maximise retention, such as regular contact, incentive programs, and tracking participants who move.'),
('db467f5b-e1a6-5b3d-be8f-8cab6165d49c'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'If loss to follow-up is 30% and differs by exposure group, this introduces:', '["Lead-time bias","Detection bias","Publication bias","Recall bias","Attrition bias"]'::jsonb, 'Attrition bias', 'Differential loss to follow-up between exposure groups introduces attrition bias. If dropouts differ systematically from completers, the association estimate will be biased.'),
('c99a844f-d342-5631-b99e-02c09dbe797e'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'Washout period in crossover trials prevents:', '["Confounding","Recall bias","Selection bias","Publication bias","Carryover effects"]'::jsonb, 'Carryover effects', 'A washout period eliminates carryover effects - the persistence of the first treatment''s effect into the second treatment period. This is essential for crossover trial validity.'),
('341c1435-816f-55a2-8f48-e0f8a2bee60a'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'Regression to the mean refers to:', '["Cox regression clinical presentation","Linear regression clinical presentation","Logistic regression clinical presentation","Statistical regression models clinical presentation","Extreme values tending to be closer to average on repeated measurement"]'::jsonb, 'Extreme values tending to be closer to average on repeated measurement', 'Regression to the mean occurs when extreme measurements on one occasion tend to be less extreme on subsequent occasions. This can create false treatment effects if not accounted for.'),
('ea570715-85c6-5242-b9df-3b451ae6dfe2'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'A new diagnostic test for tuberculosis is being evaluated against the gold standard in 1000 patients. A new diagnostic test for diabetes mellitus is being evaluated against the gold standard in 1000 patients. A new diagnostic test for lung cancer is being evaluated against the gold standard in 500 patients. Placebo effect is controlled by:', '["Using a no-treatment group","Using a placebo group and blinding","Using active control","Matching participants","Increasing sample size"]'::jsonb, 'Using a placebo group and blinding', 'A placebo group (receiving an inert treatment) controls for the placebo effect when combined with blinding. Both groups should receive identical-looking treatments.'),
('d7c7b025-372d-5d0f-9ea2-8267e0799023'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'A researcher is conducting an epidemiological study. Which is a strategy to reduce information bias?', '["Restricting the sample","Randomising participants","Increasing sample size","Matching cases and controls","Standardising data collection procedures"]'::jsonb, 'Standardising data collection procedures', 'Standardised data collection procedures, validated instruments, and blinding of assessors reduce information bias. These ensure consistent and accurate measurement across groups.'),
('d560f845-2091-582d-a478-626a518400f3'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'A new diagnostic test for HIV/AIDS is being evaluated against the gold standard in 200 patients. Assessment bias can be reduced by:', '["Increasing sample size","Blinding outcome assessors","Stratification","Matching","Restriction"]'::jsonb, 'Blinding outcome assessors', 'Blinding of outcome assessors prevents assessment (detection) bias by ensuring that the person evaluating outcomes does not know the group assignment.'),
('08c69117-72c1-56b6-8423-f298304089e0'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'The effect of confounding can be in:', '["Either direction","Only upward","No direction","Only toward the null","Only away from the null"]'::jsonb, 'Either direction', 'Confounding can either overestimate or underestimate the true association. It can make a harmless exposure appear harmful or a harmful exposure appear protective.'),
('8809c3ff-3d4e-53d6-9e65-3de539d24597'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'A randomized controlled trial is conducted to evaluate a screening program for tuberculosis. A double-blind RCT minimises:', '["Only publication bias","Only selection bias","Only confounding","Only attrition bias","Both performance and detection bias"]'::jsonb, 'Both performance and detection bias', 'Double-blinding prevents performance bias (participants and providers altering behaviour) and detection bias (biased outcome assessment). This is the highest level of blinding.'),
('2677e2b7-759e-5ed8-b22a-8a94b589f43c'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'Screening is defined as:', '["Diagnosing symptomatic patients","Treating confirmed disease","Performing surgery on diseased patients","Testing asymptomatic individuals to detect disease early","Rehabilitating after treatment"]'::jsonb, 'Testing asymptomatic individuals to detect disease early', 'Screening involves applying a test to asymptomatic individuals to identify those at higher risk of disease or those with early disease. It is a form of secondary prevention.'),
('60783116-3188-5644-a70a-70ddf55a391f'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'A new diagnostic test for tuberculosis is being evaluated against the gold standard in 200 patients. The main purpose of screening is:', '["Eliminate all risk factors clinical presentation","Replace diagnostic tests clinical presentation","Cure all diseases clinical presentation","Early detection to reduce morbidity and mortality","Provide reassurance clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Early detection to reduce morbidity and mortality', 'The goal of screening is to detect disease at an early stage when treatment is more effective, ultimately reducing morbidity, mortality, and improving outcomes.'),
('03f51e3c-8e5f-523c-9693-49d540ce033a'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'A good screening test should be:', '["Low cost","High sensitivity","Both high sensitivity and specificity","High positive predictive value","High specificity"]'::jsonb, 'Both high sensitivity and specificity', 'A good screening test should have both high sensitivity (to detect most cases) and high specificity (to avoid false positives). The balance depends on the disease and consequences of misclassification.'),
('ded33d77-cff0-5f96-b35b-ae65c7c5defa'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'A new diagnostic test for breast cancer is being evaluated against the gold standard in 200 patients. A new diagnostic test for breast cancer is being evaluated against the gold standard in 500 patients. A new diagnostic test for hypertension is being evaluated against the gold standard in 500 patients. A new diagnostic test for tuberculosis is being evaluated against the gold standard in 200 patients. Sensitivity of a test is:', '["True positives /","True negatives /","True negatives /","True positives /","(True positives + True negatives) / Total"]'::jsonb, 'True positives /', 'Sensitivity = a/(a+c) where a = true positives and c = false negatives. It is the proportion of people WITH the disease who test positive - the ability to correctly identify those with disease.'),
('e0ee1ead-f52f-57ff-9604-510aa1bf0be5'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'Specificity of a test is:', '["True positives /","True negatives /","True positives /","True negatives /","(True positives + True negatives) / Total"]'::jsonb, 'True negatives /', 'Specificity = d/(b+d) where d = true negatives and b = false positives. It is the proportion of people WITHOUT the disease who test negative - the ability to correctly identify those without disease.'),
('11090b66-feee-5435-8a10-d6ed2be0705f'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'A researcher is conducting an epidemiological study. Positive Predictive Value (PPV) is:', '["True positives /","True negatives /","Sensitivity × Prevalence","True positives /","True negatives /"]'::jsonb, 'True positives /', 'PPV = a/(a+b). It is the probability that a person with a positive test actually HAS the disease. PPV depends on both test accuracy and disease prevalence.'),
('f4a40390-e1dc-5d33-8739-864d5a72f3df'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'Negative Predictive Value (NPV) is:', '["True negatives /","Specificity ×","True positives /","True positives /","True negatives /"]'::jsonb, 'True negatives /', 'NPV = d/(c+d). It is the probability that a person with a negative test truly does NOT have the disease. High NPV is important for ruling out disease.'),
('bbfee22a-b341-5211-adc8-38a9e66ad1df'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'A researcher is conducting an epidemiological study. As disease prevalence decreases, PPV:', '["Decreases","Increases","Remains unchanged","Approaches 100%","Becomes zero"]'::jsonb, 'Decreases', 'PPV is directly related to disease prevalence. When prevalence is low, even a highly specific test will generate more false positives than true positives, lowering PPV.'),
('53e1d93c-3a00-5a8e-b731-2e63eba6a810'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'As disease prevalence increases, NPV:', '["Approaches zero","Increases","Remains unchanged","Becomes 100%","Decreases"]'::jsonb, 'Decreases', 'As prevalence increases, the probability that a negative result is truly negative (NPV) decreases because more people actually have the disease and might be missed (false negatives).'),
('aaeeee53-6241-5cb9-a12f-91dac5818dd3'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'A new diagnostic test for hypertension is being evaluated against the gold standard in 2000 patients. A new diagnostic test for breast cancer is being evaluated against the gold standard in 1000 patients. The formula for PPV can be expressed as:', '["(Sensitivity × Prevalence) / [(Sensitivity × Prevalence) + (1 - Specificity) × (1 - Prevalence)]","Sensitivity / clinical presentation clinical presentation","Prevalence × Odds ratio clinical presentation clinical presentation","Sensitivity × Prevalence clinical presentation clinical presentation","Specificity × clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, '(Sensitivity × Prevalence) / [(Sensitivity × Prevalence) + (1 - Specificity) × (1 - Prevalence)]', 'PPV = (Sens × Prev) / [Sens × Prev + (1 - Spec) × (1 - Prev)]. This formula shows PPV depends on sensitivity, specificity, and prevalence.'),
('ab2cdbfc-63a6-5d4d-8a89-409091a6ebf2'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'Accuracy of a test is:', '["Sensitivity × Specificity","(True positives + True negatives) / Total population","PPV + NPV","Sensitivity + Specificity","(Sensitivity + Specificity) / 2"]'::jsonb, '(True positives + True negatives) / Total population', 'Accuracy = (a+d)/(a+b+c+d). It is the overall proportion of correct classifications (both true positives and true negatives) among all tested individuals.'),
('7e651acd-57ec-597b-9a37-239d107d6a9b'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'A researcher is conducting an epidemiological study. A test with 100% sensitivity will have:', '["100% NPV","100% specificity","No false positives","No false negatives","100% PPV"]'::jsonb, 'No false negatives', '100% sensitivity means the test correctly identifies ALL individuals with the disease (no false negatives). However, there may still be false positives (limited specificity).'),
('8c951946-865b-5a70-a0a9-8d45c339e956'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'A test with 100% specificity will have:', '["No false negatives","No false positives","100% sensitivity","100% PPV","100% NPV"]'::jsonb, 'No false positives', '100% specificity means the test correctly identifies ALL individuals without the disease (no false positives). However, there may still be false negatives (limited sensitivity).'),
('03681ac8-b38f-5e08-9e24-1d2f02619a78'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'A new diagnostic test for hypertension is being evaluated against the gold standard in 100 patients. ROC curve plots:', '["Sensitivity vs Specificity","True positives vs False positives","Prevalence vs Accuracy","Sensitivity vs 1 - Specificity","PPV vs NPV"]'::jsonb, 'Sensitivity vs 1 - Specificity', 'The Receiver Operating Characteristic (ROC) curve plots sensitivity (true positive rate) against 1-specificity (false positive rate) at various threshold values. It assesses overall test performance.'),
('48f50a2e-efdf-5da5-9ebd-4be8ebe2acd9'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'Area Under the ROC Curve (AUC) of 0.9 indicates:', '["Random classification","No discriminatory ability","Excellent discriminatory ability","Poor performance","Perfect test"]'::jsonb, 'Excellent discriminatory ability', 'AUC = 0.9 indicates excellent test performance. AUC ranges from 0.5 (no discrimination, like a coin flip) to 1.0 (perfect discrimination). Generally, AUC > 0.8 is considered good.'),
('4e3c7612-ee62-54d8-a127-bb6b0f4b9a79'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'Youden''s index is calculated as:', '["Sensitivity - Specificity","(Sensitivity + Specificity) / 2","Sensitivity × Specificity","Sensitivity + Specificity - 1","Sensitivity + Specificity"]'::jsonb, 'Sensitivity + Specificity - 1', 'Youden''s index = Sensitivity + Specificity - 1. It ranges from 0 (poor) to 1 (perfect) and is used to select the optimal cut-off point that maximises both sensitivity and specificity.'),
('af97c54e-1e11-5dab-b881-c8c67ec933c5'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'A researcher is conducting an epidemiological study. Lead-time bias in screening refers to:', '["Apparent increase in survival time due to earlier diagnosis, not true prolongation of life","Time between screening tests clinical presentation","Delays in treatment clinical presentation","The time the test takes clinical presentation","Longer life due to treatment clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Apparent increase in survival time due to earlier diagnosis, not true prolongation of life', 'Lead-time bias makes survival appear longer simply because the diagnosis was made earlier, even if the patient dies at the same time they would have without screening. The survival time is artificially inflated.'),
('905ed0f5-57ec-598c-8066-2eee58c25749'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'Length-time bias in screening refers to:', '["Aggressive tumours being missed clinical presentation","Screening being ineffective clinical presentation","Faster-growing tumours being detected clinical presentation","All tumours being detected equally clinical presentation","Screening preferentially detecting slower-growing, less aggressive tumours"]'::jsonb, 'Screening preferentially detecting slower-growing, less aggressive tumours', 'Length-time bias occurs because screening is more likely to detect slow-growing, less aggressive tumours that have a longer preclinical detectable phase. This makes screening appear more beneficial than it truly is.'),
('fdbfaf07-db4c-5017-90fc-f045524412c8'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'A new diagnostic test for tuberculosis is being evaluated against the gold standard in 1000 patients. Overdiagnosis in screening refers to:', '["Detection of all cases of disease","Detection of disease that would never have caused symptoms","False positive results","False negative results","Detection of aggressive disease"]'::jsonb, 'Detection of disease that would never have caused symptoms', 'Overdiagnosis is the detection of a condition that would never have caused symptoms or death. This leads to unnecessary treatment and is a major harm of screening (e.g., some prostate and thyroid cancers).'),
('fd739770-459d-5a0a-ab5e-d35c3bfbe3f8'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'Wilson and Jungner criteria for screening include:', '["The disease should be an important health problem","The disease should be untreatable","The test should be expensive","Screening should be mandatory","The test should be invasive"]'::jsonb, 'The disease should be an important health problem', 'Wilson and Jungner (1968) criteria state that the disease should be an important health problem, have a recognisable latent stage, have an acceptable treatment, and the test should be simple, safe, and inexpensive.'),
('8bb780a8-9ac0-5b28-8e69-bcf7e651f6b5'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'Which is a Wilson and Jungner criterion?', '["The test must be 100% accurate","The disease must be very common","Screening must be annual","All diseases should be screened","There should be an accepted treatment for the disease"]'::jsonb, 'There should be an accepted treatment for the disease', 'One key Wilson and Jungner criterion is that there should be an accepted treatment for the disease. Screening without effective treatment is unethical as it provides no benefit.'),
('f48a5f56-fca5-5876-8234-83a074036672'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'A screening programme should:', '["Do more good than harm","Be performed only once","Replace clinical diagnosis","Be mandatory for all","Use the most expensive tests"]'::jsonb, 'Do more good than harm', 'Screening should have net benefit, meaning the benefits (reduced morbidity/mortality) outweigh the harms (overdiagnosis, false positives, anxiety, cost).'),
('e04c92d1-914a-5408-9c27-13665c4ff507'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'Informed consent for screening requires:', '["Only discussing benefits clinical presentation","Forcing participation clinical presentation","No discussion needed clinical presentation","Only discussing harms clinical presentation","Discussing benefits, harms, and uncertainties"]'::jsonb, 'Discussing benefits, harms, and uncertainties', 'Ethical screening requires informed consent. Participants should understand the potential benefits, risks/harms, and uncertainties before deciding whether to participate.'),
('4bc3f53a-865e-582b-8f12-c9fc875863e2'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'A new diagnostic test for coronary artery disease is being evaluated against the gold standard in 100 patients. A new diagnostic test for coronary artery disease is being evaluated against the gold standard in 200 patients. Cut-off point for a screening test is chosen to:', '["Minimise cost","Maximise speed","Balance sensitivity and specificity","Maximise sensitivity only","Maximise specificity only"]'::jsonb, 'Balance sensitivity and specificity', 'The cut-off point balances sensitivity and specificity based on the consequences of false positives and false negatives. Youden''s index and ROC curves help identify the optimal cut-off.'),
('2c29476e-8f53-539f-8804-d14e060548fc'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'A researcher is conducting an epidemiological study. If false negatives are more dangerous than false positives, the cut-off should favour:', '["Higher sensitivity","Lower cost","Higher NPV","Higher PPV","Higher specificity"]'::jsonb, 'Higher sensitivity', 'When missing a disease (false negative) is dangerous (e.g., cancer), the cut-off should be set to maximise sensitivity. This will increase false positives but minimise missed cases.'),
('842a7586-0903-5adc-aba0-cae22295af50'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'If false positives cause significant harm, the cut-off should favour:', '["Higher NPV","Higher specificity","Higher PPV","Lower cost","Higher sensitivity"]'::jsonb, 'Higher specificity', 'When false positives are dangerous (e.g., invasive follow-up), the cut-off should maximise specificity. This will reduce unnecessary interventions but may miss some cases.'),
('9de82592-ab78-52e3-938c-8ca94654294a'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'A new diagnostic test for coronary artery disease is being evaluated against the gold standard in 100 patients. Multiple screening tests are used:', '["To replace clinical judgment clinical presentation","To create more data clinical presentation","To confuse the diagnosis clinical presentation","When a single test lacks sufficient sensitivity or specificity","To increase cost clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'When a single test lacks sufficient sensitivity or specificity', 'Multiple tests (e.g., sequential or parallel testing) are used to improve overall accuracy. Parallel testing increases sensitivity; sequential testing increases specificity.'),
('7cbaf07b-a4df-5828-938e-c261ed63a30a'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'A new diagnostic test for breast cancer is being evaluated against the gold standard in 500 patients. A new diagnostic test for coronary artery disease is being evaluated against the gold standard in 1000 patients. A new diagnostic test for coronary artery disease is being evaluated against the gold standard in 1000 patients. Parallel testing (multiple tests simultaneously) increases:', '["Sensitivity","NPV","Cost","PPV","Specificity"]'::jsonb, 'Sensitivity', 'Parallel testing performs multiple tests at once - a positive on any test is considered positive. This increases sensitivity (fewer false negatives) but decreases specificity (more false positives).'),
('03c98583-32ea-5d02-b5bb-0bb01eb74ebd'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'Sequential testing (multiple tests in sequence) increases:', '["NPV","Specificity","Accuracy","PPV","Sensitivity"]'::jsonb, 'Specificity', 'Sequential (two-stage) testing performs a second test only if the first is positive. This increases specificity (fewer false positives) but reduces sensitivity. Used in HIV testing.'),
('0269a6aa-5259-5e3a-bd1e-be2d5f9cd022'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'Gold standard in diagnostic testing refers to:', '["The least invasive test clinical presentation","The best available reference test for confirming the disease","The newest test clinical presentation","The fastest test clinical presentation","The most expensive test clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'The best available reference test for confirming the disease', 'The gold standard is the best available method for establishing the presence or absence of disease against which new tests are evaluated. It may be invasive or expensive but provides the most accurate diagnosis.'),
('c1de0a0f-5c25-50de-a426-76c8d0afa202'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'What is PPV?', '["32%","50%","Approximately 68%","95%","90%"]'::jsonb, 'Approximately 68%', 'Using Bayes'' theorem: PPV = (0.95 × 0.10) / [0.95 × 0.10 + (1-0.95) × (1-0.10)] = 0.095 / (0.095 + 0.045) = 0.095/0.14 = 0.679 ≈ 68%.'),
('cbde5756-f4c0-50bd-90e6-2fcc3bce7160'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'In the same scenario (95% sens, 95% spec, 10% prevalence), what is NPV?', '["99.9%","95%","90%","Approximately 99.4%","85%"]'::jsonb, 'Approximately 99.4%', 'NPV = (0.95 × 0.90) / [0.95 × 0.90 + (1-0.95) × 0.10] = 0.855 / (0.855 + 0.005) = 0.855/0.860 = 0.994 ≈ 99.4%.'),
('a99cac3c-025b-5492-9876-dba38eb932c8'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'A new diagnostic test for tuberculosis is being evaluated against the gold standard in 2000 patients. A new diagnostic test for HIV/AIDS is being evaluated against the gold standard in 1000 patients. A new diagnostic test for HIV/AIDS is being evaluated against the gold standard in 1000 patients. If prevalence drops to 1% (with the same 95% sens/spec test), PPV becomes:', '["Approximately 16%","99%","95%","1%","50%"]'::jsonb, 'Approximately 16%', 'PPV = (0.95 × 0.01) / [0.95 × 0.01 + (1-0.95) × (1-0.01)] = 0.0095 / (0.0095 + 0.0495) = 0.0095/0.059 = 0.161 ≈ 16%.'),
('0b22170a-0e0c-5f50-993e-6ab165fec84a'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'If prevalence drops to 1% (same test), NPV becomes:', '["99%","50%","Approximately 99.99%","95%","100%"]'::jsonb, 'Approximately 99.99%', 'NPV = (0.95 × 0.99) / [0.95 × 0.99 + (1-0.95) × 0.01] = 0.9405 / (0.9405 + 0.0005) = 0.9405/0.941 = 0.9995 ≈ 99.95%.'),
('5a02b1e2-81d5-582e-b515-62b49cb62e04'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'A new diagnostic test for hypertension is being evaluated against the gold standard in 100 patients. A new diagnostic test for hypertension is being evaluated against the gold standard in 100 patients. A new diagnostic test for tuberculosis is being evaluated against the gold standard in 100 patients. Likelihood ratio positive (LR+) is calculated as:', '["Sensitivity + Specificity","(1 - Sensitivity) / Specificity","Sensitivity × Specificity","PPV /","Sensitivity /"]'::jsonb, 'Sensitivity /', 'LR+ = Sensitivity/(1 - Specificity). It indicates how much a positive test increases the probability of disease. LR+ > 10 is considered strong evidence to rule in disease.'),
('ce43682a-f01e-5b47-a8fd-2b186b8d4560'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'Likelihood ratio negative (LR-) is calculated as:', '["(1 - Sensitivity) / Specificity","Sensitivity /","NPV /","Sensitivity × Specificity","Specificity /"]'::jsonb, '(1 - Sensitivity) / Specificity', 'LR- = (1 - Sensitivity)/Specificity. It indicates how much a negative test decreases the probability of disease. LR- < 0.1 is considered strong evidence to rule out disease.'),
('b0d071ee-377f-5c0d-ab59-ad1a7edd16b1'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'Pre-test probability plus test results determines:', '["NPV","Specificity","PPV","Sensitivity","Post-test probability"]'::jsonb, 'Post-test probability', 'Post-test probability is derived by combining pre-test probability (prevalence in the clinical context) with the likelihood ratio of the test result. Bayes'' theorem formalises this update.'),
('0181fd66-05a7-5351-9e98-df71afb19181'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'Fagan''s nomogram is used to:', '["Calculate sample size clinical presentation","Conduct meta-analysis clinical presentation","Assess publication bias clinical presentation","Perform survival analysis clinical presentation","Calculate post-test probability from pre-test probability and LR"]'::jsonb, 'Calculate post-test probability from pre-test probability and LR', 'Fagan''s nomogram is a graphical tool that estimates post-test probability by drawing a line from pre-test probability through the likelihood ratio to read the post-test probability.'),
('fd4595f1-ba42-5539-afc7-d62c5f593097'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'Which of the following is a harm of screening?', '["Improved prognosis","Reduced mortality","Early detection","Overdiagnosis and overtreatment","Better treatment outcomes"]'::jsonb, 'Overdiagnosis and overtreatment', 'Harms of screening include overdiagnosis (detecting disease that would never cause symptoms), overtreatment, false positives (anxiety, further testing), false reassurance from false negatives, and cost.'),
('6847f3b5-3310-543b-9bd1-96ca2a0d9967'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'Stage shift in screening refers to:', '["No change in stage","A shift toward earlier-stage disease at diagnosis","A shift to later-stage disease","Decreased survival","Increased mortality"]'::jsonb, 'A shift toward earlier-stage disease at diagnosis', 'Stage shift occurs when screening detects disease at an earlier stage than would have been found clinically. This is necessary for screening to be beneficial but must be distinguished from lead-time bias.'),
('12b9b257-4767-5179-91a1-aea4d293587d'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'A new diagnostic test for breast cancer is being evaluated against the gold standard in 200 patients. A new diagnostic test for HIV/AIDS is being evaluated against the gold standard in 1000 patients. A new diagnostic test for HIV/AIDS is being evaluated against the gold standard in 1000 patients. All-cause mortality is preferred over disease-specific mortality as a screening trial endpoint because:', '["It is cheaper","It is easier to measure","It is more specific","It is not affected by lead-time or misclassification bias","It requires smaller sample sizes"]'::jsonb, 'It is not affected by lead-time or misclassification bias', 'All-cause mortality avoids biases in classifying cause of death and eliminates lead-time bias. However, it requires much larger sample sizes as the effect is diluted by other causes of death.'),
('a7afcc27-54e2-5436-a71e-67eb5e3422fd'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'A researcher is conducting an epidemiological study. A new diagnostic test for HIV/AIDS is being evaluated against the gold standard in 100 patients. A new diagnostic test for diabetes mellitus is being evaluated against the gold standard in 1000 patients. A new diagnostic test for lung cancer is being evaluated against the gold standard in 2000 patients. A new diagnostic test for hypertension is being evaluated against the gold standard in 1000 patients. A screening test with AUC of 0.5 is:', '["Perfect","Excellent","Good","Fair","No better than chance"]'::jsonb, 'No better than chance', 'An AUC of 0.5 means the test performs no better than random guessing (like a coin flip). The ROC curve lies along the diagonal. Such a test has no discriminatory ability.'),
('43771edb-669e-5d5f-b204-e1b73b15d7f2'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'A new diagnostic test for lung cancer is being evaluated against the gold standard in 500 patients. A new diagnostic test for breast cancer is being evaluated against the gold standard in 500 patients. A new diagnostic test for breast cancer is being evaluated against the gold standard in 500 patients. Criterion validity of a screening test refers to:', '["Whether it measures what it intends to measure","How well it compares to the gold standard","Face validity","Content of the test","Consistency of results"]'::jsonb, 'How well it compares to the gold standard', 'Criterion validity (concurrent or predictive) assesses how well the test performs compared to a gold standard. Sensitivity and specificity measure criterion validity.'),
('0ed86070-6544-575a-bc3d-822b10a3d5e9'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'A study follows 2000 patients with hypertension over 1 year to assess survival outcomes. Kappa statistic measures:', '["Agreement beyond chance between observers or tests","Test sensitivity clinical presentation","Correlation clinical presentation","Disease prevalence clinical presentation","Test specificity clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Agreement beyond chance between observers or tests', 'Cohen''s kappa measures inter-rater or test-retest agreement, correcting for agreement expected by chance. Kappa ranges from -1 to 1, with >0.8 considered excellent agreement.'),
('81e22777-d4be-5735-bdb3-c4559b1222ac'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'If two screening tests are used in parallel, the overall sensitivity is:', '["The average of both tests","The same as test A","The same as test B","Higher than either test alone","Lower than either test alone"]'::jsonb, 'Higher than either test alone', 'Parallel testing combines tests so that a positive result on ANY test is considered positive. This increases sensitivity compared to either test alone but decreases specificity.'),
('8cdc0016-4aec-5bf5-b163-515d215d50c5'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'If two screening tests are used in sequence (both must be positive), the overall specificity is:', '["The same as test B","The same as test A","The average of both tests","Higher than either test alone","Lower than either test alone"]'::jsonb, 'Higher than either test alone', 'Sequential testing requires both tests to be positive for an overall positive result. This increases specificity (fewer false positives) but decreases sensitivity (more false negatives).'),
('bdca89e3-4dd4-5a3a-a0c6-115195016f30'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'A new diagnostic test for lung cancer is being evaluated against the gold standard in 1000 patients. A new diagnostic test for breast cancer is being evaluated against the gold standard in 2000 patients. A new diagnostic test for breast cancer is being evaluated against the gold standard in 2000 patients. Which screening programme has demonstrated mortality reduction?', '["CA-125 for ovarian cancer","Mammography for breast cancer","Whole-body CT screening","PSA screening for prostate cancer","PET scans for all cancers"]'::jsonb, 'Mammography for breast cancer', 'Mammography screening has been shown in randomised trials to reduce breast cancer mortality. The evidence for other listed screenings is less clear or shows no benefit.'),
('5e3671b4-e328-54e3-b569-01d88ca93cfd'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'UK National Screening Committee criteria are based on:', '["STROBE guidelines","CONSORT guidelines","Bradford Hill criteria","PRISMA guidelines","Wilson and Jungner criteria"]'::jsonb, 'Wilson and Jungner criteria', 'The UK NSC uses adapted Wilson and Jungner criteria to evaluate screening programmes, considering the condition, test, treatment options, and the screening programme itself.'),
('2d68e11a-c52d-5234-a82b-03d1f75ea793'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'The denominator for calculating sensitivity is:', '["All individuals with positive tests","All screened individuals","All individuals WITHOUT the disease","All individuals WITH the disease","All individuals tested"]'::jsonb, 'All individuals WITH the disease', 'Sensitivity = TP/(TP+FN). The denominator is all individuals who actually HAVE the disease (true positives + false negatives).'),
('09a3d37b-46de-5459-8fd1-5cd23b47e2ef'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology', 'A researcher is conducting an epidemiological study. The denominator for calculating specificity is:', '["All individuals tested","All screened individuals","All individuals WITHOUT the disease","All individuals WITH the disease","All individuals with negative tests"]'::jsonb, 'All individuals WITHOUT the disease', 'Specificity = TN/(TN+FP). The denominator is all individuals who actually do NOT have the disease (true negatives + false positives).'),
('380a3df4-c10f-59f8-a9d2-277fc30e0db2'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'A researcher analyzes data from a clinical study. What is biostatistics?', '["Application of statistics to biological and health sciences","Collection of biological samples clinical presentation","Study of vital signs clinical presentation","Study of living organisms clinical presentation","Analysis of genetic data clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Application of statistics to biological and health sciences', 'Biostatistics is the application of statistical methods to biological, medical, and public health data. It involves designing studies, analysing data, and interpreting results in health research.'),
('897796dc-6bd3-58ed-a2a6-2aa5a134f077'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'The main role of biostatistics in medicine is:', '["To conduct physical exams","To prescribe medications","To draw valid conclusions from medical data","To perform surgery","To take patient histories"]'::jsonb, 'To draw valid conclusions from medical data', 'Biostatistics provides the quantitative foundation for evidence-based medicine by helping researchers design studies, analyse data, and draw valid conclusions from medical research.'),
('e1729d49-0fcd-57e0-b80a-80b802b869a9'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'Statistics has two main branches:', '["Univariate and multivariate","Parametric and non-parametric","Discrete and continuous","Descriptive and inferential","Theoretical and applied"]'::jsonb, 'Descriptive and inferential', 'The two main branches are descriptive statistics (summarising and organising data) and inferential statistics (making predictions and drawing conclusions from data about populations).'),
('4265e855-3b41-5f5d-b0be-fa2c75fa44db'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'A researcher analyzes data from a clinical study. Inferential statistics is used to:', '["Calculate means and medians clinical presentation","Make predictions about a population from a sample","Create tables and charts clinical presentation","Summarise collected data clinical presentation","Compute standard deviations clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Make predictions about a population from a sample', 'Inferential statistics uses sample data to make estimates, test hypotheses, and draw conclusions about populations. It accounts for sampling variation through probability theory.'),
('e3f024bc-0e8f-5052-8f02-50e13473eca8'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'Descriptive statistics involves:', '["Making predictions","Generalising from samples","Testing hypotheses","Summarising and organising data","Drawing causal conclusions"]'::jsonb, 'Summarising and organising data', 'Descriptive statistics summarises data using measures of central tendency (mean, median, mode), dispersion (range, variance, SD), and graphical displays (histograms, bar charts).'),
('338b78e5-2207-5fa7-8594-699539a108cb'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'A population in statistics is:', '["A subset of the group","A sample from the group","A data point","The entire group of interest","A single observation"]'::jsonb, 'The entire group of interest', 'A population is the complete set of individuals, objects, or measurements that share a characteristic of interest. It is the group about which conclusions are drawn.'),
('2c0b4d16-dc29-5093-9d80-58dd195ae9d0'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'A study follows 500 patients with tuberculosis over 5 years to assess survival outcomes. A sample is:', '["The entire population","A subset of the population","A single observation","A statistic","A parameter"]'::jsonb, 'A subset of the population', 'A sample is a subset of the population selected for study. It should be representative of the population to allow valid generalisations (inference).'),
('56685ceb-5cb6-5aca-96b1-340ebbe5a7f8'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'A parameter is a numerical summary of:', '["An observation","A statistic","A population","A sample","A variable"]'::jsonb, 'A population', 'A parameter is a numerical characteristic of a population (e.g., population mean μ, population proportion π). It is usually unknown and estimated from sample data.'),
('6bf0752a-bf36-50fe-905d-29771b184fc1'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'A statistic is a numerical summary of:', '["An inference","A sample","A variable","A parameter","A population"]'::jsonb, 'A sample', 'A statistic is a numerical characteristic computed from sample data (e.g., sample mean x̄, sample proportion p̂). It is used to estimate population parameters.'),
('08f874f1-23c7-58d9-92a8-161aca184ec9'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'Which of the following is a parameter?', '["Population mean","Sample standard deviation","Sample proportion","Sample variance","Sample mean"]'::jsonb, 'Population mean', 'Population mean (μ) is a parameter. Sample statistics (x̄, p̂, s, s²) are estimates of corresponding population parameters.'),
('b0f83b66-ab53-592d-b66d-2556c698fa4c'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'Which of the following is a statistic?', '["Sample mean","Population standard deviation","Population mean","Population proportion","Population variance"]'::jsonb, 'Sample mean', 'Sample mean (x̄) is a statistic calculated from sample data. The others (μ, π, σ², σ) are population parameters.'),
('7fca40c5-020d-56cd-a27a-951676460b75'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'Biostatistics is essential for:', '["Evidence-based medicine","Prescription writing","Patient counselling","Surgical techniques","Physical diagnosis"]'::jsonb, 'Evidence-based medicine', 'Biostatistics underpins evidence-based medicine by providing methods for clinical trials, epidemiological studies, systematic reviews, and meta-analyses that generate reliable evidence.'),
('4a107ce3-0580-5756-a7f2-de124092bd80'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'The first step in statistical investigation is:', '["Collecting data","Interpreting results","Defining the research question","Analysing data","Publishing findings"]'::jsonb, 'Defining the research question', 'The first and most critical step is clearly defining the research question. This guides all subsequent decisions about study design, data collection, sample size, and statistical methods.'),
('f51d6787-b1f2-5ff3-94a0-59d5f1b5bcd4'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'Variable in statistics is:', '["A statistical test clinical presentation","A population parameter clinical presentation","A constant value clinical presentation","A characteristic that can take different values","A fixed number clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'A characteristic that can take different values', 'A variable is any characteristic, number, or quantity that can be measured or counted and varies among subjects. Examples include age, blood pressure, and treatment group.'),
('9ec02690-6d33-5c1a-954e-00ff56b465d9'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'An independent variable is:', '["The constant variable clinical presentation","The variable manipulated or classified by the researcher","The response variable clinical presentation","The dependent variable clinical presentation","The outcome variable clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'The variable manipulated or classified by the researcher', 'The independent variable (predictor or exposure) is manipulated or classified by the researcher to observe its effect on the dependent variable. In an experiment, it is the treatment.'),
('dd66d8ae-a79a-50a1-848e-6cbfd67e760c'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'A dependent variable is:', '["The outcome being measured","The predictor variable","The variable manipulated by the researcher","The controlled variable","The exposure variable"]'::jsonb, 'The outcome being measured', 'The dependent variable (response or outcome) is the outcome that is measured to see if it is affected by changes in the independent variable. It depends on the independent variable.'),
('e2283ecb-1193-5f0b-81f7-19ac2c8850d6'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'Which scale of measurement is blood type (A, B, AB, O)?', '["Nominal","Continuous","Ordinal","Ratio","Interval"]'::jsonb, 'Nominal', 'Blood type is a nominal variable because categories are named but have no natural order. Nominal scales classify data into distinct, unordered categories.'),
('518dbb14-faec-5dee-bfd9-481929419272'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'Which scale of measurement is cancer stage (I, II, III, IV)?', '["Ordinal","Continuous","Nominal","Interval","Ratio"]'::jsonb, 'Ordinal', 'Cancer stage is ordinal because categories have a natural order (increasing severity) but the intervals between stages are not necessarily equal. Ordinal scales rank observations.'),
('c177443a-6aba-5712-933d-aa793b82b053'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'Which scale of measurement is temperature in Celsius?', '["Ratio","Ordinal","Discrete","Interval","Nominal"]'::jsonb, 'Interval', 'Celsius temperature is interval scale - it has equal intervals between values but no true zero (0°C does not mean absence of temperature). Ratios are not meaningful (20°C is not twice as hot as 10°C).'),
('0917b6eb-d46d-53e5-9dbc-5093c64afd21'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'Which scale of measurement is height in centimeters?', '["Categorical","Ratio","Ordinal","Interval","Nominal"]'::jsonb, 'Ratio', 'Height is ratio scale because it has equal intervals AND a true zero point (0 cm means no height). This allows meaningful ratios (100 cm is twice as tall as 50 cm).'),
('a0bc2412-8f45-53fa-86d6-8b4a0ffc5f4f'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'Which scale of measurement is pain score on a 0-10 scale?', '["Nominal","Ordinal","Continuous","Ratio","Interval"]'::jsonb, 'Ordinal', 'A pain score is ordinal because categories have order but the difference between 2 and 3 may not equal the difference between 8 and 9. It is treated as ordinal unless assumed interval.'),
('5caf1df7-07f4-5557-963c-8a4274525008'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'Discrete variables can take:', '["Only decimal values","Any value within a range","Only specific, countable values","Only integer values","Only binary values"]'::jsonb, 'Only specific, countable values', 'Discrete variables take specific, countable values (often integers). Examples include number of children, number of hospital visits. They often arise from counting.'),
('c1304c5f-9851-56d8-8e91-555e345a9dd8'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'Continuous variables can take:', '["Only categorical values","Only binary values","Any value within a range","Only integer values","Only whole numbers"]'::jsonb, 'Any value within a range', 'Continuous variables can take any value within a range (limited by measurement precision). Examples include height, weight, blood pressure, and time.'),
('f25e0354-7b2c-5014-85ff-61389fdd84f3'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'Which is an example of a continuous variable?', '["Gender","Disease stage","Blood pressure in mmHg","Blood group","Number of children"]'::jsonb, 'Blood pressure in mmHg', 'Blood pressure is continuous because it can take any value within a physiological range (e.g., 120.5 mmHg). The other options are discrete or categorical.'),
('613c58af-0b6a-5cb2-95c9-16badc936527'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'Which is an example of a discrete variable?', '["Number of hospital admissions","Height","Temperature","Weight","Blood glucose level"]'::jsonb, 'Number of hospital admissions', 'Number of hospital admissions is a countable, discrete variable (0, 1, 2, etc.). It cannot take fractional values between integers.'),
('aa2bca3c-0328-532d-bcfa-bb544fecf475'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'Qualitative data is also called:', '["Ratio data","Categorical data","Discrete data","Continuous data","Numerical data"]'::jsonb, 'Categorical data', 'Qualitative (categorical) data describes qualities or categories that cannot be measured numerically. Examples include gender, blood group, and disease diagnosis.'),
('dc4e0fbe-bd62-5b1b-956f-eac17ee67095'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'Quantitative data is also called:', '["Numerical data","Categorical data","Ordinal data","Qualitative data","Nominal data"]'::jsonb, 'Numerical data', 'Quantitative (numerical) data can be measured on a numeric scale and subjected to arithmetic operations. It can be discrete or continuous.'),
('5c74d197-f07d-5ebe-900e-9a2bd39de928'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'Binary data is a type of:', '["Categorical data with two categories","Discrete data with many values","Continuous data","Ratio data","Ordinal data"]'::jsonb, 'Categorical data with two categories', 'Binary data has exactly two categories (yes/no, alive/dead, male/female). It is a special case of nominal categorical data and is often analysed using proportions.'),
('cdbee010-374d-5978-b538-f98d5a560322'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'Which type of data is ''blood group (A, B, AB, O)''?', '["Nominal categorical","Interval","Discrete numerical","Ordinal categorical","Continuous numerical"]'::jsonb, 'Nominal categorical', 'Blood group is nominal categorical because the categories have no logical order. Each group is distinct and unordered.'),
('07fe8241-26a5-5cea-b702-0504beb7fd30'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'Which type of data is ''education level (primary, secondary, tertiary)''?', '["Nominal categorical","Discrete numerical","Continuous numerical","Ratio","Ordinal categorical"]'::jsonb, 'Ordinal categorical', 'Education level is ordinal because the categories have a natural order (increasing education) but the gaps between levels are not necessarily equal.'),
('a7980e02-e959-557e-a09e-1054688c49b9'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'A researcher analyzes data from a clinical study. Why is proper data management important in research?', '["To reduce sample size","To make data more complex","To ensure data quality and integrity","To increase variability","To simplify analysis"]'::jsonb, 'To ensure data quality and integrity', 'Proper data management ensures data accuracy, completeness, and consistency. It includes data entry validation, coding, storage, and documentation, which are essential for valid results.'),
('c272101b-2e3b-51b9-9d4e-4e53a67b19fb'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'Double data entry is used to:', '["Increase variability","Halve the time needed","Reduce data entry errors","Eliminate bias","Reduce sample size"]'::jsonb, 'Reduce data entry errors', 'Double data entry involves entering data twice by two different people and comparing entries to identify discrepancies. This significantly reduces data entry errors.'),
('d51ab91f-20a4-5d27-81a6-bd0b36dccd65'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'A researcher analyzes data from a clinical study. Data cleaning involves:', '["Checking for errors and inconsistencies","Making up missing data","Deleting all missing values","Ignoring outliers","Removing all variables"]'::jsonb, 'Checking for errors and inconsistencies', 'Data cleaning is the process of detecting and correcting (or removing) corrupt, inaccurate, or irrelevant records from a dataset. It includes checking range, consistency, and missing values.'),
('4ca627cb-f432-590c-92f1-785d7ab3c405'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'Which software is commonly used for statistical analysis in medical research?', '["PowerPoint","Internet Explorer","Outlook","SPSS","Microsoft Word"]'::jsonb, 'SPSS', 'SPSS (Statistical Package for Social Sciences) is widely used in medical research for statistical analysis. Other common tools include R, Stata, SAS, and Python.'),
('3af960f5-a50e-5f55-860e-b7d82a2e7ae3'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'R programming language is:', '["A free and open-source statistical computing environment","A paid statistical software clinical presentation","A presentation tool clinical presentation","A word processor clinical presentation","Only for data entry clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'A free and open-source statistical computing environment', 'R is a free, open-source programming language and environment for statistical computing and graphics. It is widely used in biostatistics and data science.'),
('8da75f0e-e217-56cb-8b42-2a569ff10663'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'A researcher analyzes data from a clinical study. Measurement error refers to:', '["Missing data clinical presentation","Confounding clinical presentation","The difference between measured value and true value","Sampling bias clinical presentation","Selection bias clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'The difference between measured value and true value', 'Measurement error is the difference between the observed (measured) value and the true value. It can be random (unpredictable) or systematic (biased).'),
('ed5a5f55-05dc-5bcf-beb2-bee829c745b5'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'Reliability of a measurement refers to:', '["Consistency and reproducibility of results","Validity of inferences","Generalisability","Accuracy of measurements","Sample representativeness"]'::jsonb, 'Consistency and reproducibility of results', 'Reliability (precision) refers to the consistency of a measurement when repeated. A reliable measure gives similar results under consistent conditions. It is assessed by test-retest or inter-rater reliability.'),
('c59176b2-e182-5db8-8a1a-5920eb0d8d46'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'A new diagnostic test for diabetes mellitus is being evaluated against the gold standard in 100 patients. A new diagnostic test for hypertension is being evaluated against the gold standard in 2000 patients. A new diagnostic test for hypertension is being evaluated against the gold standard in 2000 patients. Validity of a measurement refers to:', '["Whether the sample size is adequate","Whether results are consistent","Whether the p-value is significant","Whether it measures what it intends to measure","Whether the sample is representative"]'::jsonb, 'Whether it measures what it intends to measure', 'Validity (accuracy) refers to whether an instrument measures what it claims to measure. A valid blood pressure cuff accurately reflects true blood pressure. A measure can be reliable but not valid.'),
('5893337c-4b09-55f0-91d8-9d9828fce6e9'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'Which is NOT a measure of central tendency?', '["Midrange","Mode","Mean","Standard deviation","Median"]'::jsonb, 'Standard deviation', 'Standard deviation is a measure of dispersion, not central tendency. Mean, median, mode, and midrange all describe the centre of a distribution.'),
('7e7d253f-ef05-5ffc-8f93-61c5d56606b8'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'Which is NOT a measure of dispersion?', '["Range","Variance","Interquartile range","Standard deviation","Mean"]'::jsonb, 'Mean', 'Mean is a measure of central tendency, not dispersion. Standard deviation, range, variance, and IQR all describe the spread or variability of data.'),
('601e5528-23df-5ff9-9031-cdd818402da7'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'A researcher analyzes data from a clinical study. Primary data is collected:', '["From published literature clinical presentation","From hospital records clinical presentation","From government databases clinical presentation","From existing sources clinical presentation","Directly by the researcher for a specific purpose"]'::jsonb, 'Directly by the researcher for a specific purpose', 'Primary data is collected firsthand by the researcher for the specific purpose of their study. Examples include surveys, interviews, and clinical measurements.'),
('4fda925e-6df3-5bc8-9c7f-db44ba508a8f'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'A researcher analyzes data from a clinical study. Secondary data is:', '["Data collected firsthand clinical presentation","Data collected by someone else for another purpose","Original research data clinical presentation","Experimental data clinical presentation","Clinical trial data clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Data collected by someone else for another purpose', 'Secondary data is data that was collected by someone else for a different purpose but is being reused for a new research question. Examples include medical records, census data, and registries.'),
('3692dad1-2e5b-58d2-9172-b7db017e91cf'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'A new diagnostic test for HIV/AIDS is being evaluated against the gold standard in 2000 patients. A new diagnostic test for coronary artery disease is being evaluated against the gold standard in 2000 patients. A randomized controlled trial is conducted to evaluate a new drug for tuberculosis. Which is an advantage of primary data?', '["Tailored to the research question","Less time-consuming","Less expensive","Already available","Requires no ethical approval"]'::jsonb, 'Tailored to the research question', 'Primary data is collected specifically for the research question, ensuring that the right variables are measured in the right way. However, it is more time-consuming and expensive.'),
('2adc016e-023b-5c46-b3e1-20071ae44308'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'A researcher analyzes data from a clinical study. Which is an advantage of secondary data?', '["Always accurate","Perfectly fits the research question","No quality issues","Saves time and resources","Always complete"]'::jsonb, 'Saves time and resources', 'Secondary data is already collected and often readily available, saving time, effort, and money. However, it may not perfectly match the research question and may have quality issues.'),
('73e9ba8d-1c1e-5fbf-a59c-dd5558b2df60'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'Researchers design a study to investigate the association between smoking and post-operative complications. Longitudinal data collects:', '["Data at a single point in time","Data from different subjects","Data from the same subjects over time","Aggregate group data","Cross-sectional snapshots"]'::jsonb, 'Data from the same subjects over time', 'Longitudinal data follows the same subjects over multiple time points, allowing analysis of changes and trajectories. It is used in cohort studies and repeated measures designs.'),
('9639618c-e653-5c4f-8084-07de72c37be4'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'A researcher analyzes data from a clinical study. In a population with 10%, the likelihood ratio is: Cross-sectional data collects:', '["Historical records","Data over time","Data from the same subjects repeatedly","Data at a single point in time","Longitudinal trends"]'::jsonb, 'Data at a single point in time', 'Cross-sectional data captures information at one specific point in time. It provides a snapshot of the population but cannot assess change over time or establish temporal sequence.'),
('7099c3e9-0261-5898-9b80-d0e609b5295b'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'Time series data involves:', '["Observations collected sequentially over time","Cross-sectional snapshots","Comparing two groups","Observations at one time point","Aggregate data"]'::jsonb, 'Observations collected sequentially over time', 'Time series data is a sequence of observations collected at regular intervals over time. Examples include daily COVID-19 cases, monthly mortality rates, and annual disease incidence.'),
('52e1922d-a309-5795-b40b-fca19ca69635'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'A researcher analyzes data from a clinical study. Missing data can be classified as:', '["Type I, Type II","Random, systematic","Discrete, continuous","MCAR, MAR, MNAR","Parametric, non-parametric"]'::jsonb, 'MCAR, MAR, MNAR', 'Missing data mechanisms: MCAR (Missing Completely At Random), MAR (Missing At Random), and MNAR (Missing Not At Random). These determine appropriate handling methods.'),
('d08489b5-0620-5f9d-8a8f-77a96b259ba8'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'MCAR (Missing Completely At Random) means:', '["Missingness is systematic","Data is missing due to error","Missingness is unrelated to any variable","Missingness depends on observed data","Missingness depends on unobserved data"]'::jsonb, 'Missingness is unrelated to any variable', 'MCAR means the probability of missing data is the same for all observations, independent of both observed and unobserved data. Listwise deletion may be acceptable under MCAR.'),
('3ad94499-c278-56f0-863a-69d8a95833e9'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'A researcher analyzes data from a clinical study. A new diagnostic test for coronary artery disease is being evaluated against the gold standard in 1000 patients. A new diagnostic test for diabetes mellitus is being evaluated against the gold standard in 100 patients. A new diagnostic test for coronary artery disease is being evaluated against the gold standard in 1000 patients. A new diagnostic test for hypertension is being evaluated against the gold standard in 200 patients. In a population with 10%, the accuracy is: MAR (Missing At Random) means:', '["Missingness is completely random","Missingness is systematic","Missingness is unrelated to anything","Missingness depends on observed data but not on unobserved data","Missingness depends on missing values"]'::jsonb, 'Missingness depends on observed data but not on unobserved data', 'MAR means the probability of missingness depends on observed (known) variables but not on the missing values themselves. Most imputation methods assume MAR.'),
('04eef9a2-9b88-56ef-a868-59f5803d0ca8'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'A randomized controlled trial is conducted to evaluate a screening program for diabetes mellitus. MNAR (Missing Not At Random) means:', '["Missingness depends on the unobserved missing values themselves","Missingness is ignorable","Missingness is completely random","There is no missing data","Missingness depends only on observed data"]'::jsonb, 'Missingness depends on the unobserved missing values themselves', 'MNAR is the most problematic type - the probability of missingness depends on the value that would have been observed. Example: people with severe depression not reporting their depression score.'),
('0710aa65-7ce1-59d7-aa0d-e186fea4f86f'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'Which method is appropriate for handling missing data under MAR?', '["Multiple imputation","Delete all variables","Replace with zero","Mean imputation","Listwise deletion"]'::jsonb, 'Multiple imputation', 'Multiple imputation is a principled method that creates multiple completed datasets, analyses each, and combines results. It is valid under MAR and properly accounts for imputation uncertainty.'),
('9ae23285-e225-5f68-911b-ccae3d8287e4'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'Listwise deletion (complete case analysis):', '["Ignores missing data","Replaces missing values","Removes any case with missing data","Creates new data","Deletes variables with missing data"]'::jsonb, 'Removes any case with missing data', 'Listwise deletion excludes all cases with any missing data from analysis. It can introduce bias if data are not MCAR and reduces sample size and statistical power.'),
('d41e28f7-1b48-5c11-8b63-94e950b93c40'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'Mean imputation replaces missing values with:', '["The median of observed values","The maximum value","Zero","The mode","The mean of observed values for that variable"]'::jsonb, 'The mean of observed values for that variable', 'Mean imputation replaces each missing value with the mean of the observed values for that variable. It is simple but distorts the distribution, reduces variability, and weakens associations.'),
('f439ae56-cea3-5306-aa00-f0995b7e0d27'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'Coding in data management refers to:', '["Data collection clinical presentation","Decrypting data clinical presentation","Statistical analysis clinical presentation","Assigning numerical values to categorical data","Writing computer programs clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Assigning numerical values to categorical data', 'Coding is the process of assigning numerical codes to categorical data (e.g., male=1, female=2). This allows statistical analysis of categorical variables.'),
('35a98fb3-5a81-5366-b52e-42a0b8ee5077'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'A researcher analyzes data from a clinical study. A codebook in research includes:', '["Only the raw data clinical presentation","Statistical test results clinical presentation","The consent form clinical presentation","Variable names, labels, and coding instructions","The research paper clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Variable names, labels, and coding instructions', 'A codebook documents variable names, labels, values, coding, and data collection details. It is essential for data management, analysis, and reproducibility.'),
('af6b8b5e-bab0-51cc-85a1-3d4661c53082'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'A researcher analyzes data from a clinical study. Range checks in data cleaning verify:', '["The data is normally distributed","The sample size is adequate","The statistical test is valid","The conclusion is correct","Values fall within plausible limits"]'::jsonb, 'Values fall within plausible limits', 'Range checks identify values that fall outside plausible or pre-specified limits (e.g., age > 120 years). These may indicate data entry errors.'),
('60e42925-05de-57f8-89d5-7126a679f212'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'Consistency checks verify:', '["The data is complete","The sample is random","The p-value is correct","The data is normal","Logical relationships between variables"]'::jsonb, 'Logical relationships between variables', 'Consistency checks ensure logical relationships hold (e.g., date of death should not precede date of birth, pregnant should be coded as no for males). These detect data entry errors.'),
('23e815a7-2b6e-5a24-81bb-8f8fffeb88ba'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'Outliers in data are:', '["Average values clinical presentation","Missing values clinical presentation","The most common values clinical presentation","The median values clinical presentation","Extreme values that differ markedly from others"]'::jsonb, 'Extreme values that differ markedly from others', 'Outliers are observations that lie an abnormal distance from other values. They may be data entry errors, genuine extreme values, or indicate a different distribution.'),
('2f49cf97-f289-5b47-9ac3-368912e0189b'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'Which plot is best for identifying outliers?', '["Box plot","Frequency table","Bar chart","Stem and leaf plot","Pie chart"]'::jsonb, 'Box plot', 'Box plots (box-and-whisker plots) visually display outliers as points beyond the whiskers (typically >1.5 × IQR from the quartiles). They are excellent for identifying extreme values.'),
('80199857-2a97-53ee-b765-a32cb3b929b8'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'Data transformation is used to:', '["Increase variability","Normalise skewed data","Reduce sample size","Create missing data","Delete data"]'::jsonb, 'Normalise skewed data', 'Data transformation (e.g., log, square root, Box-Cox) changes the scale of data to meet statistical assumptions, particularly normality. Log transformation is common for right-skewed data.'),
('0b5897c1-e73c-53ec-8ece-7988a632771f'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'Standardisation (z-score) transforms data to have:', '["Mean = 50, SD = 10","Mean = 100, SD = 15","Mean = 0, SD = 1","Mean = median","Mean = 1, SD = 0"]'::jsonb, 'Mean = 0, SD = 1', 'Z-score = (X - μ)/σ. After standardisation, the distribution has mean 0 and standard deviation 1. This allows comparison across different scales.'),
('3917bf7c-bf08-5874-8d52-b29f57df5ca7'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'Which of the following is multivariate data?', '["Repeated measurements clinical presentation","Multiple variables measured on the same subject","Data with two variables clinical presentation","Aggregate data clinical presentation","Data with one variable clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Multiple variables measured on the same subject', 'Multivariate data involves multiple variables measured on each subject. Analysis includes methods like multiple regression, factor analysis, and MANOVA that handle intercorrelated variables.'),
('b3efd290-4fce-5a8a-b137-d869b54e9c91'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'A researcher analyzes data from a clinical study. Univariate data involves:', '["One variable measured on each subject","Multiple variables","Multidimensional data","Two variables","Longitudinal data"]'::jsonb, 'One variable measured on each subject', 'Univariate data has one variable of interest per subject. Analysis includes descriptive statistics, one-sample tests, and simple frequency distributions.'),
('49b83be9-eee7-5246-86df-796bf98ddbee'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'Bivariate data involves:', '["One variable clinical presentation","Two variables measured on each subject","Three variables clinical presentation","Multiple variables clinical presentation","Four variables clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Two variables measured on each subject', 'Bivariate data measures two variables per subject, allowing analysis of the relationship between them. Examples include correlation, regression, and paired t-tests.'),
('28c88f32-e3bd-5d93-9c37-48590bbbc2ea'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'Which type of data is ''age in years''?', '["Ordinal categorical","Discrete numerical","Binary","Continuous numerical","Nominal categorical"]'::jsonb, 'Continuous numerical', 'Age in years is continuous numerical (ratio scale) because it can take any value within a range and has a meaningful zero point.'),
('f89686df-3df9-5f02-9e9e-92e036cce427'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'Which type of data is ''number of pregnancies''?', '["Continuous numerical","Nominal categorical","Discrete numerical","Interval","Ordinal categorical"]'::jsonb, 'Discrete numerical', 'Number of pregnancies (gravida) is discrete numerical because it can only take whole number values (0, 1, 2, etc.) and arises from counting.'),
('539e3a75-0661-5f67-8892-c42a1b965b97'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'Researchers design a study to investigate the association between obesity and post-operative complications. Panel data is a type of:', '["Longitudinal data","Time series data","Cross-sectional data","Aggregate data","Experimental data"]'::jsonb, 'Longitudinal data', 'Panel data follows the same individuals or units over multiple time points. It combines cross-sectional and time series aspects. Examples include cohort studies and longitudinal surveys.'),
('b67f81e2-949f-5169-91f8-3a9e00c2e1ba'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'A researcher analyzes data from a clinical study. Aggregate data refers to:', '["Subject-level data","Individual-level data","Data summarised at the group level","Patient-level data","Raw data"]'::jsonb, 'Data summarised at the group level', 'Aggregate data (ecological data) summarises information at the group or population level (e.g., country-level obesity rates). It is used in ecological studies and is subject to the ecological fallacy.'),
('9b829b45-726d-5fdd-b0a2-50de1836467f'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'Meta-analysis combines:', '["Only RCT results","Qualitative research","Only observational studies","Individual participant data","Results from multiple studies"]'::jsonb, 'Results from multiple studies', 'Meta-analysis is a statistical method that combines quantitative results from multiple studies on the same topic to produce a pooled estimate. It increases statistical power and precision.'),
('3da6d383-4b6c-5b19-a38a-3684a488a888'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'Frequency distribution organises data by:', '["Showing how often each value occurs","Showing outliers clinical presentation","Showing trends clinical presentation","Showing the median clinical presentation","Showing the mean clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Showing how often each value occurs', 'A frequency distribution summarises data by listing values or categories and their corresponding frequencies (counts). It provides an overview of the data pattern.'),
('ebf9dd25-3752-51ba-857e-fcbc8e43a4bc'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'A histogram is used to display:', '["Correlation between variables","Distribution of continuous data","Categorical data comparisons","Trends over time","Proportions of a whole"]'::jsonb, 'Distribution of continuous data', 'A histogram displays the distribution of continuous data by grouping values into bins (intervals) and showing frequencies as bars. It reveals shape, central tendency, and spread.'),
('af506530-ff81-59ee-8eb9-20db44aaab32'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'A bar chart is appropriate for:', '["Correlation","Time series data","Continuous data","Categorical data","Distribution shape"]'::jsonb, 'Categorical data', 'Bar charts display categorical data with bars representing frequencies or proportions. The bars do not touch, emphasising the discrete nature of categories.'),
('8b2efcf4-7f06-50eb-a77c-4a5f64fb2c98'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'A researcher analyzes data from a clinical study. A pie chart is best for displaying:', '["Relationship between variables","Frequency distribution","Distribution of continuous data","Trends over time","Proportions of a whole"]'::jsonb, 'Proportions of a whole', 'Pie charts show proportions or percentages of a whole using sectors of a circle. They are best for a small number of categories and when parts sum to 100%.'),
('039d7771-38fe-5320-a572-255d6a941bee'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'A scatter plot is used to show:', '["Trends over time","Distribution of one variable","Frequency distribution","Categorical comparisons","Relationship between two continuous variables"]'::jsonb, 'Relationship between two continuous variables', 'Scatter plots display pairs of values (x,y) for each observation, revealing patterns, trends, and correlations between two continuous variables.'),
('7413d596-f555-50ea-90c7-a5ec1ad46ce5'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'A box plot displays:', '["Median, quartiles, and outliers","Mean and standard deviation","Trends over time","Correlation","Frequency distribution"]'::jsonb, 'Median, quartiles, and outliers', 'A box plot shows the median (line within box), interquartile range (box), and potential outliers (points beyond whiskers). It provides a five-number summary: min, Q1, median, Q3, max.'),
('a2ca93e8-3107-55ff-938f-37f6ade05159'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'A stem-and-leaf plot:', '["Shows correlation clinical presentation","Shows only summary statistics clinical presentation","Shows distribution while retaining individual data values","Shows trends clinical presentation","Shows categorical data clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Shows distribution while retaining individual data values', 'A stem-and-leaf plot organises data to show the distribution shape while preserving each value. The stem represents leading digits and leaves represent trailing digits.'),
('f311bc2c-e9dc-587f-aeb6-f96a9890303a'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'A frequency polygon is created by:', '["Plotting points on axes","Connecting midpoints of histogram bins with lines","Drawing bars","Connecting cumulative frequencies","Dividing a circle into sectors"]'::jsonb, 'Connecting midpoints of histogram bins with lines', 'A frequency polygon uses points at the midpoints of histogram bins connected by straight lines. It is useful for comparing distributions of multiple groups overlayed.'),
('ba4ea125-9234-56f9-99c9-17480e927320'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'Researchers design a study to investigate the association between occupational dust exposure and cardiovascular events. Cumulative frequency shows:', '["The mode clinical presentation","The median value clinical presentation","The proportion of each value clinical presentation","The frequency of each value clinical presentation","The total number of observations up to a certain value"]'::jsonb, 'The total number of observations up to a certain value', 'Cumulative frequency at a point is the sum of frequencies for all values up to and including that point. The cumulative frequency curve (ogive) helps estimate percentiles.'),
('98353136-9f22-54a8-ae17-867f0c774915'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'Ogive is a graph of:', '["Time series","Regression","Correlation","Cumulative frequency distribution","Simple frequency distribution"]'::jsonb, 'Cumulative frequency distribution', 'An ogive (cumulative frequency curve) plots cumulative frequency against the upper class boundaries. It is used to estimate percentiles and the median graphically.'),
('29dc5acd-60dd-5dc7-80ea-988d6db889b3'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'Grouped frequency distribution is used when:', '["Data has few distinct values","Data has many distinct values","Data is nominal","Data is categorical","Data is binary"]'::jsonb, 'Data has many distinct values', 'Grouped frequency distribution organises continuous data with many distinct values into classes or intervals (e.g., 0-9, 10-19). This simplifies the data for analysis.'),
('e220d0b3-f2a9-59a8-90a4-2494388b792d'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'Class interval width should be:', '["Different for each class","The same as the sample size","Arbitrary","Equal across all classes when possible","The range divided by 2"]'::jsonb, 'Equal across all classes when possible', 'Equal class intervals are preferred for consistency and interpretability. The number of classes is typically between 5 and 15, determined by the range and sample size.'),
('a48f83b0-09b4-5658-9edf-05330199a4a9'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'In a population with 10%, the accuracy is: Relative frequency is:', '["Cumulative frequency","The raw count","The mode","Frequency multiplied by 100","Frequency divided by total observations"]'::jsonb, 'Frequency divided by total observations', 'Relative frequency = frequency / total number of observations. It expresses the proportion of observations in each category. Multiplying by 100 gives percentage.'),
('1c2704b4-47d0-57a0-a790-1f6764e0a9c2'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'A contingency table is used to:', '["Show time trends","Display correlation","Show regression results","Display the relationship between two categorical variables","Display continuous data distribution"]'::jsonb, 'Display the relationship between two categorical variables', 'A contingency table (cross-tabulation) shows the joint distribution of two or more categorical variables. It is the basis for chi-square tests of association.'),
('64d85c59-af32-51f3-884e-fdd58b745c89'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'A researcher analyzes data from a clinical study. Which graph is best for comparing multiple groups on a continuous variable?', '["Histogram","Scatter plot","Side-by-side box plots","Bar chart","Pie chart"]'::jsonb, 'Side-by-side box plots', 'Side-by-side box plots allow visual comparison of distributions across groups, showing median, spread, and outliers for each group. They facilitate identification of group differences.'),
('77f28144-5dcb-50b9-94d5-6ffe151ff334'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'A researcher analyzes data from a clinical study. A study follows 200 patients with coronary artery disease over 2 years to assess survival outcomes. Which graph is best for showing the trend of a variable over time?', '["Box plot","Bar chart","Line graph","Pie chart","Histogram"]'::jsonb, 'Line graph', 'Line graphs connect data points in chronological order to display trends over time. Time is on the x-axis and the variable of interest on the y-axis.'),
('76ce48bd-bbe7-58eb-bb24-7c8472c4a9ea'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'A researcher analyzes data from a clinical study. A population pyramid is a type of:', '["Pie chart","Bar chart","Box plot","Back-to-back histogram","Scatter plot"]'::jsonb, 'Back-to-back histogram', 'A population pyramid displays the age-sex distribution of a population using back-to-back horizontal histograms (male on left, female on right). It reveals demographic patterns.'),
('3a4d2d38-fa64-59df-ba9f-a18c01490ade'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'A researcher analyzes data from a clinical study. The mode is:', '["The square root of variance","The difference between highest and lowest","The most frequent value in a dataset","The middle value","The arithmetic average"]'::jsonb, 'The most frequent value in a dataset', 'The mode is the value that appears most frequently in a dataset. It is the only measure of central tendency for nominal data.'),
('e9341e48-0824-5113-b2a0-1b7eb619e4fe'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'A bimodal distribution has:', '["A flat shape","One peak","No peak","A skewed shape","Two distinct peaks"]'::jsonb, 'Two distinct peaks', 'A bimodal distribution has two distinct peaks (modes), suggesting the data may come from two different populations. Example: distribution of heights in a mixed male-female population.'),
('29bdea3d-e085-56bd-93e8-eb36a5598642'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'Skewness describes:', '["The spread of data","The asymmetry of a distribution","The peakedness of a distribution","The variability of data","The centre of data"]'::jsonb, 'The asymmetry of a distribution', 'Skewness measures the asymmetry of a distribution. Positive (right) skew has a longer tail on the right; negative (left) skew has a longer tail on the left.'),
('7b985cdf-1607-54c6-be12-007c1f5e5496'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'A researcher analyzes data from a clinical study. Kurtosis describes:', '["The asymmetry clinical presentation","The central tendency clinical presentation","The spread clinical presentation","The peakedness or tail heaviness of a distribution","The sample size clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'The peakedness or tail heaviness of a distribution', 'Kurtosis indicates how peaked a distribution is and how heavy its tails are. High kurtosis (leptokurtic) means more data in the tails and a sharper peak than normal.'),
('41eb088b-9b33-5b2b-8864-23616cd826c9'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'A positively skewed distribution has:', '["Mean > Median","Mode > Mean","Mean = Median","Median > Mode","Mean < Median"]'::jsonb, 'Mean > Median', 'In positive (right) skew, the tail extends to the right, pulling the mean to the right of the median. The order is typically: mode < median < mean.'),
('68b46dea-5055-5f17-89b5-75157e84c34b'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'A negatively skewed distribution has:', '["Mean = Median","Mean > Median","Median < Mode","Mode < Mean","Mean < Median"]'::jsonb, 'Mean < Median', 'In negative (left) skew, the tail extends to the left, pulling the mean to the left of the median. The order is typically: mean < median < mode.'),
('12a214b5-6090-54ef-8db4-060c312f846b'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'A researcher analyzes data from a clinical study. In a symmetric distribution:', '["Mean > Median","Standard deviation is zero","Mean = Median = Mode","Mean < Median","Mode > Mean"]'::jsonb, 'Mean = Median = Mode', 'In a perfectly symmetric distribution (like the normal distribution), the mean, median, and mode are equal. The distribution is balanced around the centre.'),
('3a3f46a3-b5a5-5101-9811-a1b956db889a'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'Which measure of central tendency is appropriate for nominal data?', '["Mode","Median","Mean","Variance","Standard deviation"]'::jsonb, 'Mode', 'The mode is the only measure of central tendency that can be used with nominal (categorical) data. Mean and median require numerical data.'),
('73fb1def-0307-54cf-9971-87dd2c25091f'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'A researcher analyzes data from a clinical study. The median is defined as:', '["The most frequent value","The middle value when data is ordered","The range divided by 2","The arithmetic average","The square root of variance"]'::jsonb, 'The middle value when data is ordered', 'The median is the value that divides the ordered dataset into two equal halves. It is the 50th percentile and is not affected by extreme values (outliers).'),
('bc9e8745-a2c0-5523-b66e-e7f9895bcffe'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'A researcher analyzes data from a clinical study. To find the median, the first step is:', '["Calculate the mean","Identify the mode","Compute the standard deviation","Draw a histogram","Order the data from lowest to highest"]'::jsonb, 'Order the data from lowest to highest', 'The first step in finding the median is arranging data in ascending order. For n observations, the median is the (n+1)/2 th value for odd n, or the average of the two middle values for even n.'),
('ec5a451f-1524-5c51-91be-79e15dbbd823'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'The mean is calculated by:', '["Sum of all values divided by the number of values","The square root of variance clinical presentation","The most frequent value clinical presentation","Half the range clinical presentation","The middle value clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Sum of all values divided by the number of values', 'The arithmetic mean (average) = sum of all observations divided by the number of observations (x̄ = Σx/n). It is sensitive to outliers.'),
('ae96d0a4-aabd-59ec-80c0-dee23eea542e'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'A researcher analyzes data from a clinical study. Which measure of central tendency is most affected by outliers?', '["Mean","All are equally affected","Midrange","Median","Mode"]'::jsonb, 'Mean', 'The mean is most affected by outliers because it incorporates every value. A single extreme value can substantially shift the mean. The median is resistant to outliers.'),
('5d364aba-4bde-53c3-bcb5-7bc8c0e50a94'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'The trimmed mean is:', '["The mode clinical presentation","The geometric mean clinical presentation","The mean calculated after removing a percentage of extreme values","The median clinical presentation","The mean of all data clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'The mean calculated after removing a percentage of extreme values', 'The trimmed mean removes a specified percentage of the smallest and largest values before calculating the mean. It combines the efficiency of the mean with robustness against outliers.'),
('1d1d44e7-41d0-5ad5-85d9-5619012f0b58'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'Which measure of central tendency is best for describing income data?', '["Mean","Median","Standard deviation","Mode","Range"]'::jsonb, 'Median', 'Income data is typically right-skewed due to high earners. The median is preferred because it is not affected by extreme high values and better represents the typical income.'),
('432e5298-1cdc-5b0b-b941-7a37837f4e2f'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'A researcher analyzes data from a clinical study. Geometric mean is used for:', '["Categorical data clinical presentation","Logarithmic data clinical presentation","Exponential growth clinical presentation","Normal distributions clinical presentation","Rates, ratios, and percentage changes"]'::jsonb, 'Rates, ratios, and percentage changes', 'The geometric mean is appropriate for data that are ratios, rates, or change over time (e.g., growth rates, drug titrations). It is calculated as the nth root of the product of n values.'),
('ae0f6152-0037-57fe-bae6-39886c123d8c'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'A researcher analyzes data from a clinical study. Harmonic mean is appropriate for:', '["Normal distributions","Averaging rates and speeds","Binary outcomes","Categorical data","Symmetric data"]'::jsonb, 'Averaging rates and speeds', 'The harmonic mean is used for averaging rates, particularly when the data involves ratios with different denominators (e.g., average speed when distances are equal).'),
('1cc1e89c-6526-5c09-bb40-0b4e5dacba66'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'Standard deviation measures:', '["The total variability clinical presentation","The range clinical presentation","The average distance of values from the mean","The most frequent value clinical presentation","The middle value clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'The average distance of values from the mean', 'Standard deviation is the square root of the variance. It represents the average distance of each data point from the mean. A larger SD indicates greater spread.'),
('2c5871d9-ccea-5a9c-838b-934d89f71226'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'Variance is defined as:', '["The mean deviation","The range squared","The sum of deviations from the mean","The average squared deviation from the mean","The square root of the standard deviation"]'::jsonb, 'The average squared deviation from the mean', 'Variance = Σ(x - x̄)²/(n-1) for sample variance. It is the average of the squared differences from the mean. Standard deviation = √variance.'),
('9612ef98-2b83-5204-b045-5a0fc1e196c9'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'Range is calculated as:', '["Standard deviation squared","Maximum value - Minimum value","Sum of all values divided by n","Mean - Median","Upper quartile - Lower quartile"]'::jsonb, 'Maximum value - Minimum value', 'Range = Maximum - Minimum. It is the simplest measure of dispersion but is highly sensitive to outliers and only uses two extreme values.'),
('aea72af7-6ba7-508d-87d6-7b1aedad4094'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'Interquartile range (IQR) is:', '["Q2 - Q1","Q3 - Q2","Maximum - Minimum","Q3 - Q1","Q1 - Q3"]'::jsonb, 'Q3 - Q1', 'IQR = Q3 - Q1, where Q1 is the 25th percentile and Q3 is the 75th percentile. IQR contains the middle 50% of the data and is resistant to outliers.'),
('ee002536-816a-56ff-a534-7a0753a95eea'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'A researcher analyzes data from a clinical study. The coefficient of variation (CV) is:', '["Mean/SD²","SD/Mean","SD²/Mean","(SD/Mean) × 100%","Mean/SD"]'::jsonb, '(SD/Mean) × 100%', 'CV = (SD/Mean) × 100%. It is a relative measure of dispersion that allows comparison of variability across datasets with different units or means. A higher CV indicates greater relative variability.'),
('5886236a-3724-5113-93f9-e7fdd8ed0b5c'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'A researcher analyzes data from a clinical study. For a dataset with mean = 50 and SD = 5, the CV is:', '["0.1%","25%","5%","10%","50%"]'::jsonb, '10%', 'CV = (5/50) × 100% = 10%. This means the standard deviation is 10% of the mean. CV is unitless and useful for comparing variability across different measurements.'),
('751bdb30-7d5c-5a02-9de6-9b8764d45c36'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'Which measure of dispersion is not affected by outliers?', '["Mean deviation","Range","Standard deviation","Interquartile range","Variance"]'::jsonb, 'Interquartile range', 'IQR is resistant to outliers because it is based on percentiles (Q1 and Q3) and ignores extreme values. Range, SD, and variance are all influenced by outliers.'),
('cf42ff57-8482-5e91-a0de-2cc3434be4a9'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'Empirical rule for normal distribution states:', '["68% of data within ±1 SD, 95% within ±2 SD, 99.7% within ±3 SD","50% within ±1 SD clinical presentation","100% within ±2 SD clinical presentation","95% within ±1 SD clinical presentation","68% within ±2 SD clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, '68% of data within ±1 SD, 95% within ±2 SD, 99.7% within ±3 SD', 'The empirical rule (68-95-99.7 rule) states that for a normal distribution: 68% of data falls within 1 SD, 95% within 2 SD, and 99.7% within 3 SD of the mean.'),
('a3b145eb-af87-5a02-a666-982dbd74597c'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'Chebyshev''s inequality states that for any distribution:', '["At least 1 - 1/k² of data lies within k standard deviations","At least 95% lies within 2 SD clinical presentation","All data lies within 3 SD clinical presentation","Exactly 50% lies within 1 SD clinical presentation","At least 68% lies within 1 SD clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'At least 1 - 1/k² of data lies within k standard deviations', 'Chebyshev''s inequality applies to ANY distribution. For k=2, at least 1-1/4 = 75% of data lies within 2 SD. For k=3, at least 1-1/9 = 89% within 3 SD.'),
('03f959d4-56c1-5e31-89cc-22fdb03356e3'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'Standard error of the mean (SEM) is:', '["SD × √n","n/SD","SD/√n","√","SD/n"]'::jsonb, 'SD/√n', 'SEM = SD/√n. It measures the precision of the sample mean as an estimate of the population mean. SEM decreases as sample size increases.'),
('41f22f81-1263-5dd1-80d9-2b221310e288'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'As sample size increases, SEM:', '["Becomes zero","Stays the same","Approaches infinity","Decreases","Increases"]'::jsonb, 'Decreases', 'SEM = SD/√n. As n increases, the denominator increases, so SEM decreases. Larger samples provide more precise estimates of the population mean.'),
('3c394854-611e-5b30-a4b2-a81ae29d6df6'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'Mean deviation (MD) is:', '["The sum of squared deviations","The square root of variance","The average of absolute deviations from the mean","The range divided by 2","The median of deviations"]'::jsonb, 'The average of absolute deviations from the mean', 'Mean deviation = Σ|x - x̄|/n. It is the average absolute distance from the mean. Unlike variance, it uses absolute values, not squares.'),
('ada4e9ba-c156-50d2-b3f2-b1174d6b99c1'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'Which statement about variance is true?', '["Variance is always negative","Variance is the median","Variance equals the range","Variance is in the original units","Variance is in squared units"]'::jsonb, 'Variance is in squared units', 'Variance is expressed in squared units of the original measurement (e.g., kg² for weight). This is why standard deviation (square root of variance) is preferred for interpretability.'),
('533dae1b-cf27-504d-89a5-dd3d0203d132'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'The sum of deviations from the mean is always:', '["Zero","Equal to variance","Positive","Negative","Depends on the data"]'::jsonb, 'Zero', 'The sum of (x - x̄) always equals zero because the positive and negative deviations cancel out. This is why we square deviations to calculate variance.'),
('682ae796-4728-5750-ba76-5c310431e97f'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'Degrees of freedom for sample variance (n-1) is used because:', '["It provides an unbiased estimate of population variance","It simplifies calculations clinical presentation","It is required by software clinical presentation","It minimises variance clinical presentation","It maximises variance clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'It provides an unbiased estimate of population variance', 'Using n-1 (Bessel''s correction) instead of n provides an unbiased estimate of the population variance. This corrects for the fact that deviations are calculated from the sample mean, not the true population mean.'),
('9f9acb94-b419-50c6-9b6c-8f84707e3af9'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'Percentiles divide data into:', '["5 equal parts","100 equal parts","2 equal parts","10 equal parts","4 equal parts"]'::jsonb, '100 equal parts', 'Percentiles divide ordered data into 100 equal parts. The kth percentile is the value below which k% of observations fall. The 50th percentile is the median.'),
('0d195b54-9e28-59f4-8128-d2c6f4c8913a'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'Quartiles divide data into:', '["10 equal parts","100 equal parts","5 equal parts","2 equal parts","4 equal parts"]'::jsonb, '4 equal parts', 'Quartiles divide ordered data into 4 equal parts. Q1 = 25th percentile, Q2 = median (50th percentile), Q3 = 75th percentile. IQR = Q3 - Q1.'),
('e9ca2bfa-6200-5cc3-9d46-049c7453fad7'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'Deciles divide data into:', '["10 equal parts","100 equal parts","5 equal parts","4 equal parts","2 equal parts"]'::jsonb, '10 equal parts', 'Deciles divide ordered data into 10 equal parts. D1 = 10th percentile, D5 = 50th percentile (median), D9 = 90th percentile.'),
('4bf5b530-b641-5030-b443-fed3a29d0295'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'Z-score is calculated as:', '["SD /","X / SD","(X - Mean) / SD","(X - Mean) × SD","(Mean - X) / SD"]'::jsonb, '(X - Mean) / SD', 'Z-score = (X - μ)/σ. It indicates how many standard deviations an observation is from the mean. Z > 0 means above the mean; Z < 0 means below the mean.'),
('fda736e6-ab7c-5d48-a2f5-403de69ee8e4'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'A Z-score of +2.0 means:', '["The value is 2 standard deviations below the mean","The value is 2 times the mean","The value is 2 standard deviations above the mean","The value is 2 units above the mean","The value equals the mean"]'::jsonb, 'The value is 2 standard deviations above the mean', 'A Z-score of +2.0 indicates the observation is exactly 2 standard deviations above the mean. In a normal distribution, this is approximately the 97.5th percentile.'),
('0a5c4bb4-8468-5aa2-b0bd-cc350e8405a4'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'A set of data with zero variance has:', '["Maximum variability","Normal distribution","All different values","All identical values","Equal mean and median"]'::jsonb, 'All identical values', 'Variance = 0 when all values in the dataset are identical (no spread). Every observation equals the mean, so there is no variation.'),
('8d21aacf-7dc5-54dc-a1ec-a00453dab855'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'Mean absolute deviation (MAD) is:', '["Σ(x - x̄)² / n","Σ(x - x̄) / n","Σ|x - x̄| / n","√[Σ(x - x̄)²/n]","(Σ|x - x̄|)²"]'::jsonb, 'Σ|x - x̄| / n', 'MAD = Σ|x - x̄|/n. It measures the average absolute deviation from the mean. It is less sensitive to extreme values than variance because it does not square deviations.'),
('d3ad24d1-db77-5504-8e8c-8501240c2ac8'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'Which is true about standard deviation?', '["SD = Variance/2","SD = √","SD = Σ(x - x̄)²","SD = Variance²","SD = √Variance"]'::jsonb, 'SD = √Variance', 'Standard deviation is the positive square root of the variance. This returns the measure to the original units of measurement, making it interpretable.'),
('82c3a518-8dcd-5ee8-bc75-4500ed8467b1'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'In a right-skewed distribution:', '["Mean = Median","Mean = Mode","Median > Q3","Mean > Median","Mean < Median"]'::jsonb, 'Mean > Median', 'In right (positive) skew, the tail on the right pulls the mean to the right of the median. The mean is most affected by the extreme high values.'),
('54966e0e-7bd3-5190-baa5-ebf324ecfd31'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'For ordinal data, the most appropriate measure of central tendency is:', '["Median","Mean","Mode","Variance","Standard deviation"]'::jsonb, 'Median', 'The median is appropriate for ordinal data because it uses only the ordering of values. The mean requires equal intervals (interval/ratio data).'),
('5a5d92cf-c4c2-57a7-b3be-7f929f5e8abf'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'Winsorized mean involves:', '["Taking log of values clinical presentation","Removing extreme values clinical presentation","Replacing extreme values with less extreme values","Squaring values clinical presentation","Adding a constant to values clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Replacing extreme values with less extreme values', 'Winsorized mean replaces extreme values (e.g., top and bottom 5%) with the nearest non-extreme value rather than trimming them. This reduces outlier influence.'),
('a31014eb-9105-5662-972c-c0efe97f1c22'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'A researcher analyzes data from a clinical study. Midrange is calculated as:', '["Maximum - Minimum","(Minimum + Maximum) / 2","Minimum + Maximum","(Maximum - Minimum) / 2","(Mean + Median) / 2"]'::jsonb, '(Minimum + Maximum) / 2', 'Midrange = (Min + Max)/2. It is the midpoint of the range. While simple, it is highly sensitive to outliers and rarely used in medical research.'),
('72a4a52e-e844-5993-baa6-28e227f3f50d'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'The best measure of central tendency for data with outliers is:', '["Geometric mean","Mean","Mode","Midrange","Median"]'::jsonb, 'Median', 'The median is resistant to outliers and is the preferred measure of central tendency when data contain extreme values or are skewed.'),
('e2a59c7a-1160-55f2-a61e-0f2648df19a9'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'Standard deviation can be zero only when:', '["All values are identical","The mean is zero","The range is zero","Variability is maximum","The data is perfectly normal"]'::jsonb, 'All values are identical', 'SD = 0 only when all values are equal (no variability). This is extremely rare in real medical data, as biological measurements always show some variation.'),
('fc2944a6-9713-526d-979f-41e04de5d804'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'A researcher analyzes data from a clinical study. Which measure is expressed in the same units as the original data?', '["Standard deviation","Squared deviation","Mean squared deviation","Sum of squares","Variance"]'::jsonb, 'Standard deviation', 'Standard deviation is the square root of variance and is expressed in the original units. Variance is expressed in squared units, making it less interpretable.'),
('50fb71be-0a55-57fa-945f-0ea212e9a00e'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'What happens to the standard deviation if every value is multiplied by 2?', '["It quadruples","It stays the same","It becomes zero","It halves","It doubles"]'::jsonb, 'It doubles', 'If each value is multiplied by a constant c, the standard deviation is multiplied by |c|. Multiplication by 2 doubles the spread (SD = 2 × original SD).'),
('3f8e8620-91ad-535d-a733-d52db33fe5a8'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'What happens to the standard deviation if a constant is added to every value?', '["It increases by the constant","It decreases by the constant","It becomes zero","It doubles","It stays the same"]'::jsonb, 'It stays the same', 'Adding a constant to every value shifts the centre but does not change the spread. The standard deviation remains unchanged because deviations from the mean are unchanged.'),
('66d19fab-cabb-5d9b-8165-1bd7c3e5059f'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'In a normal distribution, the interquartile range is approximately:', '["2 × SD","0.675 × SD","SD","3 × SD","1.35 × SD"]'::jsonb, '1.35 × SD', 'For a normal distribution, IQR ≈ 1.35 × SD. Specifically, Q1 = μ - 0.675σ and Q3 = μ + 0.675σ, so IQR = 1.35σ.'),
('7daa2c40-8fc4-5637-83c6-a976b905a57b'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'Coefficient of variation is useful when:', '["Sample size is large clinical presentation","There are no outliers clinical presentation","Comparing variability across different units or scales","Variance is zero clinical presentation","Data is normally distributed clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Comparing variability across different units or scales', 'CV allows comparison of variability between datasets with different units (e.g., weight vs height) or different means (e.g., blood pressure in children vs adults). It is dimensionless.'),
('cc3196a1-8729-5c4b-bb72-340dfbf9a29e'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'A researcher analyzes data from a clinical study. The standard deviation of the sampling distribution of the mean is called:', '["Mean squared error","Standard error","Variance","Sampling variance","Standard deviation"]'::jsonb, 'Standard error', 'The standard error (SE) is the standard deviation of the sampling distribution of a statistic. SE = σ/√n for the mean. It quantifies sampling variability.'),
('1381ba22-1e79-5596-b067-0169e21f6087'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'In a sample of 100 patients with SD = 10 mmHg for blood pressure, the SEM is:', '["5 mmHg","0.1 mmHg","100 mmHg","10 mmHg","1 mmHg"]'::jsonb, '1 mmHg', 'SEM = 10/√100 = 10/10 = 1 mmHg. This means the sample mean has a standard error of 1 mmHg.'),
('bd678dcc-ecb2-5c32-a7d7-35797b7c0142'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'Which is more affected by sampling variation?', '["Mean","Range","Median","Standard deviation","Mode"]'::jsonb, 'Standard deviation', 'The mean is more affected by sampling variation than the median because it uses all values including extremes. However, standard error quantifies this variation.'),
('b58921aa-7d56-50c9-975d-0f0d54306258'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'Bias in estimation means:', '["The confidence interval is wide","The estimator systematically differs from the true value","The estimator has high variance","The data is normally distributed","The sample is too small"]'::jsonb, 'The estimator systematically differs from the true value', 'Bias is the difference between the expected value of an estimator and the true population parameter. An unbiased estimator has expected value equal to the parameter.'),
('a55163cb-fe7e-5ff4-8a05-d4a948ba0bff'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'A researcher analyzes data from a clinical study. A new diagnostic test for breast cancer is being evaluated against the gold standard in 100 patients. Efficiency of an estimator refers to:', '["Its accuracy","Its bias","Its reliability","Its precision","Its consistency"]'::jsonb, 'Its precision', 'Efficiency refers to how precisely an estimator estimates the parameter. Among unbiased estimators, the one with the smallest variance is the most efficient.'),
('4cdf1442-7b95-587d-a7d3-87189c26506c'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'Consistency of an estimator means:', '["The estimator is unbiased clinical presentation","As sample size increases, the estimator converges to the true value","The estimator is robust clinical presentation","The estimator is normally distributed clinical presentation","The estimator has minimum variance clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'As sample size increases, the estimator converges to the true value', 'Consistency means that as the sample size (n) increases, the estimator gets closer and closer to the true population parameter. It is a large-sample property.'),
('aa002f89-e0d0-5de9-8aa3-335ab3bd01a7'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'Robustness of a statistical method refers to:', '["Its ease of interpretation","Its insensitivity to violations of assumptions","Its computational efficiency","Its theoretical elegance","Its popularity"]'::jsonb, 'Its insensitivity to violations of assumptions', 'A robust method produces valid results even when its underlying assumptions (e.g., normality) are violated. Non-parametric tests are often more robust than parametric tests.'),
('958928f9-132f-5ae6-b692-f20a4d242fd4'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'Maximum likelihood estimation (MLE) finds:', '["The minimum variance estimator clinical presentation","The median of the data clinical presentation","The range of plausible values clinical presentation","The mode of the distribution clinical presentation","Parameter values that maximise the likelihood of observing the data"]'::jsonb, 'Parameter values that maximise the likelihood of observing the data', 'MLE selects parameter estimates that make the observed data most probable (maximum likelihood). MLE estimators have desirable properties: consistency, asymptotic normality, and efficiency.'),
('6e09d736-ef11-5f1e-9d1a-230b6f57feb0'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'The method of moments estimates parameters by:', '["Minimising sum of squares","Equating sample moments to population moments","Maximising likelihood","Bootstrapping","Using Bayesian priors"]'::jsonb, 'Equating sample moments to population moments', 'The method of moments sets sample moments (e.g., mean, variance) equal to population moment equations and solves for parameters. It is simpler but often less efficient than MLE.'),
('862c754b-3f71-5eca-b8dd-dda126be6dee'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'Bayesian statistics incorporates:', '["Prior information along with observed data","Only frequentist methods","Only observed data","Only prior beliefs","No probability"]'::jsonb, 'Prior information along with observed data', 'Bayesian statistics combines prior knowledge (prior distribution) with observed data (likelihood) to produce a posterior distribution for parameters. Prior × Likelihood ∝ Posterior.'),
('db361a85-fed0-51bb-8f9c-f147d2aeff8c'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'A researcher analyzes data from a clinical study. In Bayesian statistics, the prior distribution represents:', '["The sampling distribution","The true parameter value","The posterior distribution","The likelihood of data","Beliefs about the parameter before seeing data"]'::jsonb, 'Beliefs about the parameter before seeing data', 'The prior distribution quantifies existing knowledge about the parameter before collecting new data. Priors can be informative (based on previous studies) or non-informative (vague).'),
('0183a55d-4d2c-5ded-8d2c-078ee8cc4c05'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'Posterior distribution in Bayesian analysis combines:', '["Data and errors","Sample and population","Type I and Type II errors","Mean and variance","Prior and likelihood"]'::jsonb, 'Prior and likelihood', 'The posterior distribution = (Prior × Likelihood) / Evidence. It represents updated beliefs about the parameter after observing the data.'),
('78c52190-b40e-59c8-be40-4c878357d68f'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'A non-informative prior is used when:', '["Bayesian methods are not appropriate","Strong prior information exists","The data is categorical","Little is known about the parameter before the study","The sample size is small"]'::jsonb, 'Little is known about the parameter before the study', 'A non-informative (flat or vague) prior is used when we want the data to dominate the posterior. It expresses minimal prior knowledge, allowing the likelihood to drive the results.'),
('5595786a-fef2-5a3c-b17d-ba68c4174307'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'A researcher analyzes data from a clinical study. The normal distribution is also known as:', '["Uniform distribution","Exponential distribution","Poisson distribution","Gaussian distribution","Binomial distribution"]'::jsonb, 'Gaussian distribution', 'The normal (Gaussian) distribution is a symmetric, bell-shaped distribution fully described by its mean (μ) and standard deviation (σ). It is fundamental in statistics.'),
('c6a65dbb-3501-5fb7-85ab-1ce81055eceb'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'The normal distribution is:', '["Skewed to the left","Uniform","Symmetric about its mean","Bimodal","Skewed to the right"]'::jsonb, 'Symmetric about its mean', 'The normal distribution is perfectly symmetric about its mean. The mean, median, and mode are all equal and located at the centre of the distribution.'),
('47fd710e-242b-5c86-85cb-81767a5c7c9f'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'The total area under a normal distribution curve is:', '["Depends on the mean","Depends on the SD","1","50%","0"]'::jsonb, '1', 'The total area under any probability density curve (including normal) equals 1 or 100%. This represents the total probability.'),
('b2162657-da63-5423-ae4a-60e0a46992f3'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'Which of the following is NOT a feature of the normal distribution?', '["It is discrete","It is symmetric","It is bell-shaped","Mean = Median = Mode","Area under curve = 1"]'::jsonb, 'It is discrete', 'The normal distribution is continuous, not discrete. It is defined for all real numbers and has a smooth, continuous probability density function.'),
('b5f0585b-28d6-53ab-b375-8d6a13cacec6'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'A researcher analyzes data from a clinical study. The standard normal distribution has:', '["Mean = 0, SD = 1","Mean = 50, SD = 10","Mean = 1, SD = 0","Mean = 100, SD = 15","Mean = 0, SD = 100"]'::jsonb, 'Mean = 0, SD = 1', 'The standard normal distribution (Z-distribution) has mean = 0 and standard deviation = 1. Any normal distribution can be standardised using Z = (X - μ)/σ.'),
('90e90dc0-b653-527a-84ce-a8bf92f7bd56'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'A new diagnostic test for lung cancer is being evaluated against the gold standard in 100 patients. In a population with 5%, the negative predictive value is: In a standard normal distribution, approximately 95% of values lie between:', '["-1.96 and +1.96","-2.58 and +2.58","-1 and +1","-0.67 and +0.67","-3 and +3"]'::jsonb, '-1.96 and +1.96', 'In the standard normal distribution, approximately 95% of values fall between Z = -1.96 and Z = +1.96. This corresponds to the critical value for a 95% confidence interval.'),
('5524ac81-bc7a-5de3-81ad-72fc8feb8bba'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'Approximately 99.7% of values in a normal distribution lie within:', '["1 standard deviation","5 standard deviations","2 standard deviations","3 standard deviations of the mean","4 standard deviations"]'::jsonb, '3 standard deviations of the mean', 'The 68-95-99.7 rule: 68% within ±1 SD, 95% within ±2 SD, 99.7% within ±3 SD of the mean for a normal distribution.'),
('3e2b654d-b74a-50d5-9975-3851f6e97e9d'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'A researcher analyzes data from a clinical study. Which of the following is an example of a normally distributed biological variable?', '["Number of hospital visits","Disease stages","Height in a homogeneous population","Blood type distribution","Age at death from cancer"]'::jsonb, 'Height in a homogeneous population', 'Height in a homogeneous population (same age, sex) often follows a normal distribution. Many biological measurements are approximately normal, but some (like hospital visits) follow other distributions.'),
('e331bda4-4a9a-5dd6-858f-3ecd469f84ab'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'The central limit theorem states that:', '["All data are normally distributed clinical presentation","Variance equals the mean clinical presentation","The population is always normal clinical presentation","The median equals the mean clinical presentation","The sampling distribution of the mean approaches normality as sample size increases"]'::jsonb, 'The sampling distribution of the mean approaches normality as sample size increases', 'The Central Limit Theorem (CLT) states that the distribution of sample means approaches a normal distribution as the sample size increases, regardless of the population distribution shape. This underpins parametric inference.'),
('17a3e091-9648-5e2b-8350-2189204b8bf0'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'The central limit theorem applies when:', '["Data is categorical","Sample size is sufficiently large","Variance is zero","Sample size is small","Population is normal"]'::jsonb, 'Sample size is sufficiently large', 'The CLT holds for sufficiently large samples (typically n ≥ 30). For smaller samples, the population must be approximately normal for parametric tests to be valid.'),
('98f05235-ffd8-5b3e-9e83-a0ca81f62904'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'The sampling distribution of the mean has:', '["Mean = 0, SD = σ/√n","Mean = population mean, SD = σ/√n","Mean = 0, SD = 1","Mean = sample mean, SD = σ","Mean = population mean, SD = σ"]'::jsonb, 'Mean = population mean, SD = σ/√n', 'The sampling distribution of the mean has the same mean as the population (μ) but a smaller standard deviation equal to σ/√n (standard error).'),
('a4e9a074-73a2-5e43-90b8-95868e2a4848'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'Which distribution is used for small sample hypothesis testing when σ is unknown?', '["Z-distribution","F-distribution","Binomial distribution","Chi-square distribution","t-distribution"]'::jsonb, 't-distribution', 'The t-distribution is used when sample size is small (typically n < 30) and the population standard deviation (σ) is unknown and estimated from the sample (s).'),
('ef7a406a-565d-5611-bd4c-0b2a81c31fd4'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'The t-distribution compared to the normal distribution has:', '["Equal tails","No tails","Heavier tails","Uniform tails","Lighter tails"]'::jsonb, 'Heavier tails', 'The t-distribution has heavier (fatter) tails than the normal distribution, reflecting greater uncertainty when estimating σ from small samples. As n increases, the t-distribution approaches the normal.'),
('acc6c4a5-7ab7-5e47-8123-7c89fedec13c'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'A new diagnostic test for hypertension is being evaluated against the gold standard in 1000 patients. A new diagnostic test for breast cancer is being evaluated against the gold standard in 1000 patients. A new diagnostic test for HIV/AIDS is being evaluated against the gold standard in 200 patients. Degrees of freedom for a one-sample t-test are:', '["n + 1","n - 2","n - 1","n","2n"]'::jsonb, 'n - 1', 'Degrees of freedom for a one-sample t-test = n - 1. This accounts for estimating the population standard deviation from the sample.'),
('f66905ef-5ba3-5397-96d8-1608136b8e7d'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'As degrees of freedom increase, the t-distribution:', '["Has higher kurtosis clinical presentation","Approaches the standard normal distribution","Becomes more skewed clinical presentation","Becomes more spread out clinical presentation","Has heavier tails clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Approaches the standard normal distribution', 'As degrees of freedom increase (sample size increases), the t-distribution converges to the standard normal distribution. At n = 30, they are very similar.'),
('fa3778fc-05e4-5eaa-b212-a510ddb59311'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'Which distribution is always positive and right-skewed?', '["Chi-square distribution","t-distribution","Uniform distribution","Binomial distribution","Normal distribution"]'::jsonb, 'Chi-square distribution', 'The chi-square (χ²) distribution is always positive (≥0) and right-skewed. It is used for variance tests, goodness-of-fit tests, and tests of association in contingency tables.'),
('464551fe-0467-50f4-8919-0f3bee9d64bd'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'The F-distribution is used in:', '["Correlation tests","Proportion tests","One-sample mean tests","Normality tests","ANOVA and comparison of variances"]'::jsonb, 'ANOVA and comparison of variances', 'The F-distribution is used in analysis of variance (ANOVA) to compare means across multiple groups and for comparing two variances (F-test). It is always positive and right-skewed.'),
('47b3d6e4-ff6b-5e01-9ac8-b27579dd2891'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'The binomial distribution is appropriate for:', '["Skewed data clinical presentation","Time-to-event data clinical presentation","Continuous outcomes clinical presentation","Normally distributed data clinical presentation","Binary outcomes from a fixed number of independent trials"]'::jsonb, 'Binary outcomes from a fixed number of independent trials', 'The binomial distribution models the number of successes (events) in a fixed number of independent binary trials with constant probability of success. Example: number of heads in 10 coin flips.'),
('b13a115d-7ffb-50b5-88d3-b46009aa77ca'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'The Poisson distribution models:', '["Count of rare events over time or space","Normal continuous data","Correlated data","Time to event","Binary outcomes"]'::jsonb, 'Count of rare events over time or space', 'The Poisson distribution models the count of events occurring in a fixed interval of time or space when events occur independently at a constant average rate (e.g., number of hospital admissions per month).'),
('afcdc8df-5978-5aae-b480-36aaf22afdb1'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'For a Poisson distribution, the mean is:', '["Greater than the variance","Less than the variance","Always 1","Equal to the variance","Always zero"]'::jsonb, 'Equal to the variance', 'A key property of the Poisson distribution is that its mean equals its variance (both = λ, the rate parameter). This equidispersion property helps identify Poisson-distributed data.'),
('3b181e08-c87c-55aa-939b-a0d5a07f7418'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'Which distribution is used to model time-to-event (survival) data?', '["Binomial distribution","Poisson distribution","Normal distribution","Chi-square distribution","Exponential and Weibull distributions"]'::jsonb, 'Exponential and Weibull distributions', 'The exponential and Weibull distributions are commonly used to model survival (time-to-event) data. The exponential has a constant hazard rate; the Weibull allows for varying hazard rates.'),
('d40f0d0d-7a1e-59bc-bfaa-f79f4de8bfc9'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'A Q-Q plot is used to:', '["Test for homogeneity of variance","Quantify correlation","Calculate confidence intervals","Compare means","Assess whether data follows a particular distribution"]'::jsonb, 'Assess whether data follows a particular distribution', 'A Q-Q (quantile-quantile) plot compares the quantiles of the data against the quantiles of a theoretical distribution (e.g., normal). If the points follow a straight line, the data matches the distribution.'),
('6ade3633-8f4e-5934-9634-262ff38e51c9'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'In a normal Q-Q plot, points falling along the diagonal line suggest:', '["The sample size is small","The data is normally distributed","Outliers are present","The data is skewed","The variance is unequal"]'::jsonb, 'The data is normally distributed', 'When data are normally distributed, the points on a normal Q-Q plot will approximately follow a straight diagonal line. Systematic deviations from the line indicate non-normality.'),
('9e011b4e-e867-5ca3-82e3-33ddf42857fc'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'The Shapiro-Wilk test tests:', '["Correlation between variables","Normality of data","Equality of means","Independence of observations","Homogeneity of variance"]'::jsonb, 'Normality of data', 'The Shapiro-Wilk test is a statistical test for normality. A significant p-value (p < 0.05) suggests the data significantly deviates from a normal distribution.'),
('e78d04ca-e297-5a35-afc6-38228414cdf6'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'A new diagnostic test for tuberculosis is being evaluated against the gold standard in 200 patients. A new diagnostic test for coronary artery disease is being evaluated against the gold standard in 1000 patients. A new diagnostic test for tuberculosis is being evaluated against the gold standard in 200 patients. Kolmogorov-Smirnov test can be used to test:', '["Whether correlation is significant","Whether two means are equal","Whether data are independent","Whether a sample comes from a specified distribution","Whether variances are equal"]'::jsonb, 'Whether a sample comes from a specified distribution', 'The Kolmogorov-Smirnov (K-S) test compares the cumulative distribution of sample data to a reference distribution (one-sample) or compares two samples (two-sample). It tests for distributional differences.'),
('766bcfd3-2362-5a9f-8117-17c3b3cf4053'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'Skewness of a normal distribution is:', '["1","-1","0","3","Depends on variance"]'::jsonb, '0', 'A normal distribution has skewness = 0 (perfectly symmetric). Positive skewness indicates right-tailed distribution; negative skewness indicates left-tailed distribution.'),
('2f2578c3-fa2c-54e4-a246-c1bc562a3a91'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'A researcher analyzes data from a clinical study. Kurtosis of a standard normal distribution is:', '["3","0","-3","2","1"]'::jsonb, '3', 'The standard normal distribution has kurtosis = 3 (excess kurtosis = 0). Leptokurtic distributions have kurtosis > 3 (heavier tails); platykurtic have kurtosis < 3 (lighter tails).'),
('a47f10b9-2d73-594e-8faf-0b26edc56def'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'Excess kurtosis is calculated as:', '["Kurtosis + 3","Kurtosis / 3","3 - Kurtosis","Kurtosis - 3","Kurtosis × 3"]'::jsonb, 'Kurtosis - 3', 'Excess kurtosis = kurtosis - 3. The normal distribution has excess kurtosis = 0. Positive excess kurtosis indicates heavier tails; negative indicates lighter tails.'),
('e81eb309-b99b-50d8-8cd2-477efb9328f2'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'If data is not normally distributed, which test should be used?', '["Non-parametric tests","Z-test","Parametric tests","t-test","ANOVA"]'::jsonb, 'Non-parametric tests', 'When data significantly deviates from normality, non-parametric tests (e.g., Mann-Whitney U, Wilcoxon signed-rank, Kruskal-Wallis) should be used as they do not assume normality.'),
('381f20cf-885e-5483-a636-0abd0ea90e56'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'Data transformation to achieve normality is most commonly:', '["Round to integers","Log transformation","Square transformation","Absolute value","Cube transformation"]'::jsonb, 'Log transformation', 'Log transformation (natural log or base 10) is the most common method to normalise right-skewed data. Other transformations include square root (for count data) and Box-Cox (a family of power transformations).'),
('379b3669-1b71-5324-bb89-5a99da7c1152'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'Box-Cox transformation is:', '["A correlation coefficient clinical presentation","A non-parametric test clinical presentation","A graphical method clinical presentation","A family of power transformations to achieve normality","A test for normality clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'A family of power transformations to achieve normality', 'The Box-Cox transformation identifies the optimal power transformation (λ) to make data more normally distributed. It includes log (λ=0), square root (λ=0.5), and inverse (λ=-1) as special cases.'),
('790b9a8c-9511-54b8-a0cb-f97d8edcedf9'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'When sample size is very large (>100), violation of normality:', '["Prevents any statistical analysis","Requires non-parametric tests","Has less impact due to the central limit theorem","Still invalidates all tests","Always requires data transformation"]'::jsonb, 'Has less impact due to the central limit theorem', 'With large samples, the Central Limit Theorem ensures the sampling distribution of the mean is approximately normal, making parametric tests (t-test, ANOVA) robust to non-normality.'),
('4ecda5fc-5be4-58c4-9a1a-a24820db0fe8'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'The uniform distribution has:', '["Two peaks clinical presentation","A left skew clinical presentation","A bell shape clinical presentation","A right skew clinical presentation","Equal probability across all values"]'::jsonb, 'Equal probability across all values', 'In a uniform distribution, every value in the range has equal probability of occurring. It looks like a flat rectangle and is often used for random number generation.'),
('7b357b4e-6ea6-5fd9-8e98-13aff4c340c8'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'The exponential distribution models:', '["Binary outcomes clinical presentation","Proportions clinical presentation","Time between events in a Poisson process","Count of events clinical presentation","Normal continuous data clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Time between events in a Poisson process', 'The exponential distribution models the time between events in a Poisson process (constant rate). It is memoryless - the probability of an event in the next interval does not depend on how long we have waited.'),
('0f598753-a34f-5f60-b220-b86f9d09dd0e'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'The memoryless property is characteristic of:', '["Binomial distribution","Poisson distribution","Uniform distribution","Exponential distribution","Normal distribution"]'::jsonb, 'Exponential distribution', 'The exponential distribution is memoryless: P(T > t + s | T > s) = P(T > t). This means the remaining waiting time does not depend on how long we have already waited.'),
('bccd7406-2034-5a00-ac0d-8dbfc59d3fb2'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'A new diagnostic test for tuberculosis is being evaluated against the gold standard in 200 patients. Which test is used to assess normality in small samples?', '["Chi-square test","F-test","Z-test","Shapiro-Wilk test","t-test"]'::jsonb, 'Shapiro-Wilk test', 'The Shapiro-Wilk test is specifically designed for testing normality and is most appropriate for small to moderate sample sizes. The Kolmogorov-Smirnov test can also be used but is less powerful for normality testing.'),
('0125c243-960a-56a5-b940-810c05b4f5f4'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'Kernel density estimation is used to:', '["Test normality clinical presentation","Calculate variance clinical presentation","Estimate the probability density function from data","Perform regression clinical presentation","Estimate the mean clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Estimate the probability density function from data', 'Kernel density estimation is a non-parametric method to estimate the probability density function of a variable. It produces a smooth density curve without assuming a specific distribution shape.'),
('dd972d9b-6e4a-5c28-be4a-4aa51a84d756'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'Multi-modal distribution has:', '["A flat shape","Two or more peaks","One peak","A bell shape","No peak"]'::jsonb, 'Two or more peaks', 'A multi-modal distribution has multiple distinct peaks (modes). It suggests the data may be a mixture of two or more different populations (e.g., a mixture of heights from males and females).'),
('7c361158-ccc7-5efb-a08e-bf3d35096cd3'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'In a population with 1%, the positive predictive value is: Which is NOT a characteristic of a standard normal distribution?', '["Variance = 1","Skewness = 0","Standard deviation = 1","Median = 1","Mean = 0"]'::jsonb, 'Median = 1', 'The standard normal distribution has mean = 0, SD = 1, variance = 1, and skewness = 0. The median equals the mean = 0, not 1.'),
('1480e199-5769-5df1-adac-59beee740aeb'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'If X ~ N(100, 15²), the Z-score for X = 130 is:', '["30","2.0","-2.0","0.5","1.0"]'::jsonb, '2.0', 'Z = (130 - 100)/15 = 30/15 = 2.0. So X = 130 is 2 standard deviations above the mean of 100.'),
('90411f12-17aa-5c5a-928c-faf9e994d462'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'Point estimate is:', '["The standard error clinical presentation","The true population value clinical presentation","A range of values clinical presentation","The confidence level clinical presentation","A single value used to estimate a population parameter"]'::jsonb, 'A single value used to estimate a population parameter', 'A point estimate is a single value (statistic) calculated from sample data that serves as the best guess for a population parameter. Example: sample mean x̄ as estimate of population mean μ.'),
('dcbee0ef-1e3d-5e94-a03e-8e7a4b86bd9c'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'A researcher analyzes data from a clinical study. Interval estimate provides:', '["The exact parameter value","The standard error","A range of plausible values for the parameter","The p-value","A single value estimate"]'::jsonb, 'A range of plausible values for the parameter', 'An interval estimate gives a range of values within which the population parameter is believed to lie. A confidence interval is the most common type of interval estimate.'),
('862613b0-f75a-563a-ac0f-9ef87d3993b6'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'A 95% confidence interval for the mean means:', '["There is a 95% chance the true mean lies in the interval","The mean has a 95% probability of being in the interval","If we repeated the study many times, 95% of CIs would contain the true mean","The interval contains 95% of the data","95% of sample means lie in this interval"]'::jsonb, 'If we repeated the study many times, 95% of CIs would contain the true mean', 'The correct interpretation: 95% confidence means that if we were to take many samples and compute a CI from each, approximately 95% of those intervals would contain the true population mean.'),
('6dea9adb-8e26-5d0f-954d-ae95ab774329'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'A researcher analyzes data from a clinical study. The width of a confidence interval depends on:', '["Only the mean clinical presentation","Only variability clinical presentation","Sample size, variability, and confidence level","Only sample size clinical presentation","Only confidence level clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Sample size, variability, and confidence level', 'CI width is determined by sample size (n), variability (SD), and confidence level (e.g., 95%). Larger n and lower variability narrow the CI; higher confidence levels widen it.'),
('37188c99-1df1-54fb-815b-3b2720587801'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'As sample size increases, the width of the confidence interval:', '["Stays the same","Decreases","Becomes unpredictable","Increases","Doubles"]'::jsonb, 'Decreases', 'CI width = 2 × critical value × (SD/√n). As n increases, the standard error (SD/√n) decreases, narrowing the confidence interval and improving precision.'),
('8a4532d1-2186-5eb0-a672-e0059befa079'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'A researcher analyzes data from a clinical study. As variability (SD) increases, the width of the confidence interval:', '["Increases","Stays the same","Decreases","Is halved","Becomes unpredictable"]'::jsonb, 'Increases', 'More variability (larger SD) increases the standard error (SD/√n), widening the confidence interval. This reflects greater uncertainty in the estimate.'),
('d7a6b66f-36e6-59f6-bd51-f5daf0f83371'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'A researcher analyzes data from a clinical study. A new diagnostic test for hypertension is being evaluated against the gold standard in 200 patients. A new diagnostic test for breast cancer is being evaluated against the gold standard in 500 patients. A new diagnostic test for hypertension is being evaluated against the gold standard in 500 patients. In a population with 1%, the accuracy is: A 99% confidence interval is ___ than a 95% CI for the same data:', '["The same width","Half as wide","Narrower","Wider","Unrelated"]'::jsonb, 'Wider', 'Higher confidence requires a larger critical value (Z = 2.58 for 99% vs 1.96 for 95%), resulting in a wider interval. Higher confidence = less precision.'),
('b71ea2d6-c61e-5807-b192-f37af088ab88'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'The formula for 95% CI of the mean is:', '["x̄ ± 1.96 × √n","x̄ ± 1.96 × σ","x̄ ± 2.58 ×","x̄ ± 1.96 ×","x̄ ± σ/√n"]'::jsonb, 'x̄ ± 1.96 ×', '95% CI = sample mean ± 1.96 × standard error = x̄ ± 1.96 × (σ/√n). When σ is unknown, we use t-distribution: x̄ ± t(0.05, df) × (s/√n).'),
('a90a2020-7271-5efd-a97f-fa8b772f6d8b'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'When the population standard deviation is unknown, the CI for the mean uses:', '["F-distribution","Z-distribution","t-distribution","Chi-square distribution","Binomial distribution"]'::jsonb, 't-distribution', 'When σ is unknown (the usual case), we estimate it with s (sample SD) and use the t-distribution with n-1 degrees of freedom. This accounts for the added uncertainty of estimating σ.'),
('07af6828-4cb5-5a2f-9079-66111c3a71fd'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'The formula for 95% CI for a proportion is:', '["p̂ ± 1.96 × p̂","p̂ ± √[p̂(1-p̂)/n]","p̂ ± 1.96 × √[p̂(1-p̂)/n]","p̂ ± 1.96 × n","p̂ ± 1.96 × √"]'::jsonb, 'p̂ ± 1.96 × √[p̂(1-p̂)/n]', 'CI for proportion = p̂ ± Z × √[p̂(1-p̂)/n], where p̂ is the sample proportion. This uses the normal approximation to the binomial distribution, valid when np ≥ 5 and n(1-p) ≥ 5.'),
('f6f9706c-fd8c-54d1-aa17-b1086304ed4b'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'If n = 100 and 40 patients improved, the 95% CI for the proportion improved is approximately:', '["0.40 ± 0.20","0.40 ± 0.096","0.40 ± 0.10","0.40 ± 0.05","0.40 ± 0.01"]'::jsonb, '0.40 ± 0.096', 'SE = √[0.4(0.6)/100] = √(0.24/100) = √0.0024 = 0.049. 95% CI = 0.40 ± 1.96 × 0.049 = 0.40 ± 0.096 ≈ 0.304 to 0.496.'),
('0f2c6d40-889a-5b5f-b7f4-72f29866517f'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'Which is NOT true about confidence intervals?', '["They are affected by variability","They are affected by sample size","They quantify precision","They can be calculated for various parameters","They always contain the true parameter"]'::jsonb, 'They always contain the true parameter', 'Confidence intervals do NOT always contain the true parameter. A 95% CI has a 5% chance of missing the true value. We say we are 95% confident, not 100% certain.'),
('82058ddc-2757-5216-ad13-3abffda4fabc'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'A 95% CI for a mean difference that includes zero indicates:', '["The treatment is harmful clinical presentation","No statistically significant difference at the 0.05 level","A significant difference clinical presentation","The treatment is beneficial clinical presentation","The sample size is adequate clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'No statistically significant difference at the 0.05 level', 'If the 95% CI for the difference between two means includes zero, the difference is not statistically significant at α = 0.05. We cannot conclude there is a true difference.'),
('2839ed69-4590-5384-a367-0b7dc9b3913e'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'A researcher analyzes data from a clinical study. A 95% CI for an odds ratio that does NOT include 1.0 indicates:', '["The study is biased","The sample is inadequate","A statistically significant association","No association","The OR is clinically insignificant"]'::jsonb, 'A statistically significant association', 'If the 95% CI for OR excludes 1.0 (the null value), the association is statistically significant. The OR is significantly different from 1.0 at the 0.05 level.'),
('23b037fe-a5fc-58a1-846f-35f279697c88'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'Which is NOT a desirable property of a point estimator?', '["It is sufficient","It is unbiased","It is always biased","It is consistent","It is efficient"]'::jsonb, 'It is always biased', 'Desirable properties of estimators include unbiasedness (expected value = parameter), efficiency (minimum variance), consistency (converges to true value as n increases), and sufficiency (uses all information).'),
('534bf9bd-9f97-5642-b791-d80824c86e8b'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'Margin of error in a confidence interval is:', '["The critical value","The sample mean","The confidence level","The critical value × standard error","The standard error"]'::jsonb, 'The critical value × standard error', 'Margin of error = critical value × standard error. For 95% CI of the mean, margin of error = 1.96 × (σ/√n). It defines half the width of the CI.'),
('4f2c80a0-fcb2-502f-8d07-03c96c31d76c'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'A 90% CI uses a Z-critical value of approximately:', '["1.96","1.645","2.326","2.576","0.842"]'::jsonb, '1.645', 'The Z-critical value for a 90% CI is 1.645 (compared to 1.96 for 95% and 2.576 for 99%). The 90% CI is narrower than the 95% CI.'),
('93bb8cd9-d984-5b9b-9c8b-4ade4d964819'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'The Wilson score interval is preferred over the Wald interval for:', '["Normal data","Large proportions","Proportions near 0 or 1","Large samples","Continuous data"]'::jsonb, 'Proportions near 0 or 1', 'The Wilson score interval performs better than the Wald (normal approximation) interval for proportions, especially when the proportion is near 0 or 1 or when sample size is small.'),
('6b395c7a-7e27-52b1-867c-4354db0294f0'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'Exact confidence intervals are based on:', '["The exact binomial distribution","Normal approximation","The F-distribution","The Z-distribution","The t-distribution"]'::jsonb, 'The exact binomial distribution', 'Exact (Clopper-Pearson) confidence intervals for proportions use the binomial distribution directly, guaranteeing at least the nominal coverage. They are wider than approximate intervals.'),
('f9c5405b-f414-59e4-8090-5d05f6ceee37'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'A researcher analyzes data from a clinical study. Bootstrap confidence intervals are based on:', '["Resampling from the observed data","The chi-square distribution","The F-distribution","The t-distribution","The theoretical normal distribution"]'::jsonb, 'Resampling from the observed data', 'Bootstrapping is a resampling method that draws many samples (with replacement) from the observed data to estimate the sampling distribution and construct CIs. It makes minimal assumptions.'),
('0c238252-42cf-5614-8193-f4cc2af722fe'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'A researcher analyzes data from a clinical study. A 95% CI for the difference between two means that ranges from 1.2 to 5.6 suggests:', '["No significant difference","A significant difference favouring the first group","A significant difference favouring the second group","The difference is clinically unimportant","The sample is too small"]'::jsonb, 'A significant difference favouring the first group', 'Since the CI (1.2 to 5.6) does not include 0, the difference is significant. The positive values suggest the first group has a higher mean than the second.'),
('000d0f92-a13e-5530-8b68-d63fbf8e2cac'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'Precision of an estimate is reflected by:', '["The point estimate","The sample mean","The median","Width of the confidence interval","The p-value"]'::jsonb, 'Width of the confidence interval', 'Precision is reflected in the width of the confidence interval. A narrow CI indicates a precise estimate; a wide CI indicates imprecision (large sampling variability).'),
('9df2298a-864f-528f-ad75-0f1d38a743c3'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'The confidence level (e.g., 95%) refers to:', '["The probability the CI contains the parameter for this specific sample","The long-run proportion of CIs that contain the parameter","The accuracy of the point estimate","The power of the study","The percentage of data in the interval"]'::jsonb, 'The long-run proportion of CIs that contain the parameter', 'The confidence level is a property of the procedure, not a single interval. In repeated sampling, 95% of computed 95% CIs will contain the true parameter.'),
('bff32667-4117-5868-a9f7-dbf14de25d80'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'A researcher analyzes data from a clinical study. For a given sample, a 90% CI is ___ a 95% CI:', '["The same as","Wider than","Unrelated to","Half the width of","Narrower than"]'::jsonb, 'Narrower than', 'The 90% CI uses a smaller critical value (1.645 vs 1.96), producing a narrower interval. The trade-off is lower confidence that the interval contains the true parameter.'),
('d2df008d-bae8-53a9-94e6-09e0619c7746'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'If sample size is quadrupled (×4), the standard error is:', '["Doubled","Quartered","Unchanged","Halved","Reduced by 75%"]'::jsonb, 'Halved', 'SE = σ/√n. If n is multiplied by 4, SE is divided by √4 = 2. Quadrupling the sample halves the standard error.'),
('3b05c86d-7df6-50f5-934c-6ac364b93809'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'Sample variance (s²) is an unbiased estimator of:', '["Population mean","Population variance","Standard error","Population standard deviation","Population proportion"]'::jsonb, 'Population variance', 's² = Σ(x - x̄)²/(n-1) is an unbiased estimator of population variance σ². The n-1 correction (Bessel''s correction) ensures unbiasedness.'),
('8ae003e2-597f-5ce8-8652-3d7b4323cac8'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'A researcher analyzes data from a clinical study. Sample standard deviation (s) is:', '["An unbiased estimator clinical presentation","A biased estimator of population standard deviation","Smaller than σ clinical presentation","Larger than σ clinical presentation","Always equal to σ clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'A biased estimator of population standard deviation', 'Unlike sample variance, the sample standard deviation (s) is a biased estimator of population standard deviation (σ). The bias is small in large samples.'),
('69930632-7616-5c36-a1b3-36b6839bb106'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'Maximum likelihood estimator of the mean is:', '["Sample trimmed mean","Sample median","Minimum value","Sample mode","Sample mean"]'::jsonb, 'Sample mean', 'The MLE of the population mean (μ) is the sample mean (x̄). For normally distributed data, x̄ is also the least squares and minimum variance unbiased estimator.'),
('953341f6-d8e7-50c4-be40-33ef79cce00d'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'A researcher analyzes data from a clinical study. The standard error of the median is approximately:', '["σ/n","√","1.25 ×","σ/√n","σ²/n"]'::jsonb, '1.25 ×', 'The standard error of the median is approximately 1.25 × (σ/√n) for normally distributed data. This is larger than the SE of the mean, reflecting the median''s lower efficiency.'),
('7e7d2159-01d6-5fed-b50a-0cf4badec487'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'A confidence interval for variance uses:', '["Chi-square distribution","t-distribution","Binomial distribution","Normal distribution","F-distribution"]'::jsonb, 'Chi-square distribution', 'The CI for a population variance (σ²) uses the chi-square distribution: [(n-1)s²/χ²(α/2), (n-1)s²/χ²(1-α/2)] with n-1 degrees of freedom.'),
('9c272c6d-81f0-5ecf-b6c4-ec56db064ab3'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'A researcher analyzes data from a clinical study. A new diagnostic test for hypertension is being evaluated against the gold standard in 2000 patients. A new diagnostic test for hypertension is being evaluated against the gold standard in 500 patients. A new diagnostic test for coronary artery disease is being evaluated against the gold standard in 500 patients. A new diagnostic test for diabetes mellitus is being evaluated against the gold standard in 100 patients. In a population with 20%, the positive predictive value is: Probability ranges from:', '["0 to 100","0 to 1","-∞ to +∞","-1 to 1","0 to infinity"]'::jsonb, '0 to 1', 'Probability is a number between 0 (impossible event) and 1 (certain event). It is the long-run relative frequency of an event occurring.'),
('182e2bab-48c0-55f6-a860-79e254b8cb33'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'If two events are mutually exclusive:', '["They have equal probability","One is the complement of the other","They cannot occur simultaneously","They always occur together","They are independent"]'::jsonb, 'They cannot occur simultaneously', 'Mutually exclusive events cannot occur at the same time. For example, being both alive and dead is impossible. P(A and B) = 0 for mutually exclusive events.'),
('64abd589-7686-5fd5-a459-555c921b116d'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'P(A or B) for mutually exclusive events is:', '["P(A) + P(B) - P","P(A) - P","P(A) × P","P(A) + P","P(A)/P"]'::jsonb, 'P(A) + P', 'For mutually exclusive events: P(A ∪ B) = P(A) + P(B). Since P(A ∩ B) = 0, no subtraction is needed. For non-mutually exclusive events, subtract P(A ∩ B).'),
('32cb1ef4-05f7-5460-8220-06ee1bad644a'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'P(A and B) for independent events is:', '["P(A) - P","P","P(A) + P","P(A)/P","P(A) × P"]'::jsonb, 'P(A) × P', 'For independent events: P(A ∩ B) = P(A) × P(B). Independence means occurrence of A does not affect the probability of B.'),
('5d3f07f0-14e7-5cb3-bd3b-9e8990f6a294'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'A study follows 500 patients with diabetes mellitus over 2 years to assess survival outcomes. Conditional probability P(A|B) is defined as:', '["P(A) × P","P(A ∩ B) / P","P(A) + P","P(A ∪ B) / P","P(A ∩ B) / P"]'::jsonb, 'P(A ∩ B) / P', 'P(A|B) = P(A ∩ B)/P(B), the probability of A occurring given that B has occurred. It is only defined when P(B) > 0.'),
('3a3d1a3d-d8b3-5a2b-ae38-73b6c5a8a20d'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'A researcher analyzes data from a clinical study. Bayes'' theorem is:', '["P(A ∩ B) = P(A) × P","P(A ∪ B) = P(A) + P","P(A|B) = P","P(A|B) = P(A) × P","P(A|B) = P(B|A) × P(A) / P"]'::jsonb, 'P(A|B) = P(B|A) × P(A) / P', 'Bayes'' theorem: P(A|B) = [P(B|A) × P(A)] / P(B). It describes how to update the probability of A based on evidence B. It is the foundation of Bayesian statistics.'),
('b18225a3-23f7-5535-898b-09201d75c10d'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'Sensitivity in diagnostic testing is:', '["P","P","P","P","P"]'::jsonb, 'P', 'Sensitivity = P(Test Positive | Disease Present). It is a conditional probability representing the test''s ability to detect disease when present.'),
('cd089280-098b-5ee6-b987-896aa93a24f8'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'Specificity in diagnostic testing is:', '["P","P","P","P","P"]'::jsonb, 'P', 'Specificity = P(Test Negative | Disease Absent). It is the conditional probability that a person without disease tests negative.'),
('9bf22f11-035f-5031-b238-05e50e3724e7'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'A new diagnostic test for tuberculosis is being evaluated against the gold standard in 100 patients. Positive predictive value (PPV) is:', '["P","P","P","P","P"]'::jsonb, 'P', 'PPV = P(Disease Present | Test Positive). Unlike sensitivity, PPV depends on disease prevalence and is a patient-relevant probability.'),
('21610914-4f73-51cc-b1ad-d84e14690fb0'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'The law of total probability states:', '["P(A) = P(A|B) × P","P(A) = Σ P(A|Bi) × P(Bi) for a partition Bi","P(A) = 1 - P","P(A) = P(A ∩ B) + P","P(A ∪ B) = P(A) + P(B) - P"]'::jsonb, 'P(A) = Σ P(A|Bi) × P(Bi) for a partition Bi', 'The law of total probability expresses the total probability of A as a weighted average of conditional probabilities over a partition of the sample space.'),
('04b7b7ec-cdf2-59d7-8e3a-2b30e71c2d14'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'A researcher analyzes data from a clinical study. If P(A) = 0.4 and P(B) = 0.3, and A and B are independent, P(A ∩ B) is:', '["0.7","0.1","0.58","0.4","0.12"]'::jsonb, '0.12', 'For independent events: P(A ∩ B) = P(A) × P(B) = 0.4 × 0.3 = 0.12. Independence simplifies the joint probability to the product of marginal probabilities.'),
('b5d95412-ac5c-5f6e-9d9d-7dbf2314206c'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'If P(A) = 0.4 and P(B) = 0.3, and A and B are mutually exclusive, P(A ∪ B) is:', '["0.58","0.1","0.02","0.12","0.7"]'::jsonb, '0.7', 'For mutually exclusive events: P(A ∪ B) = P(A) + P(B) = 0.4 + 0.3 = 0.7. The events cannot both occur so intersection probability is zero.'),
('c6142e15-6732-5499-bacf-0d5e96c6d461'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'A new diagnostic test for diabetes mellitus is being evaluated against the gold standard in 1000 patients. A new diagnostic test for tuberculosis is being evaluated against the gold standard in 200 patients. A new diagnostic test for diabetes mellitus is being evaluated against the gold standard in 1000 patients. Odds in favour of an event with probability 0.75 are:', '["75:100","0.75:0.25","4:1","3:1","1:3"]'::jsonb, '3:1', 'Odds = P/(1-P) = 0.75/0.25 = 3. Odds are 3 to 1 in favour of the event. Odds and probability are different ways of expressing likelihood.'),
('321bc4ab-81e7-5071-9d36-bf32b4f56458'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'In a population with 1%, the Youden index is: Probability of disease given a positive test depends on:', '["Only sensitivity clinical presentation","Only the test clinical presentation","Only prevalence clinical presentation","Prevalence, sensitivity, and specificity","Only specificity clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Prevalence, sensitivity, and specificity', 'PPV (probability of disease given positive test) depends on disease prevalence (pre-test probability), sensitivity, and specificity. This is calculated using Bayes'' theorem.'),
('3893a9fa-eca5-59c1-87ba-14cf7e71f51f'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'Random variable is:', '["A fixed number clinical presentation","An observed value clinical presentation","A variable whose values are determined by chance","A parameter clinical presentation","A constant value clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'A variable whose values are determined by chance', 'A random variable is a variable whose possible values are outcomes of a random phenomenon. It can be discrete (e.g., count) or continuous (e.g., height).'),
('14d023a7-49e1-505d-af50-32e4eed57e88'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'Expected value of a random variable is:', '["The most likely value","The minimum value","The median value","The long-run average value","The maximum value"]'::jsonb, 'The long-run average value', 'The expected value E(X) is the long-run average of a random variable over many repetitions. For a discrete variable: E(X) = Σ x × P(X = x).'),
('5cf48a73-cb3f-526e-ba25-d3e1c77f76c1'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'Variance of a random variable is:', '["E","E(X)²","E[(X - μ)²]","E(X) - μ","[E(X)]²"]'::jsonb, 'E[(X - μ)²]', 'Variance of a random variable = E[(X - μ)²] = E(X²) - [E(X)]². It measures the spread of the distribution.'),
('2b809c1f-1508-5903-892c-cb9d2b56cd0d'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'For a binomial distribution with n = 10 and p = 0.5, the expected value is:', '["5","10","0.05","0.5","2.5"]'::jsonb, '5', 'E(X) = np for binomial distribution = 10 × 0.5 = 5. Variance = np(1-p) = 10 × 0.5 × 0.5 = 2.5.'),
('5dbcb21a-e72b-5619-a7dc-dbb28b38c06f'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'For a Poisson distribution with mean λ = 4, the variance is:', '["4","8","2","16","0"]'::jsonb, '4', 'For Poisson distribution, variance = mean = λ = 4. The equality of mean and variance is a defining characteristic of the Poisson distribution.'),
('49d56ef8-a3bc-5da6-bad6-8683128b89cb'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'The complement rule states:', '["P(Ac) = P(A) - 1","P(Ac) = 0","P(Ac) = P","P(Ac) = 1 + P","P(Ac) = 1 - P"]'::jsonb, 'P(Ac) = 1 - P', 'The complement rule: P(not A) = 1 - P(A). The complement includes all outcomes not in event A. Probability of an event and its complement always sum to 1.'),
('42353148-32f4-52f0-9f89-9e4ca9dbf89c'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'If A and B are independent, then P(A|B) = P(A|Bc) = P(A)', '["Never true","Only for large samples","True","False","Depends on sample size"]'::jsonb, 'True', 'Independence means P(A|B) = P(A) and P(A|Bc) = P(A). Knowledge of B provides no information about A. This is equivalent to P(A ∩ B) = P(A) × P(B).'),
('b488cf65-fee9-51a8-bd4c-96af330ddc41'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'A new diagnostic test for lung cancer is being evaluated against the gold standard in 2000 patients. Sensitivity analysis of diagnostic tests uses:', '["Bayes'' theorem","Addition rule","Central limit theorem","Multiplication rule","Law of large numbers"]'::jsonb, 'Bayes'' theorem', 'Bayes'' theorem is fundamental to diagnostic test interpretation, allowing calculation of post-test probability from pre-test probability and test characteristics (sensitivity, specificity).'),
('4dec9c26-7878-56da-9921-97253d6aa9ae'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'A new diagnostic test for coronary artery disease is being evaluated against the gold standard in 1000 patients. Likelihood ratio positive (LR+) = Sensitivity / (1 - Specificity). An LR+ of 10 means:', '["Specificity is 10%","The test is 10% accurate","The patient is 10 times more likely to have the disease","Sensitivity is 10%","A positive test is 10 times more likely in diseased than non-diseased"]'::jsonb, 'A positive test is 10 times more likely in diseased than non-diseased', 'LR+ = P(T+|D+)/P(T+|D-) = Sensitivity/(1-Specificity). LR+ = 10 means the positive test is 10 times more likely in diseased than non-diseased individuals.'),
('7351b32e-0423-5ed0-9020-e39d43b63bf4'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'Prior probability in Bayes'' theorem is also called:', '["Conditional probability","Joint probability","Posterior probability","Post-test probability","Pre-test probability"]'::jsonb, 'Pre-test probability', 'Prior probability (pre-test probability) is the probability of disease before the test result is known. For screening, it is often the disease prevalence. Bayes'' theorem updates this to post-test probability.'),
('f619c569-cd19-59f2-ace0-b8278380d5b7'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'A study follows 1000 patients with tuberculosis over 1 year to assess survival outcomes. Posterior probability in Bayes'' theorem is:', '["Specificity clinical presentation","Sensitivity clinical presentation","The probability of disease after considering test results","The probability before testing clinical presentation","PPV for all tests clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'The probability of disease after considering test results', 'Posterior probability is the updated probability of disease after incorporating test results. It combines prior probability (prevalence) with the likelihood of the test result.'),
('44fa1153-23e0-53a1-982f-bcbfa7453764'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'Randomisation ensures that treatment groups are comparable with respect to:', '["Gender only clinical presentation","Both known and unknown confounding factors","Only known factors clinical presentation","Only unknown factors clinical presentation","Age only clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Both known and unknown confounding factors', 'Randomisation balances both known and unknown confounders across groups by chance. This is the key strength of RCTs, as probability theory ensures group comparability in the long run.'),
('57960093-c7eb-5af2-b84d-fb4dac055bce'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'Gambler''s fallacy is:', '["The mistaken belief that past independent events affect future probabilities","A correct statistical principle clinical presentation","The law of large numbers clinical presentation","Bayes'' theorem clinical presentation","Regression to the mean clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'The mistaken belief that past independent events affect future probabilities', 'The gambler''s fallacy is the incorrect belief that if an event occurs more frequently than expected in the past, it will occur less frequently in the future (e.g., after 10 heads, tails is ''due''). Independent events have no memory.'),
('31a32dee-1a26-568e-b138-9686228a347e'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'The law of large numbers states:', '["Small samples are more accurate clinical presentation","Large samples are biased clinical presentation","The mean never converges clinical presentation","As sample size increases, the sample mean approaches the population mean","The variance increases with sample size clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'As sample size increases, the sample mean approaches the population mean', 'The law of large numbers: as the number of trials increases, the sample average converges to the expected value. This is why larger studies give more stable and reliable estimates.'),
('f015df8e-dd8b-5aea-b962-d7d760accdab'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'If P(A) = 0.2 and P(B|A) = 0.3, then P(A ∩ B) is:', '["0.5","0.06","0.6","0.1","0.23"]'::jsonb, '0.06', 'P(A ∩ B) = P(A) × P(B|A) = 0.2 × 0.3 = 0.06. This is the multiplication rule for conditional probability.'),
('608915d8-e491-577e-807c-55c05493dd7d'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'A researcher analyzes data from a clinical study. The probability of at least one event occurring can be calculated as:', '["1 - P","P(A) + P(B) - P","P(A) × P","P(A) + P","P(A ∪ B) - P"]'::jsonb, '1 - P', 'P(at least one) = 1 - P(none). For independent events, P(none) = (1-p1)(1-p2)...(1-pk). This is often easier than calculating the union probability directly.'),
('af670743-a6be-5f0f-b6a9-b72b19775530'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'Null hypothesis (H0) typically states:', '["The alternative is true","There is a significant effect","There is a difference","There is no effect or no difference","The treatment works"]'::jsonb, 'There is no effect or no difference', 'The null hypothesis (H0) is a statement of no effect, no difference, or no association. It is the default assumption that researchers aim to test against evidence.'),
('3e2f8196-0e2f-5920-99c1-a1dc1091c535'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'A researcher analyzes data from a clinical study. Alternative hypothesis (H1 or Ha) states:', '["There is an effect or difference","The parameter equals a specific value","No effect exists","The null is true","There is no difference"]'::jsonb, 'There is an effect or difference', 'The alternative hypothesis (H1) states that there is an effect, difference, or association. It contradicts the null and represents what the researcher hopes to support.'),
('64416e1f-088c-5c43-9368-1927898f5736'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'A new diagnostic test for breast cancer is being evaluated against the gold standard in 200 patients. A new diagnostic test for coronary artery disease is being evaluated against the gold standard in 1000 patients. A new diagnostic test for breast cancer is being evaluated against the gold standard in 200 patients. One-tailed (one-sided) test is used when:', '["The direction of the effect is specified in advance","Two outcomes are possible","The direction is unknown","The sample size is small","Multiple comparisons are made"]'::jsonb, 'The direction of the effect is specified in advance', 'A one-tailed test specifies the direction of the effect (e.g., new drug is BETTER than placebo). It has more power but cannot detect an effect in the opposite direction.'),
('4f9b6a34-af38-5ca0-a936-fc4e05e4901a'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'Two-tailed (two-sided) test is used when:', '["The direction of effect is not specified","The effect can only be positive","The sample is large","The effect can only be negative","The null is one-sided"]'::jsonb, 'The direction of effect is not specified', 'A two-tailed test detects effects in either direction (positive or negative). It is more conservative and is the standard for most medical research.'),
('9d061c41-fb0a-533c-a14f-7b6ba7887694'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'Type I error (α) is:', '["Correctly rejecting a false null","Rejecting a true null hypothesis","Accepting a false null hypothesis","Failing to reject a false null","Correctly accepting a true null"]'::jsonb, 'Rejecting a true null hypothesis', 'Type I error is concluding there is an effect when there actually is none. The significance level α (usually 0.05) is the pre-specified maximum acceptable Type I error rate.'),
('439a8863-f0f8-5ab7-9186-ed0b50cefdb0'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'Type II error (β) is:', '["Correctly rejecting a false null","Rejecting a true null","Correctly accepting a true null","Failing to reject a false null hypothesis","Rejecting a false null"]'::jsonb, 'Failing to reject a false null hypothesis', 'Type II error is failing to detect a true effect. β is the probability of Type II error. Power = 1 - β is the probability of detecting a true effect.'),
('7ad6a721-da10-5d71-b467-aae3055ee7a6'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'A researcher analyzes data from a clinical study. The p-value is defined as:', '["The probability of Type I error clinical presentation","The probability that H0 is true clinical presentation","The probability of Type II error clinical presentation","The probability of observing the data (or more extreme) given that H0 is true","The probability that H1 is true clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'The probability of observing the data (or more extreme) given that H0 is true', 'P-value = P(data or more extreme | H0 true). It is NOT the probability that the null hypothesis is true. A small p-value suggests the data are unlikely under H0.'),
('40229baa-5332-58d7-8c76-87059f849fed'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'A researcher analyzes data from a clinical study. If p < 0.05, we typically:', '["Conclude the null is true","Fail to reject the null","Reject the null hypothesis","Accept the null hypothesis","Increase the sample size"]'::jsonb, 'Reject the null hypothesis', 'When p < α (typically 0.05), we reject the null hypothesis. The result is called statistically significant. We conclude there is evidence against H0.'),
('1719b706-6f8a-59bc-8e01-d8599908bc4a'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'If p > 0.05, we typically:', '["Reduce the significance level","Accept the alternative","Fail to reject the null hypothesis","Reject the null hypothesis","Conclude no effect exists"]'::jsonb, 'Fail to reject the null hypothesis', 'When p > α, we fail to reject H0. This does NOT mean H0 is true - it means there is insufficient evidence to reject it. Absence of evidence is not evidence of absence.'),
('98d83d75-da19-5f34-a33b-77121edd7ed2'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'Statistical significance does NOT imply:', '["The effect is real","Clinical significance or practical importance","The null hypothesis is false","The p-value is small","The result is unlikely under H0"]'::jsonb, 'Clinical significance or practical importance', 'Statistical significance (p < 0.05) does not necessarily mean clinical significance. A very small effect can be statistically significant with a large sample size. Clinical judgment is needed.'),
('8235abd3-e3bb-5342-a412-f77dace7d9ed'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'A new diagnostic test for hypertension is being evaluated against the gold standard in 200 patients. A new diagnostic test for coronary artery disease is being evaluated against the gold standard in 1000 patients. A new diagnostic test for hypertension is being evaluated against the gold standard in 200 patients. Power of a test is:', '["1 - β","β","p-value","α","1 - α"]'::jsonb, '1 - β', 'Power = 1 - β = P(reject H0 | H0 is false). It is the probability of detecting a true effect. Studies should be designed with adequate power (usually ≥ 80%).'),
('18834131-d989-53b0-966a-e8bbbead64e8'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'Power depends on:', '["Only variability clinical presentation","Only sample size clinical presentation","Sample size, effect size, alpha, and variability","Only alpha clinical presentation","Only effect size clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Sample size, effect size, alpha, and variability', 'Power = f(n, effect size, α, variability). Larger samples, larger effects, higher α, and lower variability all increase power.'),
('788aa0d2-3954-5754-9a42-90e390760750'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'For a patient presenting with symptoms, Increasing sample size:', '["Increases power","Increases Type II error","Increases β","Has no effect on power","Decreases power"]'::jsonb, 'Increases power', 'Larger sample sizes reduce standard errors, making it easier to detect true effects. This increases power without increasing Type I error rate.'),
('596404ed-c91b-55e0-9acf-7b9678250982'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'A study follows 2000 patients with HIV/AIDS over 2 years to assess survival outcomes. Effect size quantifies:', '["The p-value clinical presentation","The standard error clinical presentation","The alpha level clinical presentation","The sample size clinical presentation","The magnitude of the difference or association"]'::jsonb, 'The magnitude of the difference or association', 'Effect size measures the magnitude of the effect, independent of sample size. Common effect sizes: Cohen''s d (mean difference/SD), correlation r, odds ratio, and risk difference.'),
('2b149ab6-1567-5b9e-8cb4-fe4d7cfde757'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'Cohen''s d is calculated as:', '["Pooled SD /","(Mean1 - Mean2) / n","(Mean1 - Mean2) / Pooled SD","Mean1 - Mean2","(Mean1 - Mean2) / SE"]'::jsonb, '(Mean1 - Mean2) / Pooled SD', 'Cohen''s d = (M₁ - M₂)/SDpooled. It is a standardized effect size. d = 0.2 is small, 0.5 is medium, and 0.8 is large. It allows comparison across different scales.'),
('15fbbc34-a7c7-59a0-955f-74e10319a4da'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'A researcher analyzes data from a clinical study. Sample size calculation for a study requires specifying:', '["Only power clinical presentation","Only effect size clinical presentation","Alpha, power, effect size, and variability","Only variability clinical presentation","Only alpha clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Alpha, power, effect size, and variability', 'Sample size calculation requires: significance level (α), desired power (1-β), expected effect size, and variability (SD). These parameters determine the minimum sample needed.'),
('cb546baa-4454-5278-9889-e6e486d7d8b5'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'If alpha is increased from 0.05 to 0.10:', '["Power decreases","Power increases","Type II error increases","The study becomes more conservative","The required sample size increases"]'::jsonb, 'Power increases', 'Increasing α from 0.05 to 0.10 makes it easier to reject H0 (less stringent), which increases power. However, it also increases the Type I error rate.'),
('1d7f8fff-0564-567c-8378-d8b2b20f775d'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'If the effect size is larger than expected:', '["Power decreases","Sample size needs to increase","The study becomes underpowered","The study will have more power than planned","Type I error increases"]'::jsonb, 'The study will have more power than planned', 'A larger effect size is easier to detect statistically. For a fixed sample size, larger effects yield higher power. That is why sample size calculations assume a minimum clinically important effect.'),
('98682e83-ffff-56b9-8810-0d084f138a52'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'A researcher analyzes data from a clinical study. A new diagnostic test for lung cancer is being evaluated against the gold standard in 2000 patients. A new diagnostic test for breast cancer is being evaluated against the gold standard in 1000 patients. A new diagnostic test for lung cancer is being evaluated against the gold standard in 2000 patients. One-sample t-test compares:', '["The mean of a sample to a known population mean","More than two groups","Two independent groups","Two paired groups","A proportion to a known value"]'::jsonb, 'The mean of a sample to a known population mean', 'A one-sample t-test tests whether the sample mean differs significantly from a known or hypothesised population mean (μ₀).'),
('1fcd140f-63ba-5dc3-94d5-8db5ac879571'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'Independent samples t-test compares:', '["Proportions","More than two groups","Means of two independent groups","Means of two related groups","A single mean to a population mean"]'::jsonb, 'Means of two independent groups', 'The independent samples (two-sample) t-test compares means between two unrelated groups (e.g., treatment vs control). It assumes normality and equal variance (or uses Welch''s correction).'),
('47606d60-4fae-5f63-b5c2-95e505914259'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'A new diagnostic test for hypertension is being evaluated against the gold standard in 200 patients. Paired t-test compares:', '["More than two groups","Proportions","Means of two independent groups","Means of two related (matched) groups","A single mean to a population mean"]'::jsonb, 'Means of two related (matched) groups', 'The paired t-test compares means from the same subjects measured twice (e.g., before-after) or matched pairs. It accounts for the correlation between paired observations.'),
('684e7d1f-6b7b-565e-8ec0-b8e2c015de34'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'ANOVA is used to compare:', '["Proportions","Variances","Two groups only","Means of three or more groups","Correlations"]'::jsonb, 'Means of three or more groups', 'Analysis of variance (ANOVA) tests whether means differ across three or more groups. It uses F-test to compare between-group variability to within-group variability.'),
('ea0c4139-5a38-56bf-9188-6b44462032d1'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'A researcher analyzes data from a clinical study. Chi-square test of independence tests:', '["Survival differences","Correlation between continuous variables","Normality of data","Association between two categorical variables","Difference between means"]'::jsonb, 'Association between two categorical variables', 'The chi-square (χ²) test of independence assesses whether there is a significant association between two categorical variables in a contingency table.'),
('79109f32-c2dd-5614-ba2f-a6d7ef125611'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'A new diagnostic test for HIV/AIDS is being evaluated against the gold standard in 2000 patients. Fisher''s exact test is preferred over chi-square test when:', '["Data is normally distributed","Expected cell frequencies are less than 5","There are multiple groups","Data is continuous","Sample size is very large"]'::jsonb, 'Expected cell frequencies are less than 5', 'Fisher''s exact test is used for 2×2 tables when expected cell counts are small (<5). Unlike chi-square, it computes the exact probability without approximation.'),
('e5804714-99c7-57bf-8611-d817a199c8ce'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'One-way ANOVA tests:', '["Differences in means across one factor","Correlation","Differences across two factors","Survival curves","Proportions"]'::jsonb, 'Differences in means across one factor', 'One-way ANOVA compares means across groups defined by a single categorical independent variable. It extends the independent t-test to more than two groups.'),
('690e435f-688d-5c94-9aa5-3083e4ae7205'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'Post-hoc tests after ANOVA are needed because:', '["The data is not normal","ANOVA does not identify which groups differ","ANOVA is not significant","Both A and B","Multiple pairwise comparisons inflate Type I error"]'::jsonb, 'Both A and B', 'ANOVA only tells you that at least one group differs. Post-hoc tests (e.g., Tukey, Bonferroni) identify which specific groups differ while controlling for multiple comparisons.'),
('9ec4a06f-0b4e-5981-bf82-81c5d3724087'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'Mann-Whitney U test is:', '["The same as paired t-test clinical presentation","A parametric test clinical presentation","A test for correlation clinical presentation","A non-parametric alternative to independent t-test","A test for proportions clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'A non-parametric alternative to independent t-test', 'The Mann-Whitney U test (Wilcoxon rank-sum test) is the non-parametric equivalent of the independent t-test. It compares medians and does not assume normality.'),
('04fabda2-5a52-5f82-9f03-3770bf842f51'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'Wilcoxon signed-rank test is:', '["A non-parametric alternative to paired t-test","A test for chi-square","A test for ANOVA","The same as independent t-test","A parametric test"]'::jsonb, 'A non-parametric alternative to paired t-test', 'The Wilcoxon signed-rank test is the non-parametric equivalent of the paired t-test. It compares paired data without assuming normality.'),
('baf8015e-42e9-5d07-92ca-aaf675618d4a'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'Kruskal-Wallis test is:', '["A parametric test clinical presentation","The same as ANOVA clinical presentation","A non-parametric alternative to one-way ANOVA","A test for paired data clinical presentation","A test for proportions clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'A non-parametric alternative to one-way ANOVA', 'The Kruskal-Wallis test is the non-parametric equivalent of one-way ANOVA. It compares medians across three or more groups and does not assume normality.'),
('4ce89f87-5ad4-543b-99a5-15a8486230e4'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'A new diagnostic test for diabetes mellitus is being evaluated against the gold standard in 500 patients. Multiple testing correction (e.g., Bonferroni) adjusts:', '["The sample size","The significance level downward","The power","The effect size","The significance level upward"]'::jsonb, 'The significance level downward', 'Bonferroni correction divides α by the number of comparisons (α/n). This controls the family-wise error rate but reduces power. Other methods include Holm-Bonferroni and false discovery rate (FDR).'),
('fcfe2a84-f62b-5ce3-ba4f-bd0553beb48e'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'Sampling is the process of:', '["Analysing complete datasets clinical presentation","Studying the entire population clinical presentation","Selecting a subset from a population to estimate population characteristics","Collecting all available data clinical presentation","Ignoring population characteristics clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Selecting a subset from a population to estimate population characteristics', 'Sampling selects a representative subset of the population to make inferences about the whole. It is practical when studying the entire population is impossible or too costly.'),
('0ed5910c-a8d5-5fe8-bbb9-516d2c507ada'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'The main advantage of sampling is:', '["Complete information","Feasibility and cost-effectiveness","Guaranteed representativeness","Elimination of all errors","Perfect accuracy"]'::jsonb, 'Feasibility and cost-effectiveness', 'Sampling makes research feasible by reducing time, cost, and resources needed. A well-chosen sample can provide accurate estimates of population parameters.'),
('d0deec87-9ba5-5bfe-be44-b4fe7bf233ec'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'A randomized controlled trial is conducted to evaluate a new drug for diabetes mellitus. Simple random sampling involves:', '["Selecting based on convenience","Selecting every kth individual","Deliberately choosing specific individuals","Every member of the population has an equal chance of selection","Dividing population into groups and sampling from each"]'::jsonb, 'Every member of the population has an equal chance of selection', 'In simple random sampling (SRS), each population element has an equal and independent probability of being selected. It is the gold standard for representativeness.'),
('45bb65dc-177d-53fc-82f6-153c4208d6a4'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'A study follows 2000 patients with hypertension over 1 year to assess survival outcomes. Stratified sampling involves:', '["Purposive selection","Selecting the most accessible individuals","Dividing the population into strata and sampling from each","Using a random number table","Selecting every kth individual"]'::jsonb, 'Dividing the population into strata and sampling from each', 'Stratified sampling divides the population into homogeneous subgroups (strata) based on a characteristic (e.g., age, sex) and samples from each. This ensures representation of all subgroups.'),
('c44380ba-fdf2-5fb6-a6d8-efe1c40f6ee1'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'Cluster sampling involves:', '["Stratifying by variable clinical presentation","Systematic selection clinical presentation","Randomly selecting groups (clusters) and studying all individuals within them","Convenience sampling clinical presentation","Sampling individuals directly clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Randomly selecting groups (clusters) and studying all individuals within them', 'Cluster sampling randomly selects clusters (e.g., schools, villages) and includes all (or a random sample of) individuals within selected clusters. It is efficient when a sampling frame is unavailable.'),
('0ffd78a2-8327-5c94-b646-38156dd8d5f8'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'A study follows 500 patients with breast cancer over 10 years to assess survival outcomes. Systematic sampling involves:', '["Convenience selection","Selecting every kth element from a list","Randomly selecting from strata","Snowball sampling","Purposive selection"]'::jsonb, 'Selecting every kth element from a list', 'Systematic sampling selects starting point randomly, then picks every kth element. It is simple and efficient but can be biased if the list has periodic patterns.'),
('0160a200-c349-5e9e-b14a-c1ecb88ce12c'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'Convenience sampling is:', '["Systematic selection clinical presentation","Random selection clinical presentation","Stratified selection clinical presentation","Cluster selection clinical presentation","Selecting the most readily available participants"]'::jsonb, 'Selecting the most readily available participants', 'Convenience sampling selects whoever is easiest to reach. It is prone to selection bias and does not represent the population. Used in pilot studies or when other methods are impossible.'),
('afa1f916-dfc3-55d1-8421-7ef5abc4350a'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'Purposive sampling involves:', '["Random selection clinical presentation","Convenience selection clinical presentation","Quota sampling clinical presentation","Snowball sampling clinical presentation","Deliberately selecting participants based on specific characteristics"]'::jsonb, 'Deliberately selecting participants based on specific characteristics', 'Purposive (judgmental) sampling selects participants based on the researcher''s knowledge of the population. It is used in qualitative research and certain quantitative studies.'),
('7ae46601-acf6-54a4-abf5-5894b1125429'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'A study follows 2000 patients with lung cancer over 2 years to assess survival outcomes. Snowball sampling is useful for:', '["General population surveys","Studying hard-to-reach or hidden populations","Large epidemiological studies","Experimental research","Clinical trials"]'::jsonb, 'Studying hard-to-reach or hidden populations', 'Snowball sampling relies on referrals from initial participants to recruit more. It is used for hidden populations (e.g., drug users, sex workers) but may introduce bias.'),
('74d32a72-7303-501b-93d7-ccf40bed06bc'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'Quota sampling involves:', '["Random selection clinical presentation","Snowball sampling clinical presentation","Systematic selection clinical presentation","Purposive sampling clinical presentation","Selecting a pre-specified number of participants from each subgroup"]'::jsonb, 'Selecting a pre-specified number of participants from each subgroup', 'Quota sampling sets quotas for subgroups (like stratified sampling) but selects participants within quotas non-randomly (convenience). It is cheaper than stratified but less representative.'),
('398e8b23-23f4-5d57-98b0-d848f43af14c'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'A researcher analyzes data from a clinical study. Sampling frame is:', '["A statistical test clinical presentation","A sampling method clinical presentation","The list of all elements in the population","The sampling fraction clinical presentation","The sample itself clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'The list of all elements in the population', 'The sampling frame is the complete list or enumeration of all elements in the population from which the sample is drawn. Ideally, it matches the target population exactly.'),
('16d3f749-cbec-5778-9592-527f92167137'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'Sampling error is:', '["Bias in selection clinical presentation clinical presentation","A mistake in sampling clinical presentation clinical presentation","Confounding clinical presentation clinical presentation","Measurement error clinical presentation clinical presentation","The difference between sample statistic and population parameter due to chance"]'::jsonb, 'The difference between sample statistic and population parameter due to chance', 'Sampling error is the natural variation between sample estimates and the true population value due to random sampling. It decreases as sample size increases.'),
('ada6f8f9-47d8-53cd-8e62-c5bd987bdf7d'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'Sampling bias occurs when:', '["The sampling frame is complete","The population is heterogeneous","Random sampling is used","The sample size is small","The sample is not representative of the population"]'::jsonb, 'The sample is not representative of the population', 'Sampling bias arises when some population members have a lower probability of being selected, leading to a non-representative sample. This invalidates statistical inference.'),
('824f552d-fe20-5fc7-b9fc-4d76c021b948'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'A researcher analyzes data from a clinical study. Non-response bias occurs when:', '["The sample is random clinical presentation","The response rate is 100% clinical presentation","All selected individuals participate clinical presentation","The survey is anonymous clinical presentation","Selected individuals do not participate and differ from respondents"]'::jsonb, 'Selected individuals do not participate and differ from respondents', 'Non-response bias occurs when non-respondents differ systematically from respondents. Low response rates increase the risk of non-response bias.'),
('bc9cfb66-1c75-5c43-aaa3-a3e27d048e63'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'Sampling with replacement means:', '["The same element cannot be selected twice clinical presentation","Only one element is selected clinical presentation","Elements are not returned clinical presentation","Each selected element is returned to the population before the next selection","The sample is replaced by a new sample clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Each selected element is returned to the population before the next selection', 'Sampling with replacement returns each selected element to the population before the next draw. This keeps selection probabilities constant and maintains independence.'),
('4c71d05b-ade9-58f0-9aae-09a9c46ac81d'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'Sampling without replacement means:', '["The sample size increases","Independence is maintained","Elements are returned","Selection probabilities change","Each selected element is removed from the population"]'::jsonb, 'Each selected element is removed from the population', 'Sampling without replacement removes selected elements from the population, slightly changing selection probabilities. Most real-world sampling is without replacement.'),
('76adb465-e025-5012-b83f-56135dfcabff'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'The finite population correction (FPC) is used when:', '["The population is normally distributed","The sample is a large fraction (>5%) of the population","The population is infinite","The sample is random","The sample is very small"]'::jsonb, 'The sample is a large fraction (>5%) of the population', 'FPC = √[(N-n)/(N-1)] adjusts the standard error when sampling a substantial fraction of a finite population. When n/N > 0.05, ignoring FPC overestimates the standard error.'),
('ff84fb07-838e-544f-b137-1e4c870b918b'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'Multistage sampling involves:', '["Only cluster sampling","Only stratified sampling","Two or more stages of sampling","Only systematic sampling","Single-stage selection"]'::jsonb, 'Two or more stages of sampling', 'Multistage sampling combines multiple sampling methods in stages. For example, first randomly select cities (clusters), then randomly select households within selected cities.'),
('c1047257-857a-599c-8d18-86c31842f6b5'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'Probability sampling methods include all EXCEPT:', '["Cluster sampling","Systematic sampling","Simple random sampling","Convenience sampling","Stratified sampling"]'::jsonb, 'Convenience sampling', 'Convenience sampling is a non-probability method - selection is not random. Simple random, stratified, cluster, and systematic sampling are all probability methods where every element has a known selection probability.'),
('1ab60b1e-601a-594c-a58e-fabc8d800495'::uuid, 'Research and Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics', 'The best sampling method for ensuring representativeness is:', '["Snowball sampling","Quota sampling","Convenience sampling","Stratified random sampling","Purposive sampling"]'::jsonb, 'Stratified random sampling', 'Stratified random sampling ensures representation from all important subgroups and uses random selection within strata. This reduces sampling error and bias compared to non-probability methods.'),
('da681298-a3a8-5b7c-a1de-0b60c75bae24'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'The principle of "autonomy" in medical ethics primarily refers to:', '["The duty to do good for the patient","The right of patients to make informed decisions about their own care","The obligation to avoid causing harm","The fair distribution of healthcare resources","Maintaining patient confidentiality"]'::jsonb, 'The right of patients to make informed decisions about their own care', 'Autonomy is the right of patients to self-determination and making informed choices about their medical care without coercion. It respects the patient values and preferences.'),
('eb34ad96-3fdc-587b-ad19-d9b35ebebb5c'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'The ethical principle of "beneficence" requires physicians to:', '["Treat all patients equally","Obtain informed consent","Respect patient confidentiality","Act in the best interest of the patient","Avoid causing harm to patients"]'::jsonb, 'Act in the best interest of the patient', 'Beneficence refers to the duty of healthcare providers to act in ways that benefit patients and promote their welfare. It involves taking positive steps to help patients and prevent harm.'),
('44940526-3de3-54b3-b8bd-cf99591026ed'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'The principle of "non-maleficence" is best described as:', '["The fair distribution of resources","The duty to do good","The obligation to tell the truth","The right to refuse treatment","The duty to avoid causing harm"]'::jsonb, 'The duty to avoid causing harm', 'Non-maleficence is the fundamental ethical principle first do no harm (primum non nocere). It obligates healthcare providers to avoid causing intentional harm or injury to patients.'),
('80398af4-7396-509d-9e37-76660df4a21f'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'Which principle of medical ethics addresses the fair distribution of healthcare resources?', '["Confidentiality","Non-maleficence","Beneficence","Justice","Autonomy"]'::jsonb, 'Justice', 'Justice in medical ethics requires fair and equitable distribution of healthcare resources and treatments. It ensures that similar cases are treated similarly and that no group is unfairly discriminated against.'),
('3dd91631-f9f2-5701-ad48-9ad9410c9b60'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'Informed consent requires that a patient must be:', '["Over 18 years of age","Competent to make the decision","Accompanied by a family member","In agreement with the doctor opinion","Fluently literate"]'::jsonb, 'Competent to make the decision', 'For informed consent to be valid the patient must be competent to make the decision understand the information provided and give it voluntarily. Competence is the legal capacity to make healthcare decisions.'),
('935c6089-02be-5f79-9778-08efa23068fb'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'Which of the following is a core element of valid informed consent?', '["The patient must understand the information provided","Consent must be obtained by a specialist","The doctor must make the final decision","The consent form must be notarized","The consent must be written"]'::jsonb, 'The patient must understand the information provided', 'Valid informed consent requires that the patient receives adequate information understands that information and voluntarily agrees to the proposed intervention without coercion.'),
('02198ea3-8ab4-56a2-9b7b-5f77d0754ca6'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'The Health Insurance Portability and Accountability Act (HIPAA) primarily protects:', '["Physician licensing requirements","Patient health information privacy","Patient employment rights","Insurance premium rates","Hospital accreditation standards"]'::jsonb, 'Patient health information privacy', 'HIPAA establishes national standards to protect sensitive patient health information from being disclosed without the patient consent or knowledge. It is a cornerstone of patient privacy protection in the United States.'),
('85d856c3-bff8-578f-9b7b-d768a6e1fb26'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'What is the primary purpose of an Institutional Review Board (IRB)?', '["To manage hospital budgets","To protect the rights and welfare of human research subjects","To review hospital financial records","To approve new medical devices","To credential healthcare professionals"]'::jsonb, 'To protect the rights and welfare of human research subjects', 'An IRB is an independent committee that reviews approves and monitors biomedical and behavioral research involving human subjects. Its primary purpose is to protect the rights safety and welfare of research participants.'),
('8b71983b-23d7-556d-81bf-b6bd764433ba'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'When breaking bad news to a patient the SPIKES protocol recommends:', '["Give all information at once to avoid prolonging distress","Avoid discussing prognosis","Provide information in a quiet private setting","Only inform the family members","Use medical jargon to maintain authority"]'::jsonb, 'Provide information in a quiet private setting', 'The SPIKES protocol (Setting Perception Invitation Knowledge Emotions Strategy/Summary) emphasizes delivering bad news in a private setting. This ensures patient privacy and allows for appropriate emotional support.'),
('2d6c878d-3f54-54e7-9d77-63c8ea3762ca'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'According to the biopsychosocial model health is influenced by:', '["Only social factors","Only psychological factors","Only genetic factors","Only biological factors","Biological psychological and social factors"]'::jsonb, 'Biological psychological and social factors', 'The biopsychosocial model proposed by Engel recognizes that health and illness result from the complex interaction of biological (genetic/viral) psychological (behavior thoughts) and social (cultural family socioeconomic) factors.'),
('28414b2a-1241-5f78-b8e5-464465bb25aa'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'Which health belief model construct refers to a person belief about the likelihood of acquiring a disease?', '["Perceived barriers","Self-efficacy","Perceived severity","Perceived benefits","Perceived susceptibility"]'::jsonb, 'Perceived susceptibility', 'Perceived susceptibility is the belief about the probability of contracting a disease or condition. It is a key construct of the Health Belief Model (HBM) that influences health behavior change.'),
('6b1dd449-372d-5039-b36e-bdd6dace87c4'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'The term "iatrogenic" refers to:', '["A disease caused by genetic factors","A condition caused by medical treatment","A naturally occurring illness","A psychiatric disorder","A communicable disease"]'::jsonb, 'A condition caused by medical treatment', 'Iatrogenic conditions are those caused inadvertently by medical examination diagnosis treatment or other healthcare interventions. The term comes from Greek iatros (physician) and genesis (origin).'),
('36916868-87eb-5523-81e4-5be08e5a808a'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'The physician should:', '["Only provide treatment with parental presence","Require parental consent before treatment","Refer the patient to another physician","Refuse treatment and inform parents","Treat the patient and maintain confidentiality within legal limits"]'::jsonb, 'Treat the patient and maintain confidentiality within legal limits', 'Many jurisdictions allow minors to consent to treatment for STIs without parental involvement under mature minor exceptions. Confidentiality should be maintained unless there is serious harm or legal mandate.'),
('82664ad0-438e-563f-afdc-712518f18e0f'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'The principle of "double effect" in medical ethics allows:', '["Withholding treatment without consent clinical presentation","Ignoring patient preferences in emergencies clinical presentation","Directly causing harm for a good outcome clinical presentation","Always choosing the option with least harm clinical presentation","Performing an action with good effect even if a foreseen harmful effect also occurs"]'::jsonb, 'Performing an action with good effect even if a foreseen harmful effect also occurs', 'The principle of double effect states that an action with both good and bad effects may be permissible if the action itself is good the good effect is intended and not the bad one and the good outweighs the bad.'),
('01056b04-c8b0-5761-9522-3534cca7f52b'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'Which communication technique involves restating the patient words to confirm understanding?', '["Direct questioning","Open-ended questioning","Reassurance","Reflection or echoing","Summarizing"]'::jsonb, 'Reflection or echoing', 'Reflection or echoing involves repeating or paraphrasing what the patient has said to confirm understanding and encourage them to elaborate. It shows active listening and validates the patient feelings.'),
('677d454c-5b85-5eae-8959-7d80d7bba5d1'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'In medical ethics "fiduciary duty" means:', '["The duty to report colleagues clinical presentation","The duty to act in the patient best interest with trust and confidence","The duty to treat all patients equally clinical presentation","The duty to maintain patient records clinical presentation","The duty to minimize healthcare costs clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'The duty to act in the patient best interest with trust and confidence', 'The fiduciary duty in medicine means that physicians must act in the highest trust and confidence placing the patient interests above their own or any third party interests.'),
('a51a6a0e-abfb-57bd-9581-3584cb77ea72'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'What does the Health Belief Model primarily explain?', '["How doctors make clinical decisions clinical presentation","How medical research is conducted clinical presentation","How hospitals are managed clinical presentation","How health insurance works clinical presentation","Why people engage or do not engage in health-promoting behaviors"]'::jsonb, 'Why people engage or do not engage in health-promoting behaviors', 'The Health Belief Model is a psychological model that attempts to explain and predict health behaviors by focusing on individuals attitudes and beliefs about health conditions and recommended health behaviors.'),
('85046324-22bc-57e0-a940-26ab0c93b865'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'Which of the following is a key element of the doctor-patient relationship?', '["Paternalism always","Shared decision-making based on mutual respect","Exclusive decision-making by the physician","Minimal communication","Financial profit as primary goal"]'::jsonb, 'Shared decision-making based on mutual respect', 'The ideal doctor-patient relationship is built on shared decision-making mutual respect trust and open communication. It recognizes both the physician expertise and the patient values and preferences.'),
('c5ea9c4e-bb5a-54b9-8eee-f99dfdcd0cf4'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'The most appropriate action is:', '["Ignore the request clinical presentation","Continue treatment without discussion clinical presentation","Refuse the request due to sanctity of life clinical presentation","Transfer the patient to another facility clinical presentation","Consult the ethics committee and respect the patient autonomous decision if competent"]'::jsonb, 'Consult the ethics committee and respect the patient autonomous decision if competent', 'A competent patient has the right to refuse or withdraw life-sustaining treatment under the principle of autonomy. The ethics committee can provide guidance but ultimately the patient informed decision must be respected.'),
('ce4a22be-21c2-5ca4-8d8d-d2a639a3de20'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'The Transtheoretical Model of Change identifies how many stages of change?', '["Four","Seven","Six","Five","Three"]'::jsonb, 'Five', 'The Transtheoretical Model (Prochaska and DiClemente) identifies five stages of change: Precontemplation Contemplation Preparation Action and Maintenance. A sixth stage Termination is sometimes added.'),
('5a4cfa06-64e3-5751-8f29-ea358e325d0f'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'Which ethical principle is most directly violated when a physician reveals patient information without consent?', '["Justice","Beneficence","Autonomy","Confidentiality","Non-maleficence"]'::jsonb, 'Confidentiality', 'Confidentiality is the ethical duty of physicians to protect patient information from unauthorized disclosure. Revealing patient information without consent directly violates this fundamental principle.'),
('a6df0395-1d7c-5a2a-bee7-150a901f525f'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'A living will is an example of:', '["Medical insurance form","An informed consent document","A research protocol","An advance directive","Hospital admission form"]'::jsonb, 'An advance directive', 'A living will is a type of advance directive that documents a person wishes regarding medical treatment when they are incapacitated and unable to communicate their decisions. It guides end-of-life care.'),
('8eea12c1-8cf3-5f73-aed7-be6246a5563f'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'The ethical dilemma in the Tarasoff case primarily involves:', '["Confidentiality versus duty to protect third parties","Research ethics clinical presentation","Resource allocation clinical presentation","Informed consent clinical presentation","End-of-life care clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Confidentiality versus duty to protect third parties', 'The Tarasoff case established the duty of therapists to protect identifiable third parties from potential harm by a patient. This creates an ethical tension between maintaining patient confidentiality and protecting others.'),
('3044bbba-feb5-5946-b9d2-229936202268'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'Which of the following is NOT a component of the SPIKES protocol for breaking bad news?', '["Perception","Incision","Knowledge and Emotion","Invitation","Setting"]'::jsonb, 'Incision', 'SPIKES stands for Setting Perception Invitation Knowledge Emotion and Strategy/Summary. Incision is not a component; it is a medical procedure unrelated to the communication protocol.'),
('7a30d161-5ca4-5277-ab90-06a2795e7d55'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'Paternalism in the doctor-patient relationship refers to:', '["Collaborative treatment planning clinical presentation","Patient autonomy clinical presentation","The physician acting as a father figure making decisions for the patient","Shared decision-making clinical presentation","Patient-centered care clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'The physician acting as a father figure making decisions for the patient', 'Medical paternalism occurs when the physician makes decisions for the patient without their full input believing they know what is best. It conflicts with the modern emphasis on patient autonomy.'),
('95ef332d-724d-57c0-9e2e-9eff67b7ea02'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'The primary purpose of an ethics committee in a hospital is to:', '["Discipline unethical physicians","Conduct medical research","Make legal decisions for patients","Manage hospital finances","Provide consultation for ethical dilemmas in patient care"]'::jsonb, 'Provide consultation for ethical dilemmas in patient care', 'Hospital ethics committees provide consultation education and policy development regarding ethical issues in patient care. They offer guidance on complex ethical dilemmas but do not replace legal authorities.'),
('2b30f057-e8e6-522d-9103-ec69b165f78a'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'Which communication skill is most important when obtaining informed consent?', '["Using complex medical terminology","Rushing through the explanation","Asking the patient to repeat the information in their own words","Having a witness present at all times","Recording the conversation"]'::jsonb, 'Asking the patient to repeat the information in their own words', 'Asking the patient to explain the information in their own words (teach-back method) confirms their understanding. This ensures that informed consent is truly informed and not merely a signed form.'),
('dbea460a-f28c-53a2-a860-2f26c0fb6689'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'The concept of "therapeutic privilege" allows physicians to:', '["Break confidentiality for any reason clinical presentation","Withhold information if disclosure would cause severe harm to the patient","Ignore advance directives clinical presentation","Force treatment on patients clinical presentation","Prescribe medications without consent clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Withhold information if disclosure would cause severe harm to the patient', 'Therapeutic privilege is a limited exception allowing physicians to withhold information if disclosure would cause serious psychological or physical harm to the patient. It is rarely invoked and must be carefully justified.'),
('45a6b5ff-2da3-5673-b72c-413b43a45c05'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'According to the WHO health is defined as:', '["Absence of symptoms","Longevity without illness","Ability to work productively","Complete physical mental and social well-being","Absence of disease"]'::jsonb, 'Complete physical mental and social well-being', 'The WHO defines health as a state of complete physical mental and social well-being and not merely the absence of disease or infirmity. This holistic definition encompasses multiple dimensions.'),
('c20aa042-2001-51cf-b72e-efffe6a855e1'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'The Locus of Control concept in health psychology refers to:', '["An individual belief about what controls health outcomes","The physical location of pain","The control group in a study","Where healthcare facilities are located","The site of medical treatment"]'::jsonb, 'An individual belief about what controls health outcomes', 'Locus of control refers to the degree to which individuals believe they can control outcomes affecting their health. Internal locus means they believe they control their health; external means they believe external factors do.'),
('0554aec2-1d94-5a2f-9e53-e421063fdafa'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'What is conflict of interest in medical practice?', '["Disagreement between patient and doctor clinical presentation","A situation where a physician personal interests may compromise professional judgment","Disagreement among medical team members clinical presentation","Lack of available treatment options clinical presentation","Hospital administrative issues clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'A situation where a physician personal interests may compromise professional judgment', 'A conflict of interest occurs when a physician professional judgment regarding a primary interest (patient welfare) may be unduly influenced by a secondary interest (financial gain personal relationships).'),
('b4bb9e8c-ba89-54f8-9a84-656b9b5316cd'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'The Four Boxes method is used for:', '["Medical diagnosis","Ethical case analysis","Surgical planning","Prescribing medications","Patient billing"]'::jsonb, 'Ethical case analysis', 'The Four Boxes method (Jonsen Siegler and Winslade) is a structured approach to analyzing ethical dilemmas using four categories: medical indications patient preferences quality of life and contextual features.'),
('51bf241d-90f5-5956-b249-1634115d8c3c'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'In research ethics equipoise refers to:', '["Balancing the research budget","Equal distribution of subjects in groups","Giving placebo to all subjects","A genuine uncertainty about which arm of a trial is superior","Equal funding for all research"]'::jsonb, 'A genuine uncertainty about which arm of a trial is superior', 'Equipoise is the ethical requirement for conducting a randomized controlled trial. It exists when there is genuine uncertainty in the expert medical community about which treatment is superior.'),
('24453fa3-14d5-5858-be61-d7c21820e39f'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'Which of the following is a key component of cultural competence in healthcare?', '["Ignoring cultural beliefs that differ from Western medicine","Only treating patients from one own culture","Awareness and respect for cultural differences in health beliefs and practices","Requiring all patients to speak English","Treating all patients the same regardless of culture"]'::jsonb, 'Awareness and respect for cultural differences in health beliefs and practices', 'Cultural competence involves recognizing respecting and adapting to the cultural beliefs values and practices of diverse patients. It improves communication trust and health outcomes.'),
('9b657eb7-2098-5e56-8a45-07d3377b4cd9'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'The Placebo Effect in clinical trials is best controlled by:', '["Using only healthy volunteers","Using large sample sizes","Double-blind randomization with placebo control","Retrospective analysis","Open-label treatment"]'::jsonb, 'Double-blind randomization with placebo control', 'The double-blind randomized placebo-controlled trial is the gold standard for controlling the placebo effect. Neither the patient nor the researcher knows who receives the active treatment minimizing bias.'),
('a906a9b2-dda5-561a-a813-dd77cbd8a128'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'Regarding clinical scenarios, The physician should:', '["Obtain a court order for treatment","Respect the patient refusal if the patient is competent and informed","Administer the transfusion anyway to save life","Only respect refusal if the patient is over 18","Transfer care immediately"]'::jsonb, 'Respect the patient refusal if the patient is competent and informed', 'A competent adult patient has the right to refuse even life-saving treatment based on religious beliefs under the principle of autonomy. Physicians should respect this decision and provide alternative care.'),
('77d79ec0-5fb2-5d14-bd96-e1e0024e5706'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'The patient-centered care approach emphasizes:', '["The physician convenience and schedule clinical presentation","Treating the patient as a whole person with respect for their preferences and values","Following hospital protocols exclusively clinical presentation","Minimizing time spent with patients clinical presentation","Treating only the disease not the person clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Treating the patient as a whole person with respect for their preferences and values', 'Patient-centered care focuses on respecting and responding to individual patient preferences needs and values ensuring that patient values guide all clinical decisions. It is a core quality dimension.'),
('1ee0813c-cc40-5b4b-ae4f-a8d35bddfef4'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'Which ethical principle is most relevant to allocating scarce medical resources like ventilators during a pandemic?', '["Non-maleficence","Justice","Autonomy","Confidentiality","Beneficence"]'::jsonb, 'Justice', 'Justice is the ethical principle most relevant to resource allocation as it requires fair equitable and transparent distribution of limited resources. It considers who gets what and on what basis.'),
('9f905e3a-1cef-5c95-a315-3100073a21dd'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'The Cognitive Behavioral Therapy approach is based on the premise that:', '["Thoughts feelings and behaviors are interconnected and can be modified","Behavior cannot be changed","Medication is always required","Behavior is primarily genetically determined","Only unconscious conflicts cause mental illness"]'::jsonb, 'Thoughts feelings and behaviors are interconnected and can be modified', 'CBT is based on the cognitive model that thoughts influence feelings and behaviors. By identifying and changing maladaptive thinking patterns patients can change their emotional responses and behaviors.'),
('ae22a414-5827-5457-ac25-aee1fb0839eb'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'Medical futility refers to:', '["Treatments the patient does not want clinical presentation","Treatments that are experimental clinical presentation","Treatments that are too expensive clinical presentation","Treatments that have no reasonable chance of benefiting the patient","Treatments that are painful clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Treatments that have no reasonable chance of benefiting the patient', 'Medical futility describes interventions that are unlikely to produce any significant benefit to the patient. Determining futility involves clinical judgment and may raise ethical issues when patients or families demand futile treatment.'),
('40639db5-cbb8-5bbd-ba4e-be2bbbf815ae'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'Which of the following actions best demonstrates respect for autonomy?', '["Choosing the cheapest treatment option","Making treatment decisions for the patient","Deferring all decisions to family members","Withholding distressing information","Providing complete information and supporting the patient decision"]'::jsonb, 'Providing complete information and supporting the patient decision', 'Respecting autonomy involves providing patients with all relevant information ensuring understanding and supporting their voluntary decisions even when the physician disagrees with the choice.'),
('9fab39cb-0b8d-520e-8099-c0758ca3789d'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'The stages of grief according to Kubler-Ross include all EXCEPT:', '["Anger","Denial","Bargaining","Depression","Resentment"]'::jsonb, 'Resentment', 'Kubler-Ross five stages of grief are Denial Anger Bargaining Depression and Acceptance. Resentment is not one of the recognized stages though it may occur as part of the grief process.'),
('3c4e20f1-ecd4-5705-8bc7-06c313d5dc1c'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'The primary goal of medical ethics is to:', '["Guide ethical decision-making in patient care","Protect physicians from lawsuits","Promote medical research","Standardize all treatments","Increase hospital profits"]'::jsonb, 'Guide ethical decision-making in patient care', 'Medical ethics provides a framework for analyzing and resolving moral dilemmas in healthcare. Its primary goal is protecting patients and guiding ethical clinical practice not protecting physicians from litigation.'),
('ad6beae9-6975-5e51-961f-b3bb7d19f8d2'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'What does implied consent mean in medical practice?', '["Consent that never requires documentation","Consent that is assumed based on the patient actions or situation","Consent obtained over the phone","Consent that is written on a form","Consent given by a family member"]'::jsonb, 'Consent that is assumed based on the patient actions or situation', 'Implied consent is inferred from a patient actions (e.g. holding out an arm for blood pressure) or from emergency situations where a patient is unconscious and treatment is necessary to prevent harm.'),
('f9f4cce9-e249-50b5-a16d-399466e91e85'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'The Hawthorne Effect describes:', '["The nocebo phenomenon","Reducing bias through randomization","Research subjects changing behavior because they know they are being observed","The effect of placebo on pain","Patients improving due to receiving active medication"]'::jsonb, 'Research subjects changing behavior because they know they are being observed', 'The Hawthorne Effect refers to the phenomenon where individuals modify their behavior in response to their awareness of being observed. It is a consideration in research design and clinical trials.'),
('5340a161-bd41-5b88-953b-842d33640d84'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'A physician who accepts gifts from a pharmaceutical company in exchange for prescribing their medication has:', '["An appropriate business arrangement","A standard practice","A conflict of interest","No ethical concern","A healthy professional relationship"]'::jsonb, 'A conflict of interest', 'Accepting gifts from pharmaceutical companies in exchange for prescribing their products creates a conflict of interest that can compromise professional judgment and patient care. Most guidelines prohibit such arrangements.'),
('b67bd9de-6cd6-5d94-9b67-914875bab8fb'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'Which of the following is a key feature of shared decision-making?', '["The family makes decisions without patient input","Both patient and physician share information and reach consensus","The physician makes all decisions alone","The patient makes all decisions alone","Only evidence-based guidelines are considered"]'::jsonb, 'Both patient and physician share information and reach consensus', 'Shared decision-making is a collaborative process where patients and healthcare providers communicate using best evidence to reach a mutually agreed-upon decision respecting patient preferences and values.'),
('4484182d-85e6-57ae-aeba-b1f742a5b930'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'The Nuremberg Code established ethical principles for:', '["Geriatric medicine","Animal research","Human experimentation","Palliative care","Pediatric care"]'::jsonb, 'Human experimentation', 'The Nuremberg Code was developed after World War II in response to Nazi human experimentation. It established fundamental ethical principles for human research including voluntary consent and the necessity of beneficial results.'),
('5c4b4514-5dbe-5127-8a20-cb6fa28756db'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'In the Stages of Change model which stage follows Contemplation?', '["Action","Preparation","Termination","Maintenance","Precontemplation"]'::jsonb, 'Preparation', 'In the Transtheoretical Model after Contemplation (where a person intends to change within 6 months) comes Preparation where the person intends to take action soon and may have taken some preliminary steps.'),
('09c140a3-aa19-5125-963c-f1c6f57c17da'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'Non-maleficence is violated when a physician:', '["Refuses to treat uninsured patients clinical presentation","Fails to obtain a second opinion clinical presentation","Provides treatment without consent clinical presentation","Charges high fees clinical presentation","Prescribes medication that causes foreseeable harm without justification"]'::jsonb, 'Prescribes medication that causes foreseeable harm without justification', 'Non-maleficence requires avoiding unnecessary harm. Prescribing medication that causes foreseeable harm without strong justification (like the harm from chemotherapy justified by cancer treatment) violates this principle.'),
('b63ce12a-31b5-521e-9911-19a73c5d2245'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'Which type of validity refers to whether a measurement tool accurately measures what it intends to measure?', '["Content validity","Construct validity","Criterion validity","Face validity","Internal validity"]'::jsonb, 'Construct validity', 'Construct validity refers to whether a test or instrument accurately measures the theoretical concept or construct it claims to measure. It is fundamental in medical education and psychological assessment.'),
('1d73a01c-95ff-590a-9201-469f62517164'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'The ethical duty of veracity means:', '["The duty to be fair","The duty to provide care to all","The duty to maintain confidentiality","The duty to tell the truth to patients","The duty to avoid harm"]'::jsonb, 'The duty to tell the truth to patients', 'Veracity is the ethical principle of truthfulness. It obligates healthcare providers to be honest with patients providing accurate information about their diagnosis treatment and prognosis.'),
('d92d623a-bb5c-5eda-b144-23070bf8257e'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'A Do Not Resuscitate (DNR) order is valid when:', '["Only if the patient is over 80","The family alone requests it","The physician decides unilaterally","It is written in the patient will","The patient or authorized surrogate consents after discussion"]'::jsonb, 'The patient or authorized surrogate consents after discussion', 'A DNR order must be based on discussion with the patient or their legally authorized surrogate. It should reflect the patient wishes and medical appropriateness not unilateral physician or family decisions.'),
('b199adfd-5dfa-50ab-9c1e-8fb7d1a0cc3a'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'The Glasgow Coma Scale is used to assess:', '["Anxiety levels clinical presentation","Social support clinical presentation","Level of consciousness in brain injury","Quality of life clinical presentation","Pain intensity clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Level of consciousness in brain injury', 'The Glasgow Coma Scale (GCS) is a neurological scale used to assess a patient level of consciousness after traumatic brain injury. It evaluates eye verbal and motor responses.'),
('6b129ab1-6958-5569-898e-9d575b853efb'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'Which ethical issue arises when a physician refers a patient to a facility in which the physician has a financial interest?', '["Informed consent clinical presentation","Confidentiality clinical presentation","Self-referral conflict of interest","Malpractice clinical presentation","Battery clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Self-referral conflict of interest', 'Self-referral where a physician refers patients to a facility they own or have financial interest in creates a conflict of interest under the Stark Law in the US and similar regulations elsewhere.'),
('aeb7a8df-8718-5f9e-9c7f-2668a082acea'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'The primary difference between assent and consent in pediatric care is:', '["Assent requires a lawyer","Assent is written; consent is verbal","Consent is only for adults","They are the same legally","Assent is from the child; consent is from the parent or guardian"]'::jsonb, 'Assent is from the child; consent is from the parent or guardian', 'Assent is the agreement of a child (typically 7-17 years) to participate in treatment or research while consent (permission) must be provided by a parent or legal guardian. Both are ethically important.'),
('03220fe4-9182-5223-af7c-96c8bceb6d63'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'Health literacy refers to:', '["The ability to perform medical procedures","The capacity to obtain process and understand basic health information","The ability to read medical journals","Understanding hospital administration","Knowledge of medical terminology"]'::jsonb, 'The capacity to obtain process and understand basic health information', 'Health literacy is the degree to which individuals can obtain communicate process and understand health information to make informed health decisions. Low health literacy is associated with poorer health outcomes.'),
('9d9b56fc-e6fa-5419-b3d5-d39d20ebe8c0'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'Which of the following best describes therapeutic communication?', '["Communication that is primarily one-directional","Minimizing emotional expression","Purposeful communication focused on achieving specific health goals","Using only medical terminology","Communication without feedback"]'::jsonb, 'Purposeful communication focused on achieving specific health goals', 'Therapeutic communication is a purposeful goal-directed form of communication between healthcare providers and patients that focuses on the patient needs emotions and concerns to promote healing and understanding.'),
('be0dbd46-e35a-5a22-af73-d162130aa957'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'The ethical principle most directly applicable to research involving vulnerable populations is:', '["Non-maleficence","Beneficence","Justice","Autonomy","Confidentiality"]'::jsonb, 'Justice', 'Justice in research ethics requires that vulnerable populations (prisoners children pregnant women cognitively impaired) are not unfairly burdened with research risks and should have fair access to research benefits.'),
('0aa70d81-c0a6-555c-8e9c-cf3802f3d175'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'Capacity differs from competence in that:', '["They are identical terms","Capacity is a clinical assessment; competence is a legal determination","Capacity is legal; competence is clinical","Capacity applies to children; competence applies to adults","Capacity requires a court; competence is assessed by doctors"]'::jsonb, 'Capacity is a clinical assessment; competence is a legal determination', 'Capacity is a medical/clinical assessment of a patient ability to make a specific decision at a specific time. Competence is a legal determination made by a judge about overall decision-making ability.'),
('1b30fd9f-9d24-567b-80ba-80483a01c833'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'A patient belief that they can successfully quit smoking is an example of:', '["Self-efficacy","Perceived susceptibility","Perceived severity","Cues to action","Perceived barriers"]'::jsonb, 'Self-efficacy', 'Self-efficacy is the confidence in one ability to successfully perform a behavior. In the Health Belief Model it was added later to improve the model predictive power for behavior change.'),
('321dffd6-9edb-52a1-bbcf-36fb61c836cb'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'Which of the following is a violation of patient confidentiality?', '["Holding a conversation about a patient in the hospital elevator","Using de-identified data for research","Discussing a case in a private room with the patient","Sharing information with the patient designated caregiver","Obtaining consent before sharing information"]'::jsonb, 'Holding a conversation about a patient in the hospital elevator', 'Discussing patient information in public areas like hospital elevators where others can overhear violates confidentiality. Healthcare providers must protect patient privacy by discussing sensitive matters in private settings.'),
('922d57a1-3d67-5e51-b19c-91ac98afdc88'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'The Sick Role concept introduced by Talcott Parsons describes:', '["How doctors diagnose illness clinical presentation","Societal expectations of behavior for someone who is ill","The cost of healthcare clinical presentation","The natural history of disease clinical presentation","How hospitals function clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Societal expectations of behavior for someone who is ill', 'Parsons sick role describes the patterned set of expectations defining appropriate behavior for ill individuals including the right to be exempt from normal responsibilities and the obligation to want to get well and seek help.'),
('9dba7850-9dff-56a3-8cb8-0aed441bba37'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'The AIDET framework for communication stands for:', '["Acknowledge Introduce Duration Explanation Thank you","Assess Inform Diagnose Evaluate Treat","Ask Identify Decide Explain Test","Admit Inject Discharge Evaluate Transfer","Approach Interview Discuss Exit Treat"]'::jsonb, 'Acknowledge Introduce Duration Explanation Thank you', 'AIDET is a communication framework used in healthcare: Acknowledge the patient Introduce yourself Duration of the process Explanation of what to expect and Thank you. It improves patient experience and satisfaction.'),
('d3aefd4b-84c0-5db4-a656-17b4fad2c6a2'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'Substituted judgment in medical ethics refers to:', '["A judge making decisions for a patient","The physician deciding what the patient would have wanted if competent","The family making decisions based on what they want","The patient changing their mind","Ignoring advance directives"]'::jsonb, 'The physician deciding what the patient would have wanted if competent', 'Substituted judgment is a standard for surrogate decision-making where the surrogate attempts to make the decision the patient would have made if competent based on knowledge of the patient values and preferences.'),
('e42bdf75-6f36-55d1-b94e-179fde1567e8'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'Which medication non-adherence rate is approximately 50% for chronic disease medications?', '["10%","25%","50%","75%","90%"]'::jsonb, '50%', 'Non-adherence to long-term therapies for chronic conditions is estimated at approximately 50% in developed countries according to WHO. This represents a significant public health challenge.'),
('445c3de8-3045-5af8-bffd-d3458530b995'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'The White Coat Hypertension phenomenon describes:', '["The effect of hospital environment on health","Blood pressure rising when taken by a physician due to anxiety","Hypertension caused by wearing a white coat","A rare genetic disorder","A type of resistant hypertension"]'::jsonb, 'Blood pressure rising when taken by a physician due to anxiety', 'White coat hypertension is a phenomenon where patients exhibit elevated blood pressure readings in clinical settings due to anxiety or stress from being examined by a healthcare professional.'),
('042197c8-915f-5113-a23b-a7c74dfdddb6'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'In ethics consequentialism judges the morality of an action based on:', '["The character of the person","Its consequences and outcomes","The inherent rightness of the action","Virtues and moral excellence","The duty to follow rules"]'::jsonb, 'Its consequences and outcomes', 'Consequentialism is an ethical theory that judges the morality of an action by its outcomes or consequences. Utilitarianism is a well-known form of consequentialism focusing on greatest good for greatest number.'),
('8cd23997-560d-5913-ac02-cd9c38c5b606'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'What does Euthanasia mean in medical ethics?', '["Refusing life support clinical presentation","Withholding treatment clinical presentation","Natural death with dignity clinical presentation","Painless killing of a patient suffering from an incurable disease","Administering palliative sedation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Painless killing of a patient suffering from an incurable disease', 'Euthanasia is the intentional act of ending a life to relieve suffering. It is distinguished from physician-assisted suicide where the patient self-administers medication prescribed by the physician.'),
('c3023d60-8450-5051-8f14-868a0b831940'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'The four principles of biomedical ethics as described by Beauchamp and Childress are:', '["Autonomy Beneficence Justice and Confidentiality","Beneficence Non-maleficence Confidentiality and Truth-telling","Autonomy Beneficence Non-maleficence and Justice","Beneficence Non-maleficence Justice and Veracity","Autonomy Justice Confidentiality and Fidelity"]'::jsonb, 'Autonomy Beneficence Non-maleficence and Justice', 'Beauchamp and Childress influential Principles of Biomedical Ethics defines four core principles: Respect for Autonomy Beneficence Non-maleficence and Justice. These form the foundation of modern bioethics.'),
('8b708d33-1db0-59dc-aad0-c301337d747c'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'Which communication barrier involves using complex medical terms with patients?', '["Reflection","Summarizing","Empathy","Active listening","Using jargon"]'::jsonb, 'Using jargon', 'Using medical jargon with patients is a significant communication barrier as patients may not understand technical terms. Effective communication requires plain language that patients can comprehend.'),
('d4ffd5bf-42ab-510e-b8bf-c20f4809d35f'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'The primary ethical obligation regarding emancipated minors is:', '["Only emergency treatment can be provided","They still require parental permission","They cannot consent to any treatment","They are treated as adults for medical decision-making","Pediatric consent laws do not apply"]'::jsonb, 'They are treated as adults for medical decision-making', 'Emancipated minors (those who are married financially independent or in military) are legally recognized as adults and can consent to their own medical treatment without parental involvement.'),
('886c1daa-65bf-5549-8ada-09d32c71ee96'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'The Nocebo Effect refers to:', '["Effects of lifestyle on health clinical presentation","Positive effects of treatment clinical presentation","Genetic predisposition to illness clinical presentation","Effects of placebo on symptoms clinical presentation","Negative effects resulting from negative expectations or beliefs"]'::jsonb, 'Negative effects resulting from negative expectations or beliefs', 'The nocebo effect is the phenomenon where negative expectations about treatment lead to negative outcomes or side effects even from inert substances. It is the opposite of the placebo effect.'),
('ee179ac0-ed24-51ef-aa84-7584ee9e43c7'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'In breaking bad news the P in SPIKES stands for:', '["Pain","Perception","Permission","Prescription","Prognosis"]'::jsonb, 'Perception', 'In SPIKES the P stands for Perception - assessing the patient perception of their medical situation before delivering the news. This helps tailor the information to the patient level of understanding.'),
('517d56fe-ee61-5d58-b67e-1d5d01a407d5'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'A physician duty to report child abuse overrides:', '["Patient confidentiality","Informed consent","The principle of autonomy","All of the above","None of the above"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'The duty to report suspected child abuse or neglect is mandatory in most jurisdictions and overrides patient confidentiality autonomy and consent. The protection of the child takes precedence.'),
('4f063206-7fac-57e7-ad18-9d4287fe9a7b'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'The Best Interest Standard is used when:', '["The patient is competent and making decisions","A patient lacks decision-making capacity and no advance directive exists","The patient refuses treatment","The family wants to terminate care","The physician disagrees with the patient"]'::jsonb, 'A patient lacks decision-making capacity and no advance directive exists', 'When a patient lacks capacity and there is no advance directive or known preferences the best interest standard guides surrogates and clinicians to choose what would provide the most benefit and least harm to the patient.'),
('232f0030-1027-5de1-994a-4440a27e2622'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'Which type of study design is considered the gold standard for establishing causality in medical research?', '["Randomized controlled trial","Cohort study","Case-control study","Cross-sectional study","Case series"]'::jsonb, 'Randomized controlled trial', 'The randomized controlled trial (RCT) is considered the gold standard for establishing causality because randomization minimizes confounding variables and selection bias allowing attribution of outcomes to the intervention.'),
('d9a9fe1e-3003-5bdb-8760-53fc395152b6'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'The Hidden Curriculum in medical education refers to:', '["The unofficial implicit lessons learned through the culture of medical training","The formal written curriculum clinical presentation","Online learning modules clinical presentation","Elective course content clinical presentation","Board examination content clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'The unofficial implicit lessons learned through the culture of medical training', 'The hidden curriculum comprises the informal unwritten norms values and behaviors transmitted through the social environment of medical training. It can sometimes contradict the formal ethical curriculum.'),
('1df7ba74-58d4-5c42-b921-910b70c1dfda'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'What is the primary ethical concern with ghost surgery?', '["Incorrect surgical site clinical presentation clinical presentation","The patient being operated on by a different surgeon than the one who obtained consent","Infection risk clinical presentation clinical presentation","Anesthesia complications clinical presentation clinical presentation","Surgical delay clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'The patient being operated on by a different surgeon than the one who obtained consent', 'Ghost surgery occurs when a different surgeon performs the operation than the one who discussed the procedure and obtained consent. This violates informed consent and patient autonomy.'),
('1986a7d0-1eb8-544a-bcd3-afe10bf701d9'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'The Milgram Experiment is ethically controversial because:', '["It had no research question clinical presentation","It was not published clinical presentation","It used ineffective treatments clinical presentation","It involved deception and potential psychological harm to participants","It only involved animals clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'It involved deception and potential psychological harm to participants', 'Stanley Milgram obedience experiments are ethically controversial because participants were deceived about the true nature of the study and subjected to severe psychological stress believing they were harming others.'),
('2224f748-74a0-5986-9dac-3808651dcba0'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'Which of the following statements is true regarding therapeutic privilege?', '["It is supported by all ethical frameworks","It is commonly used in everyday practice","It allows withholding any information the physician chooses","It should rarely be invoked and requires careful documentation","It applies to all elderly patients"]'::jsonb, 'It should rarely be invoked and requires careful documentation', 'Therapeutic privilege is a narrow exception that should be used rarely and only when disclosure would cause serious foreseeable harm. It must be carefully documented and justified.'),
('3ae7d271-dc11-5cff-ae88-5bac029ded26'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'Patient adherence differs from compliance in that:', '["They are identical clinical presentation","Adherence implies active patient agreement; compliance implies passive following of orders","Compliance is better than adherence clinical presentation","Adherence is about medication only clinical presentation","Compliance is about appointments only clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Adherence implies active patient agreement; compliance implies passive following of orders', 'Adherence implies that the patient actively agrees with and follows the treatment plan based on shared decision-making while compliance suggests the patient passively follows doctor orders without active agreement.'),
('c7919cc9-cc7f-50ff-bc06-530c1202fa17'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'Which of the following is an example of non-verbal communication in healthcare?', '["Writing a prescription","Reading medical records","Facial expressions during consultation","Ordering lab tests","Medical history taking"]'::jsonb, 'Facial expressions during consultation', 'Non-verbal communication includes facial expressions body language eye contact posture and gestures. It is a significant component of doctor-patient communication often conveying more than words.'),
('52cfed77-8547-525d-a28d-b9d46e6a7df5'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'The Principle of Proportionality in ethics states:', '["Procedures must be proportional to hospital size","Every patient deserves the same treatment","All treatments are equally important","The benefits of an intervention must outweigh its burdens and risks","Treatment should be proportional to cost"]'::jsonb, 'The benefits of an intervention must outweigh its burdens and risks', 'The principle of proportionality requires that the benefits of a medical intervention should outweigh its risks and burdens. This is particularly relevant in end-of-life care and resource allocation decisions.'),
('a135246a-dae6-5095-b38b-b7ee7548dbc4'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'What is research misconduct as defined by most institutional policies?', '["Fabrication falsification or plagiarism in research","A mistake in statistical analysis","Using small sample sizes","An honest error in data collection","Not publishing negative results"]'::jsonb, 'Fabrication falsification or plagiarism in research', 'Research misconduct typically includes fabrication (making up data) falsification (manipulating data) and plagiarism (using others work without attribution). Honest errors are not considered misconduct.'),
('4813f824-fe95-5de9-a61a-04def51defbf'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'The Dunning-Kruger Effect describes:', '["Bias in medical diagnosis","Superior performance in experts","The effect of training on performance","The placebo effect","Overestimating ability in those with low competence"]'::jsonb, 'Overestimating ability in those with low competence', 'The Dunning-Kruger effect is a cognitive bias where people with low ability at a task overestimate their ability. It has implications for medical education and self-assessment among trainees.'),
('decf38d8-6b17-5eff-b8dd-b9223ce0acd9'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'What is the most appropriate action?', '["Use family members as interpreters exclusively","Speak loudly and slowly","Obtain a qualified medical interpreter","Refuse treatment","Proceed with treatment using gestures"]'::jsonb, 'Obtain a qualified medical interpreter', 'Using qualified medical interpreters is essential for patients with limited English proficiency. Family members may not be adequate due to potential bias confidentiality concerns and lack of medical terminology knowledge.'),
('921134cc-b321-5f67-91f8-8a4beae91b97'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'The Standard of Care in medical ethics refers to:', '["The most expensive treatment available","The minimum acceptable level of medical care in a given situation","Care provided in teaching hospitals","The least appropriate level of care","The newest treatment option"]'::jsonb, 'The minimum acceptable level of medical care in a given situation', 'The standard of care is the degree of care and skill expected of a reasonably competent practitioner in the same field under similar circumstances. It defines the legal and ethical benchmark for medical practice.'),
('999dba5d-3694-5a8c-99cd-0da14bd50982'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'The Ethics of Care perspective emphasizes:', '["Cost-effectiveness as primary consideration clinical presentation","Absolute patient rights clinical presentation","Maximizing utility in all situations clinical presentation","Following strict rules regardless of context clinical presentation","The importance of relationships empathy and responsibility in moral decision-making"]'::jsonb, 'The importance of relationships empathy and responsibility in moral decision-making', 'The ethics of care (or care ethics) emphasizes interpersonal relationships care empathy and responsibility. It contrasts with principle-based ethics by focusing on the contextual and relational aspects of moral reasoning.'),
('1c6d0e79-fada-5912-9c19-367ce9d2fa41'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'Moral distress in healthcare professionals refers to:', '["Burnout from long hours clinical presentation","Distress caused by physical demands of work clinical presentation","Financial stress from medical practice clinical presentation","Knowing the right ethical action but being unable to take it due to constraints","Emotional distress from patient death clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Knowing the right ethical action but being unable to take it due to constraints', 'Moral distress occurs when a healthcare professional knows the ethically correct action but feels unable to carry it out because of institutional constraints power dynamics or legal barriers.'),
('2a2c0940-62d5-5e88-8144-bac79158f74e'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'Which of the following is the best definition of palliative care?', '["Long-term care in nursing homes clinical presentation","Emergency care for terminally ill patients clinical presentation","Care focused only on end-of-life patients clinical presentation","Care that replaces curative treatment clinical presentation","Specialized medical care focused on relief from symptoms and stress of serious illness"]'::jsonb, 'Specialized medical care focused on relief from symptoms and stress of serious illness', 'Palliative care is interdisciplinary care focused on improving quality of life for patients with serious illness through symptom management communication and support. It can be provided alongside curative treatment.'),
('99a9d6fa-5ba0-5414-8e13-a5b66843f4f1'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'The Dual Loyalty issue in medical ethics occurs when:', '["A physician works in two hospitals clinical presentation","A physician obligations to a patient conflict with obligations to a third party such as an employ","Two patients need the same treatment clinical presentation","The physician treats family members clinical presentation","The patient has two doctors clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'A physician obligations to a patient conflict with obligations to a third party such as an employ', 'Dual loyalty arises when physicians face conflicting obligations between their duty to patients and demands from employers insurance companies government or other third parties.'),
('f20843c1-1caa-560d-ad6a-514220371911'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'Which of the following is a cognitive bias that can affect medical decision-making?', '["Evidence-based practice clinical presentation","Comprehensive history taking clinical presentation","Differential diagnosis clinical presentation","Confirmation bias seeking information that confirms pre-existing beliefs","Shared decision-making clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Confirmation bias seeking information that confirms pre-existing beliefs', 'Confirmation bias is the tendency to search for interpret or recall information that confirms one pre-existing beliefs or hypotheses. It can lead to diagnostic errors and poor clinical decisions.'),
('af611497-f539-5afe-b984-f4b061f34cd7'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'The Declaration of Helsinki primarily addresses:', '["Ethical principles for medical research involving human subjects","Hospital administration clinical presentation","Medical education standards clinical presentation","Animal research ethics clinical presentation","Pharmaceutical pricing clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Ethical principles for medical research involving human subjects', 'The Declaration of Helsinki developed by the World Medical Association is a set of ethical principles for medical research involving human subjects. It is the cornerstone of research ethics globally.'),
('4625d952-9451-52de-a37b-876edd962235'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'When a physician suspects a patient is a victim of domestic violence the most appropriate initial action is:', '["Screen privately and provide supportive resources","Accuse the abuser directly","Ignore it unless the patient mentions it","Report immediately to police","Confront the patient about hiding it"]'::jsonb, 'Screen privately and provide supportive resources', 'Physicians should routinely screen for domestic violence in a private confidential setting providing non-judgmental support and information about resources. Immediate reporting to police may not be appropriate without patient consent in some jurisdictions.'),
('6d8b2b6d-6c35-5ec4-8134-b10e5ecc3841'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'The term iatrogenic poverty refers to:', '["Genetic causes of poverty","Government healthcare policies","Poverty caused by illness","Poverty leading to poor health","Poverty caused by the cost of medical treatment"]'::jsonb, 'Poverty caused by the cost of medical treatment', 'Iatrogenic poverty describes the phenomenon where the cost of medical treatment itself pushes patients or families into poverty often due to out-of-pocket expenses lost wages and catastrophic health expenditures.'),
('3a254b92-ab44-570a-9b1d-cd8f5521adf4'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'The ethical concept of privacy differs from confidentiality in that:', '["Privacy is legal; confidentiality is ethical","Privacy is the right to control access to personal information; confidentiality is the duty to pr","Privacy applies to research; confidentiality applies to clinical care","Privacy is about physical space only","They are the same"]'::jsonb, 'Privacy is the right to control access to personal information; confidentiality is the duty to pr', 'Privacy is the right of individuals to control access to their personal information and bodies. Confidentiality is the professional duty of healthcare providers to protect information shared within the therapeutic relationship.'),
('a3041798-1314-58b3-83e9-6f5da717a906'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'In the Transtheoretical Model what stage is characterized by no intention to change behavior in the foreseeable future?', '["Contemplation","Precontemplation","Preparation","Action","Maintenance"]'::jsonb, 'Precontemplation', 'Precontemplation is the stage where individuals do not intend to take action in the foreseeable future (usually defined as the next 6 months). They may be unaware of the need for change or have previously failed.'),
('94f72af3-b228-5f44-971b-021edd35d934'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'This is legally considered:', '["Battery","Breach of contract","Negligence","Malpractice","Assault"]'::jsonb, 'Battery', 'Performing a medical procedure without consent is legally considered battery (unlawful touching). This is distinct from negligence or malpractice which involves deviation from the standard of care.'),
('63cb343a-4d7a-57fe-bbcf-14db033e55ef'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'The Gatekeeper role in healthcare systems refers to:', '["The patient controlling their own care","The government regulating healthcare","A hospital administrator","The physician controlling access to specialist care","The insurance company"]'::jsonb, 'The physician controlling access to specialist care', 'In many healthcare systems primary care physicians act as gatekeepers controlling access to specialist services. This role involves coordinating care and determining appropriate referrals.'),
('453e1f57-5d4f-5eaa-ad24-8a6a3ab89cdb'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'Which of the following best describes social desirability bias in research?', '["Statistical manipulation of data clinical presentation","Participants providing socially acceptable answers rather than truthful ones","The Hawthorne Effect clinical presentation","The study design favoring certain outcomes clinical presentation","Researchers choosing favorable results clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Participants providing socially acceptable answers rather than truthful ones', 'Social desirability bias is the tendency of survey participants to answer questions in a manner that will be viewed favorably by others. It can lead to overreporting of desirable behaviors and underreporting of undesirable ones.'),
('edab1e61-e77f-50c5-9b34-4771aee80b3e'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'In a surgical context, The physician should:', '["Tell the family they cannot keep secrets and assess the patient preference for information disclo","Honor the family request without question clinical presentation","Withhold the diagnosis permanently clinical presentation","Only tell the diagnosis to the family clinical presentation","Transfer care immediately clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Tell the family they cannot keep secrets and assess the patient preference for information disclo', 'While respecting family concerns the physician primary ethical duty is to the patient. The physician should explore the patient preference for information disclosure and generally truth-telling is the standard.'),
('6660c094-1954-5f71-82d7-38ed11e2766b'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'The Emergency Medical Treatment and Active Labor Act (EMTALA) requires:', '["Transfer of all uninsured patients","Free treatment for all patients","Minimal care for undocumented patients","Insurance verification before treatment","Treatment regardless of ability to pay in emergency departments"]'::jsonb, 'Treatment regardless of ability to pay in emergency departments', 'EMTALA is a US federal law requiring hospital emergency departments to provide a medical screening examination and stabilizing treatment to anyone regardless of insurance status or ability to pay.'),
('ebbd28d3-b4b4-5ad8-a75c-b8ebbbc9080b'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'Cognitive dissonance occurs when:', '["A patient ignores medical advice","A researcher finds unexpected results","A person feels confident in their decisions","Two doctors disagree on a diagnosis","A person holds two conflicting beliefs causing discomfort"]'::jsonb, 'A person holds two conflicting beliefs causing discomfort', 'Cognitive dissonance is the mental discomfort experienced when holding two or more conflicting beliefs values or attitudes. People are motivated to reduce dissonance by changing beliefs or rationalizing.'),
('66b997b0-1577-5ede-b432-2e7998c61bd6'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'Which of the following is a key component of the SBAR communication tool?', '["Symptoms Before After Review","Summary Brief Assessment Review","Stanford Brief Assessment Record","Situation Background Assessment Recommendation","Standardized Basic Assessment Report"]'::jsonb, 'Situation Background Assessment Recommendation', 'SBAR (Situation Background Assessment Recommendation) is a standardized communication tool used in healthcare to improve information transfer between team members especially during handoffs and critical situations.'),
('7648debd-b1d9-57c1-b036-85c48314b66e'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'The ethical principle of fidelity refers to:', '["Being fair in resource allocation","Avoiding harm to patients","Faithfulness and keeping promises made to patients","Respecting patient decisions","Telling the truth to patients"]'::jsonb, 'Faithfulness and keeping promises made to patients', 'Fidelity is the ethical principle of being faithful and keeping commitments and promises. In healthcare it involves honoring trust maintaining loyalty and following through on obligations to patients.'),
('6c613679-61fb-593f-b38b-5cdfc3b48235'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'Which of the following describes physician-assisted suicide?', '["Withdrawing life support","Refusing to treat","The physician provides the means (medication) for the patient to end their own life","The physician directly administers a lethal injection","Providing palliative sedation"]'::jsonb, 'The physician provides the means (medication) for the patient to end their own life', 'In physician-assisted suicide the physician prescribes lethal medication but the patient self-administers it. This differs from euthanasia where the physician directly administers the lethal agent.'),
('f96e55fc-5cc3-5011-9d0d-2f03a469990c'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'The Diathesis-Stress Model proposes that:', '["Stress always causes illness clinical presentation","Psychopathology results from the interaction of vulnerability (diathesis) and stressful life events","Only biological factors cause mental illness clinical presentation","Stress has no effect on health clinical presentation","Diathesis is always genetic clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Psychopathology results from the interaction of vulnerability (diathesis) and stressful life events', 'The diathesis-stress model explains psychopathology as the result of the interaction between an individual vulnerability (diathesis which can be biological psychological or social) and environmental stressors.'),
('3425e0ce-f52d-58bd-8509-e036ad2ac832'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'A clinical trial comparing a new drug to placebo should be stopped early if:', '["The principal investigator changes jobs","The trial exceeds the budget","The drug company requests it","The sample size is reached","Interim analysis shows clear benefit or harm to one group"]'::jsonb, 'Interim analysis shows clear benefit or harm to one group', 'Trials should be stopped early if interim analysis shows clear evidence of benefit or harm (determined by a Data Safety Monitoring Board). This is ethically required to protect participants from unnecessary harm or to provide beneficial treatment.'),
('e9975cc6-f949-509a-bd4c-6f676cdfae0b'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'Which of the following practices is most likely to improve patient adherence to treatment?', '["Providing clear instructions and involving the patient in planning","Minimizing follow-up appointments","Prescribing the most expensive medication","Using complex dosing regimens","Ignoring patient preferences"]'::jsonb, 'Providing clear instructions and involving the patient in planning', 'Patient adherence improves when instructions are clear simple and when patients are involved in decision-making. Understanding the patient beliefs preferences and barriers is essential for improving adherence.'),
('6dd6eede-0513-51af-bb33-947b9f11fc72'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'On diagnostic evaluation, The physician should:', '["Maintain confidentiality as always","Break confidentiality and warn the potential victim and authorities","Ignore the disclosure as it is not credible","Advise the patient not to do it","Refer to a psychiatrist"]'::jsonb, 'Break confidentiality and warn the potential victim and authorities', 'Under the Tarasoff ruling physicians have a duty to protect identifiable third parties from serious harm. This requires breaching confidentiality to warn the potential victim and notify law enforcement.'),
('69ef315f-704c-54e9-997b-8c25d065d64f'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'Defensive medicine refers to:', '["Treating patients in self-defense clinical presentation","Practicing medicine defensively against diseases clinical presentation","Refusing to treat high-risk patients clinical presentation","Ordering tests or procedures primarily to avoid malpractice liability rather than benefit the pat","Using protective equipment during procedures clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Ordering tests or procedures primarily to avoid malpractice liability rather than benefit the pat', 'Defensive medicine involves medical practices driven primarily by fear of litigation rather than patient benefit. It can lead to unnecessary tests procedures and increased healthcare costs.'),
('225e5fa8-2e7b-5487-b6ed-faabd74c23ea'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'The Principle of Totality in medical ethics states:', '["The whole body is more important than parts clinical presentation","All patients must be treated equally clinical presentation","The parts of the body exist for the whole and may be sacrificed for the good of the whole person","Total health is the goal clinical presentation","All treatments must be total clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'The parts of the body exist for the whole and may be sacrificed for the good of the whole person', 'The principle of totality derived from Catholic bioethics states that an individual body part may be sacrificed (removed/destroyed) if it threatens the health of the whole person. It justifies necessary amputations organ removals etc.'),
('1c9a7c75-0561-5519-ba81-383a77310077'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'With respect to management, The physician should:', '["Provide the treatment anyway clinical presentation","Explain the medical opinion and explore the patient reasons refusing to provide treatment that ha","Refer the patient to someone who will provide it clinical presentation","Accuse the patient of wasting time clinical presentation","Prescribe a placebo clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Explain the medical opinion and explore the patient reasons refusing to provide treatment that ha', 'The physician is not obligated to provide treatments that are not medically indicated. The physician should explain their reasoning explore the patient motivations and if needed help the patient find another provider while maintaining the therapeutic relationship.'),
('fd231e3e-4f88-5768-b2ad-65148ca91e6f'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'The Cognitive Continuum Theory of clinical reasoning describes:', '["Clinical reasoning as purely analytical clinical presentation","Clinical reasoning as irrelevant clinical presentation","Clinical reasoning as always analytical clinical presentation","Clinical reasoning as always intuitive clinical presentation","Clinical reasoning on a spectrum from intuitive to analytical depending on task complexity"]'::jsonb, 'Clinical reasoning on a spectrum from intuitive to analytical depending on task complexity', 'Cognitive Continuum Theory (Hammond) proposes that clinical decision-making operates along a continuum between pure intuition (pattern recognition) and pure analysis (hypothetico-deductive reasoning) depending on task features.'),
('8b18e739-199e-5769-b5bf-cb48f4bfe1d4'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'Which of the following violates the ethical principle of justice in healthcare?', '["Providing different treatments for patients with the same condition based on race","Informing patients about treatment risks clinical presentation","Obtaining consent for procedures clinical presentation","Maintaining patient confidentiality clinical presentation","Respecting patient treatment refusal clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Providing different treatments for patients with the same condition based on race', 'Justice requires treating similar cases similarly. Providing different treatments based on irrelevant characteristics like race ethnicity or socioeconomic status rather than clinical need violates the principle of distributive justice.'),
('82020d9d-d0a9-5a43-9a1b-83e897e439d9'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'The Myers-Briggs Type Indicator (MBTI) is a tool that assesses:', '["Intelligence quotient clinical presentation","Medical knowledge clinical presentation","Personality types based on Jungian psychology","Emotional intelligence clinical presentation","Clinical skills clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Personality types based on Jungian psychology', 'The MBTI is a self-report personality questionnaire based on Carl Jung theory of psychological types. It categorizes individuals along four dimensions: Extraversion/Introversion Sensing/Intuition Thinking/Feeling and Judging/Perceiving.'),
('c6e11821-f5dd-56aa-b617-89b7ebd2b38e'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'Which type of ethical theory states that the rightness of an action depends on its adherence to moral rules regardless of consequences?', '["Deontology","Relativism","Virtue ethics","Consequentialism","Utilitarianism"]'::jsonb, 'Deontology', 'Deontology (duty-based ethics) judges morality based on adherence to moral rules and duties regardless of consequences. Immanuel Kant is the most famous deontologist emphasizing that certain actions are inherently right or wrong.'),
('d99ee360-b965-566a-9197-c420e842e5e6'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'The Stockholm Syndrome describes:', '["The placebo effect","The doctor-patient rapport","Psychological dependence on medication","Bonding between a hostage and captor as a survival strategy","Positive feelings between patient and doctor"]'::jsonb, 'Bonding between a hostage and captor as a survival strategy', 'Stockholm Syndrome is a psychological phenomenon where hostages develop positive feelings towards their captors as a coping mechanism during captivity. It is relevant to understanding trauma responses.'),
('86a9a201-a485-5192-bd47-2fbafbcef14f'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'A physician prescribes an expensive medication because they receive bonuses for meeting prescription targets rather than because it is the best treatment. This is:', '["A violation of the fiduciary duty and a conflict of interest","Acceptable if disclosed to the patient","Standard practice","Only a concern if the medication is harmful","Ethically acceptable if the patient agrees"]'::jsonb, 'A violation of the fiduciary duty and a conflict of interest', 'Prescribing based on financial incentives rather than patient benefit violates the physician fiduciary duty. It represents a conflict of interest that prioritizes the physician financial gain over the patient welfare.'),
('5fb739ab-5ad9-55c7-a9c8-8e99ac69bda6'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'Which model of the doctor-patient relationship involves the most active patient participation?', '["Interpretive model","Deliberative model","Paternalistic model","Informative model","Engineering model"]'::jsonb, 'Deliberative model', 'The deliberative model (Emanuel and Emanuel) involves the physician helping the patient determine the best health-related values and how to achieve them. It is more collaborative than informative and more patient-centered than paternalistic.'),
('3f26466b-2951-5b8a-8bf8-d04b99ca6fac'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'The Type A personality has been associated with increased risk of:', '["Cancer","Infectious diseases","Diabetes","Alzheimer disease","Coronary heart disease"]'::jsonb, 'Coronary heart disease', 'Type A personality characterized by competitiveness time urgency and hostility has been associated with increased risk of coronary heart disease particularly the hostility component is believed to be most cardiotoxic.'),
('a2da034a-bc96-544a-b6c1-acd1b28a9753'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'Epidemiological transition refers to:', '["Transition from hospital to home care clinical presentation","Changes in healthcare financing clinical presentation","Changes in physician training clinical presentation","The shift from infectious diseases to chronic degenerative diseases as leading causes of death","The evolution of medical technology clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'The shift from infectious diseases to chronic degenerative diseases as leading causes of death', 'Epidemiological transition (Omran) describes the shift in disease patterns from infectious and deficiency diseases to chronic degenerative diseases as societies develop and life expectancy increases.'),
('e451958e-9a8e-514d-8d36-98cc27ff5dba'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'Which of the following is an appropriate exception to the requirement of informed consent?', '["The treatment is very safe clinical presentation","The patient is unconscious in an emergency requiring immediate life-saving intervention","The patient prefers not to know any details clinical presentation","The patient is elderly clinical presentation","The procedure is minor clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'The patient is unconscious in an emergency requiring immediate life-saving intervention', 'In emergency situations where a patient is unconscious and requires immediate life-saving treatment consent is implied. This is the emergency exception to informed consent.'),
('b3772ce2-2003-572a-a29d-ea9f4acd5d63'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'The Kubler-Ross model is most commonly applied to:', '["Normal grief and bereavement following loss","The development of healthcare policy","The stages of surgical recovery","The process of medical education","Patients receiving a new diagnosis"]'::jsonb, 'Normal grief and bereavement following loss', 'Kubler-Ross five stages model was originally based on work with terminally ill patients but has been widely applied to grief and bereavement following death loss or significant life changes.'),
('51e0059e-0742-50fe-925a-0348c1112b70'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'What does Capacity assessment evaluate in a patient?', '["The patient financial capacity clinical presentation clinical presentation","The ability to understand appreciate reason and communicate a choice about a specific decision","The patient IQ level clinical presentation clinical presentation","Physical strength clinical presentation clinical presentation","Endurance for surgery clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'The ability to understand appreciate reason and communicate a choice about a specific decision', 'Capacity assessment evaluates four abilities: understanding relevant information appreciating the situation and consequences reasoning about options and communicating a choice. It is decision-specific.'),
('b0ebcdbd-d341-56fc-b032-a7a1e76ac713'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'The Common Rule in the United States governs:', '["Hospital accreditation clinical presentation","Health insurance regulations clinical presentation","Ethical standards for federally funded human subjects research","Physician licensing clinical presentation","Medical malpractice claims clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Ethical standards for federally funded human subjects research', 'The Common Rule (45 CFR 46) is the US federal policy for the protection of human subjects in research. It establishes requirements for IRB review informed consent and protections for vulnerable populations.'),
('5cbe9ce9-c3d1-5282-8a93-c2416553d36c'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'Countertransference in the therapeutic relationship refers to:', '["The medication effect on mood clinical presentation","The placebo effect clinical presentation","Transfer of patients between providers clinical presentation","The patient feelings towards the therapist clinical presentation","The therapist emotional reactions towards the patient based on the therapist own unresolved issues"]'::jsonb, 'The therapist emotional reactions towards the patient based on the therapist own unresolved issues', 'Countertransference refers to the therapist unconscious emotional reactions to the patient that are based on the therapist own personal history and unresolved conflicts. Awareness of countertransference is important for professional boundaries.'),
('2907ce18-914c-5883-8e6f-e6299c92c973'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'Which of the following is true regarding electronic health records (EHRs) and ethics?', '["EHRs raise concerns about data security access and patient privacy that require ongoing ethical v","EHRs always improve patient privacy clinical presentation","EHRs make confidentiality irrelevant clinical presentation","EHRs eliminate all documentation errors clinical presentation","EHRs automatically obtain patient consent clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'EHRs raise concerns about data security access and patient privacy that require ongoing ethical v', 'EHRs offer benefits but also raise ethical concerns about data breaches unauthorized access patient privacy and the challenge of maintaining accurate secure records while enabling appropriate information sharing for care coordination.'),
('be84715f-4abe-5ff9-bd5f-ab903ff7721e'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'The No Free Lunch principle in medical ethics suggests that:', '["Physicians should not accept meals from patients clinical presentation","Medical conferences should charge for meals clinical presentation","Patients should pay for all consultations clinical presentation","Physicians should not accept any gifts from pharmaceutical companies as these can create conflict","Insurance should not cover meals clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Physicians should not accept any gifts from pharmaceutical companies as these can create conflict', 'The No Free Lunch movement advocates that physicians should not accept any gifts including meals from pharmaceutical companies because these can unconsciously influence prescribing habits and create conflicts of interest.'),
('3ad61fca-7a32-582a-ac5b-20fa22029b56'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'A patient self-efficacy is BEST defined as:', '["The severity of the patient illness clinical presentation","The patient belief in their ability to successfully perform a specific behavior","The effectiveness of prescribed treatment clinical presentation","The patient intelligence level clinical presentation","How quickly a patient recovers clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'The patient belief in their ability to successfully perform a specific behavior', 'Self-efficacy (Bandura) is the belief in one ability to succeed in specific situations or accomplish a task. High self-efficacy is associated with better health behavior change and outcomes.'),
('4efdaa4f-9a81-5485-a077-3122f93fefa8'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'The Natural History of Disease includes all stages EXCEPT:', '["Stage of susceptibility","Stage of clinical disease","Stage of treatment","Stage of recovery/disability/death","Stage of preclinical disease"]'::jsonb, 'Stage of treatment', 'The natural history of disease includes four stages: susceptibility preclinical (pathogenesis without symptoms) clinical (symptoms appear) and recovery/disability/death. Treatment is an intervention not a stage of natural history.'),
('59d13548-0073-5afe-92c8-464f955ce46b'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'The ethical principle most relevant to mandatory reporting of infectious diseases is:', '["The common good and public health protection may justify limiting individual autonomy","Autonomy is absolute and overrides all concerns clinical presentation","Reporting is voluntary clinical presentation","Confidentiality can never be breached clinical presentation","Only the patient can decide to report clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'The common good and public health protection may justify limiting individual autonomy', 'Mandatory reporting of certain infectious diseases balances individual autonomy against the public right to protection. The common good justifies limited breaches of confidentiality to protect population health.'),
('14fffc69-b074-5559-9efd-df481854df4b'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'Cultural humility is described as:', '["Assuming all patients from the same culture are alike clinical presentation","Ignoring cultural differences clinical presentation","Mastering all cultural knowledge clinical presentation","Being an expert on all cultures clinical presentation","A lifelong commitment to self-evaluation and critique to address power imbalances in the patient-"]'::jsonb, 'A lifelong commitment to self-evaluation and critique to address power imbalances in the patient-', 'Cultural humility (Tervalon and Murray-Garcia) involves a lifelong commitment to self-reflection self-critique and redressing power imbalances. It differs from cultural competence which can imply mastery of a finite body of knowledge.'),
('6afd1f41-090c-5a2f-9e04-29fb64b4c101'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'Evidence-based medicine (EBM) integrates:', '["Clinical expertise best research evidence and patient values and preferences","Only cost considerations clinical presentation","Only randomized controlled trials clinical presentation","Only the physician personal experience clinical presentation","Only patient preferences clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Clinical expertise best research evidence and patient values and preferences', 'EBM is defined as the integration of best research evidence with clinical expertise and patient values. It recognizes that clinical decisions require considering all three elements not just research evidence.'),
('0a7a2427-f79c-55ce-b2cf-a259333feae8'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'Under Ordinary vs. Extraordinary Means distinction in medical ethics:', '["Ordinary means are optional; extraordinary means are required","Both ordinary and extraordinary means are always required","Neither is ethically relevant","Only the patient can decide which is ordinary","Ordinary means are always required; extraordinary means may be optional"]'::jsonb, 'Ordinary means are always required; extraordinary means may be optional', 'In Catholic moral theology ordinary means of treatment (offering reasonable hope of benefit without excessive burden) are morally obligatory while extraordinary means (excessive burden or no reasonable hope) may be forgoed. This has influenced end-of-life ethics.'),
('b1916889-4e34-54ee-9ce4-b1be08adb81a'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'Which of the following is a patient right under the Patient Bill of Rights?', '["Right to access their medical records","Right to refuse treatment","All of the above","Right to informed consent","Right to receive care without discrimination"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Modern patient bills of rights generally include the right to receive care without discrimination access medical records refuse treatment and provide informed consent among other rights designed to protect patient autonomy and dignity.'),
('0b484b59-324d-5d6a-b153-2816316baa66'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'Medicalization of society refers to:', '["Standardizing medical treatments clinical presentation","Improving medical education clinical presentation","Regulating medical devices clinical presentation","Rationalizing healthcare clinical presentation","The process by which non-medical problems become defined and treated as medical conditions"]'::jsonb, 'The process by which non-medical problems become defined and treated as medical conditions', 'Medicalization describes how previously non-medical aspects of life (childbirth aging sadness shyness) become defined as medical conditions requiring medical intervention. It raises questions about over-treatment and disease mongering.'),
('e16800e5-77a2-586d-801b-31d7022d24de'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'The Cognitive Interview technique is used to:', '["Improve medical history taking","Conduct research interviews","Diagnose mental illness","Assess personality","Enhance eyewitness memory recall"]'::jsonb, 'Enhance eyewitness memory recall', 'The cognitive interview is a method developed for enhancing eyewitness memory recall in legal settings. It uses techniques like context reinstatement and multiple retrieval attempts to improve recall accuracy.'),
('38ee1d4c-6655-53ca-bb22-13de16ed777c'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'A physician who is also a researcher must prioritize:', '["The welfare of the individual patient/subject over research goals","The funding source interests","Completing the research on time","Getting statistically significant results","Publishing in high-impact journals"]'::jsonb, 'The welfare of the individual patient/subject over research goals', 'When a physician is also a researcher the primary ethical obligation is to the welfare of the individual patient/subject. Research goals funding or publication should never supersede the protection of participants.'),
('5c5bb8eb-b5df-5ec9-b25f-eb008a38410a'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'Which of the following is a key component of Therapeutic Alliance?', '["Mutual trust and agreement on treatment goals between patient and therapist","The physician making all decisions clinical presentation","Minimal communication clinical presentation","Strict formality clinical presentation","Financial agreement clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Mutual trust and agreement on treatment goals between patient and therapist', 'The therapeutic alliance or working alliance involves the collaborative relationship between patient and therapist characterized by agreement on goals assignment of tasks and development of bonds. It predicts treatment outcomes.'),
('70015a59-9e8a-5198-83fc-83aee8e1f8f2'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'Organizational ethics in healthcare relates to:', '["Hospital architectural design clinical presentation","Medical-legal issues clinical presentation","The ethical responsibilities of healthcare institutions regarding their business practices and va","Ethical issues in individual patient care clinical presentation","Organ transplant ethics clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'The ethical responsibilities of healthcare institutions regarding their business practices and va', 'Organizational ethics addresses ethical issues at the institutional level including resource allocation mission consistency fair employment practices and the institution responsibilities to patients employees and the community.'),
('8dabe443-a2c2-5e76-9782-85776c5f68e3'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'The concept of Informed Refusal means:', '["Only competent patients can refuse clinical presentation","Patients have the right to refuse treatment after being fully informed of the risks and benefits","A patient who refuses treatment is never informed clinical presentation","Refusal is always irrational clinical presentation","Refusal requires no documentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Patients have the right to refuse treatment after being fully informed of the risks and benefits', 'Just as patients must give informed consent they can also exercise informed refusal which requires that they understand the risks of refusing recommended treatment. Documentation of the discussion is important.'),
('5dc568b2-f4ad-58c3-9896-b71d9850e567'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'Which physician behavior is most likely to enhance patient satisfaction?', '["Showing empathy and actively listening","Spending minimal time with patients","Minimizing patient concerns","Using medical jargon","Rushing through explanations"]'::jsonb, 'Showing empathy and actively listening', 'Patient satisfaction is strongly associated with physician empathy active listening respectful communication and spending adequate time addressing patient concerns. These skills improve the therapeutic relationship and outcomes.'),
('bb7e2436-0148-5e8d-978e-b136f3504933'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'The Rosenhan Experiment demonstrated:', '["The reliability of psychiatric diagnosis","The power of labeling in mental health settings","The genetic basis of schizophrenia","The effectiveness of psychotherapy","Effective treatments for mental illness"]'::jsonb, 'The power of labeling in mental health settings', 'Rosenhan On Being Sane in Insane Places study showed that pseudopatients were admitted to psychiatric hospitals and labeled as schizophrenic demonstrating the power of diagnostic labels and contextual factors in psychiatric diagnosis.'),
('0eae57e4-f284-5e62-a44b-ed76905f9eec'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'A patient family history of cancer is an example of which component of the Health Belief Model?', '["Perceived susceptibility","Perceived severity","Perceived benefits","Cues to action","Modifying variables"]'::jsonb, 'Perceived susceptibility', 'Family history can influence perceived susceptibility as patients who have relatives with a disease may believe they are more likely to develop it themselves. It is a modifying variable in the HBM.'),
('0e6ac67d-50dc-5b5f-b3aa-dc987b3eaae4'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'The ethical requirement that research participants be selected fairly relates to which principle?', '["Beneficence","Fidelity","Respect for persons","Justice","Veracity"]'::jsonb, 'Justice', 'The principle of justice in research ethics requires fair distribution of both the burdens and benefits of research. No group should bear a disproportionate share of risks or be excluded from benefits unfairly.'),
('4a700980-aab3-543c-8aa6-28a628e76ce9'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'Active listening in medical communication includes:', '["Making eye contact and providing feedback","Multitasking during the consultation","Using only closed-ended questions","Interrupting to save time","Taking notes without looking at the patient"]'::jsonb, 'Making eye contact and providing feedback', 'Active listening involves fully concentrating understanding responding and remembering what the patient says. It includes non-verbal cues like eye contact nodding and verbal feedback like summarization.'),
('86f58d49-48b6-53a4-8b1a-ec0adfab3869'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'The Slippery Slope Argument in medical ethics suggests:', '["Certain treatments are always wrong clinical presentation","All medical procedures are safe clinical presentation","Accepting one practice may lead to acceptance of increasingly extreme practices","Medical progress is dangerous clinical presentation","Ethics committees are unnecessary clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Accepting one practice may lead to acceptance of increasingly extreme practices', 'The slippery slope argument posits that accepting a seemingly acceptable practice may lead to a chain of events resulting in an undesirable outcome. It is often used in debates about euthanasia and assisted suicide.'),
('7e29b7df-6c57-54fc-8a75-3003bb02f810'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'Epidemiology is defined as:', '["The study of epidemics only clinical presentation","The study of the distribution and determinants of health-related states in populations","The study of individual patient care clinical presentation","The study of hospital management clinical presentation","The study of genetics clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'The study of the distribution and determinants of health-related states in populations', 'Epidemiology is the study of the distribution (frequency pattern) and determinants (causes risk factors) of health-related states and events in specified populations and its application to health control.'),
('99d2e9cf-61b1-50cf-955c-54b238a718cd'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'The Hopkins Symptom Checklist is used to:', '["Diagnose cancer","Evaluate surgical outcomes","Assess psychiatric symptoms","Measure blood pressure","Screen for medical diseases"]'::jsonb, 'Assess psychiatric symptoms', 'The Hopkins Symptom Checklist (SCL) is a self-report inventory used to screen for psychiatric symptoms across multiple dimensions including depression anxiety and somatization.'),
('8bf4c1b5-9583-5bf4-a042-f0efae7ad09c'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'Which ethical principle is primarily involved when a physician reports an impaired colleague to a regulatory body?', '["Autonomy clinical presentation","Patient protection and non-maleficence","Confidentiality clinical presentation","Fidelity clinical presentation","Justice clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Patient protection and non-maleficence', 'Reporting an impaired colleague (e.g. due to substance abuse or mental illness) is ethically justified to protect patients from potential harm (non-maleficence) even though it may breach collegial loyalty.'),
('8eb19114-2e0f-5d31-8af0-c118c555b4e1'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'The Socioeconomic Gradient in Health refers to:', '["That health is unrelated to income clinical presentation","That the rich never get sick clinical presentation","That genetics determine health outcomes clinical presentation","The observation that health outcomes improve with each step up the socioeconomic ladder","That only the poor get sick clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'The observation that health outcomes improve with each step up the socioeconomic ladder', 'The socioeconomic gradient in health describes the consistent finding that at every level of the socioeconomic hierarchy those with higher status have better health outcomes than those just below them not just a difference between poor and non-poor.'),
('94386d7f-e399-522d-8d74-e84b966e1e1a'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'Which of the following is an ethical requirement for placebo-controlled trials?', '["Placebo can always be used","Placebo should only be used when there is no proven effective treatment for the condition","Placebo should be used even if effective treatment exists","Patients must not know they might receive placebo","Placebo is never ethical"]'::jsonb, 'Placebo should only be used when there is no proven effective treatment for the condition', 'According to the Declaration of Helsinki placebo-controlled trials should only be used when no proven effective intervention exists or when there are compelling methodological reasons and patients will not be at risk of serious harm.'),
('d6f0ff3d-d629-5eac-ba36-09806760dcf1'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'The Best Pharmaceuticals for Children Act addresses:', '["Pediatric medication research and labeling","Pharmaceutical pricing clinical presentation","Adult medication safety clinical presentation","Pediatric surgery clinical presentation","Elderly care clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Pediatric medication research and labeling', 'This US legislation aims to improve the safety and efficacy of medications for children by encouraging research on pediatric indications labeling and dosing through incentives and requirements.'),
('e2b02032-13b5-5ba7-8f43-bb2626df0937'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'In medical communication The Invitation step in SPIKES refers to:', '["Inviting the medical team to participate clinical presentation","Scheduling follow-up appointments clinical presentation","Asking the patient how much information they want to know about their condition","Requesting permission from administration clinical presentation","Inviting family members to the discussion clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Asking the patient how much information they want to know about their condition', 'In SPIKES I stands for Invitation where the clinician asks the patient how much detail they want to know and whether they want family members present. It respects patient preferences for information.'),
('f33cd722-f36f-5512-96b6-00d904380d97'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'Cultural Encapsulation refers to:', '["Cultural sensitivity training clinical presentation","Integrating all cultures into healthcare clinical presentation","The tendency of professionals to be bound by their own cultural assumptions and unaware of other","The legal protection of cultural practices clinical presentation","Protecting patients from cultural influences clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'The tendency of professionals to be bound by their own cultural assumptions and unaware of other', 'Cultural encapsulation describes the tendency of healthcare professionals to practice within the framework of their own cultural assumptions without recognizing how these may differ from their patients cultural perspectives.'),
('c746043c-fc4c-5307-aaaf-018734ffbea2'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'Physician burnout is characterized by:', '["Better clinical performance clinical presentation","Emotional exhaustion depersonalization and reduced personal accomplishment","Improved patient relationships clinical presentation","Increased empathy clinical presentation","Enhanced job satisfaction clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Emotional exhaustion depersonalization and reduced personal accomplishment', 'Physician burnout is a syndrome of emotional exhaustion depersonalization (cynicism) and reduced sense of personal accomplishment. It has significant implications for patient safety physician health and healthcare quality.'),
('370c29d1-ad61-5ed8-9486-7af69fd3000e'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'The Tuskegee Syphilis Study is notorious for violating which ethical principles?', '["Informed consent and beneficence","Confidentiality only","Justice only","Veracity only","Non-maleficence only"]'::jsonb, 'Informed consent and beneficence', 'The Tuskegee Syphilis Study (1932-1972) violated multiple ethical principles including informed consent (participants were not told they had syphilis) and beneficence (effective treatment penicillin was withheld even after it became available).'),
('a64633e4-2e5c-5d9f-8f80-c82e9b887cbd'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'A health disparity refers to:', '["Differences in health outcomes that are closely linked to social economic or environmental disadv","The natural variation in health between individuals clinical presentation","Random differences in health clinical presentation","Genetic differences in disease susceptibility clinical presentation","Geographic variation in climate clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Differences in health outcomes that are closely linked to social economic or environmental disadv', 'Health disparities are preventable differences in health outcomes that disproportionately affect disadvantaged populations. They are closely linked to social economic and environmental factors not biological differences.'),
('5142d6bc-2d7c-55fd-9189-c24754fc037e'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'Which of the following is the BEST example of a closed-ended question?', '["How are you feeling today?","What brings you to the clinic?","Tell me about your pain.","On a scale of 1-10 how would you rate your pain?","Can you describe what happened next?"]'::jsonb, 'On a scale of 1-10 how would you rate your pain?', 'Closed-ended questions can be answered with a limited response (yes/no or specific choice). On a scale of 1-10 limits the response options while open-ended questions encourage elaboration.'),
('f128aa45-4cec-5190-a4d1-0e737774c1c8'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'The Precautionary Principle in public health ethics states:', '["Lack of full scientific certainty should not be used as a reason to postpone cost-effective measu","Precautions are always unnecessary clinical presentation","All interventions require absolute proof of safety clinical presentation","Burden of proof is always on regulators clinical presentation","Prevention is always more expensive clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Lack of full scientific certainty should not be used as a reason to postpone cost-effective measu', 'The precautionary principle holds that when an activity raises threats of harm to human health or the environment precautionary measures should be taken even if cause-and-effect relationships are not fully established scientifically.'),
('42ce5f40-c50d-533d-8884-29327e9927a7'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'Narrative Medicine emphasizes:', '["Narrative-based billing clinical presentation","The importance of understanding the patient story and illness narrative in clinical care","Writing prescriptions clinical presentation","Medical storytelling as entertainment clinical presentation","Only quantitative research clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'The importance of understanding the patient story and illness narrative in clinical care', 'Narrative medicine (Rita Charon) is a clinical approach that recognizes the importance of understanding patients narratives of illness. It focuses on the skills of absorbing interpreting and responding to stories.'),
('40bad7a5-a1ff-5ca0-8d65-15c3e68443d5'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'Which of the following represents a breach of professional boundaries?', '["A physician having a sexual relationship with a current patient","A physician treating a patient respectfully","A physician maintaining appropriate emotional distance","A physician referring a patient to a specialist","A physician obtaining informed consent"]'::jsonb, 'A physician having a sexual relationship with a current patient', 'Sexual relationships between physicians and current patients represent a clear breach of professional boundaries and are explicitly prohibited by medical ethics codes as they exploit the inherent power imbalance and compromise objectivity.'),
('c29d2b71-3ae2-5d17-9ab0-dd4b52036d30'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'The WHO Surgical Safety Checklist is designed to:', '["Reduce surgical complications and improve patient safety","Increase surgical costs","Eliminate the need for anesthesia","Slow down operating room turnover","Replace the surgeon judgment"]'::jsonb, 'Reduce surgical complications and improve patient safety', 'The WHO Surgical Safety Checklist is a simple tool designed to improve the safety of surgical care by ensuring that critical safety steps are completed before during and after surgery. It significantly reduces complications and mortality.'),
('77a21b6a-8683-55f9-99f7-a2cace11a5cc'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'Respect for Persons in research ethics involves two distinct ethical convictions:', '["The requirement to disclose conflicts and report results","The requirement to maximize benefits and minimize harms","The requirement to distribute risks fairly and select subjects equitably","The requirement to obtain consent and maintain confidentiality","The requirement to acknowledge autonomy and protect those with diminished autonomy"]'::jsonb, 'The requirement to acknowledge autonomy and protect those with diminished autonomy', 'Cited from the Belmont Report respect for persons incorporates two ethical convictions: individuals should be treated as autonomous agents and persons with diminished autonomy are entitled to protection.'),
('1388754c-02e9-59d1-9355-4cad9edfede8'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'The Lake Woebegone Effect (also known as above-average effect) is:', '["An ethical principle clinical presentation","A cognitive bias where people overestimate their abilities relative to others","A type of medication effect clinical presentation","A research methodology clinical presentation","A statistical phenomenon clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'A cognitive bias where people overestimate their abilities relative to others', 'The Lake Woebegone effect is a cognitive bias where individuals believe they are above average in various domains (driving ability intelligence ethics). It has implications for self-assessment in medical education.'),
('d94a2a84-3a42-515d-9283-9a5e389f9b56'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'Which ethical framework focuses on the character and virtues of the moral agent rather than rules or consequences?', '["Principilism","Virtue ethics","Deontology","Relativism","Consequentialism"]'::jsonb, 'Virtue ethics', 'Virtue ethics (Aristotelian tradition) focuses on the character of the moral agent rather than rules (deontology) or consequences (consequentialism). Key medical virtues include compassion integrity and trustworthiness.'),
('548c6e2d-edd0-504a-96c7-d56e5215b0fa'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'The Theory of Planned Behavior includes all of the following constructs EXCEPT:', '["Subjective norm","Perceived behavioral control","Attitude toward the behavior","Perceived susceptibility","Behavioral intention"]'::jsonb, 'Perceived susceptibility', 'The Theory of Planned Behavior (Ajzen) includes attitude subjective norm perceived behavioral control and behavioral intention. Perceived susceptibility is a construct from the Health Belief Model not the Theory of Planned Behavior.'),
('efaa1060-f861-5263-966c-d164323b5558'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'Guided imagery as a therapeutic technique involves:', '["Direct visual examination of body parts","Using virtual reality for education","Taking photographs during surgery","Interpreting patient drawings","Using the patient imagination to promote relaxation and healing"]'::jsonb, 'Using the patient imagination to promote relaxation and healing', 'Guided imagery is a mind-body technique where patients are led through imagined scenarios to promote relaxation reduce stress and improve health outcomes. It is used in pain management and behavioral medicine.'),
('6f7672a9-8ea2-5706-bbef-60c2f2b3b1e3'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'Which of the following is a limitation of evidence-based medicine?', '["It ignores research evidence","It may not account for individual patient circumstances and preferences","It never changes clinical practice","It is only applicable in research settings","It requires no clinical expertise"]'::jsonb, 'It may not account for individual patient circumstances and preferences', 'While EBM integrates best evidence it can be challenging to apply evidence from population studies to individual patients with unique circumstances preferences and comorbidities. Clinical judgment remains essential.'),
('002a78b4-2e3d-5901-a03d-52eb55b1794f'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'The term quaternary prevention refers to:', '["Actions taken to avoid unnecessary or excessive medical interventions","Preventing the initial occurrence of disease","Rehabilitation after illness","Preventing complications of established disease","Early detection of disease"]'::jsonb, 'Actions taken to avoid unnecessary or excessive medical interventions', 'Quaternary prevention (the first do no harm of prevention) involves identifying patients at risk of overmedicalization and protecting them from unnecessary medical interventions that may cause more harm than good.'),
('6e65380f-71f1-589b-a136-c0457d204073'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'For a patient presenting with symptoms, The physician should:', '["Release the records immediately to the employer","Release only if the employer pays for them","Release only with the patient written authorization","Release them because employers have a right to know","Ignore the request"]'::jsonb, 'Release only with the patient written authorization', 'Medical records cannot be disclosed to third parties including employers without the patient written authorization. The physician must maintain confidentiality unless the patient provides explicit consent.'),
('ecbb2e45-0336-57f5-bcb8-0e2b9f7331e0'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'The Grief Cycle described by Kubler-Ross is:', '["Exactly five stages that always occur in order clinical presentation","A model that may not be universal and individuals may move between stages non-linearly","A linear process with predictable fixed order clinical presentation","Scientifically proven to be universal clinical presentation","Only applicable to patients not families clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'A model that may not be universal and individuals may move between stages non-linearly', 'Kubler-Ross herself noted that the stages are not necessarily sequential and that individuals may experience them in varying orders or not at all. The model describes possible experiences not a rigid framework.'),
('5a9f6317-20f2-5ae3-b818-63204b80a6ea'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'In medical ethics the Sanctity of Life position holds that:', '["Only conscious life has value clinical presentation","Life can be ended for quality reasons clinical presentation","All human life is intrinsically valuable and should be preserved regardless of quality of life","Life value depends on age clinical presentation","Only quality of life matters clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'All human life is intrinsically valuable and should be preserved regardless of quality of life', 'The sanctity of life position (often associated with religious bioethics) holds that human life has intrinsic worth and should be preserved. This position influences debates on abortion euthanasia and end-of-life care.'),
('7e3916da-3bad-56a6-9234-fac5ff2ee9b7'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'Adherence to medical treatment is best predicted by:', '["The complexity of the regimen alone clinical presentation","The number of medications prescribed clinical presentation","The physician authority clinical presentation","The patient understanding beliefs and the quality of the patient-provider relationship","The cost of medication alone clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'The patient understanding beliefs and the quality of the patient-provider relationship', 'Adherence is multifactorial and best predicted by the patient understanding of the condition and treatment their beliefs about efficacy and safety and the quality of the therapeutic relationship with their healthcare provider.'),
('aaea89b3-af3a-596e-9aa5-eaa8bf6ce2e9'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'The Biomedical Model of health focuses primarily on:', '["Social determinants of health","Biological factors and disease pathology","Psychological aspects of illness","Environmental causes of disease","Cultural factors in health"]'::jsonb, 'Biological factors and disease pathology', 'The biomedical model focuses on biological disease mechanisms pathophysiology and treatment of the physical body. It is criticized for neglecting psychological social and environmental factors in health and illness.'),
('9e8b0d25-9dd4-5f40-a403-896c8c64cb44'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'Which of the following is a core element of professionalism in medicine?', '["Maximizing personal income","Working independently without consultation","Avoiding all emotions","Competing with colleagues","Putting patient interests above self-interest"]'::jsonb, 'Putting patient interests above self-interest', 'Professionalism in medicine includes altruism placing patient interests above self-interest accountability excellence duty and integrity. It is a fundamental expectation of physicians.'),
('3668053f-2ab0-55f2-827d-26cf354e5e2e'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'Subjective norm in the Theory of Planned Behavior refers to:', '["The patient own opinion clinical presentation","Perceived social pressure to perform or not perform a behavior","Objective societal rules clinical presentation","Normative lab values clinical presentation","The physician recommendation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Perceived social pressure to perform or not perform a behavior', 'Subjective norm is the perceived social pressure from important others (family friends peers) regarding whether to engage in a behavior. It is a key predictor of behavioral intention in the Theory of Planned Behavior.'),
('87c0a5e5-d9c6-5729-9df4-6b69c8b5bfe5'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'The Sick Role theory by Parsons exempts ill individuals from:', '["All legal obligations","Their usual social roles and responsibilities","All financial responsibilities","Paying for medical care","Family obligations"]'::jsonb, 'Their usual social roles and responsibilities', 'Parsons sick role exempts individuals from normal social role responsibilities (work family duties) and defines being sick as undesirable requiring the person to want to get well and seek competent professional help.'),
('18b2904b-5c7e-50e8-835f-048769e40758'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'Which of the following best describes patient satisfaction as a healthcare quality measure?', '["It is the most objective measure of care quality","It is the only important quality measure","It reflects the patient perception of care which may differ from clinical quality","It replaces clinical outcomes","It is unrelated to communication"]'::jsonb, 'It reflects the patient perception of care which may differ from clinical quality', 'Patient satisfaction measures the patient subjective experience of care including communication respect and involvement. While important it may not perfectly correlate with clinical quality or outcomes.'),
('680a7c96-eae0-5a92-b22b-a15546473523'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'The Gold Standard for surrogate decision-making when a patient wishes are known is:', '["Physician judgment","Substituted judgment","Best interest standard","Healthcare proxy decision","Family consensus"]'::jsonb, 'Substituted judgment', 'When a patient wishes are known (from prior statements or advance directives) substituted judgment is preferred. The surrogate attempts to decide as the patient would have decided based on known values and preferences.'),
('9cae9295-2996-5f01-88a2-705dd079c3c2'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'Allied Health Professionals include all EXCEPT:', '["Nurses","Physicians","Speech therapists","Occupational therapists","Physical therapists"]'::jsonb, 'Physicians', 'Allied health professionals are healthcare professionals distinct from medicine nursing and pharmacy. They include physical therapists occupational therapists speech therapists and others but physicians are not classified as allied health professionals.'),
('fc7ff1e7-f469-5b30-b7a5-f921b89f2508'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'The ethical concept of Double Effect is most often invoked in discussions of:', '["Blood transfusion","Surgery consent","Vaccination","Antibiotic prescribing","Palliative sedation at end of life"]'::jsonb, 'Palliative sedation at end of life', 'The principle of double effect is frequently invoked to justify palliative sedation where opioids may hasten death (unintended side effect) while the intended effect is pain relief. This differs from euthanasia where death is intended.'),
('dfe0423b-1e99-52e2-99f3-cc1a1a701f75'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'Labeling Theory in medical sociology suggests that:', '["Labels have no effect on patients clinical presentation","Being labeled with a disease can lead to changes in self-concept and behavior that perpetuate the","Labels only affect children clinical presentation","Labels are irrelevant to healthcare clinical presentation","Labels are only positive clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Being labeled with a disease can lead to changes in self-concept and behavior that perpetuate the', 'Labeling theory suggests that applying a diagnostic label to a person can lead to the person adopting behaviors consistent with that label. It can create self-fulfilling prophecies and stigmatization.'),
('0fd908e7-8836-5d13-b63d-ad9077002a4f'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'Which of the following is a feature of Motivational Interviewing?', '["Referring to specialists clinical presentation","Expressing empathy and rolling with resistance","Directive confrontation clinical presentation","Ordering tests clinical presentation","Prescribing medication clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Expressing empathy and rolling with resistance', 'Motivational Interviewing (Miller and Rollnick) is a patient-centered counseling approach that involves expressing empathy developing discrepancy rolling with resistance and supporting self-efficacy to enhance behavior change motivation.'),
('2befc7c0-606c-5115-9646-c6650e815067'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'The Confucian approach to medical ethics emphasizes:', '["Individual autonomy above all","Family-centered decision-making and filial piety","Complete patient confidentiality","Strict legal rules","Cost-effectiveness"]'::jsonb, 'Family-centered decision-making and filial piety', 'Confucian medical ethics emphasizes family-centered decision-making where the family rather than the individual patient often makes medical decisions. Filial piety (respect for elders) and harmony are central values.'),
('c82843f8-b6ee-5f07-9598-961b56b4af93'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'Statistical significance (p < 0.05) means:', '["The sample size is adequate","There is a 95% probability the null hypothesis is true","The result is clinically important","The study has no errors","The observed result is unlikely to have occurred by chance alone assuming the null hypothesis is"]'::jsonb, 'The observed result is unlikely to have occurred by chance alone assuming the null hypothesis is', 'A p-value < 0.05 indicates that if the null hypothesis were true the probability of observing results at least as extreme as those obtained would be less than 5%. It does not necessarily mean clinical significance.'),
('85de6b87-a518-5647-aa68-e20e2f476b5d'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'Regarding the medical ethics context, Regarding clinical scenarios, The physician should:', '["Continue treatment against the patient wishes","Respect the patient autonomous decision and provide palliative care","Require a psychiatric consultation","Only stop treatments if the family agrees","Transfer the patient to another physician"]'::jsonb, 'Respect the patient autonomous decision and provide palliative care', 'A competent patient has the right to refuse or discontinue any medical treatment including life-sustaining treatment. The physician should respect this decision and provide appropriate palliative care.'),
('07cf68dc-c558-5761-b883-3276ca50004e'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'Social Cognitive Theory emphasizes:', '["The role of genetics in behavior clinical presentation","Only environmental influences on behavior clinical presentation","The interaction between personal factors behavior and environment in learning and behavior change","Only rewards and punishments clinical presentation","Only internal unconscious drives clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'The interaction between personal factors behavior and environment in learning and behavior change', 'Social Cognitive Theory (Bandura) posits that behavior is influenced by the dynamic interplay of personal (cognitive affective) behavioral and environmental factors. Self-efficacy and observational learning are key constructs.'),
('e6a0c14e-4e89-50d8-9559-fcc93bf46472'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'A physician who falsifies credentials on a resume has violated:', '["Confidentiality clinical presentation","Paternalism clinical presentation","Patient autonomy clinical presentation","Professional integrity and honesty","Informed consent clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Professional integrity and honesty', 'Falsifying credentials violates the principle of veracity (honesty/truthfulness) and professional integrity. It undermines trust in the profession and may expose patients to risk if the physician is not properly qualified.'),
('c775ffce-7fe3-5c25-ba8e-8a5caa3c6d70'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'Which of the following scenarios represents distributive justice in healthcare?', '["Maintaining patient confidentiality","Informing patients about treatment risks","Respecting a patient decision to refuse treatment","A physician being fair to all patients in a clinical encounter","Allocating scarce vaccines to the most vulnerable populations first"]'::jsonb, 'Allocating scarce vaccines to the most vulnerable populations first', 'Distributive justice concerns the fair allocation of resources among members of a population. Prioritizing vulnerable populations for scarce vaccines is an example of applying distributive justice principles.'),
('248de52e-a8ae-54c0-8fd9-f3dcd42cdf9b'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'The Placebo Effect is believed to be mediated by:', '["Pure imagination of the patient","Psychological mechanisms including expectation and conditioning","The chemical composition of the placebo","Genetic factors","The weather"]'::jsonb, 'Psychological mechanisms including expectation and conditioning', 'The placebo effect is mediated by psychological mechanisms such as expectation (believing treatment will work) and conditioning (previous positive experiences). Neurobiological mechanisms including endorphin release also contribute.'),
('f91de546-95ed-537e-9e96-0797efb95707'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'MSE is performed. A 70-year-old male is brought to the clinic by family members who report Social withdrawal. A researcher fails to obtain IRB approval before starting a study. This is:', '["Acceptable if the study is low-risk","Always approved retroactively","A serious ethical violation that violates regulations protecting human subjects","Only a problem if the study has negative results","A minor oversight that can be corrected later"]'::jsonb, 'A serious ethical violation that violates regulations protecting human subjects', 'Failure to obtain IRB approval before initiating research involving human subjects is a serious violation of research ethics regulations. Studies cannot be approved retroactively.'),
('9947be38-d2a2-565f-b067-3d05cacde87f'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'Cultural safety in healthcare goes beyond cultural competence to include:', '["Memorizing cultural facts about patients clinical presentation","An understanding of power differentials and the impact of colonization and discrimination on health","Learning a foreign language clinical presentation","Only treating patients from one own culture clinical presentation","Avoiding cultural discussions clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'An understanding of power differentials and the impact of colonization and discrimination on health', 'Cultural safety (developed in New Zealand/Canada) emphasizes understanding power relationships and addressing the impact of historical and social contexts such as colonization on health. It requires healthcare providers to reflect on their own cultural identity and biases.'),
('8c707f64-503a-5793-9fca-ac0365b41dd4'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'The Disability Paradox refers to:', '["Disability always reduces happiness","Disability is always a tragedy","Many people with disabilities report good quality of life despite their limitations","Only able-bodied people can have good quality of life","Disabled people always report poor quality of life"]'::jsonb, 'Many people with disabilities report good quality of life despite their limitations', 'The disability paradox describes the finding that many people with serious or chronic disabilities report a quality of life that is good or even excellent contrary to assumptions by able-bodied observers. This has implications for end-of-life and disability ethics.'),
('bd207568-e44a-5098-8279-f9f5cb80cdf7'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'Significance level (alpha) in statistical testing is typically set at:', '["0.05","0.001","0.50","0.01","0.10"]'::jsonb, '0.05', 'The conventional significance level (alpha) in medical research is 0.05 meaning a 5% risk of concluding that a difference exists when there is no actual difference (Type I error).'),
('22c5ff00-fb3a-5da0-b6b6-afb2db1cf822'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'The consent from a child parent for the child medical treatment is legally called:', '["Assent","Informed consent","Parental permission","Implied consent","Expressed consent"]'::jsonb, 'Parental permission', 'Legally the term parental permission is used when parents authorize treatment for their child while assent refers to the child agreement. This distinction clarifies the different roles in pediatric decision-making.'),
('0d98cf6f-02b0-5b42-b44e-3565015d755b'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'Emotional Intelligence (EQ) in physicians is associated with:', '["Better patient communication and empathy","Lower clinical knowledge","More medical errors","Poorer teamwork","Lower patient satisfaction"]'::jsonb, 'Better patient communication and empathy', 'Emotional intelligence the ability to recognize understand and manage emotions in oneself and others is associated with better physician-patient communication empathy teamwork and patient satisfaction.'),
('1c014c11-f5a5-5430-a65d-521bbec35120'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'Which of the following is an example of primary prevention?', '["Rehabilitation after stroke","Physical therapy after surgery","Administering vaccines to prevent infectious diseases","Medication to prevent diabetes complications","Mammography screening for breast cancer"]'::jsonb, 'Administering vaccines to prevent infectious diseases', 'Primary prevention aims to prevent disease before it occurs through measures like vaccination health education and lifestyle modification. Mammography is secondary prevention (early detection).'),
('dff95b14-c30a-509e-92aa-ea7c84955689'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'A healthcare proxy is:', '["A person legally authorized to make healthcare decisions for an incapacitated patient","The physician assigned to the patient clinical presentation","An insurance representative clinical presentation","A hospital administrator clinical presentation","A type of advance directive document clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'A person legally authorized to make healthcare decisions for an incapacitated patient', 'A healthcare proxy (also called durable power of attorney for healthcare) is a person designated to make medical decisions on behalf of a patient if the patient becomes unable to make decisions themselves.'),
('5208d7dd-0e12-5753-a355-bf117d2af724'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'The Heisenberg Uncertainty Principle in the context of clinical measurement reminds us that:', '["The observer has no effect on the system","Measurements are always precise","Only physical measurements matter","The act of measuring can change what is being measured","Clinical measurements are always objective"]'::jsonb, 'The act of measuring can change what is being measured', 'Applied metaphorically to clinical medicine the observer effect (akin to Heisenberg principle in physics) reminds that the process of measurement and observation in clinical encounters can change patient behavior and physiology such as in white coat hypertension.'),
('cd2b4794-9f78-5964-b959-5c6196339caa'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'Medical paternalism is justified when:', '["The patient refuses treatment clinical presentation","The patient is temporarily incapacitated and treatment is needed to prevent imminent serious harm","The patient is competent and informed clinical presentation","The patient requests information clinical presentation","The family disagrees with the patient clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'The patient is temporarily incapacitated and treatment is needed to prevent imminent serious harm', 'Paternalism is most justified in emergencies where a patient is temporarily incapacitated (e.g. unconscious after an accident) and immediate intervention is necessary to prevent death or serious harm. This is sometimes called weak paternalism.'),
('f8546c59-b29b-5ee4-b8d2-69e5469a9548'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'Which of the following is a key feature of Patient-Reported Outcome Measures (PROMs)?', '["They directly capture the patient perspective about their health status","They replace clinical examination clinical presentation","They measure laboratory values clinical presentation","They are completed by physicians clinical presentation","They are only used in research clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'They directly capture the patient perspective about their health status', 'PROMs are instruments that collect health status information directly from the patient without interpretation by clinicians or others. They capture the patient own view of their symptoms functioning and quality of life.'),
('60f0a9b2-4c9f-525b-9ebb-10fa9adbf628'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'The Default Effect in organ donation refers to:', '["The standard surgical technique for organ retrieval","The impact of presumed consent (opt-out) policies on donation rates","The physiological default in transplant rejection","Most people opting out of donation","The legal default regarding organ allocation"]'::jsonb, 'The impact of presumed consent (opt-out) policies on donation rates', 'The default effect describes how choice architecture significantly influences outcomes. Countries with presumed consent (opt-out) systems for organ donation generally have higher donation rates than opt-in systems.'),
('31abd221-f5ea-563f-805e-9fb2e64af0aa'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'Transference in the doctor-patient relationship refers to:', '["Transferring insurance coverage","The physician transferring care to another provider","Transferring patients to other facilities","The patient unconsciously transferring feelings from past relationships onto the physician","Transferring medical records between hospitals"]'::jsonb, 'The patient unconsciously transferring feelings from past relationships onto the physician', 'Transference describes the phenomenon where patients unconsciously redirect feelings and expectations from important figures in their past (often parents) onto their physician or therapist. It can affect the therapeutic relationship.'),
('b263fcc1-11c6-5be1-8641-8c2245777917'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'The Stanford Prison Experiment raised ethical concerns about:', '["Cost overruns clinical presentation","Animal cruelty clinical presentation","Statistical methods clinical presentation","Ineffective medical treatment clinical presentation","Lack of informed consent and psychological harm to participants"]'::jsonb, 'Lack of informed consent and psychological harm to participants', 'Zimbardo Stanford Prison Experiment was ethically controversial due to the severe psychological distress experienced by participants lack of adequate informed consent and the failure to stop the study earlier despite clear harm to participants.'),
('08cab670-f71a-5cf6-ac7f-53a046f3e053'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'Which of the following best defines health equity?', '["Equal spending on every patient","Everyone having equal access to healthcare only","Equal health outcomes for everyone","Everyone receiving the same treatment","The absence of unfair and avoidable differences in health among groups of people"]'::jsonb, 'The absence of unfair and avoidable differences in health among groups of people', 'Health equity means that everyone has a fair and just opportunity to be as healthy as possible. This requires removing obstacles to health such as poverty discrimination and their consequences.'),
('14f0334f-2a04-5a5c-8f30-53d5b5a7dd21'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'The Pittsburgh Sleep Quality Index (PSQI) is:', '["Surgical evaluation for sleep problems","A measure of dream content","A measure of sleep quality and disturbances","A diagnostic test for sleep apnea","A blood test for sleep disorders"]'::jsonb, 'A measure of sleep quality and disturbances', 'The PSQI is a self-report questionnaire that assesses sleep quality over a one-month interval. It measures seven components including subjective sleep quality sleep latency duration efficiency disturbances and daytime dysfunction.'),
('295332cb-5e61-5ee9-8c14-122376fa3966'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'Which statement about informed consent for research is TRUE?', '["Consent is a one-time event at enrollment","Informed consent is an ongoing process that continues throughout the study","Consent forms need not be signed","Only principal investigators can obtain consent","IRB approval replaces the need for consent"]'::jsonb, 'Informed consent is an ongoing process that continues throughout the study', 'Informed consent in research is an ongoing process of information exchange and reaffirmation not a single event at enrollment. Participants should be informed of changes and can withdraw at any time.'),
('275dad53-093a-519f-aa85-faff1a5a10aa'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'Deception in research is ethically acceptable when:', '["It saves money clinical presentation","It is necessary for the study validity the study has significant potential benefit and participan","It makes recruitment easier clinical presentation","Participants are never told the truth clinical presentation","It is always acceptable clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'It is necessary for the study validity the study has significant potential benefit and participan', 'Deception in research is only acceptable when it is necessary for methodological reasons the research has significant value no alternative exists and participants will be debriefed as early as possible about the deception.'),
('a0544649-51a6-5da1-af44-3538c1826b64'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'The Concept of Health Control includes all of the following LOCI EXCEPT:', '["External health locus of control","Chance health locus of control","Internal health locus of control","Genetic health locus of control","Powerful others health locus of control"]'::jsonb, 'Genetic health locus of control', 'The Multidimensional Health Locus of Control scales measure three dimensions: Internal Powerful Others and Chance. Genetic is not a recognized locus of control dimension in this model.'),
('47352145-daea-5d9b-afc9-8b57c666da2b'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'Social Support has been shown to:', '["Only be relevant for elderly patients","Have no effect on health outcomes","Improve health outcomes and buffer against stress","Only matter for mental health","Negatively affect health"]'::jsonb, 'Improve health outcomes and buffer against stress', 'Social support (emotional informational instrumental support from social networks) is consistently associated with better health outcomes and serves as a buffer against the negative health effects of stress.'),
('8a77d3a2-ef9d-5032-8233-60f0bcd21468'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'Which of the following is a legal document that appoints someone to make healthcare decisions for a person if they become incapacitated?', '["Medical license","Durable power of attorney for healthcare","Living will","Health insurance card","Last will and testament"]'::jsonb, 'Durable power of attorney for healthcare', 'The durable power of attorney for healthcare (healthcare proxy) is a legal document that designates an individual to make healthcare decisions for a person if they become unable to make decisions themselves.'),
('98300a01-c34d-5d65-929e-9dad8f7b7a31'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'The Common Cold and its relationship to psychological stress is an example of:', '["Allergic reaction clinical presentation clinical presentation","Psychoneuroimmunology - the interaction between psychological processes and the immune system","The placebo effect clinical presentation clinical presentation","Genetic predisposition clinical presentation clinical presentation","Environmental toxins clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Psychoneuroimmunology - the interaction between psychological processes and the immune system', 'Psychoneuroimmunology studies the interactions between psychological processes (stress emotion) the nervous system and the immune system. Stress has been shown to increase susceptibility to viral infections like the common cold.'),
('389c5794-bd2c-5b41-81a1-c1b72d3e5e05'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'The Biopsychosocial Model was proposed by:', '["Hippocrates","Freud","Bandura","George Engel","Beauchamp"]'::jsonb, 'George Engel', 'George Engel proposed the biopsychosocial model in 1977 as a critique of the biomedical model. He argued that a comprehensive understanding of illness must include biological psychological and social dimensions.'),
('48a78873-acfa-575a-ae2f-ec329aa4a4c0'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'A physician who prescribes antibiotics for a viral infection to satisfy a patient request is:', '["Practicing evidence-based medicine clinical presentation","Providing good patient-centered care clinical presentation","Acting unethically by contributing to antibiotic resistance and violating beneficence","Following the principle of autonomy clinical presentation","Respecting non-maleficence clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Acting unethically by contributing to antibiotic resistance and violating beneficence', 'Prescribing antibiotics for viral infections is inappropriate contributes to antibiotic resistance and violates beneficence (doing good) and non-maleficence (avoiding harm). Patient requests do not obligate physicians to provide unnecessary or harmful treatments.'),
('b58fbf81-f451-5cba-bae2-a4f7a6d1824d'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'Framing Effect in medical decision-making refers to:', '["The structure of medical education clinical presentation","How the presentation of information (gain vs. loss framing) influences decisions","The organizational framework of hospitals clinical presentation","The legal framework for healthcare clinical presentation","The physical frame of medical equipment clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'How the presentation of information (gain vs. loss framing) influences decisions', 'The framing effect is a cognitive bias where people react differently to a choice depending on whether it is presented as a loss or a gain. For example patients may choose differently if told 90% survival vs. 10% mortality for the same procedure.'),
('360f98ce-b4e7-5379-95e9-4bf76dc8b3e9'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'Which of the following is a characteristic of Health Maintenance Organizations (HMOs)?', '["Fee-for-service payment model","Emphasis on preventive care and managed care through a network of providers","No limits on out-of-network care","Patients pay all costs upfront","Patients can see any specialist without referral"]'::jsonb, 'Emphasis on preventive care and managed care through a network of providers', 'HMOs are managed care organizations that emphasize preventive care and coordinate care through a network of providers. Primary care physicians often serve as gatekeepers and referrals are required for specialists.'),
('52433a16-0abc-5f3d-8dae-74b9570d0112'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'The principle of Respect for Autonomy does NOT require:', '["Forcing patients to make decisions they do not want to make","Supporting patients in their decisions","Respecting irrational or harmful decisions made by competent patients","Providing information needed for decision-making","Allowing competent patients to make their own decisions"]'::jsonb, 'Forcing patients to make decisions they do not want to make', 'Respecting autonomy involves supporting patients decision-making but does not require forcing unwilling patients to make decisions. Patients can waive decision-making and delegate it to others if they choose.'),
('b4d1f071-36b3-5446-970c-bf8b6242fd96'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'The Self-Fulfilling Prophecy in healthcare refers to:', '["Patients predicting their own deaths","Prophecies about medical discoveries","Predictions always coming true","A prediction that directly or indirectly causes itself to become true","Physicians predicting outcomes with certainty"]'::jsonb, 'A prediction that directly or indirectly causes itself to become true', 'A self-fulfilling prophecy is a belief or expectation that influences behavior in ways that make the belief come true. In healthcare a physician expectation about a patient outcome can unconsciously influence treatment decisions and patient behavior.'),
('6b4c100e-d49e-51a9-bf52-c09810e9bb44'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'A physician obligation to maintain confidentiality continues:', '["Until the patient requests otherwise","Indefinitely even after the patient death","Only during active treatment","Only for five years","For a limited time after treatment ends"]'::jsonb, 'Indefinitely even after the patient death', 'The duty of confidentiality extends beyond the patient death. Even after a patient dies healthcare providers generally cannot disclose information without appropriate authorization (usually from the estate or as required by law).'),
('a665194c-8e7d-5e43-81be-f8a68f4ef57a'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'Which ethical theory focuses on the greatest good for the greatest number?', '["Virtue ethics","Rights-based ethics","Utilitarianism","Deontology","Natural law theory"]'::jsonb, 'Utilitarianism', 'Utilitarianism (a form of consequentialism associated with Bentham and Mill) holds that the morally right action is the one that maximizes overall happiness or well-being for the greatest number of people.'),
('c0057c37-e984-53eb-ae9f-21c795522378'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'The Two-Physician Rule for medical assistance in dying (MAID) typically requires:', '["The patient must see two specialists","The procedure must be done in two stages","Two family members must consent","Two physicians must independently confirm eligibility criteria","Two physicians must perform the procedure"]'::jsonb, 'Two physicians must independently confirm eligibility criteria', 'In MAID legislation two physicians must independently assess and confirm that the patient meets all eligibility criteria including capacity grievous and irremediable condition and voluntary request to prevent errors or coercion.'),
('874c1b57-78cd-5b1b-9e2f-812a3a3c7376'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'Health behavior is defined as:', '["The behavior of insurance companies clinical presentation","The behavior of doctors clinical presentation","Any activity undertaken by a person believing themselves to be healthy for the purpose of prevent","The behavior of healthcare systems clinical presentation","The behavior of hospitals clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Any activity undertaken by a person believing themselves to be healthy for the purpose of prevent', 'Health behavior (Kasl and Cobb) refers to activities undertaken by people who believe themselves to be healthy to prevent disease or detect it in an asymptomatic stage. It includes lifestyle choices screening and vaccination.'),
('6c25684c-f71d-574f-95ac-73046eec1959'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'The appropriate decision-making model requires:', '["No consent is needed for minors","The child can make the decision independently","The child consent alone","Only the surgeon decision","Parental permission and the child assent if developmentally appropriate"]'::jsonb, 'Parental permission and the child assent if developmentally appropriate', 'For pediatric patients parents provide legal permission while the child assent should be obtained if developmentally appropriate (typically age 7+). This respects the child developing autonomy while recognizing parental authority.'),
('f1578d80-95f9-59d8-9ad9-9793a9e04cfe'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'The Broken Heart Syndrome (Takotsubo cardiomyopathy) is associated with:', '["Valvular heart disease","Coronary artery disease","Genetic cardiomyopathy","Viral infection","Acute emotional or physical stress"]'::jsonb, 'Acute emotional or physical stress', 'Takotsubo cardiomyopathy (stress-induced cardiomyopathy or broken heart syndrome) is a temporary heart condition often triggered by intense emotional or physical stress. It mimics heart attack but without coronary artery obstruction.'),
('e64bfd18-8bf5-5425-b59b-91c7fcd5a198'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'Coping is defined as:', '["Constantly changing cognitive and behavioral efforts to manage specific external and/or internal","The avoidance of all challenges clinical presentation","Medication management clinical presentation","The absence of stress clinical presentation","The elimination of all stressors clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Constantly changing cognitive and behavioral efforts to manage specific external and/or internal', 'Lazarus and Folkman define coping as constantly changing cognitive and behavioral efforts to manage specific demands appraised as taxing or exceeding resources. Coping can be problem-focused or emotion-focused.'),
('4309b222-94ee-56cc-872c-d203573c32da'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'The Justice principle in the context of pandemics may require:', '["Random allocation as the only fair method","Priority to healthcare workers and the most vulnerable when allocating scarce resources","Only paying patients receive care","All patients are treated identically regardless of need","First-come-first-served as the only ethical approach"]'::jsonb, 'Priority to healthcare workers and the most vulnerable when allocating scarce resources', 'Justice during pandemics often involves a tiered approach prioritizing those with the greatest need and those essential to the pandemic response (healthcare workers) while ensuring equitable access and avoiding discrimination.'),
('6a5fde19-8e91-5aa0-8ff4-980f774b45ac'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'The Mini-Mental State Examination (MMSE) is used to:', '["Assess cognitive function and screen for dementia","Measure physical fitness","Test visual acuity","Assess cardiovascular fitness","Evaluate hearing"]'::jsonb, 'Assess cognitive function and screen for dementia', 'The MMSE is a widely used screening tool for cognitive impairment. It assesses orientation attention memory language and visuospatial skills with a maximum score of 30. Scores below 24 suggest possible cognitive impairment.'),
('82a423ac-e4e2-5747-883c-e6ff727480d9'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'Type II error (beta) in statistics refers to:', '["Rejecting the null hypothesis when it is true","Failing to reject the null hypothesis when it is false","Rejecting the null hypothesis when it is false","Accepting the alternative hypothesis automatically","Failing to reject the null hypothesis when it is true"]'::jsonb, 'Failing to reject the null hypothesis when it is false', 'A Type II error occurs when the null hypothesis is not rejected when it is actually false (false negative). Power (1-beta) is the probability of correctly rejecting a false null hypothesis.'),
('c1413afa-6b76-5b2a-9783-4783854a4003'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'The Futility Debate in medical ethics centers on:', '["Whether any treatment is ever futile","Who should decide when treatment is futile and whether patients/families can demand futile treatment","Whether all treatments should be offered regardless of benefit","Cost of futile treatments","Legal definitions of death"]'::jsonb, 'Who should decide when treatment is futile and whether patients/families can demand futile treatment', 'The futility debate addresses whether physicians must provide treatments they deem medically futile and who has authority to make futility determinations. It also addresses whether patients/families can demand treatments physicians consider futile.'),
('be9a7169-a5ad-5c86-b85e-1af1aef1197f'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'The CAGE Questionnaire screens for:', '["Anxiety","Depression","PTSD","Dementia","Alcohol use disorders"]'::jsonb, 'Alcohol use disorders', 'The CAGE questionnaire (Cut down Annoyed Guilty Eye-opener) is a brief screening tool for alcohol use disorders. Two or more positive responses suggest clinically significant alcohol problems.'),
('f117ae2d-b7be-5962-97db-62e154b5e553'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'Which of the following best describes Resilience in psychology?', '["The absence of all stress","The ability to adapt and bounce back from adversity trauma or stress","The complete avoidance of difficult situations","A personality type that never experiences stress","Genetic immunity to stress"]'::jsonb, 'The ability to adapt and bounce back from adversity trauma or stress', 'Resilience is the process of adapting well in the face of significant sources of stress - trauma tragedy threats or adversity. It involves behaviors thoughts and actions that can be learned and developed.'),
('b51372bb-be5d-5616-a63b-79c815bad5f5'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'The Duty to Warn established in the Tarasoff case specifically applies when:', '["The physician feels uncomfortable clinical presentation","The patient is angry clinical presentation","The patient has a psychiatric diagnosis clinical presentation","There is a specific threat of serious violence against an identifiable victim","Any patient makes any threat clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'There is a specific threat of serious violence against an identifiable victim', 'The Tarasoff duty specifically applies when a patient makes a credible threat of serious physical violence against a reasonably identifiable victim. It does not apply to vague threats or to threats without identifiable targets.'),
('ffc8fcbd-4571-56e8-8e9d-1d179b80ac65'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'Nominal Group Technique is used in healthcare for:', '["Group decision-making and consensus building","Statistical analysis clinical presentation","Naming diseases clinical presentation","Patient identification clinical presentation","Surgical planning clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Group decision-making and consensus building', 'Nominal Group Technique is a structured method for group brainstorming that encourages contributions from all members. It is used in healthcare for priority setting guideline development and quality improvement.'),
('d1b2e443-728e-5c6e-b52b-7889244cb2b0'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'The Allais Paradox is an example of:', '["A research methodology issue","A logical contradiction in expected utility theory","An ethical dilemma in medicine","A surgical complication","A diagnostic paradox"]'::jsonb, 'A logical contradiction in expected utility theory', 'The Allais Paradox demonstrates a violation of the independence axiom of expected utility theory and illustrates how people choices under uncertainty often violate rational choice theory. It is relevant to medical decision-making under risk.'),
('0fd34342-a2ae-54b1-96a8-135a7be78784'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'Which of the following is an example of secondary gain in illness behavior?', '["The side effects of medication clinical presentation","The placebo effect clinical presentation","The nocebo effect clinical presentation","A patient recovering from illness clinical presentation","A patient deriving benefits from being ill such as attention or avoidance of responsibility"]'::jsonb, 'A patient deriving benefits from being ill such as attention or avoidance of responsibility', 'Secondary gain refers to the social interpersonal or financial advantages that a patient may gain from being ill or in the sick role such as receiving attention sympathy relief from responsibilities or disability benefits.'),
('229002dc-c65d-50f2-9e23-293f0d9a56a0'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'A physician performs a surgery to remove a healthy organ from a patient who has requested it for non-medical reasons. This is:', '["Standard cosmetic surgery clinical presentation","An acceptable cultural practice clinical presentation","Respecting autonomy clinical presentation","A violation of non-maleficence and beneficence as surgery has risks without medical benefit","Ethically acceptable if the patient is competent clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'A violation of non-maleficence and beneficence as surgery has risks without medical benefit', 'Performing surgery without medical indication that exposes the patient to significant risks violates non-maleficence (do no harm) and beneficence (do good). Even respecting autonomy does not obligate physicians to provide harmful unnecessary procedures.'),
('40ff2dd1-f8e7-5d91-b962-0e1c35a9916e'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'The Alma Ata Declaration (1978) emphasized:', '["Primary healthcare as the key to achieving health for all","Research funding clinical presentation","Hospital-based care clinical presentation","Privatization of healthcare clinical presentation","Specialist medicine clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Primary healthcare as the key to achieving health for all', 'The Alma Ata Declaration affirmed that primary healthcare is essential to achieving health for all. It emphasized community participation appropriate technology and intersectoral collaboration for health.'),
('96765bcd-1148-50ed-8854-f88e722fcd2a'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'Risk Perception in health behavior is influenced by:', '["Actual statistical risk only clinical presentation","Genetic testing only clinical presentation","How likely and severe a person perceives a threat to be","The physician opinion only clinical presentation","Media reports only clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'How likely and severe a person perceives a threat to be', 'Risk perception in health behavior is influenced by perceived likelihood (probability of harm) and perceived severity (seriousness of consequences). These are key constructs in the Health Belief Model.'),
('c5704723-d574-5d76-a5f6-799a7718f767'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'Brain Death is defined as:', '["The loss of cognitive function only clinical presentation","A coma with some brainstem reflexes clinical presentation","The irreversible cessation of all brain function including the brainstem","The cessation of heartbeat clinical presentation","The cessation of consciousness only clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'The irreversible cessation of all brain function including the brainstem', 'Brain death is the irreversible cessation of all functions of the entire brain including the brainstem. It is legally recognized as death in most jurisdictions and differs from persistent vegetative state or coma.'),
('ac83cc42-f561-5203-9e6d-edc1aac74860'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'A patient Illness Narrative is important to understand because:', '["It provides the patient perspective on their illness experience which is essential for patient-ce","It is mostly irrelevant to clinical care clinical presentation","It replaces the medical history clinical presentation","It is only useful in psychiatric settings clinical presentation","It takes too much time clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'It provides the patient perspective on their illness experience which is essential for patient-ce', 'Understanding the patient illness narrative (their personal story of how they experience and make sense of their illness) is essential for patient-centered care building trust and understanding the impact of illness on their life.'),
('8b43164e-b793-5920-9764-c8b7583e2ead'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'The Type D (Distressed) personality is associated with:', '["Better surgical recovery","Better cardiovascular outcomes","Lower stress levels","Increased risk of heart disease and poorer prognosis","No health effects"]'::jsonb, 'Increased risk of heart disease and poorer prognosis', 'Type D personality (Denollet) is characterized by negative affectivity and social inhibition. It is associated with an increased risk of cardiovascular disease and poorer prognosis in cardiac patients.'),
('3b293ff7-54c6-5951-b8c8-24600f278055'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'Which of the following demonstrates respect for patient dignity?', '["Calling a patient by their first name without asking","Knocking before entering a patient room and addressing them respectfully","Discussing the patient case loudly in the hallway","Ignoring patient modesty during examinations","Completing procedures without explaining"]'::jsonb, 'Knocking before entering a patient room and addressing them respectfully', 'Respecting patient dignity involves simple courtesies like knocking before entering addressing patients respectfully maintaining privacy during examinations and treating patients with respect regardless of their condition or background.'),
('58cca79d-790b-5c5b-aff9-9fffd244ea82'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'Pure Altruism in medicine refers to:', '["Providing care for research purposes clinical presentation","Providing care because it is mandatory clinical presentation","Providing care motivated solely by concern for the patient welfare without expectation of persona","Providing care only to specific patients clinical presentation","Providing care primarily for financial benefit clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Providing care motivated solely by concern for the patient welfare without expectation of persona', 'Altruism in medicine involves placing the patient interests above one own and providing care motivated by concern for the patient welfare. It is a core value of medical professionalism.'),
('65aa5b85-057e-5408-948c-71c22c366d34'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'Vulnerable Populations in research include all EXCEPT:', '["Medical students when appropriate safeguards are in place","University professors","Prisoners","Pregnant women","Children"]'::jsonb, 'University professors', 'Vulnerable populations in research include prisoners pregnant women fetuses children cognitively impaired persons and economically/educationally disadvantaged persons. University professors are not inherently a vulnerable population.'),
('56badacd-154d-5025-8812-1cf7a7e8d2f0'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'Compliance with medical regimens is more likely when:', '["The patient understands the rationale and the regimen fits their lifestyle","The patient has multiple providers clinical presentation","The physician is authoritarian clinical presentation","The cost is high clinical presentation","The regimen is complex and frequent clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'The patient understands the rationale and the regimen fits their lifestyle', 'Compliance/adherence improves when patients understand why treatment is needed when the regimen is simple and fits into their daily routine and when they have a good relationship with their healthcare provider.'),
('2914f976-a911-5ab4-8b4c-f0b7b0b9ef53'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'The Healthy Worker Effect in occupational epidemiology refers to:', '["Workers being less healthy than the general population","The effect of workplace safety on health","The effect of exercise on workers","The psychological benefits of work","Workers being healthier than the general population due to selection of healthier individuals int"]'::jsonb, 'Workers being healthier than the general population due to selection of healthier individuals int', 'The healthy worker effect is a selection bias in occupational studies where workers are generally healthier than the general population because people who are ill are less likely to be employed. This can underestimate occupational health risks.'),
('c1b0ac4c-f23e-52ea-9fc7-f2c788873256'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'Prognostic Paralysis occurs when:', '["Patients make decisions too quickly clinical presentation","Patients become unable to make decisions due to overwhelming anxiety about prognosis","Physicians fail to make diagnoses clinical presentation","Paralysis from medical conditions clinical presentation","The effect of anesthesia clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Patients become unable to make decisions due to overwhelming anxiety about prognosis', 'Prognostic paralysis describes the phenomenon where patients or families become unable to make decisions when faced with overwhelming information about a poor prognosis. This requires sensitive communication and support from the healthcare team.'),
('91cddb62-3180-5e98-959b-3538b364402b'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'Which of the following is a core function of a Clinical Ethics Committee?', '["Case consultation policy development and education","Patient billing clinical presentation","Pharmacy management clinical presentation","Disciplining physicians clinical presentation","Surgical scheduling clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Case consultation policy development and education', 'Clinical ethics committees typically have three core functions: case consultation (advising on ethical issues in specific cases) policy development (creating institutional ethics policies) and education (teaching ethics to healthcare professionals).'),
('70d5539f-3ed5-5df6-b38e-6090bb8a50c0'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'Disease Mongering concept refers to:', '["The treatment of rare diseases","The study of disease patterns","The global burden of disease","The marketing of treatments for genuine diseases","Widening the boundaries of treatable illness to expand markets for medical products"]'::jsonb, 'Widening the boundaries of treatable illness to expand markets for medical products', 'Disease mongering describes the practice of promoting the medicalization of normal conditions or minor symptoms to expand markets for pharmaceutical or medical products. It raises ethical concerns about over-diagnosis and over-treatment.'),
('1539ef16-75f0-5d9c-8568-89cfd0783a34'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'Tolerance in pain management refers to:', '["The need for increasing doses to achieve the same effect","An adverse reaction to medication","The development of addiction","Allergic reaction","The ceiling effect of medication"]'::jsonb, 'The need for increasing doses to achieve the same effect', 'Tolerance is a pharmacological phenomenon where repeated exposure to a drug results in diminished effect requiring higher doses to achieve the same effect. It differs from addiction and dependence.'),
('127b97a4-ccc2-51f4-834e-55aa15a9af4c'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'The appropriate ethical action is:', '["Ignore it to protect the colleague","Report the colleague to appropriate authorities to protect patient safety","Confront the colleague alone","Discuss it with other colleagues informally","Wait until a patient is harmed"]'::jsonb, 'Report the colleague to appropriate authorities to protect patient safety', 'Physicians have an ethical duty to report impaired colleagues to appropriate monitoring or regulatory bodies to protect patient safety. This is consistent with the principle of non-maleficence.'),
('d361cd8c-0ff0-5290-a9a1-58ffc929b8e3'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'The Conflict Between Absolute and Relative Poverty in health contexts highlights that:', '["Only developed countries have health disparities clinical presentation","The relative position in social hierarchy affects health even after basic needs are met","Only absolute poverty affects health clinical presentation","Poverty has no effect on health clinical presentation","Wealth has no effect on health clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'The relative position in social hierarchy affects health even after basic needs are met', 'Research shows that health is affected by relative deprivation not just absolute poverty. Even in affluent societies those lower in the social hierarchy have worse health outcomes than those above them.'),
('381d02b4-6642-5017-8143-2e13e5b179e4'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'Environmental Sensitivity in medical ethics considers:', '["The effect of climate on health clinical presentation","How the healthcare environment affects patient well-being and ethical care","The sensitivity of medical equipment clinical presentation","Environmental pollution clinical presentation","The sensitivity of diagnostic tests clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'How the healthcare environment affects patient well-being and ethical care', 'Environmental sensitivity in ethics considers how the physical and social environment of healthcare (including privacy noise lighting and design) affects patient comfort dignity and quality of care.'),
('aefecd85-40af-5859-8ff6-d6460b8fd5f8'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'The Dunning-Kruger Effect is relevant to medical education because:', '["It is not relevant to medicine","It shows that expert clinicians always underestimate their abilities","It demonstrates that medical students are always overconfident","It highlights the need for accurate self-assessment and feedback in training","It proves that confidence equals competence"]'::jsonb, 'It highlights the need for accurate self-assessment and feedback in training', 'The Dunning-Kruger effect highlights how those with limited competence tend to overestimate their abilities while experts may underestimate theirs. This underscores the importance of objective feedback and assessment in medical training.'),
('53f31505-01d9-5582-acf4-f787936e80a8'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'Therapeutic Non-Disclosure in dementia care involves:', '["Always telling the truth regardless of consequences clinical presentation","Balancing truth-telling with protecting the patient from psychological harm based on their capaci","Disclosing information only to the family clinical presentation","Lying to the patient always clinical presentation","Withholding all information clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Balancing truth-telling with protecting the patient from psychological harm based on their capaci', 'Therapeutic non-disclosure in dementia involves careful consideration of whether disclosing certain information would cause the patient more harm than good given their cognitive status. It should be individualized and rarely applied.'),
('a564f3e9-e5be-5eae-8c92-6505ed9fa6fb'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'Palliative Sedation differs from euthanasia in that:', '["The intention is to cause death","The intention is to relieve suffering not to cause death","Euthanasia is always legal","Palliative sedation is not used in terminal care","It involves the same medications"]'::jsonb, 'The intention is to relieve suffering not to cause death', 'Palliative sedation involves using medications to reduce consciousness in patients with severe refractory symptoms at end of life. The intention is symptom relief not hastening death which distinguishes it ethically from euthanasia.'),
('9c85dff2-5f6f-513e-b965-9f4d35f6e6f2'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'The McGill Pain Questionnaire measures:', '["The location of pain only clinical presentation","The sensory affective and evaluative dimensions of pain","Only the intensity of pain clinical presentation","The cause of pain clinical presentation","Only the duration of pain clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'The sensory affective and evaluative dimensions of pain', 'The McGill Pain Questionnaire is a multidimensional pain assessment tool that measures sensory (quality/location) affective (emotional impact) and evaluative (overall intensity) dimensions of pain experience.'),
('5ee15145-ed38-5912-a3f3-310922350f83'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'This violates:', '["Beneficence","Confidentiality","Respect for persons","Informed consent","Justice"]'::jsonb, 'Justice', 'The principle of justice requires that research fairly distributes benefits and burdens. Including disadvantaged groups without ensuring they benefit from the research (helicopter research) exploits their vulnerability and violates justice.'),
('781da0a5-a7b3-520c-8f11-5ff8de0ff505'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'Which is NOT one of the Four Principles of biomedical ethics?', '["Justice","Beneficence","Autonomy","Fidelity","Non-maleficence"]'::jsonb, 'Fidelity', 'The four principles of biomedical ethics (Beauchamp and Childress) are: Respect for Autonomy Beneficence Non-Maleficence and Justice. Fidelity while important is not one of the four core principles.'),
('07ebb686-a4d1-59bc-9fbb-98ff09297cb8'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'Response Bias in survey research refers to:', '["The biological response to medication","The tendency of respondents to answer inaccurately or falsely","The response rate of surveys","The speed of response","The clinical response to treatment"]'::jsonb, 'The tendency of respondents to answer inaccurately or falsely', 'Response bias is a general term for a wide range of tendencies for participants to respond inaccurately or falsely to questions. It includes social desirability bias acquiescence bias and extreme response bias.'),
('8d46e500-a8a0-5129-8e04-a99a4f4ba544'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'Bad News in healthcare is defined as:', '["Only terminal diagnoses","Only cancer diagnoses","Any information that negatively alters a patient view of their future","Minor test abnormalities","Information that makes the doctor uncomfortable"]'::jsonb, 'Any information that negatively alters a patient view of their future', 'Bad news is defined as any information that adversely and seriously affects an individual view of their future or life. It includes not just terminal diagnoses but also chronic conditions disability and treatment failures.'),
('c81d246f-f639-5eff-9f07-880a2c172c91'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'Patient Activation refers to:', '["The activation of hospital protocols","The patient knowledge skills and confidence in managing their own health","The physician activating treatment plans","The patient being fully passive","The activation of medical devices"]'::jsonb, 'The patient knowledge skills and confidence in managing their own health', 'Patient activation describes the knowledge skills and confidence a person has in managing their own health and healthcare. Activated patients are more likely to engage in healthy behaviors and have better health outcomes.'),
('c3233cb8-af5d-52fb-ab66-df637d857744'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'The Rule of Rescue in medical ethics describes:', '["The military medical code","The duty to rescue someone in an emergency","The tendency to prioritize identifiable individuals in need over statistical lives","The ethical code for rescue workers","The legal requirement to provide emergency care"]'::jsonb, 'The tendency to prioritize identifiable individuals in need over statistical lives', 'The rule of rescue describes the powerful human tendency to intervene to help identifiable individuals in immediate danger even when the same resources could save more anonymous lives. This creates ethical tension in resource allocation.'),
('f55cf020-6526-5fa8-90f2-474f5b98fd9e'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'Illness Behavior as described by Mechanic refers to:', '["The way individuals monitor their bodies define symptoms take remedial action and use healthcare","The biological progression of disease clinical presentation","The behavior of healthcare providers clinical presentation","The behavior of healthcare systems clinical presentation","The cost of illness clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'The way individuals monitor their bodies define symptoms take remedial action and use healthcare', 'Mechanic concept of illness behavior describes the varying ways individuals respond to bodily symptoms monitor their internal states define and interpret symptoms make attributions and decide to take remedial action including seeking healthcare.'),
('2687e2f1-0671-5515-99fe-887581c6bca5'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'Which of the following best describes nudging in healthcare?', '["Subtle changes in choice architecture that predictably influence behavior without restricting opt","Ignoring patient preferences clinical presentation","Forcing patients to make specific choices clinical presentation","Deceiving patients into making decisions clinical presentation","Eliminating patient choices clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Subtle changes in choice architecture that predictably influence behavior without restricting opt', 'Nudging (Thaler and Sunstein) involves altering choice architecture in predictable ways to influence behavior without forbidding any options or significantly changing economic incentives. Examples include default options and simplifying information.'),
('900da2c6-cc79-5838-ade9-12f28de038fe'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'The Expanded version of the Health Belief Model includes which additional construct?', '["Severity","Benefits","Self-efficacy","Barriers","Susceptibility"]'::jsonb, 'Self-efficacy', 'The original Health Belief Model included perceived susceptibility severity benefits and barriers and cues to action. Self-efficacy was added later to improve the model predictive power for behavior change particularly for complex behaviors.'),
('be10b9a1-567c-5357-aa56-a16365a53ed9'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'A physician who maintains a sexual relationship with a former patient:', '["Is always permitted after treatment ends clinical presentation","Is encouraged in some cultures clinical presentation","May still be unethical due to the ongoing power imbalance and trust established during the therap","Is always legal clinical presentation","Is never a concern clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'May still be unethical due to the ongoing power imbalance and trust established during the therap', 'Even after the physician-patient relationship ends sexual relationships may be unethical because of the enduring power imbalance trust and dependency established during treatment. Many medical boards prohibit or restrict such relationships.'),
('b054ed2e-0ca5-5403-9a10-622be72a6add'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'Comorbidity refers to:', '["The simultaneous presence of two or more diseases or conditions in a patient","Only mental health conditions clinical presentation","The cause of a disease clinical presentation","The treatment of disease clinical presentation","The prevention of disease clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'The simultaneous presence of two or more diseases or conditions in a patient', 'Comorbidity describes the coexistence of two or more distinct diseases or health conditions in the same patient. It has implications for treatment complexity prognosis and healthcare utilization.'),
('7dfdbab6-299e-5fff-b057-bd01af1ef2d5'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'The Ethics of Artificial Intelligence in healthcare raises concerns about:', '["Algorithmic bias transparency accountability and impact on the doctor-patient relationship","The color of AI devices clinical presentation","The cost of AI development clinical presentation","The speed of AI processing clinical presentation","The hardware requirements of AI clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Algorithmic bias transparency accountability and impact on the doctor-patient relationship', 'AI in healthcare raises ethical concerns about algorithmic bias (perpetuating health disparities) transparency (black box problem) accountability (who is responsible for AI errors) and the impact on the human aspects of medical care.'),
('736afb1a-45a0-56c5-957c-2e6d3d352783'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'Somatoform Disorders are characterized by:', '["Imaginary symptoms clinical presentation","Malingering for financial gain clinical presentation","Intentional symptom production clinical presentation","Physical symptoms with clear organic cause clinical presentation","Physical symptoms suggesting medical disease but without demonstrable organic findings or known p"]'::jsonb, 'Physical symptoms suggesting medical disease but without demonstrable organic findings or known p', 'Somatoform disorders involve physical symptoms that suggest a medical condition but are not fully explained by a general medical condition substance use or another mental disorder. They are distinct from factitious disorders and malingering.'),
('f67eb82b-c693-5a35-8797-e2ede53ded05'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'Regarding the medical ethics context, In a surgical context, The physician should:', '["Respect the patient wishes regarding disclosure to family members","Ignore the patient request","Inform the family against the patient wishes","Only disclose to the family if they ask","Refuse to treat the patient"]'::jsonb, 'Respect the patient wishes regarding disclosure to family members', 'The physician primary duty is to the patient. If a competent patient does not want information disclosed to family members the physician should respect this unless there is a legal requirement or serious harm to others.'),
('62598622-93b0-5ae9-96f8-6160be4503bc'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'The Screening Paradox refers to:', '["Screening tests always preventing disease clinical presentation","The phenomenon where screening may detect indolent conditions that would not have caused harm lea","Screening is always effective clinical presentation","Screening never prevents disease clinical presentation","Screening is only for rich countries clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'The phenomenon where screening may detect indolent conditions that would not have caused harm lea', 'The screening paradox includes overdiagnosis where screening detects abnormalities (like some cancers) that would never have caused symptoms or death leading to unnecessary treatment and anxiety. This creates ethical tension in screening programs.'),
('45ab7549-27ea-590f-a108-fa9fdb7b3b41'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'Paradigm Shift in the context of medical ethics refers to:', '["A fundamental change in the underlying assumptions or framework of ethics","A small adjustment in policy clinical presentation","A change in hospital administration clinical presentation","A change in medication clinical presentation","A change in surgical technique clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'A fundamental change in the underlying assumptions or framework of ethics', 'A paradigm shift (Kuhn) is a fundamental change in the basic concepts and experimental practices of a scientific discipline. The transition from paternalistic medicine to patient autonomy represents a paradigm shift in medical ethics.'),
('b5cf0029-1f7a-5634-b8ae-c49476afb1ef'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'The Cochrane Collaboration is known for:', '["Conducting clinical trials clinical presentation","Licensing physicians clinical presentation","Developing new drugs clinical presentation","Systematic reviews and meta-analyses of healthcare interventions","Accrediting hospitals clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Systematic reviews and meta-analyses of healthcare interventions', 'The Cochrane Collaboration (now Cochrane) is an independent international network that produces systematic reviews of healthcare interventions. It is a leading resource for evidence-based medicine.'),
('7ae44858-f3f6-55ca-9109-cf53686f131b'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'Paternalism is most ethically problematic when:', '["It is used in emergencies when the patient is incapacitated","It involves children","It involves elderly patients","It disregards the known wishes of a competent patient","It is used in psychiatric settings"]'::jsonb, 'It disregards the known wishes of a competent patient', 'Paternalism overriding the known wishes of a competent patient is deeply problematic because it violates patient autonomy. The strongest justification for paternalism is when the patient is incapacitated and there is no known preference.'),
('e5c6cd1b-15fe-5936-a685-7df0f8e3e3d4'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'Which of the following is an example of primary data in research?', '["Data from government statistics clinical presentation","Data from insurance claims clinical presentation","Data collected directly by the researcher for the purpose of the study","Data from hospital records clinical presentation","Data from published literature clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Data collected directly by the researcher for the purpose of the study', 'Primary data is data collected firsthand by the researcher specifically for the research question at hand. Secondary data is data that already exists collected for other purposes.'),
('389d4709-8697-5e7e-be70-2dc9c3b707d0'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'Recall Bias is most likely in which study design?', '["Randomized controlled trial","Cross-sectional study","Clinical trial","Case-control study","Cohort study"]'::jsonb, 'Case-control study', 'Recall bias is a systematic error that occurs when participants do not remember past events accurately. It is particularly problematic in case-control studies where cases may recall exposures differently than controls.'),
('028a1c10-3465-545d-ba1e-1ea48797e58d'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'Evidence-Based Practice in psychology emphasizes:', '["Using clinical judgment alone clinical presentation clinical presentation","The integration of best available research with clinical expertise and patient characteristics","Ignoring patient preferences clinical presentation clinical presentation","Only using medication clinical presentation clinical presentation","Relying solely on tradition clinical presentation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'The integration of best available research with clinical expertise and patient characteristics', 'Evidence-based practice in psychology (and medicine) involves integrating the best available research evidence with clinical expertise and patient characteristics culture and preferences to guide interventions.'),
('e65c3339-32e0-503d-8d02-0b3ed257dc19'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'A physician who cherry-picks healthy patients and avoids sicker patients to maintain better outcomes is:', '["Complying with hospital policy clinical presentation","Following evidence-based practice clinical presentation","Demonstrating good business sense clinical presentation","Respecting patient autonomy clinical presentation","Violating the ethical duty of non-abandonment and professionalism"]'::jsonb, 'Violating the ethical duty of non-abandonment and professionalism', 'Selectively accepting only healthy patients (cherry-picking) while avoiding complex or sicker patients violates professional obligations to care for all patients in need and undermines the trust in the medical profession.'),
('9540b5a1-4ed3-54c1-bef0-2da6914fdf8d'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'The Glossary of Terms in medical ethics distinguishes euthanasia from physician-assisted suicide based on:', '["The setting of care","Who administers the lethal agent","The cost of the procedure","The type of medication","The patient age"]'::jsonb, 'Who administers the lethal agent', 'The key distinction is who performs the final act. In euthanasia the physician administers the lethal agent. In physician-assisted suicide the physician provides the means (prescription) but the patient self-administers.'),
('80f11ec4-f586-5152-bb22-c618e20f2b68'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'Which statement best describes the Social Construction of Illness?', '["The meaning and experience of illness are shaped by social cultural and historical contexts","The biological basis of illness is irrelevant clinical presentation","Illness is always socially determined clinical presentation","Only mental illness is socially constructed clinical presentation","Illnesses are purely biological facts clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'The meaning and experience of illness are shaped by social cultural and historical contexts', 'The social construction of illness perspective argues that how we define understand experience and respond to illness is shaped by social cultural and historical factors not just biological reality. Examples include how homosexuality was pathologized and de-pathologized.'),
('4db73b95-c051-528f-b1ab-12801770c9c1'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'Therapeutic Privilege is generally considered:', '["An absolute right of the physician clinical presentation","Commonly used by all physicians clinical presentation","Required by law clinical presentation","The patient right to know everything clinical presentation","A narrow exception that should be exercised rarely and with careful documentation"]'::jsonb, 'A narrow exception that should be exercised rarely and with careful documentation', 'Therapeutic privilege (withholding information to prevent harm) is considered a narrow exception that must be exercised cautiously and documented thoroughly. Most ethical guidelines emphasize truth-telling as the default.'),
('1a7db1c4-b132-50df-afbf-ce27bbb131a2'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'The Diffusion of Innovation theory explains:', '["The diffusion of gases in the lungs","The spread of infectious diseases","How new ideas and practices spread through populations","The spread of cancer","The diffusion of medication in the body"]'::jsonb, 'How new ideas and practices spread through populations', 'Diffusion of Innovation theory (Rogers) explains how why and at what rate new ideas and technology spread through cultures. It identifies adopter categories: innovators early adopters early majority late majority and laggards.'),
('4fb6429b-b995-57b5-9388-37155eb5e6a7'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'Which of the following is an example of secondary prevention in public health?', '["Health education about healthy diet","Palliative care","Vaccination","Screening for hypertension","Rehabilitation after stroke"]'::jsonb, 'Screening for hypertension', 'Secondary prevention involves early detection of disease in asymptomatic individuals through screening (e.g. blood pressure screening mammography) to enable early intervention and prevent progression.'),
('a2fe9fe9-7116-58d0-937d-cf8de182f6eb'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'The Gini Coefficient is a measure of:', '["Income inequality within a population","Healthcare spending","Life expectancy","Healthcare quality","Medical education quality"]'::jsonb, 'Income inequality within a population', 'The Gini coefficient is a statistical measure of income or wealth inequality within a population. It ranges from 0 (perfect equality) to 1 (maximal inequality) and is used in health equity research.'),
('26611ca4-dfa9-5c79-a219-f0cc9c0dfe36'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'A physician who is religiously opposed to providing contraception should:', '["Ignore the patient request","Refuse all care to any patient who asks about contraception","Impose their beliefs on the patient","Provide information about contraception or refer the patient to another provider who can help","Only discuss it if the patient insists"]'::jsonb, 'Provide information about contraception or refer the patient to another provider who can help', 'Physicians with conscientious objections should not abandon patients. They should inform patients of their objection provide information about available options and refer to another provider who can provide the service.'),
('562542f2-aa18-576e-b91b-658026c658a1'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'Social Capital in health refers to:', '["Financial resources for healthcare clinical presentation","The stock of medical equipment clinical presentation","The capital needed for building hospitals clinical presentation","The networks relationships and norms that shape the quality and quantity of social interactions","The government budget for health clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'The networks relationships and norms that shape the quality and quantity of social interactions', 'Social capital refers to the features of social organization such as networks norms and social trust that facilitate coordination and cooperation for mutual benefit. Higher social capital is associated with better health outcomes.'),
('189dd5a9-e4db-5bba-bb44-87c9279e056a'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'The Biopsychosocial Model is particularly important in understanding:', '["Trauma which is purely surgical clinical presentation","Acute infections which are purely biological clinical presentation","Emergency conditions clinical presentation","Genetic disorders which are purely biological clinical presentation","Chronic pain conditions where biological psychological and social factors all contribute"]'::jsonb, 'Chronic pain conditions where biological psychological and social factors all contribute', 'The biopsychosocial model is especially valuable for understanding chronic pain where biological factors (tissue damage) interact with psychological factors (catastrophizing fear) and social factors (workplace issues litigation) to determine outcomes.'),
('230ea741-c615-5f0e-afa5-bff4f5d15b18'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'This obligation is based on:', '["Personal preference clinical presentation","Professionalism and the social contract of medicine","Profit motive clinical presentation","Legal requirement only clinical presentation","Avoiding lawsuits clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Professionalism and the social contract of medicine', 'The duty to treat patients with communicable diseases even at personal risk has long been recognized as part of medical professionalism. It is rooted in the social contract and the historical tradition of physicians caring for patients during epidemics.'),
('166bcccd-92b5-54d0-acb6-1f2691add2ce'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'Whistleblowing in healthcare refers to:', '["The process of patient discharge clinical presentation","The sound of medical instruments clinical presentation","The reporting of unethical or illegal practices within an organization","The routine reporting of patient data clinical presentation","The whistle used in surgery clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'The reporting of unethical or illegal practices within an organization', 'Whistleblowing involves reporting unethical illegal or unsafe practices within an organization to those who can take action. Healthcare whistleblowers often face retaliation but are protected by various laws and ethical codes.'),
('bf2289e1-0a6f-5f6b-a576-5c0ec60aeedd'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'The Number Needed to Treat (NNT) is a measure of:', '["The number of patients who experience side effects","The number of patients who need to be treated to prevent one adverse outcome","The total number of patients in a study","The number of treatments a patient receives","The number needed to cause harm"]'::jsonb, 'The number of patients who need to be treated to prevent one adverse outcome', 'NNT is the average number of patients who need to be treated with a specific intervention to prevent one additional bad outcome. It is a useful measure of clinical effectiveness and helps communicate treatment benefits.'),
('fcc5b8ce-8574-57cb-81cd-759739009ee4'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'Moral Hazard in health insurance refers to:', '["Insurance fraud clinical presentation","Increased utilization of healthcare services when insured because the cost is reduced","People committing immoral acts clinical presentation","Hazards of medical treatment clinical presentation","The hazard of moral dilemmas clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Increased utilization of healthcare services when insured because the cost is reduced', 'Moral hazard in health economics describes the tendency of insured individuals to use more healthcare services than they would if they bore the full cost because insurance reduces the out-of-pocket price they face at the point of service.'),
('1cc69a98-6739-5177-a214-80dcea345686'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'Caregiver Burden refers to:', '["The cost of healthcare clinical presentation","The physical emotional and financial strain experienced by those caring for ill or disabled famil","The burden of disease on society clinical presentation","The workload of healthcare professionals clinical presentation","The burden of hospital administration clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'The physical emotional and financial strain experienced by those caring for ill or disabled famil', 'Caregiver burden is the multidimensional response to the physical psychological emotional social and financial stressors associated with caring for a family member or loved one with a chronic or disabling condition.'),
('45a1e819-9d3d-5e51-b9e5-94a825cf8fde'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'Non-Abandonment in medical ethics means that:', '["Physicians must treat all patients forever","Physicians can only treat patients in hospitals","Abandonment is never an issue","Physicians can stop treating patients at any time without notice","Once a physician-patient relationship is established the physician should not unilaterally withdr"]'::jsonb, 'Once a physician-patient relationship is established the physician should not unilaterally withdr', 'Non-abandonment is the ethical obligation that once a physician accepts a patient for care the physician should continue to provide care and not terminate the relationship without reasonable notice and opportunity for the patient to find another provider.'),
('fafaf0ed-225b-5b98-98df-c77788b8c0bb'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'The Elicit-Provide-Elicit framework is used in:', '["Discharge planning clinical presentation clinical presentation","Surgical technique clinical presentation clinical presentation","Diagnostic procedures clinical presentation clinical presentation","Prescribing medications clinical presentation clinical presentation","Communication about behavior change where the healthcare provider elicits the patient perspective"]'::jsonb, 'Communication about behavior change where the healthcare provider elicits the patient perspective', 'Elicit-Provide-Elicit is a patient-centered communication technique used in motivational interviewing. The provider first asks about the patient understanding then provides information tailored to the patient and finally asks for the patient interpretation or reaction.'),
('259f6a0b-2ef5-59c1-929f-1351f45084d1'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'Which of the following is most likely to improve cross-cultural communication?', '["Speaking louder clinical presentation","Using medical interpreters when needed and showing respect for cultural differences","Refusing to discuss cultural issues clinical presentation","Assuming all cultural groups are the same clinical presentation","Only using written materials clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Using medical interpreters when needed and showing respect for cultural differences', 'Using qualified medical interpreters respecting cultural differences showing curiosity and avoiding assumptions are key to effective cross-cultural communication. This improves trust adherence and health outcomes.'),
('03955542-0786-5dcb-8ed5-12a47897726e'::uuid, 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Behavioral Science and Medical Ethics', 'The ultimate goal of applying the biopsychosocial model in clinical practice is to:', '["Provide the most expensive care possible clinical presentation","Standardize all treatments clinical presentation","Provide comprehensive care that addresses biological psychological and social aspects of the pati","Eliminate all patient suffering clinical presentation","Reduce healthcare costs clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Provide comprehensive care that addresses biological psychological and social aspects of the pati', 'The biopsychosocial model aims to provide holistic patient-centered care that recognizes the interplay of biological psychological and social factors in health and illness. It leads to more comprehensive effective and humane healthcare.');
